data: {"stage": "done", "message": "{\"query\": \"garantie de qualité vice caché conditions grave antérieur inconnu de l’acheteur caché\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-16T13:38:11.548072-04:00\", \"title_query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\", \"results\": {\"legislation\": [{\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1726: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1726 Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l’acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté, ou n’aurait pas donné si haut prix, s’il les avait connus. Il n’est, cependant, pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l’acheteur ni le vice apparent; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert. 1991, c. 64, a. 1726 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1726 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1719. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (Projet de loi 125). Livre V - Des obligations (De l'Affrètement, du Transport et de l'Assurance maritime) , p. 7 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1726 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l'acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l'usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l'acheteur ne l'aurait pas acheté, ou n'aurait pas donné si haut prix, s'il les avait connus. Il n'est, cependant, pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l'acheteur ni le vice apparent; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert Article 1726 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1522, 1523 O.R.C.C. : L. V, articles 373, 374 Commentaires Cet article reprend en substance le droit antérieur qu'énonçaient les articles 1522 et 1523 C.C.B.C., quant à la garantie du vendeur contre les vices cachés, mais en y apportant certaines précisions. Ainsi, le premier alinéa précise, conformément à la doctrine et à la jurisprudence, que le vice doit, pour être garanti, exister lors de la vente. De même, le second alinéa, conformément à la doctrine et à la jurisprudence, crée une exception pour le vice caché connu de l'acheteur et pour le vice apparent. Il met également un terme à une longue controverse, en rejetant expressément l'obligation pour l'acheteur d'avoir recours à un expert pour examiner le bien avant l'achat. Il consacre, en fait, une règle qui se dégage de la jurisprudence majoritaire en matière mobilière et l'étend au domaine immobilier. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03333333507180214, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1726: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\", \"@search.score\": 0.03333333507180214, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\"}, \"token_count\": 988, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1726\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1728: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1728 Si le vendeur connaissait le vice caché ou ne pouvait l’ignorer, il est tenu, outre la restitution du prix, de réparer le préjudice subi par l’acheteur. 1991, c. 64, a. 1728 ; N.I. 2014-05-01 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1728 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1720.1. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 587-588 Amendement : ajout | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1728 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Si le vendeur connaissait le vice caché ou ne pouvait l'ignorer, il est tenu, outre la restitution du prix, de tous les dommages-intérêts soufferts par l'acheteur. Article 1728 (SQ 1991, c. 64) If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1527, 1528 Commentaires Cet article s'inspire des articles 1527 et 1528 C.C.B.C. et complète l'article 1727; il accorde explicitement à l'acheteur le droit de réclamer des dommages-intérêts du vendeur de mauvaise foi qui connaissait le vice caché ou ne pouvait l'ignorer. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.0320020467042923, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1728: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\", \"@search.score\": 0.0320020467042923, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\"}, \"token_count\": 514, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1728\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1733: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1733 Le vendeur ne peut exclure ni limiter sa responsabilité s’il n’a pas révélé les vices qu’il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer et qui affectent le droit de propriété ou la qualité du bien. Cette règle reçoit exception lorsque l’acheteur achète à ses risques et périls d’un vendeur non professionnel. 1991, c. 64, a. 1733 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1733 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1724. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588-589 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : suspendu Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 599 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 34 (11 décembre 1991),  p. 1371-1373 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1733 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur ne peut exclure ni limiter sa responsabilité s'il n'a pas révélé les vices qu'il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer et qui affectent le droit de propriété ou la qualité du bien. Cette règle reçoit exception lorsque l'acheteur achète à ses risques et périls d'un vendeur non professionnel. Article 1733 (SQ 1991, c. 64) A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property. An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys property at his own risk from a seller who is not a professional seller. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1510, 1524 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 361 Commentaires Cet article, qui s'inspire des articles 1510 et 1524 C.C.B.C., introduit un régime identique pour les clauses limitatives ou exclusives de garantie, plutôt que de retenir les régimes différents établis par le droit antérieur, selon qu'il s'agissait de la garantie d'éviction ou de la garantie des vices cachés. Il précise que le vendeur ne peut, par garantie conventionnelle, se soustraire aux obligations de garantie du droit de propriété et de garantie de qualité, énoncées aux articles 1723 à 1731 dans l'hypothèse où il n'a pas dénoncé les vices qu'il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer. De plus, il protège l'acheteur contre le vendeur professionnel qui pourrait exclure sa responsabilité en mentionnant simplement au contrat que l'acheteur achète à ses risques et périls. Une telle exclusion ne sera possible que si le vendeur n'est pas professionnel. Le caractère impératif de cette règle s'infère du texte de l'article 1733, sans qu'il ait paru nécessaire de l'énoncer expressément. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.031054403632879257, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1733: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\", \"@search.score\": 0.031054403632879257, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\"}, \"token_count\": 883, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1733\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1739: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1739 L’acheteur qui constate que le bien est atteint d’un vice doit, par écrit, le dénoncer au vendeur dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte. Ce délai commence à courir, lorsque le vice apparaît graduellement, du jour où l’acheteur a pu en soupçonner la gravité et l’étendue. Le vendeur ne peut se prévaloir d’une dénonciation tardive de l’acheteur s’il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer le vice. 1991, c. 64, a. 1739 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1739 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1730. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 589-592 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1739 (LQ 1991, c. 64) L'acheteur qui constate que le bien est atteint d'un vice doit, par écrit, le dénoncer au vendeur dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte. Ce délai commence à courir, lorsque le vice apparaît graduellement, du jour où l'acheteur a pu en soupçonner la gravité et l'étendue. Le vendeur ne peut se prévaloir d'une dénonciation tardive de l'acheteur s'il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer le vice. Article 1739 (SQ 1991, c. 64) A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective may give notice in writing of the defect to the seller only within a reasonable time after discovering it. The time begins to run, where the defect appears gradually, on the day that the buyer could have suspected the seriousness and extent of the defect. The seller may not invoke tardy notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1530 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 377 Convention sur la vente internationale de marchandises : articles 39,40 Commentaires Cet article s'inspire de l'article 39 de la Convention des Nations Unies sur la vente internationale de marchandises (Convention de Vienne, 1980). Il complète l'article précédent. Ainsi, il substitue à l'obligation qu'avait l'acheteur d'intenter une action rédhibitoire pour vice caché dans un délai raisonnable en vertu de l'article 1530 C.C.B.C., celle de dénoncer le vice au vendeur, par écrit. Plutôt que de déterminer un délai fixe, il a semblé préférable de retenir un délai raisonnable, afin de mieux tenir compte des situations diverses qui peuvent se présenter, tant en matière mobilière qu'en matière immobilière. L'exigence d'un délai raisonnable est par ailleurs conforme à la Convention. Afin de ne pas imposer à l'acheteur l'obligation de donner l'avis requis dès la première apparition du vice lorsque le vice apparaît graduellement, le délai raisonnable pour remettre l'avis ne commencera à courir que du jour où l'acheteur aura pu soupçonner la gravité et l'étendue du vice. Enfin, le second alinéa précise, comme le fait d'ailleurs la Convention de Vienne, que le vendeur qui connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer le vice, ne peut se prévaloir du caractère tardif de la dénonciation faite par l'acheteur, ce qui ne l'empêche pas d'invoquer le cas échéant, les délais de prescription prévus aux articles 2923 et 2925 du nouveau code. Comme l'article 1738, cet article est de nature à inciter les parties à régler leur différend à l'amiable. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03226646035909653, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1739: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\", \"@search.score\": 0.03226646035909653, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\"}, \"token_count\": 1022, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1739\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1729: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1729 En cas de vente par un vendeur professionnel, l’existence d’un vice au moment de la vente est présumée, lorsque le mauvais fonctionnement du bien ou sa détérioration survient prématurément par rapport à des biens identiques ou de même espèce; cette présomption est repoussée si le défaut est dû à une mauvaise utilisation du bien par l’acheteur. 1991, c. 64, a. 1729 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1729 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1721. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : suspendu Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 30 (5 décembre 1991),  p. 1244-1245 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1729 (LQ 1991, c. 64) En cas de vente par un vendeur professionnel, l'existence d'un vice au moment de la vente est présumée, lorsque le mauvais fonctionnement du bien ou sa détérioration survient prématurément par rapport à des biens identiques ou de même espèce ; cette présomption est repoussée si le défaut est dû à une mauvaise utilisation du bien par l'acheteur. Article 1729 (SQ 1991, c. 64) A defect is presumed to have existed at the time of a sale by a professional seller if the property malfunctions or deteriorates prematurely in comparison with identical items of property or items of the same type; such a presumption is not made, however, where the defect is due to improper use of the property by the buyer. Sources Loi sur la protection du consommateur, L.R.Q., chap. P-40.1 : article 38 Commentaires Cet article, de droit nouveau, complète la garantie de qualité à laquelle est tenu un vendeur professionnel, c'est-à-dire la personne qui a pour occupation habituelle la vente de biens, en présumant l'existence d'un vice lorsque le mauvais fonctionnement du bien ou sa détérioration survient prématurément. Cette garantie de bon fonctionnement s'inspire de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur (L.R.Q., chapitre P-40.1, art. 38). Ainsi, la garantie de qualité que doit offrir le vendeur professionnel reçoit, par le jeu de la présomption, une certaine extension. Il sera, par voie de conséquence, plus facile d'établir la preuve du vice, d'autant que le vendeur sera tenu de garantir l'acheteur contre le fonctionnement anormal ou la détérioration prématurée du bien vendu, par rapport à des biens identiques ou de même espèce. Cette garantie cesse cependant si le défaut résulte d'une mauvaise utilisation du bien par l'acheteur. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03306011110544205, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1729: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\", \"@search.score\": 0.03306011110544205, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\"}, \"token_count\": 859, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1729\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1723: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1723 Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l’acheteur que le bien est libre de tous droits, à l’exception de ceux qu’il a déclarés lors de la vente. Il est tenu de purger le bien des hypothèques qui le grèvent, même déclarées ou inscrites, à moins que l’acheteur n’ait assumé la dette ainsi garantie. 1991, c. 64, a. 1723 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1723 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1716. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 586-587 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 9 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1723 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l'acheteur que le bien est libre de tous droits, à l'exception de ceux qu'il a déclarés lors de la vente. Il est tenu de purger le bien des hypothèques qui le grèvent, même déclarées ou inscrites, à moins que l'acheteur n'ait assumé la dette ainsi garantie. Article 1723 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property is free of all rights except those he has declared at the time of the sale. The seller is bound to discharge the property of all hypothecs, even declared or registered, unless the buyer has assumed the debt so secured. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1508 O.R.C.C. : L. V, articles 363, 364 Commentaires Cet article modifie le droit antérieur qu'exprimait l'article 1508 C.C.B.C., en ce qu'il remplace la garantie contre l'éviction par une garantie plus étendue, la garantie du droit de propriété. Cette garantie oblige le vendeur à fournir non seulement une possession utile et paisible du bien vendu, mais aussi à fournir un bon titre au moment de la vente. L'acheteur n'aura donc plus à attendre d'être menacé d'éviction avant de pouvoir intenter ses recours. Cette garantie est complétée par celles instituées en matière de publicité des droits réels. On pense notamment à la nouvelle présomption de connaissance des droits inscrits aux registres, introduite par l'article 2943, de même qu'à la nouvelle règle énoncée à l'article 2962, visant à protéger les tiers de bonne foi qui se sont fiés aux registres. Cet article, de même que les articles 1724 et 1725, sont complétés par l'article 1738 qui impose à l'acheteur l'obligation de dénoncer au vendeur le risque d'éviction qu'il découvre. Ni l'article 1511 sur les recours particuliers de l'acheteur évincé ni les articles 1512 ss. sur les restrictions à l'exercice de ces recours que l'on retrouvait au Code civil du Bas Canada n'ont été retenus et l'on appliquera plutôt que ces distinctions, les règles générales du droit des obligations, notamment celles prévues au chapitre sixième De l'exécution de l'obligation . Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02943722903728485, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1723: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\", \"@search.score\": 0.02943722903728485, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\"}, \"token_count\": 891, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1723\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1725\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1725\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1725: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1725 Le vendeur d’un immeuble se porte garant envers l’acheteur de toute violation aux limitations de droit public qui grèvent le bien et qui échappent au droit commun de la propriété. Le vendeur n’est pas tenu à cette garantie lorsqu’il a dénoncé ces limitations à l’acheteur lors de la vente, lorsqu’un acheteur prudent et diligent aurait pu les découvrir par la nature, la situation et l’utilisation des lieux ou lorsqu’elles ont fait l’objet d’une inscription au Bureau de la publicité foncière. 1991, c. 64, a. 1725 ; 2020, c. 17, a. 27 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1725 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1718. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 587 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 9 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. 2. Loi visant à moderniser certaines règles relatives à la publicité foncière et à favoriser la diffusion de l’information géospatiale, LQ 2020, c. 17, a. 27 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 35 ,  1re sess, 42e lég, Québec, 2019, 24 (thème 2) Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Commission de l'agriculture, des pêcheries, de l'énergie et des ressources naturelles (23 septembre 2020),  vol. 45, no 48, p. 16h Amendement : non | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1725 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur d'un immeuble se porte garant envers l'acheteur de toute violation aux limitations de droit public qui grèvent le bien et qui échappent au droit commun de la propriété. Le vendeur n'est pas tenu à cette garantie lorsqu'il a dénoncé ces limitations à l'acheteur lors de la vente, lorsqu'un acheteur prudent et diligent aurait pu les découvrir par la nature, la situation et l'utilisation des lieux ou lorsqu'elles ont fait l'objet d'une inscription au bureau de la publicité des droits. Article 1725 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller of an immovable is warrantor towards the buyer for any violation of restrictions of public law affecting the property which are exceptions to the ordinary law of ownership. The seller is not warrantor towards the buyer where he has given notice of these restrictions to the buyer at the time of the sale, where a prudent and diligent buyer could have discovered them by reason of the nature, location and use of the premises or where such restrictions have been registered in the registry office. Sources O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 394 Commentaires Cet article est de droit nouveau. Il vise à clarifier le droit antérieur, quant à l'obligation du vendeur de dénoncer à l'acheteur toute violation aux limitations du droit de propriété imposées par le droit public. Il tient compte de certains critères dégagés par la jurisprudence, de même que des nouvelles règles instituées en matière de publicité des droits. Ainsi, l'article 1725 énonce que le vendeur d'un immeuble doit garantir l'acheteur contre toute violation aux limitations de droit public qui grèvent le bien et qui échappent au droit commun de la propriété. Le vendeur n'est pas tenu à cette garantie lorsqu'il a dénoncé ces limitations à l'acheteur lors de la vente, lorsqu'un acheteur prudent et diligent aurait pu les découvrir par la nature, la situation et l'utilisation des lieux ou lorsqu'elles ont fait l'objet d'une inscription au bureau de la publicité des droits. À titre d'exemple de limitation de droit public, on peut mentionner l'existence d'un règlement de zonage, d'urbanisme ou de protection de l'environnement. À cause de l'article 2943, le vendeur ne sera pas tenu à cette garantie si, par exemple, une servitude est dûment inscrite au registre foncier ou si une aire de protection d'un site ou d'un monument historique a été publiée au registre approprié. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1725\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02628900855779648, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1725\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1725: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1725\", \"@search.score\": 0.02628900855779648, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1725\"}, \"token_count\": 1147, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1725\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1727: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1727 Lorsque le bien périt en raison d’un vice caché qui existait lors de la vente, la perte échoit au vendeur, lequel est tenu à la restitution du prix; si la perte résulte d’une force majeure ou est due à la faute de l’acheteur, ce dernier doit déduire, du montant de sa réclamation, la valeur du bien, dans l’état où il se trouvait lors de la perte. 1991, c. 64, a. 1727 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1727 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1720. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1727 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Lorsque le bien périt en raison d'un vice caché qui existait lors de la vente, la perte échoit au vendeur, lequel est tenu à la restitution du prix; si la perte résulte d'une force majeure ou est due à la faute de l'acheteur, ce dernier doit déduire, du montant de sa réclamation, la valeur du bien, dans l'état où il se trouvait lors de la perte. Article 1727 (SQ 1991, c. 64) If the property perishes by reason of a latent defect that existed at the time of the sale, the loss is borne by the seller, who is bound to restore the price; if the loss results from superior force or is due to the fault of the buyer, the buyer shall deduct from his claim the value of the property in the state it was in at the time of the loss. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1529 Commentaires Cet article est en substance conforme au droit antérieur édicté à l'article 1529 C.C.B.C. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.027783136814832687, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1727: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\", \"@search.score\": 0.027783136814832687, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\"}, \"token_count\": 607, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1727\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1510\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1510\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCBC - 1510: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Remplacé le 1er janvier 1994 par le Code civil du Québec (RLRQ, c. C-1991) Article 1510 Dans le même cas de stipulation de non garantie, le vendeur, au cas d’éviction, est tenu à la restitution du prix de la chose vendue, à moins que l’acheteur n’ait connu, lors de la vente, le danger de l’éviction, ou qu’il n’ait acheté à ses risques et périls. 1866 a. 1510 Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1510\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.026631392538547516, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1510\", \"title\": \"CCBC - 1510: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1510\", \"@search.score\": 0.026631392538547516, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 4, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1510\"}, \"token_count\": 121, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1510\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1716: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1716 Le vendeur est tenu de délivrer le bien, et d’en garantir le droit de propriété et la qualité. Ces garanties existent de plein droit, sans qu’il soit nécessaire de les stipuler dans le contrat de vente. 1991, c. 64, a. 1716 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1716 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1709. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 585 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 8 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1716 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur est tenu de délivrer le bien, et d'en garantir le droit de propriété et la qualité. Ces garanties existent de plein droit, sans qu'il soit nécessaire de les stipuler dans le contrat de vente. Article 1716 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller is bound to deliver the property and to warrant the ownership and quality of the property. These warranties exist of right whether or not they are stipulated in the contract of sale. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1491, 1506, 1507 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 359 Commentaires Cet article énonce les obligations du vendeur. Il reprend en premier lieu l'obligation de délivrance à laquelle le vendeur est tenu, précisée aux articles 1717 à 1722. Il modifie cependant le droit antérieur, en remplaçant la garantie contre l'éviction par une garantie du droit de propriété, de même qu'en remplaçant la garantie contre les défauts cachés à laquelle le vendeur est tenu, par une garantie de qualité. Enfin, il reprend la règle du droit antérieur, selon laquelle ces garanties existent de plein droit, sans qu'il soit nécessaire de les stipuler dans le contrat de vente. La portée des garanties légales du vendeur est énoncée aux articles 1723 à 1731. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03057890012860298, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1716: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\", \"@search.score\": 0.03057890012860298, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\"}, \"token_count\": 650, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1716\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1731\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1731\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1731: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1731 La vente faite sous contrôle de justice ne donne lieu à aucune obligation de garantie de qualité du bien vendu. 1991, c. 64, a. 1731 ; N.I. 2016-01-01 (NCPC) Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1731 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1722. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 588 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1731 (LQ 1991, c. 64) La vente faite sous l'autorité de la justice ne donne lieu à aucune obligation de garantie de qualité du bien vendu. Article 1731 (SQ 1991, c. 64) Sale under judicial authority does not give rise to any obligation of warranty of the quality of the sold property. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1531 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 378 Commentaires Cet article reprend en substance le droit antérieur qu'énonçait l'article 1531 C.C.B.C. Il prévoit qu'il n'existe aucune garantie de qualité du bien vendu, lorsque la vente est faite sous l'autorité de la justice. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1731\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03083491325378418, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1731\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1731: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1731\", \"@search.score\": 0.03083491325378418, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1731\"}, \"token_count\": 477, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1731\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1529\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1529\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCBC - 1529: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Remplacé le 1er janvier 1994 par le Code civil du Québec (RLRQ, c. C-1991) Article 1529 Si la chose périt par suite de vices cachés qui existaient lors de la vente, la perte tombe sur le vendeur qui est tenu envers l’acheteur à la restitution du prix et aux autres dédommagements, tel que réglé dans les deux articles qui précèdent. Si elle périt par la faute de l’acheteur, ou par cas fortuit, l’acheteur doit en déduire la valeur, dans l’état où elle se trouvait lors de la perte, sur sa réclamation contre le vendeur. 1866 a. 1529 Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1529\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.028324153274297714, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1529\", \"title\": \"CCBC - 1529: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1529\", \"@search.score\": 0.028324153274297714, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1529\"}, \"token_count\": 164, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1529\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-p-40.1-fr\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-p-40.1-fr\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Loi sur la protection du consommateur, RLRQ c P-40.1 - Article 38.9\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... En vig.: 2026-10-05 38.9 . Après la conclusion d’un contrat de vente ou de louage à long terme d’un bien qui comporte une garantie de bon fonctionnement prévue à l’ article 38.1 , le commerçant doit transmettre au consommateur, de la manière et aux conditions prescrites par règlement, les informations relatives à cette garantie que détermine ce règlement. 2023, c. 21 , a. 3 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-p-40.1-fr\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.029906954616308212, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-p-40.1-fr\", \"title\": \"Loi sur la protection du consommateur, RLRQ c P-40.1 - Article 38.9\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-p-40.1-fr\", \"@search.score\": 0.029906954616308212, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 1.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-p-40.1-fr\"}, \"token_count\": 100, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-p-40.1-fr\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1740: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1740 Le vendeur d’un bien meuble peut, lorsque l’acheteur n’en paie pas le prix et n’en prend pas délivrance, considérer la vente comme résolue si l’acheteur est en demeure de plein droit d’exécuter ses obligations ou s’il ne les a pas exécutées dans le délai fixé par la mise en demeure. Il peut aussi, lorsqu’il apparaît que l’acheteur n’exécutera pas une partie substantielle de ses obligations, arrêter la livraison du bien en cours de transport. 1991, c. 64, a. 1740 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1740 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1731. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 593 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 20 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1740 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur d'un bien meuble peut, lorsque l'acheteur n'en paie pas le prix et n'en prend pas délivrance, considérer la vente comme résolue si l'acheteur est en demeure de plein droit d'exécuter ses obligations ou s'il ne les a pas exécutées dans le délai fixé par la mise en demeure. Il peut aussi, lorsqu'il apparaît que l'acheteur n'exécutera pas une partie substantielle de ses obligations, arrêter la livraison du bien en cours de transport. Article 1740 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller of movable property may, if the buyer fails to pay the sale price and to accept delivery of it, consider the sale resolved if the buyer is in default by operation of law or if he fails to perform his obligations within the time allowed in the notice of default. The seller may also, where it appears that the buyer will not perform a substantial part of his obligations, stop delivery of the property in transit. Sources C.C.B.C. : article 1544 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 388 Convention sur la vente internationale de marchandises : article 71 Commentaires Le premier alinéa de cet article reprend l'article 1544 C.C.B.C. qui donne au vendeur le droit de considérer la vente d'un meuble résolue de plein droit, lorsque l'acheteur n'en paie pas le prix et n'en prend pas livraison. Il y apporte certaines adaptations en fonction des règles générales introduites par l'article 1605, en matière de résolution de plein droit. Ainsi, le vendeur pourra, dans ce cas, considérer la vente comme résolue si l'acheteur est en demeure de plein droit d'exécuter ses obligations ou s'il ne les a pas exécutées dans le délai fixé par la mise en demeure. L'article 1597 prévoit les cas où le débiteur est considéré en demeure de plein droit. Le second alinéa ajoute au droit antérieur, en ce qu'il complète le droit de suspendre l'exécution qui résulte des règles générales applicables en matière d'obligations. Il permet au vendeur d'arrêter la livraison du bien en cours de transport, lorsqu'il apparaît que l'acheteur n'exécutera pas une partie substantielle de ses obligations. Ce nouveau principe comble une lacune du droit antérieur, en protégeant les droits du vendeur impayé qui a remis le bien vendu à un transporteur. Cette règle s'inspire de la Convention des Nations Unies sur la vente internationale de marchandises (Convention de Vienne, 1980). Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.029138512909412384, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1740: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\", \"@search.score\": 0.029138512909412384, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\"}, \"token_count\": 998, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1740\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1741: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1741 Lorsque la vente d’un bien meuble a été faite sans terme, le vendeur peut, dans les 30 jours de la délivrance, considérer la vente comme résolue et revendiquer le bien, si l’acheteur, alors qu’il est en demeure, fait défaut de payer le prix et si le meuble est encore entier et dans le même état, sans être passé entre les mains d’un tiers qui en a payé le prix ou d’un créancier hypothécaire qui a obtenu le délaissement du bien. La saisie par un tiers, alors que l’acheteur est en demeure de payer le prix et que le bien est dans les conditions prescrites pour la résolution, ne fait pas obstacle au droit du vendeur. 1991, c. 64, a. 1741 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1741 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1732. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 592-593 Amendement : oui | Commentaires : oui | Vote : adopté Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1741 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Lorsque la vente d'un bien meuble a été faite sans terme, le vendeur peut, dans les trente jours de la délivrance, considérer la vente comme résolue et revendiquer le bien, si l'acheteur, alors qu'il est en demeure, fait défaut de payer le prix et si le meuble est encore entier et dans le même état, sans être passé entre les mains d'un tiers qui en a payé le prix ou d'un créancier hypothécaire qui a obtenu le délaissement du bien. La saisie par un tiers, alors que l'acheteur est en demeure de payer le prix et que le bien est dans les conditions prescrites pour la résolution, ne fait pas obstacle au droit du vendeur Article 1741 (SQ 1991, c. 64) Except in the case of a sale with a term, the seller of movable property may, within thirty days of delivery, consider the sale resolved and revendicate the property if the buyer, being in default, has failed to pay the price and if the property is still entire and in the same condition and has not passed into the hands of a third person who has paid the price thereof, or of a hypothecary creditor who has obtained surrender thereof. Where the buyer is in default to pay the price and the property meets the conditions prescribed for resolution of the sale, the seizure of the property by a third person in no hindrance to the rights of the seller. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1998, 1999, 2000 O.R.C.C. : L. IV, articles 288, 289 Commentaires Cet article qui associe directement le droit de revendication du bien, auparavant accordé au vendeur par les articles 1998 et 2000 C.C.B.C., à la résolution de plein droit de la vente, modifie quelque peu les recours antérieurement accordés au vendeur d'un bien meuble, lorsque la vente est faite sans terme et que l'acheteur fait défaut de payer le prix. L'article demeure cependant proche du droit antérieur, en ce que les conditions d'exercice du droit de résolution sont les mêmes que celles qui étaient exigées pour l'exercice du droit de revendication. Ainsi, la vente devra être faite sans terme, le meuble devra être encore entier et dans le même état et il ne devra pas être passé entre les mains d'un tiers qui en a payé le prix ou d'un créancier hypothécaire qui a obtenu le délaissement du bien. En ce dernier cas, le vendeur ne peut plus avoir droit à la résolution de la vente et à la revendication du bien; il peut, cependant, se prévaloir de la priorité que lui confère l'article 2651-2° s'il remplit les conditions qui y sont édictées. Quant au délai pour exercer ce recours, il est fixé à 30 jours : en effet, rien ne justifie que l'on conserve la distinction du droit antérieur qui fixait ce délai à 30 jours, en cas de faillite, et à 8 jours dans les autres cas. L'article 1741 modifie aussi le droit antérieur, car conformément aux conditions générales de la résolution de plein droit introduites par l'article 1605, il exige une mise en demeure préalable; la demeure peut être, dans certains cas, de plein droit (article 1597). Le second alinéa modifie lui aussi le droit antérieur, en ce qu'il conserve au vendeur le droit à la résolution de plein droit de la vente en cas de saisie du bien par un tiers. L'acheteur doit toutefois être en demeure de payer le prix et le bien doit être dans les conditions prescrites pour la résolution. Si les conditions de l'article 1741 ne se rencontrent pas, la résolution de la vente, pour défaut de paiement du prix, peut alors s'obtenir en vertu des règles générales du droit des obligations. En effet, les restrictions antérieures qu'énonçait l'article 1543 C.C.B.C., selon lesquelles le bien devait être demeuré entre les mains de l'acheteur et, qu'en cas de faillite, ce droit devait être exercé dans les 30 jours de la délivrance, ne sont pas reprises. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.026610644534230232, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1741: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\", \"@search.score\": 0.026610644534230232, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\"}, \"token_count\": 1279, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1741\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1724\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1724\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1724: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1724 Le vendeur se porte garant envers l’acheteur de tout empiétement exercé par lui-même, à moins qu’il ne l’ait déclaré lors de la vente. Il se porte garant, de même, de tout empiétement qu’un tiers aurait, à sa connaissance, commencé d’exercer avant la vente. 1991, c. 64, a. 1724 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1724 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1717. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 587 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 9 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1724 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Le vendeur se porte garant envers l'acheteur de tout empiétement exercé par lui-même, à moins qu'il ne l'ait déclaré lors de la vente. Il se porte garant, de même, de tout empiétement qu'un tiers aurait, à sa connaissance, commencé d'exercer avant la vente. Article 1724 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The seller is warrantor towards the buyer for any encroachment on his part unless he has declared it at the time of the sale. The seller is also warrantor for any encroachment commenced with his knowledge by a third person before the sale. Sources O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 393 Commentaires Cet article, qui s'inscrit dans le prolongement du droit antérieur, est de droit nouveau. Le premier alinéa tient le vendeur garant envers l'acheteur de tout empiétement exercé par lui-même, à moins qu'il ne l'ait déclaré lors de la vente. De cette façon, l'acheteur n'est pas induit en erreur sur la qualité ou la quantité de ce qu'il achète. Le second alinéa ne tient le vendeur garant des empiétements exercés par un tiers, que si ces empiétements ont commencé avant la vente et qu'ils ont été faits à sa connaissance. On évite ainsi que le vendeur devienne garant de l'empiétement commencé par un tiers, avant la vente, mais sans qu'il en ait eu connaissance. Cette règle a pour but de fournir à l'acheteur la meilleure information possible et de lui permettre d'empêcher l'achèvement d'une prescription en cours. Cet article doit être complété par les règles énoncées à l'article 992 du livre Des biens , relativement au propriétaire de bonne foi ayant empiété sur le terrain d'autrui. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1724\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.027313265949487686, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1724\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1724: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1724\", \"@search.score\": 0.027313265949487686, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1724\"}, \"token_count\": 761, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1724\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCBC - 1539: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Remplacé le 1er janvier 1994 par le Code civil du Québec (RLRQ, c. C-1991) Article 1539 Le vendeur ne peut rentrer en possession de la chose vendue, sur résolution de la vente faute de paiement du prix, avant d’avoir remboursé à l’acheteur ce qu’il a reçu de lui sur le prix, avec les frais de toutes les réparations nécessaires et des améliorations qui ont augmenté la valeur de la chose, et jusqu’à concurrence de cette valeur. Si ces améliorations sont de nature à être enlevées, il a le choix de les laisser enlever par l’acheteur. 1866 a. 1539 Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02857142873108387, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"title\": \"CCBC - 1539: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\", \"@search.score\": 0.02857142873108387, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\"}, \"token_count\": 168, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1539\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1577\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1577\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCBC - 1577: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Remplacé le 1er janvier 1994 par le Code civil du Québec (RLRQ, c. C-1991) Article 1577 Lorsque le vendeur, par une simple clause de garantie, répond de la solvabilité du débiteur, cette garantie ne s’applique qu’à la solvabilité au temps de la vente et jusqu’à concurrence seulement du prix que l’acheteur a payé. 1866 a. 1577 Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1577\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.026144906878471375, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1577\", \"title\": \"CCBC - 1577: Code civil du Bas Canada et Code civil du Québec (1980) (remplacés)\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1577\", \"@search.score\": 0.026144906878471375, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1577\"}, \"token_count\": 113, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCBC/article1577\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1732\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1732\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1732: Code civil du Québec\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... À jour au 7 avril 2026 Article 1732 Les parties peuvent, dans leur contrat, ajouter aux obligations de la garantie légale, en diminuer les effets, ou l’exclure entièrement, mais le vendeur ne peut, en aucun cas, se dégager de ses faits personnels. 1991, c. 64, a. 1732 Version officielle Débats parlementaires et positions 1. Code civil du Québec, LQ 1991, c. 64, a. 1732 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 125,  1re sess, 34e lég, Québec, 1990, a. 1723. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Sous-commission des institutions, vol. 31, no 14 (16 octobre 1991),  p. 589 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Positions du Barreau : Mémoire du Barreau du Québec sur le Code civil du Québec (projet de loi 125) - Livre V - Des obligations (de la Vente et de la Donation) , p. 12-19 Le Mémoire du Barreau reflète la position officielle du Barreau du Québec . Le ou les Mémoires du Barreau intégrés dans cette publication résultent d'une sélection effectuée par le CAIJ. D'autres Mémoires sur ce sujet peuvent être disponibles sur le site Internet du Barreau. 2. Loi visant à assurer une meilleure concordance entre les textes français et anglais du Code civil, LQ 2016, c. 4, a. 212 Référence à la présentation : Projet de loi 89 ,  1re sess, 41e lég, Québec, 2016, a. 212. Étude détaillée dans le Journal des débats : Commission des institutions, vol. 44, no 94 (25 février 2016),  p. 5 Amendement : non | Commentaires : non | Vote : adopté Législation citée Lancer une requête de législation citée, pour l'article, en Jurisprudence Doctrine Commentaires du ministre de la Justice Article 1732 (LQ 1991, c. 64) Les parties peuvent, dans leur contrat, ajouter aux obligations de la garantie légale, en diminuer les effets, ou l'exclure entièrement, mais le vendeur ne peut, en aucun cas, se dégager de ses faits personnels. Article 1732 (SQ 1991, c. 64) The parties may, in their contract, add to the obligations of legal warranty, diminish its effects or exclude it altogether but in no case may the seller exempt himself from his personal fault. Sources C.C.B.C. : articles 1507, 1509 O.R.C.C. : L. V, article 360 Commentaires Cet article reprend en substance les articles 1507 et 1509 C.C.B.C., en matière de garantie conventionnelle. Ainsi, il énonce que les parties peuvent, dans leur contrat, ajouter aux obligations de la garantie légale, en diminuer les effets, ou l'exclure entièrement. De plus, conformément au droit antérieur, il interdit au vendeur, dans tous les cas, de se dégager de ses faits personnels. Il s'infère du texte de l'article que cette prohibition a un caractère impératif. Extrait de : Ministère de la Justice, Commentaires du ministre de la Justice - Le Code civil du Québec ,  t. 1, Québec, Les Publications du Québec, 1993....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1732\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.0251582283526659, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1732\", \"title\": \"CCQ-1991 - 1732: Code civil du Québec\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1732\", \"@search.score\": 0.0251582283526659, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 3, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"ELOIS\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1732\"}, \"token_count\": 701, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/lois-annotees/CCQ-1991/article1732\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Loi sur la régie du logement, RLRQ c R-8.1 (QUÉBEC) - Article 54.11\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 54.11 . L’acheteur d’une fraction dans un immeuble locatif converti en copropriété divise peut, dans les trois ans de la signature du contrat de vente, réclamer du vendeur la réduction de ses obligations si le rapport d’expert, la circulaire d’information ou le contrat de vente contiennent des informations fausses, trompeuses ou incomplètes sur un élément substantiel, ou si le vendeur n’a pas remis à l’acheteur le rapport d’expert ou la circulaire d’information. Le tribunal rejette la demande si le vendeur démontre que l’acheteur n’en a subi aucun préjudice. 1987, c. 77, a. 2 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03333333507180214, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"title\": \"Loi sur la régie du logement, RLRQ c R-8.1 (QUÉBEC) - Article 54.11\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\", \"@search.score\": 0.03333333507180214, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 2, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"LEGISLATION\", \"kind\": \"hyde:legislation\", \"query\": \"Le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur contre les vices cachés qui rendent le bien impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui en diminuent tellement l’utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acquis, ou n’en aurait donné qu’un moindre prix, s’il les avait connus. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché. L’acheteur peut demander la résolution du contrat ou une réduction du prix, selon les circonstances.\"}], \"legislation_id\": \"/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\"}, \"token_count\": 152, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/legislation/qc/lois-rlrq-c-r-8.1-fr#se:54.11\"}], \"jurisprudence\": [{\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Orlov c. Ali, 2023 QCCS 1790 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"--- BEGIN PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 0\\n2023 QCCS 1790 (CanLII) | Orlov c. Ali | CanLII Date: 2023-05-19 File number: 500-17-115483-219 Citation: Orlov c. Ali, 2023 QCCS 1790 (CanLII) Orlov c. Ali 2023 QCCS 1790 SUPERIOR COURT (Civil Division) CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL No.: 500-17‑115483‑219 DATE: May 19, 2023 _____________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE ANDRES C. GARIN, J.S.C. _____________________________________________________________________ INNA ORLOV and MICHAEL ORLOV Plaintiffs v. KHOSRO MOHAMMAD ALI and VÉRONIQUE MARIE Defendants _____________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT _____________________________________________________________________ OVERVIEW ................................................................................................................................... 2 CONTEXT ...................................................................................................................................... 3 1. The Purchase of the Property and the Inspection .................................................... 3 2. Issues Discovered by the Plaintiffs Upon Taking Possession of the Property .... 3 3. The Expert Evidence ..................................................................................................... 5 POINTS IN ISSUE ....................................................................................................................... 6 ANALYSIS ..................................................................................................................................... 7 1. The Defendants’ Objection to the Admission into Evidence of the Plaintiffs’ Photos 7 2. The Claims for Latent Defects ..................................................................................... 9 2.1 The Governing Principles ......................................................................................... 9 2.2 The Exterior Wall in the Area of the Rear Sliding Patio Door ........................... 11 2.3 The Basement Water Infiltration Issue ................................................................. 13 3. The Damages to which the Plaintiffs are Entitled as a result of the Latent Defects Affecting the Property ............................................................................................................................. 17 3.1 The Governing Principles ....................................................................................... 17 3.2 The Plaintiffs’ Claims ............................................................................................... 18 4. The Plaintiffs’ Other Claims for Compensation ...................................................... 23 4.1 The Damaged Front Door ...................................................................................... 23 4.2 The Removal of the Closet Shelving .................................................................... 25 4.3 The Defendants’ Failure to Remove Sundry Items from the Property ............ 27 LEGAL COSTS & EXPERT FEES .......................................................................................... 27 DISPOSITION ............................................................................................................................. 28 OVERVIEW [ 1 ] On July 28, 2020, the Plaintiffs, Inna Orlov and Michael Orlov, purchased a residential home on Crestwood Avenue in Montreal West (the Property ) from the Defendants, Khosro Mohammad Ali and Véronique Marie, for the sum of $785,000. [1] [ 2 ] The Plaintiffs claim that their new home is affected by latent defects. They also claim that the Defendants caused damage to the front door of the home and that, when vacating the premises, the latter both improperly removed certain fixtures and failed to remove unwanted objects from the Property. [ 3 ] In compensation for the alleged latent defects and the injury they claim to have suffered, the Plaintiffs seek damages in the amount of $108,366.23. [ 4 ] For the reasons that follow, I find that the Property was affected by latent defects that resulted in water infiltration in two areas of the home. These latent defects entitle the Plaintiffs to a price reduction in the amount of $30,527.05. The Plaintiffs are also entitled to $3,038.67 for damage to the front door, improper removal of certain fixtures and for failure to fully remove their personal belongings from the Property. CONTEXT 1. The Purchase of the Property and the Inspection [ 5 ] In the spring of 2020, the Plaintiffs were looking for a larger home for their family. They were interested in the Property and, after visiting it, decided to make an offer which was accepted on May 24, 2020. [2] [ 6 ] Prior to completing the purchase of the Property, which was built in 1957, the Plaintiffs had it inspected by Mr. Giuseppe Laurieri of Abitat Inspect Inc. On May 29, 2020, Mr. Laurieri conducted a visual, non-obtrusive, pre-purchase inspection of the Property. [ 7 ] Mr. Laurieri provided the Plaintiffs with a report (the Pre-Purchase Inspection Report ) setting out his observations and recommendations arising from his visual pre‑purchase inspection. [3] [ 8 ] Following receipt by the Plaintiffs of the Pre-Purchase Inspection Report, the parties negotiated a $5,000 price reduction “to account for the brick and masonry work to be performed on the chimney”. [4] [ 9 ] Moreover, the Defendants, who had disclosed a basement water infiltration issue in relation to a foundation crack in the laundry room, [5] had the crack in question repaired by the injection of epoxy resin. 2. Issues Discovered by the Plaintiffs Upon Taking Possession of the Property [ 10 ] The Plaintiffs took possession of their new home on or about July 31, 2020. They had been given the keys to the premises a few days earlier and visited the Property on July 27 or 28. On that occasion, Ms. Orlov noticed that the front door was noticeably dented. [6] No such dent was observed by the Plaintiffs or by their home inspector during previous visits. [ 11 ] Ms. Orlov also observed that the Defendants had left sundry unwanted items in the basement and the garage. [ 12 ] She further noticed that shelving affixed to the walls in several of the closets (main entrance hall and certain bedrooms) had been removed. [7] [ 13 ] The Plaintiffs, who intended to move into their new home one week after taking possession, had hired a contractor to paint the interior of the entire dwelling. One of the rooms at the rear of the house has a sliding patio door that leads to the backyard. The Plaintiffs’ contractor was having difficulty painting this area. The paint would not dry and the wall was noticeably wet. The Plaintiffs noted as well that the paint around the patio door had “bubbled”. [8] [ 14 ] Finding the situation unusual, the Plaintiffs’ contractor, Mr. Ilya Ruso, opened the area to explore. Significant water infiltration behind the brick siding was discovered. [ 15 ] The Plaintiffs also noticed a pervasive musty odour in the basement. Neither the Plaintiffs, nor their inspector, had detected any unusual smell during previous visits. [9] The basement odour raised concerns for Ms. Orlov. It reminded her of a smell she had previously experienced in a workplace that had a mold issue. [ 16 ] The Plaintiffs retained Mr. David Mitch of Axxonlab to conduct an air quality analysis in order to assess the level of mold spores in the air. Mr. Mitch’s report, which will be discussed further below, revealed elevated concentrations of mold spores in the basement. [ 17 ] The Plaintiffs’ contractor, Mr. Ruso, also found the smell in the basement to be abnormal. A particularly strong odour emanated from one area where he found the floor to be spongy or soft. Mr. Ruso obtained permission from the Plaintiffs to conduct some exploratory work. [ 18 ] When the flooring was removed, Mr. Ruso discovered that the area was wet. A decision was made to find the source of the moisture. The flooring and the wall panels were thus removed to expose the concrete slab and foundation. The removal of the flooring and wall panelling revealed a crack in the foundation and a hole in the slab. [ 19 ] On August 20, 2020, the Plaintiffs sent the Defendants a notice of defects and default. [10] They also had Mr. Ruso perform significant work to address a number of the issues discovered upon taking possession of the Property. They claim the cost of this work from the Defendants. They also advance claims for other heads of damages, including moral damages for inconvenience and compensation for their complaints in respect of the front door, the closets and the items abandoned on the premises. 3. The Expert Evidence [ 20 ] Two experts produced reports and were called to testify by the Plaintiffs. [ 21 ] The first was Mr. Laurieri, who was qualified without objection as an expert in residential building inspection. As noted, Mr. Laurieri conducted a pre-purchase inspection in May 2020. At the Plaintiffs’ request, he returned to the property on August 8, 2020. Following this visit, he produced a “Site Evaluation & Expertise Report” (the Expert Report ). [11] [ 22 ] Mr. Laurieri’s Expert Report deals with three issues that were not observed at the time of the pre‑purchase inspection: (1) water infiltration in the area of the sliding patio door at the rear of the house, (2) damage to the front door, and (3) damage to the closets. [ 23 ] With respect to water infiltration in the area of the sliding patio door, the Expert Report includes pictures showing water damage to the sliding door frame. Mr. Laurieri observes in his report (exact reproduction of the text): 1 – Signs of water infiltration as well as high moisture readings were noted along the top and upper side portions of the interior sliding door frame in the rear room in the lower ground floor area. 2 – The cause of the water infiltration appears to be from a pre-existing water infiltration from behind the exterior brick veneer wall directly above the said sliding patio door. Indications of previously attempted repairs to the brick veneer mortar was also noted. 3 – High moisture readings as well as active water infiltration was noted on the interior surface finishes at the affected areas. 4 – Removal of the interior finishing materials along the top and upper side portions of the interior sliding door frame indicated previous repairs to the affected area. [ 24 ] Mr. Laurieri then offers the following conclusions (exact reproduction of the text): 1 – The previously performed repairs to stop the water infiltration at the rear sliding door appear to be insufficient to resolve the water infiltration issue. The repairs to the exterior brick veneer do not appear to have been performed as per best standard practices. 2 – Given the type and configuration of the repair, that this situation appears to be a pre existing condition and was present prior to the purchase of the home. Indications of ongoing repairs to the interior finishes to dissimulate the water infiltration appears to have been made. This situation was not visible at the time of the visual pre purchase inspection. […] [ 25 ] Mr. Laurieri recommends: 1 – That the affected exterior brick veneer surfaces be evaluated to determine and identify the area of the water infiltration and that the proper and required repairs be performed by a qualified contractor to stop the source of the water infiltration. 2 – That the damaged interior surface materials be removed and that the affected materials be repaired / replaced and restored by a qualified contractor. 3 – That the damages to the sliding door and frame be evaluated and repaired / replaced as required by a qualified contractor. […] [ 26 ] With respect to the damage to the front door, the report notes that the door was not damaged at the time of the pre-purchase inspection. The report also notes that the removal of permanently anchored shelving had damaged the closets. [12] [ 27 ] The Plaintiffs also adduced the evidence of Mr. David Mitch, who was recognized as an expert in air quality control. Mr. Mitch attended at the residence on July 31, 2020, to test for the presence of mold. Mr. Mitch’s report discloses an elevated level of aspergillus/penicillium-like mold spores in the basement of the residence (i.e., a mold spore count of 3,780/m 3 ). [13] [ 28 ] Mr. Mitch explained that he was not a medical expert and could not opine on whether the quantity of spores detected in his analysis was harmful to human health. [ 29 ] In cross-examination, Mr. Mitch testified that he had frequently seen mold spore counts in the range that was detected in the basement and that he had also seen far more elevated spore counts (i.e., 10,000 /m 3 , 15,000/m 3 and 20,000/m 3 ). [14] POINTS IN ISSUE [ 30 ] The Plaintiffs’ claim raises the following issues: 1. Is the Property affected by one or more latent defects? 2. If so, what reduction in price are the Plaintiffs entitled to? 3. Are the Defendants liable for the damage to the front door? 4. Are the Defendants liable for the removal of shelving from the closets? 5. Are the Defendants liable for failing to remove sundry unwanted items from the Property? [ 31 ] A further point that I must rule on is the Defendants’ objection to the admission into evidence of a number of photos taken by Ms. Orlov and that were not communicated to the Defendants until the first day of trial. [ 32 ] I will begin my analysis with the Defendants’ objection to the photos. I will then examine the Plaintiffs’ claims for latent defects. Finally, I will address their various other claims of lesser monetary significance. ANALYSIS 1. The Defendants’ Objection to the Admission into Evidence of the Plaintiffs’ Photos [ 33 ] On the morning of the first day of the trial, the Plaintiffs provided the Defendants with a bundle of approximately 30 photographs taken by Ms. Orlov in August 2020 [15] . Given the tardy disclosure of these photographs, the Defendants objected to their admission into evidence. [ 34 ] Article 248 of the Code of Civil Procedure ( CCP ) provides that a party must provide the other party with the evidence it intends to use at trial at the latest with the declaration accompanying the request for setting the matter down for trial. If a party fails to do so, such evidence cannot be produced at trial without the court’s authorization: 248. A party in possession of evidence it intends to use at trial must send it to the other parties not later than with the declaration accompanying the request for setting down for trial. The party is dispensed from doing so if the evidence is an exhibit in support of a pleading or if the case protocol provides otherwise. In any other case, the evidence must be sent to the other parties within 30 days after the order to set down for trial is issued or the date of the trial is set, unless the court determines another time limit. A party that has failed to so disclose evidence cannot produce it at trial except with the authorization of the court. 248. La partie qui entend invoquer à l’instruction un élément de preuve en sa possession le communique aux autres parties au plus tard avec la déclaration qui accompagne la demande d’inscription. Elle en est dispensée s’il s’agit d’une pièce au soutien d’un acte de procédure ou si le protocole de l’instance en dispose autrement. Dans les autres cas, la communication est faite dans les 30 jours qui suivent l’ordonnance d’inscription ou la fixation de la date de l’instruction, à moins que le tribunal n’ait fixé un autre délai. La partie qui omet de communiquer ses éléments de preuve ne peut les produire lors de l’instruction si ce n’est qu’avec l’autorisation du tribunal. [ 35 ] Pursuant to article 250 CCP, exhibits must be filed with the court office at least 15 days prior to trial. 250. Unless they have already been filed with the court office for the purposes of the pre-trial conference, exhibits and other evidence must be filed by the parties at least 15 days before the scheduled trial date, or at least three days before that date if the trial is to be held within 15 days. However, in all instances, the court may require that exhibits and other evidence be delivered to it within the time it specifies. […] 250. À moins que les pièces et les autres éléments de preuve n’aient déjà été produits au greffe du tribunal en vue de la conférence préparatoire à l’instruction, les parties les produisent, au moins 15 jours avant la date fixée pour l’instruction; ce délai est d’au moins trois jours à l’avance si la date de l’instruction est fixée à moins de 15 jours. Cependant, dans tous les cas, le tribunal peut demander que les pièces et les autres éléments de preuve lui soient remis dans le délai qu’il indique. […] [ 36 ] Evidently, the Plaintiffs did not comply with these provisions. Under Quebec procedural law, the power to admit exhibits into evidence that have not been disclosed or filed within the requisite delays is discretionary. In this regard, Quebec courts are frequently lenient when it comes to the tardy disclosure and filing of exhibits. [ 37 ] That said, disclosure of the Plaintiffs’ photos to the Defendants on the morning of trial is belated in the extreme. Had the photos been disclosed earlier, the Defendants may well have defended the case differently or entered into settlement negotiations. [ 38 ] Although the existence of photos is mentioned in the Plaintiffs’ notice of defects and default of August 20, 2020, [16] no explanation was provided for their disclosure to the Defendants on the morning of trial. In the circumstances, I find that the extreme tardy disclosure of the photos was inconsistent with the Plaintiffs’ duty of cooperation and transparency under article 20 CCP. I have thus decided to sustain the Defendants’ objection. Accordingly, I have had no regard to the photos in these reasons and my judgment. 2. The Claims for Latent Defects [ 39 ] The Plaintiffs argue that the Property suffers from latent defects resulting in: (1) water infiltration in the area of the rear patio sliding door; and (2) water infiltration in the basement. [ 40 ] Prior to embarking on an analysis of these two issues, it is useful to set out the principles that govern a claim\\n\\n\\n--- END PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... 20 CCP. I have thus decided to sustain the Defendants’ objection. Accordingly, I have had no regard to the photos in these reasons and my judgment. 2. The Claims for Latent Defects [ 39 ] The Plaintiffs argue that the Property suffers from latent defects resulting in: (1) water infiltration in the area of the rear patio sliding door; and (2) water infiltration in the basement. [ 40 ] Prior to embarking on an analysis of these two issues, it is useful to set out the principles that govern a claim for latent defects. 2.1 The Governing Principles [ 41 ] The deed of sale provides that the Property was sold with legal warranty. [17] Legal warranty consists of the warranty of ownership and the warranty of quality. [18] The warranty of quality is codified at article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec ( CCQ ) and requires the seller to warrant that the property sold is free of latent defects: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. 1726. Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l’acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté, ou n’aurait pas donné si haut prix, s’il les avait connus. Il n’est, cependant, pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l’acheteur ni le vice apparent; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert. [ 42 ] A “defect” within the meaning of article 1726 can be material functional or conventional. [19] The notion of defect refers to a loss or deficit of use of the purchased property, as assessed in light of the buyer’s reasonable expectations. [20] The case law has identified four conditions for a defect to be “latent” thus engaging the legal warranty of quality: 1. the defect must be a serious one that renders the property unfit for its intended use or that diminishes its usefulness to such an extent that the buyer would not have purchased for the price paid; 2. the defect must have existed at the time of the sale of the property; 3. the defect must be hidden. The hidden nature of the defect is assessed objectively. Moreover, buyers have a duty to inform themselves; and 4. the defect must have been unknown to the buyer. This inquiry is subjective and the burden of proving the buyer’s knowledge of the defect falls on the seller. [21] [ 43 ] Frequently, the issue in dispute in latent defect cases will relate to the third element of the cause of action – the hidden or apparent nature of the defect at issue. [ 44 ] A hidden defect is one which the reasonably prudent and diligent buyer would not detect. [22] As explained by the Supreme Court in ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. , the standard to be applied “is whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances could have detected the defect at the time of the sale”. [23] [ 45 ] As previously noted, the above standard is an objective one and buyers are not obliged to have recourse to an expert. [24] They must perform a serious and attentive visual examination of the property to be purchased, but are not required to undertake invasive or destructive work. [25] [ 46 ] That said, when an attentive examination of the property reveals the existence of a potential problem, buyers must retain an expert and perform a more in‑depth inspection. [26] Failure to do so may well result in the defect being considered apparent. [ 47 ] The buyer who establishes that the purchased property is affected by a latent defect has a choice of recourse under article 1604 CCQ. [27] If the consequences of the defect are severe and reduce the usefulness of the property to such an extent that the buyer would not have completed the purchase, he or she may seek to have the sale set aside (formerly the redhibitory action). [ 48 ] On the other hand, if the buyer would have bought the property notwithstanding the defect, but only at a lower price, he or she may seek a reduction in the purchase price (formerly the estimatory or quanti minoris action). The principles governing the determination of the appropriate price reduction in the context of a latent defect claim are discussed below. [ 49 ] Moreover, if the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, the buyer can also obtain damages that do not relate directly to the property [28] . [ 50 ] In the present case, the Plaintiffs have brought an action in reduction of price based principally on the cost to repair the alleged latent defects. The Plaintiffs also seek a further award of damages for inconvenience and for cleaning costs. 2.2 The Exterior Wall in the Area of the Rear Sliding Patio Door [ 51 ] The evidence establishes the existence of a water infiltration problem in the area of the rear sliding patio door. In his Expert Report, Mr. Laurieri describes signs of water infiltration and high moisture meter readings in this area. A number of photos included in the Expert Report reveal damage to the sliding door frame resulting from water infiltration. [29] [ 52 ] For the reasons that follow, I find that the water infiltration problem in the rear sliding door area results from a latent defect affecting the impermeability of the exterior wall. [ 53 ] The Expert Report indicates that the “cause” of the water infiltration appears to be a pre-existing water infiltration from behind the exterior brick veneer wall directly above the sliding door. When Mr. Ruso opened the interior wall to identify the source of the humidity affecting the rear sliding door area, he discovered that there was no vapour barrier between the bricks and the interior gyprock and that the wood was dark from humidity. The absence of a vapour barrier is abnormal and the presence of water in this area demonstrates that the external wall was not built properly or in accordance with the rules of the trade at the time of construction. [ 54 ] The Expert Report and Mr. Ruso’s observations confirm that the construction of the exterior wall above the sliding door – whether original or as a result of subsequent repairs or renovations – was defective and allowed water to penetrate into the dwelling. It was not contested that repelling water is one of the key functions of an external wall and it was evidently not being performed as required, thereby resulting in damage to the interior. The defect affecting the exterior wall is serious in that it diminishes the usefulness of the Property. [ 55 ] Given the extent of the damage discovered, Mr. Laurieri opined that the water infiltration issue was pre-existing. Based on this evidence, and since the water infiltration issue affecting the rear exterior wall in the area of the sliding door was discovered very shortly after the Plaintiffs took possession of the Property, I find that this defect existed at the time of the sale. [ 56 ] In view of the foregoing, the key question is whether the defect was hidden or apparent. [ 57 ] As noted, the test in this regard queries whether a reasonably prudent and diligent buyer would have detected the defect. The evidence establishes that the Plaintiffs acted as reasonably prudent and diligent buyers. They retained the services of Mr. Laurieri, who performed a pre‑purchase inspection and prepared a report that was read by the Plaintiffs. This in itself indicates that the Plaintiffs met the requisite standard of prudence and diligence. [30] [ 58 ] Mr. Laurieri conducted a thorough visual inspection of the premises and nothing in the evidence suggests that it was in any way inadequate. While Mr. Laurieri’s inspection was non-invasive, that is perfectly consistent with what is required for a serious and attentive visual examination of the property. [ 59 ] Mr. Laurieri’s inspection did reveal that the exterior brick façade, including in the area of the rear sliding door, was “in generally poor condition”. [31] However, in his testimony, Mr. Laurieri explained that the issue with the brick siding observed during his pre-purchase inspection was not structural. He further testified that the poor condition of the brick exterior was not indicative of a water infiltration problem and that there were no visible signs of such a problem. The moisture meter used by Mr. Laurieri during the pre‑purchase inspection did not reveal any elevated humidity readings in the relevant area. Mr. Laurieri’s evidence on these points was uncontradicted. [ 60 ] In short, the evidence establishes that a serious and attentive examination of the property did not reveal a water infiltration issue in the area of the rear sliding door. Such examination did reveal the existence of a general issue with the exterior brick veneer. However, the evidence before me is that the issue thus observed is not indicative of the existence of a water infiltration problem either generally or in the specific area of the rear sliding door. [32] [ 61 ] Based on the evidence, I therefore find that the reasonably prudent and diligent buyer would not have detected the water infiltration problem affecting the rear sliding door area and that the source of such problem constituted a hidden defect. [ 62 ] The fourth and final condition to establish a claim for latent defects relates to the buyer’s knowledge of the defect before the sale. The Defendants have not suggested, nor attempted to discharge their burden of proving that the Plaintiffs were aware of the defect resulting in the water infiltration problem. [ 63 ] It follows that the Plaintiffs have satisfied the conditions for establishing a latent defect claim in respect of the water infiltration problem affecting the rear sliding door area. 2.3 The Basement Water Infiltration Issue [ 64 ] The Plaintiffs also submit that the basement of the Property is affected by a latent defect resulting in a water infiltration problem that they discovered only after taking possession of their new home. [ 65 ] The expert evidence relating to the basement consists of Mr. Mitch’s report and testimony. [33] This evidence simply discloses the existence of elevated concentrations of mold spores in the ambient basement air. As explained by Mr. Mitch, such concentrations, while not exceedingly high, are indicative of an underlying issue of water infiltration or moisture build-up requiring further investigation. [ 66 ] The most comprehensive evidence relating to the basement is the testimony of Mr. Ruso. Given the unpleasant musty smell in that area of the Property, he was asked to carry out exploratory work to determine the source of the odour. He observed that the laminate flooring was a bit soft, but was unable to detect any moisture without removing it. [ 67 ] When the laminate flooring was removed, Mr. Ruso observed the presence of water. He also noted that portions of the plywood subfloor were black from moisture and rotten. Further exploration, including removal of some of the paneling on the basement walls, led to the discovery of a hole in the concrete slab and a significant crack in the foundation wall. [ 68 ] The hole discovered by Mr. Ruso was approximately 10 inches wide and 10 inches deep and was situated in the slab adjacent to the foundation wall in the area of the chimney. As for the crack, it was situated in the front right corner of the foundation, and it extended up the foundation wall. Mr. Ruso observed that the water discovered under the laminate flooring originated from both the hole and this crack. [ 69 ] In his testimony, Mr. Mohammad Ali explained that he never had any water infiltration problem in this part of the basement over the 11 or so years that the Defendants owned the Property. The Defendants argue that the most probable explanation for the water under the basement floor is that there must have been an unusually severe rainfall at some point between the date they vacated the Property and the date that the Plaintiffs took possession. They submit that such an event likely resulted in flooding of the basement. [ 70 ] With respect, the explanation proffered by the Defendants is speculative and unsupported by any evidence. Based on the evidence adduced before me, I am compelled to conclude that the probable sources of the water observed on the basement floor are the hole in the slab and the foundation crack discovered by Mr. Ruso. [ 71 ] The Defendants also suggest that the water infiltration results from the age of the building, which was constructed in 1957. Beyond the mere fact that the home is about 70 years old, there is no evidence to support this theory. [ 72 ] In particular, there was no evidence adduced by the Defendants to show that the hole or the crack resulted from normal wear and tear. The preponderance of the evidence thus leads me to conclude that they both result from defective work performed at the time of construction. Indeed, had the home been properly built, there would not be a hole in the slab nor a crack in the foundation wall of the sort discovered by Mr. Ruso. [ 73 ] It is evident that a hole and a crack in the foundation that allow water to penetrate into the interior of the dwelling’s basement diminish and impair the normal usefulness of the Property. These defects are therefore serious. [ 74 ] I further find that the defects existed at the time of the sale of the Property to the Plaintiffs. Indeed, Mr. Ruso observed that the water he discovered had been there for some time and that portions of the subfloor were black and rotten. Moreover, the musty smell observed by the Plaintiffs upon taking possession of the Property, and the elevated mold concentrations observed by Mr. Mitch shortly after the purchase, all suggest that water had been infiltrating into the basement for some time prior to the sale. [ 75 ] I turn now to whether these defects were hidden or apparent. [ 76 ] With respect to the exterior of the Property, Mr. Laurieri’s Pre-Purchase Inspection Report notes that “The visible portions of the exterior poured concrete foundations ate [ sic ] in good and serviceable condition”. [34] On the other hand, Mr. Laurieri’s inspection of the basement interior did reveal some issues. He writes in his Pre-Purchase Inspection Report: Indications of water infiltration, high humidity as well as some cracking and spalling of the lower portions of the interior of the poured concrete foundation walls was [ sic ] noted. [35] [ 77 ] In his testimony, Mr. Laurieri stated that the above comment related to a visible foundation crack in a separate unfinished part of the basement. This visible crack, which was disclosed and repaired by the Defendants prior to the sale, [36] was not the source of the water infiltration discovered by Mr. Ruso in the finished portion of the basement. [ 78 ] Mr. Ruso explained that he had to remove the flooring and some of the basement panelling to reveal the hole and crack in issue. These defects in the slab and the foundation were thus covered by the basement flooring and the wall paneling and could only be discovered by invasive or destructive work. Defects that are concealed in such fashion are considered hidden. [37] [ 79 ] That said, once he discovered the crack, Mr. Ruso was able to observe, on careful examination, that it could also be seen from the exterior of the Property. Mr. Ruso explained that the portion of the crack visible from the outside was extremely narrow and fine and that such cracks are very common in foundations. [ 80 ] In view of its size, the prevalence of such fine cracks, and the fact that it was not noted by Mr. Laurieri, I find that the portion of the crack that was visible from the exterior does not constitute an indicium or sign that ought to have raised the Plaintiffs’ suspicions, acting as reasonable and prudent buyers, of a water infiltration problem. [ 81 ] On the other hand, when they visited the Property prior to making an offer to purchase, the Plaintiffs noted the presence of a dehumidifier in the basement area. Mr. Laurieri similarly observed that a dehumidifier was being used by the Defendants. [38] [ 82 ] Mr. Laurieri explained that 90% of humidity issues are caused by a building’s occupants, rather than by a structural problem. Mr. Laurieri’s evidence on this point was uncontradicted. In the circumstances, I find that the mere use of a dehumidifier in the basement of the Property did not suggest the existence of a water infiltration problem to a reasonably prudent and diligent buyer. In doing so, I note that the Defendants state that they were unaware of any water infiltration problem in that part of the basement and that they had used the space in question for many years without incident. [ 83 ] In short, a serious and attentive visual examination of the Property would not lead the reasonably prudent buyer to detect the existence of a defect affecting the basement. [39] Nor would it lead such a buyer to suspect a potential problem with the foundation wall or concrete slab requiring further investigation. [ 84 ] As noted by Côté J.A. writing for the Court of Appeal in Lahaie v. Laperrière : [51] La prudence et la diligence requises des acheteurs sont établies par leur conduite en exigeant la réparation par le vendeur des fissures apparentes et en requérant une inspection avant l'achat par un professionnel de la construction. Cette inspection a d'ailleurs révélé que la maison était en bon état et bien entretenue. [52] En utilisant les services d'un...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\nThe evidence offers no assistance in assessing the cost of this more limited excavation. In the circumstances, I must again arbitrate and I will award a price reduction of $10,000 for excavation and repairing the crack. [ 124 ] In view of the foregoing, I find that the Plaintiffs are entitled to a price reduction of $24,013.05 in respect of the latent defects affecting the basement. 4. The Plaintiffs’ Other Claims for Compensation [ 125 ] I turn now to the Plaintiffs’ other claims for compensation that are unrelated to the latent defects affecting the Property. 4.1 The Damaged Front Door [ 126 ] Upon visiting the Property on July 27 or 28, the Plaintiffs noticed that the front door was dented. [56] Since no such dents were apparent in May, the Plaintiffs assume that the door was dented when the Defendants moved out their personal effects from the Property. [ 127 ] In his testimony, Mr. Mohammad Ali states that the Defendants did not hire movers and that they themselves transported their furniture and personal effects to their new residence. He explains that the items were mostly moved out through the garage and not the front door. He denies having damaged the door and asserts that it was intact when the Defendants vacated the Property. [ 128 ] In his report, Mr. Laurieri indicates that the damage to the front door was caused at the time of the move by the seller. [57] This statement is wholly speculative and I give it no weight. [58] [ 129 ] The evidence is inconclusive as to how the front door was damaged and by whom. However, based on the parties’ testimony, I find that, in all probability, the door was damaged at some point prior to the purchase of the Property on July 28, 2020. [ 130 ] In these circumstances, the “theory of risk” (“ théorie des risques ”) governs which of the parties bears the risk of the damage to the front door of the Property. Under the general law, the second paragraph of article 1456 CCQ imposes on the debtor of the obligation to deliver property [59] (i.e., the seller) the risk of loss of or damage to the property until it is delivered: 1456. […] The debtor of the obligation to deliver the property continues, however, to bear the risks attached to the property until it is delivered. 1456. […] Toutefois, tant que la délivrance du bien n’a pas été faite, le débiteur de l’obligation de délivrance continue d’assumer les risques y afférents. [ 131 ] Pursuant to article 1456 CCQ, the Defendants would thus bear the risk of damage to the Property prior to delivery of same to the Plaintiffs. However, this provision is not of public order [60] and, in this case, the parties chose to contract out of it. [ 132 ] The deed of sale provides that the risks are transferred to the buyers as of the date of its conclusion: TRANSFER OF RISK Notwithstanding paragraph 2 of article 1456 of the Civil Code of Québec , the Purchaser shall assume the risks attached to the immovable, in accordance with article 950 of the Civil Code of Québec , as of the date hereof. [61] [ 133 ] Article 950 CCQ codifies the principle that the risks related to the loss of property fall on the owner thereof ( res perit domino ): 950. The owner of the property assumes the risks of loss. 950. Le propriétaire du bien assume les risques de perte. [ 134 ] Accordingly, the risks of damage to or loss of the Property passed to the Plaintiffs on July 28, 2020, upon execution of the deed of sale, rather than on July 31, 2020, the date upon which they took possession. [ 135 ] In view of my finding that the damage to the front door occurred in all probability prior to July 28, 2020, such damage occurred while the Defendants were the owners of the Property. As a result, they bear the risk of the damage in question. [ 136 ] The damage to the front door of the Property is partial, and indeed relatively minor. In the circumstances, the Plaintiffs are bound to take the property in the state in which it was, but are entitled to a price reduction. [62] [ 137 ] The Plaintiffs claim $4,823.20 for replacing the front door. At the time of trial, the door had yet to be replaced and the amount claimed is based on an evaluation received by the Plaintiffs from their contractor. [ 138 ] There are two difficulties with the amount claimed by the Plaintiffs for the damage to the front door. The first is that while the dents to the door are obvious, it is unclear that the door itself needs to be replaced and can’t simply be repaired. In his Expert Report, Mr. Laurieri recommends replacing the door. This recommendation, however, is unaccompanied by any supporting analysis or explanation. The Defendants have not sought an estimate for fixing the door as opposed to replacing it. [ 139 ] The second difficulty is that there is no documentary evidence in support of the estimate of $4,823.20 for replacing the door. Presumably, a written estimate with a breakdown for parts and labour would not be difficult to obtain. In the absence of such a document, I am unable to assess whether the amount claimed for replacing the door is reasonable. [ 140 ] In the circumstances, the precise amount of the price reduction to which the Plaintiffs are entitled on account of the damage to the front door has not been established and I am left with no choice but to “arbitrate”. In the exercise of my discretion, I will grant an amount of $1,500 for the front door. 4.2 The Removal of the Closet Shelving [ 141 ] When the Plaintiffs first visited the Property, they observed what they believed to be “built-in” shelving in four of the closets. This shelving was removed by the Defendants when they moved out. [63] [ 142 ] In his testimony, Mr. Mohammad Ali recognizes that he made a mistake by removing the fixtures that had been affixed to the walls in two of the closets. As for the other two closets, he explains that he did not remove anything affixed to the walls. What he had removed were “cabinets” that he had placed inside the closets. Mr. Mohammad Ali states that these cabinets constitute furniture and not “fixtures”. [ 143 ] I accept Mr. Mohammad Ali’s testimony as to the nature of the items removed from the closets. After all, he had lived in the Property for approximately 12 years and is the witness best acquainted with the items in the Property when it was occupied by the Defendants. [ 144 ] Accordingly, I find that the Defendants were entitled to remove and take with them the cabinets that they had placed in two of the closets. These cabinets are furniture, not permanent fixtures, and are not included in the deed of sale. [ 145 ] However, as recognized by Mr. Mohammad Ali, the Defendants improperly removed permanently attached fixtures from the walls of two other closets. In doing so, the Defendants committed a contractual fault and are liable for the damage thereby caused to the Plaintiffs. [ 146 ] The Plaintiffs claim $2,246.76 for shelves they purchased at Ikea to replace the removed fixtures and $400 for assembly of the purchased items. [ 147 ] The Plaintiffs have produced receipts for the purchase of items amounting to $2,246.76. [64] However, on cross-examination it was revealed that the receipts include various items unrelated to the closets. [65] The cost of these unrelated purchases total $169.43, and this amount must be deducted from the Plaintiffs’ claim. [ 148 ] The total spent by the Plaintiffs on new closet fixtures is thus $2,077.33. This amount is for four closets. In view of my conclusion that the Defendants improperly removed fixtures from only two of the four closets, I will award the Plaintiffs half of the amount they spent on replacing fixtures. They are thus entitled to $1,038.67 for the shelving removed from the closets. I will also award them $200 for closet assembly, which also represents half the amount sought for four closets. [ 149 ] Mr. Mohammad Ali explains that he only paid about $150 for the fixtures he removed from the closets. That may be. On the other hand, the Defendants improperly removed these fixtures, which forced the Plaintiffs to act quickly in order to purchase replacement shelving required to make the closets usable. In the circumstances, the Defendants can hardly complain that the Plaintiffs overspent. [ 150 ] I will thus award the Plaintiffs the sum of $1,238.67 for the fixtures improperly removed from two closets by the Defendants. 4.3 The Defendants’ Failure to Remove Sundry Items from the Property [ 151 ] The Defendants failed to remove certain items from the Property when they moved out their personal belongings. The abandoned items included boxes and furniture left in the basement, an electric fireplace and even some tires. [ 152 ] Mr. Mohammad Ali admits to having left these items believing that the Plaintiffs might want them. [66] Excepting such items that were specifically included in the sale, I find that the Defendants were required to remove their personal belongings from the Property. Failure to do so constitutes a contractual fault. [ 153 ] The Plaintiffs claim $300 in compensation for the removal of the Defendants’ unwanted items. This amount is reasonable, and I will award it. LEGAL COSTS & EXPERT FEES [ 154 ] Legal costs will be awarded to the Plaintiffs in accordance with the general principle that the unsuccessful party bears the costs (article 340 CCP). An issue arises, however, in respect of the expert fees claimed by the Plaintiffs as legal costs. [ 155 ] With respect to Mr. Mitch, both his report and testimony were useful. The Plaintiffs’ claim $436.90 as expert fees for Mr. Mitch’s services. [67] The amount claimed is reasonable and will be awarded to the Plaintiffs as legal costs. [ 156 ] As for Mr. Laurieri, the situation is slightly different. His Expert Report was of some use, but it also addresses matters – the dent to the front door and the fixtures removed from the closets – that are not appropriate subjects for expert evidence. Moreover, Mr. Laurieri testified both as an expert and, in respect of his pre‑purchase inspection, as an ordinary witness. Only his fees for testifying as an expert may be claimed as legal costs. [ 157 ] In the circumstances, I will award the Plaintiffs $437.43 for Mr. Laurieri’s fees, which is half the amount claimed. [68] DISPOSITION FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 158 ] SUSTAINS the objection of the Defendants, Khosro Mohammad Ali and Véronique Marie, to Exhibit P‑13; [ 159 ] GRANTS in part the Introductory Application of the Plaintiffs, Inna Orlov and Michael Orlov; [ 160 ] CONDEMNS the Defendants, Khosro Mohammad Ali and Véronique Marie, to pay the Plaintiffs, Inna Orlov and Michael Orlov, the amount of $33,565.72 bearing interest at the legal rate from the date of service, February 2, 2021; [ 161 ] WITH legal costs including expert fees fixed at $874.33. __________________________________ ANDRES C. GARIN, J.S.C. Maître Asher Neudorfer Sarna Neudorfer s.e.n.c. Counsel for the Plaintiffs Maître Patrick Lamanna Aedifico Avocats inc. Counsel for the Defendants Hearing dates: March 2 and 3, 2023 [1] Exhibit P‑1. [2] Exhibit D‑6. [3] Exhibit P‑2. [4] Exhibit D‑3. This problem was noted in the Pre-Purchase Inspection Report (see Exhibit P‑2, p. 2 and 14). [5] Exhibit D‑10 (items D3.1 and D14). This problem was noted in the Pre-Purchase Inspection Report (see Exhibit P‑2, p. 2 and 27‑28). [6] Exhibit P‑10 (photos of front door). [7] Ibid. (closet interior photos). [8] Ibid. (photos of patio door frame). [9] They had noticed that a dehumidifier was running in the basement laundry room. Apparently, the Defendants had constantly used a dehumidifier in the basement over many years. [10] Exhibit P‑3. [11] Exhibit P‑10. [12] In cross-examination, Mr. Laurieri conceded that an easy fix would be to patch the holes in the closets. [13] Exhibit P‑9, p. 3 and 6; testimony of D. Mitch. [14] Mr. Mitch believed that the highest mold spore count he had ever observed was 50,000/m 3 . [15] Exhibit P‑13. [16] Exhibit P‑3. [17] Exhibit P‑1 (p. 2). [18] Article 1716 CCQ. [19] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. , 2007 SCC 50 , para. 48 [ ABB Inc. ]. As explained by LeBel and Deschamps JJ., “material defects” relate to the specific good and “functional defects” relate to the good’s design. Such defects are assessed by reference to the normal use to which buyers put the goods in question. A “conventional defect” is assessed in light of the particular use of the good intended by the buyer and communicated to the seller. [20] ABB Inc. , supra , para. 49 . [21] See Leroux v. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 , para. 40 [ Leroux ]; ABB Inc. , supra , paras. 50‑54 ; Verville v. 9146‑7308 Québec Inc. , 2008 QCCA 1593 , para. 37 [ Verville ]; Lahaie v. Laperrière , 2009 QCCA 1285 , para. 33 [ Lahaie ]. [22] Article 1726(2) CCQ; Leroux , supra , paras. 42‑46 ; Verville , supra , para. 38 ; Lahaie , supra , para. 49 ; Marcoux v. Picard , 2008 QCCA 259 , para. 20 [ Marcoux ]; St‑Louis v. Morin , 2006 QCCA 1643 , paras. 27‑28 [ St‑Louis ]. [23] ABB Inc. , supra , para. 42 ; see also para. 51. [24] St‑Louis , supra , para. 27 ; Lahaie , supra , para. 49 . [25] Leroux , supra , para. 44 ; Marcoux , supra , para. 21 ; Rivard v. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 , para. 7 [ Rivard ]; Paulin v. Gauthier , 2014 QCCA 1897 , para. 4 [ Paulin ]; Thériault v. Martin , 2020 QCCA 1294 , para. 10 . [26] Rivard , supra , para. 7 ; Leroux , supra , para. 44‑46 ; St‑Louis , supra , paras. 38‑39 ; Paulin , supra , para. 4 . [27] Verville , supra , para. 56 ; Lahaie , supra , paras. 35‑38 . [28] Article 1728 CCQ. [29] See the photos in Exhibit P‑10. [30] St-Pierre v. Levy , 2022 QCCS 1066 , paras. 94‑95 . [31] Exhibit P‑2, p. 2 and 18‑19. [32] See by analogy: Crooks v. Nguyen , 2022 QCCS 55 , para. 81 . [33] Mr. Laurieri’s August 2020 Expert Report does not address the basement. [34] Exhibit P‑2, p. 27. [35] Ibid. [36] Exhibit D‑10 (items D3.1 and D14). [37] J. Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 nd Ed. (Montreal: Wilson & Lafleur, 2008) [ Edwards ], no. 385 (p. 181‑182). [38] At the time, no unpleasant musty odour was noted in the basement by either the Plaintiffs or by Mr. Laurieri. [39] With the exception, of course, of the visible crack detected by Mr. Laurieri. This crack was declared and repaired by the Defendants prior to the purchase. As noted, it is not the source of the water infiltration problem in the finished portion of the basement. [40] Lahaie , supra , paras. 51‑52 . See also Leroux , supra , para. 46 : “ On ne peut reprocher à l’acheteur qui fait appel à un inspecteur d’avoir manqué à son devoir de prudence et de diligence si l’inspecteur n’a pas décelé d’indice de vice que l’acheteur raisonnable ne pouvait pas lui-même déceler. C’est seulement lorsque cet inspecteur décèle un indice sérieux que l’acheteur doit pousser son étude ”. [41] Verville , supra , paras. 71 and 77 . [42] Ibid. , para. 67; Lahaie , supra , para. 92 . [43] Verville , supra , paras. 61‑62 ; Lahaie , supra , para. 91 . [44] Edwards, supra , no. 539 (p. 258). [45] Ibid . [46] P.‑G. Jobin and M. Cumyn, La vente , 3rd Ed. (Cowansville : Y\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 4\\nude ”. [41] Verville , supra , paras. 71 and 77 . [42] Ibid. , para. 67; Lahaie , supra , para. 92 . [43] Verville , supra , paras. 61‑62 ; Lahaie , supra , para. 91 . [44] Edwards, supra , no. 539 (p. 258). [45] Ibid . [46] P.‑G. Jobin and M. Cumyn, La vente , 3rd Ed. (Cowansville : Yvon Blais, 2007) no. 180 (p. 239-240); Verville , supra , para. 65 ; Lahaie , supra , paras. 97‑100 . [47] Tomlin v. Lelliott , 2016 QCCS 5530 , para. 137 . [48] Edwards, supra , no. 612 (p. 297). [49] Exhibit P‑7 [50] See Exhibit P‑6 for the descriptions. [51] Exhibit P‑2, p. 2 and 14; and Exhibit D‑3. [52] Exhibit P‑7. [53] Exhibit P‑7 [54] See Exhibit P‑6 for the descriptions. [55] Exhibit P‑7. [56] Photos of the dented front entrance door are found in Exhibit P‑10. [57] Exhibit P‑10. [58] In cross-examination, Mr. Laurieri recognized that he did not know how the damage to the front door was caused. [59] The obligation to deliver does not mean physical delivery in the sense of “ livraison ”. It is defined at article 1717 CCQ: 1717 . The obligation to deliver the property is fulfilled when the seller puts the buyer in possession of the property or consents to his taking possession of it and all hindrances are removed. 1717 . L’obligation de délivrer le bien est remplie lorsque le vendeur met l’acheteur en possession du bien ou consent à ce qu’il en prenne possession, tous obstacles étant écartés . Delivery of the property is distinct from transfer of ownership which, in principle, occurs instantaneously upon the conclusion of the contract of sale. See on these points : Kingsway, compagnie d'assurances générales v. Bombardier Produits récréatifs inc. , 2010 QCCA 1518 , paras. 25‑28 [ Kingsway ]. [60] Kingsway , supra , para. 39 . [61] Exhibit P‑1, p. 2. [62] J.‑L. Baudouin and P.‑G. Jobin, Les obligations, 7th Ed. (Cowansville (QC): Yvon Blais, 2013), by P.‑G. Jobin and N. Vézina, at No. 866 (p. 1072). [63] The photos in Exhibit P‑10 shows that fixtures had been unscrewed from the walls of two closets. [64] See Exhibit P‑7 ( Ikea receipts). [65] See Exhibit D‑14. [66] He recognizes that the tires were left at the Property by error. [67] Exhibit P‑14. [68] Exhibit P‑11.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03279569745063782, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\", \"title\": \"Orlov c. Ali, 2023 QCCS 1790 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.03279569745063782, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\"}, \"token_count\": 13432, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs1790-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Hannan c. Allcut, 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII) | Hannan c. Allcut | CanLII Hannan c. Allcut, 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII) Date: 2009-07-17 File number: 500-17-025251-052 Citation: Hannan c. Allcut, 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/24pbv > Hannan c. Allcut 2009 QCCS 3254 JM 2257 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL No: 500-17-025251-052 DATE: July 17, 2009 ______________________________________________________________________ IN THE PRESENCE OF: THE HONOURABLE GENEVIÈVE MARCOTTE, J.S.C. ______________________________________________________________________ MONICA HANNAN TIM ROZON Plaintiffs/Cross-Defendants v. STELLA MARGARET ALLCUT ESTATE OF ROBERT MUIR STELLA MARGARET ALLCUT , in her capacity as liquidator to the estate of Robert Muir Defendants/Cross-Plaintiffs ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ INTRODUCTION [ 1 ] Monica Hannan (\\\" Hannan \\\") and Tim Rozon (\\\" Rozon \\\") (collectively \\\" Buyers \\\") are claiming damages in the amount of $181,225.21 from the Estate of Robert Muir (\\\" Muir \\\") and Stella Margaret Allcut (\\\" Allcut \\\") (collectively \\\" Vendors \\\") as a result of latent defects affecting the foundation of the house, discovered in the year following the purchase of the house. [ 2 ] Vendors deny the existence of a latent defect given that the defects were visible at the time of purchase.  They assert that Buyers' legal action is an abuse of \\\" ester en justice \\\" which has forced them to incur significant attorney fees amounting to $58,622.87 [1] , which they are claiming in cross-demand. THE FACTS First visit and offer [ 3 ] On March 29, 2003, the Buyers became interested in the Vendors' property located in Beaconsfield, a few doors away from their home. [ 4 ] The house had only just been put up for sale a few days earlier.  At the time, Muir and Allcut, respectively 78 and 75 years old, had lived there for 40 years.  They were looking to sell their house to move into a seniors' residence, given that Allcut was confined to a wheelchair. [ 5 ] The Buyers, who were expecting a second child were and looking to purchase a larger home with the potential to build a playroom in the basement, found the home to fit their needs and budget. [ 6 ] Following a two-hour visit with their respective fathers during which they were assured by Muir that the unfinished basement was \\\"high and dry\\\" condition, the Buyers offered to purchase the home for the full asking price of $209,000. [2] [ 7 ] The next day, on March 30, 2003, the Vendors accepted the offer which was subject to a pre-purchase inspection. Pre-purchase Inspection [ 8 ] The Buyers hired Joe Rodrigues, a structural engineer (\\\" the Inspector \\\"), to conduct a pre-purchase inspection, which took place on April 1, 2003. [ 9 ] His inspection of the interior and exterior of the home, including the \\\"cluttered\\\" basement took two hours. [ 10 ] During the inspection, the Inspector noticed signs of settlement, on each side of the support beam, given the particular layout of the support walls, which were off-center from the main support beam . [ 11 ] The Inspector asked Muir some questions regarding signs of prior water infiltration on the basement floor near the back stairwell.  Although he admitted to having had water near the back stairwell a few years before, Muir claimed to have resolved the problem by installing a small drain measuring 2 inches by 4 inches and assured him that no other infiltration has occurred since. [ 12 ] The Inspector noticed cracks on the front masonry veneer.  He recommended that the Buyers seal these cracks to avoid further expansion. [ 13 ] At the end of the inspection, the Inspector provided the Buyers with his written report [3] , which he briefly reviewed with them on site. [ 14 ] His report acknowledges extensive signs of prior leakage near the rear basement stairwell and the probability of water infiltration.  It also confirms the presence of mold along the perimeter of the interior walls of the basement. [ 15 ] It mentions that the foundations are made of concrete blocks but that 95% of interior foundation walls were not visible at the time of inspection due to furniture , wall coverings and debris. [4] [ 16 ] The report refers as well to the vendor's statement that he never had water in the basement, except near the rear basement access door. [ 17 ] Insofar as the parget covering the exterior foundation walls is concerned, the report states that it is in excellent condition while there are cracks in the front masonry veneer which need to be sealed. [ 18 ] It also indicates that the level of settlement noticed is typical for homes of this era and location, but suggests that an engineering investigation be carried out. [5] Lifting of condition and transaction [ 19 ] Further to their discussion with the Inspector and their summary review of his inspection report, the Buyers declared themselves satisfied with the outcome of the inspection and lifted the condition on their offer. [ 20 ] While the sale transaction was scheduled to take place in June 2003, Muir gave the Buyers a set of keys to the house as soon as they moved out of the property in early May 2003, to enable them to plan their repair and renovation work.  However, the Buyers refrained from carrying out any work prior to the closing on June 19, 2003. [ 21 ] Before the signing of the deed of sale, members of the Vendors' family emptied the house of its contents, except for certain items which were left behind covering up the back wall of the basement, namely old doors, a large freezer, a stand-up shower and some shelves. [ 22 ] The house was sold with legal warranty and the Deed signed by the parties [6] incorporates by reference the Promise to purchase, as well as the conditions set out in the Listing. [7] After the purchase / Appearance of defect [ 23 ] After the closing, Rozon and his father removed the items left behind in the basement and cleaned its white Masonite walls. They noticed no signs of leaking water.  They installed a piano and a sofa in the basement. [ 24 ] In February 2004, Allcut phoned up Hannan asking whether any water infiltrations had occurred in the basement and suggesting that the Buyers \\\"give the sump pump a knock\\\". [ 25 ] On March 5, 2004, Hannan noticed water pouring in through the back wall onto the basement floor, as well as crumbling stone and cement. [8] [ 26 ] The same day, the Buyers contacted their real estate agent. On the following day, they removed the wall panelling and noticed that the foundation wall was bulging. [ 27 ] The real estate agent visited the house on March 6 th . She suggested that there may be a latent defect and advised the Buyers to contact an expert in foundation and that they contact a lawyer. [ 28 ] On March 9, 2004, Hannan called Allcut to let her know of the problems and offered them the opportunity to visit the house to make their own observations. [9] [ 29 ] Given their concern about the structural capacity of the foundation walls, the Buyers installed posts to reinforce the structural support of the bulging wall. [ 30 ] They also contacted a number of foundation specialists. [10] These specialists recommended that they first obtain an expert opinion in respect to the structural capacity of the house. Hiring of expert Hosseini [ 31 ] On March 16, 2004, Dr. Mohammed Hosseini (\\\" Hosseini \\\"), a geotechnical engineer, visited the house. [ 32 ] At the time, the concrete blocks were visible since the Masonite walls had been removed. [11] [ 33 ] The bottom of the back foundation wall was seriously disintegrated.  Several cracks in the blocks and around the windows appeared to have been repaired with grout. Hosseini was unable to determine when these repairs were done. [ 34 ] He noticed several cracks in the masonry veneer covering the front external walls of the house.  Wooden studs behind the Masonite walls were in an advanced stage of rot while others showed no signs of deterioration and appeared to have been replaced three or four years earlier. [ 35 ] On March 18, 2004, Hosseini conducted further testing, including material sampling, water resistance testing and measurements of differential movements along the foundation walls. [ 36 ] He issued a report dated March 30, 2004. [12] He concludes that there are signs of distortion of the building and that the concrete block foundation was seriously damaged, with the bottom of the back wall completely disintegrated. He finds the quality of the concrete at the base of the foundation walls to be mediocre. He recommends that all foundation walls be replaced. [ 37 ] In his report, Hosseini criticizes the Inspector for failing to further investigate, after he reported the existence of cracks in the front masonry veneer and water infiltrations near the back stairwell. [ 38 ] In light of his findings, Hosseini recommends that the foundation be replaced to restore the structural integrity of the house, and that a new French drain be installed. Notices and letters of demand [ 39 ] Further to Hosseini's report, Hannan called Allcut to inform her of the expert's findings. [ 40 ] Despite this call, Vendors made no attempts to visit the premises. [ 41 ] Further to their silence, the Buyers put the Inspector on notice by letter of attorney dated March 23, 2004. [13] [ 42 ] On March 25, 2004, the Buyers' attorney also sent a letter of demand to the Vendors [14] , accusing Muir of bad faith in failing to disclose the latent defect affecting the foundation wall. This letter marked the beginning of a long legal battle between the parties. [ 43 ] A second letter dated June 18, 2004 [15] was sent to the Inspector, based on Hosseini's comments regarding his failure to conduct a further investigation after noticing cracks in the front masonry wall. [ 44 ] In a letter dated July 5, 2004 [16] , the Inspector denied any wrongdoing and raised the fact that the Buyers failed to retain a structural engineer notwithstanding his suggestion that they do so. Visit by Vendors' expert Beauchamp [ 45 ] On August 11, 2004, shortly before the repairs began, the Vendors sent their expert, Gilles Beauchamp (\\\" Beauchamp \\\"), an architect with a Masters degree in material aging and deterioration, to visit the premises. [17] [ 46 ] He conducted a 45 minute visit, during which he did not question Hannan about the representations made by the Vendors at the time of the sale, the condition of the house at the time, nor about whether the uncovered walls were visible at the time of the inspection. [ 47 ] Following this visit, Beauchamp prepared a written report in which he denies the existence of a latent defect since 30% of the basement walls were visible at the time of purchase, and given that there were visible signs of mold and cracks in repaired concrete blocks, some of which were even displaced. [18] [ 48 ] Beauchamp concludes that punctual repairs may be carried out and that it is not necessary to replace the foundation walls. He insists on the added value brought to the property as a result of the replacement of the existing foundation with a new poured concrete one. He also underlines the fact that the recommended repair work entails the addition of a new French drain, an insulated slab on grade and the replacement of wall membranes. Performance of repair work [ 49 ] In September 2004, Hénault & Gosselin, who were the lowest of two contractors invited to bid, carried out the repair work. [19] [ 50 ] Given changes brought to municipal regulations regarding foundations, the Buyers were required to replace the concrete block foundation with a poured reinforced concrete foundation. [20] [ 51 ] Because of the increased height of the foundations and the need to install a new front porch, the Buyers were also required to move the house back a few feet in order to meet municipal requirements, adding an extra $6,000 plus taxes to the cost of repairs. [21] [ 52 ] Although, Hénault & Gosselin's work took three weeks, the Buyers spent three months outside the home, living with Hannan's parents, given that the repair work entailed not only lifting the house, excavating and pouring a new foundation, but also the removal and replacement of the old furnace, oil and water tanks, sum pump and all electrical, telephone wires and other plumbing components. [ 53 ] Following the lifting and relocation of the house, new cracks appeared around the house, on the parget and inside, around doors and wall/ceiling intersections and mouldings. The Buyers also noticed air infiltrations around window openings. [ 54 ] At the time of the hearing, these new cracks and air infiltration problems had not been repaired. [22] Hosseini's complementary report [ 55 ] Hosseini went back to visit the house a few months prior to the hearing.  He prepared a complementary report dated December 18, 2008. [23] [ 56 ] In this report, he examines the outcome of the repair work and comments on the findings and conclusions of the Beauchamp report. In addition, he reviews the bids prepared by the contractors for the repair work. Evidence at the hearing [ 57 ] While both Rozon and Hannan testified at the hearing, Muir and Allcut did not. [ 58 ] Unfortunately, by the time the case was heard, Muir had passed away and the Court must rely on Muir's Ad Futur Memoriam Examination conducted on May 8, 2006, which provides rather limited insight into the relevant facts of the case. [ 59 ] Allcut was not able to testify at the time of the trial either, due to her poor health. [24] The transcripts of her Examination after plea were produced into the Court record. [ 60 ] In his testimony, Muir admits to having had one prior water infiltration, which he corrected immediately, as per the following extract of his Examination: \\\"That was at the back door and I had an expert bring a back-hoe in, clean off all the soil and the stuff, go down, find the leak, the crack in the cement, fix it and put the stuff back, and that was…that must have been eight, ten years ago […] then years ago at that stage, now we're talking three years under that. That was the only infiltration.\\\" [25] [ 61 ] During her Examination after plea, Allcut confirmed a single occurrence of water infiltration, which would have been resolved 20 years prior to the sale. [26] She also admits that some cracks were repaired around the same time in the stucco walls prior to the installation of aluminium siding on the house. [27] ISSUES TO BE DECIDED 1. Are the damages to the back foundation wall latent defects? 2. In the affirmative, what damages should be awarded to the Buyers? 3. Does the Buyers' claim constitute an abuse of ester en justice justifying the reimbursement of extrajudicial fees? ANALYSIS 1. Are the damages to the back foundation wall latent defects? Position of the parties [ 62 ] According to the Buyers, the defect in the foundation wall constitutes a latent defect given that it was serious, hidden and unknown to the buyer at the time of purchase and that the Vendors failed to divulge the excavation and repair work carried out on this wall prior to the sale. [ 63 ] The Vendors acknowledge that the back wall constitutes a serious defect, which existed prior to the sale. They contend however that the Buyers and their expert ignored visible cracks in the front masonry wall and water infiltration signs, which were pointed out by the Inspector. [ 64 ] In essence, according to the Vendors, the Buyers knew of the defect and should have expected that repairs would have to be carried out on the concrete block foundations if they wanted to convert the basement into a waterproof area. [ 65 ] Pursuant to Article 1726 C.C.Q. [28] which deals with the vendor's obligation of warranty of quality, in order to constitute a latent defect giving rise to a claim in damages, the alleged defect must: i) be serious ii) be unknown to the buyer at the time of the purchase iii) pre-date the purchase iv) not have been apparent to a prudent and intelligent buyer at the time of purchase. [ 66 ] In ABB Inc. c. Domtar Inc . [29] , the Supreme Court repeated these four requirements, further insisting on the fact that the nature of the defect must be appreciated objectively. [ 67 ] The buyer is required to demonstrate that he conducted a serious and reasonable inspection, either on his own or with an expert. [30] [ 68 ] Where such an expert has been retained, the Court is justified in examining the his findings to determine whether the inspection was in fact serious and reasonable and whether visible signs of defects were brought to the buyer's attention. [31] [ 69 ] The Court enjoys some discretion in its appreciation of what constitutes a reasonable inspection, while also taking in consideration subjective criteria such as the technical abilities of the buyer, the nature and age of the property [32] , as well as the agreed price: a lower price might actually serve as an indication that the buyer should be more cautious or prudent in his inspection. [ 70 ] That being said, neither the buyer nor his expert are obliged to take unusual measures such as digging in the foundations or opening walls and floors to discover defects, unless there...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n... ) All work done to the foundation by Hénault & Gosselin (Exhibit P-11 ). $54,723.14 p) Landscaping work, by Greenteam (Exhibit P-22 ). $11,157.42 q) Landscaping work completed by \\\"Les Gazons Verkest\\\" (Exhibit P-33 ). $9,650.60 [ 123 ] The percentage used for the calculation also takes into account the hard costs related to the work performed and the fact that a straight linear calculation of 25% (for the repair of one of four walls) would not accurately reflect the total cost of repairs for a single wall. [ 124 ] The Court sets aside for the purpose of calculating a reasonable value of price reduction, the following items which are not directly related to the repair of the failed back wall, but are rather the consequence of Buyers' decision to remove and replace the entire foundation: Cost of building permit from the City of Montreal (Exhibit P-8 ) $159.00 Movers fees to move the belongings and piano from the basement to the first floor, invoice Transport (Exhibit P-9 ) $264.56 Notary fees of Mtre France Sauvé, required to get a loan from the Bank (Exhibit P‑10 ). $590.00 All electrical work, removing wiring and breaker panels in addition of the reinstallation having to be done at the completion of all the work, by RPE Electric Services (Exhibit P‑13 ) $1,058.23 All plumbing work by Plomberie Mario Bastien (Exhibit P-14 ). $1,248.71 Five and a half yards of stone dust for outdoor plumbing by Sources Nursery (Exhibit P-15 ). $183.18 Rental of a vibrating plate for outdoor plumbing stone packing at Location d'outils Simplex (Exhibit P-16 ). $45.91 Disconnecting and reconnecting phone lines by Bell (Exhibit P-17 ). $96.06 Replacement of furnace, lining, oil tank and reconnections by Huile Norco (Exhibit P‑18 ). $5,469.44 All other work related to plumbing, gypsum, installation by Denis Rozon (Exhibit P‑19 ). $2,141.89 Reconnection of wiring, by Hydro-Québec (Exhibit P-20 ). $189.79 Masonry work and parging required to the front of the house due to damage of the lifting of the home by CMS (Exhibit P-21 ). $10,237.23 All other plastering and painting relating to the shifting of the walls, by Rooney and Moffat Inc. (Exhibit P-23 ). $11,853.33 TOTAL: $33,537.33 [ 125 ] Indeed, the Court finds that all work related to the removal and replacement of the foundation, including all removal, disconnection, re-installation of electrical, telephone wires and plumbing components as well as all work required to repair cracks and parging further to the lifting of the house were not directly caused by the failed back wall but are rather the result of Buyers' decision to remove and replace the entire foundation. [ 126 ] Furthermore, the replacement of the foundation leading to the relocation of the house towards the back of their property and the replacement of the existing French drain, which was not proven to be defective, are not the direct result of the latent defect.  Nor is the cost incurred for the permit, which would not likely have been required for punctual repairs. [ 127 ] Furthermore, the notary fees pertaining to the additional financing required for the corresponding increase in the mortgage were not direct and foreseeable damages. [ 128 ] In terms of additional damages over and above the price reduction, the Court is of the opinion that the Buyers are entitled to claim same, based on Article 1728 C.C.Q.  and in light of the Court's earlier findings regarding Muir's failure to divulge the repair work conducted on the failed wall. [ 129 ] The Court finds that the Buyers did suffer some inconvenience as a result of the discovery of the latent defect and the repairs that had to be carried out, for which it grants a total amount of $7,000, being $3,500 to each Plaintiff. [ 130 ] The Court denies however the Buyers' claim for future repairs.  None had been incurred at the time of the hearing and there has been no demonstration on the balance of probabilities that any such damages are related to the failed back wall. [ 131 ] Moreover, given the Court's conclusion that the cracks found in the masonry veneer were not related to the failed back wall, the Court is unable to award any additional damages in relation to eventual repairs of the walls. [ 132 ] In any event, given that the cracks in the masonry were validly reported in the pre-purchase inspection report, the Court is of the opinion that they were visible at the time of purchase and do not constitute a latent defect giving rise to a claim. 3.         Does the Buyers' claim constitute an abuse of ester en justice justifying the reimbursement of extrajudicial fees? [ 133 ] In V iel c. Les Entreprises immobilières du terroir limitée [50] , the Court of Appeal set out the exceptional circumstances in which a party may be held to the reimbursement of extrajudicial fees incurred by the other party in the context of civil proceedings. There must be a real abuse of proceedings such that the lawsuit is the pursuit of a non-existent right or constitutes the multiplication of dilatory or useless proceedings. [ 134 ] Under the circumstances, in light of the Court's finding that there was a latent defect justifying a claim in damages, the cross-demand is unfounded and is dismissed accordingly. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 135 ] GRANTS the Plaintiffs' action in part; [ 136 ] CONDEMNS the Defendants to pay Plaintiffs an amount of $37,000 in damages together with interest and additional indemnity calculated since March 25, 2004; [ 137 ] DISMISSES Defendants' cross-demand; [ 138 ] WITH COSTS , including expert fees. __________________________________ GENEVIÈVE MARCOTTE, J.S.C. M e Ricardo Hrtschan Attorney of Plaintiffs/Cross-Defendants M e Luc Morin and M e Sébastien Lebel Fasken Martineau DuMoulin Attorneys of Defendants/Cross-Plaintiffs Dates of hearing: February 5, 6, 9, and 10 th 2009 [1] Exhibit D-10 . [2] As per Listing filed as Exhibit P-3 and Promise to purchase filed under Exhibit P-46 . [3] Exhibit P-1 . [4] See p. 6 of Report filed under Exhibit P-1 and photograph taken at time of visit, filed under Exhibit P‑24A . [5] See Report filed under Exhibit P-1 , p. 7, under the heading \\\"Structure\\\". [6] Exhibit P-2 . [7] See Exhibit P-46 at paragr. 6.1, which refers to the Listing (Exhibit P-3 ). [8] See photograph filed under Exhibit P-48 . [9] Exhibit P-50 . [10] As per list of business cards filed under Exhibit P-51 . [11] See Photo no. 12 filed under Exhibit P-4A . [12] Exhibit P-4A . [13] Exhibit D-4 . [14] Exhibit P-5 . [15] Exhibit D-5 . [16] Exhibit D-6 . [17] See curriculum vitae filed under Exhibit D-8 . [18] Report dated August 27, 2004, filed under Exhibit D-1 . [19] See photographs during construction, filed under Exhibit P-40 . [20] See Municipal regulations filed under exhibit P-45 , article 3.2.3. [21] See Contract filed under Exhibit P-6A. [22] As per photograph filed under Exhibit P-42 . [23] Report dated December 18, 2008, filed under Exhibit P-4B . [24] As per letter by Dr. Dignazio of Manoir Pierrefonds dated January 13, 2009, filed under Exhibit D-9 . [25] At pages 24 and 255 of the transcripts of the Examination of Robert Muir held on May 8, 2006, pursuant to Article 404 C.p.c. [26] Transcripts of Stella Margaret Allcott (sic) Examination of November 21, 2005, at pages 6-7. [27] Id , at page 10. [28] See Article 1726 C .C.Q. which reads as follows: \\\"1726.   The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance.\\\" [29] 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461. [30] Lavoie c. Comtois , 1999 CanLII 11787 (QC CS) , [2000] R.D.I. 36 (C.S.). [31] See St-Louis c. Morin , 2006 QCCA 1643 (C.A.). [32] See Dragon c. Crépeau-Lavallée , 2009 QCCS, 1132 (C.S). [33] See Supra , note 32. [34] See Jacpar inc. c. Bensakour et al., 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , EYB 1989-63151 (C.A.). [35] See photographs filed under Exhibit P-35 , photos 6, 7, 8, 9,11, as well as Exhibits P-40 and P-44 , photo 5. [36] See report filed under Exhibit P-1 at page 6. [37] Exhibit P-24B . [38] Based on the limitations set out in the Engineers Act Engineers Act , R.S.Q., c. I-9, article 2: \\\" 2. Works of the kinds hereinafter described constitute the field of practice of an engineer: (…) (e)    the foundations, framework and electrical and mechanical systems of buildings the cost of which exceeds $100,000 and of public buildings within the meaning of the Public Buildings Safety Act (chapter S-3 ).\\\" [39] Article 1604 C.C.Q. reads as follows: \\\"     1604.            Where the creditor does not avail himself of the right to force the specific performance of the contractual obligation of the debtor ins cases which admit of it, he is entitled either to the resolution of the contract, or to its resiliation in the case of a contract of successive performance. However and notwithstanding any stipulation to the contrary, he is not entitled to resolution or resiliation of the contract if the default of the debtor is of minor importance, unless, in the case of an obligation of successive performance, the default occurs repeatedly, but he is then entitled to a proportional reduction of his correlative obligation. All the relevant circumstances are taken into consideration in assessing the proportional reduction of the correlative obligation . If the obligation cannot be reduced, the creditor is entitled to damages only.\\\" (Our underlining) [40] Article 1728 C .C.Q. reads as follows: \\\"1728.   If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer.\\\" [41] Pierre-Gabriel JOBIN et Michelle CUMYN , La vente , 3 e éd. Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2007, at p.239-241; see also authors Jean-Louis BAUDOUIN and Patrice DESLAURIERS, La responsabilité civile , Volume I - Principes généraux, Cowansville, 7 e édi., Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2007, paragr. 438, 439, 1057, 1058. [42] (2008) 2008 QCCA 1593 (CanLII) , R.J.Q. 2025 (C.A.), at paragr. 61 and following. [43] 2009 QCCA 1285 , at paragr. 91 ; see also Chevalier c. Gariépy , 1987 CanLII 726 (QC CA) , [1987] R.L. 626 (C.A.) and Charrette c. Côté , J.E. 2006-2324 (C.A.) . [44] See Verville c. 9146-7308 Québec inc. , supra note 41, parag. 78; see also Lahaie c. Laperrière , supra note 42, paragr. 58 and following. [45] See Pierre-Gabriel JOBIN and Michelle CUMYN, supra note 40, at p. 24: \\\" Une même situation factuelle peut parfois donner ouverture soit à l'action en annulation ou en diminution de prix sur le fondement du dol, soit à l'action résolutoire ou en diminution de prix en raison d'un vice caché, ce qui n'est pas sans avoir causé une certaine confusion dans la jurisprudence (infra no 173). Il importe pourtant de bien distinguer ces deux recours, afin de déterminer si les conditions d'exercice de l'un ou de l'autre sont réunies. La violation, par le vendeur, de la garantie de qualité constitue une inexécution du contrat qui donne lieu à un recours de l'acheteur en résolution ou en diminution de prix, même si le vendeur ignorait l'existence du vice affectant la chose vendue; s'il connaissait le vice, le vendeur est de plus tenu au versement de dommages-intérêts (article 1728, infra no 164). C'est là une première différence très importante entre ce recours et le recours pour dol, puisque, dans ce dernier cas, il faut aussi démontrer que le vendeur avait l'intention de tromper l'acheteur. Une deuxième différence concerne le domaine du dol, qui est beaucoup plus étendu que celui des vices cachés : le dol peut porter sur autre chose que la qualité du bien vendu, comme on vient de le voir .\\\" [46] See 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461, at paragr.107 to 109 reproduced as follows: \\\"107.  The trial judge found that C.E. had breached its duty to inform, whereas in the Court of Appeal’s view, the issue related to the warranty against latent defects. The two concepts overlap, but it is important to distinguish them in order to identify the circumstances in which each rule will be applied. 108. Whereas the warranty against latent defects is expressly provided for in the C.C.L.C. and the C.C.Q., the duty to inform derives instead from the general principle of good faith ( Bank of Montreal v. Bail Ltée , 1992 CanLII 71 (SCC) , [1992] 2 S.C.R. 554, at p. 586; arts. 6, 7 and 1375 C.C.Q.) and the principle of free and informed consent. Furthermore, the scope of the general duty to inform is much broader than that of the disclosure of a latent defect. This duty encompasses any information that is of decisive importance for a party to a contract, as Gonthier J. stated in Bail (see pp. 586‑87). It is therefore easy to imagine a situation in which a seller would be in breach of the duty even though no latent defect exists. 109. Where a seller fails to discharge the duty to disclose a defect, on the other hand, it can probably be said at the same time that he or she has also breached the general duty to inform the buyer of a factor of decisive importance in respect of the good sold, namely the existence of a latent defect. The instant case is one example of this. If a party invokes the seller’s warranty against latent defects, the duty to inform is in a sense subsumed in the analysis of the seller’s liability for latent defects, and there is no need for the court to conduct a separate analysis on the seller’s duty to inform.  As a result, our analysis and conclusion regarding C.E.’s liability under the warranty against latent defects are sufficient to dispose of the case before the Court.\\\" [47] Exhibit P-6A and exhibit P-11 . [48] Exhibit P-45 , article 3.2.3. [49] See supra notes 40 to 43. [50] [2002] CanLII 41120 (Q.C. C.A.), 22 octobre 2002....\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n69 ] The Court enjoys some discretion in its appreciation of what constitutes a reasonable inspection, while also taking in consideration subjective criteria such as the technical abilities of the buyer, the nature and age of the property [32] , as well as the agreed price: a lower price might actually serve as an indication that the buyer should be more cautious or prudent in his inspection. [ 70 ] That being said, neither the buyer nor his expert are obliged to take unusual measures such as digging in the foundations or opening walls and floors to discover defects, unless there is a visible sign that should have raised suspicion. [ 71 ] The vendor is responsible for such defect which, had it been known by the purchaser, would have caused the buyer to refuse to go forward with the purchase or to pay a lower price for the property being purchased. [33] [ 72 ] Where the vendor fails to denounce or disclose the defect at the time of the sale, he has the burden of proving that the buyer was fully aware of the defect.  Otherwise, the vendor will have to demonstrate that the defect was apparent and that the buyer should have noticed the defect at the time of his visit or pre-purchase inspection. [ 73 ] False representations made by the vendor to reduce the concerns of the buyer regarding visible signs of defects may lead a Court to conclude that an otherwise apparent defect is in fact latent. [34] Analysis [ 74 ] Applying those principles to the case, the Court finds that the failed back foundation wall was affected by a latent defect for the following reasons. [ 75 ] Firstly, while there were some prior visible signs of water which should have alerted the Buyers to the risk of future water infiltration, the Court is unable to conclude that these signs should have led a prudent buyer to realize that the foundation wall would fail altogether and need to be repaired within the year that followed the purchase. [ 76 ] Secondly, the Buyers knew that they were purchasing a home built in 1952.  They could not legitimately expect its concrete block foundation to be completely waterproof. They should not, however, be held to expect the disintegration of the bottom portion of the back foundation wall, to the extent revealed by the photographs taken during the repairs. [35] [ 77 ] Thirdly, the price of the house alone could not serve as an indication that there was a serious problem affecting it, in the absence of evidence that the asking price was lower than the market. [ 78 ] Fourthly, by minimizing the extent of the work carried out to remedy prior problems to the back wall foundation and by failing to report on the nature and extent of work carried out to repair cracks in said walls or to replace the wooden studs supporting the walls in the basement, the Vendors falsely reassured the Buyers. Their silence denied the Buyers the opportunity to investigate further and ascertain the need for extensive repairs, or to allow them to reconsider their purchase. [ 79 ] Had the Vendors been more transparent about the extent of repairs carried out in relation to the back foundation wall, they would have transferred onto the Buyers the burden to conduct their own investigation, especially given the location of the repairs in relation to water infiltration signs. [ 80 ] Yet, the Court is of the opinion that the Buyers could not altogether ignore the signs of water infiltration as well as the risk of water in the basement despite the representations in the listing about the high and dry basement and Muir's statement of only a single occurrence of water in the basement. [ 81 ] For one, the Inspector admitted at the hearing that he normally avoids relying on the answers given by elderly people selling their home, given their limited capacity to recollect events or repairs. [ 82 ] Also, the Buyers knew that there was \\\" active leakage at rear basement stairwell \\\", \\\" evidence of extensive prior leakage \\\" and \\\" medium probability of infiltration \\\" as reported by the Inspector. [36] His photographs [37] showed signs of humidity and corrosion on the sump pump pipes. [ 83 ] Moreover, Rozon admitted that he saw rust at the base of the furnace during the pre-purchase inspection and that there was mold around the perimeters of the basement, which he did not consider significant enough to address in the year following the purchase. [ 84 ] Furthermore, the Buyers and their Inspector knew that the foundation walls were made of concrete blocks which, according to Hosseini and Beauchamp, were vulnerable to water infiltration and required more constant repair and maintenance than a poured concrete foundation. [ 85 ] As a result, the Buyers could not legitimately expect the concrete block foundation to be waterproof in a more than 50 year old house. [ 86 ] As for the cracks in the front masonry veneer, those noted by the Inspector in his report were not qualified as \\\"hairline\\\" cracks. While the Buyers and the Inspector made serious efforts to convince the Court that the cracks noted by Hosseini in March 2004 had only appeared in the year that followed the purchase, the Court affords little credibility to their versions, which contradict the inspection report and are self-serving. [ 87 ] In terms of credibility, the Court cannot ignore the Inspector's own admission at the hearing that upon being served with Buyers' letter of demand in March 2004, he was willing to do anything to defend himself for the purpose of avoiding liability regarding the content of his report. [ 88 ] Hosseini himself expressed some scepticism regarding his clients' claim that the cracks suddenly appeared in the veneer one year after the purchase. Beauchamp acknowledges that the cracks had in all likelihood been present in the veneer for a number of years. [ 89 ] While Hosseini was of the opinion that the cracks in the veneer should have led a prudent inspector to investigate further, Beauchamp, who testified under reserve of the objection raised by the Buyers' attorney regarding his ability to testify on structural integrity, a practice domain reserved to engineers [38] , denied that the cracks could be interpreted as signs of weakness of the structural integrity of the house. [ 90 ] Even setting aside Beauchamp's testimony on the absence of relationship between the cracks in the masonry and the failed back foundation wall, the Court is unable to find a relation between the cracks noticed in the front masonry veneer and the existence of a latent defect affecting the back foundation wall, given their difference in location. 2.         What is the extent of the damages that may be claimed by the Buyers in the context of this action? The position of the parties [ 91 ] The Buyers allege that if they had been made aware of the problems, they would not have paid such a high price for the property. [ 92 ] They are claiming the full amount of repairs incurred that is, the sum of $111,225.21, which includes the removal and replacement of a concrete block foundation with a poured concrete one, all ancillary work, the replacement of the existing French drain, the removal and replacement of the oil tank, furnace, electrical, plumbing and telephone components, landscaping, etc., the professional fees incurred by the Buyers to increase their mortgaged loan in order to pay the repairs. [ 93 ] Their claim is best summarized in the following table: a) Cost of Dr. Mohammad Hosseini's report (Exhibit P-7 ). $1,265.28 b) Cost of building permit from the City of Montreal (Exhibit P-8 ). $159.00 c) Movers fees to move the belongings and piano from the basement to the first floor, invoice Transport (Exhibit P-9 ). $264.56 d) Notary fees of Mtre France Sauvé, required to get a loan from the Bank (Exhibit P‑10 ). $590.00 e) All work done to the foundation by Hénault & Gosselin (Exhibit P-11 ). $54,723.14 f) All mason work having been done to the chimney, fireplace and sealing of all cracks, by Maçonnerie LPG Enr. (Exhibit P-12 ). $891.44 g) All electrical work, removing wiring and breaker panels in addition of the reinstallation having to be done at the completion of all the work, by RPE Electric Services (Exhibit P-13 ). $1,058.23 h) All plumbing work by Plomberie Mario Bastien (Exhibit P-14 ). $1,248.71 i) Five and a half yards of stone dust for outdoor plumbing by Sources Nursery (Exhibit P-15 ). $183.18 j) Rental of a vibrating plate for outdoor plumbing stone packing at Location d'outils Simplex (Exhibit P-16 ). $45.91 k) Disconnecting and reconnecting phone lines by Bell (Exhibit P-17 ). $96.06 l) Replacement of furnace, lining, oil tank and reconnections by Huile Norco (Exhibit P‑18 ). $5,469.44 m) All other work related to plumbing, gypsum, installation by Denis Rozon (Exhibit P‑19 ). $2,141.89 n) Reconnection of wiring, by Hydro-Québec (Exhibit P-20 ). $189.79 o) Masonry work and parging required to the front of the house due to damage of the lifting of the home by CMS (Exhibit P-21 ). $10,237.23 p) Landscaping work, by Greenteam (Exhibit P-22 ). $11,157.42 q) Landscaping work completed by \\\"Les Gazons Verkest\\\" (Exhibit P-33 ). $9,650.60 r) All other plastering and painting relating to the shifting of the walls, by Rooney and Moffat Inc. (Exhibit P-23 ). $11,853.33 TOTAL: $111,225.21 [ 94 ] The Buyers also claim $20,000 for inconveniences suffered by the entire family in relation to the latent defects and repairs, as well as an additional amount of $50,000 for anticipated damages: they suspect that they will discover further damages when they remove the masonry veneer, which they have been unable to investigate, given their limited financial means and the costs of the ongoing litigation. [ 95 ] As part of their $20,000 claim, Hannan claims to have suffered form a major depression from May 29 to September 3, 2006, following Muir's examination of May 8, 2006, in which he admitted that he had carried out excavation work near the failed back wall, while he refrained from disclosing it at the time of sale. This depression caused her to stop work for three months, with a $3,600 shortfall in revenues for the period of work. [ 96 ] The Vendors deny that any of these repairs were the result of a latent defect.  They contend that the work went beyond the required remedial work and brought an added value to the house, which they should not be held to pay for.  Moreover, they contest any prior knowledge of the defect or bad faith on their part which could lead to additional damages. The law [ 97 ] When faced with a latent defect, article 1604 C.C.Q. [39] provides the buyer with a choice: he may seek to annul the sale or demand a price reduction. [ 98 ] Where the buyer is able to demonstrate that the vendor knew of the existence of the latent defect, Article 1728 C.C.Q. [40] also entitles him to seek additional damages suffered over and above a reduction in price, on the basis that the defect was known to him or that he could not ignore it at the time of the sale. [ 99 ] By seeking a price reduction, the buyer chooses to keep the property but asks that the price be reduced to take into consideration the defect. [ 100 ] Québec authors Jobin and Cumyn agree the price reduction cannot be disproportionate to the value of the property or to the price paid for same. [41] [ 101 ] In Verville c. 9146-7308 Québec inc. [42] as well as more recently in Lahaie c. Laperrière [43] , the Court of Appeal reiterated that in claiming a price reduction the buyer cannot enrich himself to the detriment of the vendor. [ 102 ] In assessing the value of the reduction, the Court may rely on the costs of repairs but it must also take into account all appropriate circumstances to determine what price reduction would have been reasonably applied at the time of purchase. [ 103 ] The Court must consider not only the cost of the repairs incurred but the value of the property sold, as well as the components of the property (land and building), and the fact that the buyer chose to keep the property notwithstanding the defect rather than seek the annulment of the transaction altogether.  This serves as a sign that the property does retain some value for the buyer. [44] [ 104 ] Authors Jobin and Cumyn have distinguished the annulment of transaction and/or reduction in price, from the claim in damages, insisting in this case on the buyer's burden to demonstrate the vendor's knowledge of the defect. [45] [ 105 ] When deciding whether the vendor knew or could not have ignored the defect, the Court may consider whether the vendor had the duty to inform the buyer of certain circumstances which would have led the buyer to investigate further and whether the vendor did perform his duty. [ 106 ] Indeed, in ABB inc. c. Domtar inc. [46] , while the Supreme Court of Canada distinguished the warranty of quality from the duty to inform, it did acknowledge that the duty to inform was subsumed in the Court's analysis of the vendor's liability for latent detects. Analysis [ 107 ] Given the fact that the latent defect relates to the failed back wall rather than to the entire foundation, the Court is unable to accept the Buyers' entire claim as being a direct result of the latent defect. [ 108 ] The Buyers' expert Hosseini recognized that punctual repairs to the failed back wall could be done, he recommended that the entire foundation be replaced rather than the failed back wall alone because it had the benefit of ensuring a permanent waterproof foundation. [ 109 ] At Court, Hosseini also justified his recommendation for the full removal and replacement of the foundation by the fact that the price submitted for the work by Hénault & Gosselin was reasonably low. [47] [ 110 ] Along the same lines, Contractor Hénault & Gosselin's representative, Yvon Malenfant, acknowledged that punctual repairs could be done at a lower cost, although they would not have entitled the Buyers to the same warranty protection. [ 111 ] Yet, as a result of the decision to replace the entire foundation, a poured concrete foundation had to be installed to comply with municipal regulations. [48] [ 112 ] To enable the lifting of the house, the removal and replacement of the foundation, the Buyers were also forced to disconnect, remove, as well as reinstall all heating, electrical, telephone wires and plumbing components, including the aging furnace, oil tank and sump pump, which the Buyers were expecting to eventually replace. [ 113 ] Given the increased height of the standard poured concrete foundation, the Buyers were also required to move the house back a few feet on their lot, to allow for the construction of a higher front porch. [ 114 ] Overall, the decision to replace the entire foundation rather than just repair the failed back wall had an important impact on the price of the total work as well as on the inconveniences suffered by the Buyers given that it a caused the Buyers to move out of the house for the duration of the work.  While the replacement of the foundation itself only lasted three weeks, the Buyers moved out of their home for three months during which the new connections and installations were completed. [ 115 ] During this time, the Buyers stayed with Hannan's parents, thus suffering a variety of inconveniences mostly related to the distance between their house and Hannan's parents. [ 116 ] While the Buyers' decision to remove and replace the entire foundation may have been logical and warranted, knowing that the house was more than 50 years old and had a concrete block foundation that would remain vulnerable to humidity from this point on, the Court cannot hold the Vendors to pay for all of this. [ 117 ] Granting the Buyers' entire claim would lead to an unjust enrichment on their part which the Court is unable to sanction as per the doctrine and case law cited extensively above [49] , particularly given that the amount of their entire claim is nearly the price paid for the house, which they will continue to enjoy for the future. [ 118 ] Moreover, the Court is unable to accept the Buyers' argument that the work brought no added value to their house. The work provided them with a brand new waterproof foundation (which they could not expect at the time of purchase), a new insulated slab, and a higher basement. This gives them the ability to eventually install brick walls in replacement of the masonry walls. [ 119 ] What amount is the Court justified to award under the circumstances? [ 120 ] In terms of the claim for price reduction, the Vendors' assessment of the work required for the punctual repair of the failed back wall at $5,000 is insufficient. The Court is of the opinion that the Buyers are entitled to claim a larger sum, which the Court sets at $30,000. [ 121 ] In the Court's opinion, this sum represents a fair and reasonable reduction in the sale price in light of all appropriate circumstances, including the cost of repairs, value of the entire property, the total price paid at the time of purchase and the Buyers' choice of remedy. [ 122 ] This determination represents 40% of the following amounts incurred for the replacement of the foundation and the repair of the landscape work, totalling $75,531.16, being: e) All work done to the foundation by Hénault & Gosselin (Exhibit P-11 ). $54,723.14 p) Landscaping work, by Greenteam (Exhibit P-22 ). $11,157.42 q) Landscaping work completed by \\\"Les Gazons Verkest\\\" (Exhibit P-33 ). $9,650.60 [ 123 ] The percentage used for the calculation also takes into account the hard costs related to the work performed and the fact that a straight linear calculation of 25% (\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03125763311982155, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\", \"title\": \"Hannan c. Allcut, 2009 QCCS 3254 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.03125763311982155, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\"}, \"token_count\": 12281, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2009qccs3254-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"De Castris v. Banque Nationale du Canada, 1999 CanLII 10304 (QC CQ)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 1999 CanLII 10304 (QC CQ) | De Castris v. Banque Nationale du Canada | CanLII Date: 1999-11-22 File number: 500-32-042779-993 Other citation: EYB 1999-15929 Citation: De Castris v. Banque Nationale du Canada, 1999 CanLII 10304 (QC CQ) REJB 1999-15929 - Texte intégral CITATION: De Castris c. Banque Nationale du Canada Cour du Québec (Division des petites créances) CANADA PROVINCE DE QUÉBEC DISTRICT DE Montréal NO : 500-32-042779-993 DATE :  1999-11-22 EN PRÉSENCE DE : Raoul-P. Barbe , J.C.Q. Linda De Castris & Thomas Silvestre Plaintiffs v. Banque Nationale du Canada Defendant Barbe J.C.Q.:- 1 By a motion served on April 6, 1999, the plaintiffs claim $3,000 from the defendant as a reduction in the purchase price of an immovable. The amount corresponds to the cost of repairing a latent defect in the fireplace chimney. The petition was contested. The hearing took place on 18 October 1999. Proof of the Plaintiffs 2 Ms. Linda De Castris represented herself and Mr. Thomas Silvestre as the plaintiffs. She testified as to the events surrounding the case. 3 On 29 September 1997, the plaintiffs signed a promise to purchase a house owned by the defendant. The price was $90,000 and the plaintiffs' promise was conditional upon the completion of a satisfactory building inspection. A deed of sale was concluded between the parties on 3 December 1997 at the agreed price. 4 A real-estate broker evaluated the house for the defendant on 18 September 1997, before the promise to purchase was signed. The broker was Ms. DeCastris' father, who testified that he did the evaluation before he knew that his daughter was considering purchasing the house. The report states that the roof and the chimney are in bad condition and suggests a price of $104,000. The report doesnot clarify the fact that there are two chimneys: one for the furnace and one for the fireplace. The chimney referred to in the broker's report is the furnace chimney and there is no mention of a second chimney. The plaintiffs testified that they were fully aware that these repairs were needed when purchasing the house. 5 The building inspection was done (see R-4) and refers to the same needed repairs as the broker's evaluation. It indicates that there are two chimneys in the house, but does not say that the fireplace chimney needed repairs. However, the building inspector advised that the fireplace chimney be cleaned before using it. 6 The plaintiffs followed the advice of the building inspector and had the fireplace chimney cleaned on 15 October 1998 by a certified chimney services company. It was necessary to use a special cleaning process because the fireplace chimney was caked with “glazed 3 rd degree creosote” which cannot be removed by the usual method. After removing the creosote, it wasdiscovered that some of the tiles in the chimney were cracked and completely broken. The chimney is unsafe in this condition and the plaintiffs were told not to use the fireplace until the chimney was repaired. The Fire Prevention Service of the Town of Pierrefonds confirmed this assessment after carrying out its own inspection of the chimney. The chimney cleaning technicians told theplaintiffs that such damage could only have been caused by a previous chimney fire. 7 The plaintiffs were told that they had two options for repairing the chimney: either it could be torn down and rebuilt for a cost of $4,500, or the existing chimney could be lined with a stainless steel liner for a cost of $3,000. The plaintiffs informed the defendant of the situation by a letter sent in November 1998, and gave it the choice to cover the cost of either option. The defendantdid not make a payment and so the plaintiffs have brought the matter to court. 8 The plaintiffs testified that while they were fully aware of the problems with the roof and the furnace chimney at the time of the sale, they would not have agreed to the purchase price had they known of the condition of the fireplace chimney. Proof of the Defendant 9 Ms. Nicole Parent is a hypothec agent and represented the defendant at the hearing. The defendant was the owner of the house at the time of the sale, having acquired it following a default on a hypothec. She agreed with the plaintiffs version of the events but stressed that the plaintiffs were aware of the roof and furnace problems. It is the defendant's position that the terms of the deedof sale exclude its liability for latent defects. The defendant did not seem aware that the plaintiffs were referring to a second, separate chimney in the house. 10 The defendant relied on several clauses contained in the deed of sale: the “Object of Contract” clause, the “Warranty” clause and the “Obligations” clause. 11 The “Object of Contract” clause states that: “ The vendor sells without any other warranty than those hereinafter mentioned to the purchaser, hereto present and accepting, the immovable hereinafter described .” 12 The “Warranty” clause provides that: “ This sale is made without any other warranty than that of the vendor's personal fault or acte [ sic .] with regard to the title of the property and without any warranty as to apparent or latent defects, the Purchaser buying the property at his own risks and perils .” [Emphasis added]. 13 The “Obligation” clause reads as follows: “ The purchaser obliges himself to: 1 ) to take the property “ as is ”, with the purchaser declaring that he has visited and inspected the same to his satisfaction and has himself verified with the competent authorities to ensure that his intended use of the property complieswith the statutes, regulations, and by-laws in force ”, 2 ) to refrain from requiring the Vendor to make any repairs whatsoever with regard to any apparent or latent defects .” [Emphasis added]. Reasons for Judgment 14 The facts of this case are not in dispute. The main issue is to determine whether plaintiff is still owed a legal guarantee of quality against latent defects despite the terms of the deed of sale. Relevant Provisions of the Civil Code of Quebec 15 This case comes within the domain of a contract of sale, which is regulated by articles 1708 and following of the Civil Code of Quebec . The plaintiff bases its claim on article 1726 of the CCQ. 16 The law imposes two legal obligations on the seller through article 1716 of the Civil Code of Quebec : the obligation to deliver the thing sold and the obligation to guarantee it with respect to ownership and quality. 17 Article 1716 CCQ provides that: The seller is bound to deliver the property and to warrant the ownership and quality of the property. These warranties exist of right whether or not they are stipulated in the contract of sale. 18 This case is concerned with the seller's legal guarantee of quality, which includes a legal guarantee against latent defects present in the thing sold. This guarantee is set out in article 1726 CCQ, which states that: The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against anylatent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. 19 However, the seller and buyer are permitted to vary the legal guarantee of quality as they wish in a contract of sale. This is expressed in article 1732 of the CCQ which provides that: The parties may, in their contract, add to the obligations of legal warranty diminish its effects or exclude it altogether but in no case may the seller exempt himself from his personal fault. 20 Article 1733 CCQ creates a restriction on this ability to freely determine the extent of the obligation of quality. Article 1733 CCQ states that: A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property. An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys the property at his own risk from seller who is not a professional seller. 21 In order to settle the issue, it must be determined if the broken fireplace chimney is a latent defect in the sense of the law expressed in 1726 CCQ. Is the broken fireplace chimney a latent defect? 22 A defect must meet several criteria in order to be a latent defect. 1 To be a “latent defect” the problem must be subjectively and objectively unknown to the prudent and diligent buyer. 2 Such a buyer is one who has taken reasonable precautions before purchasing the property. While it is not necessary that the buyer have an expert examine the property to be purchased in order to be considered a reasonable buyer, 3 and a defect is necessarily considered latent if an expert could not discover it. 4 23 In this case, the plaintiffs had the house inspected before finalizing their promise to purchase. This meets the requirement that the buyer be reasonably prudent and diligent. The building inspector's report indicates that he looked at both the furnace and fireplace chimneys, but only made recommendations with respect to the furnace chimney. Moreover, the fact that the inspector did notnotice the true condition of the fireplace chimney means that it is incontestably a latent defect and indicates that it was objectively unknowable. 24 In addition, it is clear from both the plaintiffs and defendant's testimony that presence of the broken fireplace chimney was subjectively unknown. There is no indication that either party was aware of the broken chimney at the time of the sale or that either was ignoring it. As both parties were acting in good faith, there is no issue of fraud or deceit. 25 However, more is required for a defect to be latent. To be considered a latent defect the problem must also result in a serious reduction of the usefulness of the property and must have been present before the sale. These criteria are set out in the doctrine as: Le vice doit être grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l'acheteur et occulte. En revanche, tout fait provoquant un déficit d'usage constitue un vice au sens de la garantie. 5 26 In this case, these additional requirements are met. The plaintiff's testimony and reports by the chimney cleaners and fire department indicate that the fireplace is completely unusable in its present condition and that the damage could only have resulted from a “previous chimney fire”. Having established that the condition of the chimney does constitute a latent defect in thelegal sense, the effect of the clauses in the deed of sale can now be considered. Do the clauses in the deed of sale effectively exclude the legal guarantee against latent defects? 27 The material clauses of the deed of sale are those set out in the “proof of the defendant” section above. Clauses limiting or excluding the guarantee of quality are restrictively interpreted. 6 In order to have effect they must be drafted in clear and unambiguous language. It has been held that clauses specifying that the buyer accepts the property “as is” and after “having seen and examined the property” do not exclude the guarantee with respect to latent defects. 7 Rather, clear reference must be made to latent defects. 28 The deed excludes the defendant's obligation to guarantee against latent defects since the words “latent defects” are used in both the Warranty and Obligations clauses. I would note here the Obligations clause, point 2, refers to the request of “repairs” whereas in this case the plaintiff is requesting only reduction of the purchase price. In this case, the language ofthe Warranty clause still covers the situation and would operate to exclude the defendant's obligation to guarantee against latent defects. 29 However, the law prevents, by article 1733 CCQ, the limitation of the seller's liability if he was aware or could not have been unaware of defects. If the seller has awareness of the latent defect, such clauses will be of no force or effect. Although the evidence establishes that the defendant was not actually aware of the broken fireplace chimney, there are situations in which the law willpresume that the seller did have awareness of the defect. 30 This presumption operates with respect to “professional sellers” and is contained in article 1733 paragraph 1 CCQ (cited above) and 1728 CCQ which provides that: If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all the damages suffered by the buyer. 31 The term “professional seller” did not exist in the Civil Code of Lower Canada but was introduced into the CCQ. However, the idea of “professional sellers” is not new to the law and had been treated in several cases under the CCLC. 8 32 While the jurisprudence has not yet indicated precisely how this presumption of knowledge on the part of the professional sellers operates through articles 1733 and 1728 of the Civil Code of Quebec , the doctrine is clear that such a presumption exists, although some debate remains as to whether it is rebuttable. 9 33 The presumption is embodied in phrase “could not have been unaware” or “ne pouvait l'ignorer” in the French version of both articles 1728 and 1733 CCQ, which replaces the language used in articles 1527 and 1528 of the Civil Code of Lower Canada. The CCLC stated that the seller could be “legally presumed to know the defects” or “légalementprésumé connaître” in the French version. 34 The link between articles 1733 and 1728 can be made by noting that the use of the phrase “ not a professional seller” in the second paragraph of article 1733 implies that the first paragraph includes professional sellers. Since it is professional sellers who will be presumed to be unable to have been unaware of the defect, they cannot exclude their liability. 35 Therefore, lack of precision in the scope of this presumption arises mostly from the nature of concept of “professional seller” in the CCQ. According to Edwards, 10 the presumption is part of the Civil Code 's goal to protect economically weaker parties and derives from the writings of DuMoulin, Domat, and Pothier. 11 It is meant to express the idea that “ la profession publique du marchand a une certaine compétence dans les biens qu'il choisit de vendre .” 12 In addition, it has been expanded from including specialized sellers to also including general sellers of a diversity of products and remains for the courts to determine the precise scope of the presumption. 13 Can the defendant be considered a professional seller and so presumed to be aware of the defect? 36 The term “professional seller” is not defined in the CCQ, but the Commentaries of the Minister of Justice provide some guidance. They state that a professional seller is “ la personne qui a pour son occupation habituelle la vente des biens ”. 14 This definition has been used by the courts to determine whether a seller could be characterized as a professional seller. 15 37 Can it be said that the defendant comes within this definition? There are only a few cases dealing with this issue since the coming into the force of the CCQ in 1994. 38 In the case of Bergeron v. Société d'habitation et de développement de Montréal , 16 the Court of Appeal held that a developer and seller of immovables was a specialized seller to whom the presumption applied. The Société had purchased a college and converted it into apartments, which it then sold individually. In its decision the court provided several criteria for determining who is a specialized seller. The court did not address the issueof general sellers. 39 The case of Durand v. Jobidon 17 refered to jurisprudence existing before the CCQ in making its determination that the sellers were not professional sellers. The sellers were a divorcing couple who had extensively renovated a house built in approximately 1815. Judge Sheehan, referring to Ouellet v. Eymann , 18 stated that, La présomption de connaissance des vices de la chose repose essentiellement sur l'expertise que possède le vendeur parce qu'il est un “commerçant en semblables matières” dont les connaissances font présumer qu'il ne peut ignorer les vices cachés de la chose. Cette présomption ne s'applique pas au vendeur ordinaire de bonne foiqui ne possède pas de connaissance technique, ni d'expérience commerciale. 19 40 Judge Bossé treats the idea of professional sellers briefly in the case of Kack 20 . It involves the sale of a mobile home by a seller who worked as a truck driver in the transport industry and bought the mobile home solely to resell it. Commenting on the definition of professional seller provided by the Minister of Justice he notes that, Toute insuffisante cette définition puisse-t-elle être, en autant qu' elle ne fait pas la distinction entre le vendeur ordinaire et le vendeur spécialisé , elle contient, pour les fins de la présente affaire, les éléments essentiels. Dans le présent cas, le défendeur n'avait pas 'pour occupation habituelle la vente debiens'. La vente de roulottes fut très occasionnelle, son commerce étant le transport. [Emphasis added]. 21 41 Are there any principles which emerge from these cases? The decision of Bergeron does not assist much as...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... pas la distinction entre le vendeur ordinaire et le vendeur spécialisé , elle contient, pour les fins de la présente affaire, les éléments essentiels. Dans le présent cas, le défendeur n'avait pas 'pour occupation habituelle la vente debiens'. La vente de roulottes fut très occasionnelle, son commerce étant le transport. [Emphasis added]. 21 41 Are there any principles which emerge from these cases? The decision of Bergeron does not assist much as it is limited to specialized sellers. As discussed above the doctrine indicates that the presumption now extends to general sellers. 42 The case of Durand refers to sellers “ en semblables matières ” which translates as “dealing in similar goods”. This language was used in decisions made under the CCLC. The phrase is not specifically defined in the case law. It is not necessary to determine its scope in this case for the reason that the presumption is now considered to includegeneral sellers of a variety of products. In addition, the judge refers to other elements in reaching his conclusion: the ordinary seller who has neither technical knowledge or commercial experience. 43 Finally, the case of Kack makes the important observation that the Minister of Justice's definition does not distinguish between ordinary sellers and specialized sellers. This suggests that it is the Legislator's intention that there no longer be a difference in legal effect between general and specialized sellers with respect to latent defects and is in keeping with the doctrineciting an expansion of the presumption. 44 In Kack , Judge Bossé goes on to base his decision on the frequency of the seller's selling activity and the fact that his principle occupation was in a different field. He held that the seller in the case only sold mobile homes very occasionally and that his usual occupation was transport, not selling. 45 In the present case, the employee representing the defendant identified herself as a “hypothec agent”. This suggests that the defendant deals frequently with immovables if it has a specialized department and employees devoted to hypothecs. It can be assumed that this is not the first house that the defendant has sold. In addition, banks are engaged in the business of selling awide variety of products to the public so that it cannot be said that the defendant has no commercial experience or that selling is not one of its main activities. 46 Given the pervasiveness of banks into the day-to-day affairs of the public and the fact that one of the general objectives of the Civil Code of Quebec is to protect the economically weaker party, 22 it seems reasonable to classify them as professional sellers. 23 47 As such, the defendant is presumed to have been aware of the latent defect. The obligation to guarantee quality cannot be excluded in this case according to 1733 CCQ, despite the clear language of the deed of sale. Conclusion 48 Since the presumption has the effect of putting article 1726 CCQ back into operation, the existence of the broken fireplace chimney means that the defendant has failed in its obligation. The plaintiffs are entitled to a remedy and have requested, in effect, a reduction in the purchase price. The CCQ does not provide a specific remedy for latent defects and so we must resort to the generalrules of obligations. 49 The plaintiffs' claim falls within the remedy known as quantum minoris that is expressed in the second paragraph of article 1604 CCQ, 24 which states: However and notwithstanding any stipulation to the contrary, he is not entitled to resolution or resiliation of the contract if the default of his debtor is of minor importance, unless, in the case of an obligation of successive performance, the default occurs repeatedly, but he is then entitled to a proportional reduction of his correlative obligation . [Emphasisadded]. 50 The defendant had the obligation to guarantee against latent defects and, in return, the plaintiffs had the obligation to pay the purchase price of $90,000. Because the defendant has defaulted in its obligation, the plaintiffs are entitled to reduce their obligation and this is done by reducing the purchase price. 25 51 However, the reduction in the purchase price does not necessarily correspond to the cost of repair work required to fix the latent defect. In Bergeron , 26 Judge Forget recognizes this when stating that, “ La diminution du prix de vente, on le sait, correspond assez souvent -- bien que pas nécessairement -- au coût des réparations ....” 52 Judge Sheehan in Durand 27 discusses why this is so and some of the other factors which should be considered when determining how much to reduce the price of a sale when there are hidden defects: Il suffit de réaliser qu'une réparation peut non seulement éliminer un vice de la chose vendue, mais également lui donner une plus-value. Par ailleurs, un prix de vente résulte normalement de concessions de part et d'autre ... la Cour d'appel s'est prononcée sur la manière d'évaluer le montant que constitue la diminution du prix devente en raison d'un vice caché. Il y a plusieurs façons de s'y prendre et la méthode utilisée dépend des circonstances de chaque cas. Des critères tels que l'âge de la bâtisse, le prix de vente, l'emplacement de l'immeuble, la nature des vices cachés et, surtout, le résultat des travaux de réparation peuventêtre considerés pour pondérer le coût des réparations qui est réclamé. ....La Cour d'appel souligne que si les termes du Code civil sont assez larges pour viser le calcul du coût réel pour remédier au vice, cela ne veut pas dire que, en tout état de cause, ce sont les coûts des réparations quiconstituent la juste diminution du prix de vente . [Emphasis added]. 53 In this case, the plaintiffs purchased the house for $90,000, a price which is $14,000 less than the amount of $104,000 suggested by the real-estate broker. The house is a large house with eight rooms, and is situated on a relatively large piece of land, 8,505 square feet. In addition, the report indicates that it is in an excellent location as it is near schools, a shopping centre, acultural center, and a library. Given all these circumstances, the court considers $1,500 to be a fair reduction in the price. For these Reasons, The Court: 54 CONDEMNS the defendant to pay plaintiff $1,500 with interest from the date of service of the action and the additional indemnity and judicial costs of $69. Barbe J.C.Q. 1. see Placement Jacpar c. Benzakour [1989] R.J.Q. 2317 . 2. Droit de la vente , Denys-Claude Lamontagne, Les éditions Yvon Blais Inc, Cowansville Qc. (1995) at p. 100 paragraphs 229 and 230 [hereinafter Lamontagne]. 3. Placement Jacpar Inc. c. Benzakour 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , [1989] R.J.Q. 2309 C.A. . 4. see Laurier Vachon Ltée c. Girard [1968] B.R. 497 , cited in Lamontagne, supra note 2. 5. La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit Québécois , Jeffery Edwards, Wilson & Lafleur ltée, Montréal Qc. (1998) at 135 paragraphe 304 [hereinafter Edwards]. 6. Edwards, supra note 5 at 123 paragraph 283. 7. Edwards, supra note 5 at 124 paragraph 284; see also Maltais c. Tremblay [1990] R.D.I. 143 C.Q. . 8. see General Motors Products of Canada v. Leo Kravitz 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790 , Caisse Populaire Desjardins de Saint-Nicholas c. Rouette (C.A.Q) , [1988], A.Q. No. 1842 , Ouellet c. Eymann (C.A.) 1988 CanLII 1013 (QC CA) , [1988] R.J.Q. 2448 . 9. LaMontagne, supra note 2, refers to “présomptions de connaissance” at 102 and in paragraph 232 states that “Les présomptions de connaissance s'appliquent au vendeur (ou à l'acheteur) professionnel” and refers to articles 1728 , 1729, and 1733 CCQ; see also the discussion in Edwards, supra note 5, at 62 paragraph 140, at 285-289 paragraphs 612-619 andat 289-300 paragraphs 620-636; see also Kack c. Sirois [1999] J.Q. no 2349 [hereinafter Kack ] . 10. Edwards, supra note 5 at 62 paragraph 142. 11. see discussion in Edwards, supra note 5, at 279 -281 paragraphs 598-604. 12. Ibid. at 287 paragraph 615. 13. Ibid. paragraphs 616-618. 14. Québec, Minister of Justice, Commentaries of the Minister of Justice: the Civil Code of Quebec , tome 1, Quebec, Publications du Quebec, 1993 at p. 1079 with respect to article 1729 CCQ. 15. See Kack , supra note 9. 16. 1996 CanLII 5767 (QC CA) , [1996] R.J.Q. 2088 [hereinafter Bergeron ]. 17. [1997] A.Q. no. 3680 [hereinafter Durand ] . 18. (C.A.) 1988 CanLII 1013 (QC CA) , [1988] R.J.Q. 2448 . 19. Supra note 17 at paragraph 37. 20. Supra note 9. 21. Ibid. at paragraph 11. 22. See Edwards, supra note 10. 23. The case of Caisse populaire Des jardins de Saint Nicolas c. Rouette (C.A.Q) [1988] A.Q. No. 1842 held that Caisse populaire was not a professional seller. It can be distinguished from the present case in that it was under the old Code which did not express the same general objective of the CCQ to protect economically weaker parties and was decided before the expansion of thepresumption. In addition, Caisse populaires are different entities from banks. 24. Lamontagne, supra note 2, at 107 paragraph 241. 25. Ibid. 26. Supra note 16 at paragraph 31. 27. Supra note 17 at paragraphs 29-30....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03201844170689583, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\", \"title\": \"De Castris v. Banque Nationale du Canada, 1999 CanLII 10304 (QC CQ)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.03201844170689583, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\"}, \"token_count\": 6675, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/1999canlii10304-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette, 2009 QCCQ 472 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2009 QCCQ 472 (CanLII) | Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette | CanLII Date: 2009-02-03 File number: 550-32-015780-072 Citation: Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette, 2009 QCCQ 472 (CanLII) Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette 2009 QCCQ 472 COURT OF QUEBEC «Small Claims Division» CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF HULL TOWN OF GATINEAU Civil Division No: 550-32-015780-072 DATE: February 3 rd , 2009 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE ROSEMARIE MILLAR, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ ANTHONY GILCHRIST […] Kanata (Ontario) […] Plaintiff v. DENISE ALBERT BRUNETTE […] Gatineau (Québec)  […] Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] The plaintiff, Anthony Gilchrist, claims from the defendant, Denise Albert Brunette, the amount of 7 000,00 $ for latent defect (« vice caché ») discovered after buying a property from the defendant. [ 2 ] On July 28 th , 2006, the plaintiff purchased the defendant's residence located at […], in Gatineau. [ 3 ] The house, built approximately 50 years ago, was purchased for the amount of 166 000,00 $. [ 4 ] The plaintiff did visit the house a few times (at least 9 times according to the defendant which is denied by the plaintiff), before the purchase and, on one occasion, with a friend who had some experience in construction. [ 5 ] According to the defendant, the plaintiff was told that, in the year 2000, repairs had to be done on the foundation after a water infiltration (see invoice D-3). [ 6 ] According to the plaintiff, this information was given by the defendant only after the purchase when the plaintiff discovered water infiltration in the basement. [ 7 ] A few days after the purchase, the plaintiff took possession of the house and began some painting. [ 8 ] Shortly after, after an heavy rain, the plaintiff discovered water on the basement floor. [ 9 ] The plaintiff removed the « subfloor » in the basement and the panelling on the wall and discovered water leaking in 3 different places. [ 10 ] The plaintiff did not expected to find water infiltration in the house. [ 11 ] After talking to his notary, the plaintiff called the defendant and asked her to come in order to find a solution. [ 12 ] After series of communications and estimates, the matter could not be settle between the parties. [ 13 ] The plaintiff had to hire a contractor to repair the leaks and the cost was            11 964,75 $. [ 14 ] The plaintiff incured expenses for a total amount of 28 000,00 $ to repair the basement. [ 15 ] The plaintiff claims that the latent defect is covered by the legal warranty when he purchased the house. [ 16 ] According to the plaintiff, he did not know or see, before purchasing the house, that there was water infiltration. [ 17 ] To the knowledge of the defendant, except for the one in 2000, there was no other water infiltration in the basement. [ 18 ] The defendant, a widow, sold her house with the help of her son without a real estate agent. [ 19 ] According to the defendant's son the water infiltration which occured in 2000 and the repair done at the time were mentioned to the plaintiff as they were for all the visitors who came to see the house. [ 20 ] Mr. Daniel Levert, technologist in civil engineering for 30 years and home inspector for 15 years, visited the house on August 21 st , 2006, and prepared a report in which he concluded that there were obvious signs of wear and tear and deficiencies regarding drains and « hydrofuge barrier ». [ 21 ] According to Mr. Levert, the purchase's price of the house was below the market's value and the obvious signs of water infiltration's problem should prompt a diligent purchaser to obtain an expertise before buying such 50 years old house. ANALYSIS [ 22 ] Sections 1726 and 1728 of the Civil Code of Quebec state: \\\"Art. 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use of which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect;  an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. Art. 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer.\\\" [ 23 ] To win a claim for latent defect, the buyer needs to prove that the defect was harmful, serious, not apparent, that the defect existed before the sale and that it was unknown to the buyer. [ 24 ] The evidence presented by the defendant is to the effect that the plaintiff was informed that a water infiltration occured in 2000.  This evidence is denied by the plaintiff. [ 25 ] Nevertheless, even without this contradictory element, evidence presented before the court is to the effect that: 1. The house was 50 years old; 2. The price of the purchase was below the value of the market; 3. No inspection was done by an expert before the sale; 4. There were signs of apparent deficiencies related to water infiltration as it shows from the home inspector's report: « Lors de notre visite nous avons fait certaines observations au périmètre entier du sous-sol et dans la salle de lavage qui avait les murs non fini [sic] au moment de l'achat. Dans la salle de lavage il y avait des cernes d'eau au coin et de la peinture soulevé [sic] par des cristaux d'efflorescence venant du mur de la fondation.  De l'efflorescence était aussi visible au niveau du plancher de béton. L'efflorescence (dépôt de sels sur la surface) est un phénomène provoqué par un béton chargé d'eau, rejettant [sic] l'humidité dans l'air ambiant du sous-sol.  Cela peut contribuer à des excès d'humidité dommageables.  L'efflorescence témoigne souvent d'un manque d'étanchéité de l'hydrofuge appliqué du côté extérieur des fondations ou d'une déficience du drainage des fondations (ex.:  position incorrecte du drain perforé, obstruction partielle ou totale du conduit, etc.), ce qui occasionne une absorption anormale de l'eau souterraine par le béton.  L'imperméabilisant à fondation est un composé organique et il est normal qu'il se désagrège avec le temps. Compte tenu de l'âge de la construction, il est fort probable que la barrière hydrofuge de la fondation ne soit plus efficace.  La fondation est donc soumise à l'humidité du sol. Il y avait autre [sic] signes apparent [sic] d'une [sic] manque d'étanchéité de la fondation. Les cernes d'eau visibles dans le coin de la salle de lavage.  Une fissure apparente à la fondation sous le palier de l'entrée avant, endroit ou le propriétaire avait observé une infiltration de l'intérieur. Des cernes d'eau à la base du revêtement intérieur en panneaux préfinis ainsi que l'oxydation des clous et les moisissures au quart de rond sont aussi des indices apparents d'un problème d'imperméabilisation de la fondation. L'inspecteur ne peut se prononcer avec exactitude sur cette condition, car seul un sondage du côté extérieur (excavation partielle) pourrait nous informer sur la qualité du drainage des fondations. En conclusion il était sans doute évident qu'il y avait des signes visibles d'usure et de lacune au niveau de la barrière hydrofuge et des drains.  Ceci aurait du [sic], lors d'une inspection diligente des acheteurs, leurs [sic] incité [sic] d'avoir une expertise plus approfondie sur ce point avant de finaliser la transaction. » 5. These signs were visible without removing the walls or the « subfloor »; [ 26 ] The evidence does not show the existence of latent defect according to the Civil Code of Quebec . FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the action, without costs. __________________________________ ROSEMARIE MILLAR, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: January 6 th , 2009...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.031159421429038048, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\", \"title\": \"Gilchrist c. Albert-Brunette, 2009 QCCQ 472 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.031159421429038048, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\"}, \"token_count\": 2035, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq472-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Kalimidis c. Tao, 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII) | Kalimidis c. Tao | CanLII Kalimidis c. Tao, 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII) Date: 2018-06-07 File number: 500-32-153201-167 Citation: Kalimidis c. Tao, 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/hsqth > Kalimidis c. Tao 2018 QCCQ 3811 COURT OF QUÉBEC (Small Claims Division) CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-153201-167 DATE: June 7, 2018 ______________________________________________________________________ BEFORE THE HONOURABLE DOMINIQUE GIBBENS, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ ATHANASIOS KALIMIDIS -and- helen kalimidis-kluger Plaintiffs v. tao tao -and- xinzhi he Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiffs claim $15,000 from Defendants, from whom they purchased a duplex in 2011. They claim that Defendants falsely represented that water infiltrations in the basement had been resolved by the installation of a French drain. The system installed, however, was not a French drain, and it did not resolve the infiltration issue. [ 2 ] The amount claimed represents the cost incurred by Plaintiffs to install a French drain ($8,761.23) and eliminate mold ($4,254.08), as well as damages ($1,984.69). [ 3 ] Defendants deny any liability towards Plaintiffs. They admit their mistake in describing the work completed in 2008 to remedy the water infiltration issue, but claim that the documentation provided to Plaintiff prior to the purchase of the property accurately described the work. They also argue that the claim must be dismissed because Plaintiffs did not notify them of the alleged defect or put them in default prior to completing corrective work on the property. FACTUAL CONTEXT [ 4 ] On February 26, 2011, Plaintiffs purchased from Defendants a duplex located on West Hill Avenue in Montréal (the “ Property ”) for a price of $408,000. [1] The Property, a row house with adjoining properties on both sides, includes a basement rental unit which had been occupied by the same tenant since 2004. [ 5 ] Prior to the sale, Defendants completed the standard form entitled Declarations by the seller of the immovable , [2] in which they stated that there had been water infiltrations in the past in the basement or elsewhere, but that these “[…] ha[d] been fixed with French drain ”. [3] Defendants also mentioned the installation of the French drain in 2008 as a major improvement to the Property. [4] [ 6 ] These statements were inaccurate, in that Defendants did not install a French drain in 2008. They retained 2896788 Canada Inc. (doing business under the name of BWN (“ BWN ”)) to “ waterproof subsoil masonry walls of the property ” by installing a drainage system on the inside of the Property, comprised of perforated pipes installed in the concrete floor of the basement (along the back and North walls) and connected to a sump pump. [5] This is different from a French drain, which is located on the outside perimeter of a property. [6] [ 7 ] The evidence shows that Defendants did not intentionally mislead Plaintiffs, as they believed that the system installed was a “French drain”. They provided the BWN contract, which was communicated to Plaintiffs’ real estate broker before Plaintiffs decided to purchase the Property. [7] [ 8 ] In 2012, when the basement-unit tenant moved out, Plaintiffs discovered what they believed to be mold on the walls in the washroom and kitchen of that unit. They believed at the time that this was the result of past water infiltrations, before the installation of a French drain. Humidity and what appeared to be mold were again noted the following year, when the subsequent tenant moved out. [ 9 ] In the fall of 2014, Plaintiffs retained a foundation expert, Les Goudrons du Québec (“ Goudrons ”), to determine the cause of the apparent mold and humidity. They were advised that contrary to what Defendants had stated, no French drain had been installed and water infiltrations were ongoing at the back of the Property. [ 10 ] In October 2014, Plaintiffs excavated the foundation at the back of the Property, installed a waterproofing membrane on the exterior of the foundation wall, and installed a French drain, at a total cost of $8,761.23. [8] [ 11 ] Afterwards, in November 2014, Plaintiffs notified Defendants of the absence of a French drain and ongoing water infiltration, summoning them to assess the damages themselves without mentioning that corrective work had already been completed. [9] [ 12 ] Shortly after receiving this letter of demand, Defendants went over to the Property and saw that corrective work had been carried out. After that, several exchanges took place between the parties. [10] Defendants provided Plaintiffs’ lawyer with a copy of the BWN contract [11] and BWN was approached on the basis of the conventional guarantee granted to Defendants. BWN denied liability, however, because it was not advised in a timely manner of the change of ownership as required by the contract. [ 13 ] Defendants were given the opportunity to assess the damage on the inside of the Property and they were provided with an estimate of the interior work required to fix the water infiltration damage. [12] The interior work was completed in March 2015 at a total cost of $4,254.08. [13] ANALYSIS a) Do Plaintiffs have a recourse based on the legal warranty of quality (warranty against latent defects)? [ 14 ] Plaintiffs’ claim calls into question, firstly, the legal warranty of quality to which sellers are bound pursuant to Articles 1726 and following of the Civil Code of Québec (the “ C.C.Q. ”). [ 15 ] Under these provisions, the seller must guarantee to the buyer that the property sold is free of latent defects that render it unfit for its intended purpose or significantly diminish its usefulness. The warranty does not extend to latent defects known to the buyer or to apparent defects (i.e., defects that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the assistance of an expert). [14] [ 16 ] A vendor is held to this warranty whether or not he is aware of the defect. If he is, his liability is greater: in addition to the cost of the work needed to correct the defect, the vendor is liable for the damages suffered by the purchaser. [15] [ 17 ] To succeed on a claim based on latent defects, a purchaser must prove, on a balance of probabilities, [16] that the property was, at the time of the sale, affected by a defect that was serious , hidden and unknown to the purchaser . [ 18 ] The purchaser must also demonstrate that he gave notice of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time of discovering it [17] and that the seller was given the opportunity to remedy the defect. [18] [ 19 ] In addition, the purchaser must place the seller in default of executing his obligation of warranty. [19] A letter of demand must be given before corrective work is carried out, unless it is possible to demonstrate that the seller was in default by the effect of the law. [20] [ 20 ] The Court concludes that these conditions are not met in the instant case, for the following reasons. [ 21 ] The absence of a French drain does not, in and of itself, constitute a latent defect that can give rise to liability under the legal warranty of quality. Many properties do not have French drains and have no water infiltration issues. [ 22 ] The evidence shows, however, that when Plaintiffs purchased the Property in 2011, water infiltration was ongoing through the back foundation wall. This constitutes a serious defect, which was hidden and unknown to Plaintiffs. Although the pre-purchase inspection revealed high levels of humidity in the basement, this problem was associated to the absence of a fan; [21] there were no visible signs of ongoing water infiltrations at the time. Moreover, Defendants’ representation that past infiltrations had been resolved by the installation of a French drain understandably reassured Plaintiffs that all was resolved. [ 23 ] Plaintiffs did not, however, give notice to the Defendants of the defect within a reasonable time of discovering it and, moreover, they proceeded to corrective work without giving them the opportunity of remedying the situation. This is fatal to their claim from the point of view of the warranty against hidden defects. [ 24 ] Notification of a defect within a reasonable time is intended to allow the seller to verify the existence and seriousness of the alleged defect and to correct it and it is a substantive condition of the implementation of the warranty. [22] Failure to give notice within a reasonable time – and before proceeding to corrective work – is fatal to a claim based on the warranty of quality, except where there is urgency or where the failure to give notice does not prejudice the seller’s rights. [23] [ 25 ] Plaintiffs realized or should have realized long before 2014 that there was a serious infiltration problem in the basement. Mr. Kalimidis discovered what he believed to be mold in the basement unit in 2012 and again in 2013. From then on, he could certainly suspect that there was a serious problem and should have given notice to Defendants without delay, as required by article 1739 C.C.Q.: 1739. A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective shall give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. Where the defect appears gradually, the time begins to run on the day that the buyer could suspect the seriousness and extent of the defect . The seller may not invoke the tardiness of a notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it. [Emphasis added] [ 26 ] Yet, Plaintiffs waited until November 2014 before giving notice to the Defendants and putting them in demand and by then, they had already installed a French drain. On the facts, there was no urgency to act and Defendants were not in default by the effect of the law. Defendants were therefore deprived of the opportunity of remedying the situation. [ 27 ] In view of the foregoing, the Court concludes that Plaintiffs have no recourse against Defendants based on the legal warranty of quality (warranty against latent defects). b) Do Plaintiffs have a recourse based on a breach of Defendants’ obligation of delivery given the inaccurate representation that a French drain had been installed? [ 28 ] Defendants’ potential liability must also be considered in light of their obligation of delivery as sellers as provided in Articles 1616 and 1717 C.C.Q. [ 29 ] Under these provisions, a seller must ensure that the property he or she delivers strictly complies with what was described or represented and what the purchaser agreed to. [24] If it does not, the seller is liable, irrespective of whether he or she was aware of the non-conformity. The purchaser can then obtain a reduction of the price and damages. [25] [ 30 ] It the instant case, Plaintiffs have established that the Property is not as it was described and represented by Defendants in that a French drain was not installed in 2008. This is a violation of Defendants’ obligation of delivery that can give rise to liability and provide a recourse against them. [ 31 ] To succeed on such a recourse, however, a purchaser must place the seller in default and give him the opportunity to remedy such default prior to proceeding to any corrective work. [ 32 ] Here, Plaintiffs did not place Defendants in default before installing the French drain. Their recourse must therefore fail. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the action; WITH LEGAL COSTS of $200 representing the judicial stamp on the Contestation. __________________________________ DOMINIQUE GIBBENS, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: February 26, 2018 [1] Exhibit D-2. [2] Exhibit D-1. [3] Exhibit D-1, clause D3.1. [4] Exhibit D-1, clause D15. [5] Contract between Defendants and BWN (Exhibit D-3). [6] Letter of Alain Ricard of Les Goudrons du Québec dated December 16, 2014 (Exhibit P-7). [7] Affidavit in lieu of testimony of Mrs. Marie-Claire LePessec dated November 17, 2011 and Exhibit D-3A. [8] Les Goudrons du Québec invoice (Exhibit P-3). [9] Letter of demand dated November 13, 2014 (Exhibit P-1). [10] Exhibit P-2. [11] Email of November 26, 2014 (Exhibit P-2, p. 2). [12] Email of December 31, 2014 (Exhibit P-2, p. 3). [13] Invoice of Dominovo inc. (Exhibit P-4). [14] Article 1726 C.C.Q. [15] Article 1728 C.C.Q. [16] As required by articles 2803-2804 C.C.Q. [17] Article 1739 C.C.Q. [18] Article 1590 C.C.Q. [19] Articles 1590 and 1594 C.C.Q. See also Claude Joyal inc. v. CNH Canada Ltd. , 2014 QCCA 588 , para. 40 to 45 . [20] Jeffrey EDWARDS , La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit Québécois , 2e Éd., Montréal, Wilson & Lafleur, 2008, para. 420, p. 203 to 205. [21] Exhibit P-8, p. 56. [22] Claude Joyal inc. v. CNH Canada Ltd. , 2014 QCCA 588 , para. 22 to 30 . [23] Id. , para. 31 to 36. See also Immeubles Bernadet inc. v. Kerzérho , 2015 QCCA 644 , para. 13 . [24] Pierre-Gabriel JOBIN et Michelle CUMYN, La vente , 4 th ed., Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2017, n o 107, at pages 139-140. See also Labrie v. Vanasse , 2005 QCCA 52 , para. 7-9 . [25] Munger v. Beaudoin , 2008 QCCQ 9315 , para. 64-65 ; Ouellette v. Giroux , 2009 QCCQ 3951 , para. 29 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.029323630034923553, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\", \"title\": \"Kalimidis c. Tao, 2018 QCCQ 3811 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.029323630034923553, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\"}, \"token_count\": 3353, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq3811-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Waters c. Passmore, 2019 QCCS 3805 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2019 QCCS 3805 (CanLII) | Waters c. Passmore | CanLII Date: 2019-09-06 File number: 550-17-008706-150 Citation: Waters c. Passmore, 2019 QCCS 3805 (CanLII) Waters c. Passmore 2019 QCCS 3805 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF GATINEAU No.: 550-17-008706-150 DATE: September 6, 2019 ______________________________________________________________________ PRESIDING: THE HONOURABLE DOMINIQUE GOULET, J.S.C. ______________________________________________________________________ KAREN WATERS JONATHAN GOODMAN Plaintiffs v. VANESSA PASSMORE ROBERT WAUGH HENDRIK HYMANS (A TO Z HOME INSPECTIONS) ANN-MARIE BABIN THOMAS E. KEELY Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ Procedural Context [ 1 ] On November 24, 2015, the plaintiffs filed an application seeking $158,600.64 [1] from the defendants Passmore and Waugh arising from an action for the reduction of the purchase price of a residence, damages and latent defects. [ 2 ] The action also concerns pre-purchase inspector Hendrik Hymans. [ 3 ] On or about March 9, 2016, the defendants Passmore and Waugh made an assignment in bankruptcy. [ 4 ] Following the bankruptcy, on September 16, 2016, the plaintiffs amended their applications to add two new defendants, Ann-Marie Babin and Thomas E. Keely, who sold the property at issue to the defendants Passmore and Waugh in 2008. [2] [ 5 ] The application was also amended at that time to increase the amount claimed to $182,227.80. [3] [ 6 ] An application for authorization to continue the proceedings despite the bankruptcy was filed on September 21, 2016. That application was granted on November 23, 2016. [ 7 ] Pursuant to that application, the plaintiffs sought authorization to continue the proceedings in order to establish that, at the time of the sale, the bankrupt individuals had knowledge of the defects and that they made fraudulent misrepresentations so the debt would be declared non-dischargeable within the meaning of section 178 of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act . [4] [ 8 ] On or about April 17, 2017, the action was again amended to ask the Court to declare that the debt was not extinguished under section 178 of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act and to vary the quantum to $155,981.58. [5] [ 9 ] In or about April 2018, a partial settlement of the dispute was reached with the inspector pursuant to which $23,000 was paid to the plaintiffs. The agreement was made without any admission of liability. No amendment to the action was filed at the hearing to take this amount into consideration. [ 10 ] That is the procedural context. [ 11 ] The Court will now summarize the facts and then present the issues in dispute. Finally, the Court will conclude by setting out the positions of the parties. The final segment will also include the arguments of the parties. The facts [ 12 ] In or about June 2014, Mr. Goodman’s [6] employer informed the plaintiffs that they would be transferred to Ottawa in August 2014. [ 13 ] Faced with this short notice, they went to the Outaouais region in July 2014 to visit properties and buy one. They visited 12 to 15 residences during that period. [ 14 ] On July 4, 2014, [7] they made an offer to purchase the property located at 25 Xavier in Gatineau. [8] The residence was built in approximately 1962. [9] [ 15 ] The offer to purchase was conditional on, among other things, an inspection to be performed by a professional within 10 days. [10] [ 16 ] On July 6, 2014, the plaintiffs accompanied Mr. Hymans for the inspection. Subsequently, a report [11] was prepared. [ 17 ] The report provides a description of the residence and several recommendations, including repairing the damaged roof and chimneys. [12] [ 18 ] On the basis of this inspection, on July 10, 2014, the plaintiffs signed a document titled “notice and follow-up on fulfilment of conditions-immovable.” [13] [ 19 ] It is worth reproducing certain excerpts from this document, i.e., AV4.3, AV4.4 and AV4.5. AV4.3 IN ACCORDANCE WITH CLAUSE 8.1 OF THE PROMISE TO PURCHASE FORM IDENTIFIED ABOVE, NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT: – The BUYER has had the IMMOVABLE inspected by a building inspector or a professional, he has read the inspection report and declares himself satisfied with the inspection. Consequently, this condition is fulfilled. AV4.4 IN ACCORDANCE WITH CLAUSE 9.1 OF THE PROMISE TO PURCHASE FORM IDENTIFIED ABOVE, NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT: – The BUYER has examined and verified the documents listed under this clause and declares himself satisfied therewith. Consequently, this condition is fulfilled. AV5 OTHERS: -  CLAUSE 6.2 (FINANCING) THE CONDITION IS FULFILLED. ATTACH A COPY OF THE APPROVAL OF THE BANK. THE VENDOR HAS AGREED TO REPAIR A PORTION OF THE ROOF AS IDENTIFIED IN THE BUILDING INSPECTION REPORT AS WELL THE MASONRY OF BOTH CHIMNEYS ALSO AS IDENTIFIED IN THE BUILDING INSPECTION REPORT. THE WORK SHALL BE DONE BY A PROFESSIONNAL AT THE SELLER’S EXPENSE ANS SHALL PROVIDE A COPY OF PROOF OF SAID REPAIRS BEING COMPLETED WITH THE CONTACT INFORMATION FOR FUTURE REFERENCE PROVEDED. WORK SHALL BE COMPLETED PRIOR BUYER TAKING POSSESSION OF THE PROPERTY IF NOT COMPLETED A AMOUNT OF MONEY WILL BE HELD AT THE NOTARY UNTIL THE WORK IS COMPLETED. [ 20 ] In late July 2014, the plaintiffs received an email from their real estate agent along with photographs showing that the work requested had been performed. [ 21 ] It should be noted that the defendants Passmore and Waugh retained a licensed contractor to install a new membrane on the roof and a mason to repair the chimneys. [ 22 ] On August 8, 2014, the plaintiffs purchased the residence at issue in this dispute for $237,000. [ 23 ] It is worth mentioning that no discussions were ever held between the buyers and the sellers Passmore and Waugh. [ 24 ] The plaintiff Waters stated that after the purchase, she noticed the presence of mouse droppings. She installed traps and caught some twenty mice over time. It seems that this problem did not reoccur. In 2015, the plaintiff also noticed the presence of carpenter ants. [ 25 ] In the spring of 2015, the plaintiffs began landscaping work, in particular to change some of the patio slabs on the entrance walkway on the right side. [14] [ 26 ] While removing the patio slabs, the contractor noticed that the foundation made of concrete blocks had deteriorated. [ 27 ] The plaintiffs therefore retained an expert engineer, Pierre Beaupré. [ 28 ] The expert went on site and confirmed that foundation was in a state of deterioration. [ 29 ] The foundation was located under the demarcation line where the parging of the former walkway ended. [ 30 ] Using a bricklayer’s hammer, the expert noted that the front section on the right side of the building was damaged and that there were several deformations. [ 31 ] The expert also performed several hammering tests on the rest of the foundation’s perimeter and noted that the surface of the blocks was not crumbling, leading him to conclude that the problem detected was located in the section near the walkway. [ 32 ] Without restating the report in detail, since the Court will return to it later in its analysis, it should be noted that the expert considered the causes of this deterioration and concluded, further to spray tests performed on the roof, that water was flowing through the soffit directly into the excavation, i.e., the former walkway. [ 33 ] The expert stated that he was told by the plaintiff Waters that during the winter, the flow of water caused major accumulations of ice. [ 34 ] The expert said he was of the view that these accumulations of ice led to the use of calcium and de-icing products, which significantly affected the resistance of the concrete blocks. [ 35 ] He stated the following in summary with respect to the cause: L’application de produits de déverglaçage à cet endroit explique la détérioration localisée des fondations [15] . [ 36 ] In this context, on April 17, 2015, the plaintiffs sent the defendants Passmore and Waugh a formal notice of the defects noted. [ 37 ] It should be noted that upon receipt of the formal notice, the defendants Passmore and Waugh sent the defendants Babin and Keely a letter informing them of the defects alleged at that time. The plaintiffs did not send those defendants a formal notice. [ 38 ] In the fall of 2015, work was performed on the residence, and the plaintiffs allege that additional defects were discovered, affecting the plumbing and electricity. The plaintiffs also allege that they found mould. [ 39 ] As a result, according to them, significant work was required, hence the action in damages amended in 2017 to claim the amount of $155,981.58 in regard to an immovable that they purchased for $230,000. Issues [ 40 ] Because of the bankruptcy, the Court proposes to first consider the liability of the defendants Babin and Keely. To that end, it will be necessary to determine whether the latent defects existed at the time of the sale to the defendants Passmore and Waugh in 2008. [ 41 ] Next, it will be necessary to rule on the damages claimed. [ 42 ] As for the defendants Passmore and Waugh, it will be necessary to determine whether the conditions for the operation of section 178 of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act have been met. [ 43 ] This Court proposes the following questions: Regarding the defendants Babin and Keely [ 44 ] Has it been established on a balance of probabilities that there were latent defects within the meaning of article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec on September 2, 2008? If so, what is the appropriate quantum of damages? Regarding the defendants Passmore and Waugh [ 45 ] Are the conditions for the operation of s. 178(1) ( e ) of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act applicable such that the order of discharge does not release the   provable claim? Analysis and discussion Defendants Babin and Keely: [ 46 ] At the outset, it should be noted that it has been established since General Motors v. Kravitz [16] that the guarantee against latent defects follows the property sold. Moreover, articles 1442 and 1730 C.C.Q . codify this principle. [ 47 ] In this regard, the Court of Appeal stated the following in 2016: [17] [43]  Avec l’avènement du Code civil du Québec , en 1994, le législateur a codifié, aux articles 1442 et 1730 C.c.Q., la solution adoptée par la Cour suprême dans l’arrêt Kravitz pour la garantie contre les vices cachés [11] . En matière immobilière, le recours du sous-acquéreur contre un vendeur antérieur, consacré par la jurisprudence et la doctrine [12], est assuré par l’article 1442 C.c.Q. , comme il l’était auparavant, jusqu’à un certain point, en vertu de « la règle Kravitz » [13] . [44]  La garantie contre les vices cachés suit, en quelque sorte, le bien. Elle est l’accessoire de la chose vendue [14] . En somme, le sous-acquéreur, en acquérant la propriété de la chose, devient créancier de la garantie des vices cachés. [45]  Les principes énoncés par le juge Pratte, au nom de la Cour suprême, dans Kravitz , apportent des éléments pertinents de réflexion, même si cet arrêt porte sur le caractère solidaire des obligations auxquelles sont tenus les vendeurs professionnels et sur la résolution de la vente d’un bien mobilier de consommation et non d’un bien immobilier : L’on doit donc dire que le sous-acquéreur, en acquérant la propriété de la chose, devient le créancier de la garantie légale des vices cachés due par le premier vendeur à raison de la vente qu’il a consentie au premier acheteur. Cette créance, on le sait, a deux objets principaux: le premier est la résiliation de la vente alors que le deuxième est le paiement de dommages-intérêts. La créance transmise au sous-acquéreur comprend donc à la fois le droit à la résiliation de la vente consentie par le premier vendeur au premier acheteur et le droit aux dommages-intérêts. La créance est transmise au sous-acquéreur dans son intégralité […] [15] . [50] La garantie de qualité (art. 1522-1528 C.c.B.‑C. ; art. 1726 - 1731 C.c.Q. ) peut être invoquée par un sous-acquéreur contre un vendeur antérieur dans la mesure où le vice existait au moment où ce dernier était propriétaire du bien et que son auteur n’avait pas renoncé expressément à cette garantie. Il exerce les droits que lui a transmis son auteur avec l’immeuble. Le recours du sous-acquéreur est de nature contractuelle. Ce dernier y trouvera « un avantage dans les cas où son propre vendeur est introuvable, insolvable ou encore s’il n’est pas tenu à l’obligation de garantie » [18] ou encore, comme en l’espèce, si la société qui a vendu le bien est dissoute. [ 48 ] This last excerpt reveals that while a recourse against the previous seller exists, it must nevertheless satisfy criteria attached to the original sale, that is, the defect must have existed at the time of the sale, and the sale price at the time must also be taken into account. [ 49 ] Herein lies the first challenge in this case. Did the alleged defects exist in 2008? [ 50 ] Before embarking on a review of the evidence, it is worth describing the conditions attached to actions for latent defects. [ 51 ] First of all, pursuant to article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec , the seller must warrant the quality of the property sold. Art. 1726: The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. . The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert . [ 52 ] It is apparent in this article that there are certain elements attached to latent defects protected by the warranty of quality. [ 53 ] My colleague Jean-Jude Chabot J. summarized them well in a 2017 decision, [18] and the Court will reproduce his analysis in its entirety. [44] Comme le rappelle la Cour d'appel dans l'arrêt récent Leroux c. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 (CanLII) , pour donner ouverture à la garantie de qualité, le vice doit répondre à certaines exigences : [40] Pour se prévaloir de la garantie légale contre les vices cachés, quatre conditions doivent donc être respectées : (i) que le bien soit affecté d'un vice grave, l'intensité de cette gravité ayant été définie par la jurisprudence à partir des expressions « impropre à l'usage » et « diminuent tellement son utilité »; (ii) que le vice existait au moment de la vente; (iii) que le vice soit caché, qualité qui s'évalue objectivement et qui est accompagnée d'une obligation de s'informer; et (iv) que le vice soit inconnu de l'acheteur, qualité qui s'évalue subjectivement et dont le fardeau de preuve appartient au vendeur. [45]      Il s’agit d’une norme objective comme le souligne la Cour suprême du Canada dans l’arrêt ABB inc. c. Domtar , 2007 CSC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 R.C.S. 461 : […]  Cependant, quelle que soit la qualification du vice, il doit présenter quatre caractères, tous essentiels à la garantie : il doit être caché, suffisamment grave, existant au moment de la vente et inconnu de l’acheteur. 3.6.1.2  Caractère caché du vice Le caractère caché du vice s’...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... , 2007 CSC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 R.C.S. 461 : […]  Cependant, quelle que soit la qualification du vice, il doit présenter quatre caractères, tous essentiels à la garantie : il doit être caché, suffisamment grave, existant au moment de la vente et inconnu de l’acheteur. 3.6.1.2  Caractère caché du vice Le caractère caché du vice s’apprécie selon une norme objective, c’est-à-dire en évaluant l’examen fait par l’acheteur en fonction de celui qu’aurait fait un acheteur prudent et diligent de même compétence : P.-G. Jobin, « Précis sur la vente », dans La réforme du Code civil (1993), vol. 2, 359, p. 466; M. Pourcelet, La vente (5 e éd. 1987), p. 149.  Autrement dit, on ne s’interroge pas simplement sur l’ignorance du vice; on cherchera aussi à déterminer si un acheteur raisonnable placé dans les mêmes circonstances aurait constaté le vice. [46] La notion de l'apparence du vice ou de son caractère occulte doit donc être évaluée objectivement, c'est-à-dire s'appuyer sur des indices positifs : [43] Notre Cour précisait dans Marcoux [2008] QCCA 259. Autres références omises], le danger que constitue le fait d'attribuer à l'acheteur l'obligation de moyens de l'inspecteur préachat et d'ainsi fausser la norme objective, la seule qui existe : [20]  Notre Cour rappelle que selon les termes de l' article 1726 in fine C.c.Q. , il existe une seule norme applicable, celle qui examine la conduite de l'acheteur prudent et diligent .  Le juge de première instance ne peut écarter cette règle en appréciant la conduite de l'inspecteur et en imputant à l'acheteur, en l'absence de signes annonciateurs d'un vice potentiel, ce qu'il considère un manque de prudence et un manquement aux règles de l'art de la part de l'inspecteur. Le test de l' article 1726 C.c.Q . est celui de l'acheteur prudent et diligent et non pas celui de l'expert tatillon sur qui reposerait une sorte d'obligation de résultat . [21]  Par ailleurs, l'inspection préachat n'est pas une expertise. En principe, cet examen doit être attentif et sérieux quoique plutôt rapide et non approfondi. En l'absence d'un indice révélateur, l'acheteur ou l'inspecteur n'a pas à ouvrir les murs ou creuser autour des fondations. (soulignement de la Cour d’appel) (Leroux c. Gravano, précité) [47] Cela dit, en présence d'un indice révélateur, l'acheteur a l'obligation de faire une vérification plus poussée : [44] Quoiqu'il ne soit pas nécessaire d'ouvrir les murs, de creuser les fondations, il n'en demeure pas moins qu'un indice puisse soulever des soupçons et requérir de pousser l'examen. [45]   Dans St-Louis [2006 QCCA 1643], la Cour écrivait : [39]  En résumé, lorsque l'immeuble présente un indice permettant de soupçonner l'existence d'un vice potentiel, l'acheteur prudent et diligent, qui n'a pas fait appel à un expert, doit le faire ou vérifier autrement et de façon satisfaisante ce qui est suspect. Dans le cas où l'acheteur a déjà fait appel à un expert, la présence de signes annonciateurs d'un vice potentiel oblige l'expert à faire une inspection plus approfondie.  S'il ne la fait pas et qu'un vice est mis à jour, la conclusion que le vice n'était pas caché s'imposera ; [soulignement de la cour] [46] Bien sûr, la présence d'un signe annonciateur n'oblige pas l'expert, mais bien l'acheteur, à pousser son étude, en recourant à un expert ou au même expert ou inspecteur.  On ne peut reprocher à l'acheteur qui fait appel à un inspecteur d'avoir manqué à son devoir de prudence et de diligence si l'inspecteur n'a pas décelé d'indice de vice que l'acheteur raisonnable ne pouvait pas lui-même déceler.  C'est seulement lorsque cet inspecteur décèle un indice sérieux que l'acheteur doit pousser son étude. (id.) [48] Si l'on n'exige pas de l'acheteur des compétences particulières en matière immobilière, on exige de lui qu'il soit prudent et diligent dans son inspection de l'immeuble.  L'analyse de son comportement prend en compte les circonstances particulières de l'espèce, notamment le statut professionnel ou non du vendeur, celui de l'acquéreur, la  nature, l'âge et le  prix  du  bâtiment, le  type  de vice  et même le comportement des parties ( Placement Jacpar inc. c. Benzakour , 1989 Can LII 976 (QCCA)).  L'acheteur ne peut pas se décharger de son obligation sur le travail de l'inspecteur et refuser de voir ce qui est visible.  Il ne peut pas se reposer sur le travail de l'inspecteur s'il ne tient pas compte de ses observations ni de ses recommandations. [49] Particulièrement en présence d’une vieille maison, l’acheteur prudent et avisé doit l’examiner soigneusement et ne peut se rabattre sur des déclarations générales du vendeur à l’effet que la maison est en bon état général et que des réparations ont été faites : 14  L’acheteur le moindrement prudent et avisé se sentira obligé, avant d’acquiescer à l’achat d’une vieille maison, de l’examiner soigneusement.  Cette proposition a d’autant plus de pertinence en l’espèce qu’il s’agit d’une maison dont la construction remonte à cent ans. […] ‘‘ Des vices ne sont pas cachés lorsque l’acheteur devait les prévoir d’après la nature de la chose ou les usages connus ’’ (Baudry – Laçantinerie, 2 è éd. Vol XVII, no 420).  De la même façon peut-on dire que l’acheteur d’une maison centenaire doit prévoir qu’il y trouvera des détériorations dues à la vétusté et s’il ne fait aucune réserve, il est censé accepter la maison avec les défauts résultant de la façon de construire à l’époque et les détériorations dues à son ancienneté. 16  Sans doute l’appelant a-t-il mentionné à ses acheteurs que la maison était généralement en bon état; sans doute a-t-il fait mention aussi des réparations faites; Mais ce qu’on doit entendre du ‘‘bon état’’ d’une maison centenaire ne permet pas à l’acheteur d’ignorer le travail du temps sur ses parties constituantes.  Une affirmation aussi large que celle qu’avoue l’appelant (‘‘j’ai dit que la maison en général était en bon état, sauf le problème de condensation qui avait (sic) dans le toit’’) (m.i. p.81), n’a pas ici pour effet de dispenser un acheteur prudent de faire un examen soigneux de la maison.  L’acheteur est alors tenu de voir lors de cet examen, ce qui est visible et de prévoir le travail et l’usure du temps. (souligné du soussigné) ( Gélinas c. Beaumier , CAQ 200-09-000561-867, 14/11/1989, EYB 1989-58268) [50]      Le « travail et l’usure du temps » peuvent affecter gravement l’usage du bien sans nécessairement constituer un vice au sens de la loi.  L’acheteur d’une maison neuve s’attend à une maison sans malfaçons, point à la ligne.  Par contre, les attentes légitimes de l’acheteur d’une maison centenaire ne peuvent être les mêmes.  Les normes et les règles de l’art en matière de construction ont évolué, le sol peut s’être tassé, les fondations avoir commencé à s’effriter ou se disloquer, les poutres à fléchir, à pourrir, la tuyauterie à couler, le filage électrique être inadéquat, bref la maison centenaire, comme les êtres vivants, porte l’outrage des ans : La notion de gravité doit être appréciée selon les faits de chaque situation afin de déterminer la cause du vice allégué.  Par exemple, les tribunaux ont reconnu qu’une insuffisance importante d’isolation thermique peut constituer un vice grave. Ceux-ci ont aussi reconnu qu’une insuffisance d’isolation d’un édifice bicentenaire ne constitue pas un vice grave, puisque l’installation respectait les normes qui étaient en vigueur lors de la construction dudit immeuble . Il faut rappeler que « le passage du temps et la manipulation d’un bien engendrent inévitablement son usure, son vieillissement.  […]  Ces phénomènes peuvent compromettre la prestation d’un bien, tout comme un vice peut le faire.  Éventuellement, ils rendent le bien impropre à son usage ». De ce fait, nous pouvons établir qu’un vice provoque un défaut éventuel d’usage.  Il faut alors se demander si la vétusté engendrant un déficit d’usage du bien peut être soumise à la garantie de qualité. La réduction d’utilité doit être analysée en prenant en considération plusieurs faits.  D’abord, il est nécessaire de déterminer les attentes de l’acheteur d’une propriété comprenant des composantes vétustes.  À cet effet, Jeffrey Edwards soutient que : L’usage assuré par la garantie est délimité par les attentes de l’acheteur raisonnable.  […]  Les détériorations dues à l’usure, au vieillissement ou à la vétusté ne constituent donc pas de vices, car, en raison de la révision à la baisse de l’usage attendu, elles n’occasionnent aucun déficit d’usage au sens de la garantie : l’usage protégé varie selon l’état de l’usure, du vieillissement et de la vétusté du bien au moment de la vente. Il apparaît alors que l’attente de l’acheteur sera ajustée selon l’âge du bâtiment. De cette manière, l’acheteur doit s’attendre à la détérioration normale du bien acheté et ne peut pas déclarer un déficit d’usage.  Au surplus, il est entendu que les attentes de l’acheteur raisonnable doivent être orientées par les normes qui étaient en vigueur au moment de la construction de l’immeuble.  Il est donc clair que la perte d’usage ne peut résulter de l’usure normale. Il convient également d’évaluer si l’usure du bien est normale.  À cette fin, l’acheteur doit tenir compte de la durée normale d’autres biens du même genre afin de déterminer la période durant laquelle l’usage du bien est garanti. Ainsi, lorsque l’usage du bien dépasse les attentes que l’acheteur se devait d’anticiper, la vétusté ne pourra pas être jugée comme un vice caché .  Récemment, la Cour supérieure dans Berger c. Trudel a repris le test de la Cour suprême dans ABB inc. c. Domtar inc. qui est le suivant : « Est-ce que les vices dépassent ce que l’acheteur prudent d’une maison centenaire peut s’attendre au niveau des nécessités de réparations normales d’une telle maison ? ». Advenant une réponse positive à cette question, il est entendu que le vice dû à l’usure accélérée du bien pourra être assimilé à un vice caché. Toutefois, nous remarquons qu’il est possible qu’un bien tombe prématurément de vétusté sans être nécessairement associé à un vice. D’abord, la Cour supérieure a indiqué que l’absence de ventilation ayant provoqué l’usure prématurée d’une toiture est une situation apparente qui « aurait dû amener un acheteur prudent ou un expert à porter une attention particulière » .  […] (soulignement ajouté par le juge Chabot) (Luc Lachance et Guillaume C. Branconnier, Le passage du temps sur structure de l’immeuble : vétusté ou vice caché? , EYB 2015 RDN37, 1.1.1.2) (Références omises) [ 54 ] Let us now apply these principles to the facts of this case. We will first consider the problem that gave rise to this dispute, i.e., the foundation. [ 55 ] As mentioned above, the expert Beaupré traced the location of the problem to the walkway close to the steps. [ 56 ] At the hearing, he specified that according to his calculations, only 12.5% of the perimeter of the entire foundation is defective. The defective portion is located near the walkway. Moreover, he estimates the life expectance of a concrete block wall to be 75 years. [ 57 ] The Court must make a short digression here. If a party’s own expert assesses the affected portion of the foundation to be 12.5% and it had 52 years of wear in 2014 on a life expectancy of 75 years, why present a claim for excavation work for the entire perimeter of the immovable, and, in addition, fail to take the depreciation factor into account. The Court will return to this. [ 58 ] The expert determined that the damage was caused by the water flowing from the roof through the soffit and subsequently onto the walkway and adjacent wall. [19] The use of de-icing products and calcium led to the deterioration noted. [ 59 ] The source of the damage is therefore alleged to be the roof. [ 60 ] The expert noted that the roof membrane shows signs of premature wear. [ 61 ] He said that in some places, there is no longer any gravel on sections of the membrane. [ 62 ] It is interesting to note that the defendant Keely said he was surprised about the lack of gravel. He lived in the house for many years and went up on the roof several times. There was always gravel there. [ 63 ] He said that before the property was put up for sale in 2008, the roof was redone because it was at the end of its useful life. He remembers well that a licensed contractor put gravel on the newly installed membrane. He saw it himself. [ 64 ] The plaintiffs’ inspector did not make any mention of this anomaly in his report, which is surprising. [ 65 ] Similarly, some photographs of the roof taken at the time the property was put up for sale in 2008 show characteristics that are different from those in photographs that are more contemporaneous. [ 66 ] An eloquent example is the configuration of the roof where there is a spout «gargouille», which allowed water to infiltrate behind the fascia. [20] [ 67 ] The photographs accompanying the descriptive listing at the time the property was put up for sale in 2008 show that there were simply no spout on the roof. [21] [ 68 ] The photographs attached to the 2014 descriptive listing also do not seem to show this element. [ 69 ] In short, there were no spout in 2014, but this element appears eight months later in the expert’s photograph. [ 70 ] Like the gravel, the roof seems different than the one sold in 2008. [ 71 ] The expert said that he did not see any water infiltration inside the residence. [ 72 ] He confirmed that he...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 4\\nmore and Waugh to co-defendants Babin and Keely was valid. [ 151 ] What about the fall 2015 work, in regard to which the Court finds that the notices received by Passmore and Waugh were never transmitted to the co-defendants? Can we conclude, as the plaintiffs suggest, that notice to the defendants Passmore and Waugh is sufficient? [ 152 ] The Court does not think so. [ 153 ] Professors Jobin and Cumyn state the following in their book on sale: [34] Lorsque l’acheteur veut exercer ses droits contre un vendeur antérieur (par exemple le fabricant) selon les articles 1442 ou 1730, doit-il lui donner un préavis? Selon nous, la réponse est affirmative, en règle générale [35] . En effet, on ne voit pas de justification à une dispense de préavis ici, qui ferait perdre au vendeur antérieur la possibilité de vérifier s’il s’agit vraiment d’un vice couvert par la garantie et la chance de le réparer à meilleur compte. Une exception doit toutefois être admise, malgré sa sévérité, quand le vendeur antérieur et le vendeur immédiat sont solidairement tenus à la garantie : alors comme une mise en demeure (article 1599), le préavis donné à l’un des débiteurs produit ses effets sur le codébiteur solidaire, qui ne peut pas se plaindre de ne pas avoir reçu de préavis [36] . [ 154 ] In the Court’s view, while the professional seller may be held solidarily liable, the same cannot be said for ordinary sellers. The Court cannot see how the rules of solidarity could be applicable, and hence, how written notice given to Passmore and Waugh in October 2015 could be set up against the defendants Babin and Keely. [ 155 ] Solidarity between debtors is not presumed; it exists only where it is expressly stipulated by the parties or by law (art. 1525 C.C.Q.). [ 156 ] Nobody here is claiming that a contractual provision to that effect exists, so are we in a case provided for by law? [ 157 ] This is clearly not a situation involving the operation of an enterprise or an obligation to make reparation for injury caused to another in accordance with art. 1526 C.C.Q. [ 158 ] Also in Joyal , cited above, Dalphond J.A. stated the following on the nature of the debtors’ obligations under art. 1730 C.C.Q.: [49] Il convient de préciser que les vendeurs successifs, s’ils ne sont pas des vendeurs professionnels, sont débiteurs de l’acheteur, mais pas nécessairement au même titre. Ainsi, certains auteurs suggèrent que leur responsabilité est in solidum plutôt que solidaire (Jobin, par. 219). [50]  Par contre, si les vendeurs successifs sont des vendeurs professionnels et qu’il y a vice de qualité, l’ article 1730 C.c.Q. trouvera application. Cet article assimile le vendeur au fabricant et énonce que ce dernier, de même que les intermédiaires, sont désormais tenus à la même responsabilité que le vendeur quant aux vices de qualité. [51]  Dans leur traité La responsabilité civile , 7 e éd., vol. 2, Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2007, les auteurs Jean-Louis Baudouin et Patrice Deslauriers écrivent : 2-360 — Généralités — Que ce soit sous le régime de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur ou sous celui du Code civil , le fabricant est désormais tenu à la même responsabilité que le vendeur. Cette assimilation, conforme à l'évolution jurisprudentielle, est la rançon du rôle prépondérant que joue aujourd'hui le fabricant dans la mise en marché de ses propres produits. Elle obéit à une logique de protection accrue du consommateur, qui ne doit pas se retrouver uniquement face à un intermédiaire au statut juridique et à la solvabilité généralement plus incertains. Leur responsabilité solidaire vis-à-vis du consommateur ou de l'utilisateur est maintenant bien acceptée tant par la jurisprudence que par la loi. Seule la Convention des Nations Unies sur les contrats de vente internationale de marchandises n'amalgame pas vendeur et fabricant. [références omises] [ 159 ] Holding the professional seller liable for the property sold along with its buyer does not cause any problem and responds to a mindset of consumer protection. [37] This mindset has no place, however, in the presence of ordinary successive sellers who are not professionals. [ 160 ] If the plaintiffs wish to sue more than one seller, why should they be exempt from the requirement of providing written notice to one of them? [ 161 ] The obvious problem in this case results from the fact that the initial intention was not to sue Babin and Keely. It was only after the bankruptcy of Passmore and Waugh that the plaintiffs changed their minds. Why must Babin and Keely suffer from this strategic change? [ 162 ] The written notice serves the important purpose, among others, of allowing the sellers to assess the defect and if necessary, identify the steps that should be taken. [ 163 ] In Joseph c . Fortin , [38] the Court of Appeal stated the following in response to the appellants’ argument that the lack of notice had not caused any prejudice: [9]  Cependant, lorsque les appelants ont reçu dénonciation du vice de la part de leurs acheteurs, ils ne l’ont pas transmise aux défendeurs en garantie intimés. Leur propre dénonciation n’est survenue qu’une fois la décontamination et la remise en état des lieux complétée et a privé les intimés en garantie de l’opportunité de pouvoir même tenter de démontrer que le vice (la contamination) était subséquent à la vente de 2003. [10]  De toute façon, le fardeau de prouver l’antériorité du vice appartenait aux appelants, qui ne s’en sont pas déchargés. [ 164 ] In this case, the defendants Babin and Keely were deprived of a written notice concerning the fall 2015 work, and because the written notice is a condition for the operation of the remedy, the claim regarding that work must be dismissed. [ 165 ] However, at the risk of repetition and like in Joseph c. Fortin , cited above, the Court finds it useful to note that the evidence on a balance of probabilities does not allow us to establish that the latent defects existed in 2008, except for the beam and the joists that were cut ($2,400). [ 166 ] For these last elements, in addition to the absence of notice, it should be noted that the plaintiffs received $23,000 in a settlement with the inspector, which is more than sufficient to cover this portion of the claim. [ 167 ] In closing, it may seem paradoxical to question the lack of written notice in view of the conclusion that the evidence on a balance of probabilities does not support the claim for latent defects. [ 168 ] Indeed, the Court cannot conclude that there was any prejudice resulting from the absence of written notice if it finds that there were no latent defects in 2008. The defence has thus successfully rebutted the plaintiff’s evidence. [ 169 ] The Court understands this nuance but adds that it is only after a detailed examination of the mostly circumstantial evidence that it has arrived at this conclusion. [ 170 ] The fact remains that defendants found themselves in a way obliged to rely on circumstantial elements, whereas had they received a written notice, they would have had the benefit of direct evidence. [ 171 ] In the circumstances, the Court found it useful to make reference to the lack of written notice in order to demonstrate that even if it had found that latent defects did exist, the absence of one of the conditions for operation of the remedy, in this case the written notice concerning the fall 2015 work, would nevertheless have been fatal to the claim. [ 172 ] The Court will now address the situation of the other defendants, Passmore and Waugh. Defendants Passmore and Waugh [ 173 ] Are the conditions of section 178( 1 )( e ) of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act met such that the order of discharge does not release the provable claim? [ 174 ] Section 178(1) ( e ) of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act states: 178 (1) An order of discharge does not release the bankrupt from ( e ) any debt or liability resulting from obtaining property or services by false pretences or fraudulent misrepresentation, other than a debt or liability that arises from an equity claim ; [ 175 ] In Salesse (Syndic de) , [39] Catherine La Rosa J. set out the conditions for operation and identified a number of judgments concerning latent defects where the failure to give notice of the latent defects resulted in the operation of section 178(1) ( e ). [ 176 ] With respect to the conditions for operation of the remedy, La Rosa J. stated: [31] Ainsi, lors d’une demande introduite sur la base de l’article 178(1)e) LFI , les étapes suivantes doivent être franchies pour avoir gain de cause : a. Le demandeur doit prouver les fausses allégations; b. Une fois ces fausses allégations prouvées nait une présomption d’ intention frauduleuse qui détruit celle de la bonne foi; c. Le défendeur peut renverser cette présomption d’intention frauduleuse en fournissant des explications qui, au final, démontrent l’absence d’ intention frauduleuse [2]. [32] Il est important de mentionner que l’article 178 LFI doit recevoir une interprétation restrictive puisque la règle générale consiste en la libération du failli qui éteint toutes les dettes susceptibles de constituer une réclamation personnelle[3]. [ 177 ] The judgments identified by Justice La Rosa consists of situations where the latent defects were concealed or where there was voluntary failure to disclose a known fact. [ 178 ] What is the case here? [ 179 ] Did the defendants Passmore and Waugh make false statements, either directly or by voluntary concealment? [ 180 ] The Court does not think so. [ 181 ] Let us recall at the outset that the purchase of the house happened quickly because the buyers had very little time to relocate, and they visited a dozen houses over a few days. [ 182 ] Thus, there were never any meetings or even discussions between the buyers and the sellers. [ 183 ] The plaintiffs, of course aware of this situation, base the theory of their case on certain elements that could lead to the conclusion, in accordance with their position, that the sellers knew about the defects and tried to conceal them. [ 184 ] For example, there was a crack in the parging that had been patched by the defendant Passmore. [ 185 ] Next, they noted the presence of rocks in the mix to compensate for the depth of the crack. From this, they conclude that the sellers tried to hide the crack and mislead them. [ 186 ] Finally, they also assert that the residence was freshly painted, probably to hide marks inside. [ 187 ] The plaintiffs allege that there had to have been marks confirming the infiltration and therefore also the presence of mould. [ 188 ] Without repeating every element or each of the plaintiff’s suspicions, the Court will begin by stating at the outset that the plaintiffs did not discharge their burden of proof. [ 189 ] More specifically, it is worth mentionning that the parging work was explained by Ms. Passmore, and the Court believes her version. [ 190 ] She said that she did the parging work herself at the request of her real estate agent. The work was done for purely aesthetic reasons, with mixed results, as everyone agrees. [ 191 ] She says, however, that she never thought there was an infiltration problem. [ 192 ] The experts [40] heard confirmed that the crack in the parging was in no way indicative of the problem affecting the foundation. [ 193 ] The pre-purchase inspector in fact refers to it in his report. [41] [ 194 ] Even the plaintiff’s expert said that the deteriorations were located under the walkway and below ground level. [ 195 ] There is no doubt that it is a defect but that does not necessarily lead to the operation of section 178(1) ( e ) of the Bankruptcy and Insolvency Act . [ 196 ] Moreover, the fact that the residence had recently been painted does not lead to the conclusion of an intention to conceal the latent defects. [ 197 ] It does not seem unusual for sellers to try to improve the appearance of their residence by painting it. [ 198 ] What is more, the sellers even left cans of recently used paint on the premises so that the buyers would be aware of the new colours used. [ 199 ] Suffice it to say that this does not really suggest an intention to conceal. [ 200 ] As for the argument that there were likely marks or some other manifestation of mould, the Court adds that it does not find that there was mould present as described. [ 201 ] In addition, a particular situation cannot go unmentioned. [ 202 ] Between the purchase of the residence in 2008 and its sale to the plaintiffs, the Passmore–Waugh family went through a very sad period. [ 203 ] Their son was diagnosed with cancer. Without going into detail, it is worth noting that the type of cancer afflicting the child and the numerous treatments greatly affected his immune system. [ 204 ] At the hearing, his mother said that his frailty forced the family to be overly cautious and avoid all contact with germs. For that reason, she said, she would never have allowed her child to be in a mouldy environment. The Court believes her. [ 205 ] Another element corroborating the absence of knowledge of the problems by the defendants Passmore and Waugh results from the exhibits filed by the other defendants. [ 206 ] A review of the various discussions through Facebook [42] between the defendants in the months preceding the sale to the plaintiffs shows that Passmore and Waugh expressed their satisfaction of the residence acquired. Their move was not the result of any dissatisfaction. [ 207 ] In short, if the house was affected by all the defects alleged, and the sellers Passmore and Waugh were aware of it, the Court doubts they would express as much satisfaction. [ 208 ] In conclusion, the Court is of the view that the plaintiffs did not discharge their burden of proof and that section 178(1) ( e ) is not applicable in this case. [ 209 ] FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 210 ] DISMISSES the originating application against the defendants Babin and Keely; [ 211 ] WITH legal costs. [ 212 ] DISMISSES the application seeking the operation of section 178(1) ( e ) against the defendants Passmore and Waugh; [ 213 ] WITHOUT legal costs. ____________________ DOMINIQUE GOULET , J.S.C. Mtre Alexandra Roy Daoust Siroy Counsel for the plaintiffs Vanessa Passmore and Robert Waugh Representing themselves Mtre Benoit Duchesne Gowling WLG Counsel for the defendants Ann-Marie Babin and Thomas E. Keely Dates of hearing: March 25, 26, 27, 28 and 29 and June 21, 2019 [1] $107,381.25 + $51,219.39 = $158,600.64. [2] The sale price was $220,000, P-20. [3] $70,418.89 + $111,808.91 = $182,227.80. [4] That statement is set out in paragraph 26 of the application for authorization. [5] $80,805.03 + $75,176.55 = $155,981.58. [6] Mr. Goodman is in the military. [7] The offer P-1 states that it is made further to the July 3, 2014, visit (section 7.1). [8] P-1. [9] According to the inspector, see P-2. [10] P-1, section 8.1. [11] P-2. [12] P-2 at 4 and 5. [13] P-3. [14] P-28. [15] See report P-7 at 4 under the heading “cause of the defects.” [16] General Motors v. Kravitz 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790. [17] Dupuy c . Leblanc , 2016 QCCA 1141 . [18] Vermette c . Boisvert , 2017 QCCS 3159 (CanLII) , aff’d 2019 QCCA 829 ; with respect to the conditions that must be met, see also the judgment rendered by Serge Gaudet J. in Pesant c. St-Louis , 2019 QCCS 1472 at paras. 47 to 54 . [19] P-28. [20] See in particular photograph AP-36, exhibit P-7. [21] See P-26 at 4 of 6. [22] Paragraph 20 of the re-amended originating application\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 5\\nS 3159 (CanLII) , aff’d 2019 QCCA 829 ; with respect to the conditions that must be met, see also the judgment rendered by Serge Gaudet J. in Pesant c. St-Louis , 2019 QCCS 1472 at paras. 47 to 54 . [19] P-28. [20] See in particular photograph AP-36, exhibit P-7. [21] See P-26 at 4 of 6. [22] Paragraph 20 of the re-amended originating application. [23] The Court notes that there is indeed a report prepared by Eskis Architect that was never introduced into evidence although the opportunity to do so was provided. Eskis is a company related to the contractor. [24] In September 2016. [25] P-15. [26] Except for the roof and 12.5% of the foundation. [27] See supra note 15. [28] La Cour d’’appel réitère ces principes dans une décision récente du 26 mars 2019; 6983499 Canada Inc. c. Gagné , 2019 QCCA 536 . [29] Except, however, for the beam and the joists. [30] See supra note 22. [31] Paragraph 18 a) of the re-amended originating application. [32] Claude Joyal Inc. c . CNH Canada Ltd et Axa Assurances Inc. , 2014 QCCA 588 . [33] Nadeau c. Mercedes Benz Canada Inc. 2017 QCCA 470 . [34] Pierre-Gabriel Jobin & Michelle Cumyn, La vente , 4th ed. (Montreal: Yvon Blais, 2017) at 250. [35] St-Germain c. Lévesque , EYB 2009-164515 (C.S.) ; Immeubles Rochefort inc. c. Charest , EYB 2009-153970 (C.Q) ; 7507836 Canada inc. c. Pouliot , 2017 QCCS 1759 , EYB 2017-279245. Contra : 9041-9086 Québec inc. c. Usinage Bernier , J.E. 2005-1995, REJB 2005-94177 (C.Q) . [36] Royal & Sunalliance c. Hewitt Equipement ltée , EYB 2008-148026 (C.S) ; Claude Joyal inc. c. CNH Canada Ltd , EYB 2014-234922 (C.A.) , inf. EYB2014-202477 (C.S). [37] See Jean-Louis Baudouin & Patrice Deslauriers, La responsabilité civile , 7th ed., vol. 2 (Cowansville, Qc.: Yvon Blais, 2007) at 2-360. [38] Joseph c. Fortin , 2018 QCCA 74 . [39] Salesse (Syndic de) , 2016 QCCS 3091 ; see also Poulin c. Anglehart , 2017 QCCS 1258 . [40] The defendants Passmore and Waugh called an expert to testify on this aspect. [41] P-2 at 8. [42] DBK-2.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.029709506779909134, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\", \"title\": \"Waters c. Passmore, 2019 QCCS 3805 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.029709506779909134, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\"}, \"token_count\": 11652, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs3805-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Brabant c. Shaw, 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII) | Brabant c. Shaw | CanLII Brabant c. Shaw, 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII) Date: 2009-05-26 File number: 505-32-023039-077 Citation: Brabant c. Shaw, 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/240nh > Brabant c. Shaw 2009 QCCQ 5455 COURT OF QUÉBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF LONGUEUIL Civil Division No: 505-32-023039-077 DATE: May 26, 2009 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE VIRGILE BUFFONI, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ ISABELLE BRABANT et MICHEL CLOUTIER-BRABANT Plaintiffs vs. MARY SHAW Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiffs claim from the defendant the sum of $5,751.25, alleging a latent defect. facts [ 2 ] Plaintiffs purchased the defendant's property on August 24, 2005. [ 3 ] On December 23, 2005, the plaintiffs discovered a leak in the basement, pouring water on the electrical installations. The plaintiffs phoned the defendant on January 12, 2006, to inform her of the problem and ask her to come to the house and evaluate the origin of the leak and assess the damage. The defendant denied any involvement or responsability in the situation and refused to discuss the matter further. [ 4 ] Due to the urgency of the situation, more particularly the fire hazard, the plaintiffs proceeded with the repairs. [ 5 ] On January 5, 2006, the plaintiffs asked Mr. O'Saughnessy, who previously had inspected the house prior to the sale, to evaluate the situation. He found that the leak was due to an infiltration of water in the bathroom wall under the window. The insulation material was black and moldy. Due to the extent of the damage, he concluded that the leak had existed for a long time, well before the sale. [ 6 ] The plaintiffs obtained three estimates. All three contractors estimated the cost of the repairs between $4,350 to $5,500, plus all applicable taxes. [ 7 ] The plaintiffs hired the contractor with the lowest estimate to proceed with the repairs. Upon opening the wall, the contractor discovered that the wall studs were also black and moldy and the floor underneath the bathtub had deteriorated and had to be replaced. The final cost of the repairs reached $5,000, plus taxes. [ 8 ] The defendant testified that the bathroom was renovated in 2003. She was also unaware of any defect affecting the house or any indication of a defect at the time of the sale. [ 9 ] Article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec provides that « ( T)he seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them .» [ 10 ] The legal warranty against latent defects applies to the seller even though the seller has no knowledge wathsoever of the defect in the thing sold. [1] The fact that the inspector could not detect the defect prior to the sale corroborates both plaintiffs' and defendant's testimony as to the absence of any apparent defect or indication of same. [ 11 ] The defect discovered by the plaintiff was latent, serious, existing at the time of the sale and unknown to the buyers. The defect rendered the house unfit for the use it was intented or seriously diminished its usefulness. [ 12 ] The Court concludes that the house sold by the defendant to the plaintiffs contained a latent defect at the time of the sale, according to Article 1726 C.c.Q ., which entitles the plaintiffs to a reduction of the sale price. [ 13 ] In the instance, the Court will reduce the sale price by the cost of the repairs, which are not contested. WHEREFORE, THE COURT : [ 14 ] CONDEMNS the defendant to pay to the plaintiffs the sum of $5,751.25, with interest at the legal rate and the additional indemnity provided for in Article 1619 of the Civil Code of Québec since August 31, 2006, with costs (151 $). __________________________________ VIRGILE BUFFONI, J.C.Q. [1] A.B.B. inc. c. Domtar inc ., [2007] 3 R.S.C. 461....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02249845862388611, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\", \"title\": \"Brabant c. Shaw, 2009 QCCQ 5455 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02249845862388611, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\"}, \"token_count\": 1075, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq5455-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq688\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq688-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Leclair c. Baker, 2024 QCCQ 688 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2024 QCCQ 688 (CanLII) | Leclair c. Baker | CanLII Date: 2024-02-13 File number: 760-32-019139-213 Citation: Leclair c. Baker, 2024 QCCQ 688 (CanLII) Leclair c. Baker 2024 QCCQ 688 COURT OF QUÉBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF BEAUHARNOIS LOCALITY OF SALABERRY-DE-VALLEYFIELD Civil Division No. : 760-32-019139-213 DATE : FEBRUARY 13, 2024 BEFORE THE HONOURABLE PHILIPPE DE GRANDMONT, J.C.Q. MICHAEL LECLAIR and GENEVIÈVE RICHER Plaintiffs v. DONALD BAKER Defendant JUDGMENT [ 1 ] The Plaintiffs, Mr. Michael Leclair and Ms. Geneviève Richer, claim damages in the amount of $15,000 from the Defendant, Mr. Donald Baker, from whom they purchased a house located in Senneville. [ 2 ] Plaintiffs allege that said house is, upon its sale, affected by latent defects of two kinds, namely water infiltrations in the basement, and decay of a retaining wall. [ 3 ] Defendant contests the claim, and responds by asserting, among others, the apparent nature of the alleged defects. [ 4 ] All parties appeared personally and testified before the Court. QUESTIONS AT ISSUE [ 5 ] In order to decide the present case, the Court will answer the following questions: a) Do the water infiltrations constitute a latent defect ? b) Does the retaining wall decay constitute a latent defect ? ANALYSIS a) Do the water infiltrations constitute a latent defect ? [ 6 ] Defendant sells the house to Plaintiffs in January 2020. Such sale is subject to the legal warranty of quality [1] , commonly known as the warranty against latent defects. [ 7 ] The house in question is a two-story house, with a fieldstone foundation, built around 1920 and sitting in near proximity of the Bay of Vaudreuil’s shoreline. [ 8 ] Prior to the sale, Defendant provides Plaintiffs with a seller’s declaration made to the best of his knowledge, which notably states as follows,: - There are, or have been, water infiltrations in the basement; [2] - There is not nor has there been a foundation crack, rot or other problem with the basement structure; [3] - The house has a sump pump; to the question “How often does the sump pump operate?”, Defendant answered: “in the spring” [4] [ 9 ] Defendant adds the following statement in this declaration: “Periodically over the years there has been some minor humidity migrating through fieldstone foundation walls in spring thaw period. Given the year of construction the house does not have French drains, however several measures have been taken to reduce moisture near the foundation (eavestroughs, downspouts, grading, addition of dry wells, sump pumps, etc) - Humidity in recent years has been very minimal as a result.” [5] [ 10 ] Also prior to the sale, in October 2019, Plaintiffs commission a property inspection report from EXCEL HERON INC. (“Heron”). This report states that “Leakage into the basement is reported in the seller’s declaration.” [6] [ 11 ] It also contains the following remark, in relation to the basement sump pumps: “It is prudent to be aware of what causes these pumps to operate and how often they operate. Given that the basement is currently mostly unfinished, occasional seepage is not a major concern, if however, there is a desire to finish a portion (gym) then it is more important to reduce the risk.” [7] [ 12 ] In late March and April 2020, Plaintiffs witness the water infiltrations giving rise to their claim. Videos introduced into evidence show, during a few seconds, a sustained stream of water appearing to seep into the basement (or cellar) area from the bottom of the fieldstone foundation wall. [ 13 ] A buyer invoking the legal warranty of quality bears the burden of establishing that the defect satisfies all four following conditions : it must be of sufficiently serious nature; it must exist at the time of the sale; it must be unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale; it must be hidden at the time of the sale, such that cannot be perceived by a buyer acting in a reasonably prudent and diligent manner. [8] [ 14 ] After careful consideration of the evidence, this Court finds that said infiltrations do not constitute a latent defect within the legal sense. [ 15 ] Firstly, the existence of water infiltrations in the basement is disclosed by Defendant in his seller’s declaration. Moreover, the infiltrations experienced by Plaintiffs occur during the same period as the one disclosed in seller’s declaration, i.e. spring thaw period. [ 16 ] In essence, Plaintiffs plead that the severity of the infiltrations they sustained is greater than the ones sustained by Defendant, thereby transforming the more recent infiltrations into a hidden defect of their own. [ 17 ] The Court respectfully does not agree with this argument. [ 18 ] There is no objective measure of the extent of the seepage occurring after the sale as compared to the one occurring prior to the sale, a lthough Defendant acknowledges that the severity of the infiltrations sustained by Plaintiffs appears greater than that which he experienced as owner. [ 19 ] Failing such an objective measure, tracing a line in the sand between an infiltration amounting to a hidden defect and an infiltration amounting to an apparent defect would be an arbitrary exercise. [ 20 ] It should be noted that Defendant did not declare that water infiltrations had in fact ceased, only that they were “minor”. They were nonetheless still occurring, or susceptible to occur, at the time of the sale. [ 21 ] Although no expert evidence is offered at to the specific cause and source of the infiltrations, the aforementioned videos show that, in all probability, the infiltrations originate from structural cracks or interstices in the foundation walls. [ 22 ] A prudent and diligent buyer must follow through on clues or indications of a defect, and investigate the same further. [9] Water infiltrations which appear minimal on first impression may point towards a situation of greater concern. [10] [ 23 ] Plaintiffs did, in a commendable measure of prudence, implement actions recommended by Heron, such as to install new sump pumps. But there is no evidence that they commissioned a deeper, more specific, inspection of the condition of the foundation walls, despite their knowledge of pre-existing water migration “through [the] fieldstone foundation walls”. [ 24 ] In addition, Plaintiffs knew of the absence of a French drain, which is an additional clue or indication, as the same increases the risk of water pooling along the external face of the foundation walls. [ 25 ] Secondly, the case law establishes that ageing, wear or obsolescence of property, does not, in and of itself, amount to a sufficiently serious defect. [11] [ 26 ] In the case at hand, the foundation wall is about 100 years old. As the Superior Court reminds us: Le « travail et l’usure du temps » peuvent affecter gravement l’usage du bien sans nécessairement constituer un vice au sens de la loi.  L’acheteur d’une maison neuve s’attend à une maison sans malfaçons, point à la ligne. Par contre, les attentes légitimes de l’acheteur d’une maison centenaire ne peuvent être les mêmes.  Les normes et les règles de l’art en matière de construction ont évolué, le sol peut s’être tassé, les fondations avoir commencé à s’effriter ou se disloquer, les poutres à fléchir, à pourrir, la tuyauterie à couler, le filage électrique être inadéquat, bref la maison centenaire, comme les êtres vivants, porte l’outrage des ans […]. [12] [ 27 ] Consequently, as the infiltrations do not constitute a latent defect, Plaintiffs may not invoke the legal warranty against Defendant in respect of the same. b) Does the retaining wall decay constitute a latent defect ? [ 28 ] A retaining wall, about 50 centimetres in height, made of unmortared and unglued cement pavers, lies on Plaintiffs’ property, adjacent to the neighbour’s property line. A wooden fence sits atop it. [ 29 ] Plaintiffs argue that the deteriorating condition of such wall is a latent defect, as some pavers have given way and the wall is crumbling. [ 30 ] They acknowledge that, at the time of their pre-purchase visits, the wooden fence is visibly and markedly tilting away from Defendant’s property, but contend that the condition of the retaining wall underneath is then invisible to them as the latter is covered in snow. [ 31 ] According to his testimony, Heron did not inspect such portion of Plaintiffs’ property. [ 32 ] Plaintiffs’ contention is rebutted by Defendant, who testifies that they visited the property two times in late June and early July 2019. The condition of the retaining wall was thus visible by Plaintiffs prior to the sale. [ 33 ] The record contains photographs taken by Mr. Leclair in August 2020 [13] , which show that although observation is partially hindered by the neighbour’s overgrown cedar hedge, a visual assessment of the retaining wall can be performed with minimal effort. As no remediation work has yet then been undertaken, the Court believes that these photographs are a fair representation of the situation as it would have been visible by Plaintiffs in late June and early July 2019. [ 34 ] Although a significantly tilting fence is in itself an anomaly, Plaintiffs did not investigate the matter further, as they assumed, according to their testimony, that it was a “minor defect”, and nothing more than just a leaning fence. In so doing, they did not act as prudent and diligent buyers. [ 35 ] Although Plaintiffs allege that the wall condition doesn’t merely amount to an aesthetical damage, but rather constitutes a threat to integrity of the terrain, no expert evidence supports this assertion. [ 36 ] Therefore, the hidden, unknown and sufficiently serious nature of the defect has not been proven, such that this defect does not constitute a latent defect under law. [ 37 ] Finally, no fraud or intentional misrepresentation on the part of Defendant is demonstrated. [ 38 ] In conclusion, there is no denying that the aforementioned defects regrettably led to Plaintiffs to experience worries and bear unplanned remediation work. Nevertheless, the conditions which may give rise to Defendant’s legal liability are not fulfilled. WHEREFORE, THE COURT: DISMISSES the claim; WITH LEGAL COSTS awarded in favour of the Defendant. Philippe de Grandmont, J.C.Q. NOTICE TO THE PARTIES FILING OF PLEADINGS AND DOCUMENTS Code of Civil Procedure (CQLR, c. C-25.01 ), Art. 108 , 2 nd par.: Any document or real evidence that is filed in the record as an exhibit must remain in the record until the end of the proceeding, unless all the parties consent to its being removed. Once the proceeding has ended, the parties must retrieve the exhibits they have filed; otherwise, the court clerk may destroy them one year after the date on which the judgment becomes final or the date of the pleading terminating the proceeding. In either case, the chief justice or chief judge, if of the opinion that the exhibits can still be useful, may stay their destruction . [1] Civil Code of Québec, CQLR, c. CCQ-1991 (the “Civil Code”), art. 1726 [2] Exhibit P-3, s. D3.1 [3] Ibid ., s. D5.2 [4] Ibid ., s. D8.2 [5] Ibid ., s. D14 [6] Exhibit P-2, p. 4 [7] Ibid ., p. 5 [8] Civil Code, art. 1726 ; Leroux c. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 , par. 40 ; cf. aussi Verville c. 9146-7308 Québec inc. , 2008 QCCA 1593 , par. 37 ; ABB Inc. c. Domtar lnc. , 2007 CSC 50 , par. 50 ; Oluwasola c. Marcelin , 2023 QCCQ 6723 , par. 14 [9] Thériault c. Martin , 2020 QCCA 1294 , par. 10 [10] Lupien-Pilon c. Daneault , 2015 QCCS 6146 , par. 283 ; Boudreault c. Mailloux , [2004] R.D.I. 423 (C.S.), par. 65 ; Dumas Roy c. Lagacé , 2022 QCCQ 3264 , par. 31 [11] Ajram c. Dadoun , 2023 QCCS 539 , par. 23 [12] Vermette c. Boisvert , 2017 QCCS 3159 , par. 50 [13] Exhibit P-19...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq688-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.028431374579668045, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq688-en\", \"title\": \"Leclair c. Baker, 2024 QCCQ 688 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq688-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.028431374579668045, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq688-en\"}, \"token_count\": 2943, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq688-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2025qccs1525\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2025qccs1525-fr\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Valicenti c. Kulaveerasingam, 2025 QCCS 1525 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"=== INTRODUCTION ===\\n\\n[1]           Les demandeurs achètent au défendeur un bien résidentiel rénové en octobre 2017. Une importante infiltration d’eau se produit le 10 janvier 2018. Durant l’année suivante, les demandeurs effectuent d’importants travaux de réparation et de rénovation du Bien (tel que ce terme est défini ci-dessous).\\n\\n[2]           Les demandeurs affirment que les infiltrations d’eau ont été causées par des vices cachés dans les lucarnes. Ils affirment également que le défendeur a fait plusieurs fausses déclarations dans sa déclaration du vendeur, déclarations qui les ont incités à acheter le Bien. Ils demandent une indemnité financière d’un montant total de 417 232 $.\\n\\n[3]           Le défendeur affirme que les vices étaient apparents et que les demandeurs n’ont pas suivi la recommandation de l’inspecteur préachat de consulter un spécialiste en ventilation. Il fait valoir que les dommages-intérêts réclamés sont excessifs et couvrent des rénovations qui n’étaient pas nécessaires pour corriger les vices cachés ou réparer les dégâts d’eau causés par ceux-ci. Il soutient en outre que l’entrepreneur qui a construit les lucarnes, la défenderesse en garantie, est responsable des dommages-intérêts subis par les demandeurs.\\n\\n=== TEXTE ===\\n\\n[4]           Pour les motifs suivants, le Tribunal constate que le Bien présentait de graves vices cachés au moment de la vente, que le défendeur connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer l’existence de la plupart des vices et qu’il a faussement représenté des faits importants aux demandeurs.\\n\\n[5]           Le Tribunal conclut donc que le défendeur doit assumer le coût des travaux nécessaires à la correction des vices cachés et à la réparation du dégât d’eau (soit la somme de 211 360,83 $) ainsi que des dommages-intérêts moraux (soit la somme de 15 000 $).\\n\\n[6]           L’action en garantie est accueillie partiellement et l’entrepreneur est condamné à rembourser au défendeur la moitié (50 %) des dommages-intérêts que ce dernier est condamné à payer aux demandeurs.  CONTEXTE\\n\\n[7]           Le 29 octobre 2012, Selliah Kulaveerasingam (le « Défendeur ») achète une maison située avenue A, à Montréal (le « Bien ») avec l’intention de réaliser une opération immobilière de vente-achat.\\n\\n[8]           À cette fin, il s’associe à MM. Joseph d’Angelo et Umberto Napoletano, des relations d’affaires qui ont rénové et revendu des biens immobiliers avec succès dans le passé. Il fournit les fonds nécessaires à l’achat et à la rénovation du Bien, et MM. d’Angelo et Napoletano supervisent les travaux. Les partenaires d’affaires doivent partager les bénéfices qu’ils espèrent réaliser lors de la vente du Bien.\\n\\n[9]           Les éléments de preuve documentaire indiquent que le Défendeur dépense plus de 350 000 $ pour effectuer les rénovations[1]. Lors de l’instruction, il témoigne avoir dépensé plus de 450 000 $.\\n\\n[10]        Le Défendeur et ses partenaires d’affaires modifient substantiellement le Bien. Une rallonge contenant une chambre principale est construite au-dessus du garage (la « Rallonge ») par la défenderesse en garantie Constructions Inox inc. (« Inox »). La Rallonge est pourvue de lucarnes. Inox ajoute également une lucarne dans l’une des chambres d’origine située à l’arrière du Bien.\\n\\n[11]        Les plans de l’architecte prévoient que les toitures des nouvelles lucarnes seront des « toitures chaudes[2] ». Dans une « toiture chaude », la cavité de la toiture est remplie de polyuréthane pulvérisé et est complètement étanche à l’air. Elle ne nécessite pas de ventilation, et par conséquent n’est pas pourvue de soffites.\\n\\n[12]        Cependant, les lucarnes n’ont pas été construites selon le plan. Le représentant d’Inox, M. Patrick Kearny, témoigne que pour réduire les coûts, les toitures des lucarnes ont été pourvues d’un isolant ordinaire et celles-ci ne sont pas étanches à l’air. Comme nous le verrons plus loin, cela crée un problème de ventilation dans les nouvelles lucarnes.\\n\\n[13]        Le Défendeur met le Bien en vente en avril 2013. Entre 2013 et 2017, il met la maison sur le marché et la retire du marché pas moins de six (6) fois[3]. La situation financière du Défendeur est difficile pendant cette période et il souhaite vendre la maison et récupérer l’argent qu’il a investi dans le projet.\\n\\n[14]        En janvier 2014, une infiltration d’eau se produit dans l’une des lucarnes de la Rallonge, à l’arrière de la chambre principale. Le Défendeur témoigne que MM. d’Angelo et Napoletano corrigent le problème et qu’il n’y a pas d’autres infiltrations d’eau. Cependant, il n’est pas en mesure de décrire les travaux effectués à ce moment et ne transmet aucune facture pour des travaux ou des matériaux.\\n\\n[15]        Le Défendeur n’est toujours pas en mesure de vendre le Bien. Il loue la maison à un locataire pour quelques mois en 2014. Il décide finalement d’y emménager, en août 2016. Il habite dans la maison lorsque Jonathan Valicenti et Maria Sparagis (les « Demandeurs ») achètent le Bien en octobre 2017.\\n\\n[16]        Les Demandeurs sont tous deux des chefs d’entreprise prospères. En septembre 2017, ils vivent dans un appartement d’une chambre à coucher dans le Vieux-Montréal. Ils attendent leur premier enfant et décident d’acheter un logement plus grand dans un quartier leur permettant de se rapprocher de leur famille.\\n\\n[...]\\n\\n[22]        Le 29 septembre 2017, MM. Mello et Despins remettent un rapport écrit exposant leurs observations et recommandations (le « Rapport d’AmeriSpec[6] »). Le Rapport d’AmeriSpec est analysé plus en détail ci-dessous. Le rapport signale des problèmes de moisissures ainsi qu’une ventilation déficiente dans la partie principale des combles et recommande un examen plus poussé. Il ne mentionne pas les lucarnes.\\n\\n[23]        Dès la réception du Rapport d’AmeriSpec, les Demandeurs négocient une réduction du prix, soit une somme forfaitaire de 9 000 $, afin de tenir compte des conclusions et recommandations suivantes des inspecteurs : 1) les moisissures dans les combles doivent être éliminées; 2) la pompe à chaleur doit être remplacée; 3) une partie du plancher de la véranda doit être refaite; 4) une partie de l’aménagement paysager doit être refaite.\\n\\n[24]        Un avenant à la promesse d’achat est signé le 3 octobre 2017 sur les lieux du Bien[7]. Les Demandeurs allèguent qu’à cette occasion, le Défendeur les rassure quant à l’état du Bien. Le Défendeur leur remet sa carte professionnelle, qui indique qu’il est entrepreneur général et fabricant d’armoires de cuisine par l’intermédiaire de sa société, Armoires de cuisine Castel inc. (« Castel[8] »). Selon les Demandeurs, le Défendeur affirme que le plancher de la véranda n’est pas un problème majeur et qu’il suffit de changer quelques lattes. Le Défendeur nie avoir fait de telles déclarations.\\n\\n[25]        Les Demandeurs achètent le Bien le 16 octobre 2017 au prix de 1 160 000 $. Le Bien est vendu avec la garantie légale contre les vices cachés[9].\\n\\n[26]        Les Demandeurs prennent possession des lieux le 1er novembre 2017. Ils n’ont pas l’intention d’emménager avant le printemps suivant, car leur enfant est attendu en janvier 2018 et leur bail pour l’appartement du Vieux-Montréal court jusqu’en mars de la même année. Ils prévoient d’effectuer des rénovations mineures en attendant.\\n\\n[27]        En novembre, les Demandeurs retiennent les services d’un technologue en architecture, M. Maxime Lacasse, pour repenser la conception du foyer.\\n\\n[28]        Début décembre, Espace RénoVert leur transmet un devis pour enlever les moisissures dans les combles et désobstruer les soffites[10]. Ils prévoient d’effectuer les travaux à la fin du mois de janvier, après l’accouchement de Mme Sparagis.\\n\\n[...]\\n\\n[38]        Les Demandeurs réclament en outre la somme de 23 600 $ pour le loyer qu’ils ont dû payer pour se loger pendant les rénovations (les « Coûts de relogement ») et la somme de 20 000 $ à titre de dommages-intérêts moraux.\\n\\n[39]        Le Défendeur soutient que les vices qui ont causé l’infiltration d’eau ont été divulgués dans le Rapport d’AmeriSpec. Il soutient que la demande des Demandeurs est nettement exagérée et abusive et demande le remboursement des frais d’avocat qu’il a engagés pour se défendre contre l’action en justice intentée par ces derniers.\\n\\n[40]        Le Défendeur appelle Inox en qualité de défenderesse en garantie. Il soutient qu’Inox est responsable des vices de construction des lucarnes qui ont été ajoutées au Bien en 2013. Inox n’a présenté aucun élément de preuve ni aucun argument lors de l’instruction, mais son représentant, M. Patrick Kearny, a été appelé à témoigner par les Demandeurs. DISCUSSION\\n\\n[41]        La demande des Demandeurs soulève deux causes d’action. La première est fondée sur la garantie légale contre les vices cachés de l’article 1726 du Code civil du Québec[14]. La seconde est fondée sur le dol au sens des articles 1401 et 1407 du Code civil du Québec. I.        Garantie de qualité (art. 1726 C.c.Q.)\\n\\n[42]        La garantie de qualité impose au vendeur de garantir que le bien vendu est exempt de vices cachés. Il est codifié à l’article 1726 C.c.Q., lequel est rédigé en ces termes : 1726. Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l’acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté, ou n’aurait pas donné si haut prix, s’il les avait connus. Il n’est, cependant, pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l’acheteur ni le vice apparent; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert.\\n\\n[43]        La notion de vice renvoie à une perte ou à un déficit d’usage du bien acheté, apprécié à la lumière des attentes raisonnables de l’acheteur[15].\\n\\n[44]        Quatre conditions doivent être réunies pour qu’un vice engage la garantie légale de qualité. Le vice doit (i) être grave (c’est-à-dire qu’il doit rendre le bien impropre à l’usage auquel il est destiné ou en diminuer l’utilité au point que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté à ce prix); (ii) avoir existé au moment de la vente; (iii) avoir été caché au moment de la vente (critère évalué objectivement); et (iv) avoir été inconnu de l’acheteur (critère apprécié subjectivement[16]).\\n\\n[45]        Si ces conditions sont remplies, l’acheteur a le choix des recours. Il peut demander la résolution de la vente ou, s’il préfère garder le bien, une diminution du prix de vente. En outre, il a droit à des dommages-intérêts si, et seulement si, le vendeur connaissait l’existence du vice, ou ne pouvait ignorer son existence, et a omis de le révéler[17].  A.   Vices graves  1)    Vices dans les combles et les lucarnes\\n\\n[46]        Le Tribunal estime que les infiltrations d’eau dans les lucarnes ont été causées par des vices de ventilation dans les combles et les lucarnes, et dont le facteur déterminant provient des vices de la toiture sur les lucarnes ou autour de celles-ci.\\n\\n[47]        Les Demandeurs ont appelé M. Lacasse en qualité de témoin expert. M. Lacasse est titulaire d’un diplôme en technologie de l’architecture obtenu au cégep de Saint-Laurent en 2014. Il détient une licence d’entrepreneur général et effectue des travaux de construction sous le nom d’Entreprise Générale M.L. M. Lacasse est un expert en technologie de l’architecture reconnu.\\n\\n[48]        M. Lacasse a témoigné que l’infiltration d’eau s’est produite dans les lucarnes et a été causée par les vices de construction suivants touchant la toiture et les lucarnes : 1) la toiture des lucarnes était inadaptée à leur pente (absence de membrane et choix de bardeaux inadéquat); 2) l’existence de divers vices de toiture autour des lucarnes et à la jonction avec la toiture principale; 3) les lucarnes présentaient des vices de construction (de l’air chaud circulait dans les lucarnes depuis les espaces de vie situés en dessous, il n’y avait pas de soffites permettant à l’air de circuler dans les lucarnes et le matériau isolant était tassé jusqu’au revêtement de toiture, restreignant davantage la circulation de l’air).\\n\\n[...]\\n\\n[58]        Le premier phénomène est la condensation. La condensation se forme lorsque l’air chaud s’accumule dans des combles non ventilés et rencontre la charpente de toiture froide. Par temps de gel, la condensation se transforme en glace. Une hausse soudaine de la température entraîne la fonte de la glace et l’infiltration de l’eau dans les plafonds et les murs de la maison. L’eau semble arriver de l’extérieur, alors qu’en fait elle s’écoule depuis l’intérieur de la charpente de toiture.\\n\\n[59]        Le deuxième phénomène est la création d’une « digue de glace » sur le toit. Ce phénomène se produit lorsque l’air chaud s’accumule dans les combles non ventilés et fait fondre la neige sur le toit. Lorsque la neige fondue gèle par la suite, elle crée une barrière de glace ou une digue de glace. Lorsque la température ambiante augmente, la neige au-dessus de la glace fond et l’eau s’accumule derrière la digue de glace. L’eau accumulée s’infiltre ensuite dans la maison, par diverses fissures et ouvertures dans la toiture extérieure. Cela se produira même si la toiture est correctement installée[21]. Une infiltration d’eau est évidemment plus importante en présence de vices de toiture.\\n\\n[60]        Le Tribunal accueille le témoignage de M. Guertin concernant ces causes d’infiltration d’eau dans le Bien. Son opinion est parfaitement cohérente avec les faits prouvés lors de l’instruction.\\n\\n[61]        Les éléments de preuve montrent qu’il y avait de graves problèmes de ventilation dans toute la charpente de toiture, qui comprend les lucarnes d’origine et celles qui ont été ajoutées par le Défendeur en 2013. Les problèmes de ventilation suivants ont été décelés : 61.1.        Les soffites de la toiture principale étaient obstrués (empêchant une bonne ventilation par les combles et par les évents de toit). 61.2.        La Rallonge ajoutée en 2013 n’était pourvue d’aucun évent de toit, et l’ouverture entre la toiture principale et la Rallonge était trop petite pour permettre une ventilation adéquate de cette partie des combles. 61.3.        Aucune des lucarnes n’était pourvue de soffites, ce qui empêchait une bonne ventilation à évent de toit travers les lucarnes, vers les combles et vers l’extérieur par les évents de toit. 61.4.        L’ouverture entre l’une des lucarnes ajoutées en 2013 et la partie principale des combles ne permettait pas une bonne ventilation de cette lucarne vers les combles.  61.5.        La laine isolante de toutes les lucarnes était tassée jusqu’au revêtement de toiture des lucarnes, ce qui ne laissait aucune lame d’air et empêchait une ventilation adéquate des lucarnes vers les combles.\\n\\n[62]        Ces problèmes de ventilation ont probablement conduit à l’accumulation d’air chaud à la fois dans les lucarnes et dans les combles, puis à d’importantes infiltrations d’eau en 2014 et en 2018, dues aux deux phénomènes déterminés par M. Guertin, à savoir la condensation et l’accumulation de glace sur le toit.\\n\\n[63]        En effet, les éléments de preuve montrent des conditions météorologiques extrêmes pendant ces deux années. Plus précisément, les températures étaient glaciales durant les mois de décembre 2013 et 2017 et au début des mois de janvier 2014 et 2018[22].\\n\\n[64]        Le gel a probablement provoqué une accumulation de glace sous la charpente de toiture (par condensation) et sur le toit (création d’une digue de glace). Les photos prises par M. Lacasse en janvier 2018 contenues dans son rapport témoignent de la présence d’une digue de glace[23].\\n\\n[65]        Lorsque la température extérieure a rapidement augmenté, en janvier 2014 et en 2018[24], la glace a fondu et l’eau s’est infiltrée dans la maison, tant de l’intérieur que de l’extérieur ou de la toiture.\\n\\n[66]        L’infiltration d’eau causée par la condensation dans la toiture ne provenait pas exclusivement des lucarnes. En effet, comme nous le verrons plus loin, il y avait des signes de condensation sous la partie du revêtement de toiture juste au-dessus des lucarnes, dans la partie principale des combles. L’eau provenant de cette zone s’est probablement infiltrée dans les lucarnes et, de là, dans les plafonds des lucarnes et les murs de la maison.\\n\\n[67]        Cet élément est confirmé par le témoignage de M. Jonathan Léon, représentant de l’entrepreneur général engagé par les Demandeurs durant l’été 2018 (Groupe Cartier). Il a témoigné que les matériaux de toiture étaient endommagés et qu’ils ont dû être enlevés jusqu’à huit pieds au-dessus des lucarnes. Cela confirme que la condensation et l’infiltration ont pris naissance au-dessus des lucarnes, du moins en partie, et pas seulement à l’intérieur et autour des lucarnes, comme l’a suggéré M. Lacasse.\\n\\n[68]        Le Tribunal constate également que l’infiltration d’eau résultant de l’accumulation de glace sur la toiture (digue de glace) a été causée par un problème de ventilation tant dans les combles que dans les lucarnes. En effet, les photos contenues dans le rapport de M. Lacasse montrent une accumulation de glace sur l’ensemble du toit, et pas seulement sur les lucarnes[25].\\n\\n[69]        Le Tribunal accueille donc le témoignage de M. Guertin et estime que l’infiltration d’eau a été principalement causée par la ventilation défectueuse des combles et des lucarnes, et non par les vices de toiture. Cependant, les vices de toiture ont contribué à l’infiltration d’eau. 2)    Autres vices\\n\\n[70]        Les rapports de M. Lacasse font état de deux autres vices de construction : un vice touchant le balcon situé au-dessus de la porte-fenêtre coulissante de la cuisine, dans le bureau de Mme Sparagis[26], et un second dans la véranda, où le sol est inégal et l’isolation insuffisante[27]. Il s’agit de vices graves qui déclenchent également l’application de la garantie de qualité. B.   Existence de vices au moment de la vente\\n\\n[71]        Le Défendeur ne prétend pas que les vices décrits ci-dessus n’existaient pas lorsqu’il a vendu le Bien aux Demandeurs.  C.   Vices cachés au moment de la vente\\n\\n[72]        Un vice caché est un vice que l’acheteur raisonnablement prudent et diligent n’aurait pas décelé au moment de la vente[28]. Cette norme est objective et les acheteurs ne sont pas obligés d’avoir recours à un expert[29]. Ils doivent procéder à un examen visuel sérieux et attentif du bien[30].\\n\\n[73]        En général, un examen attentif du bien ne nécessite pas d’entreprendre des travaux invasifs ou destructifs[31]. Cela dit, lorsqu’il révèle l’existence d’un problème potentiel, l’acheteur doit faire appel à un expert et procéder à une inspection plus approfondie[32], faute de quoi le vice peut être considéré comme apparent[33].\\n\\n[74]        Le comportement du vendeur est important pour évaluer si un acheteur raisonnable aurait pu détecter un vice au moment de la vente. Lorsqu’un vendeur fait des déclarations fausses ou trompeuses sur l’état d’un bien, l’acheteur peut être dispensé de l’obligation de procéder à des inspections complémentaires, même s’il y a des signes ou un problème potentiel[34]. Les déclarations fausses ou trompeuses du vendeur peuvent rendre juridiquement caché un vice qui aurait autrement été apparent[35]. 1)    Vices dans les combles et les lucarnes                                                  i)        Importants signes annonciateurs des vices\\n\\n[75]        Lors de l’inspection préachat effectuée par MM. Mello et Despins pour le compte des Demandeurs, il y avait d’importants signes annonciateurs des problèmes de ventilation dans la partie principale des combles et dans les lucarnes.\\n\\n[76]        Les inspecteurs ont noté que le revêtement de la toiture principale était noirci par de la moisissure juste au-dessus de la zone où les lucarnes d’origine sont reliées aux combles sous la partie principale de la toiture[36].\\n\\n[77]        Selon le témoignage de M. Guertin, que le Tribunal accueille, cette moisissure est un signe révélateur de la condensation qui s’est formée en raison de la ventilation insuffisante dans les combles, ce qui peut entraîner des infiltrations d’eau à l’intérieur de la maison, comme nous l’avons vu plus haut[37].\\n\\n[...]\\n\\n=== MOTIFS ===\\n\\nLe Tribunal ordonnera donc au Défendeur de rembourser la somme de 17 448,10 $ aux Demandeurs pour leurs frais de démolition. 2)    Rénovations et réparations par le Groupe Cartier\\n\\n[195]     Le Groupe Cartier a procédé à d’importantes rénovations du Bien, pour lesquelles les demandeurs ont payé la somme totale de 339 359,56 $ (soit 343 777,12 $[80] moins 4 417,56 $ pour les travaux de démolition dont il est question ci-dessus).\\n\\n[196]     Les Demandeurs concèdent qu’une partie des travaux effectués par le Groupe Cartier consiste en des améliorations du Bien, pour lesquelles le Défendeur ne peut être tenu responsable.\\n\\n[197]     En effet, les Demandeurs ont repensé l’aménagement de la chambre principale dans son intégralité et modifié substantiellement la salle de bain attenante, ils ont changé le bardage extérieur des lucarnes, ils ont remplacé deux portes-fenêtres coulissantes dans le salon par une porte surdimensionnée, etc.\\n\\n[198]     Les Demandeurs évaluent à 32 435,16 $ le coût des améliorations apportées au Bien qui ne sont pas liées à la correction des vices ou à la réparation des dégâts d’eau. Le Tribunal estime que ce montant est largement sous-estimé.\\n\\n[199]     L’examen du devis détaillé du Groupe Cartier[81] montre que leur demande comprend toujours des travaux qui n’étaient pas nécessaires pour corriger les vices cachés ou réparer les dégâts d’eau, en tout ou en partie.\\n\\n[200]     Par exemple, les Demandeurs réclament plus de 27 000 $ pour le coût du remplacement des matériaux d’isolation destinés à l’ensemble du deuxième étage[82]. Cependant, comme nous l’avons vu plus haut, les Demandeurs n’établissent pas qu’il était nécessaire d’enlever toute l’isolation.\\n\\n[201]     En outre, les matériaux d’isolation posés par le Groupe Cartier (cellulose et polyuréthane) semblent être de meilleure qualité que la laine isolante observée sur les photos du Rapport d’AmeriSpec.\\n\\n[202]     Les Demandeurs réclament également plus de 12 000 $ pour le coût des travaux électriques, y compris le nettoyage électrique complet du deuxième étage (« nettoyage électrique complet du 2e étage ») et le remplacement des lampes, des gradateurs, des interrupteurs et des prises dans l’ensemble du Bien[83].\\n\\n[203]     M. Leon a déclaré que le câblage électrique avait dû être remplacé au deuxième étage parce que les murs avaient été démolis. Cependant, les travaux de démolition menés par le Groupe Isakor et le Groupe Cartier étaient excessifs, comme nous l’avons vu plus haut.\\n\\n[204]     En outre, les Demandeurs n’établissent pas que les lampes, les gradateurs, les interrupteurs et les prises ont tous été endommagés dans la maison et qu’aucun d’entre eux n’a pu être récupéré. Le Tribunal n’est pas convaincu que les travaux d’électricité étaient nécessaires en conséquence directe et immédiate des dégâts d’eau ou des vices du Bien.\\n\\n[205]     Les Demandeurs réclament plus de 15 000 $ pour le remplacement des portes des chambres à coucher, de toutes les moulures du deuxième étage et pour repeindre l’ensemble du deuxième étage[84]. En revanche, il n’est pas prouvé que les portes des chambres aient subi de quelconques dégâts d’eau ni qu’il ait été nécessaire d’enlever toutes les moulures de l’ensemble du deuxième étage ni de repeindre l’ensemble du sol.\\n\\n[206]     De même, les Demandeurs réclament près de 8 000 $ pour le coût de la peinture de l’ensemble du premier étage du Bien (y compris le plafond cathédrale[85]) et près de 4 000 $ pour le remplacement des moulures de l’ensemble du rez-de-chaussée[86]. Cependant, il n’est pas prouvé que les moulures de cet étage ont subi des dégâts d’eau, sauf dans la cuisine, ni qu’il a fallu repeindre tous les murs et le plafond cathédrale.\\n\\n[207]     Les Demandeurs réclament une somme de près de 6 000 $ pour l’installation de nouveaux ventilateurs de salle de bain, d’évents et de siphons d’appareil sanitaire dans la salle de bain principale et la salle de bain des invités au deuxième étage, ainsi que pour le nettoyage des évents[87]. Cependant, rien ne prouve que ces éléments aient été endommagés ou qu’ils aient dû être remplacés ou nettoyés en raison de l’infiltration d’eau.\\n\\n[208]     Les Demandeurs réclament plus de 1 700 $ pour remplacer l’escalier entre la chambre principale et le palier du deuxième étage[88]. Toutefois, ces escaliers ne sont pas situés à proximité des lucarnes où s’est produite l’infiltration d’eau, et rien ne prouve qu’ils aient été endommagés.\\n\\n[209]     Les Demandeurs réclament près de 17 000 $ pour les travaux de menuiserie nécessaires à la reconstruction des lucarnes[89]. M. Lacasse a témoigné qu’il a entièrement repensé la conception des lucarnes; il a ajouté des soffites et créé un espace supplémentaire sous la toiture de celles-ci pour améliorer la circulation de l’air en changeant l’angle de leurs chevrons[90]. Il considère qu’il était moins coûteux de modifier les éléments structurels des lucarnes et de les transformer en « toitures froides », car cette option ne nécessite pas de pulvériser du polyuréthane, qui est plus coûteux que l’isolation par la laine.\\n\\n[210]     Cependant, l’expert du Défendeur, M. Guertin, a témoigné qu’il aurait été moins coûteux de conserver les lucarnes existantes telles qu’elles avaient été construites et de les réaffecter de manière à ce qu’elles puissent fonctionner comme des « toitures chaudes » conformément à leur conception d’origine[91]. Le Tribunal accueille le témoignage de M. Guertin, qui est un tiers désintéressé et indépendant, contrairement à M. Lacasse. Il va de soi que la modification de la structure des lucarnes a nécessité plus de travail et de matériaux de construction que la réutilisation des lucarnes existantes.\\n\\n[211]     Les Demandeurs réclament en outre plus de 50 000 $ pour la démolition et le remplacement de l’ensemble de la toiture principale du Bien et de la Rallonge[92]. Cependant, M. Léon a témoigné qu’il n’était nécessaire d’enlever seulement huit pieds de matériaux autour de chaque lucarne, ce qui aurait représenté environ 80 % de la toiture. Il n’était donc pas nécessaire de remplacer les 20 % restants de la toiture, laquelle avait été rénovée en 2013 et en 2017.\\n\\n[212]     Enfin, les travaux effectués par le Groupe Cartier comprennent la correction des vices dans la véranda, dont le Tribunal a constaté qu’ils étaient apparents au moment de la vente. Le Tribunal n’est pas en mesure d’évaluer le coût des travaux de la véranda à partir des devis transmis par le Groupe Cartier.\\n\\n[213]     Ces exemples montrent que la demande des Demandeurs est nettement exagérée et inclut le coût de travaux qui n’étaient pas nécessaires pour corriger les vices ou réparer les dégâts d’eau.\\n\\n[214]     Le Tribunal doit se prononcer sur le pourcentage de travail qui a été nécessaire pour corriger les vices ou réparer les dégâts d’eau. Sans l’aide d’experts, il est impossible de le faire avec précision. Le Tribunal doit faire une approximation.\\n\\n[215]     Compte tenu de l’ensemble des éléments de preuve transmis, le Tribunal estime qu’entre 50 % et 60 % des travaux effectués par le Groupe Cartier étaient nécessaires pour remédier aux vices et aux dégâts d’eau.\\n\\n[216]     Le Tribunal doit également tenir compte d’un facteur de dépréciation. Les Demandeurs ont acheté une maison qui avait été partiellement rénovée en 2013, soit cinq ans auparavant. Ils sont aujourd’hui propriétaires d’une maison qui a été entièrement rénovée avec des matériaux neufs pour la plupart. MM.\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2025qccs1525-fr\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 2844.0, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2025qccs1525-fr\", \"title\": \"Valicenti c. Kulaveerasingam, 2025 QCCS 1525 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app-uat.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2025qccs1525-fr\", \"@search.score\": 2844.0, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 2, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"lexical\", \"search_type\": null, \"kind\": \"keyword\", \"query\": \"(\\\"vice caché\\\" vente immobilière conditions caractère grave antérieur à la vente inconnu de l’acheteur réellement caché Cour du Québec 2025)\"}, {\"source\": \"lexical\", \"search_type\": null, \"kind\": \"keyword\", \"query\": \"(garantie de qualité vente immobilière Cour du Québec 2025)\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2025qccs1525-fr\"}, \"token_count\": 6966, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-uat.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2025qccs1525-fr\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq11512\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq11512-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Saleem c. Rosenthal, 2017 QCCQ 11512 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2017 QCCQ 11512 (CanLII) | Saleem c. Rosenthal | CanLII Saleem c. Rosenthal, 2017 QCCQ 11512 (CanLII) Date: 2017-09-15 File number: 500-32-149647-150 Citation: Saleem c. Rosenthal, 2017 QCCQ 11512 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/h6msb > Saleem c. Rosenthal 2017 QCCQ 11512 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-149647-150 DATE: September 15, 2017 ______________________________________________________________________ BEFORE THE HONOURABLE ENRICO FORLINI, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ AYESHA SALEEM and MOHAMMED SALEEM Plaintiffs v. HAIM ROSENTHAL Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Ayesha Saleem and Mohammed Saleem claim $10,922 from Haim Rosenthal on the grounds that the property they purchased from him was afflicted with a latent defect. Mr. Rosenthal contests the claim and argues that the alleged defect was apparent at the time of the sale. Moreover, he argues that the damages claimed are grossly exaggerated. Questions in Issue a) Was the water leak and infiltration originating from the hallway washroom an apparent defect at the time of the sale? b) If so, what damages, if any, are the plaintiffs entitled to? Context [ 2 ] By deed of sale entered into on August 26, 2015, Mr. Rosenthal sold to plaintiffs a residential property located in Dollard-des-Ormeaux for $410 000 (the “Property”). [1] The sale was made with legal warranty. [ 3 ] Prior to the sale, the Property had been listed for sale at $439 000. The Property is a split level detached home with notably two washrooms on the first floor: one in the master bedroom and one in the hallway. This litigation deals with the water leakage and infiltration from the hallway washroom. [ 4 ] The plaintiffs visited the Property twice prior to the purchase, namely on June 20 and on June 24, 2015. The seller’s declaration was remitted to the plaintiffs on June 24, 2015. [2] [ 5 ] On June 21, 2015, the plaintiffs made a promise to purchase the Property at $420 000. [3] On June 22, Mr. Rosenthal made a counter proposal of $425 000 which the plaintiffs accepted on June 24, 2015. [4] [ 6 ] Following the acceptance of the counter-proposal, the plaintiffs had the Property inspected by a home inspector. The plaintiffs also had in hand a pre-purchase inspection report of the Property prepared by Grimaldi Home Inspection dated April 11, 2015 which had been obtained by a prior potential purchaser. [5] [ 7 ] Based on the findings contained in the Grimaldi Inspection Report, Mr. Rosenthal agreed to lower the selling price of the Property to $410 000 and plaintiffs agreed to this revised price. [6] On August 26, 2015, the deed of sale was signed by plaintiffs and Mr. Rosenthal. [ 8 ] On August 28, 2015, the plaintiffs took possession of the Property. They moved in 3 days later. A few days after moving in, Mrs. Saleem noticed that whenever a shower was taken in the hallway washroom, water would seep onto the hallway floor and also down through the ceiling of the powder room located directly below the hallway washroom. [7] [ 9 ] Plaintiffs communicated with Mr. Rosenthal to inform him of this problem and he mandated a plumber to attend the premises and inspect the hallway washroom to determine if there was indeed a leak. [ 10 ] On September 3, 2015, the plumber, Sam Mirarchi, attended the Property to inspect the allegedly leak. [8] He replaced two faucet spindles and seats and also added silicone caulking to insure that there were no leaks. [9] [ 11 ] It appears that the problem persisted as by letter dated October 8, 2015, Mrs. Saleem sent Mr. Rosenthal a notice of defect in which she writes that the hallway washroom is still leaking despite the plumber’s repairs. [10] She includes with her notice an estimate for the repairs to the washroom in the amount $10,922.63. [11] [ 12 ] On November 4, 2015, plaintiffs filed with the Court of Québec, Small Claims Division, their application against Mr. Rosenthal. Analysis and Decision a) Was the water leak and infiltration originating from the hallway washroom an apparent defect at the time of the sale? [ 13 ] The plaintiffs’ claim against Mr. Rosenthal is based on the legal warranty against latent defects, a warranty which is included in the Deed of Sale. This warranty is set forth in article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec (C.C.Q.), which reads as follows: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. [ 14 ] To succeed on a claim alleging a breach of the legal warranty against latent defects, a plaintiff must meet and prove the following five conditions which flow from articles 1726 and 1739 C.C.Q.: 1. The defect must be sufficiently serious, in other words it must render the property unfit for the use for which it was intended or so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high of price if he had been aware of the defect; 2. The defect must be unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale; 3. The defect was unapparent at the time of the sale. An apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert; 4. The defect must have existed prior to the sale; 5. The buyer must give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. [ 15 ] In the present case, only the third of the above-mentioned conditions is in issue. Indeed, the proof reveals that the defect was serious, that it existed prior to the sale. Moreover, the plaintiffs gave written notice of the alleged defect to Mr. Rosenthal within a reasonable time after discovering it, namely roughly one month after the discovery of the defect. [12] [ 16 ] The issue that the Court must adjudicate and which Mr. Rosenthal raises in his defense is whether the problem related to the water leak and infiltration originating from the hallway bathroom was apparent at the time of the sale. [ 17 ] The evidence reveals that Mr. Rosenthal purchased the Property in July 2007. From 2007 until the sale to the plaintiffs in August 2015, four persons occupied the Property, namely Mr. Rosenthal, his spouse, his child and Mr. Rosenthal’s mother. The hallway washroom was only used by Mr. Rosenthal’s mother. Moreover, she was wheelchair bound and did not use the shower in this washroom.  The other inhabitants of the residence used the master bedroom washroom to bathe or shower. [ 18 ] Over that 8 year period during which he inhabited the Property, Mr. Rosenthal never witnessed water leaking form the hallway washroom into the hallway or down to the ground floor powder room. This explains why in Seller’s Declaration, there is no reference to any water infiltration emanating from the hallway washroom. [13] [ 19 ] Prior to purchasing the Property and during their inspections, plaintiffs had in their possession the Grimaldi Inspection Report. [14] [ 20 ] The Grimaldi Inspection Report indicates in regards to the hallway washroom the following: - ‘’Ceramic cracks noted. Repairs required . Caulk damage observed. Replacing with new caulking required to prevent water infiltration damage ’’ (page 6 of the Report); - The 2 pictures included at page 7 of the Report show arrows pointing to cracks in the ceramic tiles and missing caulking on the edge of the bath tub; - At page 38 of the report, the inspector writes: ‘’Ceramic cracks noted. Repairs required . Caulk damage observed. Replacing with new caulking required to prevent water infiltration and damage’’ . (underlining added) [ 21 ] Mr. Rosenthal visited the property and specifically the hallway washroom after the plaintiffs complained of the water infiltration. He noticed that the shower had been used, that no shower curtain was in place and that the floor was wet. [ 22 ] Mr. Mirarchi, the plumber, inspected the hallway washroom on September 3 to determine the cause of the water infiltration in the powder room directly below the hallway washroom.  He filled the tub with water to overflow and no leaks were observed.  He too noticed that no shower curtain was in place. [ 23 ] Plaintiffs argue that the cracks in the ceramic and the deficient caulking cannot be the origin of the water infiltration in the hallway and in the ceiling of the ground floor powder room because all leaks stopped after the plumber’s visit on September 3, 2015. However, this statement is contradicted by plaintiffs own evidence, since in their October 8, 2015 notice of defect sent to Mr. Rosenthal [15] , they write that the washroom is still leaking notwithstanding the repairs carried out by the plumber in September. This contradiction significantly weakens the plaintiffs’ credibility on the origin of the water infiltration [ 24 ] Based on the evidence adduced at trial, the cracks in the tiles and the damaged caulking of the hallway washroom are the most probable causes of the water infiltration they complain of. [ 25 ] While this constitutes a defect, the evidence reveals that it was nonetheless apparent to a prudent and diligent buyer when the plaintiffs visited the Property prior to the purchase. Not only was this defect apparent, the Grimaldi Inspection Report explicitly points out that the cracks in the ceramic tiles and the defective caulking (pages 6 and 338) and twice warns the buyer to repair this to prevent water infiltration damage. [ 26 ] There were sufficient red flags raised by the Grimaldi Inspection Report such that a prudent and diligent buyer would have carried out additional investigations to assess the seriousness of the cracks in the ceramic tiles and the damaged caulking. [16] [ 27 ] The plaintiffs did not heed the inspector’s warnings and what he anticipated eventually happened.  They cannot look to their seller for compensation. [ 28 ] Considering that the defect the plaintiffs complain was an apparent defect at the time of the sale, it is not covered by the legal warranty against latent defects. Mr. Rosenthal cannot be held responsible for the damages which may have been caused by this apparent defect. b) What damages, if any, are the plaintiffs entitled to? [ 29 ] Considering that the Court has concluded that the conditions required to trigger the warranty against latent defect have not been met by the plaintiffs, the Court need not address the second and last question in issue. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 30 ] DISMISSES the plaintiffs’ application; [ 31 ] WITH COSTS of $190 payable to Haim Rosenthal, representing the Court fees paid on the filing of the Contestation. __________________________________ ENRICO FORLINI, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: May 16, 2017 [1] D-6: Deed of Sale. [2] D-1: Seller’s Declaration. [3] D-2: Promise to purchase and Counter proposal ( en liasse) . [4] D-2: Promise to purchase and Counter proposal ( en liasse ). [5] D-3: Grimaldi Inspection Report. [6] D-4: Mandatory form amendment #67423. [7] P-7: See photos filed en liasse . [8] See statement in lieu of testimony signed by Mr. Mirarchi. [9] D-5: Plumber’s invoice #5472. [10] P-1: October 8, 2015 Demand letter/Notice of defect and P-3: Screenshots from Mrs. Saleem’s smartphone relating to the transmittal of the Notice on October 8, 2015. [11] P-2: Estimate from Gestion CVA Plus inc. dated September 26, 2015 (also filed as exhibit D-6). [12] P-1. [13] D-1: the Seller’s Declaration does refer to water leakage from a toilet, which was repaired in 2008,but this is the master bedroom toilet and thus unrelated to the problem the plaintiffs complain of. [14] D-3: Grimaldi Inspection Report. See also Counter-Proposal to Purchase (D-2) which mentions that the plaintiffs received the Grimaldi Inspection Report. [15] P-1. [16] Italia v. Robitaille , 2012 QCCA 180 (CanLII) , paras. 33 & 35 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq11512-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02674258127808571, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq11512-en\", \"title\": \"Saleem c. Rosenthal, 2017 QCCQ 11512 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq11512-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02674258127808571, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq11512-en\"}, \"token_count\": 3111, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq11512-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau, 2019 QCCQ 1416 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2019 QCCQ 1416 (CanLII) | Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau | CanLII Date: 2019-02-28 File number: 455-22-004695-169 Citation: Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau, 2019 QCCQ 1416 (CanLII) Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau 2019 QCCQ 1416 COURT OF QUEBEC CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF BEDFORD TOWN OF COWANSVILLE Civil Division No: 455-22-004695-169 DATE: February 28, 2019 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE MARTIN TÉTREAULT, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ ALEXIS SPARLING and ANDREA SPARLING Plaintiffs v. DAVID MARCHAND-DUCHESNEAU And MAUDE TOUGAS Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiffs seek an order for reduction of the price sale and damages as a result of hidden defects and false representations by Defendants with respect to the sale of an immoveable property located in Mansonville (the “Property”). [ 2 ] The defects are: 1) absence of/or broken dispersal fields; 2) non-functional wells; 3) blocked french drain and 4) blocked gutters. [ 3 ] Defendants deny having provided false informations to Plaintiffs and submit that the defects are not covered by the warranty of quality provided by the Civil Code of Quebec (“CCQ”). CONTEXT [ 4 ] In the spring of 2013, Defendants decide to sale the Property that they had bought in 2007 [1] . [ 5 ] The Property, a 2.5 acres land on which a house was built in 1980, is located next to the Missisquoi North River [2] . [ 6 ] The listing mentions the following characteristics: “Système d’égoûts: Champ d’épuration, Fosse septique. Approvisionnement  eau: Puits artésien, Puits de surface [3] ” [ 7 ] The selling price is 165 000$. [ 8 ] On June 27, Plaintiffs visited the Property with Defendants’ real estate agent, Ms. Sandra Williams (“Ms Williams”). Defendants were not present. A Vendors’ Declaration signed by Defendants on March 12, 2013 was handed over at this visit [4] . [ 9 ] On the same day, Plaintiffs presented a Promise to Purchase to Defendants [5] . The offered price was 162 000$ and the sale was not conditional upon an inspection by a person chosen by the buyer. This offer was accepted by Defendants without any counter proposal. [ 10 ] On August 1, the parties signed the Deed of Sale [6] . Plaintiffs took possession the same day. [ 11 ] On August 4, Defendant David Marchand-Duchesneau (“Mr Marchand-Duchesneau”) came to the Property notably to explain Plaintiffs how to run the Water Supply System (“Water Supply System”). The residence was equipped with two pressure tanks located in the basement which served the surface and artesian wells. Mr Marchand-Duchesneau showed Plaintiffs how to switch from the surface to the artesian well. [ 12 ] On March 9, 2014, the surface well ran dry. [ 13 ] Eigth days later, the artesian well also ran dry. [ 14 ] On March 25, a plumber came to inspect the wells in order to solve the problem. He adjusted the pump of the Water Supply System and the water came back [7] . [ 15 ] At the end of June, the Plaintiffs Andrea Sparling (“Ms Sparling”) noticed water entering into the basement from behind the dry wall in the laundry room. [ 16 ] On July 2, a contractor came to inspect the basement. He suspected that the french drains were blocked and should be excavated. He also noticed the absence of dispersal field which accounted for a strong odor of raw sewage. [ 17 ] The next day, Plaintiffs advised Ms Williams of the situation and asked her to contact the Defendants. [ 18 ] On July 4, Mr Marchand-Duchesneu visited the Property and found that the exit pipe for the french drain was disconnected. He denied any liability regarding this situation. [ 19 ] On July 18, Ms Sparling sent a Letter of Demand to Defendants concerning the absence of a leeching field and the defects affecting the french drains [8] . [ 20 ] Upon receiving this letter, Defendants came back to the Property and identified the surface well which was beside the site chosen for the proposed dispersal field. Based upon this information, Plaintiffs suspended their plans for the construction of a dispersal field. [ 21 ] On September 14, Ms Sparling took a water sample from the surface well and sent it to be analysed. The conclusion of the analysis was that the sample did not conform to the Quebec Government’s drinking water recommendations and that the water should be boiled for one minute before drinking [9] . It also recommended that the well be disinfected. [ 22 ] On September 24, Ms Sparling sent another Letter of Demand to Defendants concerning the water, the wells, the french drains and the gutters [10] . [ 23 ] At the beginning of October, a contractor proceeded to the hydro-fracturation of the artesian well and to its reconstruction with all necessary accessories [11] . [ 24 ] On November 9, the gutters and downspouts were replaced [12] . [ 25 ] Between November 17 and 24, a new septic system was installed [13] . [ 26 ] On December 23, a Water Treatment System was installed [14] . [ 27 ] In July 2016, Plaintiffs replaced the french drains [15] . [ 28 ] On July 22, the Application for damages was served upon Defendants. ISSUES IN DISPUTE [ 29 ] 1. Are the defects affecting the Property covered by the legal warranty of quality? If so, what amount should be paid by the Defendants for these defects? [ 30 ] 2. Did Defendants make any false or misleading representations to Plaintiffs concerning the Property sold? If so, are they liable for the defects affecting the Property and what is the amount of damages owed to Plaintiffs? ANALYSIS THE LAW [ 31 ] Section 1726 of the CCQ describes the warranty of quality of the seller: “ 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. » [ 32 ] In their book on Contract Law in Quebec, Pr Campagnolo, Debruche and Grammond, summarize the elements to be proven under this section : « 665. Four elements are required to prove a latent defect, triggering the responsibility of the seller: (i) the seriousness of the defect; (ii) the anteriority of the defect to the sale; (iii) that the buyer was unaware of the defect; and (iv) and the occult (or hidden) nature of the defect. 1223 […] 667. Gravity, or the seriousness of the defect, is measured according to the consequences it entails, which must not be insignificant. In the context of the sale of a house, a window that does not close properly, for example, does not qualify. 1226 Only defects which meet the requirements of art. 1726 C.C.Q. give rise to the warranty, such that either the buyer would not have agreed to the sale had he known about the defect, or would not have paid as high a price. The case law provides many examples, ranging from defective roofs to rotten flooring and insulation, cracked foundations causing [Page 279] leaks, structural defects affecting the stability of buildings and infestations by insects and rodents. 1227 […] 668. The notion of anteriority to the sale is confirmed by the wording of art. 1726(1) C.C.Q., which states that the warranty applies to defects “at the time of sale.” It is important to note here that precedence to the sale does not mean that the defect had to manifest itself prior to the transfer of property. 1229 Rather, to trigger the warranty, it is sufficient that a defect was gestating at the time of the sale and only manifested itself once in the hands of the buyer. 669. In order not to give rise to the third condition of a latent defect – the buyer’s unawareness of the defect – it is common practice, stemming back to Roman Law, that the seller of a property fill out a declaration, informing the buyer of all defects affecting the property. 1230 Thus, the buyer may not invoke the warranty of quality against a seller who has revealed a defect in his declaration. Moreover, the entirety of the defect must be revealed. 1231 It is not enough for a seller to simply gloss over a problem, without getting into the details. This would not fulfill the seller’s duty to inform. 670. Finally, arguably most essential to the notion of latent defect, is the hidden nature of the defect. Despite common misconceptions about the necessity of an inspection by an expert (e.g., architect or engineer) and the consequences associated with the failure to engage an expert, the Civil Code states, at art. 1726(2) C.C.Q., that “an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert.” In other words, while it is common practice and not unwise to do so, there is no legal requirement that a buyer have a property professionally inspected upon purchase in order to take advantage of the warranty of quality. Rather, what is necessary, and continuously enforced by the courts, is that purchasers must be prudent and diligent in their dealings, taking care to carry out a careful examination of the property they wish to buy. The defect that is not noticed by the careful buyer is therefore a hidden defect. 1232 » [16] APPLICATION TO THIS CASE [ 33 ] Since Plaintiffs have raised different defects, we will adress them respectively. 1. Dispersal field [ 34 ] Initialy, Plaintiffs were pretending that the Property had no dispersal field. [ 35 ] However, after they received the proof that a septic tank and sweeping bed had been installed in 1988 [17] , they amended their proceeding to claim that it was not functional and non-conforming. [ 36 ] As mentioned at the trial by Mr Mike Mierzwinski, the contractor in excavation who was hired by Plaintiffs to install the Enviroseptic System, the old system was not operating correctly. The sewage was sent directly from the house to two culvert pipes with no bottom buried in the backyard [18] . Mr Mierzwinski added that the presence of green grass in this area was a proof of a leaking problem. [ 37 ] Under these circonstances, the Court comes to the conclusion that the dispersal field was affected by a serious defect which existed at the time of the sale and was unknown by Plaintiffs. [ 38 ] However, this defect cannot be qualified as hidden since it could have been perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer. [ 39 ] In a recent case, the Court of Appeal described the notion of « prudent and diligent buyer » [19] : « [7] Un acheteur prudent et diligent se doit de faire une inspection de l’immeuble qu’il se propose d’acquérir. Cet examen doit être attentif et sérieux [3] . En présence d’un indice de nature à soulever chez l’acheteur un soupçon quant à la présence de vices, il est tenu de pousser l’examen [4] . Il se doit de vérifier de façon satisfaisante ce qui est suspect [5] . Il n’est toutefois pas tenu d’ouvrir les murs et de creuser les fondations [6] ni de faire appel à un expert [7] . De plus, si le vendeur dénonce des problèmes existants, l’acheteur se doit d’en faire un examen approprié [8] . S’il n’a pas les connaissances nécessaires pour écarter la possibilité que le bien soit affecté d’un vice, l’acheteur doit obtenir une aide spécialisée. Dans ce cas, s’il omet de demander de l’aide, il agit de manière négligente et ne satisfait pas à la norme de l’acheteur prudent et diligent [9] . [ 8 ] L’âge du bien doit être tenu en compte. Plus le bien est âgé, plus s’intensifie l’obligation d’effectuer un examen approfondi. Ainsi, plus un vice se manifeste par des signes précurseurs, plus il doit être qualifié d’apparent. Si son existence n’est révélée que par des travaux exceptionnels, par exemple d’excavation ou de démolition, il s’agit d’un vice caché [10 ] » [20] [ 40 ] Here, Plaintiffs made an offer on the Property after a 30 to 60 minutes visit with Defendant’s real estate agent. Their offer was not conditional upon an inspection by a building inspector despite the fact that they had no knowledge in construction or renovation work or in septic system. Moreover, they knew that the house had been built in the 1980’s. [ 41 ] At the hearing, Ms Sparling explained that she had driven by the Property in 2008 and had loved the sight, the place and its location. Her visit in 2013 confirmed her feelings of 2008. Since the Declaration did not mention any problem with the septic system and she knew that the financial institution which would lend the money would rely on a evaluator before grantig the loan, she thought, mistakenly, that it was not necessary to proceed with an inspection by a building inspector. [ 42 ] Since Mr Sparling had never owned the Property, he relied on his mother for the sale and did not notice anything special when he visited the Property. At that time, the field in the back was overgrown with flowers so he did not notice any bad odors. It’s only after he cleared up the area after the sale that he started noticing the odors and the dampness in the area were the sewage was leaking. [ 43 ] In view of 1) the age of the house; 2) the mention on the Declaration that Defendants had no knowledge of the installation date of the septic system; and 3) their ignorance of such system, Plaintiffs should have conducted a more thorough inspection before buying. [ 44 ] As mentioned by Mr Mierzwinski, the mere presence of green grass in this area was a sign that the septic system had a leaking problem. [ 45 ] Plaintiffs’ claim under this item is therefore dismissed. [ 46 ] The Court adds that should it have granted this part of the claim, an important depreciation would have been applied since the system had been installed in 1988. 2. Water Supply System [ 47 ] Plaintiffs claim that insufficiency of water and the problems affecting its drinkability are hidden defects. Whether under the warranty of quality or because of false or misleading informations on their part, Defendants should be liable for the costs required to correct these problems. [ 48 ] These costs include tests, intervention by plumber, inspections, hydro-fracturation, installation of water treatment system and replacement of a hot water tank. [ 49 ] For the following reasons, the Court comes to the conclusion that this part of the claim should be dismissed. [ 50 ] As per the insufficiency of water, the claim refers to an incident that ocurred in March 2014, when the surface and the artesian wells ran dry. [ 51 ] At the hearing, the technician who inspected the wells, Mr Richard Giard, explained that the insufficiency of water was caused by a damaged pump. When he replaced it, the problem was solved. According to Mr Giard, it was fair wear and tear. [ 52 ] Though Defendants may not have been totally transparent when they mentionned in their Declaration that they never had any problem with the quantity of water, Mr Giard’s testimony confirms that the March 2014 incident was not related to the quantity of water in the wells. [ 53 ] Moreover, Plaintiffs never verified, prior to the sale, the age of the water supply system. [ 54 ] Since fair wear and tear is not covered by the warranty of quality, this part of the claim must be dismissed. [ 55 ] As per the quality problem, the water supply analysis done in September 2014 is clear : the water sample provided by Ms Sparling showed that it was unfit for consumption [21] . [ 56 ] Even if this type of defect is serious, the proof does not show that it existed at the time of the sale. [ 57 ] Defendants testified that they never had any problem with the drinkability of the water. [ 58 ] There is no proof that Plaintiffs had any problem prior to the reception of the Certificate of analysis in March 2014. [ 59 ] Under these circunstances, this part of the claim must also be dismissed. [ 60 ] As per the hot water tank, no proof was provided that the replacement of the hot water tank was caused by a latent defect. 3. French drains [ 61 ] According to Plaintiffs, Defendants should be liable for the replacement costs of the french drains on the Property. [ 62 ] As mentioned by Mr Mierzwinski in his testimony, a french drain was found while digging to replace the sewer pipe. The french...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n4. [ 59 ] Under these circunstances, this part of the claim must also be dismissed. [ 60 ] As per the hot water tank, no proof was provided that the replacement of the hot water tank was caused by a latent defect. 3. French drains [ 61 ] According to Plaintiffs, Defendants should be liable for the replacement costs of the french drains on the Property. [ 62 ] As mentioned by Mr Mierzwinski in his testimony, a french drain was found while digging to replace the sewer pipe. The french drain had an outlet and was not evacuating any water. He did not give is opinion as to the reason for the replacement of the french drain because he had not filed an expert’s report. [ 63 ] In fact, no expert report was filed by any party involved in this litigation. [ 64 ] Aside the fact that a part of the french drain was blocked, there is no proof as to the impact this problem had on the Property. [ 65 ] Therefore, even if this Court came to the conclusion that it was in fact a defect, there is no proof as to it’s seriousness. [ 66 ] Moreover, the Declaration mentions that there had been no modification to the french drains [22] . Since the house was built in the 1980’s, Plaintiffs should have been more careful on this aspect when they bought the Property. [ 67 ] Under these circonstance, this part of the claim is also dismissed. 4. The gutters [ 68 ] Plaintiffs alleged that the absence of gutters is a latent defect and that Defendants should be liable for the cost related to their installation. [ 69 ] The absence of gutters was clearly visible at the time Plaintiffs made their offer. Therefore, this defect can not be considered as hidden. [ 70 ] This part of the claim must also be dismissed [ 71 ] FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT : [ 72 ] DISMISSES Plaintiffs’ Re-amended originating application for damages resulting from hidden defects ; [ 73 ] WITH COSTS against Plaintiffs. __________________________________ Martin Tétreault, J.C.Q. Me Thomas A. Lavin Lavin Gosselin Avocats inc. Attorneys for Plaintiffs Me Joannie Tardif LB Avocats inc. Attorneys for Defendants [1] Exhibit PG-2. [2] Exhibit P-1. [3] Exhibit P-1. [4] Exhibit P-2 (the « Declaration »). [5] Exhibit P-3, p. 5 (the « Promise »). [6] Exhibit P-5. [7] Exhibit P-6. [8] Exhibit P-8. [9] Exhibit P-9. [10] Exhibits P-10. [11] Exhibit P-11. [12] Exhibit P-16. [13] Exhibit P-17. [14] Exhibit P-20. [15] Exhibit P-22. [16] Yan CAMPAGNOLO, Anne-Françoise DEBRUCHE & Sébastien GRAMMOND, Québec Contract Law , 2 e éd., Montréal, Wilson et Lafleur, 2016, par. 665, 667-670. [17] Exhibit P-23. [18] Exhibit P-24, pictures #5-13. [19] Rivard c. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 . [20] Id., par. 7-8. [21] Exhibit P-9. [22] Declaration, s. D8.3.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02767319232225418, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\", \"title\": \"Sparling c. Marchand-Duchesneau, 2019 QCCQ 1416 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02767319232225418, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\"}, \"token_count\": 4838, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq1416-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"--- BEGIN PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 0\\n2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) | ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. | CanLII Date: 2007-11-22 File number: 31176; 31177; 31174 Other citations: 287 DLR (4th) 385 — 369 NR 152 — 162 ACWS (3d) 1050 — AZ-50459657 — 36 BLR (4th) 1 — EYB 2007-126361 — JE 2007-2243 — [2007] SCJ No 50 (QL) — [2007] ACS no 50 Citation: ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII), [2007] 3 SCR 461 SUPREME COURT OF CANADA Citation: ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461, 2007 SCC 50 Date: 20071122 Docket: 31176, 31177, 31174 Between: ABB Inc. and Alstom Canada Inc. Appellants and Domtar Inc. Respondent and between: Chubb Insurance Company of Canada Appellant and Domtar Inc. Respondent and between: Domtar Inc. Appellant and Arkwright Mutual Insurance Company Respondent Official English Translation Coram: McLachlin C.J. and Bastarache, Binnie, LeBel, Deschamps, Fish, Abella, Charron and Rothstein JJ. Joint Reasons for Judgment: (paras. 1 to 121) LeBel and Deschamps JJ. (McLachlin C.J. and Bastarache, Binnie, Fish, Abella, Charron and Rothstein JJ. concurring) ______________________________ ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461, 2007 SCC 50 ABB Inc. and Alstom Canada Inc. Appellants v. Domtar Inc. Respondent ‑ and ‑ Chubb Insurance Company of Canada Appellant v. Domtar Inc. Respondent ‑ and ‑ Domtar Inc. Appellant v. Arkwright Mutual Insurance Company Respondent Indexed as:  ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. Neutral citation:  2007 SCC 50. File Nos.:  31176, 31177, 31174. 2006: November 8; 2007: November 22. Present:  McLachlin C.J. and Bastarache, Binnie, LeBel, Deschamps, Fish, Abella, Charron and Rothstein JJ. on appeal from the court of appeal for quebec Sale — Latent defect — Limitation of liability — Damages — Purchase of recovery boiler by paper manufacturer — Leaks and cracks in boiler’s superheater caused by tie welds connecting its tubes together — Superheater repaired but subsequently replaced by paper manufacturer with product supplied by another boiler manufacturer — Action in damages for latent defects and for breach of duty to inform buyer — Whether conditions for existence of latent defect are met — Whether clause limiting seller’s liability is applicable where seller knew or is presumed to have known about latent defect — Whether seller breached its duty to inform or buyer its duty to inquire — Whether buyer is entitled to be reimbursed for losses sustained with respect to repairs and to recover costs incurred to replace superheater — Whether insurer was subrogated to buyer’s rights in respect of payment made to buyer, and whether damages awarded to buyer should be reduced proportionally to that payment — Civil Code of Lower Canada, art. 1527. Sale — Duty to inform — Latent defect — Inclusion of seller’s duty to inform in warranty against latent defects. Domtar built a new pulp and paper mill in Windsor, Quebec.  In December 1984, it purchased a recovery boiler manufactured by C.E. (now ABB and Alstom) for $13,500,000.  The contract of sale included a clause limiting the seller’s liability.  The boiler’s design included a superheater with “H‑style” tie welds even though, at the time, C.E. was aware of problems resulting from the use of such welds and knew about the technology of hinge‑pin attachments.  In March 1989, 18 months after the boiler was put into service, tests by Domtar revealed some leaks and hundreds of cracks in the superheater’s tubes.  C.E. replaced a number of H‑style tie welds with hinge‑pin attachments, but there was disagreement as to a permanent solution to the problem.  In October 1989, at Domtar’s request, a competitor of C.E. replaced the three banks of the superheater with elements installed using hinge‑pin attachments.  Domtar brought an action in damages against C.E. that was based on the warranty against latent defects and the duty to inform.  It also sued a number of insurers, including Chubb and Arkwright, the former on the basis of a performance bond granted to C.E. for latent defects, and the latter under an “all risk” insurance policy held by Domtar.  However, Domtar discontinued its action against the insurer Lloyd’s pursuant to an agreement that included the payment of $1,578,900. The Superior Court rejected the latent defect claim, but found that C.E. had not discharged its duty to inform as regards the risks associated with tie welds.  It condemned C.E. to pay $13,366,583 in damages, less the $1,578,900 paid by Lloyd’s, on the basis that Lloyd’s was, as a result of that payment, subrogated to Domtar’s rights.  Given that there was no latent defect, the court dismissed the action against Chubb; it also dismissed the claim against Arkwright on the basis that the cost of replacing the superheater was not insured.  The Court of Appeal ruled that C.E. was liable on the basis of both the legal warranty against latent defects and the duty to inform.  It held that the amount paid by Lloyd’s should not be deducted.  It condemned the insurer Chubb, solidarily with C.E., to pay Domtar the amount provided for in the performance bond.  It affirmed the decision to dismiss Domtar’s action against Arkwright. Held :  The appeals should be dismissed. Given that all the facts alleged in support of Domtar’s action occurred before 1994, and in light of ss. 83 and 85 of the Act respecting the implementation of the reform of the Civil Code , the issues relating to the warranty against latent defects must be resolved by applying the Civil Code of Lower Canada (“C.C.L.C.”).  [30] In intervening, the Court of Appeal did not reassess the evidence in the record, but relied on the trial judge’s findings of fact to arrive at a different conclusion of law regarding the nature of the defect.  That conclusion is therefore not inconsistent with the principle of deference for a trial judge’s assessment of the facts.  Rather, the issue here is one of legal characterization.  [37] Article 1527 C.C.L.C. provides that any seller who knows or is legally presumed to know about the defects of the thing is obliged to pay for all damage suffered by the buyer.  In Quebec civil law, manufacturers are considered to be the ultimate experts with respect to goods because they have control over the labour and materials used to produce them.  Consequently, they are subject to the strongest presumption of knowledge and to the most exacting obligation to disclose latent defects.  The buyer’s expertise is also relevant as it serves to assess whether the defect is latent or apparent.  The more knowledge a buyer has of a good being purchased, the more likely it is that a defect in that good will be considered apparent.  Buyers therefore have an obligation to inform themselves by carrying out a reasonable inspection of the good.  The test is whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances could have detected the defect at the time of the sale.  However, the buyer’s expertise does not nullify the presumption applicable to the manufacturer.  The manufacturer will be unable to rely on a limitation of liability clause unless it can rebut the presumption of knowledge of the defect. [39] [41‑42] [44] Regardless of whether the defect is a material defect, a functional defect or a conventional defect, it must have four characteristics, all of which are essential to the warranty:  it must be latent, must be sufficiently serious, must have existed at the time of the sale and must have been unknown to the buyer.  All these conditions are met in the case at bar.  The trial judge erred in concluding that the cracking of the tubes was a design feature rather than a design defect.  First, he limited the definition of a defect to a problem preventing the good from being used at all and, second, he confused the sale of a lower‑performance version of a good with the sale of a defective one.  A defect will be considered to be serious if it renders the good unfit for its intended use or so diminishes its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it at the price paid.  Domtar would not have bought a boiler with H‑style tie welds if it had been informed of the risks associated with this type of attachment.  C.E. used these attachments without having independent analyses conducted to determine whether they could withstand the stress placed on the tubes by the circulation of steam at high temperatures.  It chose to rely on its customers’ assessments and delayed the adoption of hinge‑pin attachments in order to maintain its competitive position.  Domtar was unaware of the defect at the time of the sale because C.E. had not shared the information it possessed on this subject with Domtar.  Even though Domtar was described as a sophisticated operator, and however expert it was at using boilers, it cannot be characterized as a professional “of identical expertise” to C.E.  Nor is the defect apparent simply because Domtar was assisted by an expert.  The cause of the excessive cracking was unknown to both Domtar and its expert. [45] [50] [86‑88] [91] [93] [97] [99] [101] To rebut the presumption of knowledge of the defect provided for in art. 1527 C.C.L.C., it is never open to a manufacturer to rely on its ignorance of the defect as its sole defence.  The manufacturer must show that it did not know about the defect and that its lack of knowledge was justified, that is, that it could not have discovered the defect even if it had taken every precaution that the buyer would be entitled to expect a reasonable seller to take in the same circumstances. To absolve a manufacturer from liability will be justified only if the manufacturer shows that it had full knowledge of the technology in its field at the time the good was designed and that the defect in question cannot be attributed to it.  In light of the strength of the presumption of knowledge applicable to it, the manufacturer must meet a high standard of diligence, and the range of defences available to it to rebut the presumption is very narrow.  C.E.’s argument is based not on fault on the part of Domtar or a third party, or on superior force or development risk, but on its own good faith throughout its business relations with Domtar.  In this case, it can be seen from the evidence that C.E. had known about the problems associated with tie welds since the early 1980s and that a better technology was available to it as of that time.  [69‑72] [102] [104] The duty to inform and the warranty against latent defects are two concepts that overlap, but the former derives from the general principle of good faith, whereas the latter is expressly provided for in the C.C.L.C. and the Civil Code of Québec .   Furthermore, the scope of the general duty to inform is much broader than that of the disclosure of a latent defect.  The duty to inform is subsumed in the analysis of the seller’s liability for latent defects, and there is no need for the court to conduct a separate analysis on the duty.  [107‑109] Because a clause in the insurance contract between Lloyd’s and Domtar expressly excluded damage resulting from a breakdown of or defect in the recovery boiler, the payment made by Lloyd’s could not have been related to the damages being claimed by Domtar.  Lloyd’s was not, therefore, subrogated to Domtar’s rights and the damages claimed by Domtar should not be reduced by the amount of the payment. [113] Chubb agreed to guarantee the performance of C.E.’s obligations under the contract of sale for the recovery boiler. Since there was a latent defect in the superheater, Chubb is solidarily liable for the amount of its guarantee. [114] The “all risk” insurance policy issued by Arkwright for Domtar’s property contained a clause excluding damage resulting from latent defects.  To accept Domtar’s contention that the welds can be dissociated from the superheater would render the exclusion clause meaningless. [115] [119] Cases Cited Applied: Desgagné v. Fabrique de St‑Philippe d’Arvida , 1984 CanLII 129 (SCC) , [1984] 1 S.C.R. 19; Placement Jacpar Inc. v. Benzakour , 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , [1989] R.J.Q. 2309; Samson & Filion v. Davie Shipbuilding & Repairing Co. , 1924 CanLII 57 (SCC) , [1925] S.C.R. 202; Touchette v. Pizzagalli , 1938 CanLII 39 (SCC) , [1938] S.C.R. 433; Manac inc./Nortex v. Boiler Inspection and Insurance Co. of Canada , 2006 QCCA 1395 (CanLII) , [2006] R.R.A. 879; referred to: Marquis v. Saltsman , 2002 CanLII 41228 (QC CA) , J.E. 2002‑1729, SOQUIJ AZ‑50143509; Rousseau v. 2732‑1678 Québec inc. , [1999] R.D.I. 565 ; Société en commandite A.C. enr. v. Wadieh , 1997 CanLII 9977 (QC CA) , [1997] R.D.I. 345; Bertrand v. Pelletier , [1997] R.D.I. 321 ; Poirier v. Martucelli , [1995] R.D.I. 319 ; Trottier v. Robitaille , [1994] R.D.I. 537 ; Cloutier v. Létourneau , 1992 CanLII 3716 (QC CA) , [1993] R.L. 530; Rousseau v. Gagnon , 1986 CanLII 3706 (QC CA) , [1987] R.J.Q. 40; Auto Peliss ltée v. Proulx Pontiac Buick ltée , 2001 CanLII 5911 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 856; Garage Robert inc. v. 2426‑9888 Québec inc. , 2001 CanLII 9967 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 865; Trois Diamants Autos (1987) ltée v. M.G.B. Auto inc. , 2001 CanLII 39923 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 860; General Motors Products of Canada v. Kravitz , 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790; Oakwood Construction Inc. v. Ratthé , [1993] R.D.I. 181 ; Blandino v. Colagiacomo , 1988 CanLII 1193 (QC CA) , [1989] R.D.I. 148; Oppenheim v. Forestiers R.P.G.M. inc. , J.E. 2002‑1197, SOQUIJ AZ‑50133145 ; London & Lancashire Guarantee & Accident Co. of Canada v. Cie F.X. Drolet , 1944 CanLII 46 (SCC) , [1944] S.C.R. 82; Civ. 1 re , November 24, 1954, J.C.P. 1955.II.8565, obs. H. B.; Civ. 1 re , January 19, 1965, D. 1965.389, obs. Cornu; Com., November 6, 1978, J.C.P. 1979.II.19178, obs. Ghestin; Com., October 8, 1973, J.C.P. 1975.II.17927, obs. Ghestin; Civ. 3 e , October 30, 1978, J.C.P. 1979.II.19178; Tony’s Broadloom & Floor Covering Ltd. v. NMC Canada Inc. (1995), 1995 CanLII 7153 (ON SC) , 22 O.R. (3d) 244, aff’d (1996), 1996 CanLII 680 (ON CA) , 31 O.R. (3d) 481; Jenkins v. Foley (2002), 215 Nfld. & P.E.I.R. 257 , 2002 NFCA 46; Parlby Construction Ltd. v. Stewart Equipment Co. , 1971 CanLII 1426 (BC SC) , [1972] 1 W.W.R. 503; Guarantee Co. of North America v. Gordon Capital Corp. , 1999 CanLII 664 (SCC) , [1999] 3 S.C.R. 423; Hunter Engineering Co. v. Syncrude Canada Ltd. , 1989 CanLII 129 (SCC) , [1989] 1 S.C.R. 426; R. G. McLean Ltd. v. Canadian Vickers Ltd. , 1970 CanLII 386 (ON CA) , [1971] 1 O.R. 207; Bank of\\n\\n\\n--- END PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n... connected with the superheater did not constitute an insured loss: SOQUIJ AZ‑50181803 . 2.2 Quebec Court of Appeal 17 Although it did not question the trial judge’s findings of fact, the Court of Appeal ruled that C.E. was liable on the basis of the legal warranty against latent defects.  According to the Court of Appeal, the evidence clearly established that Domtar was looking for a reliable boiler that would operate without interruption, and that C.E. was aware of this.  Domtar would not have purchased this boiler had it known that the tie welds would cause unscheduled shutdowns.  The Court of Appeal then considered C.E.’s duty to inform.  Like the trial judge, it held that C.E. had breached its duty to inform Domtar. Since C.E. knew or was presumed to have known about the defect, it could not rely on the limitation of liability clause.  In the Court of Appeal’s opinion, Domtar was accordingly entitled to replace the entire superheater at C.E.’s expense.  However, the Court of Appeal disagreed with the trial judge on deducting the $1,578,900 paid by Lloyd’s.  It therefore allowed Domtar’s cross‑appeal and increased the quantum of the order against C.E. accordingly: [2005] R.J.Q. 2267, 2005 QCCA 733 . 18 The Court of Appeal condemned the insurer Chubb, solidarily with C.E., to pay Domtar $725,938.90 based on the performance bond: [2005] Q.J. No. 11601 (QL), 2005 QCCA 730 .  Finally, in the action against the insurer Arkwright, the Court of Appeal dismissed Domtar’s appeal on the basis of the clause excluding from coverage losses resulting from latent defects: [2005] R.R.A. 1046, 2005 QCCA 732 . 3. Analysis 3.1 Issues 19 In the main case, the parties put five questions to this Court.  First, was there a latent defect in the superheater?  Second, did C.E. breach its duty to inform or Domtar its duty to inquire?  Third, in what circumstances may a limitation of liability clause be set up against a buyer?  Fourth, is Domtar entitled to recover the costs it incurred to replace the superheater?  Fifth, was Lloyd’s subrogated to Domtar’s rights in respect of the payment it made to Domtar? 20 In the two incidental cases, the parties ask the Court to decide whether Domtar may require Chubb to execute its performance bond and whether the replacement of the superheater is a loss covered by Arkwright’s all risk insurance policy. 21 We will begin by summarizing the parties’ arguments and reviewing the most important of the relevant statutory provisions.  We will then turn to the questions of law raised in the three cases. 3.2 Arguments of the Parties in the Main Case 22 C.E. has changed its arguments since the case began.  At first, one of its main submissions was that the problems were due to improper use of the superheater.  This defence has been abandoned.  C.E. now submits that the rigidity of the attachments or the cracking inside the superheater does not constitute a latent defect.  It argues that Domtar, as an informed user of recovery boilers, cannot claim to have been unaware of the characteristics of tie welds and hinge‑pin attachments and that Domtar breached its own duty to inquire. 23 C.E. also submits that it was protected by a limitation of liability clause because Domtar had a power of negotiation equal to its own.  In C.E.’s view, the voluntary agreement of the parties must therefore prevail.  Finally, C.E. argues that Domtar failed to mitigate its damages and that the $1,578,900 payment by Lloyd’s to Domtar should reduce Domtar’s claim against C.E. accordingly. 24 Domtar defends the Court of Appeal’s conclusion that there was a latent defect in the superheater.  According to Domtar, C.E. knew or should have known at the time of the sale about the problem with the type of attachments to be used and should have proposed hinge‑pin attachments to Domtar.  It therefore breached its duty to inform with regard to the likelihood of leaks and cracks associated with the use of tie welds.  The decision to replace the superheater was reasonable in light of the information disclosed by C.E. 25 Before turning to the merits of the case, we must resolve two preliminary questions.  First, in light of the date and nature of the claim, is it the Civil Code of Lower Canada (“C.C.L.C.”) or the Civil Code of Québec , S.Q. 1991, c. 64 (“C.C.Q.”), that applies under the transitional law rules?  Second, did the Court of Appeal have jurisdiction to interfere with the trial judge’s conclusion that there was no latent defect? 3.3 Transitional Law 26 The main case concerns a contract of sale signed by the parties on December 31, 1984.  As mentioned above, the parties’ arguments require that this Court determine whether there was a latent defect in the superheater and, if so, whether C.E. may rely on a limitation of liability clause. 27 Section 83 of the Act respecting the implementation of the reform of the Civil Code , S.Q. 1992, c. 57 (“A.I.R.C.C.”), provides that the rules of the C.C.L.C. governing legal and conventional warranties continue to apply to a contract entered into before 1994.  It reads as follows: 83. In any contract made before 1 January 1994, the former legislation continues to apply to the warranties, both legal or conventional, to which the contracting parties are obliged between themselves or in respect of their heirs or successors by particular title. This provision represents a specific application of the general rule in s. 4 A.I.R.C.C. that the former supplementary legislation subsists for the purpose of determining the extent and scope of the parties’ rights and obligations and the effects of the contract: Commentaires du ministre de la Justice (1993), vol. 3, at p. 71. 28 Section 83 A.I.R.C.C. is complemented in civil liability matters by s. 85 A.I.R.C.C. which reads as follows: 85. The conditions of civil liability are governed by the legislation in force at the time of the fault or act which causes the injury. In his commentary on this provision (at p. 72), the Minister of Justice took care to specify [ translation ] that since the conditions of civil liability are thus governed by the legislation in force at the time of the injurious fault or act, the grounds for exemption from liability, which are necessarily tied to the conditions of that liability, will also be governed by that same legislation . . . . 29 Sections 83 and 85 A.I.R.C.C. belong to the chapter setting out special provisions ( ss. 11 to 170 ).  In the event of conflict, they take precedence over the Act’s general provisions (ss. 2 to 10):  P.‑A. Côté and D. Jutras, Le droit transitoire civil:  Sources annotées (loose‑leaf), at pp. I/3‑1 et seq . 30 In the case at bar, Domtar has brought against C.E. an action in contract for damages that is based on the warranty against latent defects.  All the facts alleged in support of this action occurred before 1994.  In light of ss. 83 and 85 A.I.R.C.C. , we conclude that in this case, the issues relating to the warranty against latent defects must be resolved by applying the C.C.L.C. 31 This being said, whether it is the C.C.L.C. or the C.C.Q. that is applied will have no impact on the outcome of the case, since the C.C.Q. essentially reproduces the C.C.L.C.’s rules where the warranty against latent defects in issue here is concerned, despite certain changes in the wording of the provisions relating to the issues of this case.  The relevant provisions are reproduced in the Appendix. 32 Finally, the issue of subrogation in respect of the payment made by Lloyd’s and the incidental actions involving Chubb and Arkwright do not raise any special problems of transitional law.  The answers to these questions depend essentially on a review of the stipulations in the contracts between the parties. 33 At this point, it will be necessary to consider whether the Court of Appeal had the authority to interfere with the trial judge’s finding that there was no latent defect.  C.E. contests the legitimacy of the Court of Appeal’s recharacterization of the defects as latent defects, which it sees as improper interference with the trial judge’s assessment of the facts. 3.4 Standard for Intervention by the Court of Appeal 34 In discussing the Court of Appeal’s position, it is important to clearly understand the nature of its intervention with regard to the facts.  Some fundamental distinctions must be drawn.  In Desgagné v. Fabrique de St‑Philippe d’Arvida , 1984 CanLII 129 (SCC) , [1984] 1 S.C.R. 19, this Court distinguished the simple assessment of facts from the legal characterization of those facts.  Thus, an appellate court has the power, in exercising its jurisdiction, to reach its own legal characterization of the facts even though it accepts the trial judge’s assessment of them.  Beetz J. stated the following (at p. 31): Counsel for the respondents and appellant Lauréanne Harvey Desgagné argued that the gradual appearance of the construction defects is a question of fact which is within the exclusive province of the trial judge.  That is not my view.  Rather, I think it is a question of characterization and so considerably more than a simple question of fact.  It is necessary to apply to the facts the legal concept of gradual emergence under art. 2259, just as, for example, in a civil liability case the Court has to decide whether a person’s act or omission should be characterized as fault within the meaning of art. 1053.  This requires making an essentially normative judgment.  It therefore does not entail substituting my own view of the evidence for that of the trial judge, but drawing conclusions in law based on the facts which she herself considered to have been established.  When an appellate court  accepts all the conclusions of fact as such made by the trial judge, as I do, it is in as good a position as he is to characterize those facts. 35 A few years later, in Placement Jacpar Inc. v. Benzakour , 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , [1989] R.J.Q. 2309, at p. 2318, the Court of Appeal confirmed that the legal nature of the distinction between a latent defect and an apparent defect is also a question of law: [ translation ] To [Benzakour], the characterization of a latent defect is essentially a question of fact.  With respect for the opinion expressed in Lafontaine v. Audet , in which this question was seen primarily as one coming within the trier of fact’s power of assessment [p. 8 of the reasons of Monet J.A.], this Court’s position appears instead to be that this is a legal characterization issue.  This Court is in as good a position as the trial judge to rule on it once the trial judge has established the facts on which the conclusions are based. See to the same effect: Marquis v. Saltsman , 2002 CanLII 41228 (QC CA) , J.E. 2002‑1729 (C.A.), SOQUIJ AZ‑50143509, at para. 51 ; Rousseau v. 2732‑1678 Québec inc. , [1999] R.D.I. 565 (C.A.), at pp. 568‑69 ; Société en commandite A.C. enr. v. Wadieh , 1997 CanLII 9977 (QC CA) , [1997] R.D.I. 345 (C.A.), at p. 348; Bertrand v. Pelletier , [1997] R.D.I. 321 (C.A.), at p. 325 ; Poirier v. Martucelli , [1995] R.D.I. 319 (C.A.), at p. 320 ; Trottier v. Robitaille , [1994] R.D.I. 537 (C.A.), at p. 538 ; Cloutier v. Létourneau , 1992 CanLII 3716 (QC CA) , [1993] R.L. 530 (C.A.), at p. 531; Rousseau v. Gagnon , 1986 CanLII 3706 (QC CA) , [1987] R.J.Q. 40 (C.A.), at p. 46. 36 In the instant case, the Court of Appeal began by noting that the parties were not contesting the trial judge’s findings of fact ( 2005 QCCA 733 (CanLII) , [2005] R.J.Q. 2267, at para. 42 ).  Thus, because tie welds were used, it was inevitable that cracks would develop, and probable that leaks would occur (paras. 100‑101). According to the Court of Appeal, the trial judge had not attached enough importance to Domtar’s intention to keep the recovery boiler in continuous use.  It noted that the trial judge had found even so that Domtar would not have purchased the boiler had it known that the tie welds were likely to cause unscheduled shutdowns.  Because the unscheduled shutdowns of the boiler resulted in an inability to use it, the Court of Appeal characterized this defect as a latent defect. 37 In intervening, the Court of Appeal did not reassess the evidence in the record.  Rather, it relied directly on the trial judge’s findings of fact to arrive at a different conclusion of law regarding the nature of the defect (at para. 99): [ translation ] The trial judge did not find that there was a latent defect in the equipment sold.  With respect, we are of the opinion that his findings of fact should have led him to an affirmative conclusion on this point.  [Emphasis added.] This conclusion is not inconsistent with the principle of deference for a trial judge’s assessment of the facts.  Rather, the issue here is one of legal characterization, and therefore a question of law.  Consequently, the Court of Appeal had the power to vary the trial judge’s finding on the existence of a latent defect.  Now that these preliminary issues have been settled, we will turn to the issue of the legal warranty against latent defects in a contract of sale governed by the C.C.L.C. 3.5 Effect of the Status of the Manufacturer and the Buyer on Their Obligations Under the Contract of Sale 38 In the case at bar, C.E. argues that a separate scheme must govern contracts of sale between professional sellers and professional buyers.  In its view, the seller’s warranty against latent defects should vary depending on the respective expertise of the seller and the buyer.  It will therefore be necessary to consider the legal effect of this expertise on rights and obligations under a contract of sale. 39 Where the warranty against latent defects is concerned, the characterization of a party as a manufacturer or a professional seller plays an important role in determining whether that party can be presumed to have known about the defects in the good offered for sale.  Article 1527 C.C.L.C. provides that any seller who knows or is legally presumed to know about the defects of the thing is obliged to pay for all damage suffered by the buyer. This provision has been interpreted in Quebec civil law as recognizing three categories of sellers corresponding to the individual’s level of expertise: manufacturers, professional sellers (specialized or non‑specialized) and non‑professional sellers. 40 This categorization will determine whether the presumption of knowledge on the seller’s part is applicable and will also, as a corollary, determine the scope of the seller’s duty to disclose latent defects.  Thus, a non‑professional seller is not legally presumed to know about the defects of the good being sold, since selling the good in question is not this person’s usual occupation.  Professional sellers, however, being far more aware of the characteristics of their merchandise, are subject to the presumption of knowledge. 41 In the case at bar, the category of sellers that interests us most is that of the manufacturer.  Manufacturers are considered to be the ultimate experts with respect to the goods, because they have control over the labour and materials used to produce them:  J. Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois (1998), at p. 289.  Moreover, buyers are entitled to expect that manufacturers guarantee the quality of the products they design and market.  Consequently, manufacturers are subject to the strongest presumption of knowledge and to the most exacting obligation to disclose latent defects. 42 In the context of the warranty against latent defects, the buyer’s expertise is also relevant to the analysis, but for a different purpose than the seller’s expertise.  Whereas the seller’s expertise serves to determine the scope of his or her obligation to disclose, that of the buyer serves, rather, to assess whether the defect is latent or apparent.  Thus, the more knowledge a buyer has of a good being purchased, the more likely it is that a defect in that good will be considered apparent.  An apparent defect is one that the buyer either detected at the time of the sale or could have detected given his or her knowledge (art. 1523 C.C.L.C. and art. 1726, para. 2 C.C.Q.).  Buyers therefore have an obligation to inform themselves by carrying out a reasonable inspection of the good.  In all cases, the test is whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances could have detected the defect at the time of the sale. 43 We will conclude the discussion in...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\n... will be considered apparent.  An apparent defect is one that the buyer either detected at the time of the sale or could have detected given his or her knowledge (art. 1523 C.C.L.C. and art. 1726, para. 2 C.C.Q.).  Buyers therefore have an obligation to inform themselves by carrying out a reasonable inspection of the good.  In all cases, the test is whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances could have detected the defect at the time of the sale. 43 We will conclude the discussion in this section with a few comments on the specific situation raised by the parties in the case at bar, that is, where the seller and the buyer are characterized as [ translation ] “professionals of identical expertise”, in which case they would be able to contract out of the warranty against latent defects.  This argument is based on three judgments of the Quebec Court of Appeal: Auto Peliss ltée v. Proulx Pontiac Buick ltée , 2001 CanLII 5911 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 856; Garage Robert inc. v. 2426‑9888 Québec inc. , 2001 CanLII 9967 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 865; Trois Diamants Autos (1987) ltée v. M.G.B. Auto inc. , 2001 CanLII 39923 (QC CA) , [2001] R.J.Q. 860. 44 The position taken by the Court of Appeal in this trilogy of cases does not support the appellants’ argument.  The court’s decisions did not relate to the situation of a manufacturer.  Nor did the court recognize a presumption of knowledge on the buyer’s part that would be incompatible with the warranty against latent defects.  Furthermore, the position taken by this Court on the manufacturer’s warranty in General Motors Products of Canada v. Kravitz , 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790, is clear.  In that case, at pp. 798‑99, the Court confirmed that, for the purposes of art. 1527 C.C.L.C., a manufacturer and a professional seller are always presumed to be in bad faith and that the fraudulence associated with the manufacturer’s actual or presumed knowledge of a defect is unaffected by the professional status of a buyer who is a dealer.  The implication of this line of reasoning is that the buyer’s expertise does not nullify the presumption applicable to the manufacturer. Even if a professional buyer is personally in the business of selling a manufacturer’s automobiles, he or she cannot be considered to have the same expertise as the manufacturer.  Professional buyers, too, are protected by the warranty against latent defects.  If a defect is latent, the manufacturer will be unable to rely on a limitation of liability clause unless it can rebut the presumption of knowledge of the defect. 45 At any rate, in the case at bar, C.E. is a manufacturer of recovery boilers, and Domtar bought one of C.E.’s boilers for use in the production of pulp and paper.  However expert Domtar may be at using boilers, it cannot be characterized as a professional “of identical expertise” to C.E.  As a manufacturer, C.E. had greater expertise than Domtar as regards the characteristics of the recovery boiler in question.  The warranty against latent defects is applicable in this case. 3.6 Manufacturer’s Liability for Defects in the Item Sold 46 When enforcing a legal warranty, a court must first determine whether there was a latent defect in the good sold.  At this stage, the analysis relates essentially to the good and to the buyer’s conduct.  Second, the court will determine whether the seller is liable; this part of the analysis consists in establishing whether the seller knew or is legally presumed to have known about the alleged defect.  This determination will enable the court to decide whether a clause limiting the seller’s liability can be set up against the buyer.  It is therefore important to distinguish the conditions of the seller’s liability for a latent defect from the scope of his or her liability. 3.6.1 Conditions of Liability: Existence of a Latent Defect 3.6.1.1 Different Types of Latent Defects 47 The legislature has not expressly defined what constitutes a “defect”. Article 1522 C.C.L.C. does, however, contain some useful information.  For example, the first criterion for determining whether a latent defect exists is the loss of use it causes.  The purpose of the warranty against latent defects is thus to ensure that the buyer of a good will be able to make practical and economical use of it. 48 There are three main types of latent defects: the material defect, which relates to a specific good; the functional defect, which relates to the good’s design; and the conventional defect, which arises where the buyer has disclosed that the good is to be put to a particular use.  Material and functional defects are assessed in light of the normal use to which buyers put the good, whereas a conventional defect is assessed in light of the particular use indicated by the buyer to the seller.  However, it is necessary, in discussing this classification, to briefly consider the problem of technological change. 49 Technological change is a modern‑day reality that is characterized by the rapid pace at which improvements are made to products.  The trial judge rightly noted that manufacturers are constantly redesigning their products: 2003 CanLII 52306 (QC CS) , [2003] R.J.Q. 2194, at para. 161 .  He was wary, and rightly so, of a tendency to condemn a manufacturer simply because a different version of the original product has since emerged on the market.  Selling an improved or better performing version of a product does not render the previous version defective.  Differences in quality and possible use between these two versions of the product cannot be characterized as a latent defect.  The key factor in the analysis resides in the loss of use, as assessed in light of the buyer’s reasonable expectations. 50 The categories of defects can sometimes overlap.  In the case at bar, Domtar complains that the tie welds, which were integral to the superheater, compromised the normal operation of the boiler by causing cracks and unforeseeable shutdowns.  According to Domtar, the argument that it should not have to accept untimely shutdowns flows from the very nature of the equipment purchased and from the fact that this equipment operates continuously.  In this sense, the defect of which Domtar complains is both functional and conventional. However, regardless of how the defect is characterized, it must have four characteristics, all of which are essential to the warranty:  it must be latent, must be sufficiently serious, must have existed at the time of the sale and must have been unknown to the buyer. 3.6.1.2 Latency of the Defect 51 The latency of the defect is assessed objectively, that is, by reviewing the buyer’s examination of the good in light of what a prudent and diligent buyer of identical expertise would have done:  P.‑G. Jobin, “Précis on sale”, in Reform of the Civil Code (1993), vol. 3A, at p. 61; M. Pourcelet, La vente (5th ed. 1987), at p. 149. In other words, the issue is not limited to ignorance of the defect; it must also be determined whether a reasonable buyer in the same circumstances would have realized that there was a defect. 3.6.1.3 Seriousness of the Defect 52 Loss of use does not in itself suffice to support the conclusion that a latent defect exists.  The loss must also be serious, that is, it must render the good unfit for its intended use or must so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have purchased it at the price paid.  This second criterion, the seriousness of the defect, flows from the words of art. 1522 C.C.L.C. However, the defect does not have to render the good completely unusable but simply has to reduce its usefulness significantly in relation to the legitimate expectations of a prudent and diligent buyer. 3.6.1.4 Existence of the Defect at the Time of the Sale 53 The defect must also have existed at the time of the sale.  This third criterion was developed by the courts and commentators before being codified in art. 1726, para. 1 C.C.Q.: Commentaires du ministre de la Justice (1993), vol. I, at p. 1078. What this basically means is that a seller cannot be held liable for a defect caused by abnormal use of the good by the buyer. 3.6.1.5 Defect Unknown to the Buyer 54 It is not enough for the defect to be latent.  It must also be unknown to the buyer, as is required by the concluding words of art. 1522 in fine C.C.L.C.  This criterion is assessed subjectively.  Unlike the seller, the buyer is not subject to a presumption of knowledge, since the buyer is always presumed to be in good faith.  As a result, the burden of proving actual knowledge of the defect always rests on the seller:  Jobin, at p. 60; Pourcelet, at p. 149; T. Rousseau‑Houle, Précis du droit de la vente et du louage (2nd ed. 1986), at p. 134. 55 The seller’s liability is not the same in every latent defect case.  It will accordingly be necessary to review the conditions that determine the scope and applicability of the seller’s liability. 3.6.2 Scope of the Seller’s Liability 3.6.2.1 Presumption of Knowledge of Latent Defects on the Seller’s Part 56 According to art. 1524 C.C.L.C., when a buyer has shown that a latent defect exists, the seller will be held liable “unless it is stipulated that he shall not be obliged to any warranty”.  However, even if the contract contains a limitation of liability clause, the seller will not always be able to invoke it.  Presumed or actual knowledge of the defect can, in certain circumstances, bar the seller from relying on such a clause.  Since actual or presumed knowledge of a defect on the seller’s part is an indication of bad faith, the seller, in such a case, has not only to repay the sale price, but also to compensate the buyer for any damage caused by the latent defect.  A limitation of liability clause may not be set up against the buyer if the seller knew or is legally presumed to have known about the defect:  J. Deslauriers, “La vente 1”, in Cours de la formation professionnelle du Barreau du Québec 1988‑1989, vol. 3, 51, at p. 82; Pourcelet, at p. 160; Rousseau‑Houle, at pp. 135 and 156.  The presumption of knowledge is thus determinative not only of the issue of whether a seller may limit the warranty against latent defects, but above all of that of the scope of the seller’s liability. 57 Although the warranty against latent defects originated in Ancient Rome, the presumption of knowledge made its first appearance only in old French law, in particular in the works of DuMoulin, Domat and Pothier:  Edwards, at p. 275.  Before then, a seller had to have actual knowledge of a defect to be liable for it since all sellers were presumed to be in good faith.  The new presumption came into being as a result of the work of certain authors, who were of the opinion that manufacturers and specialized sellers, by reason of their occupations, should know about any defects in their goods.  According to Pothier, manufacturers should be considered to be masters of their craft and specialized sellers should be presumed to have a detailed knowledge of the products they sell, and both should accordingly be deprived of the protection afforded sellers in good faith: [ translation ] There is one case in which the seller, despite having no knowledge whatsoever of the defect in the thing sold, is nevertheless obliged to make reparation for the wrong this defect has caused to the buyer in respect of the buyer’s other property:  where the seller is an artisan, or a merchant who sells works of his craft, or of the trade that is his profession. Even if this artisan or merchant should claim not to know about the defect, he is obligated to make reparation for any damage the buyer has sustained because of the defect in using the thing for its intended purpose. . . . The reason is that an artisan, by profession of his craft, spondet peritiam artis .  He becomes liable to all who contract with him for the quality of his works, when they are put to the use for which they are by nature intended. His incompetence or lack of knowledge in any aspect of his craft is a fault on his part, since no one should publicly profess a craft if he does not possess all the knowledge needed to exercise it properly: Imperitia culpae annumeratur ; l. 132, ff. Reg. J .  The same is true of the merchant, whether or not he is also the manufacturer.  By reason of his public profession of his trade, he becomes liable for the quality of the wares he sells, when they are put to their intended use.  If he is a manufacturer, he must employ, to make his wares, only good artisans, and he is answerable for this.  If he is not a manufacturer, he must display only good wares for sale; he must have expert knowledge of his wares and sell only good ones.  [Emphasis added.] ( Œuvres de Pothier (new ed. 1823), vol. II, No. 214) 58 This passage, sometimes referred to as [ translation ] “Pothier’s rule”, was regarded as the main source of art. 1527, para. 2 C.C.L.C.: Edwards, at pp. 280‑81; Traité de droit civil du Québec , vol. 11, by L. Faribault, 1961, at p. 295; P.-B. Mignault, Le droit civil canadien (1906), vol. VII, 1906, at p. 112.  Inspired by the principles enunciated by Pothier, the commissioners who drafted the C.C.L.C. introduced presumed knowledge into Quebec civil law:  Edwards, at p. 276.  This attributed knowledge is provided for in arts. 1527 and 1528 C.C.L.C.  In principle, sellers are presumed to be in good faith (art. 2202 C.C.L.C.), but they will be held liable if they had actual knowledge of defects (art. 1527, para. 1 C.C.L.C.). Moreover, art. 1527, para. 2 C.C.L.C. relieves the buyer of the burden of proving bad faith on the seller’s part if the seller is legally presumed to have known about the defects.  What remains is to identify the sellers to whom this presumption applies. 3.6.2.2 Sellers to Whom the Presumption of Knowledge Applies 59 The authors agree that the presumption of knowledge under the C.C.L.C. applies to manufacturers and to specialized professional sellers.  Thus, they would not apply it to non‑specialized professional sellers or non‑professional sellers, since such sellers lack technical expertise or commercial experience with respect to the good, which makes it impossible for them to personally verify the quality of the good:  Pourcelet, at p. 158; Rousseau‑Houle, at pp. 139‑52; F. Langelier, Cours de droit civil de la province de Québec , vol. V, 1909, at p. 77; Mignault, at p. 118. 60 It should be noted, however, that since the enactment of art. 1733 C.C.Q., the presumption of knowledge has applied to all professional sellers without distinction.  Only non‑professional sellers are now exempt from the presumption. 3.6.2.3 Rebuttable Nature of the Presumption of Knowledge 61 The nature of the presumption must now be examined to determine its effect, that is, whether it can ultimately be rebutted and, if so, under what conditions.  This Court has considered this question in a number of judgments. Samson & Filion v. Davie Shipbuilding & Repairing Co. , 1924 CanLII 57 (SCC) , [1925] S.C.R. 202, was one of the first leading decisions in which the presumption of knowledge on the seller’s part was addressed.  That case concerned the sale of used pipes.  The contract of sale did not contain a limitation of liability clause.  This Court held that the presumption of knowledge does not apply to a non‑professional seller with no expertise.  Such a seller is not liable for damage caused by the good unless he or she had actual knowledge of the defect.  Manufacturers and professional sellers ([ translation] “who [sell] works of [their] craft, or of the trade that is [their] profession”), on the other hand, are subject to a presumption of knowledge, which is rebuttable. 62 A few years later, this Court rendered its decision in Touchette v. Pizzagalli , 1938 CanLII 39 (SCC) , [1938] S.C.R. 433, which concerned the sale of a new car by a professional seller to a non‑professional buyer without the manufacturer’s involvement.  Under the contract, the warranty was limited to the one provided by the manufacturer.  The Court noted that professional sellers are presumed to know about any defects in the goods they sell, although it implicitly acknowledged that a manufacturer or a specialized professional seller can rebut the presumption of knowledge: It is now settled that the seller is responsible in respect of all damages sustained by the purchaser by reason of latent defect where the...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 5\\nthe whole benefit of the agreement”: Guarantee Co. of North America v. Gordon Capital Corp. , 1999 CanLII 664 (SCC) , [1999] 3 S.C.R. 423, at para. 50 . Thus, the existence of a latent defect does not automatically amount to a fundamental breach of contract:  G. H. L. Fridman, The Law of Contract in Canada (5th ed. 2006), at p. 592; the latent defect must be “irreparable”, or the good must be unusable: Hunter Engineering Co. v. Syncrude Canada Ltd. , 1989 CanLII 129 (SCC) , [1989] 1 S.C.R. 426, at pp. 501‑2; Guarantee Co. of North America ; R. G. McLean Ltd. v. Canadian Vickers Ltd. , 1970 CanLII 386 (ON CA) , [1971] 1 O.R. 207 (C.A.), at pp. 211‑12. 84 Once the existence of a fundamental breach has been established, the court must still analyse the limitation of liability clause in light of the general rules of contract interpretation.  If the words can reasonably be interpreted in only one way, it will not be open to the court, even on grounds of equity or reasonableness, to declare the clause to be unenforceable since this would amount to rewriting the contract negotiated by the parties. 3.6.3.3 Conclusion on Comparative Law 85 In short, owing to certain of its characteristics, the common law, to an even greater extent than French law, cannot easily be grafted on to Quebec civil law.  Nor, despite the connections between Quebec civil law and French law, does it appear any more desirable to import the rules of the French system into Quebec law.  What now remains to be considered is how to apply the Quebec rules to the facts of the instant case. 3.7 Analysis of C.E.’s Liability 86 The trial judge made findings of fact.  The Court of Appeal saw no palpable errors in them, and the parties have not asked us to review them. Only the conclusions of law drawn from those facts are in issue.  As mentioned above, the trial judge concluded that the cracking of the tubes was a design feature rather than a design defect.  Domtar contests this conclusion and argues that the cracking constitutes a latent defect.  It must therefore be determined whether there was in fact a latent defect in C.E.’s recovery boiler and, if so, whether C.E. knew or is presumed to have known that there was such a defect. 3.7.1 Existence of a Latent Defect 3.7.1.1 Seriousness of the Loss of Use 87 In the case at bar, the trial judge’s conclusion that the superheater did not have a latent defect was based on two errors he made in interpreting this first criterion.  The first was to limit the definition of a defect to a problem preventing the good from being used at all, while the second was to confuse the sale of a lower‑performance version of a good with the sale of a defective one. 88 A defect will be considered to be serious if it renders the good unfit for its intended use or so diminishes its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it at the price paid (arts. 1522 C.C.L.C. and 1726 C.C.Q.).  An example frequently cited by the authors is where a house is at risk of water damage owing to a crack in its foundation.  The crack does not actually have to cause such damage for a latent defect to exist; it is enough that the crack exists and that it is likely to lead to serious damage. 89 C.E. considers only the first aspect of the seriousness of the defect. According to its interpretation, the boiler could not be considered to be defective unless there were absolutely no possibility of it working.  The trial judge, too, failed to ask whether the usefulness of the good had been diminished, as he stressed that the recovery boiler could continue to operate despite the cracks. 90 In the instant case, the superheater was a new product at the time of delivery.  After 18 months of use, 6 leaks had to be repaired, 789 H‑style tie welds had to be replaced, and 764 cracks were detected in the superheater’s various compartments.  The cost of these repairs reached $445,483.57, and the boiler was out of service for 13 days.  Six months later, a full inspection revealed 3 new leaks and 859 new cracks, including some that C.E.’s expert described as being severe and likely to cause new leaks.  Domtar replaced the superheater two years after it was put into service.  However, the trial judge himself stated that this type of machine is designed for continuous use, apart from scheduled shutdowns, and generally has a lifespan, free of major problems, of at least 15 years.  The fact that Domtar noticed leaks by chance does not make the defect less serious, especially since so little time had elapsed since the equipment was put into service.  This clearly suggests abnormal wear and tear of the machine. Furthermore, an engineer who testified for C.E. acknowledged that it should normally take 15 to 20 years for cracks to occur in the superheater tubes. 91 For the defect to be considered serious, it was enough for there to be a problem with the boiler so serious that the buyer would not have purchased the boiler had it known about the problem.  The trial judge himself concluded that Domtar would undoubtedly have elected to use hinge‑pin attachments, just as any other paper manufacturer provided with the relevant information about the two types of attachments would have done.  He added that if Domtar had elected to use hinge‑pin attachments, it is probable that no leaks would have been discovered in the ITSH and that fewer cracks would have been found in the ITSH and HTSH.  In our opinion, these findings of fact support the Court of Appeal’s conclusion that Domtar would not have bought a boiler with H‑style tie welds if it had been informed of the risks associated with this type of attachment. 92 There is another problem with the trial judge’s characterization of the facts:  the confusion between performance and defectiveness of a product.  In the case at bar, C.E. submitted that the hinge‑pin attachments were simply of higher quality than the H‑style tie welds.  While it is true that a manufacturer may design goods of varying quality, each version of a good must be fit for its intended purpose for as long as a buyer might reasonably be entitled to expect.  Yet the technology of hinge‑pin attachments existed at the time the boiler was manufactured.  What are in issue here are not two types of attachments of different quality or different brands, but rather two types of attachments, one of which tends to weaken the tubes, and is as a result likely to cause unscheduled shutdowns of the boiler, even after only a short time in operation. 93 C.E. knew about the problems associated with the rigidity of both A‑style and H‑style tie welds.  Furthermore, it was the rigidity of H‑style tie welds that prompted C.E.’s parent company to decide to offer hinge‑pin attachments as the standard in superheater design.  Nevertheless, C.E. used A‑style and H‑style tie welds without having independent analyses conducted to determine whether they could withstand the stress placed on the tubes by the circulation of steam at high temperatures.  According to the trial judge, C.E. chose instead to rely on its customers’ experience in this respect to assess the real efficacy of its new product.  Moreover, it can be seen from a memorandum filed at trial that C.E. had delayed the adoption of hinge‑pin attachments in order to maintain its competitive position in the marketplace, and that the decision to adopt them as the standard had been imposed on the manufacturer gradually by market forces: . . . it is apparent that it was reluctantly responding to market forces that were to the effect that “the pulp and paper industry no longer considers the use of tie welds to be an acceptable superheater attachment . . . .” ( 2003 CanLII 52306 (QC CS) , [2003] R.J.Q. 2194, at para. 41 ) 94 Where there is a serious problem that affects the use of a good and the manufacturer is aware of the problem, the manufacturer must address the problem to avoid being held liable for it.  The manufacturer cannot simply put the product on the market and wait for reactions from users. 95 This being said, C.E. still uses H‑style tie welds in the design of some of its products, sometimes in combination with hinge‑pin attachments.  The present reasons for judgment should not be understood to imply that H‑style tie welds must never be used in the design of superheaters.  However, as a boiler manufacturer, C.E. is required to take its customers’ needs and objectives into account in designing its boilers.  This is the standard that Domtar was entitled to expect C.E. to meet. 3.7.1.2 Existence of the Defect at the Time of the Sale 96 In the case at bar, the cracking caused by the use of H‑style tie welds was not the result of abnormal use of the recovery boiler, as C.E. originally claimed.  Moreover, the trial judge found that the cause of the cracking was related to the rigidity of the attachments used.  C.E. decided, with good reason, not to pursue its original argument in this Court.  In all probability, the defect existed at the time of the sale. 3.7.1.3 Domtar Was Unaware of the Defect at the Time of the Sale 97 This criterion requires the application of a subjective standard.  The trial judge found that Domtar did not have actual knowledge of what he described as a design feature.  According to the evidence, C.E. did not share the information it possessed on this subject with Domtar. 3.7.1.4 Latency of the Defect Despite Domtar’s Expertise 98 The latency of a defect is analysed in relation to the buyer’s level of expertise.  In other words, should Domtar have known it was running a risk of excessive cracking because of the use of H‑style tie welds? 99 The trial judge found that Domtar’s “principal interlocutor” was aware of the two different opinions on the attachments since he had been a member of the Black Liquor Recovery Boiler Advisory Committee which met to discuss recent cases in which problems with recovery boilers had led to unscheduled shutdowns.  The trial judge also mentioned the expertise of Domtar’s engineering department and of Simons‑Sandwell as regards the operation of pulp and paper mills.  Although Domtar was characterized as a sophisticated operator of the equipment used in operating a pulp and paper mill, the issue here is the design of a custom‑made piece of industrial equipment.  C.E. is a company that specializes in the design of recovery boilers.  According to the trial judge, approximately 50 to 60 percent of C.E.’s revenue came from the design and sale of boilers.  The company enjoyed an 80 percent share of the Canadian market for these machines.  Expertise in operating such equipment does not automatically entail expertise in the actual design of the product.  The manufacturer inevitably has more complete knowledge of the product it has designed since it controls the materials and labour. 100 In discussing the professional qualifications of Domtar’s representatives, the trial judge stated that Simons‑Sandwell was knowledgeable to a certain extent about boilers and was aware of, among other things, the existence and potential advantages of hinge‑pin attachments. However, neither Simons‑Sandwell nor Domtar knew that the tie welds were in all probability the cause of the numerous cracks that had appeared. 101 C.E. submits that the trial judge did not attach sufficient importance to Simons‑Sandwell’s knowledge.  This argument must fail.  The defect is not apparent simply because Domtar was assisted by an expert.  Since the cause of the excessive cracking was unknown to both Domtar and Simons‑Sandwell, the defect in this case was indeed latent. 3.7.2 Applicability of the Limitation of Liability Clause 102 As mentioned above, the limitation of liability clause may be relied on only if C.E., as a manufacturer, can rebut the presumption of knowledge applicable to it.  C.E. had to prove that another manufacturer in the same circumstances would not have known of the defect: Samson & Filion . Its argument in this Court is based not on fault on the part of Domtar or a third party, or on superior force or development risk, but on its own good faith throughout its business relations with Domtar.  Although good faith has not yet been recognized as a means of rebutting the presumption, it will be helpful to review the facts as alleged by C.E. and found by the trial judge. 103 The trial judge reviewed in detail the evolution of the types of attachments used in recovery boilers.  In 1977, C.E. had decided to replace A‑style tie welds with H‑style tie welds because A‑style tie welds had been causing serious leakage problems over the previous five years.  C.E. decided that H‑style tie welds would be preferable, even though they had not been tested. 104 Manufacturers started using hinge‑pin attachments in the early 1980s.  It can be seen from internal C.E. memoranda that a number of customers in the United States had complained about problems with cracks and leaks linked to tie welds.  As a result, C.E.’s parent company adopted hinge‑pin attachments as its standard in the United States in 1984.  But a similar problem was occurring in Canada:  C.E. had received complaints about A‑style tie welds from Canadian customers in January 1983, May 1983 and December 1984.  In June 1985, C.E. recommended to one of its customers that it use hinge‑pin attachments to avoid cracks and leaks.  In 1988, C.E. decided to limit its use of hinge‑pin attachments in order to maintain its competitive position in the marketplace: An internal memorandum insisted on the use of tie welds because of the additional cost to manufacture hinge pins, and that in order to maintain the competitive position of C.E. in the marketplace, hinge pins should only be used when there was a “known failure, deterioration, misalignment, etc. of our present standard tangent tube flex ties”, or, “if specifically requested by the customer or when ‘latest state‑of‑the‑art’ design is noted.” ( 2003 CanLII 52306 (QC CS) , [2003] R.J.Q. 2194, at para. 39 ) C.E. did not finally adopt hinge‑pin attachments as its Canadian standard until the following year.  However, C.E. had known about the problems associated with tie welds since the early 1980s, that is, before Domtar submitted its purchase order in September 1985. 105 It can be seen from the evidence that the technical knowledge of the time had led some of C.E.’s competitors, and even its parent company, to change their standards.  To successfully rebut the presumption of knowledge, it matters little that C.E. believed in good faith that it had solved the problem.  Simply having an honest belief in the adequacy of its product is not enough to relieve a manufacturer of liability.  It must be concluded that, since C.E. has failed to raise a valid defence, it has not rebutted the presumption of knowledge applicable to it in accordance with the standard established in Samson & Filion . 106 It is therefore our view that the Court of Appeal correctly found that there was a latent defect in the superheater that C.E. knew or should have known about.  Since C.E. has failed to rebut the presumption of knowledge applicable to it, it cannot rely on the limitation of liability clause in its defence. 3.8 Duty to Inform 107 The trial judge found that C.E. had breached its duty to inform, whereas in the Court of Appeal’s view, the issue related to the warranty against latent defects.  The two concepts overlap, but it is important to distinguish them in order to identify the circumstances in which each rule will be applied. 108 Whereas the warranty against latent defects is expressly provided for in the C.C.L.C. and the C.C.Q., the duty to inform derives instead from the general principle of good faith ( Bank of Montreal v. Bail Ltée , 1992 CanLII 71 (SCC) , [1992] 2 S.C.R. 554, at p. 586; arts. 6, 7 and 1375 C.C.Q.) and the principle of free and informed consent.  Furthermore, the scope of the general duty to inform is much broader than that of the disclosure of a latent defect.  This duty encompasses any information that is of decisive importance for a party to a contract, as Gonthier J. stated in Bail (see pp. 586‑87). It is therefore easy to imagine a situation in which a seller would be in breach of the duty even though no latent defect exists. 109 Where a seller fails to discharge the duty to disclose a defect, on the other hand, it can probably be said at the same time that he or she has also breached the general duty to inform the buyer of a factor of decisive importance in respect of the good sold, namely the existence of a latent defect.  The instant case is one example of this.  If a party invokes the seller’s warranty against latent defects, the duty to inform is in a sense subsumed in the analysis of the seller’s liability for latent defects, and there is no need for the court to conduct a separate analysis on the seller’s duty to inform.  As a result, our analysis and conclusion regarding C.E.’s liability under the warranty against latent defects are sufficient to dispose of the case\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 6\\nin respect of the good sold, namely the existence of a latent defect.  The instant case is one example of this.  If a party invokes the seller’s warranty against latent defects, the duty to inform is in a sense subsumed in the analysis of the seller’s liability for latent defects, and there is no need for the court to conduct a separate analysis on the seller’s duty to inform.  As a result, our analysis and conclusion regarding C.E.’s liability under the warranty against latent defects are sufficient to dispose of the case before the Court. 110 What now remains is to consider C.E.’s argument concerning the reduction of the quantum awarded to Domtar in light of Domtar’s failure to mitigate its damages and of the payment it received from its insurer, Lloyd’s, as well as the actions against Chubb, based on the performance bond, and Arkwright, based on the all risk insurance policy. 3.9 Reduction of the Quantum of the Damages 111 In this Court, C.E. has submitted two arguments for reducing the quantum of the damages awarded to Domtar by the Court of Appeal.  First, C.E. contends that by replacing a superheater that was fully operational, Domtar failed to mitigate its damages and that Domtar is accordingly entitled to be reimbursed only for the repairs effected in the spring of 1989.  Second, C.E. submits that the determination a posteriori by a court that the damage was not covered by the insurance policy does not alter the compensatory nature of the payment that has already been made.  According to C.E., the payment made to Domtar by Lloyd’s was intended to settle certain claims, including the one related to the purchase of the boiler from C.E., and once that payment was made, Lloyd’s was subrogated to Domtar’s rights and Domtar’s claim for damages against C.E. had to be reduced accordingly. 112 The trial judge rejected C.E.’s suggestion that Domtar could have repaired the superheater instead of replacing it.  He noted that Domtar had had to make a decision in light of the available information and that its inability to adequately assess the alternatives was due to C.E.’s breach of the duty to inform.  Regarding the subrogation argument, the trial judge agreed with C.E. and held that the damages claimed by Domtar should be reduced by the amount it had received from Lloyd’s. 113 The Court of Appeal also rejected the repair scenarios proposed by C.E., citing Domtar’s need for reliable equipment.  However, it reversed the trial judge’s holding on the subrogation issue.  According to the Court of Appeal, for a payment to result in subrogation of an insurer to the rights of the insured, it must be made to the insured on account of an obligation arising from the insurance contract or by operation of law.  In the instant case, because a clause in the insurance contract between Lloyd’s and Domtar expressly excluded damage resulting from a breakdown of or defect in the recovery boiler, the payment made by Lloyd’s could not have been related to the damages being claimed by Domtar.  Lloyd’s was not, therefore, subrogated to Domtar’s rights. The Court of Appeal’s reasons are sound, and there is no need to intervene. 3.10 Chubb’s Appeal 114 This Court granted leave to appeal in this incidental case because its outcome depended directly on the outcome of the main case.  In this case, Domtar relies on a contract establishing a performance bond in its favour. Under the contract, Chubb agreed to guarantee the performance of C.E.’s obligations under the contract of sale for the recovery boiler.  Since there was a latent defect in the superheater, Chubb is solidarily liable for the amount of its guarantee as surety in favour of Domtar, as the Court of Appeal held. 3.11 Domtar’s Appeal Against Arkwright 115 This incidental case concerns an “all risk” insurance policy issued by Arkwright that covered Domtar’s property, including the recovery boiler, at the time of the events in question.  Domtar has already been indemnified under this policy for the damage it sustained as a result of the repairs effected in the spring of 1989.  However, the policy contained a clause excluding damage resulting from latent defects in the boiler. 116 In its appeal, Domtar seeks to demonstrate that the losses were nonetheless covered by the insurance policy.  More specifically, it submits that the lost profits it suffered in the fall of 1989 when C.E.’s superheater was replaced with one from B. & W. resulted from the faulty design of the H‑style tie welds.  In other words, the latent defect affected not the entire boiler or superheater, but strictly the H‑style tie welds, and the damage was accordingly covered by the policy. 117 The trial judge agreed with Arkwright.  In his view, replacing the superheater was not the only possible solution for Domtar, since at the time the machine was replaced, it could have continued operating indefinitely. Domtar could not, therefore, rely on its all risk insurance policy to recover disbursements made to prevent a hypothetical future loss. 118 Domtar appealed that judgment.  The Court of Appeal considered the replacement of the superheater to have been necessary.  In its view, however, the tie welds could not be dissociated from the superheater itself and the insurer could rely on the exclusion clause because the design defect affected the entire superheater and not just the tie welds.  According to the Court of Appeal, the narrow interpretation Domtar seeks to give the exclusion clause would deprive the clause of any effect, and the loss to which Domtar’s claim applied was clearly not covered by the insurance policy with Arkwright. 119 In the main case, we held that the superheater’s design was defective because of the excessive cracking of the tubes caused by the use of H‑style tie welds, and that C.E., as the manufacturer, was liable for, inter alia , the cost of replacing the superheater.  To accept Domtar’s contention that the welds can be dissociated from the superheater would render the exclusion clause meaningless, since it would in almost every case be possible to isolate one component as the cause of the defect. 120 To sum up, Domtar’s submissions in this appeal against Arkwright are unfounded in law.  For the reasons given by the Court of Appeal, we are therefore of the opinion that the cost of replacing the defective superheater was excluded under the insurance policy. 4. Disposition 121 For these reasons, we would dismiss the three appeals and affirm the judgments of the Court of Appeal, with costs throughout. APPENDIX Civil Code of Lower Canada 1522. The seller is obliged by law to warrant the buyer against such latent defects in the thing sold, and its accessories, as render it unfit for the use for which it was intended, or so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it, or would not have given so large a price, if he had known them. 1523. The seller is not bound for defects which are apparent and which the buyer might have known of himself. 1524. The seller is bound for latent defects even when they were not known to him, unless it is stipulated that he shall not be obliged to any warranty. 1527. If the seller knew the defect of the thing, he is obliged not only to restore the price of it, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer. He is obliged in like manner in all cases in which he is legally presumed to know the defects. 1528. If the seller did not know the defects, or is not legally presumed to have known them, he is obliged only to restore the price and to reimburse to the buyer the expenses caused by the sale. Civil Code of Québec 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer. 1733. A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property. An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys property at his own risk from a seller who is not a professional seller. Appeals dismissed with costs . Solicitors for the appellants ABB Inc., Alstom Canada Inc. and Chubb Insurance Company of Canada:  Stikeman Elliott, Montréal. Solicitors for the appellant/respondent Domtar Inc.:  Ogilvy, Renault, Montréal. Solicitors for the respondent Arkwright Mutual Insurance Company:  Kugler, Kandestin, Montréal.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.032786883413791656, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\", \"title\": \"ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.032786883413791656, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\"}, \"token_count\": 18388, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/ca/scc/2007scc50-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2022qccq1415\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2022qccq1415-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Zhou c. Bodington, 2022 QCCQ 1415 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2022 QCCQ 1415 (CanLII) | Zhou c. Bodington | CanLII Date: 2022-04-04 File number: 500-32-705401-182 Citation: Zhou c. Bodington, 2022 QCCQ 1415 (CanLII) Zhou c. Bodington 2022 QCCQ 1415 COURT OF QUÉBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL « Civil Division » N° : 500-32-705401-182 DATE : April 4, 2022 ______________________________________________________________________ PRESIDED BY JUDGE HENRI RICHARD, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ LANG FENG ZHOU and TAO XU Plaintiffs v. GÉRALD (GÉRARD) BODINGTON Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Ms. Tao Xu and Mr. Lang Feng Zhou (the buyers ) allege that the duplex sold to them by Mr. Gérald (Gérard) Bodington (the seller ) has latent defects and are claiming $61,000 from him, which they agree to reduce to $15,000 in order to confer jurisdiction on the Small Claims Division. [ 2 ] In defense, the seller alleges that the building was not affected by any latent defect because there were sufficient indications allowing the buyers to detect them. Furthermore, the seller adds that the buyers aggravated the damage by not performing any repair and maintenance work . ISSUE [ 3 ] Did the buyers act in a prudent and diligent manner in the purchase of the property involved? CONTEXT [ 4 ] By deed of sale dated December 23, 2011, the seller sells to the buyers a duplex located in LaSalle and built at least as far back as 1955. After taking possession, the buyers occupy a portion of the duplex. [ 5 ] Prior to the signing of the deed of sale, the buyers obtain a copy of the document entitled \\\"Declarations by the seller of the immovable\\\" (P-2) which contains positive answers to the following questions : D3.1     To your knowledge, are there or have there ever been water infiltrations in the basement or elsewhere (e.g. roof, patio, balcony, greenhouse, skylight, door, window, chimney or other)? D6.3     To your knowledge, are there or have there ever been evidence of mould or rot? D8.1     To your knowledge, are there or have there ever been plumbing-related problems such as marked variations in water pressure or flow when using fixtures, freezing pipes, water links, rusty water, odours, plumbing drainage or backup problems, abnormal noises or other? D11.1   To your knowledge, are there or have there ever been insects or other pests? [ 6 ] To clarify his answer to question D3.1, the seller states : « Roof water ‑ Ice storm ». [ 7 ] Before signing the deed of sale, the buyers retained the services of a pre-purchase inspector, Mr. Fang Wang, who proceeded to visit the premises on November 2, 2011 and wrote a report filed as Exhibit P-3. [ 8 ] In the \\\" Basement \\\" section of this report, the inspector notes the following : 1. This is a finished basement with laundry room and washroom. 2. Humidity level was not able to be measured due to running dehumidifier (pic.1) 3. Seepage occurred in laundry room (pic.2) Moisture source was not identified due to floor covering. [ 9 ] In the \\\"Summary\\\" section of his report, the inspector, with accompanying photos, makes the following observations : […] 4. Cracks on left foundation wall need treatment to prevent deterioration caused by further water infiltration (pic.3 & 4). Consult qualified professional for evaluation. […] 6.  Infrared camera detected moisture in corner of bedroom drywall in unit 465 (pic.8 & 9). […] 14.  Efflorescent foundation wall at corner of basement window on right side (pic.17) need to be repaired to prevent water infiltration. […] 16.  Visible leak at toilet base in basement (pic.19) need repair. […] 19.  Fill gap between electrical entrance and foundation wall to avoid water intrusion (pic.23). 20.  Repair broken aluminum siding to avoid structure damage caused by water infiltration (pic.24). [ 10 ] The buyers did not make any repairs or perform any maintenance on the building between the time of purchase in 2011 and October 2016 when they experienced water damage in the basement laundry room as a result of a bad return flow from the washing machine. [ 11 ] It was then that the buyers began to strip the wood floor of the laundry room and noticed that the basement had been infiltrated by water for many years, resulting in the deterioration of the wood structure of the basement floor, in addition to the appearance of mould. [ 12 ] The photographs produced by the buyers are unequivocal: the entire basement is affected by major recurring water infiltration problems that require complete repair. [ 13 ] For the purposes of this case, the buyers retained the services of engineer Louis Denoncourt, who produced two reports dated August 15, 2017 (P-6) and September 29, 2017 (P-6A). [ 14 ] In his first report, the engineer concluded that four sources of water combined to cause rotting of the subfloor framing elements and the bottom of the basement finish walls : 1. Le refoulement des égouts dans la salle de lavage; 2. Des infiltrations par les joints au bas des murs de fondation qui sont la conséquence de l’absence ou de la vétusté des systèmes de protection et de drainage souterrain des fondations; 3. Des infiltrations par les joints décalés entre les blocs de béton de la partie supérieure de la fondation; 4. Des infiltrations par la jonction du toit de la remise, au coin arrière-gauche. [ 15 ] In support of their claim, the buyers submit a quote (P-9) dated September 20, 2017 from Vinet & Fils, general contractors, at a total cost of $61,060. This quote contains, among other things, the costs for the supply and installation of a French drain at the base of the foundation, two waterproofing membranes around the foundation, four window-wells and a well equipped with a sump pump. [ 16 ] At trial, the buyers produced a document (P-11) which detailed the costs of repairs to date as follows: Outdoor work: Installation of a French drain and a membrane on the foundations:       $15,000.00 Waterproof painting treatment of basement two inside walls:                   $2,299.50 Moving the removed soil elsewhere:                                                          $3,449.25 Interior work: Workmanship:                                                                                           $17,830.00 Materials:                                                                                                  $10,865.00 Expertise fees:                                                                                            $1,052.02 [ 17 ] Notwithstanding these amounts, as previously indicated, the buyers agree to reduce their claim to $15,000 in order to file their application with the Small Claims Division. [ 18 ] On his part, the seller retained the services of Richard Santo, an expert with a bachelor's degree in architecture, who visited the premises on June 29, 2017 and wrote his report on July 4, 2017 (D-1). [ 19 ] In his report, Mr. Santo concludes as follows : This whole residence now requires French Drains and membrane on outside foundation wall to protect the finished basement. In addition a sump pump and a floor drain are required to keep the basement floor dry. The above are required with normal aging more so on this residence, and sump pump for preventive is suggested. [ 20 ] At trial, the expert Santo testified that the alleged defects were apparent because several clues existed prior to the purchase, which should have led the buyers to further question and investigate before purchasing the building. ANALYSIS [ 21 ] The article 1726 of Civil code of Québec ( CCQ ) provides the basis for the buyers' recourse : 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. [ 22 ] Four conditions must be met in order for the defect to qualify as a latent defect within the meaning of this article. It must be serious, prior to the sale, unknown to the buyer and hidden. [ 23 ] In this file, only the non-apparent or hidden nature of the defect is at issue since the other three conditions are met. [ 24 ] In other words, the Court must determine whether the defects affecting the immovable in question are latent, for prudent and diligent buyers. [ 25 ] Over the years, the jurisprudence has established certain principles that are worth recalling : - The degree of care and diligence on the part of the buyer, which may be decisive in determining whether a defect is apparent or not, is modulated according to the particular circumstances, including the status of the seller and the buyer, the nature, age and price of the property, the type of defect and the behaviour of the parties [1] ; - The hidden nature of the defect is assessed according to an objective standard, namely by evaluating the adequacy of the buyer's examination against that of a prudent and diligent buyer of similar skill [2] ; - The prudent and diligent buyer could not have detected the defect before the sale [3] ; - In order to meet the standard of the prudent and diligent buyer, a buyer may be required to seek specialized assistance to conduct the required adequate examination, particularly if the buyer lacks the skills to do so or if there are indications of certain defects in the property [4] ; - In order to qualify as prudent and diligent, the buyer who retains the services of a pre-purchase inspector must follow his recommendations [5] ; - The number and seriousness of the apparent signs noted by the pre-purchase inspector should alert the prudent and diligent buyer to have the condition of the property verified by an expert [6] ; - The buyer who requires the services of a pre-purchase inspector should review his report and, if necessary, proceed with further inspection [7] ; - The prudent and diligent buyer should conduct a thorough examination when signs of water infiltration are noticed [8] ; - The prudent and diligent buyer must inquire into the cause of the problems revealed by clues, or risk having his claim dismissed [9] ; - Between the seller's declaration made in good faith and the pre-purchase inspector's recommendation to consult a specialist to check for cracks in the foundation, the prudent and diligent buyer must follow the inspector's recommendation [10] . - The buyer cannot expect a functional drain when buying a house built more than 50 years ago [11] . [ 26 ] Applying these principles, the Court concluded that the defects alleged by the buyers could not be qualified as \\\" latent \\\", in particular because of the numerous visible signs before the sale. [ 27 ] According to the buyers and their expert, the main source of water infiltration into the basement was at the base of the foundation wall and was due to the absence or obsolescence of the foundation's underground protection and drainage system. [ 28 ] Yet, at the time of the sale, the building in question was at least 56 years old, so the buyers should have expected to repair, replace or install a French drain. [ 29 ] In addition, the evidence reveals that one of the upper parts of the foundation is made of concrete blocks, with visible cracks. In this regard, the pre-purchase inspector of the buyers recommended that a professional be consulted for an assessment, which they failed or neglected to do. [ 30 ] Also, the pre-purchase inspection report P-3 mentions the presence of humidity on the first floor, traces of efflorescence on the foundation and several outside openings that could allow water to penetrate. Despite all these indications, the buyers did not carry out any repairs or maintenance between the date of purchase, in December 2011, and October 2016, when water damage occurred as a result of the malfunctioning of the washing machine's exhaust duct. [ 31 ] If the buyers had followed the recommendations of their pre-purchase inspector and had taken into account the numerous signs of water infiltration in the immovable in question, they would have gone further in their inspection and they would have discovered serious water infiltration problems in the basement. [ 32 ] The presence of these numerous indicators and the failure of the buyers to inspect more thoroughly make the alleged defects apparent. [ 33 ] Moreover, the age of the building should have led the buyers to question the presence of an adequate drainage system around the foundations, in particular the presence of a French drain. In this regard, the Court noted that the Plaintiff Lang Feng Zhou is a real estate broker and could not have been unaware of the importance of such a drainage system in order to avoid water infiltration in the basement. [ 34 ] As a result of all of the foregoing, the Court concludes that the buyers do not discharge their burden of establishing, by a preponderance of the evidence, the merits of their claim, particularly in that they did not act as prudent and diligent buyers. However, in the exercise of its judicial discretion, the Court does not impose any costs. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT : DISMISSES Lang Feng Zhou and Tao Xu’s claim against Gérald (Gérard) Bodington, each party paying its own costs. __________________________________ Henri Richard, J.C.Q. Date of hearing : March 24, 2022 [1] Placement Jacpar Inc . c. Benzakour , C.A., 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , [1989] R.J.Q. 2309; Bélanger c. Alarie , 2018 QCCS 2919 , par. 77 ; Jean-Louis c. Pillon , 2018 QCCS 2160 , par. 111 . See also : Edwards, Jeffrey , La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 e éd., Montréal, Wilson & Lafleur, 2008, p. 173 and followings. [2] ABB Inc. c. Domtar Inc. , 2007 CSC 50 , par. 51 , 98 and following. [3] Boleyn c. Germain , 2013 QCCA 326 ; Dorais c. Pourvoirie Oasis du Gouin inc. (Oasis du Gouin Outfitter Inc.) , 2012 QCCS 4735 ; Saindon c . Tremblay , 2012 QCCS 2908 ; 9165-2115 Québec inc. c . 2425-0599 Québec inc. , 2011 QCCS 2958 ; Pinsonneault c. Entreprises VFJ inc. , 2011 QCCQ 8943 . [4] St-Louis c. Morin , 2006 QCCA 1643 ; Lavoie c. Comtois , C.S., 1999 CanLII 11787 (QC CS) , [2000] R.D.I. 36; Beaudet c. Bastien , 2007 QCCQ 13454 ; Gendron c. Cartier , 2006 QCCQ 5793 . [5] Garcia Lorenzo c. Migas (Migas Home Inspections), 2016 QCCA 1661 , par. 15 ; Vermette c. Boisvert , 2017 QCCS 3159 , par. 114 , confirmed on appeal : 2019 QCCA 829 ; Martel c. Beaulieu , 2017 QCCS 6045 , par. 75 , confirmed on appeal: 2019 QCCA 1574 ; Desmeules c. 1845-0460 Québec inc ., 2009 QCCS 2683 , par. 44 ; Goodhart c. Pelletier , 2017 QCCS 4148 , par. 124 ; Vo c . Lacaille , 2017 QCCQ 14333 , par. 105 . See also : Edwards, Jeffrey, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 e éd., précité note 1, page 177. [6] Cistellini c . Jinchereau , 2012 QCCS 1776 . [7] Martel c. Beaulieu , 2017 QCCS 6045 , par. 75 , confirmed on appeal...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... 124 ; Vo c . Lacaille , 2017 QCCQ 14333 , par. 105 . See also : Edwards, Jeffrey, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 e éd., précité note 1, page 177. [6] Cistellini c . Jinchereau , 2012 QCCS 1776 . [7] Martel c. Beaulieu , 2017 QCCS 6045 , par. 75 , confirmed on appeal: 2019 QCCA 1574 ; Penafiel c. Néron , 2012 QCCS 6186 ; Fortier c . Cadieux , 2011 QCCS 3966 . [8] Groupe Best-dev c. Desrochers , 2011 QCCS 2274 ; 9165-2115 Québec inc. c. 2425-0599 Québec inc. , 2011 QCCS 2958 . [9] Beaupré c. Gélinas , C.A., 2000 CanLII 1814 (QC CA) , J.E. 2000-1823; Pominville c. Demers , C.A., J.E. 95-1144 ; Pominville c. Demers , C.A., J.E. 95-1144 ; Cloutier c. Létourneau , C.A., 1992 CanLII 3716 (QC CA) , J.E. 93-143; Gélinas c. Beaumier , C.A., 1989 CanLII 1171 (QC CA) , J.E. 90-128; Ronci c. Desroches , C.A., J.E. 88-574 ; Beaudet c. Bastien , 2007 QCCQ 13454 ; Gendron c. Cartier , 2006 QCCQ 5793 . [10] Miclette c. Dorval , 2018 QCCQ 6734 , par. 47 , 51-53. [11] Downey c. Boys , 2018 QCCS 4565 , par. 127 ; Castonguay c. Lapointe , 2012 QCCS 76 , par. 238 ; Bray c. Watson , C.S., 1994 CanLII 10697 (QC CS) , SOQUIJ AZ-95121039, [1995] R.L. 508....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2022qccq1415-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.026102179661393166, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2022qccq1415-en\", \"title\": \"Zhou c. Bodington, 2022 QCCQ 1415 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2022qccq1415-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.026102179661393166, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2022qccq1415-en\"}, \"token_count\": 4133, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2022qccq1415-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc., 2015 QCCS 5514 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2015 QCCS 5514 (CanLII) | Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc. | CanLII Date: 2015-11-23 File number: 500-17-060283-101 Other citations: AZ-51234224 — [2015] CarswellQue 11288 — [2015] QJ 12923 Citation: Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc., 2015 QCCS 5514 (CanLII) Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc. 2015 QCCS 5514 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTRÉAL No: 500-17-060283-101 DATE: November 23, 2015 _____________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE Marie-Anne Paquette, S.C.J. _____________________________________________________________________ Transport Desgagnés Inc. DESGAGNÉS TRANSARCTIK INC. NAVIGATION DESGAGNÉS INC. Plaintiffs / Cross-Defendants Lloyds underwriters and institution of Lloyds underwriters (ILU) companies subscribing to Policy number B0856 09H0016 and Aim Insurance (Barbados) SCC Plaintiffs in continuance of suit v. Wärtsilä Canada Inc. Wärtsilä Nederland b.v. Defendants / Cross-Plaintiffs _____________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT _____________________________________________________________________ OVERVIEW [ 1 ] JP2056 The Plaintiffs/Cross-Defendants Transport Desgagnés inc., Desgagnés Tansarctik Inc. and Navigation Desgagnés Inc. ( TDI ) operate a fleet of vessels in the Canadian arctic waters, on the St-Lawrence river and on international waters. The present litigation pertains to one of its ships, the MV Camilla Desgagnés ( Camilla Desgagnés ). [ 2 ] The Defendants/Cross-Plaintiffs Wärtsilä Canada inc. and Wärtsilä Netherlands B.V. are part of the Wärtsilä Group ( Wärtsilä ) and carry business as manufacturer and supplier of marine engines and propulsion systems. [ 3 ] In February 2007, Wärtsilä delivered a new bedplate and reconditioned crankshaft to the Camilla Desgagnés, in the Port of Halifax. [ 4 ] Wärtsilä assembled and mounted the crankshaft onto the bedplate in its factory in Zwolle, Netherlands from November 13 to November 28, 2006. [ 5 ] The reconditioned crankshaft sustained a catastrophic failure on October 27, 2009, while the vessel was on route on the St-Lawrence River near Les Escoumins. [ 6 ] The Court holds that the crankshaft sold was affected by a latent defect at the time of the sale, that Wärtsilä was presumably aware of same and that as a result, it is liable for all the damages resulting from this failure, in accordance with the civil law of Quebec. [ 7 ] TDI’s claim is therefore granted and Wärtsilä’s Counter-Claim for the expenses which it incurred as a result of the failure shall be rejected. 1. ADMISSIONS AND UNCHALLENGED EVIDENCE [ 8 ] The parties admit, subject to all legal and factual arguments against TDI’s claim, that the damages resulting from this failure of the crankshaft amount to $ 5,661,830.33. [ 9 ] It is also undisputed that: 9.1. the failure results form an insufficient tightening of the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L; 9.2. the incident occurred after 13,653 running hours of the engine, which represents 217 000 000 (2.17x10 8 ) stress cycles on the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L; 9.3. The cracks that led to the failure of the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L commenced on the outer edge of the 3 rd thread root in the stud and propagated outwards across the diameter of the stud. 2. POSITION OF THE PARTIES [ 10 ] TDI claims that the improper tightening of the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L is a latent defect which dates back to the assembly of the crankshaft in Wärtsilä’s factory in Zwolle. Wärtsilä, as a manufacturer and professional seller, would thus be liable for the resulting damages. [ 11 ] Wärtsilä denies the presence of a latent defect.  The improper tightening would rather result from manipulations by TDI employees during maintenance work carried on board since the installation of the engine on the Camilla Desgagnés in February 2006. [ 12 ] Wärtsilä also claims that the warranty no longer applies, as the six-month-warranty-period provided in the contract had expired long before the failure occurred. [ 13 ] The applicability of such limitation clause raises a debate as to whether, as Wärtsilä submits, Canadian maritime law applies to the present dispute or whether, as TDI submits, Quebec law applies. [ 14 ] On the one hand, TDI, submits that such limitation clauses are not enforceable in Quebec civil law in the context of this case, which pertains to the liability of the professional seller and manufacturer for latent defects. [ 15 ] On the other hand, Wärtsilä pleads that such limitation is enforceable in Canadian maritime law. 3. ISSUES IN DISPUTE 1.         Does Canadian maritime law or Quebec civil law govern the current dispute? 2.         Based on such governing law, is Wärtsilä liable for the damages resulting from the engine breakdown? 2.1.        Are the opening conditions of the legal warranty against latent defects met? 2.2.        Are the contractual limitation clauses applicable? 4. ANALYSIS 4.1 Does Canadian maritime law or Quebec civil law govern the current dispute? [ 16 ] For the following reasons, the Quebec civil law applies. 4.1.1 What is Canadian maritime law? [ 17 ] Canadian maritime law is not the law of any province of Canada.  It is the product of international conventions. [1] [ 18 ] This body of federal law encompasses the principles of English maritime law as they were developed and applied in the Admiralty Court of England.  Such admiralty law was as incorporated into Canadian law in 1934. [2] It has been amended by the federal Parliament and developed through judicial precedents since then. [3] 4.1.2 When does Canadian maritime law apply? [ 19 ] The constitutional division of powers between the federal government and the legislatures of the provinces is at the root of this governing law issue. [4] [ 20 ] While the laws of the Parliament govern matters of “Navigation and Shipping” ( Constitution Act, 1867 , s. 91(10)), the laws of the relevant province apply to matters of “Property and Civil Rights” ( Constitution Act, 1867 , s. 92(13)). [ 21 ] Presumably, the need to achieve some uniformity and compliance with applicable international law in the regulation of the various aspects of maritime commerce and water traffic has prompted the Fathers of the Confederation to grant the legislative power over navigation and shipping to the Parliament. [5] Such power extends to inland waterwaysm provided that they are navigable. As the Supreme Court of Canada held in Whitbread , the legal rights and obligations of those engaged in navigation and shipping should not arbitrarily change as their vessels cross the point at which the water ceases or, as the case may be, commences to ebb and flow. [6] [ 22 ] Hence, matters relating to the framework of the legal relationships arising out of shipping activities or relating to the infrastructure of navigation and shipping activities fall within the pith and substance of the Parliament’s jurisdiction over navigation and shipping. [7] [ 23 ] For such matters, an encroachment of the provincial power over Property and Civil Rights is justified and federal laws, including Canadian maritime law, apply. [8] [ 24 ] The test uniformly applied to determine whether Canadian maritime law applies is the following: Is the activity at stake so integrally connected to maritime matters such that it is practically necessary for Parliament to have jurisdiction over same, in order to properly exercise its legislative power over navigation and shipping ? [Emphasis added] [ 25 ] Such a line drawing exercise requires an examination of factual context of the claim. [9] The single fact that a ship or maritime undertakings are involved does not automatically trigger the application of Canadian maritime law. [10] For Canadian maritime law to apply, the matter, in view of the factual context, must be integrally connected to maritime matters. 4.1.3 Application to the present case [ 26 ] The current matter relates to the sale of a marine engine. [ 27 ] Issues relating to the obligations arising from such a contract of sale are not integrally connected to the pith and substance of the Parliament’s jurisdiction over navigation and shipping, as defined above [11] . [ 28 ] More particularly, the contract for the sale of a marine engine is not integrally connected, for instance, to issues of safe carriage of goods over the sea, movement of goods on and off a ship (shipping), seaworthiness of a ship or good seamanship (navigation).  It is also not integrally connected to applicable admiralty law, rules, principles or practices or international maritime conventions. [12] [ 29 ] Moreover, there is no practical necessity for uniform federal law to prescribe, for instance, the rules governing the seller’s obligations to provide warranty regarding the quality of the product sold.  The fact that such rules may vary depending on the applicable provincial law of contracts does no hinder the efficient and coherent conduct of the activities of navigation and shipping. [ 30 ] Neither the extraterritorial use of the good sold nor the fact that the good sold will be used in connection with maritime activities drag such sale out of the ambit of the legislature’s power over Property and Civil Rights.  Although related to maritime activities, the current dispute is not integrally connected with same. [ 31 ] Wärtsilä is sued as the seller of the crankshaft and bedplate.  It is not sued as a party performing a contract that pertains to the pith and substance of navigation and shipping activities. [ 32 ] As the contract for the sale of the engine and of the bedplate was formed in Montreal, [13] the laws of Quebec therefore apply to the present dispute. [14] 4.2 Based on such governing law, is Wärtsilä liable for the damages resulting from the engine breakdown? 4.2.1 General principles [ 33 ] They relevant provisions of the Civil Code of Quebec on the legal warranty against latent defects read as follows : III - Warranty of quality 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. […] 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but also to make reparation for the injury suffered by the buyer . 1729. In a sale by a professional seller, a defect is presumed to have existed at the time of the sale if the property malfunctions or deteriorates prematurely in comparison with identical property or property of the same type; such a presumption is rebutted if the defect is due to improper use of the property by the buyer . 1730. The manufacturer, any person who distributes the property under his name or as his own, and any supplier of the property, in particular the wholesaler and the importer, are also bound to warrant the buyer in the same manner as the seller . […] IV – Conventional warranty […] 1733. A seller may not exclude or limit his liability unless he has disclosed the defects of which he was aware or could not have been unaware and which affect the right of ownership or the quality of the property. An exception may be made to this rule where a buyer buys property at his own risk from a seller who is not a professional seller. [Emphasis added] [ 34 ] The legal warranty against latent defects thus comes into play if the defect: 1- was present at the time of the sale; 2- is serious; 3- was hidden and unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale. [ 35 ] In 2007 in Domtar, [15] the Supreme Court of Canada also reviewed the content and contours of the Quebec law on the legal warranty against latent defects, particularly in the case of a sale by a professional seller or a manufacturer.  These principles were subsequently reiterated and applied by the Quebec Court of Appeal. [16] [ 36 ] In the case of sale by a professional seller, two presumptions come into play. These presumptions also apply against the manufacturer and distributor of a good. [ 37 ] Firstly, the defect is presumed to have existed at the time of the sale (article 1729, C.C.Q.).  The first condition stated above is therefore automatically met; unless the seller or the manufacturer proves on the balance of probabilities that the defect is due to improper use by the buyer. [ 38 ] Secondly, the professional seller is presumed to have known the existence of the defect at the time of the sale and is deemed to be of bad faith. [17] [ 39 ] Professional sellers, by definition, are indeed presumably aware of the characteristics of their merchandise.  Manufacturers are also considered to be the ultimate experts with respect to the good sold. They have control over the labour and materials used to produce it. [18] [ 40 ] As the Supreme Court ruled in ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc. [19] , the presumption of knowledge and of bad faith against the professional seller and the manufacturer has a two-fold determinative impact. [20] [ 41 ] Firstly, it extends the scope of their liability beyond the price of the good sold.  In addition to their obligation to reimburse the price of the good sold, they are bound to pay for the damages which the buyer sustained as a result of the latent defect. [ 42 ] Secondly, the professional seller and the manufacturer cannot rely on a limitation of liability clause, unless they rebut the presumption of knowledge and bad faith. As they are presumed to be of bad faith and to be aware of the existence of the defect, allowing them to contractually exclude or limit their liability for such defect would amount to an endorsement of a fraudulent behaviour. [21] [ 43 ] Therefore, only a narrow range of defences are available to the professional seller and the manufacturer if they want to rebut the presumption of knowledge and bad faith against them, and avoid the resulting legal consequences of such presumption. [ 44 ] Evidence of their good faith, of their ignorance of the defect or of their honest belief in the adequacy of the product sold is not enough. [22] [ 45 ] The buyer’s expertise also does not nullify the presumption of knowledge and bad faith which applies against professional sellers and manufacturers.  Such expertise is only relevant to assess whether the defect was apparent or not. [23] [ 46 ] According to the Supreme Court of Canada, the professional seller and the manufacturer can only rebut the presumption of knowledge and bad faith in two ways. They may demonstrate, on a balance of probabilities, that (1) the buyer or a third person caused the defect or that (2) only scientific or technological discoveries made after the good was put on the market would have permitted the defect to be detected at the time the good was put on the market. [72] Even though the possibility of manufacturers rebutting the presumption [(of knowledge)] is accepted, the high standard of diligence they are required to meet means that the range of defences available to them remains very narrow. Only two defences have been recognized so far , and C.E. has invoked neither of them. Under the first of these defences, a manufacturer can rebut the presumption by proving causal fault on the part of the buyer or a third person, or superior force : Manac inc./Nortex, at para. 138; Commentaires du ministre de la Justice, vol. 1, at p. 902. The second defence is that of development risk , but it is still subject to debate in contract matters. This defence enables the manufacturer to avoid liability if it would have been impossible to detect the defect given the state of scientific and technical knowledge at the time the good was put on the market . In such a case, only scientific or technological discoveries made after the good was put on the market will have permitted the defect to be detected. This defence originated in London & Lancashire Guarantee & Accident Co. of Canada v. Cie F.X. Drolet, 1944 CanLII 46 (SCC) , [1944] S.C.R. 82, and is now partially codified in respect of extra-contractual matters in art. 1473 C.C.Q. [24] [Emphasis added] 4.2.2 Application to the present case 4.2.2.1 Are the opening conditions of the legal warranty against latent defects met? [ 47 ] As stated above, the legal warranty against latent defects applies if the defect: 1- was present at the time of the sale; 2- is serious; and 3- was hidden and unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale. [ 48 ] The seriousness and hidden nature of the defect are unchallenged. [ 49 ] The engine failure was a...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\nphasis added] 4.2.2 Application to the present case 4.2.2.1 Are the opening conditions of the legal warranty against latent defects met? [ 47 ] As stated above, the legal warranty against latent defects applies if the defect: 1- was present at the time of the sale; 2- is serious; and 3- was hidden and unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale. [ 48 ] The seriousness and hidden nature of the defect are unchallenged. [ 49 ] The engine failure was a catastrophic event. Replacement of the entire engine was required as a result. [ 50 ] Also, if the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L was insufficiently tightened at the time of the sale, it was indisputably impossible for TDI to notice it. [ 51 ] The debate rather lies with the first condition:  was the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L insufficiently tightened at the time of the sale? [ 52 ] For the following reasons, an affirmative response to this question is warranted. 4.2.2.1.1 Evidence offered to rebut the presumption of existence of the defect at the time of the sale [ 53 ] Pursuant to sections 1729 and 1739 C.C.Q., as the crankshaft and bedplate assembly were sold by a professional seller, there is a presumption that such defect existed at the time of the sale.  Hence, the insufficient tightening of the big end stud of the connecting rod of unit 5L presumably occurred while Wärtsilä employees mounted the crankshaft assembly in Wärtsilä facility in Zwolle, in November 2006. [ 54 ] Wärtsilä claims that such improper tightening results from maintenance work performed by TDI employees after the delivery of the crankshaft assembly; and that the presumption of existence of the defect at the time of the sale is therefore rebutted. 1729. In a sale by a professional seller, a defect is presumed to have existed at the time of the sale if the property malfunctions or deteriorates prematurely in comparison with identical property or property of the same type; such a presumption is rebutted if the defect is due to improper use of the property by the buyer . [Emphasis added] [ 55 ] To show improper maintenance, which is tantamount to improper use within the meaning of section 1729 C.C.Q., Wärtsilä offered the following factual evidence: 55.1. Deformations on the holes of several nuts Deformations were found on the holes of several nuts, [25] showing that significant force was applied, using a hammer against a tool inserted in the holes of the nuts, in attempts to loosen, tighten or test the tension of the nuts. Such manipulations would show the lack of skill and experience of the TDI maintenance crew; 55.2. Incidents in the course of the overhaul of cylinder units 1L and 2R in June 2009 In June 2009, during the overhaul of cylinder units 1L and 2R, [26] the engine room crew would have displayed incompetence, thereby requiring the presence of a senior technician of Wärtisilä, Mr. Hans Wyss, to assist on board. More particularly: 55.2.1. They used the instructions for line engines [27] while they should have followed the instructions for V engines. [28] As a result, they applied 750 bar of pressure, instead of the required 900 bar, to loosen the nuts on the big end studs of the units and were unable to release the nuts; 55.2.2. A small connecting rod of unit 1L was accidentally bent in the course of the operation, when the top part of the bearing was put in place. The bent stud was replaced before the overhaul job was completed; and 55.2.3. In the course of performing the job, the crew found out that they only had one set of smaller bearing shells, while they needed two. They discovered that the second set which they had was of regular size, i.e. 1 mm bigger than needed.  As a result, a new set of bearing shells was ordered and the work was completed after the receipt of same. [29] [ 56 ] The cumulative effect of these errors would show the lack of skill and care of the maintenance team on the Camilla Desgagnés. It would support Wärtsilä’s theory that the improper tightening of the nut on the big end stud of unit 5L results from improper manipulations by the TDI employees. [ 57 ] Wärtsilä also offered expert evidence from Mr. John Poulson, marine engineer, [30] and Mr. Joseph P. Crosson, metallurgical engineer, [31] to rebut the presumption of existence of improper tightening at the time of the sale. [ 58 ] To that effect, Mr. Crosson highlights that the cracks that led to the failure of the 5L big end stud originated from the surface, not from the interior, of the stud.  He further states that fracture initiation sites at the surface are compatible with failure occurring within a few thousands of hours (1,000 – 2,000 hours) [32] of operation after the improper tightening.  In the present case, the failure occurred after 13,653 hours of operation from the initial tightening of the stud.  Hence, the improper torque which led to the fracture cannot date back to the assembly of the engine.  It results from a more recent event, and is thus related to manipulations by TDI employees. [ 59 ] Mr. Poulson adds that improper tightening of such stud causes failure to happen within a relatively short period of time (within hundreds to 2,000 hours of operation). Improper tightening at the Wärtsilä facility in November 2006 is therefore not compatible with his knowledge and experience, as the stud would then have sustained stress from improper tightening for a much longer period, i.e. 13,653 hours of operation. [ 60 ] Mr. Poulson also is of the opinion that the procedure which Wärtsilä followed for the tightening of the studs was very comprehensive and thorough and that Wärtsilä employees complied with same. 4.2.2.1.2 The evidence nevertheless supports the presumption that the defect existed at the time of the sale [ 61 ] Despite the foregoing, the evidence does not show on a balance of probabilities that the improper tightening of the big stud on unit 5L results from manipulations carried on board by TDI employees, after the assembly of the crankshaft at Wärtsilä facility in Zwolle in November 2006. [ 62 ] Rebutting the presumption of article 1729 C.C.Q. indeed requires more than raising doubts on the adequacy of the buyer’s use or maintenance of the good sold. Unless the professional seller or manufacturer shows, on a balance of probabilities, that the defect is due to improper maintenance by the buyer, the presumption that the defect existed at the time of the sale still applies. [33] Such a demonstration may be made either by direct evidence or by presumption. [ 63 ] The evidence on which Wärtsilä relies raises legitimate scientific questioning on the timing of the incident and shows that TDI’s interventions were not perfect at all times and in all respects. However, it falls short of demonstrating that TDI employees have or probably have loosened the nut on the big end stud 5L. [ 64 ] The following findings indeed stem from the contradictory evidence offered and should not be disregarded or underestimated. [ 65 ] Firstly, the deformations of the holes of several nuts, including the nut on the big stud of unit 5L, indicate that TDI maintenance crew has inserted a tool in the holes of several nuts and hit such tool with a hammer in an attempt to tighten, loosen or check the tightness of the nuts.  This is an improper and useless maintenance practice. [ 66 ] All TDI maintenance crew members who were questioned in this regard [34] indicate that they never heard of such a method and never witnessed it on board the Camilla Desgagnés.  This was not the manner in which TDI employees checked the tightness of the nuts.  In this regard, they followed the recommended Wärtsilä method, i.e. sound test when hitting the nut itself with a small hammer. [ 67 ] More importantly, ordinary and expert witnesses [35] unanimously stated that it is impossible to loosen or remove a nut without using hydraulic equipment. Hence, no matter how unusual and improper it may be to hit a tool inserted in the hole of a nut, such a manipulation is useless and has no impact on the torque value of the nuts.  Also, Wärtsilä does not recommend using hydraulic tools during crankcase inspections and to check the tightness of the nuts and TDI acted along this recommendation. [ 68 ] Secondly, the three problems which Wärtsilä raises in connection with the overhaul of units 1L and 2R in June 2009 are immaterial to the issue of interest here: did the TDI employees loosen the nut on the big end stud of unit 5L? [ 69 ] Admittedly, the TDI employees were initially unable to loosen the nuts.  As they were using the instructions for a line engine, and not for a V engine, they were applying insufficient hydraulic pressure (750 bars instead of 900).  In any event, as a result of that mistake, they were unable to loosen the nuts. [ 70 ] Also, the TDI maintenance crew has, in the course of this overhaul, damaged a small stud.  In planning the work, they also overlooked the fact that the second replacement kit of bearing shells which they had on board was fit for the old engine, and thus 1 mm oversized. [ 71 ] These facts also fall short of demonstrating that the TDI employees have or may have wrongfully loosen the nut on big end stud of unit 5L. [ 72 ] Actually, the overhaul performed on unit 1L and 2R was a significant operation. It required several days of work. The fact that the process was not uneventful and the nature of the problems which were encountered in the present case do not show that TDI employees lacked the skill and knowledge to properly maintain and repair the engine on the Camilla Desgagnés. [ 73 ] Moreover, all the work performed during this overhaul was done on units 1L and 2R. Any suggestion to the effect that the manipulations would have accidentally or mistakenly extended to unit 5L is speculative and fanciful. [ 74 ] The evidence on the maintenance procedures which TDI applied on board [36] also does not show that TDI inappropriately maintained the engine and would have inadvertently or negligently loosened the nut on the big end stud of unit 5L. [ 75 ] Also, a service bulletin confirms that studs which are appropriately pre stressed during the initial assembly may nevertheless loosen with time, even in the absence of faulty subsequent manipulations or improper maintenance by the buyer [37] . [ 76 ] Finally, TDI offered convincing expert evidence to show that the defect may very well have existed at the time of the sale. [ 77 ] More particularly, Mrs. Marina Banuta, engineer expert specialized in failure analysis and mechanical breakdowns [38] , states that the findings in this case are compatible with insufficient original tightening, at the time of the assembly of the crankshaft at Wärtsilä facility. [ 78 ] She highlights that the performance and resistance of materials, as well as the technological means of submitting such material to increasing numbers of stress cycles, have significantly progressed in the recent years or decades.  She convincingly explains that as a result, the scientific knowledge on failures occurring after 10 7 stress cycles is relatively recent and that the literature in this regard should therefore not be blindly relied on. [ 79 ] For instance, the existence of a surface fracture initiation site, as opposed to an internal initiation site, would not be incompatible with the failure occurring, such as in this case, after 2.17 x 10 8 stress cycles. She even worked on a case where the failure occurred after 10 7 stress cycles, and where the fracture initiation site was at the surface. [ 80 ] In any event internal inclusions, which Mr. Crosson claims [39] are a sine qua non condition to a finding of failure after10 7 stress cycles, were also found in the Camilla Desgagnés’ specimens which Mrs. Banuta reviewed. [40] [ 81 ] That being said, Mrs. Banuta also makes it clear that the fact that the big end stud of unit 5L complied with all applicable standards of quality, in spite of its several imperfections, is irrelevant. Such imperfections are not defects. However, the improper tightening of the nut on the big end stud of unit 5L, which is a defect, created a stress which turned these acceptable imperfections into the source of the eventual failure. [ 82 ] Mrs. Banuta also explains that the nut was probably not sufficiently tightened (improper tightening) initially.  She rejects the possibility that the nut was not tightened (lack of tightening) initially, as the failure would have occurred much more rapidly in such event. [ 83 ] Mrs. Banuta’s experience is vast, undisputable, highly reliable and relevant to the expert issues in this case.  Wärtsilä also participated in the elaboration of the testing protocol which she followed for the metallurgical investigation conducted after the failure of the engine on the Camilla Desgagnés.  Wärtsilä experts fully relied on the result of her investigations. [ 84 ] The Court holds, based on Mrs. Banuta’s testimony, that the clear-cut opinion of Mr. Crosson on the absence, in this case, of an internal initiation site and of the resulting conclusion that the failure cannot result from improper original tightening, must be considerably nuanced. [ 85 ] Also, the testimony of Mr. Poulson, the other expert of the Defence, should be considered with great caution.  He heavily relied on a staged presentation which Wärtsilä had organized for his benefit, on unverified and on partial information provided by Wärtsilä to make categorical statements which were systematically favourable to the Defence. Greater independence of the expert would probably have better served the Court. [ 86 ] There is thus no probative evidence, be it direct or by presumption, that TDI employees loosened the nut on the big stud of the 5L unit.  The presumption that the defect existed at the time of the sale still applies. [ 87 ] Furthermore, such presumption is reinforced by the flaws demonstrated in the procedure which Wärtsilä followed for the assembly of the crankshaft in November 2006. [ 88 ] More particularly, Wärtsilä had no quality control was in place in Zwolle to ensure that the assembly of the crankshaft was done in accordance with its instruction and with the applicable standards.  Also, no one inspected the bedplate and crankshaft at the end of the assembly. [ 89 ] Mr. Han Noorlander, senior service engineer, was responsible for the assembly of the crankshaft and bedplate at Wärtsilä facility in Zwolle in November 2006. Mr. President, mechanics, and Mr. Kleingeld, ingeneer, worked under Mr. Noorlander’s supervision for this project. [ 90 ] The entire work for this assembly lasted from November 13, 2006 to November 28, 2006. [ 91 ] In addition to supervising the work of Mr. Kleingeld and Mr. President, Mr. Noorlander himself worked directly on the assembly of the bedplate and crankshaft. He was the supervisor of his own work. [ 92 ] Mr. Noorlander himself did not inspect the bedplate and crankshaft at the end of the assembly.  Mr. Noorlander finished his work on November 24, 2006 and did not return to the Zwolle facility after. After the departure of Mr. Noorlander, Mr. Kleingeld nevertheless worked two additional days on the assembly, on November 27 and 28, 2006.  No one supervised his work. [ 93 ] The 12 connecting rods, which each have 2 big end studs such as the one that failed, were assembled on November 21 and 22, 2005 [41] by Mr. Noorlander and Mr. Kleingeld.  This represents 24 (12 x 2) big end studs to stretch and tension with nuts.  To that end, the use of hydraulic tolls along a strict step-by-step protocol [42] had to be applied for each of the 24 big studs. [ 94 ] No checklist of the procedure was kept or followed.  Wärtsilä’s service report, [43] which is the only document concerning the work performed on the assembly of the crankshaft and bedplate, does not (1) identify the parts used in the assembly, (2) describe how the parts were mounted, (3) identify the instructions which were followed to assemble the crankshaft, (4) identify which verifications were done in the course of the assembly, and (5) indentify which approval was done of the assembly after the work was completed. [ 95 ] The assembly of the connecting rod was done during the period of time where Mr. Noorlandar worked 10 hours a day for three consecutive days (with a two-hour drive in the morning and a two-hour drive at the end of the working day). [ 96 ] In this overall context, given the series of consecutive precise manipulations necessary to make in order to stretch and tension the big studs, a step may have been missed or overlooked when tensioning one of the big studs on unit 5L. [ 97 ] The service report also includes inaccuracies which illustrate, with all due respect for Mr. Noorlander, how lenient Wärtsilä was in the planning, supervision and conduct of this work. [ 98 ] This improper torque of the nut of the big stud on\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n96 ] In this overall context, given the series of consecutive precise manipulations necessary to make in order to stretch and tension the big studs, a step may have been missed or overlooked when tensioning one of the big studs on unit 5L. [ 97 ] The service report also includes inaccuracies which illustrate, with all due respect for Mr. Noorlander, how lenient Wärtsilä was in the planning, supervision and conduct of this work. [ 98 ] This improper torque of the nut of the big stud on unit 5L was therefore present at the time of the sale. 4.2.2.2 Are the contractual limitation clauses applicable? [ 99 ] The contract which the parties entered into incorporates Wärtsilä’s standard terms and conditions for spare parts. [44] Section 6.3 of such standard terms and conditions, as modified at TDI’s request, provide that the warranty runs for 6 months from the time the Camilla Desgagnés returned to service [45] . They also limit Wärtsilä’s liability to € 50,000 (CAN $ 79,000). [46] [ 100 ] However, these limitation provisions are not enforceable in the case at hand, as Wärtsilä did not rebut the presumption of knowledge of the existence of the defect. [ 101 ] As mentioned above, [47] Wärtsilä’s bad faith and knowledge of the existence of the defect at the time of the sale is presumed.  In this context, any contractual exclusion or limitation of its liability for latent defect is considered fraudulent.  In principle, Wärtsilä is thus not authorized to contractually limit its obligations in respect of latent defects. [ 102 ] Neither of the two defences which the manufacturer can raise in such instances [48] to claim the benefit of warranty limitation provisions was demonstrated. [ 103 ] No such demonstration was made. [ 104 ] Wärtsilä is thus liable for the defect in the crankshaft and bedplate assembly which it delivered to TDI in February 2007 and for all the related damages. [ 105 ] Quantum is admitted at $ 5,661,830.33. 4.3 Wärtsilä’s Cross-Demand [ 106 ] TDI has no obligation to pay for expenses which Wärtsilä incurred in relation to a latent defect which Wärtsilä presumably knew existed at the time it sold the bedplate and crankshaft assembly to TDI and eventually caused the complete loss of the material sold, in addition to other damages. [ 107 ] The Cross-Demand shall thus be dismissed. WHEREFORE THE COURT: [ 108 ] GRANTS Plaintiffs’ Motion; [ 109 ] ORDERS the Defendants jointly and severally to pay to the Plaintiffs the sum of $ 5,661,830.33 together with interest and additional indemnity from October 27, 2009; [ 110 ] THE WHOLE with costs, including the costs of expertise. __________________________________ MARIE-ANNE PAQUETTE, S.C.J. Me Danièle Dion Me Victor De Marco BRISSET BISHOP Attorney for the Plaintiffs Me George Pollack Me Vincent Boutet-Lehouillier DAVIS WARD PHILLIPS & VINEBERG LLP Attorney for the Defendants Hearing dates: September 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 21, 22, 24, 25, 28 and 29, 2015 TABLE OF CONTENTS OVERVIEW............................................................................................................................... 1 1. ADMISSIONS AND UNCHALLENGED EVIDENCE................................................. 2 2. POSITION OF THE PARTIES........................................................................................ 2 3. ISSUES IN DISPUTE...................................................................................................... 3 4. ANALYSIS......................................................................................................................... 3 4.1 Does Canadian maritime law or Quebec civil law govern the current dispute?          3 4.1.1 What is Canadian maritime law?............................................. 3 4.1.2 When does Canadian maritime law apply?........................... 4 4.1.3 Application to the present case................................................ 5 4.2 Based on such governing law, is Wärtsilä liable for the damages resulting from the engine breakdown?......................................................................................................................... 6 4.2.1 General principles...................................................................... 6 4.2.2 Application to the present case................................................ 9 4.2.2.1 Are the opening conditions of the legal warranty against latent defects met?                                                                                                        9 4.2.2.1.1 Evidence offered to rebut the presumption of existence of the defect at the time of the sale     9 4.2.2.1.2 The evidence nevertheless supports the presumption that the defect existed at the time of the sale....................................................................................................... 11 4.2.2.2 Are the contractual limitation clauses applicable?............. 16 4.3 ........ Wärtsilä’s Cross-Demand................................................................................. 17 WHEREFORE THE COURT:............................................................................................... 17 [1] Whitbread v. Walley , 1990 CanLII 33 (SCC) , [1990] 3 S.C.R. 1273, 1294-1296. [2] Admiralty Act, 1934 , 1934 (Can.), c. 31, s. 18. [3] ITO – Int’l Terminal Operators v Miida Electronics, 1986 CanLII 91 (SCC) , [1986] 1 S.C.R. 752, 770-771; British Columbia Marine Shipbuilders Ltd. v F.M. Yorke & Son Ltd., 1981 CanLII 182 (SCC) , [1981] 1 S.C.R. 363, par. 19 . [4] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 20; ITO – Int’l Terminal Operators v Miida Electronics, 1986 CanLII 91 (SCC) , [1986] 1 S.C.R. 752, 774. [5] 9171-7701 Québec inc. (Les Surplus JT) v. Her majesty the Queen , [2013] FC 832, par. 33, 44. [6] Whitbread v. Walley , 1990 CanLII 33 (SCC) , [1990] 3 S.C.R. 1273, 1295. [7] Marine Services v. Ryan Estate, [2013] 53, par. 61; Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 25. [8] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 24, 28; Monk Corp v. Island Fertilizers Ltd , 1991 CanLII 95 (SCC) , [1991] 1 S.C.R. 779, 795, 797, 800; ITO – Int’l Terminal Operators v Miida Electronics, 1986 CanLII 91 (SCC) , [1986] 1 S.C.R. 752, 774. [9] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 21; Ordon Estate v. Grail , 1998 CanLII 771 (SCC) , [1998] 3 S.C.R. 437, par. 73 .. [10] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, par. 26; Monk Corp v. Island Fertilizers Ltd , 1991 CanLII 95 (SCC) , [1991] 1 S.C.R. 779, 797. [11] See par. [22] of the present judgment. [12] Isen v. Simms , [2006] 349, pars. 22, 28. [13] Exhibits P-2, P-15, P-19. [14] Article 1387 C.C.Q. [15] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461. [16] Entreprises d'électricité Rial inc. v. Lumen, division de Sonepar Canada inc. , 2010 QCCA 655 , pars. 31-44; Ferme avicole Héva inc. v. Coopérative fédérée de Québec, 2008 QCCA 1053 , pars. 73-75, 82-87; Fédération, compagnie d'assurances du Canada v. Joseph Élie ltée , 2008 QCCA 582 , pars. 23-27. [17] Article 1728 C.C.Q. [18] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 40-41, 44, 59, 60. [19] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461. [20] Articles 1728, 1733, C.C.Q.; ABB Inc. v Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 56, 106. [21] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 72, 73, 102. [22] Id. , pars. 69, 102, 105. [23] Id., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 42, 44. [24] ABB Inc. v. Domtar Inc., [2007] S.C.R. 461, pars. 69, 72. [25] Exhibits P-139, P-149, P-150, D-127A, D-127B. [26] Exhibit D-65 (P-25), P-77C. [27] Exhibit D-58. [28] Exhibit D-57. [29] Exhibits P-64, P-153, U-1. [30] Exhibit D-155. [31] Exhibit D-142. [32] This estimation is based on the calculation of the number of stress cycles which occur during an hour of operation of the engine at stake. Mr. Crosson’s calculations to convert the number of stress cycles into hours of operation is unchallenged. [33] Fédération, compagnie d'assurances du Canada v. Joseph Élie ltée , 2008 QCCA 582 , pars. 31, 36-37. [34] Mr. Ruslan Shylko, M. Patrick Chabot. [35] Mr. Ruslan Shylko, Mrs Marina Banuta, Mr. Poulson, Mr. Crosson. [36] Exhibits P-12, P-59, P-60, P-62, P-75, P-77, P-77A, P-133. [37] Exhibit-38B. [38] Exhibits P-38, P-38A. [39] Submission based, inter alia on scientific article by Mr. Claude Bathias,Claude BATHIAS and Paul C. PARIS, Gigacycle Fatigue in mechanical practice , New York, Marcel Dekker, p. 93, figure 4.4. [40] For instance, see Exhibit P-38, fig. 37. [41] Exhibits D-18, D-38. [42] Tension is to be applied the following way and in the following order for each of the 24 big studs: (1) 250 bars of pressure, (2) down to zero, (3) 900 bars of pressure, (4) down to zero, (5) 900 bars of pressure, (6) down to zero, (7) 900 bars of pressure, (8) down to zero. [43] Exhibit D-38. [44] Exhibit D-1. [45] Exhibits P-2, item E); D-3. [46] Exhibit D-133. [47] See par. [42] of the present judgment. [48] See par. [46] of the present judgment.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02560386434197426, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\", \"title\": \"Transport Desgagnés inc. c. Wärtsilä Canada Inc., 2015 QCCS 5514 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02560386434197426, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\"}, \"token_count\": 10716, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2015qccs5514-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq13767\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq13767-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Weng c. Suh, 2017 QCCQ 13767 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2017 QCCQ 13767 (CanLII) | Weng c. Suh | CanLII Date: 2017-11-20 File number: 500-32 149959-159 Citation: Weng c. Suh, 2017 QCCQ 13767 (CanLII) Weng c. Suh 2017 QCCQ 13767 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32 149959-159 DATE: November 20, 2017 ______________________________________________________________________ BEFORE THE HONOURABLE ENRICO FORLINI, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ JIAN WENG Plaintiff v. KENNETH SUH and RENÉE HUGHES Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Jian Weng claims $14,946.75 from Kenneth Suh and Renée Hughes on the grounds that the property he purchased from them was afflicted with a latent defect at the time of the sale.  Specifically, he argues that the wooden front balcony was tilted and in danger of collapsing because the wooden support posts were rotten. [ 2 ] Defendants deny all liability.  They argue that the property was more than a century old and thus the problem the Mr. Weng complains cannot qualify as a latent defect. Moreover, they claim that the Mr. Weng’s notice of latent defect is deficient because the repairs were already carried out when they received the notice. Questions in Dispute a) Does the defect alleged by Mr. Weng constitute a latent defect within the meaning of article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec (C.C.Q.)? b) Did Mr. Weng provide proper notice of the latent defect to the Defendants? c) If so, what amount of damages is Mr. Weng entitled to? Context [ 3 ] On October 10, 2014, Mr. Weng purchased a property from defendants with legal warranty and paid $845,000 (“Property”). [1] The Property is a three storey row house duplex located in Westmount and built in 1894. [2] Two wooden balconies supported by wooden posts are on the façade of the Property. [ 4 ] Mr. Weng purchased the Property as an investment property and rented the two apartments. [ 5 ] The Defendants had purchased the property in April 2012 and were occupying the ground floor apartment in June 2013 when they moved to the Greater Toronto Area. [ 6 ] In late April 2015, the Mr. Weng discovered that the floor of the balconies were tilting and cracks appeared in the wooden structure.  He hired a building inspector, Jean‑Claude Trudeau, to inspect the balconies. [ 7 ] Mr. Trudeau’s inspection carried out on or about April 29, 2015, revealed that there was a risk of collapse of the balcony due to the advanced state of decay of the wooden support posts. [3] [ 8 ] On April 29, 2015, Mr. Weng sent an email and a letter to the Defendants to inform them the balcony was at risk of collapsing and that urgent repairs would be carried out. [4] [ 9 ] By demand letter dated October 6, 2015, Mr. Weng enjoined the Defendants to pay $14,946.75 for the costs of emergency reinforcement of the balcony and repairs. [5] [ 10 ] By letter dated December 2, 2015, Defendants denied liability arguing notably that the Notice of Latent Defect was deficient in that they were deprived of the opportunity to inspect the balcony prior to the repairs being carried out. [6] [ 11 ] However, on November 25, 2015, Mr. Weng had already filed his application with the Court of Quebec, Small Claims Division. Analysis and Decision a) Does the defect alleged by Mr. Weng constitute a latent defect within the meaning of article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec (C.C.Q.)? [ 12 ] Mr. Weng’s claim is based on the legal warranty against latent defects, which is codified in article 1726 C.C.Q. and which reads as follows: III. Warranty of quality 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale , free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound , however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect ; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. (Underlining added) [ 13 ] To succeed on a claim based on this warranty, the buyer must prove the following four conditions: • The problem must be a serious defect; • The problem must have existed at the time of the sale; • The problem was hidden or not apparent; and • The defect has to be unknown to the buyer. [7] [ 14 ] Moreover, the Civil Code also requires, that the buyer must give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. [8] [ 15 ] There is no real issue in this litigation that the problem with the balcony was unknown to Mr. Weng at the time of the sale, that it was hidden or that it existed at the time of the sale. Rather, the issues raised by Mr. Weng’s claim and the Defendants’ contestation pertain to (i) the first condition, i.e. whether the state of the balcony constitutes a serious defect for the purposes of article 1726 C.C.Q. and (ii) whether the notice of latent defect was adequate. [ 16 ] The Property was 120 years old at the time of the sale.  The evidence also reveals that the cause of the potential collapse of the balcony is because the wooden posts supporting the balcony were decaying and rotten. [9] [ 17 ] However, the courts of the Province of Québec have consistently held that time worn or outdated building materials or components, normal wear and tear, and the end of the useful life of a building component do not constitute latent defects covered by the legal warranty of quality. [10] [ 18 ] For example, in Ouellet v. Parent [11] , the buyer argued that the rotten wooden support beams of a hundred year old house constituted a latent defect. The Superior Court dismissed the lawsuit on the following grounds: [23] La preuve révèle que la poutre centrale et les poutres transversales étaient vraisemblablement pourries. Cet état de fait ne pouvait être décelé sans l’enlèvement de la laine minérale. Le caractère caché du vice est indéniable. Il était cependant ignoré de monsieur Parent car il n’a procédé à aucuns travaux d’isolation au sous-sol. La laine minérale qui recouvrait les poutres était en place lorsque monsieur Parent a acheté la maison en 1994. Il ne l’a jamais enlevée. [24] Par ailleurs, monsieur Parent n’a jamais visualisé l’état « de la poutre de façade de la résidence ». [25] Lorsqu’il a refait la galerie, en 2013, le madrier de base lui paraissait sain et en bon état. [26] Il a enlevé le support et la planche sans retirer le madrier déjà en place, après en avoir vérifié la solidité à coups de marteau. [27] Par précaution, il a ajouté un madrier neuf pour solidifier la galerie. Il affirme toutefois qu’il aurait pu « partir » l’assise de la galerie sur le madrier de base. Ainsi, les planches de la galerie reposent sur les deux madriers. [28] Rappelons que monsieur Parent n’est pas un spécialiste de la construction. Il est un buandier retraité depuis 2015. Les réparations à la maison effectuées par lui ne sont pas de grande envergure et pour plusieurs relèvent de l’entretien. [29] Monsieur Parent est catégorique : - Que ce soit par manque d’attention ou non, il n’a pas vu de pourriture lors de la réfection de la galerie; - Il n’a pas constaté que le solage était bombé; - Il n’a pas constaté de partie manquante (photo P-9-2). [30] À l’instruction, monsieur Parent réitère ce qui suit : « La lisse, je le sais pas mais je sais que quand j’ai refaite la galerie, que le madrier qui a tenu la galerie avait d’lair solide. » [31] Il s’agit d’un maison centenaire et le Tribunal estime que la pourriture des poutres relève de la vétusté. [32] La jurisprudence reconnaît que la vétusté, l’usure normale, l’atteinte de la durée de vie utile ne constituent pas des vices cachés couverts par la garantie légale : « L’acheteur le moindrement prudent et avisé se sentira obligé, avant d’acheter une vieille maison, de l’examiner soigneusement et d’anticiper certaines réparations en regard de l’âge de l’immeuble et de ses composantes. » [33] En d’autres mots : « [68] […] La garantie contre les vices cachés s’éteignant progressivement, la détérioration due au vieillissement ou à la vétusté normale d’un bien ou de ses composantes ne constitue pas en principe un vice caché. […] » [34] Il est d’ores et déjà reconnu qu’en présence d’une maison centenaire, l’acheteur doit être vigilent s’il constate des éléments pouvant laisser croire à un vice. La Cour d’appel réitère ce principe dans l’arrêt Gélinas c. Beaumier : « L’acheteur d’une maison centenaire doit prévoir qu’il trouvera des détériorations dues à la vétusté et s’il ne fait aucune réserve, il est censé accepter la maison avec les défauts résultant de la façon de construire à l’époque et les détériorations dues à l’ancienneté. » (Citations omitted) [ 19 ] In light of this case law, the Court considers that the rotten wooden support posts of the balcony do not constitute a latent defect for the purposes of article 1726 C.C.Q. [ 20 ] Given this conclusion, the Court need not address the second and third questions in dispute. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES Jian Weng’s Application against Kenneth Suh and Renée Hughes; WITH COSTS of $200. __________________________________ ENRICO FORLINI, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: July 17, 2017 [1] P-6: Notarized Deed of Sale. [2] P-9: Property Inspection Report: see photo of Property on the cover of the Report; P-8: Sellers’ Declaration. [3] P-3: Jean-Claude Trudeau Inspection Report. [4] P-10: Notice of Latent Defect email and letter. [5] P-1: Demand letter. [6] D-1: Defendants’ response letter. [7] Leroux v. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 , para.40. [8] Article 1739 C.C.Q. [9] P-13: Global Inspect Report, December 16, 2015; see also P-3: Jean-Claude Trudeau Report. [10] Gélinas v. Beaumier , 1989 CanLII 1171 (QC CA) , [1990] R.D.I. 23, [1989] R.L. 595 (Q.C.A.); Iammateo v. Forget , 2010 QCCS 6385 , para. 64 ; Aulanier v. Vergil 2012 QCCQ 7413 , para. 20 ; Ouellet v. Parent , 2016 QCCS 5016 [11] 2016 QCCS 5016 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq13767-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.029957523569464684, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq13767-en\", \"title\": \"Weng c. Suh, 2017 QCCQ 13767 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq13767-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.029957523569464684, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq13767-en\"}, \"token_count\": 2567, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2017qccq13767-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2020qccq2887\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2020qccq2887-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Clark c. Abraham, 2020 QCCQ 2887 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2020 QCCQ 2887 (CanLII) | Clark c. Abraham | CanLII Date: 2020-06-10 File number: 500-32-705225-185 Citation: Clark c. Abraham, 2020 QCCQ 2887 (CanLII) Clark c. Abraham 2020 QCCQ 2887 COURT OF QUEBEC “Small Claims Division” CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL LOCALITY OF MONTREAL “Civil Division” No.: 500-32-705225-185 DATE: June 10, 2020 ______________________________________________________________________ PRESIDING: THE HONOURABLE JO ANN ZAOR, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ CRYSTAL CLARK and MARVIN TAYLOR Plaintiffs v. MOLLY ABRAHAM Defendant and FIRME D’INSPECTION EN BÂTIMENT MICHEL DESROSIERS & FILS Third party intervenor ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Crystal Clark and Marvin Taylor claim $15,000 from Molly Abraham in damages for latent defects as a reduction of the purchase price of the immovable that they purchased from her. [ 2 ] More specifically, the plaintiffs allege that poorly sealed windows and defective flashing caused the water damage to the basement of their residence on August 16, 2016. [ 3 ] Ms. Abraham contests the claim. She argues that her immovable had no defects at the time of the sale and that the plaintiffs are using the claim to finance the renovation of the basement. [ 4 ] In the event she is condemned, Ms. Abraham asks that the building inspection firm that the plaintiffs hired to perform the pre-purchase inspection of her immovable, Michel Desrosiers & fils ( Desrosiers ), be forced to intervene. [ 5 ] Desrosiers did not file a defence to the application for forced intervention or appear at the hearing. ISSUE IN DISPUTE [ 6 ] The issues before the Court are as follows: (a) Did the immovable purchased by Ms. Clark and Mr. Taylor have a latent defect at the time of the sale? (b) If so, what is the amount of the reduction in the purchase price to which the plaintiffs are entitled? (c) Is the application for forced intervention against Desrosiers founded in fact and in law? BACKGROUND [ 7 ] On June 13, 2016, the plaintiffs promised to purchase an immovable located on Westluke Avenue in Montreal ( the immovable ) from Ms. Abraham for an amount of $375,000 (D-2 bundled). According to the information on the descriptive listing (P-1), the immovable is a semi-detached bungalow built in 1954. [ 8 ] After the usual negotiations, Ms. Abraham accepted the plaintiffs’ offer to purchase on June 17, 2016 (P-3). [ 9 ] On June 24, 2016, at the plaintiffs’ request, Desrosiers conducted a pre-purchase inspection of the immovable. The inspector had the seller’s declaration (P-2), and he completed and signed his report that same day. [ 10 ] On July 8, 2016, the parties signed the deed of sale before notary Maxime Major-Lacombe. The sale was made with legal warranty and the plaintiffs took possession of the immovable on July 20, 2016 (P-4). [ 11 ] On August 16, 2016, it rained in Montreal. [ 12 ] Mr. Taylor explained that his wife called him at work to tell him that the basement of their residence had flooded. He quickly went home to assess the damage. [ 13 ] According to the daily precipitation report provided by Ms. Abraham, the metropolitan region received a little over 39 mm of rain on August 16, 2016 (D-1). [ 14 ] In the following days, the plaintiffs sought the advice of their building inspector and of their notary Major-Lacombe. [ 15 ] Mr. Taylor also tried to reach Ms. Abraham but ended up having to leave a message with her daughter. [ 16 ] At the same time, Mr. Taylor called Ms. Abraham’s real estate agent, Aleksandra Mieszkowska, to explain the situation. [ 17 ] In September 2016, the plaintiffs still had not heard from Ms. Abraham, so they sent her a written notice of disclosure of the defect. They asked her to schedule an appointment with them at the residence so that they could find an acceptable solution. (P-6). [ 18 ] On October 3, 2016, Ms. Abraham acknowledged receipt of their notice of disclosure and told the plaintiffs that she considered their claims entirely false and unfounded (P-7). [ 19 ] On December 20, 2016, the plaintiffs hired the professional services of engineer and building inspector Stéphane Bossus to identify the most probable cause of the water infiltration and to suggest any corrective work required by the facts observed. [ 20 ] In his report dated March 20, 2017, Stéphane Bossus wrote « que des infiltrations d’eau perdurent dans l’immeuble depuis fort longtemps, et ce de façon récurrente » (P- 5). [ 21 ] In a letter dated April 24, 2017, the plaintiffs again invited the defendant to come to their residence to assess the damage (P-8). [ 22 ] The plaintiffs, who had no news from the defendant, undertook $36,725 of repair work in the summer of 2017. [ 23 ] On April 25, 2018, the plaintiffs instituted their claim before the Small Claims Division and voluntarily waived any claim exceeding the amount of $15,000. ANALYSIS AND DECISION [ 24 ] First, let us set out the analytical framework of an action for latent defects. [ 25 ] In civil matters, the burden of proof rests on the plaintiff according to the principle of the balance of probabilities: the plaintiff must submit to the judge evidence that surpasses and dominates that of the defendant. [1] [ 26 ] The plaintiffs’ claim raises the issue of the applicability of the warranty of quality owed by a seller to a buyer pursuant to article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec (C.C.Q.): 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. [ 27 ] The criteria for applying this warranty are not controversial. They come from the case law, which is consistent and abundant. [2] In Leroux c. Gravano , [3] the Court of Appeal reiterated that to maintain an action for latent defects, the Court must find the following: • the property is affected by a serious defect, that is, a defect that renders the property unfit for its use or that significantly diminishes its usefulness; • the defect existed at the time of the sale; • the defect was latent. This notion is assessed objectively and is accompanied by the buyer’s duty to inform himself or herself; and • the defect was not known to the buyer. [ 28 ] In addition to these four criteria, article 1739 of the Civil Code of Québec provides that the purchaser must give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. [ 29 ] Although it has been recognized that a prudent and diligent buyer is not required to « ouvrir les murs » or « creuser les fondations », [4] a sign can ground suspicion and require the buyer to conduct a more thorough examination. Indeed, the reasonable buyer who has a serious doubt must investigate further. [5] [ 30 ] The particular circumstances of each case will determine whether an action will fail or succeed. It all depends on the assessment of the evidence. [ 31 ] Let us now apply this analytical framework to the facts of this case. (a) Did the immovable purchased by Ms. Clark and Mr. Taylor have a latent defect at the time of the sale? [ 32 ] In this case, Ms. Abraham’s defence rests essentially on a denial of any defect affecting her immovable. [ 33 ] Her testimony, along with that of her real estate agent and of her daughter Shermine, was to state that the immovable was free from any issue that could have caused water infiltration. [ 34 ] Ms. Abraham emphasized that her daughter and granddaughter lived in her basement, as did lodgers, which was only possible because the immovable met certain compliance requirements. [ 35 ] Ms. Abraham firmly denies that the issue reported by the plaintiffs can be attributed to the immovable; rather, she ascribes it to the plaintiffs’ actions [ 36 ] With respect, and for the reasons that follow, this defence of negation does not withstand the analysis of the facts presented by the plaintiffs. [ 37 ] The evidence establishes that the plaintiffs’ immovable suffered water damage on August 16, 2016. The plaintiffs both assert it, and the Court believes them. Even if the defendant doubts their testimony, the Court cannot give credence to her position because it has no basis; she did not visit the property in spite of an invitation to do so. [ 38 ] The extent of the water infiltration renders the property unfit for its use and significantly diminishes its usefulness. Therefore, the seriousness of the defect has been established. [ 39 ] Next, the plaintiffs claim that they were unaware of the leakage issues of the basement windows at the time of purchase. In short, they state that the defect was unknown to them. [ 40 ] Since the buyer is not subject to a presumption of knowledge, it was up to Ms. Abraham to prove that her purchasers were aware of this issue. [6] It comes as no surprise that the defendant did not adduce such evidence because she denies that the claim is well founded and insists that her immovable had no defects at the time of the sales transaction. [ 41 ] In doing so, she established the unknown nature of the defect for the buyers. [ 42 ] The case law teaches that the latency of the defect is assessed objectively, that is, by reviewing the adequacy of the buyer’s examination of the property in light of what a prudent and diligent buyer of identical expertise would have done. [7] [ 43 ] The plaintiffs claim that they visited the property three times and conducted a reasonable, careful and serious examination. They noticed a stain on the basement carpet and asked the defendant for an explanation. They were satisfied with her answer that it was just dirty. [ 44 ] The plaintiffs claim that they never noticed any signs of water infiltration in the basement. They also say that there was no reason to doubt Ms. Abraham’s declaration that there had never been any water infiltration in her immovable, and specifically, never in the basement (P-2 bundled, to question D3). [ 45 ] Mr. Taylor noted that they had hired Desrosiers to assist them, thereby establishing how seriously they examined the property before purchasing it. [ 46 ] It is appropriate to note here that the defendant did not allege that the plaintiffs tried to avoid or shorten visits of her immovable. Quite the contrary. Ms. Abraham stated that the plaintiffs visited at least seven times before presenting their promise to purchase. Real estate agent Aleksandra Mieszkowska confirmed this, and it implies that they had the opportunity to see what there was to see. [ 47 ] At the hearing, she repeated that the stains on the basement carpet were not from water damage, but from dirt. The expert Bossus denied that fact. After cutting the carpet, he noted the presence of mould on the planking of the subfloor and on the underside of the underpad. He stated that the combination of those findings and the volumetric swelling of the subfloor is evidence of recurrent water leakage (P-5 at 26). [ 48 ] Clearly, the defendant’s explanations do not counter the observations and conclusions of the expert Bossus. [ 49 ] That being said, at the time of the pre-purchase visits, there were no indications or signs alerting the plaintiffs to the issue of water infiltration, and although the stain on the carpet may have been a sign, the defendant’s reassuring remarks concealed it. As a result, the hidden nature of the defect has been proved. [ 50 ] The last criteria, that is, the anteriority of the defect, remains to be assessed. In this case, the plaintiffs must prove that the defect was present when they acquired the residence on July 8, 2016. [ 51 ] The plaintiffs’ evidence rests essentially on the expert Bossus’s written report. In his opinion, there had been recurrent water infiltration in the immovable for a very long time (P-5 at 28). [ 52 ] His conclusion is based on spray tests aimed at reproducing the effect of rain, which established that the water infiltrated mainly through the basement window frames and the joint between the top of the foundation wall and the masonry. [ 53 ] Expert Bossus wrote that the water coming from the masonry facings shows that the flashing was inadequate or missing, a condition that was hidden from everyone when the pre-purchase inspection visits took place (P-5 at 40). [ 54 ] Last, Mr. Bossus noted that the advanced state of rot of some of the structures of the subfloor support the idea that the water infiltration had been occurring in the immovable for a long time (P-5 at 41). [ 55 ] Ms. Abraham’s general denial defence is insufficient to contradict the opinion of the expert Bossus. [ 56 ] As a result, the plaintiffs have clearly established, on a balance of probabilities, that the Westluke Avenue immovable was affected by a latent defect when they purchased it in July 2016. [ 57 ] Let us now address the issue of damages. (b) What is the amount of the reduction in the purchase price to which the plaintiffs are entitled? [ 58 ] In their written claim, the plaintiffs allege that they spent an amount of $36,725 for urgent corrective work. They produced invoices from the firm Bilbao rénovations inc. and invoices for material they purchased (P-9 bundled). [ 59 ] Only $15,000 is claimed before the Tribunal. [ 60 ] In matters of compensation for the partial interference with property, the Court must find a balance between the following two imperatives: on the one hand, it must ensure that the victim is not enriched by the compensation awarded, and on the other hand, it must avoid leaving the victim in a situation that does not correspond to full restitution. [ 61 ] In this case, the Court must place the plaintiffs in the situation they would have been in had the defendant fulfilled her obligation towards them by finding that the renovation of their basement results in an increase in value for them that must be reduced. [ 62 ] According to the description in the Bilbao invoices, it is clear that significant work was carried out in the basement. Among other things, the Court notes the demolition and reconstruction of all of the walls and floors and the ceiling, the installation of new doors, the construction of closets, the replacement of the stairs, electrical work, the installation of lights, etc. [ 63 ] It appears, therefore, that in the summer of 2017, Bilbao rebuilt the plaintiffs’ entire basement. [ 64 ] Mr. Taylor justified the extent of the renovations by the discovery along the way of poor workmanship and defects. [ 65 ] That may be true, but it is not a direct consequence of the water damage on August 16, 2016. [ 66 ] It should be noted that Ms. Abraham’s immovable was built in 1954, according to the standards of that time. Those standards may no longer be current or there may be poor workmanship. The plaintiffs cannot raise the issue with the defendant, however, unless they had previously disclosed the situation to her and provided evidence of the defects they invoke. [ 67 ] As a result, only the damage and nothing but the damage resulting from the water damage can be compensated here. [ 68 ] Without the contractor’s presence, it is difficult, if not impossible, to determine which of the costs and the tasks performed are directly related to the water damage in August 2016. [ 69 ] It was incumbent upon the plaintiffs to administer that evidence, which they did not do. [ 70 ] However, since there was a clear fault committed by the defendant and damage resulted, the Court must establish the approximate fair compensation for the damage, with the information available to it. [8] [ 71 ] Given the lack of documentary evidence, the Court will use its discretion and establish the claim at $5,000. [ 72 ] In light of the conclusion with respect to the existence of a latent defect, it is not necessary for the Court to address the last issue in dispute. [ 73 ] Indeed, Ms. Abraham did not prove that Desrosiers poorly executed his property assessment. FOR THESE REASONS, the Court: GRANTS the plaintiffs’ action in part; CONDEMNS Molly Abraham to pay $5,000, with interest at the legal rate, plus the additional indemnity under article 1619 of the Civil Code of Québec , as of April 24, 2017, the date of the second formal notice; DISMISSES the forced intervention of Molly Abraham directed against Firme d’inspection en bâtiment Michel Desrosiers & Fils; THE WHOLE with legal costs of $202, representing the legal stamp. __________________________________ JO ANN ZAOR, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: March 3, 2020 [1] Arts. 2803 and 2804 C.C.Q. [2] Lussier c. Locas , 2004 CanLII...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n24, 2017, the date of the second formal notice; DISMISSES the forced intervention of Molly Abraham directed against Firme d’inspection en bâtiment Michel Desrosiers & Fils; THE WHOLE with legal costs of $202, representing the legal stamp. __________________________________ JO ANN ZAOR, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: March 3, 2020 [1] Arts. 2803 and 2804 C.C.Q. [2] Lussier c. Locas , 2004 CanLII 76488 (QC CQ) , Landry, J.C.Q. at para. 25. [3] 2016 QCCA 79 . [4] Ibid . at para . 44. See also Placement Jacpar inc. c. Benzakour 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) . [5] Ibid. at para. 32. [6] Gendron c. Cartier , 2006 QCCQ 5793 ; Beaudet c. Bastien , 2007 QCCQ 13454 ; ABB Inc. v . Domtar Inc . , 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461 at para. 54 . [7] ABB Inc. v . Domtar Inc . , 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 S.C.R. 461 at paras. 51 , 98 et seq . [8] According to the rules established in Hydro Québec c. Construction Kiewit cie , 2014 QCCA 947 at para. 101 ; Pincourt (Ville de) c. Construction Cogerex ltée , 2013 QCCA 1773 at para. 271 .\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2020qccq2887-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.020754318684339523, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2020qccq2887-en\", \"title\": \"Clark c. Abraham, 2020 QCCQ 2887 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2020qccq2887-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.020754318684339523, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2020qccq2887-en\"}, \"token_count\": 4545, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2020qccq2887-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2006qccq13856\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2006qccq13856-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Ermacora c. Nadler, 2006 QCCQ 13856 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2006 QCCQ 13856 (CanLII) | Ermacora c. Nadler | CanLII Date: 2006-12-01 File number: 500-32-090411-051 Citation: Ermacora c. Nadler, 2006 QCCQ 13856 (CanLII) Ermacora c. Nadler 2006 QCCQ 13856 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL TOWN OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-090411-051 DATE: December 1 st , 2006 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE MICHEL A. PINSONNAULT, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ A. ELIZABETH ERMACORA , 41 Merton Crescent, Hampstead (Québec), H3X 1L8 Plaintiff v. JOSEPH Y. NADLER , 5885 Cavendish, Cote-St-Luc (Québec), H4W 3H4 Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiff, A. Elizabeth Ermacora (“ Ermacora ”), represented at the hearing by her husband, Arieh Ohayon (“ Ohayon ”), is claiming a reduction of $7,000.00 of the sale price paid to Defendant, Joseph Y. Nadler (“ J. Nadler ”) for the purchase, on February 22, 2005, of the property situated at 41 Merton Crescent, in Hampstead (the “ Property ”). [ 2 ] Ermacora discovered soon after the purchase, the existence of a latent defect affecting the Property at the time of the sale. On or about March 9 th , 2005, she discovered that the sanitary (sewer) drain pipe leading the used waters from the house to the city’s sewer system was obstructed and had a major crack that prevented its normal use and necessitated a replacement. The house was built in 1954. [ 3 ] The amount of her claim is $10,855.62 that she reduced for the purposes hereof to $7,000.00 and renounced to claim the difference of $3,855.62 from J. Nadler. [ 4 ] At all relevant times including at the hearing, J. Nadler was represented by his son, Irwin Berl Nadler (“ Nadler ”), from Toronto. QUESTIONS AT ISSUE [ 5 ] The Court must determine whether the drain pipe constitutes a latent defect that existed at the date of the sale, pursuant to the provisions of Article 1726 of the Civil Code of Quebec (“ C.C.Q. ”). [ 6 ] In the affirmative, what is the amount of the reduction of the sale price to which Ermacora is entitled? THE FACTS [ 7 ] Prior to purchasing the Property, Ermacora proceeded to a pre-purchase inspection as part of the conditions stipulated in the offer to purchase submitted on October 19, 2004 and accepted by Nadler on behalf of his father on October 20, 2004 (the “ Offer ”). [ 8 ] Before making the Offer, Ermacora and her husband visited the Property and noticed that the finished basement had been damaged by water backing up from the sewer system (4 feet of water on the floor). The toilet in the basement had been removed and was inoperative. Repairs were being carried-out by the insurer who covered the damages caused by the toilet that had overflowed and flooded the basement. [ 9 ] The MLS Listing (Exhibit P-15) also indicated that the bathroom in the basement was not functional. [ 10 ] While Ohayon was proceeding with the inspection of the Property, he asked Nadler to fill a document entitled “ Formulaire de déclaration du propriétaire-vendeur ”. Nadler declines to do so as he has not lived in the Property that was the residence of his father who was obliged to move out due to age and illness. Due to his mental and physical state at the time, J. Nadler was totally incapable of filling out the Formulaire and providing any information to Nadler or Ohayon. [ 11 ] By email dated October 22, 2004 (Exhibit D-7), Ohayon advised the broker that was acting on his behalf and of his wife, Nina Miller (“ Miller ”), of the results of the inspection. Ohayon indicates, inter alia , that: The plumbing system needs to be flushed as the inspector has found clogging in the pipes. The cost has not been approximated to me yet but should not be major. [ 12 ] Ohayon raised the fact that a large amount of mould and mildew had been found in the basement of the Property. He expects the insurance company of J. Nadler to cover the additional repairs required by this finding. He concludes his email as follows: We love this house and would like to purchase it but the issues need to be resolved perhaps the insurance will cover the basement and we can come on an agreement for the reminder (sic) of the deficiencies. [ 13 ] Exhibit D-7 is communicated by Miller to Nadler as well as all other written communications between Miller and Nadler as confirmed by Miller at trial. A few days later, on October 26, 2004, one page of the pre-purchase inspection report ordered by Ermacora (Exhibit D-3) (the “ Excerpt ”) is faxed to Nadler. A full copy of pre-purchase inspection report will never be remitted or communicated to Nadler nor will it be produced at the hearing. The Excerpt  reveals: - The presence of cracks in the foundation walls requiring additional exploratory inspection; - Mold and mildew seen on the inner and exterior basement walls requiring additional exploratory inspection is warranted in the basement; - Asbestos found in the attic. [ 14 ] On October 28, 2004 at 3h43pm, Ohayon writes directly to Nadler (Exhibit D-8): Dear Berl, I am sending you this e-mail in order to make a last minute effort to save this deal. When I made you the offer of $648,000 no one disclosed to me that the house had any water infiltration damages, asbestos and cracked foundation whatsoever. Only after the inspection were the deficiencies in the house structure revealed. I really tried my best to work Nina and yourself to find a solution but after 1 week of elapsed time we are always getting nowhere… If I do not hear from you in the next hour or so I will have no choice but to cancel the sale with Nina prior to leaving town. [ 15 ] At 4h20pm on the same day, Nadler responds to Ohayon with a relatively long email (Exhibit D-2) in which he raises among other subjects, the issue of the flooding in the basement caused by a sewer backup in the basement toilet. Nadler is nevertheless trying to see if the insurer will cover the repairs of the additional damages discovered in the basement by Ohayon’s inspector. [ 16 ] In that context, Nadler sends to his father’s insurer a copy of the Excerpt in order to reassess the repairs to be done, as a result of the flood (Exhibits D-9 and D-10). The insurer is shocked by the findings (Email dated November 3, 2004, from Nadler to Miller, Exhibit D-11). This email is communicated to Ohayon and Ermacora by Miller. [ 17 ] By email dated November 12 th , Miller writes to Nadler (Exhibit D-12) and advises him of Ohayon’s position who essentially wants the insurer to perform the additional repairs caused by the presence of mould and mildew. She adds: I, Arieh [Ohayon], and his inspector totally disagree with Mr. Lebuef(sic)[the insurer’s representative] who was there yesterday. According to Arieh the house is a done deal as long as the insurance will clean up the basement… [ 18 ] On December 9, 2004, the insurer’s representative, Suzanne Gagnon, responds to Nadler by a email that was communicated to Ohayon and Ermacora (Exhibits P-13 and D-13). In that email, the insurer accepts to perform limited additional repairs to the basement and recommends, amongst other things: It would be prudent to have the sewer pipe verified to make sure this does not happen again. [ 19 ] “This” in the email refers to the overflow of sewer waters from the basement toilet that had been removed. [ 20 ] By email sent by Miller to Nadler on December 13 th at 2h14 in the morning (Exhibit D-4), Miller writes: Dear Berl, Just a question from Arieh. Ms. Gagnon [representing the insurer] suggests to have the sewer pipeverified(sic) to make sure it [flooding due to blockage] doesn’t happen again. I think the inspector had said it was leaking. Who fixed the sewer pipe in the first place-is it your responsibility or the insurance who has to have it checked? Please answer as soon as possible? Thanks, Nina [ 21 ] Nadler responds to Miller at 8h30am that morning (Exhibit D-14), as follows: As far as I know, my parents had a plumber and a drain guy clean the sewer pipe last year after the event [the flood]. I have no idea what bills were paid by insurance or what work was done. [ 22 ] At 10h10pm, Miller writes to Nadler (Exhibit D-2): Dear Berl, If you can find out who fixed the sewer pipe perhaps they can come back and check it before the insurance does the work. Please let me know, as soon as possible. Thanks, Nina [ 23 ] Miller writes again to Nadler on December 14 th at 6h09pm (Exhibit P-18) as follows: Dear Berl: I have spoken to Arieh and told him we are now at the finish. Either close or forget it. If you could ask your parents who fixed the sewer pipe he will call them tomorrow and he will pay for them to check it. If you can’t then so be it but try if possible Thank you, Nina [ 24 ] At 10h42pm, Nadler responds (Exhibit D-5) to Nadler’s earlier communication  that was not filed: Dear Berl, I got your message. What I told Arieh is exactly what you said. The problem was fixed and if he wanted to pay a plumber to check the pipe he could but we need this sale closed by Friday because it has gone too long. I will give him your message concerning your parents which was what I had already told him today before I e-mailed you. Nina [ 25 ] At 10h52pm, Nadler writes to Miller (Exhibit D-15): Just to be clear. The problem was in the basement. Since the basment(sic) bathroom has been inoperative since the flood, I have no idea what will happen if it is used again. All I can tell you for sure is that they definitely had some plumbing and drainwork done. I don’t know what exactly and they have not had a problem in the rest of the house since… [ 26 ] At 12h00am on December 14 th , Nadler writes to Nina (Exhibit D-7): Nina, I’m really tired of this. Why don’t we just call it a day. I have to go to NY tomorrow. I know there was a plumber and a drain guy. I can’t give a warranty on an old sewer pipe and I’m not going to chase this down. I can’t talk with either of my parents about this. Your purchaser is now talking an off hand comment from an insurance company and trying to turn me into his due diligence agent. I’ve truly had it. I think we should go back tomorrow to Anita’s purchaser and see what they’d offer. Your purchaser has totally compromisedour (sic) ability to sell the house now. You said he would deal with the mould as long as I got a letter from the insurance company setting out in writing what they had told me orally they were prepared to do. I did exactly that. I even tried to do more which omly(sic) delayed things. I acted in good faith and this latest request tells me that your purchaser is not… [ 27 ] On December 18 th , Miller writes to Nadler (Exhibit D-16): Dear Berl, Mazel-tov! We actually have a deal! After not hearing from Arieh I called him after Shabbat and he told me he is not going to worry about the sewer pipe . I will certify the cheque [Deposit] on Monday morning. The question I have now is when will the insurance do the work or can you just get the moneyt (sic) from them and give it to the buyer?... (emphasis added) [ 28 ] On February 17th, 2005, the Offer is modified by Ermacora (Exhibit P-6) as follows: The purchasers have agreed that rather than have the repairs in the basement done by a representant of the insurance company, they will accept the settlement of three thousand two hundred and three dollars and sixty-six cents ($3,203.66) as full payment for the work. The amount will be deducted from the selling price which will now be six hundred thousand, seven hundred and ninety-seven thousand (sic) dollars ($644,797.00). [ 29 ] On February 22 nd , Ermacora signs the deed of sale and takes possession of the Property soon after. [ 30 ] In the following days, Ermacora and Ohayon initiate renovation works in the basement. As the insurer previously recommended, Ohayon’s contractor recommends that the sewer pipe be checked before starting the renovations. Ermacora and Ohayon discover then that the sewer pipe is broken and that rocks from the surrounding grounds are blocking the pipe partially causing the back-ups in the basement toilet. The pipe has to be replaced, thus the present action. ANALYSIS [ 31 ] The Court must now determine whether the sewer pipe constituted a latent defect that existed at the date of the sale, pursuant to the provisions of Article 1726 C.C.Q. that stipulates: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. (emphasis added) [ 32 ] In Placements Jacpar , the Court of Appeal defined the “ reasonable and prudent buyer ” as follows: « Le régime juridique des garanties légales du vendeur ne constitue pas un système de protection de l'acheteur imprudent ou incompétent.  Il impose un standard de conduite à l'acquéreur d'un bien meuble ou immeuble.  C'est à la définition de cette norme que se relie le problème de l'expertise.  On ne saurait en effet, toujours considérer comme caché, ce qui n'a pas été constaté ou ce qui a été mal compris. […] En achetant un bien, l'acquéreur est tenu à une obligation de prudence et de diligence.  Cette dernière exige qu'il prête une attention suffisante à l'objet qu'il examine.  Il faut évaluer le comportement de l'acquéreur, par rapport à la norme objective de l'acheteur raisonnable et prudent . » [1] (emphasis added) [ 33 ] With the adoption of the new Civil Code of Quebec in 1994, the notion of the “ reasonable and prudent buyer” was replaced by the notion of « prudent and diligent buyer ».  The jurisprudence considers that the new notion is more exacting from the buyer: « Comme on l'a souvent répété, cet examen n'est pas un examen approfondi comportant un travail spécial. Cependant comme l'indique l' article 1726 , cet examen suppose la prudence et la diligence de l'acheteur. Ainsi celui qui est prudent, nous dit le Petit Robert: \\\" … prend les dispositions pour éviter des erreurs, des malheurs possibles, s'abstient de tout ce qu'il croit pouvoir être source de dommages. \\\" Le texte de l'article ajoute que l'acheteur doit être diligent. La diligence c'est la qualité de celui qui s'applique avec soin à ce qu'il fait . Certaines causes de jurisprudence parlent de «personne raisonnable», nous soumettons que l' article 1726 va maintenant plus loin que le concept de «personne raisonnable» et exige que le vendeur soit diligent et prudent. Ainsi celui qui procède à l'examen doit être attentif, actif et poser les questions qui s'imposent concernant l'âge de la bâtisse, la date des réparations, l'objet de ces réparations, etc. » [2] (emphasis added) [ 34 ] The age of the building is another element to consider.  The jurisprudence points out that a buyer has a greater duty of prudence when one purchases an old building. [3] [ 35 ] With respect to the “latent” aspect of the defect, it was defined as follows: « Par \\\" caché\\\" , on entend un vice qui ne peut être découvert par un acheteur prudent et diligent malgré un examen raisonnable, attentif et sérieux de l'objet de la vente . Bien que l' article 1726 du Code civil...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\nbuilding is another element to consider.  The jurisprudence points out that a buyer has a greater duty of prudence when one purchases an old building. [3] [ 35 ] With respect to the “latent” aspect of the defect, it was defined as follows: « Par \\\" caché\\\" , on entend un vice qui ne peut être découvert par un acheteur prudent et diligent malgré un examen raisonnable, attentif et sérieux de l'objet de la vente . Bien que l' article 1726 du Code civil du Québec n'exige pas que l'acheteur ait recours à un expert, il demeure que, dans certaines circonstances, l'acheteur \\\" prudent \\\" doit obtenir de l'aide.  C'est le cas de l'acheteur qui possède peu ou pas de connaissances en matière de construction qui perçoit l'indice d'un problème (ex: cernes suggérant des infiltrations d'eau, fissures dans les fondations). En l'absence d'indices révélateurs , on n'exigera pas de l'acheteur, par exemple, qu'il défasse les murs ou qu'il creuse autour des fondations pour en vérifier l'état. Ainsi, un vice n'est pas \\\" caché\\\" du seul fait que l'acheteur ne l'a pas aperçu ou n'en a pas apprécié la gravité.  Le vice qui aurait pu être constaté par un acheteur consciencieux sera considéré comme apparent . La détermination de ce qui est \\\" caché\\\" ou \\\" apparent\\\" sera influencée par l'ensemble des circonstances particulières à chaque cas .  Ainsi, le Tribunal prend en considération le statut du vendeur (vendeur spécialisé ou non), le statut de l'acheteur (profane ou qualifié en construction), la nature du bien acheté (maison, commerce, industrie), son âge, le prix payé, la nature du vice et le comportement des parties.» [4] (emphasis added) [ 36 ] Author Pierre-Gabriel Jobin wrote that a buyer or his expert do not have to take additional investigative measures to find a defect unless visible clues raise suspicion or doubts : \\\"L'acheteur ou son expert n'est pas obligé de prendre des mesures inhabituelles (comme ouvrir un mur ou creuser autour des fondations) pour chercher des vices, sauf si un indice visible soulève des soupçons .\\\" [5] (emphasis added) [ 37 ] In the present instance, the evidence shows that Ermacora and her husband had several indications warranting a further investigation of the sewer pipe. Even, J. Nadler’s insurer recommended it to Ohayon’s knowledge. With all due respect, the Court fails to understand why it was so necessary for Ohayon to obtain and use the same plumber who had previously worked to clear the drain in the past. Any competent plumber could have done the inspection at a reasonable cost. [ 38 ] As clearly appears from the above exchange of correspondence, Ohayon and Ermacora were concerned with the state of the sewer pipe that was obviously involved in if not the cause of a major flood in the basement through the only toilet that had been removed and declared non-functional by the seller. The flood caused by a backup of the sewer waters, the age of the building (1954), the finding of extensive mould and mildew in the basement by Ermacora’s own inspector who recommended further exploratory inspection were all elements that should have warranted further investigation from a “prudent and diligent buyer”. [ 39 ] Ohayon tried to get Nadler to perform the sewer pipe inspection at his (seller’s) costs. On December 14 th , 2004, Nadler refused adding I can’t give a warranty on an old sewer pipe and I’m not going to chase this down. I can’t talk with either of my parents about this. Your purchaser is now talking an off hand comment from an insurance company and trying to turn me into his due diligence agent (see Exhibit D-2). [ 40 ] Yet, four days later on December 18 th , Miller wrote to Nadler on behalf of Ermacora and Ohayon “ Mazel-tov! We actually have a deal! After not hearing from Arieh I called him after Shabbat and he told me he is not going to worry about the sewer pipe . I will certify the cheque on Monday morning. ” [ 41 ] The sale transaction took place on February 22, 2005, five days after Ermacora modified the Offer to reduce the sale price by $3,203.66 as full payment for the repairs to be done in the basement by the insurance company, the company that had recommended, as a prudent measure, a verification of the integrity of the sewer pipe to avoid further flooding from backed up sewer waters in the future. [ 42 ] Plaintiff and her husband ultimately decided to purchase the Property and take a risk with respect to the sewer pipe and Defendant does not have to bear the financial consequences of that risk. [ 43 ] Moreover, nothing in the evidence even suggests that Nadler who never lived in the Property, deceived Ermacora and Ohayon or attempted to do so, on the contrary. [ 44 ] As the Court finds that the defect (sewer pipe) identified by Ermacora was apparent according to the provisions of Article 1726 C.C.Q. and therefore not latent, it is not opportune to examine Plaintiff’s the damages or reduction of sale price and rule on same. FOR THOSE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the action of Plaintiff, A. Elizabeth Ermacora, THE WHOLE , with legal costs of $136.00 against Plaintiff, A. Elizabeth Ermacora payable to Defendant, Joseph Y. Nadler. __________________________________ MICHEL A. PINSONNAULT, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: November 13, 2006 [1] Placements Jacpar Inc. vs. Benzakour , 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , [1989] R.J.Q. 2309, 2315 (C.A.). [2] Papazian vs. Hébert, B.E. 2000BE-293 (C.Q.) . [3] Gagnon vs. Gobeil , AZ-50188298 (C.Q.) . [4] Lussier vs. Locas , J.E. 2004-957 (C.Q.) . [5] Pierre-Gabriel Jobin, La vente , 2nd edition, Les Éditions Yvon Blais, 2001, p. 160, no 135.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2006qccq13856-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02127872034907341, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2006qccq13856-en\", \"title\": \"Ermacora c. Nadler, 2006 QCCQ 13856 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2006qccq13856-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02127872034907341, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2006qccq13856-en\"}, \"token_count\": 5329, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2006qccq13856-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs1486\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs1486-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Doston c. Ashworth, 2019 QCCS 1486 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2019 QCCS 1486 (CanLII) | Doston c. Ashworth | CanLII Date: 2019-04-23 File number: 760-17-003862-151 Citation: Doston c. Ashworth, 2019 QCCS 1486 (CanLII) Doston c. Ashworth 2019 QCCS 1486 JL4478 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF BEAUHARNOIS N: 760-17-003862-151 DATE: April 23, 2019 __________________________________________________________________ PRESIDING: THE HONOURABLE CHANTAL LAMARCHE, J.S.C. __________________________________________________________________ JOSÉE DOSTON Plaintiff v. WADE ASHWORTH and JENNIFER WHITING Defendants __________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT __________________________________________________________________ 1. overview [ 1 ] Plaintiff, Ms. Josée Doston, seeks the reduction of the price of the house she purchased from Defendants as she discovered latent defects once she moved into the residence. [ 2 ] Defendants argue that the defects were not latent as clear signs were apparent and that Ms. Doston did not act as a prudent and diligent buyer by refusing to have a pre-purchase inspection done. 2. the context [ 3 ] In the spring of 2014, Ms. Doston began looking for a house after selling hers. She was assisted by the real estate agent who had sold her house, Mr. Gilles Clermont. She was looking for a house that required no renovations or repairs to be made and that had four bedrooms so that she, her two teenage children and her brother could live there. [ 4 ] In May 2014, her agent proposed to her the defendants’ residence; a one-story bungalow with a finished basement. It had four bedrooms, three of which were on the ground floor. [ 5 ] The basement had four closed rooms; a bedroom, a workshop, a laundry room and a bathroom. There was also a family room and a closet. [ 6 ] She visited the house twice. A first time on May 10, 2014, with her agent and a second time, the next day accompanied by a friend, Mr. Dominic Beaulieu. [ 7 ] Mr. Beaulieu is neither an engineer, an architect, nor a construction contractor, although he has helped Ms. Doston with various chores. [ 8 ] They visited the entire house and checked, in particular, the condition of the floors, the cabinets, if the walls had cracks and the size of the rooms. They also went outside to look at the backyard and the exterior of the house. [ 9 ] There were three apparent cracks in the parging of the foundation exterior walls. [ 10 ] There was one on the left side of the house [1] where the parking is located and one above a window, the lower half of this window was hidden by a deck. Finally, a third crack was located on the right side of the house. This last crack was covered with a white coating, which Mr. Beaulieu describes as caulking. [ 11 ] Mr. Beaulieu considered that these were superficial cracks of parging. However, he questioned Defendant, Mr. Wade Ashworth, immediately after their visit. Mr. Ashworth told them, among other things, that it was a good house, that he had repaired the crack with caulking and that there had been no infiltration. The Court will return to this meeting and its content later, but notes that the versions are contradictory. [ 12 ] Reassured by the words of Mr. Ashworth, Ms. Doston waived the inspection of the house and made an offer the same evening. She was willing to offer $240,000. However, when Mr. Clermont came to her house the same evening to sign the promise to purchase, he had in hand the Declaration of the seller [2] . They read this document together. Ms. Doston saw that in clause 5.2 the seller declared \\\" yes \\\" to the question, \\\" foundation crack, rot or other problem with basement structure? \\\". \\\" Minor repairs, \\\"seller\\\" no further issue \\\" was written by hand. She understood this to be consistent with what Mr. Ashworth told her earlier in the day. The agent then explained that there was vermiculite in the attic. Due to the presence of vermiculite, the price was lowered to $237,000 [3] . [ 13 ] Defendants submitted a counter-offer at $238,500 and they postponed the date of occupation from June 14 to July 6, 2014. On the same evening, parties agreed on the price and the date of occupation was finally set for June 22, 2014 [4] . [ 14 ] Before the Court, Mr. Clermont states that taking possession within 30 or 40 days is not exceptional. [ 15 ] The day Ms. Doston took possession of the house, she smelled dampness in the basement, she thought it was because the windows were closed since Defendants had left and because of the outside humidity. [ 16 ] A few days later, a small puddle appeared on the floor, in front of the washing machine. She wiped it down and checked the washing machine to ensure that it was not chipped and that the pipes were in good condition. Everything looked fine. [ 17 ] Ms. Doston decided to close the family room in the basement with a wall to accommodate her daughter's room. Her son would occupy the other bedroom in the basement. [ 18 ] According to Mr. Jean-François Lamer, who she hired to do this work, it was minor work. He did not even have to open the floor. He only had to put up a wall over the floor to the ceiling, install a door and move one of the walls of the closet. Mr. Lamer is a co-worker of Ms. Doston, a carpenter in the City of Vaudreuil-Dorion. [ 19 ] When the time came to do the work on her daughter's bedroom, Ms. Doston noted that the cardboard boxes filled with clothes left on the floor of the family room since the move were wet and that all the clothes smelled bad. [ 20 ] The wall was added. Mr. Lamer simply screwed the wall studs into the existing floor and also added Gyproc sheets and installed a door. Then he started to work on the closet. [ 21 ] He noticed that the carpet in the existing closet was wet. When he pulled it up, he discovered that the subfloor was soft, rotten and damp. He removed part of the wall at the bottom of the closet and discovered a crack in the foundation. He removed part of the ceramic floor of the next room, the laundry room. The subfloor was also soft and wet. [ 22 ] When he went outside to the back of the house, he saw a hole in the foundation wall under the window of the laundry room large enough to fit one of his tools. Previously, this part of the window was hidden by the deck that Ms. Doston had removed. [ 23 ] He made a hole in the drywall wall of the laundry room where the hole was on the outside. He discovered a crack that ran from the top of the wall of the foundation and down to the floor and continued on the concrete slab. The crack passed through the foundation wall. [ 24 ] Ms. Doston asked him to check the other rooms. Other people came to help him. All materials that touched the concrete slab were wet and the wood was rotten. Workers had to wear masks and exit the house frequently as they developed headaches. [ 25 ] On September 11, 2014, Ms. Doston notified Defendants about the situation [5] . Mr. Ashworth called her a few days later to tell her that he was not responsible and that the insurer will take over the case. Defendants’ agent had offered them an insurance policy protecting them against latent defects. [ 26 ] The insurer sent an inspector to the house. The coverage was refused as it was not, according to the insurer, latent defects. The Court is obviously not bound by this conclusion. [ 27 ] Ms. Doston continued the demolition of the basement as she had to remove the rotten materials, correct the defects and rebuild the basement, which was essential to house her family. During the work, Ms. Doston had to store her basement furniture. Her son went to live with a friend and her daughter shared her bedroom. [ 28 ] The foundation walls, the concrete slab and the ceilings were completely uncovered. [ 29 ] On October 28, 2014, Mr. Ashworth went to Ms. Doston's home with two contractors to obtain an estimate of the cost of the repairs. When he left, he told her that he would contact her again, which he did not. [ 30 ] On November 3, 2014 [6] , Ms. Doston's attorney served Defendants with a demand letter. [ 31 ] On November 23, 2014, Defendants responded through their attorney. They declined any responsibility and indicated that they will contest any claim [7] . [ 32 ] At the same time that these correspondence were exchanged, as the defects had to be corrected, Ms. Doston retained the services of Breault & Gosselin, Genie Conseil Inc. to obtain an engineer's expertise on the nature and scope of the defects and the appropriate repairs [8] . [ 33 ] Subsequently, she obtained some estimates for the costs of the work to be performed. [ 34 ] Ms. Doston retained the services of Jamex for turnkey work, for the repairs and the finishing of the basement. [ 35 ] In November 2014, Jamex repaired the cracks from the outside. During the excavation, Jamex realized that the French drain had to be modified. Even though, it was still in good condition, the collector pipe of the French drain was located six inches above it rather than below. Therefore, the French drain was not functional. [ 36 ] Jamex had to complete the exterior work before freezing became an issue. [ 37 ] In addition, Jamex noted, as feared by Breault & Gosselin's engineer, Génie Conseil Inc., that the concrete slab had been installed inside the footings of the foundation rather than above it. It affected the tightness of the house. A waterproofing membrane had to be installed. [ 38 ] Ms. Doston was overwhelmed by the amount of work to be done. [ 39 ] The work finally ended in March 2015. The total cost of the work, including furniture storage costs, was $81,502.61 [9] , an amount that Ms. Doston wishes to obtain. [ 40 ] She also claims $2,414.47, for the cost of Breault & Gosselin, Génie Conseil Inc.'s expertise [10] , $5,000 as damages for the loss of enjoyment of her basement for 6 months and $5,000 for moral damages. [ 41 ] Defendants contest the claim and argue that Ms. Doston is claiming the costs for an extravagant renovation of the basement that she had planned to make upon purchase. They allege that her procedure is abusive and therefore counter-claim $40,000, representing the professional fees they incurred and $25,000 as exemplary damages. 3. ISSUES ➢ Is the house affected by latent defects? ➢ Did Ms. Doston allow Defendants, in a timely manner, the opportunity to assess the situation themselves with their expert? ➢ Is Ms. Doston entitled to a price reduction of $81,502.61 and reimbursement of her expert fees of $2,414.41? ➢ Is Ms. Doston entitled to damages for loss of enjoyment and moral damages? ➢ To the extent that Ms. Doston's claim is dismissed, was her claim abusive? 4. IS THE HOUSE AFFECTED BY latent defects? 4.1 The law [ 42 ] Article 1726 of the Civil Code of Quebec provides that the seller is bound to guarantee the good he sells agai nst \\\" latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them.\\\" [11] [ 43 ] In order to benefit from this legal guarantee, four conditions must be respected [12] , the defect must: ➢ be serious; ➢ be unknown to the buyer at the time of sale; ➢ be hidden; ➢ exist at the time of sale. [ 44 ] It is up to the seller to prove the buyer's knowledge of the defect. [ 45 ] The hidden nature of the defect must be analyzed according to an objective standard. Thus, a defect will be hidden if it cannot be ascertained by a prudent and diligent buyer without having to resort to an expert [13] . However, the nature of the examination of the good that a prudent buyer must make depends on several factors. One of these factors is the seller's statements pertaining to the condition of the good. [ 46 ] Doctrine and case law recognize that the prudent buyer's obligation to further examine the property through, for example, a pre-purchase inspection when he/she finds evidence of a defect may be reduced when the seller acts so as to comfort the buyer against these indices or signs. [ 47 ] This is so when the seller makes false, misleading, deceptive, reluctant or incomplete statements. The same is true when the seller keeps silent about a component of a serious problem, which gives the buyer a false sense of security. [ 48 ] In 1989, even before the Civil Code made it clear that the buyer had no obligation to do a pre-purchase inspection, the Court of Appeal decided that the buyer’s statements could render apparent defect legally hidden and it also pointed out that one cannot blame a buyer for not suspecting the seller's bad faith [14] : Le propre comportement du vendeur jouera son rôle à l'occasion dans l'appréciation du caractère du vice. Ainsi, ces fausses représentations ou le dol du vendeur quant à l'état de la chose ou aux causes réelles de vices apparents, dégageront parfois l'acquéreur de l'obligation d'explorer davantage. Le dol ou le mensonge du vendeur peut rendre un vice juridiquement caché, surtout lorsque l'acquéreur a demandé une explication au sujet des manifestations d'un défaut et qu'on l'a orienté  sur une fausse piste.  On ne peut reprocher à l'acquéreur de soupçonner la mauvaise foi de son vendeur. Ainsi, dans B.et R. Gauthier Ltée c. Lemieux (C.A.Q. 200-09-000096-773, 2 septembre 1980 ), la Cour d'appel avait confirmé un jugement de la Cour supérieure qui accueillait une action en diminution de prix. L'acheteur s'était inquiété du mauvais état apparent d'un plancher.  Le vendeur lui avait représenté qu'il s'agissait d'une question mineure qui se corrigerait d'elle-même à la fin des travaux. L'acquéreur n'avait pas à explorer davantage. Il pouvait se satisfaire de l'explication qu'on lui donnait.  Il n'avait  pas  à  faire des  recherches  ou vérifications additionnelles si on lui représentait que l'état des choses n'était pas grave. \\\"La Cour a accepté le témoignage de l'intimé à ce sujet. Il en résulte que l'appelante a placé celui-ci, par son dol et ses fausses représentations, dans une situation de fausse sécurité.\\\" (B.R.   Gauthier  Inc.  c.  Lemieux,  C.A.Q., 200-09-000096-773, 2 septembre 1980 , opinion de monsieur le juge Paré; voir aussi Belcourt Construction Inc. c. Cretchman (1979) C.A. 595, p. 601, monsieur le juge Paré) [Emphasis added] [ 49 ] In Proulx-Robertson v. Collins , the Court of Appeal emphasizes that the seller who knows the condition of the building he/she is about to sell has a disclosure obligation [15] : De plus, à des questions posées par l'agent d'immeuble Dupont qui visitait la maison avec les acheteurs et qui s'inquiétait de taches grisâtres sur les murs du sous-sol de la maison, les appelants auraient répondu qu'il s'agissait seulement d'un problème de saleté laissée par un locataire antérieur . L'honorable juge Frenette résume ainsi l'incident: \\\"Lorsque les demandeurs et les deux agents d'immeuble ont visité la maison, ils n'ont rien remarqué d'anormal quant à l'humidité ou à l'odeur, sauf les taches grisâtres sur les murs. Le 28 avril 1984, lorsque les demandeurs et l'agent d'immeuble étaient présents et qu'une question fut posée aux défendeurs quant à ces taches, qui connaissaient le problème 1992 CanLII 3932 (QC CA) d'humidité du sous-sol et celui des insectes, puisqu'ils en avaient été avertis, ils n'ont pas relaté la situation réelle lorsqu'ils ont répondu que c'était uniquement de la saleté. Il était alors difficile de détecter l'étendue du problème, car les lieux étaient en voie d'être nettoyés et repeints. Quant au manque d'aération du rez-de-chaussée, les demandeurs ne pouvaient soupçonner ce problème, car ce n'est qu'après avoir remédié au problème d'excès d'humidité du sous-sol, qu'ils ont réalisé qu'ils affectaient le rez-de-chauss...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\ndone to the immovable other than those already mentioned? (e.g. modifications to the structure of the immovable)? □ yes □ no Any major work or renovation done to this immovable and not specifically covered by a question on this form must be reported in section D14. D14. DETAILS AND MAJOR IMPROVEMENTS TO THE IMMOVABLE Indicate the number of the clause to which the details apply: (handwritten notes: “Front windows + MBR replaced 2009”). […] [Signature of the sellers] on May 3 / 2014; at 4 P.M. [ 66 ] On May 11, 2014, during Ms. Doston's second visit, her friend, Mr. Beaulieu, accompanied her along with her agent. [ 67 ] Their testimony is summarized as follows. [ 68 ] After visiting both the inside and outside of the house, the group then chatted in front of the house. Ms. Doston was interested in buying the house. Mr. Beaulieu returned to Mr. Ashworth in the backyard and spoke to him on behalf of Ms. Doston. Because of the cracks, Mr. Beaulieu asked Mr. Ashworth if there was infiltration. He confirmed to Mr. Beaulieu that the house was in good condition and that there had been no infiltration. Mr. Beaulieu asked if the price was negotiable since Ms. Doston would be willing to pay $240,000. Mr. Ashworth told him that the price was fine for him, but he had check with his wife. [ 69 ] Mr. Ashworth went to see his wife. Mr. Beaulieu returned to Ms. Doston and her agent to inform them that there was a deal but that Mr. Ashworth had to speak with his wife to make sure that the price was fine for her too. [ 70 ] A few minutes later, Mr. Ashworth returned to see Ms. Doston, her agent and Mr. Beaulieu, who were still in front of the house and confirmed to them that the price of $240,000 was acceptable. [ 71 ] Mr. Ashworth then answered Ms. Doston's and Mr. Beaulieu's questions about the quality of the house. Ms. Doston asked if there were any infiltrations given the cracks observed. Mr. Ashworth replied that there had never been any infiltration. Ms. Doston asked if there had been infiltration from the roof. Mr. Ashworth responded no again, and that it was a good house. Mr. Ashworth even told them that they got an inspection report when they bought the house in 2008. Mr. Beaulieu asked if it was possible to get a copy. Mr. Ashworth agreed. [ 72 ] The group then moved to the right side of the house where Mr. Ashworth showed them the crack and he confirmed to Ms. Doston to have repaired it and repeated that there had never been any water infiltration. The foundation walls were covered by parging. [ 73 ] In her cross-examination, Ms. Doston repeated exactly what she said during her examination and even corrected Defendant’s attorney when she made certain errors in the facts. Her testimony is solid, credible and corroborated by Mr. Beaulieu whose cross-examination did not reveal any contradictions in his testimony. [ 74 ] The testimony of Ms. Doston's agent is a little less specific. However, the Court considers it credible. It is not surprising for a real estate agent involved in the purchase of a house four years ago, not to have a memory as accurate as the buyer. From his testimony, the Court understands that Mr. Ashworth stated that the house was in good condition, that there had never been any infiltration and that he had repaired the crack on the right side. The Court also notes that he suggested to Ms. Doston to have a pre-purchase inspection, which she refused, because she was reassured with the statements of the seller that there was no infiltration and that the cracks were repaired. [ 75 ] The content of these meetings is contradicted by Defendants. [ 76 ] Ms. Whiting did not hear the conversation between her husband and Mr. Beaulieu. She remained in the backyard at all times. [ 77 ] Mr. Ashworth submits that during the conversation between himself and Mr. Beaulieu, he raised the issue of the cracks, but Mr. Beaulieu was not interested in them. He just wanted to know if it was a good house. Mr. Ashworth replied yes. They also discussed the amount of $ 240,000 and he said that he had to discuss it with his wife. [ 78 ] Mr. Ashworth went to his wife and discussed with her the amount of $240,000. His wife told him to call their agent who told him to wait for the offer. This last part of his testimony is corroborated by Ms. Whiting. [ 79 ] Mr. Ashworth states that Ms. Doston, Mr. Beaulieu and the agent left while he was still on the phone with his agent. [ 80 ] According to him, there was never any meeting with them in front of the house and on the right side of the house. [ 81 ] The Court finds that the overriding evidence shows that there was a meeting between Mr. Ashworth, Ms. Doston, her agent and Mr. Beaulieu in front of the house and on the side thereafter and that the content of this meeting is the one reported by Ms. Doston, her agent and Mr. Beaulieu. Three witnesses who explain the detailed content of a meeting appear more credible than a single witness who simply denies the existence of such a meeting. [ 82 ] Only Mr. Ashworth denies this meeting. Moreover, his wife does not say how long it took after phoning their agent, for her husband returned to join her in the backyard. [ 83 ] Furthermore, in his testimony, Mr. Ashworth does not indicate how the conversation with Mr. Beaulieu ended. Was Mr. Ashworth supposed to come back to him with an agreement on the price after talking to his wife? Mr. Beaulieu's version of Mr. Ashworth's discussion with his wife and his return after to inform them of an agreement on the price seems more plausible. It is not likely that Ms. Doston would have left without having spoken to Mr. Ashworth, who, according to his own version, told Mr. Beaulieu that he must consult his wife on the price. The Court also finds inconceivable that Mr. Beaulieu would have told Mr. Ashworth that he was not interested in the cracks. [ 84 ] In addition, other parts of Defendants’ testimony overshadow their credibility. [ 85 ] Mr. Ashworth and his wife testified that Ms. Doston's agent came to present them with an offer for their home. He informed them that the price had been lowered because of the presence of vermiculite, which he had just discussed with Ms. Doston, reading with her for the first time the Declaration of the seller . [ 86 ] Mr. Ashworth then offered to give him the portion of the Mose Report on vermiculite. Mr. Ashworth would have gone down to the basement, found the Mose Report on his computer and printed only the two pages about vermiculite. [ 87 ] Mr. Ashworth adds that he cannot be blamed for not submitting the complete Mose Report because Ms. Doston's agent never asked him for it. [ 88 ] His version is not credible. Why would the agent want only part of the Mose Report and, moreover, only the portion concerning the vermiculite? [ 89 ] Ms. Whiting's response in cross-examination when asked why not give the complete the report rather than two pages concerning the vermiculite is not convincing either. She says it does not make sense for her husband to print a 100-page report on the printer at home. However, the report is only 34 pages long. [ 90 ] Moreover, Defendants indicated in the Declaration of the Seller that the Mose Report was not available even though they had a copy on their computer. Their explanations in this regard are not credible either. [ 91 ] For example, Mr. Ashworth states that he only recalled that he had a copy of the Mose Report a few days after signing the Declaration of the Seller on May 3, 2014. However, on May 10, 2014, when Ms. Doston’s agent came to submit the offer, Mr. Ashworth offered him a copy of the Vermiculite portion of the report. He did not give him the whole report. [ 92 ] They are aware of the content of this report. They know that there were corrections to be made concerning the cracks and that the expert even highlighted that the structure was weakened, they even monitored a crack. It is unlikely that they did not know whether or not they had kept a copy of this report. [ 93 ] Knowing the extent of the problem regarding the cracks, knowing that they followed none of the recommendations of the Mose Report and made no corrections, Defendants could not have been unaware that they still had a copy of the report. 4.2.3 Conclusion [ 94 ] Notwithstanding the sympathy the Court may have considering the amounts involved, it still cannot accept Defendants’ position. [ 95 ] In 2008, Defendants were informed by the Mose Report that, in particular, cracks were a serious problem and even the structure might have been affected. They chose to take a chance and buy the house. They did not repair anything and did not ask for any more in-depth expertise because of their financial incapacity. They decided not to make the Mose Report available when they put their house up for sale, and even worse, they indicated in the Declaration of the seller that minor crack repairs were done when this was not true. The caulking was only to monitor the crack. They reduced the severity of the problem and Mr. Ashworth specifically reassured Ms. Doston about the good condition of the house despite the existence of the cracks and his knowledge of the Mose Report. [ 96 ] Even if the Court were to believe them when they say that they never knew of any infiltration or smell, which is surprising to say the least given the testimonial and documentary evidence on the condition of the walls and of the basement floor, Defendants were still not transparent and acted to reassure Ms. Doston. [ 97 ] How can Defendants state in paragraph 20 of their defence: « The implications of these cracks are self-evident and would have required  immediate attention from any prudent and dilligent purchaser who could not rely on their impermeability in the long term . » and write about the same cracks in the Declaration of the seller : \\\"Minor repairs \\\"seller\\\" no further issues \\\". [ 98 ] It was contrary to Defendants’ disclosure obligation to indicate on the Declaration of the seller that minor crack repairs have been made and that it is no longer a problem, while in 2008, they were informed that the cracks must be repaired immediately, that there was a risk of infiltration and structural problems knowing that they did not carry out any repairs. In addition, the Declaration of the seller is also false since both Defendants acknowledged at the hearing that the caulking affixed by Mr. Ashworth in 2008 was not to repair, but only to monitor the crack. [ 99 ] Analyzed as a whole, the overriding evidence satisfies the Court that Defendants played hide-and-seek with Ms. Doston even though they had an obligation to disclose what they knew about their house and especially the problems that the Mose Report brought to their attention six years ago. Their obligation to disclose is all the greater because they knew that these problems had not been resolved and the Defendants went as far as comforting Ms. Doston with their statements. [ 100 ] Moreover, their agent Mr. Staley, asserted at the hearing that having had the Mose Report in hand, he would have mentioned its conclusions in the Declaration of the seller . [ 101 ] The Court considers that the defects are hidden despite the apparent signs. [ 102 ] These defects that existed at the time Ms. Doston purchased the house are serious enough to constitute latent defects within the meaning of Article 1726 CCQ. 5. Did Ms. Doston allow Defendants, in a timely manner, the opportunity to assess the situation themselves with their expert? [ 103 ] Defendants argue that Ms. Doston did not allow them to see for themselves with an expert the defects and damages. She rushed to demolish everything and rebuild. Her eagerness supports their thesis that from the beginning, Ms. Doston intended to renovate the basement and make them pay for her extravagant renovations. [ 104 ] Section 1739 CCQ provides for the buyer’s obligation to notify the seller of the defect, within a reasonable time: 1739. A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective shall give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. Where the defect appears gradually, the time begins to run on the day that the buyer could suspect the seriousness and extent of the defect. The seller may not invoke the tardiness of a notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it. [ 105 ] On September 11, 2014, Ms. Doston herself wrote a discloser notice addressed to Defendants [21] . It was not until September 18 that Mr. Ashworth phoned to inform her that the insurer Aviva would contact her [22] . [ 106 ] Although Defendants sustain that she did not collaborate with the insurer, Ms. Doston gives a precise testimony of her timely collaboration. She testifies as to the dates of conversation with the insurer, dates on which she returned the insurer calls, date when she provided the documents requested by the insurer as well as the date when the insurer’s inspector visited the house. Her testimony was not contradicted by the insurer who did not testify. [ 107 ] As soon as September 22, 2014, the insurer wrote to Mr. Ashworth informing him that they doubted that the coverage applied and suggested that he consult with a lawyer. [ 108 ] It was not until October 28, 2014, in the early evening, that Mr. Ashworth came to Ms. Doston's home with two contractors to assess the cost of the work to be done. When he left, he told her that he would be in contact with her. [ 109 ] On November 3, 2014, Ms. Doston, who had now retained the services of a lawyer, sent a formal demand letter to Defendants through him [23] . [ 110 ] Mr. Ashworth explains that, contrary to what he had told Ms. Doston on October 28, 2014, he did not get back to her because he had received the demand letter from her attorney. [ 111 ] Defendants chose to respond through their attorney. On November 25, 2014, Defendants’ attorney responded that her clients denied any responsibility. She concluded her letter by stating: “Any renovation, repairs or replacement Ms. Doston chooses to perform are done so for her own account for which no compensation is offered. ” [24] [ 112 ] Ms. Doston has acted quickly, but she was facing a problem of mouldy floors and walls spread throughout the entire basement. She needed this part of the residence to house members of her family, winter was approaching and outdoor work had be completed before the arrival of the possibility of freezing temperature. [ 113 ] In addition, Defendants chose to wait before hiring a lawyer, did not contact Ms. Doston after visiting the property with their contractors and denied any liability. [ 114 ] The Court cannot accept Defendants' argument that Ms. Doston did not allow them the opportunity to assess the situation for themselves, with their expert in a timely manner. [ 115 ] In any event, as the Court will conclude in the section concerning the damages, Defendants were aware of the defect, therefore, as stipulated in Section 1739 CCQ, they cannot invoke the tardiness of Ms. Doston’s notice. 6. Is Ms. Doston entitled to a price reduction of $81,502.61 and reimbursement of her expert fees of $2,414.41? [ 116 ] The only evidence concerning the necessary repairs is that of Breault & Gosselin, Génie Conseil Inc. [25] and Jamex who executed them. [ 117 ] The estimates of two contractors filed by Defendants are by no means convincing. The Akton Injection [26] estimate covers the repair of five cracks. The one from Graceland [27] covers the repair of three cracks, as stated by its representative before the Court, Mr. Andrew Peterman. He estimated the cost at $2,500 per crack. [ 118 ] However, Jamex had to repair nine or ten cracks and fixed 30 steel plates. In addition, a waterproofing membrane had to be installed, given, in particular, the defective installation of the slab. Finally, the defective installation of the French drain had to be corrected as well. [ 119 ] Jamex testifies that the French drain was still functional despite having exceeded its useful life. [ 120 ] Jamex subsequently finished the basement. Plumbing and electrical work was also done in the basement. [ 121 ] The evidence shows that the type of materials used by Jamex for finishing the basement is of the same nature as the existing ones, namely the floating floor, tiles and a suspended ceiling. [ 122 ] However, Ms. Doston had the workshop finished in a habitable room when it was not at the time of purchase. She also had a floating floor installed in the existing bedroom, whereas at the time she purchased the house the floor was in plywood. She also added a wall in the family room to create a second bedroom. [ 123 ] This does not demonstrate that Ms. Doston planned to renovate the basement as Defendants argue. [ 124 ] However, the Court cannot grant her the entire amount claimed for the work done since some of the basement has been improved, mainly the workshop and the existing bedrooms. Furthermore, she now has new materials while the evidence shows that by the time she bought the house, the basement had been finished for at least six years. Ms. Doston cannot take\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\n. She also added a wall in the family room to create a second bedroom. [ 123 ] This does not demonstrate that Ms. Doston planned to renovate the basement as Defendants argue. [ 124 ] However, the Court cannot grant her the entire amount claimed for the work done since some of the basement has been improved, mainly the workshop and the existing bedrooms. Furthermore, she now has new materials while the evidence shows that by the time she bought the house, the basement had been finished for at least six years. Ms. Doston cannot take advantage of the added value that this work brings to the house [28] . [ 125 ] The Court finds that the size of the workshop in the diagram filed by Defendants [29] is approximately 1/6 of the basement area and the same for the existing bedroom. As the improvement consists of finishing the workshop in a living room and placing a floating floor in the existing bedroom [30] , a reduction of 1/6 of Jamex’s cost of the interior finishing will be applied to reflect these improvements. [ 126 ] The Court will also deduct 20% of the total cost to reflect that Ms. Doston now has a new basement [31] . This is the amount suggested by counsel for Ms. Doston when questioned by the Court on this subject and it is reasonable. [ 127 ] Electrical work appears necessary since the Mose Report indicated a problem in this area. However, it does not apply to plumbing work. Moreover, the evidence does not show that plumbing work was necessary because of latent defects. Therefore, an amount of $3,800 will also be deducted for the rough of the plumbing [32] . [ 128 ] For the French drain, the Court will award 50% of the amount claimed, because there was a hidden defect in its installation and, although it had reached its useful life, it was still functional. According to Jamex’s testimony, the cost for the work for the French drain is about $6,600 [33] . The Court will deduct $3,300 from this amount. [ 129 ] The deductions applied by the Court are summarized in the following table: Total dEductions Exhibit Amount Deduction P-9 $3,300 French drain P-11 $6,868 1/6 of the total cost to take into account the improvement of the workshop $3,300 For the rough of the plumbing $6,108 20% of the balance of the invoice for the finishing of the basement to take into account the new materials P-12 $477 1/6 for the plumbing of the workshop 1/6 for the floor of the existing bedroom Total deductions: $20,053 [ 130 ] Ms. Doston is therefore entitled to $61,449 [34] . [ 131 ] As for the experts’ costs, the Court includes them in the judicial costs. 7. Is Ms. Doston entitled to damages for loss of enjoyment and moral damages? [ 132 ] The Civil Code of Québec stipulates that the vendor being aware of the defects but who chooses to remain silent, will have to compensate the buyer for damages suffered in addition to the reduction of the price. [35] [ 133 ] In light of the foregoing, the Court finds that there are sufficient serious, precise and concordant facts to allow the Court to conclude that Defendants were aware of the defect since they had in hand the Mose Report and knew that no repairs had been done since their purchase of the house. [ 134 ] In addition, Defendants allege in their defence and repeat in their pleadings that any reasonable person should have expected to find the basement in the state in which Ms. Doston found it because of the three cracks. If what Defendants plead is true, the Court must conclude that they too knew or ought to have known about the defect. [ 135 ] As leakage cracks are the primary cause of Ms. Doston's disturbance and loss of enjoyment, Defendants are therefore liable for all non-pecuniary damages suffered by Ms. Doston. [ 136 ] She explains that the basement was essential since her son and daughter were to occupy it, while she and her brother occupied the ground floor. Because of the repairs, she had to share her bedroom with her daughter for six months. She also had the inconvenience of having to do the laundry outside of the house during the same period of time. Due to the consequences of the latent defect, she did not have access to the basement for six months. [ 137 ] She also had to use her free time to find an expert and a contractor, take care of the purchase of materials and transport some of them. She had the trouble of having to store her furniture in a storage facility. [ 138 ] The situation greatly worried and stressed her and caused her many troubles and inconveniences in addition to not being able to use this part of the house. She who only had to add a wall with a door and shrink a closet, finds herself having to demolish the entire basement because of the rotten walls and floor and finish it all over again. [ 139 ] In light of the case law [36] , the Court considers that a total sum of $5,000 appears justified. [ 140 ] Given the above findings, Defendants’ counterclaim is dismissed. FOR THESE REASONS, the Court: [ 141 ] GRANTS partly the Demande introductive d’instance amendée et précisée ; [ 142 ] DISMISSES Defendants’ counterclaim; [ 143 ] CONDEMNS Defendants severally to pay to Plaintiff a sum of $61,449 in reduction of the price, with legal interest and the additional indemnity provided for in article 1619 CCQ, since the demand letter of November 3, 2014; [ 144 ] CONDEMNS Defendant's severally to pay Plaintiff a sum of $5,000 in damages, with legal interest and the additional indemnity provided for in article 1619 CCQ, since the demand letter of November 3, 2014; [ 145 ] WITH JUDICIAL COSTS in favour of Plaintiff, including the experts’ costs of $2,414.41. __________________________________ Chantal Lamarche, j.s.c. M tre Luc Lachance Astell, Lachance, Du Sablon, De Sua Counsel of Plaintiff M tre Élaine Rosenberg Rosenberg Avocat inc. Counsel of Defendants Dates of hearing: January 28, 29, 30 and 31, 2019 [1] The references are in relation to a person in the street facing the house. [2] Exhibit P-2. [3] Exhibit P-20. [4] Exhibit D-7. [5] Exhibit P-5. [6] Exhibit P-7. [7] Exhibit P-34. [8] Exhibit P-8. [9] Exhibit P-35. [10] Exhibit P-35. [11] Civil Code of Québec , RLRQ c CCQ-1991, art. 1726. [12] Leroux v. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 . [13] Id. [14] Placement Jacpar Inc. v. Benzakour , 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) . [15] Proulx-Robertson v. Collins , 1992 CanLII 3932 (QC CA.) . [16] Boivin v. Morin , 2013 QCCS 3732 (appeal dismissed on motion, 2013 QCCA 1766 ); to the same effect: Trahan v. Girard , 2009 QCCS 1566 . [17] Chabot (Belleau) v. Lavoie , 2003 CanLII 18516 ; to the same effect: Gingras v. Duplessis 2016 QCCQ 16137 ; Beaudet v. Bastien , 2007 QCCQ 13454 ; Hénault v. Lacelle , 2009 QCCQ 2749 (motion for leave to appeal dismissed, 2009 QCCA 1063 ); Boivin v. Morin , supra note, 16; Trahan c. Girard , supra note, 16; Leclerc v. Lemieux , 2019 QCCS 1209 . [18] Beaudet v. Bastien , supra note, 17. [19] Exhibit P-3. [20] Exhibit P-2. [21] Exhibit D-5. [22] Exhibit D-21. [23] Exhibit P-7. [24] Exhibit P-34. [25] Exhibit P-8. [26] Exhibit D-3. [27] Exhibit P-26. [28] Rivard v. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 , par. 27 and following. [29] Exhibit D-20. [30] This bedroom had a Gyproc ceiling at the time of purchase. [31] Ferreira v. Rubin , 2007 QCCQ 15085 ; Brès v. Giraud , 2016 QCCS 6307 . [32] Exhibit P-11. [33] Exhibit P-9. Jamex calculates that this invoice of $ 16 605 before taxes includes the repair of ten cracks at $750 - $800 per crack and 30 steel plates at $50 to $75 per plate The Court averaged and retained the figure of $10,000 for cracks.  The remaining amount and for the French drain is $6,600. [34] $81,502 – $20,053 = $61,449. [35] Art. 1728 CCQ. [36] Gardner v. Desjardins , 2016 QCCS 6158 ; Fournier v. Cadrin , 2008 QCCS 927 ; Gagnon v. Lacroix , 2017 QCCS 127 .\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs1486-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02162904292345047, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs1486-en\", \"title\": \"Doston c. Ashworth, 2019 QCCS 1486 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs1486-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02162904292345047, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs1486-en\"}, \"token_count\": 10173, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs1486-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq437\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq437-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Hafshejani c. Szewczyk, 2018 QCCQ 437 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2018 QCCQ 437 (CanLII) | Hafshejani c. Szewczyk | CanLII Hafshejani c. Szewczyk, 2018 QCCQ 437 (CanLII) Date: 2018-02-01 File number: 500-32-146528-155 Citation: Hafshejani c. Szewczyk, 2018 QCCQ 437 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/hqk36 > Hafshejani c. Szewczyk 2018 QCCQ 437 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-146528-155 DATE: February 1, 2018 ______________________________________________________________________ BEFORE THE HONOURABLE ENRICO FORLINI, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ LEILA DORJ HAFSHEJANI And HADI EL-DUWEINI Plaintiffs v. WLODZIMIERZ SZEWCZYK And BARBARA CICHOCKA And 9135-4894 QUÉBEC INC. d.b.u.n. AMERISPEC BUILDING INSPECTION SERVICES Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Plaintiff’s claim from the defendants $15,000 in damages following the purchase of a residential property in March 2014. They argue that the defendants Wlodzimierz Szewczyk and Barbara Cichoka (collectively referred to hereinafter as the “Sellers”) are liable on the grounds that the property they purchased from them was afflicted with latent defects at the time of the sale. [ 2 ] As for the defendant 9135-4894 Québec Inc, doing business as Amerispec Inspection Services (“AmeriSpec”), a firm which carries out the building inspections, Plaintiffs argue that its building inspector was negligent in carrying out the building inspection mandate and failed to point out in its report numerous defects with the Property. [ 3 ] AmeriSpec denies all liability and argues that it executed the building inspection contract in compliance with the terms of the agreement between the parties. [ 4 ] As for the defendant Sellers, although they were duly served with the Plaintiffs’ Claim, they did not defend the claim on the merits and therefore the Plaintiffs proceeded by default against them. Questions in Dispute a) Was the Property sold by the Sellers to the Plaintiffs afflicted with one or more latent defects at the time of the sale? b) Did AmeriSpec commit a fault in the execution of the building inspection agreement? c) What damages are Plaintiffs entitled to, if any? Context [ 5 ] In March 2014, the Plaintiffs purchased from the Sellers a split level bungalow (“Property”) located in Dollard-des-Ormeaux, Québec, and paid $370,000. [ 6 ] On February 7, 2014, prior to the purchase of the Property, the Plaintiffs entered into a residential building inspection agreement with AmeriSpec [1] (“Building Inspection Agreement”). On the same day, Co-Plaintiff Mr. El-Duweini inspected the Property along with Gary Wolff, a building inspector employed by AmeriSpec. [ 7 ] Following this inspection, AmeriSpec remitted to the Plaintiffs its Inspection Report. [2] [ 8 ] After taking possession of the Property on March 28, 2014, Plaintiffs allege that they discovered numerous hidden defects relating to the property. [ 9 ] By letter dated June 27, 2014, Plaintiffs notified the Sellers of these defects and enjoined them to repair them. [3] [ 10 ] By letter dated July 11, 2014, the Sellers denied all liability for the defects raised by the Plaintiffs. [4] [ 11 ] In March 2015, the Plaintiffs wrote to the Sellers to reiterate the existence of the defects already disclosed in June 2014, to raise additional defects and to inform them that they claim $32,075 to cover the cost of repairs. [5] [ 12 ] In the same month, the Plaintiffs also sent AmeriSpec a demand letter in which they allege that the building inspector failed to exercise an adequate and appropriate level of care while carrying out the inspection in February 2014. They claim from AmeriSpec $30,475 in damages. [6] [ 13 ] On March 31, 2015, Plaintiffs filed their Claim against the Sellers and AmeriSpec with the Court of Québec, Small Claims Division, and claim against them $15,000 in damages, plus court fees and expert fees. [ 14 ] The Plaintiffs sold the Property in 2016 for $372,500. [7] Analysis and Decision a) Was the Property sold by the Sellers to the Plaintiffs afflicted with one or more latent defects at the time of the sale? [ 15 ] Plaintiffs’ claim against the Sellers is based on the legal warranty against latent defects, which is codified in article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec (C.C.Q.). and which reads as follows: III. Warranty of quality 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale , free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound , however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect ; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. (Underlining added) [ 16 ] To succeed on a claim based on this warranty, the buyer(s) must prove the following four conditions: • The defect must be sufficiently serious; • The problem must have existed at the time of the sale; • The problem was hidden ( i.e. , latent) or not apparent; and • The defect was unknown to the buyer. [8] [ 17 ] Moreover, the Civil Code requires, that the buyer must give notice in writing of the defect(s) to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. [9] [ 18 ] Finally, the Civil Code also requires, that the buyer(s) put in default its vendor prior to carrying out any repairs, except where the debtor is in default by the sole operation of the law or exceptional circumstances dispense the buyer from sending a demand letter. [10] [ 19 ] Where the conditions for enforcing the warranty have been proved, and the seller has been properly notified of the existence of the defects and put in default, the buyer is entitled, at his discretion, to one of two remedies, Firstly, he/she can obtain the cancellation of the sale if the defect is sufficiently serious.  If the buyer elects to maintain the sale, he/she may obtain a reduction of the purchase price. The reduction amount may correspond to the cost of remedying the hidden defect or to the diminution in value of the good sold caused by the defect. [11] [ 20 ] It is also open to the buyer to seek damages if the seller knew about the defect or is presumed to have known about it (1728-1729 C.C.Q.). [ 21 ] In the present case, Plaintiffs seek compensation equivalent to the cost required to remedy the defects.  They allege that they actually suffered a loss of more than $15,000, but reduce the amount of their claim to this amount so as to bring their claim within the jurisdiction of the Small Claims Division. [ 22 ] In their demand letter to the Sellers and in their Claim, the Plaintiffs complain of no less than 13 hidden defects. These include: 1. mould and water infiltration in the closet of an upstairs bedroom; 2. defective porch roof; 3. carpenter ant infestation; 4. defective paint in one of the upstairs bathrooms; 5. improper installation of a window in the upstairs bathroom; 6. clogged plumbing in the drain of the kitchen sink; 7. rodent in the kitchen cabinet near the stove vent caused by a defective roof vent; 8. improper installation of the dishwasher; 9. inadequate insulation and heat escape of the property; 10. leak in the basement ceiling (problem with brick joints on the outside wall underneath the deck); 11. incorrect plumbing evacuation for the basement washing machine; 12. crack in the foundation; and 13. broken fence in the yard. [ 23 ] Based on the documentary evidence, namely the photos entered into evidence, as well as Mrs. Dorj’s testimony, Plaintiffs have established that the conditions required for enforcing the warranty against latent defects have been proven in regards to the following defects: 1. mould and water infiltration in the closet of an upstairs bedroom; 2. defective porch roof; 3. carpenter ant infestation; 4. defective paint in one of the upstairs bathrooms; 5. improper installation of a window in the upstairs bathroom; 6. rodent in the kitchen cabinet near the stove vent caused by a defective roof vent; 7. improper installation of the dishwasher; 8. leak in the basement ceiling (problem with brick joints on the outside wall underneath the deck); 9. incorrect plumbing evacuation for the basement washing machine; 10. crack in the foundation; and 11. broken fence in the yard. [ 24 ] As for the alleged defect with respect to the clogged kitchen sink, the Court considers that this does not fall within the legal warranty against latent defects as the Plaintiffs have not proven that this condition existed at the time of the sale. [ 25 ] Moreover, the Plaintiffs have not discharged their burden of proof as required by articles 2803 and 2804 C.C.Q. in regards to proving that the alleged inadequate insulation and heat escape of the Property constitutes a latent defect within the meaning of article 1726 C.C.Q. [ 26 ] The Court recognizes that the windows of the Property appear to have been changed in June 2015. [12] However, no probative evidence was adduced to establish that this change was caused by improper insulation.  Finally, no mention of the need to replace the windows was ever made in the notice of defect sent to the Sellers nor in the demand letter. [13] b) Did AmeriSpec commit a fault in the execution of the building inspection contract entered into with Plaintiffs? [ 27 ] Plaintiffs claim against AmeriSpec is premised on articles 1458 and 2100 C.C.Q., which read as follows: 1458. Every person has a duty to honour his contractual undertakings. Where he fails in this duty, he is liable for any bodily, moral or material injury he causes to the other contracting party and is bound to make reparation for the injury; neither he nor the other party may in such a case avoid the rules governing contractual liability by opting for rules that would be more favourable to them. 2100. The contractor and the provider of services are bound to act in the best interests of their client, with prudence and diligence. Depending on the nature of the work to be carried out or the service to be supplied, they are also bound to act in accordance with usage and good practice and, where applicable, to ensure that the work carried out or service supplied is in conformity with the contract. Where they are bound to an obligation of result, they may not be relieved from their liability except by proving superior force. [14] [ 28 ] To succeed in their claim against AmeriSpec, Plaintiffs must prove, on the balance of probability, that their provider of services failed to honour its contractual undertakings. In other words, they must prove that AmeriSpec did not carry out the building inspection services in conformity with the Building Inspection Agreement, in conformity with usage and good practice. [ 29 ] The courts have stated in numerous cases that pre-purchase building inspections, have, as a general rule, a limited scope. This principal was summarized as follows by Justice Christian Brunelle in Duplain c. Roy [15] : [81] Ceci dit, l’inspection pré-achat, qui participe pour ainsi dire de « l’obligation de s’informer » qui s’impose à l’acheteur, demeure d’une portée limitée : L’inspection préachat consiste à identifier les défauts apparents importants qui affectent l’immeuble et en diminue l’usage ou la valeur. Il s’agit d’une inspection des composantes visibles, sans qu’il ne soit requis de sonder planchers, murs, plafonds ou fondations. … [83] En d’autres termes, « la mission de l’inspecteur est de déceler les défauts apparents majeurs ou de constater des indices qui pourraient révéler des défauts cachés, susceptibles d’affecter l’usage de l’immeuble et d’en diminuer la valeur » : […] l’inspection pré-achat n’est pas une expertise. En principe, cet examen doit être attentif et sérieux quoique plutôt rapide et non approfondi. En l’absence d’un indice révélateur , l’acheteur ou l’inspecteur n’a pas à ouvrir les murs ou creuser autour des fondations. [ 30 ] In the instant case, when Plaintiffs contacted AmeriSpec to enquire as to what type of building inspection services it offers, it was informed that it could opt for a non‑exhaustive inspection or an exhaustive inspection, the latter at a greater price. [ 31 ] This distinction is also explicitly mentioned in the Building Inspection Agreement which AmeriSpec sent by email to Plaintiffs a few days before the contract was signed and the inspection was carried out. The non-exhaustive inspection provides for a “Visual inspection not technically exhaustive and excluding minor problem” whereas the exhaustive inspection includes “Detailed technical inspection including minor problems”. [16] [ 32 ] The Plaintiffs opted for the non-exhaustive inspection and paid AmeriSpec $475 (plus taxes) for its services. [ 33 ] Section 5.4 of the Agreement sets out the scope and limitation of the non-exhaustive inspection services: 1. The inspection consists of a visual examination of the ready readily accessible systems and components of the immovable, as listed in the standards of practice attached hereto. Such an examination shall be careful but brief and is intended to provide the client with the necessary information required for a better understanding of the condition of the immovable, as observed at the time of the inspection. 2. The inspection is not aimed at discovering, nor to allow for the discovery of latent defects affecting the immovable, but is aimed at discovering apparent defects and signs revealing the problems, that could substantially affect the integrity and the usefulness of the immovable, as well as meeting the requirements of a prudent and diligent examination of the immovable data is imposed on a given buyer by article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec , printed on the reverse, all in order to protect the clients rights to allow claims for latent defects against the seller. [ 34 ] Section 6 of the Agreement states that the “inspection shall be performed in accordance with the Standards of Practice for the building inspection of NBIEA… which forms an integral part of the present agreement.” Mr. El-Duweini signed a box immediately opposite this statement where he confirms that he has received and understands the Standards of Practice. He also admitted at trial that he had received the standard. [ 35 ] Finally, the Preamble to the Standards of Practice provide as follows: The visual inspection together with the written report need not deal with compliance regarding building codes, or with standards and regulations governing the construction industry and the health and safety industry, or with standards and regulations governing building insurability for any insurance risks. As the visual inspection is being performed in accordance with these standards of practice inspection is not technically exhaustive, the Inspector must recommend that a technically exhaustive inspection be performed by a specialist when a sufficiently high number of potentially important indications are identified that allow him to conclude that a deficiency or potentially important defect may affect a system or component of the building. [17] [ 36 ] The language of the Building Inspection Agreement confirms the limited scope of AmeriSpec’s obligations when it carried out the inspection of the Property in February 2014. [ 37 ] While Plaintiffs argue that they expected more than what AmeriScep wrote in its Report, they received what they bargained and paid for. [ 38 ] For example, while Plaintiffs complain of the fact that AmeriSpec should have indicated in its Report that the heating systems of the Property was inadequate or that is was a sign of a lack of proper insulation, the Standards of Practice state that the building inspector is not required to calculate the mechanical strength, adequacy or efficiency of any system or component, which includes heating systems. [18] Moreover, insulation is hidden behind walls or in the attic and is not visible or accessible for someone who carries out a “visual inspection”. [19] [ 39 ] Similarly, unless obvious, the presence of insects is specifically beyond the scope of the inspector’s mandate. [20] [ 40 ] Plaintiffs did not adduce proof as to what constitutes “usage and good practice” in the pre-purchase building inspection industry. [ 41 ] The Plaintiffs did enter into evidence a building inspection report of the Property drafted by Inspecto-Pro Inc. dated April 8, 2015. [21] They argue that the Inspecto-Pro Report is much more exhaustive than the AmeriSpec Report and shows that AmeriSpec’s building inspector was negligent in the performance of Building Inspection Agreement. [ 42 ] However, the Court ignores the scope of the building inspection carried out by Inspecto-Pro. Was it a non-exhaustive inspection or an exhaustive one? The Court has no answer to this important question. [ 43 ] The building inspection mandate the Inspecto-Pro inspector which is referred to at page 2 of its report and supposedly included in Annex E was not annexed to the report. [ 44 ] The Inspecto...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\nReport and shows that AmeriSpec’s building inspector was negligent in the performance of Building Inspection Agreement. [ 42 ] However, the Court ignores the scope of the building inspection carried out by Inspecto-Pro. Was it a non-exhaustive inspection or an exhaustive one? The Court has no answer to this important question. [ 43 ] The building inspection mandate the Inspecto-Pro inspector which is referred to at page 2 of its report and supposedly included in Annex E was not annexed to the report. [ 44 ] The Inspecto-Pro Report states that the inspection has been conducted “in accordance with the Standards of Practice as set forth by A.S.H.I. (American Society of Home Inspectors) and the Canadian Association of Home Inspectors (C.A.H.I.)”. These standards of practice were not entered it into evidence by the Plaintiffs and the Court ignores its contents and whether they are identical in scope to the Standards of Practice [22] which form an integral part of the Building Inspection Agreement entered into between Plaintiffs and AmeriSpec. [ 45 ] The author of the Inspecto-Pro Report did not testify at trial to explain the scope of his mandate. [ 46 ] Finally, the Court notes that Plaintiffs paid $475 for the AmeriSpec Report whereas they paid $2,750 plus taxes for the Inspecto-Pro Report. [ 47 ] In short, the Inspecto-Pro Report has little or no probative value in terms of assisting the Court in determining whether or not AmeriSpec breached its obligations in rendering its services to the Plaintiffs. [ 48 ] In conclusion, the Plaintiffs have not established that AmeriSpec breached its obligations in the performance of the Building Inspection Agreement.  Their claim against AmeriSpec will be dismissed. c) What damages are plaintiffs entitled to, if any? [ 49 ] Plaintiffs claim they incurred at least $15,000 to remedy the defects which the Court has found to constitute latent defects for the purposes of article 1726 C.C.Q. [ 50 ] The Court  has found that the following items constitute latent defects: 1. mould and water infiltration in the closet of an upstairs bedroom: 2. defective porch roof; 3. carpenter ant infestation; 4. defective paint in one of the upstairs bathrooms; 5. improper installation of a window in the upstairs bathroom; 6. rodent in the kitchen cabinet near the stove vent caused by a defective roof vent; 7. improper installation of the dishwasher; 8. leak in the basement ceiling (problem with brick joints on the outside wall underneath the deck); 9. incorrect plumbing evacuation for the basement washing machine; 10. crack in the foundation; and 11. broken fence in the yard. [ 51 ] For items # 1 and 3 above, Plaintiffs paid $4,069.98 to remedy the defects. [23] [ 52 ] For items # 4 and 5 above, Plaintiffs paid $631.22 to remedy the defects. [24] This amount is justified and will be awarded. [ 53 ] For item # 6 above, Plaintiffs paid $735.84 to an exterminator [25] plus $400 to repair the defective roof vent. [ 54 ] For item # 11, Plaintiffs’ paid $126.47 for a temporary repair of the fence. [26] [ 55 ] The total expended by Plaintiffs for items # 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 11 is $5,963.51. This amount is justified and will be awarded. [ 56 ] For item # 8, Plaintiffs’ contractor repaired this defect free of charge [ 57 ] For item # 2 above, Plaintiffs obtained an estimate of $1,000, but never carried out the remedial work. Likewise for item # 9, Plaintiffs obtained an estimate of $300 for the plumbing work but never carried out the repairs. For item # 7, Plaintiffs obtained an estimate of $2,000 to correct the defect. The Plaintiffs did not file into evidence written estimates for these items. For item # 10, Plaintiffs’ filed an estimate of $1,075 plus taxes. [27] [ 58 ] For items # 2, 7, 9, and 10, the corrective work was never carried out as the Plaintiffs sold the Property in 2016, roughly two years after they had purchased it from the Sellers. [ 59 ] Plaintiffs claim they nonetheless suffered a loss on the re-sale of the Property because of the defects which they claim they had to disclose to the new purchasers.  The Property was purchased in 2014 and Plaintiffs paid $370,000. It was resold in 2014 for $372,500. [ 60 ] That said, the Plaintiffs had the burden of proving that the sale price in 2016 would have been higher but for the existence of the defects which were not remedied and disclosed to the new purchasers.  This proof called for evidence on the market value of the Property with and without the unrepaired defects.  This proof was never made. [ 61 ] All the Court has to assess this part of the claim is one written estimate (item # 10) and the Plaintiffs’ verbal testimony (items # 2, 7, 9).  Using its discretion, the Court will award Plaintiffs $2,187.50 for these items. [ 62 ] Plaintiffs filed the invoice for legal fees incurred when they consulted a lawyer to seek advice with respect to their claim against the Sellers. [28] These fees are not recoverable under Québec civil law in the circumstances of the present case. [29] [ 63 ] Likewise there is no basis to order the compensation of the fees paid to Inspecto‑Pro. This expert report was not useful to the Court in the determination of the questions in dispute. [ 64 ] In conclusion, the Court will award Plaintiffs $8,151.01 ($5,963.51 + $2,187.50). FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES Plaintiffs’ Application against 9135-4894 Québec Inc. d.b.u.n. AmeriSpec Building Inspection Services, WITHOUT COSTS ; GRANTS in part Plaintiffs’ Application against Wlodzimierz Szewczyk and Barbara Cichoka; CONDEMNS Wlodzimierz Szewczyk and Barbara Cichoka to pay to Plaintiffs $8,151.01 with interest at the legal rate plus the additional indemnity provided for by article 1619 of the Civil Code of Québec , as of March 30, 2015 WITH COSTS of $200 payable by Wlodzimierz Szewczyk and Barbara Cichoka. __________________________________ ENRICO FORLINI, J.C.Q. Dates of hearing: December 8, 2016 and October 3, 2017. [1] D-3: Inspection Agreement with respect to a Chiefly Residential Immovable, entered into between AmeriSpec and co-Plaintiff, Mr. El-Duweini (hereinafter “Building Inspection Agreement”); see also D‑2A: National Building Inspectors and Experts Association Standards of Practice for Building Inspections recognized by the OACIQ (“Standards of Practice”).  The Standards of Practice form an integral part of the Building Inspection Agreement as per section 6(1) of the contract. [2] D-11: AmeriSpec Inspection Report. [3] P-1: June 27, 2014 notice of defect. [4] P-2: Sellers’ July 11, 2014 letter. [5] P-1A: Demand letter dated March 24, 2015 to Sellers. According to Mrs. El-Dorj, the Sellers never claimed this letter sent via registered mail from the post office. [6] P-1B: Demand letter dated March 24, 2015 to AmeriSpec. [7] P-18 ( en liasse ): see Counter-proposal to a promise to purchase, CP44735, signed March 31, 2016. [8] Leroux v. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 , para. 40 . See also ABB Inc . v. Domtar Inc , 2007 CSC 50 , at para. 50 . [9] Article 1739 C.C.Q. [10] Articles 1590 and 1594-1597 C.C.Q. [11] Dupuy c. Leblanc , 2016 QCCA 1141 (CanLII) ,paras. 25 and ff.; Verville v. 9146-7308 Québec inc. , 2008 QCCA 1593 . [12] P-12: Invoice from Blanca Canada. [13] P-1. [14] Article 2100 C.C.Q. applies because the Building Inspection Agreement (D-3 & D-2A) is considered a contract for services within the meaning of article 2098 C.C.Q. and thus governed by article 2100 C.C.Q. [15] Duplain c. Roy , 2016 QCCQ 6678 . [16] D-3. [17] D-2A: Standards of Practice. [18] D-3: Standards of Practice, Chapter II, Section II, §2, art. 9(3). [19] D-11: AmeriSpec Report: see page 17 as it pertains to lack of access to the attic. [20] D-3: Standards of Practice, Chapter II, Section III, §2, art. 14(9) [21] P-13: Inspecto-Pro Report. [22] D-2A. [23] P-5: M.I.S. Construction Invoice, July 10, 2014. [24] P-6: M.I.S. Construction Invoice, October 23, 2014, $287.44 and P-8: Blanca Canada Invoice #1822, $343.78. [25] P-10: Extermination Montreal work order #157919. [26] P-15: M.I.S. Construction invoice 140911. [27] P-11: Akton Injection estimate # 12734. [28] P-16. [29] See : Viel c. Entreprises Immobilières du Terroir ltée , 2002 CanLII 41120 (QC CA) , [2002] R.J.Q. 1262; also Hinse v. Canada (AG) , 2015 SCC 35 , at para. 168 .\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq437-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02295788936316967, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq437-en\", \"title\": \"Hafshejani c. Szewczyk, 2018 QCCQ 437 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq437-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02295788936316967, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq437-en\"}, \"token_count\": 6428, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2018qccq437-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs4127\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs4127-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Walsh c. Bhagwandass, 2023 QCCS 4127 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2023 QCCS 4127 (CanLII) | Walsh c. Bhagwandass | CanLII Date: 2023-10-27 File number: 500-17-111807-205 Citation: Walsh c. Bhagwandass, 2023 QCCS 4127 (CanLII) Walsh c. Bhagwandass 2023 QCCS 4127 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL No.: 500-17-111807-205 DATE: October 27, 2023 _____________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE ELENI YIANNAKIS, J.S.C. _____________________________________________________________________ RICHARD WALSH CHRISTINE SNIHUR Plaintiffs v. NEIL BHAGWANDASS CLEO IBRAHIM INDARJIT BHAGWANDASS Defendants _____________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT _____________________________________________________________________ OVERVIEW [ 1 ] Plaintiffs are claiming damages from Defendants on the grounds that the property they purchased from them was afflicted with latent defects at the time of the sale. [ 2 ] Specifically, they argue that severe water infiltration occurred after they moved in causing considerable damage to the basement. [ 3 ] For the reasons that follow, Plaintiffs’ claim is partially granted, considering that 50% of the defects at the source of the water infiltrations were serious latent defects that were unknown to them and that existed at the time of the sale. CONTEXT [ 4 ] By deed of sale signed on May 19, 2016, Defendants sold to Plaintiffs a residential property located in Hudson for $ 304 000 (the “Property”) [1] . The sale was made with legal warranty [2] . [ 5 ] Defendants, Neil Bhagwandass and Cleo Ibrahim are a married couple. They were the co-owners of the Property along with Indarjit Bhagwandass, Neil’s father, who never lived in the Property [3] . [ 6 ] Prior to the sale, the Property had been listed for $ 325 000. The Property is a single-family bungalow built in 1976 with a finished basement and a garage extension. This litigation deals with water leakage in the basement. [ 7 ] Plaintiffs, Richard Walsh and Christine Snihur, a married couple, were searching for a smaller home wishing to downsize from their house located in Beaconsfield. They visited the Property three times prior to purchase. [ 8 ] The sellers’ declaration dated February 7, 2016, which was remitted to Plaintiffs stated that there were “water spills in the basement due to cracks in the foundation” [4] . It is also stated that the cracks had been repaired in November 2011 and April 2012. Based on Defendants’ testimony, there was likely only one crack located at the back foundation wall of the Property which was repaired in November 2011 from the inside and then again in April 2012 from the outside (“Crack 3”) [5] . The evidence reveals that Crack 3 was not one of the sources of the water infiltration at issue in this case. [ 9 ] On March 14, 2016, Plaintiffs made an offer to purchase the Property for $ 295 000 [6] . The same day, Defendants made a counter proposal of $ 308 000 which Plaintiffs accepted subject to an inspection of the Property [7] . [ 10 ] On March 21, 2016, Plaintiffs had the Property inspected by a home inspector. Mr. Walsh was present with the inspector for the visit. [ 11 ] On March 22, 2016, the inspector sent the report to Plaintiffs, a copy of the report was also given to Defendants (the “Inspection Report”) [8] . The Inspection Report revealed the following items which are relevant to the current litigation: a) There is evidence of water entry or damage at the basement level and mention of a crack. The picture of “the crack” in the report reveals that it is located on the foundation wall in the hot water tank room (the “Water Tank Crack”) [9] ; b) The outside entry door is in need of repair or replacement; c) The ground level at the bottom of the windows needs to be lowered by digging out the soil and installing window wells to help prevent water from entering. The picture of the featured window is the one located in the basement bedroom (“Window 1”) [10] ; d) The window flashing is not adequate and caulking is needed; e) There is likely no French drain since at the time the house was built (1976) no such systems were required; f) The furnace unit appears to be over 20-25 years old. [ 12 ] On March 22, 2016, based on the findings contained in the Inspection Report, the parties agreed to an Amendment to the offer to purchase [11] . The purchase price was reduced by $ 4,000 because of the state and age of the furnace, thus lowering the price to $ 304 000. [ 13 ] Moreover, Defendants agreed to repair the Water Tank Crack prior to giving possession of the Property to Plaintiffs. The crack was repaired both from the inside and outside. It is admitted by both parties that the Water Tank Crack is not one of the sources of the water infiltration at issue in this case. [ 14 ] On May 19, 2016, the deed of sale was signed by the parties [12] . [ 15 ] Approximately two years later, in March 2018, Plaintiffs noticed a small amount of water on the floor under the stairs in the basement that was being used for storage. Mr. Walsh testified that he thought “it was a one off” and just kept an eye on it. No further water infiltration occurred that year. [ 16 ] However, in March 2019, severe water infiltration began seeping into the basement. Mr. Walsh testified that for two to three months [13] he removed approximately 45 gallons of water per day with a shop vac. [ 17 ] On March 22, 2019, Plaintiffs sent a letter of demand advising Defendants of the water infiltration [14] . [ 18 ] On May 31, 2019, Plaintiffs’ insurer sent an expert to visit the Property to attempt to determine the sources of water infiltration affecting the basement. On June 14, 2019, a report was provided to Plaintiffs (the “Insurance Report”) [15] . Ultimately, Plaintiffs’ insurance claim was refused. [ 19 ] On September 4, 2019, Defendants expert, Gary Bloomfield from Enspeco visited the Property in order to determine the sources of the water infiltration and provided a report (the “Bloomfield Report”) [16] . [ 20 ] On November 5, 2019, Plaintiff’s expert, Mounir Boutros from Onesta Bâtiment Conseil visited the Property and provided his report (the “Boutros Report”) [17] . [ 21 ] Finally, on December 4, 2019, Jennifer Landry of Benjel Chimistes Conseil visited the Property and provided an expert report on mold and air quality (the “Benjel Report”) [18] . [ 22 ] Since April 2019, Plaintiffs have been unable to use their basement. All the furniture has been removed and stored elsewhere. As well, the flooring and large areas of the walls were removed which notably revealed several cracks in the foundation and leaking form pins. The air in the basement was contaminated. Mold, cladosporium spores and mushrooms were found. PARTIES’ POSITIONS [ 23 ] Plaintiffs claim that the significant water infiltration which occurred in the Property’s basement is the result of latent defects that existed at the time of the sale. They argue that there are several sources of water infiltration, most of which were hidden at the time of the sale. [ 24 ] They also argue that Defendants misled them since there is evidence of previous water infiltration which was not disclosed at the time of the sale. Notably, a neighbour, Laura Bergeron, testified that she had a brief conversation with Defendants in the summer of 2015 stating that they had informed her of water coming in from the front of the Property [19] . [ 25 ] Plaintiffs seek compensation equivalent to the cost required to remedy the defects, plus other injury related to the loss of enjoyment of the Property, for a total amount of          $ 126,844.70 broken down as follows [20] : a) Cost of excavating to the footing of the Property, cleaning the walls, repairing the cracks and applying a water barrier: $ 21,238.18 (Akton) [21] ; b) Reconstruction of the basement: $ 64,387.68 (Deveau Construction) [22] ; c) Cost of removing and reinstalling the exterior deck: $ 4,377.83 [23] ; d) Decontamination costs: $ 6,841.01 (Benjel) [24] ; e) Damages for loss of use of the Property, stress, and anxiety and psychological harm: $ 30 000. [ 26 ] Plaintiffs are also seeking their experts’ fees for a total amount of $ 6,516.22: a) Boutros Report: $ 3,193.44 [25] plus $ 1,149.75 [26] for a total of $ 4,343.19; and b) Benjel Report: $ 1,483.18 [27] plus $ 689.85 [28] for a total of $ 2,173.03. [ 27 ] Defendants claim that other than Crack 3 and the Water Tank Crack which were repaired, there was never any other water infiltration during the years they lived in the Property. Ms. Ibrahim lived in the Property for almost 28 years. Her parents were the previous owners. They purchased the Property in 1988 and sold it to their daughter and her husband—the Defendants, on June 2, 2010. [ 28 ] Defendants installed the rear deck in 2010. Mr. Bhagwandass did the work himself with the help of some friends. They also renovated the basement in 2011. They hired a contractor who removed old carpeting, replacing it with a laminate raised floor and stripped the walls replacing the wood paneling with drywall. It is during these renovations that they noticed a small amount of water in the hot water tank room and found a crack at the back wall of the Property. This was Crack 3 that was repaired in 2011/2012. They are adamant that no other water infiltration occurred other than from the Water Tank Crack that was discovered in 2016 just prior to the sale, which was also repaired as per the Amendment to the offer to purchase. [ 29 ] They argue that the primary sources of the water infiltration are from the windows, primarily the one located in the basement bedroom (Window 1), a crack located at the front of the house (Crack 1) and from the front porch, where the door is not properly sealed. As such, the defects were not hidden. They add that if there are other sources of water infiltration, there is no evidence that they existed at the time of the sale. LEGAL PRINCIPLES A. Conditions for Legal Warranty against Latent Defects [ 30 ] Plaintiffs’ claim against the sellers is based on the legal warranty against latent defects, which is codified at article 1726 C.C.Q.: II. Warranty of quality 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale , free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound , however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect ; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. [Underlining added] [ 31 ] To succeed on a claim based on this warranty, the buyers must prove the following four conditions: a) The defect must be sufficiently serious; b) The defect must have existed at the time of the sale; c) The defect was hidden ( i.e. , latent); and d) The defect was unknown to the buyer. [29] [ 32 ] Regarding the third condition, a defect is deemed latent when a reasonable and prudent buyer could not have discovered it prior to the sale. This is an objective norm [30] . Notably, a prudent and diligent buyer does not have to open the walls nor excavate the foundations to uncover a defect [31] . [ 33 ] Moreover, the buyer must give notice in writing of the defects to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it [32] . [ 34 ] Finally, the Civil Code of Quebec also requires, that the buyer put in default his vendor prior to carrying out any repairs, except where the debtor is in default by the sole operation of the law or exceptional circumstances dispense the buyer from sending a demand letter [33] . B. Principles Governing Damages for Latent Defects [ 35 ] Where the conditions required for enforcing the warranty of quality have been proven and the seller has been notified of the existence of the defects and put in default, the buyer is entitled, at his discretion, to one of two remedies. Firstly, he can obtain the cancellation of the sale if the defect is sufficiently serious. Secondly, if the buyer elects to maintain the sale, he may obtain a reduction of the purchase price. [ 36 ] In this instance, Plaintiffs have elected to ask for the second remedy. [ 37 ] The reduction of the purchase price may correspond to the cost of remedying the hidden defect or to the decrease in value of the good sold caused by the defect [34] . [ 38 ] In assessing the reduction in price, the Court must not simply add all the repair costs incurred by the buyer. Rather, the amount of the reduction must be reasonable. The Court must consider all the circumstances and must be careful not to confer on the buyer an undue advantage or unduly enrich them [35] . [ 39 ] The amount of the reduction must not be disproportionate in regard to the purchase price. Moreover, where as a result of the repairs, the property has been improved and gives rise to a plus-value, the Court is justified in depreciating the reduction in price, even in the absence of expert evidence [36] . [ 40 ] Finally, as per article 1728 C.C.Q., if the seller had knowledge of the defect, he is also bound to pay all damages suffered by the buyer: 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer. [ 41 ] The buyer bears the burden of proving the seller’s knowledge of the defect, failing which the “ignorant seller” is immune from all other damages not relating to the restoration of the purchase price (“ l’immunité du vendeur ignorant ”) [37] . [ 42 ] To satisfy his burden, the buyer must establish that the seller, at the time of the sale, had the intention to deceive him: Pour condamner le vendeur à l’ensemble des dommages subis, il devient alors nécessaire de revenir à la notion de base, à savoir si le vendeur avait, au moment pertinent, l’intention de tromper son cocontractant [38] . QUESTIONS AT ISSUE [ 43 ] In the present case, the evidence reveals that the defects are serious. Defendants also do not contest that they received from Plaintiffs a written notice of the alleged defects nor that they were duly put in default.  In any event, the evidence establishes that the Plaintiffs complied with these duties [39] . [ 44 ] As such, the questions at issue are: a) Were the defects hidden and did they exist at the time of the sale? b) Did Defendants have knowledge of the defects at the time of the sale? c) What amount, if any, in reduction of the purchase price are Plaintiffs entitled to? DISCUSSION A. Were the Defects Hidden and Did they Exist at the Time of the Sale? [ 45 ] In this case, there are numerous sources of water infiltration as confirmed by both parties’ experts and by the expert mandated by Plaintiffs’ insurance company. [ 46 ] The Court finds that the Boutros Report is more reliable than the Bloomfield Report. This latter report identifies the principal sources of water infiltration as the windows, primarily Window 1, Crack 1 and the front door. However, the evidence shows that Mr. Bloomfield did not conduct any openings in the walls to investigate where the water was coming from. His primary focus appears to have been on the windows and the door. Once he confirmed that they were potential sources, he did not push his investigation any further. He also did not include in his report any of the results of the humidity tests that he conducted. [ 47 ] Conversely, the Boutros Report is more complete. Mr. Boutros conducted an exhaustive sweep of the Property attempting to identify as many sources of water infiltration as possible. [ 48 ] The Boutros Report identifies six sources [40] , coming from: a) Form pins located in the rear wall; b) Unrepaired cracks in the rear and front foundation walls; c) One repaired crack in the front foundation wall (Crack 1); d) The junction between the slab on grade and the foundation walls, due to the absence of a French drain; e) The upper rear foundation wall, where the deck is located; f) Window 1, which is beneath the ground and has no window well; [ 49 ] The Insurance Report identifies some of these sources as well but mentions an additional source stemming from an inadequate sealing at the junction of the front porch: 5.  We proceeded to vacuum off the water accumulation under the stairs, as seen in photo 28. Our examination of the exposed front porch junction to the foundation wall allows us to suspect inadequate sealing...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\nthe absence of a French drain; e) The upper rear foundation wall, where the deck is located; f) Window 1, which is beneath the ground and has no window well; [ 49 ] The Insurance Report identifies some of these sources as well but mentions an additional source stemming from an inadequate sealing at the junction of the front porch: 5.  We proceeded to vacuum off the water accumulation under the stairs, as seen in photo 28. Our examination of the exposed front porch junction to the foundation wall allows us to suspect inadequate sealing at the junction , as seen in photos 29 and 30. We proceeded to perform a water test on the porch and after only approximately a few minutes watering, severe water infiltration was observed along the foundation wall as can be seen in photos 31 to 38. Water begins to accumulate in the concrete slab under the raised floor and under the stairs after approximately 15 minutes of watering, as seen in photos 39 to 42. [41] [Underlining added] [ 50 ] Defendants’ expert also performed a water test on the front porch and observed water infiltration coming into the Property [42] . [ 51 ] The Court notes that the additional source identified by the Insurance Report places the water accumulation right under the stairs in the basement, where Mr. Walsh removed gallons of water. As such, this source should be added to the sources identified in the Boutros Report, bringing the total to seven. The Court identifies this last source, as source g). [ 52 ] Were the defects at the basis of these seven sources hidden or known to Plaintiffs? [ 53 ] The Court finds that sources a), b) and c) were hidden. The wall finish had to be removed for the cracks and form pins to be identified as leaking. The Court also finds that source e) was hidden. During his testimony, Mr. Boutros explained that the reason for this infiltration is that the deck was built higher than the foundation walls [43] . The defect at the source of this water infiltration was not visible since it was hidden by the deck. [ 54 ] The Court is also of the opinion that these defects—leaking cracks and form pins in the foundation were present at the time of the sale. Crack 1, which Defendants’ expert also admits is leaking and is an important source of the water infiltration existed at the time of the sale. Mr. Bhagwandass testified that he remembered this crack, stating it never leaked. As for the other cracks and form pins, they are part of the foundation structure and were revealed once the walls were stripped. No modifications were made by Plaintiffs to the basement and so the defects had to have existed at the time of the sale. [ 55 ] However, the Court finds that sources d), f) and g) were visible and known to Plaintiffs at the time of the purchase of the Property. Indeed, the absence of a French drain (source d)) was noted in the Inspection Report as well as the problematic situation concerning Window 1 and more generally the absence of window wells (source f)). Moreover, the Inspection Report stated that the front door needed to be replaced or repaired, also indicating a potential problem with the sealing at that junction (source g)). [ 56 ] Plaintiffs did not address any of these issues after they purchased the Property. As such, the defects stemming from sources d), f) and g) cannot be considered as latent defects. [ 57 ] Based on the above, the Court concludes that the water infiltration problems raised by Plaintiffs which stem from sources a), b), c) and e) constitute latent defects within the meaning of the warranty of quality. [ 58 ] The question then becomes: What is the percentage of damages which are attributable to the sources of infiltration that do not constitute latent defects—sources d), f) and g)? [ 59 ] While this aspect was not directly addressed in his report, during his testimony, Mr. Boutros stated that in his opinion 40% of the damages are attributable to sources d) and f). [ 60 ] However, this estimate is low. Although the Bloomfield Report cannot be relied upon to identify all the potential sources of water infiltration, the Court nevertheless considers the factual finding of this expert concerning Window 1 (source f)) to be revealing. On the morning of his visit, it had rained heavily, and upon entering the house, he observed that the water had infiltrated from Window 1 and Crack 1 [44] . This factual finding demonstrates that without any water tests performed, Window 1 is an important contributor to the water infiltration that occurred. [ 61 ] Moreover, Mr. Boutros did not perform a water test on the front porch, so he did not identify this additional source of water infiltration (source g)), which led to water directly under the basement stairs. Using its discretion, the Court is of the opinion that this additional seventh source as well as the additional weight to be given to Window 1 are responsible for another 10% of the water infiltration. [ 62 ] Thus, 50% of the damages caused to the Property stem from sources that do not constitute latent defects. B. Did Defendants have Knowledge of the Defects at the Time of the Sale? [ 63 ] Plaintiffs can only succeed on their claim of $ 30 000 in damages for loss of enjoyment of the Property, stress, anxiety and psychological harm if they can prove Defendants’ previous knowledge of the water infiltration. [ 64 ] The evidence as to whether Defendants had knowledge of the defects leading to the water infiltration at the time of the sale is contradictory. Defendants claim that save for Crack 3 repaired in 2011/2012 and the Water Tank Crack repaired in 2016 they never had any water infiltration in the Property. [ 65 ] Plaintiffs submit that since there was obvious water damage when they opened the walls (blackened wood and water rings) that this issue was longstanding and implies that previous water damage occurred. As well, they point to the fact that the bottom wall plates were cut in some areas, speculating that this was done to remove the rotten wood. Finally, they argue that the testimony of their neighbour Ms. Bergeron, a neutral witness is credible and must be relied upon. While Defendants remember an encounter with Ms. Bergeron, they categorically deny that the topic of water infiltration was ever discussed. [ 66 ] The Court finds that on the balance of probabilities, the evidence does not reveal that Defendants knew and/or hid the existence of previous water infiltrations. [ 67 ] First, the evidence revealed that Mr. Walsh removed gallons of water for two to three months in the spring of 2019. During this period, it is possible that given the significant amount of water (3000-4000 gallons) seeping into the basement, that mold and water damage occurred. Plaintiff’s experts recognized this possibility during their testimony. [ 68 ] Second, when reviewing Ms. Bergeron’s written declaration, she states that the conversation she had with Plaintiffs occurred in the summer of 2015: We spoke about the new deck built in the back, landscaping and the renovations being done before they re-list their home. It was during this conversation that they mentioned water coming in from the front, under the door sill. [45] [ 69 ] The proof reveals that the deck was built by Defendants in 2010 and that the basement was renovated in 2011. The initial listing of the house was in 2012. As such, the deck had been built and the basement renovated when the house was listed for the first time. [ 70 ] The year “2015” was a handwritten addition to Ms. Bergeron’s declaration and shows that the witness went back to pinpoint the exact timing of the discussion. Obviously, the timing of the other elements of the discussion—the building of the deck and the basement renovation are not contemporaneous to the relisting of the house in 2016. These inaccuracies indicate that Ms. Bergeron’s memory of the discussion could be unreliable. [ 71 ] Finally, the evidence did not reveal why for three years after the purchase of the Property there was very little or no water infiltration. If this was a permanent and recurring event, it remains unexplained why it just stopped for such a long period of time. [ 72 ] Therefore, the Court finds that the preponderance of the evidence does not demonstrate that Defendants had knowledge of previous water infiltration and/or that they tried to mislead Plaintiffs. Rather, their absence of knowledge supports the finding that several of the defects were hidden. [ 73 ] Plaintiffs’ claim in the amount of $ 30 000 is thus dismissed. C. What Amount, if any, in Reduction of the Purchase Price are Plaintiffs Entitled To? [ 74 ] As stated above, the Court must not only ascertain the reasonableness of the costs incurred to correct the latent defects but must also do so while considering the purchase price paid and the plus-value to the Property as well as avoid unduly enriching Plaintiffs. [ 75 ] The Court finds that the costs claimed by Plaintiffs are reasonable and directly related to remedying the water infiltration which occurred in the basement, under reserve of the following adjustments. [ 76 ] Firstly, regarding the work performed by Akton, it is admitted that a French drain was installed as well as window wells. Since these are components that Plaintiffs knew were absent at the time of purchase of the Property, the value for this work must be deducted. Moreover, these items constitute an improvement rather than the cost of remedying the consequences of the defect [46] . [ 77 ] Unfortunately, the Akton invoice does not provide a specific breakdown of the work performed [47] . Thus, it is not possible on the face of the invoice to identify the value associated with these aspects of the work. [ 78 ] Using its discretion and considering that the major aspect of the work performed by Akton was the excavation to the footing of the Property, repairing the cracks and placing a water barrier, the Court finds that the value associated to the two specific tasks of installing the French drain and window wells is minimal. As such, the Court attributes an amount of 10 % to these items, which brings the adjusted amount attributable to Akton’s work to $ 19,114.36 [48] . [ 79 ] Secondly, Plaintiffs are also claiming $ 64,387.68 for the reconstruction of the basement. [ 80 ] Plaintiffs paid $ 304 000 for a 40-year-old property. After performing the corrective work to remedy the defects, Plaintiffs will not have simply restored the basement to its original state, but rather will have a brand-new basement, which certainly increases the market value of the Property [49] . However, no evidence was adduced on the plus-value to the Property. [ 81 ] Caselaw has provided parameters to be considered by the Court when evaluating the applicable depreciation rate in the absence of expert evidence. The Court must consider the age of the property, the nature of the repairs, the quality of the materials used and the proportionality between the damages claimed and the value of the property. The caselaw reveals the presence of a spectrum of rates varying according to the Court’s discretion, between 25% to 79%. On average, a depreciation rate of approximately 33%, representing a third of the repair costs is applied [50] . [ 82 ] Here, considering the age of the Property (40 years-old), the fact that the materials used are not extravagant as well as the proportionality of the damages claimed and the value of the Property, in the absence of expert evidence on this point, using its discretion, the Court considers it reasonable to depreciate by 33% the expenses to “restore” the basement, representing $ 21,247.93 [51] . This brings the adjusted amount of these expenses to $ 43,139.75 [52] . [ 83 ] Finally, regarding the damages claimed for removing and reinstalling the deck as well as decontaminating the basement, these costs are directly related to correcting the defect and do not give rise to any plus-value. No adjustment to these amounts is required. [ 84 ] In summary, the total damages for the reduction of the purchase price are                  $ 73,472.95 broken down as follows: a) $ 19,114.36 for the work performed by Akton; b) $ 43,139.75 for the restoration of the basement; c) $ 4,377.83 for the removal and reinstalling of the deck; d) $ 6,841.01 for the decontamination work performed by Benjel. [ 85 ] Since Defendants can only be liable for 50% of these damages, the Court will award Plaintiffs an amount of $ 36,736.48. [ 86 ] Finally, the Court also grants Plaintiffs’ expert fees for the Boutros Report and the Benjel Report in the amount of $ 6,516.22 since these reports and the experts’ testimonies were useful and relevant in deciding the outcome of the case [53] . FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 87 ] GRANTS in part the Amended Motion to Institute Proceedings; [ 88 ] CONDEMS Defendants solidarily to pay to Plaintiffs the sum of $ 36,736.48 together with interest at the legal rate and the additional indemnity as of March 22, 2019, the date of the letter of demand [54] ; [ 89 ] THE WHOLE, with legal costs, including the experts’ fees in the amount of                 $ 6,516.22. __________________________________ ELENI YIANNAKIS, j.S.C. Mtre Denis Godbout LJT Lawyers Attorneys for Plaintiffs Mtre David Guénette Guénette Avocats Inc. Attorneys for Defendants Hearing dates: September 11-14, 2023. [1] Exhibit P-1. [2] Exhibit P-1, p. 2. [3] Examination out of Court of Neil Bhagwandass on November 4, 2020, p. 6. [4] Exhibit P-2. [5] See diagram of basement in Exhibit D-3, p. 2, “Crack 3”. [6] Exhibit P-3. [7] Exhibit P-3. [8] Exhibit P-5. [9] Exhibit P-5, p. 11; see also diagram of basement in Exhibit D-3, p. 2, “Repaired Crack”. [10] Exhibit P-5, p. 12; see also diagram of basement in Exhibit D-3, p. 2, “Window 1”. [11] Exhibit P-3. [12] Exhibit P-1. [13] At trial, Mr. Walsh stated that it was two months. In his out of Court examination held on November 4, 2020, he stated three months, p. 36. [14] Exhibit P-4. [15] Exhibit P-7. [16] Exhibit P-8. [17] Exhibit P-10. [18] Exhibit P-9. [19] Written Declaration of Laura Bergeron dated April 23, 2020. [20] Amended Motion to Institute Proceedings dated September 28, 2023, par. 16 and 17. [21] Exhibit P-11A. [22] Exhibit P-17. [23] Exhibits P-12 and P-12A. [24] Exhibit P-13A. [25] Exhibit P-14 [26] Exhibit P-14A. [27] Exhibit P-15. [28] Exhibit P-15A. [29] Leroux c. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 , par. 40 . See also : ABB Inc . c. Domtar Inc , 2007 CSC 50 ,  par. 50 . [30] Leroux c. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 , par. 42 . [31] Rivard c . Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 , par. 7 . [32] Article 1739 C.C.Q. [33] Articles 1590, 1594-1597 C.C.Q. [34] Dupuy c. Leblanc , 2016 QCCA 1141 , par. 25 and ff.; Verville c. 9146-7308 Québec inc ., 2008 QCCA 1593 . [35] Verville c. 9146-7308 Québec inc ., 2008 QCCA 1593 par. 56 & 62 ; article 1604(3) C.C.Q . [36] Deslauriers c. Desmangles , 2016 QCCS 3114 , par. 88-97 ; Riendeau c. Guy Brière Courtier d'assurances inc. , 2012 QCCS 6071 , par. 141-148 (Appeal dismissed, 2013 QCCA 459 ); Rivard c. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 , par. 26-29 ; Blais c. Duchesne , 2021 QCCA 978 , par. 34 ; Ajram c. Dadoun , 2023 QCCS 539 , par. 150 . [37] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 nd Ed., Montreal, Wilson & Lafleur, 2008, p. 278. [38] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 nd Ed., Montreal, Wilson & Lafleur, 2008, p. 281. [39] Exhibit P-4. [40] Exhibit P-10, p. 51. [41] Exhibit P-7, p. 2. [42] Exhibit D-3, p. 13. [43] Exhibit P-10, photos 16, 17 and 21. [44] Exhibit D-3, p. 2. [45] Written Declaration of Laura Bergeron dated April 23, 2020. [46] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 nd Ed., Montreal, Wilson & Lafleur, 2008, p. 258. [47] Exhibit P-11A. [48] $ 21,238.18\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n, p. 13. [43] Exhibit P-10, photos 16, 17 and 21. [44] Exhibit D-3, p. 2. [45] Written Declaration of Laura Bergeron dated April 23, 2020. [46] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 nd Ed., Montreal, Wilson & Lafleur, 2008, p. 258. [47] Exhibit P-11A. [48] $ 21,238.18 - $ 2,123.82 = $ 19,114.36. [49] Rivard c. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 , par. 26 , 27 . [50] Deslauriers c. Desmangles , 2016 QCCS 3114 , par. 88-97 (50%); Riendeau c. Guy Brière Courtier d'assurances inc. , 2012 QCCS 6071 , par. 141-148 (50%) (Appeal dismissed, 2013 QCCA 459 ); Grenier c. Nadeau , 2018 QCCS 4837 , par. 194 (29%); Rivard c. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 , par. 26-29 (25%); Blais c. Duchesne , 2021 QCCA 978 , par. 34 (50%); St-Pierre c. Levy , 2022 QCCS 1066 , par. 122-123 (30% and 50%); Lortie c. Deslauriers , 2023 QCCS 2549 , par. 183-184 (33%); Voyer c. Carignan , 2023 QCCS 2031 , par. 74-75 (33 %); Ajram c. Dadoun , 2023 QCCS 539 , par. 150 . 159-224 (50%-79%). [51] $ 64,387.68 x 33% = $ 21,247.93. [52] $ 64,387.68 - $ 21,247.93 = $ 43,139.75. [53] Maison Simons inc. c. Lizotte , 2010 QCCA 2126 , par. 40-46 . [54] Exhibit P-4.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs4127-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02408008649945259, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs4127-en\", \"title\": \"Walsh c. Bhagwandass, 2023 QCCS 4127 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs4127-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02408008649945259, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 5, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs4127-en\"}, \"token_count\": 8417, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2023qccs4127-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2017qccs1810\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2017qccs1810-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Ferreira c. Szeptynska (Rubin), 2017 QCCS 1810 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2017 QCCS 1810 (CanLII) | Ferreira c. Szeptynska (Rubin) | CanLII Date: 2017-05-08 File number: 500-17-072132-122 Other citation: [2017] EXP 1692 Citation: Ferreira c. Szeptynska (Rubin), 2017 QCCS 1810 (CanLII) Ferreira c. Szeptynska (Rubin) 2017 QCCS 1810 JL 4473 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF MONTRÉAL N° : 500-17-072132-122 DATE : May 8, 2017 ______________________________________________________________________ IN THE PRESENCE OF: THE HONOURABLE CHANTAL LAMARCHE, J.S.C. ______________________________________________________________________ JORGE FERREIRA LORIANA FIORI Plaintiffs v. RAJA SZEPTYNSKA (RITA RUBIN) Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ 1. INTRODUCTION [ 1 ] Plaintiffs seek damages from Mrs. Raja Szeptynska (“ Mrs. Rubin ”) [1] because of the latent defects, namely cracks in the foundation walls and footing, they discovered in 2011 with respect to the house they purchased from her in 2004. [2] [ 2 ] Further, they seek compensation for the moral damages they suffered.  They allege that Mrs. Rubin was aware of the latent defects and that she failed to divulge them.  According to them, she acted abusively and is therefore liable for their extra judicial fees.  Finally, they ask for the provisional execution of the present judgment. [ 3 ] Mrs. Rubin claims that Plaintiffs did not act as prudent and diligent buyers of a house built in 1954 when they refrained from conducting a professional inspection. There were several visible signs of past structural problems when Plaintiffs purchased the house.  The defects cannot be considered hidden.  Hence, the legal warranty does not apply.  Alternatively, she alleges that the cracks in the foundation walls appeared shortly after the erection of the house and they were properly repaired. [ 4 ] Mrs. Rubin affirms that she should not be held liable for any of the damages suffered by the Plaintiffs.  She denies she acted abusively. 2. BACKGROUND [ 5 ] In 1954, Mrs. Rubin and her late husband purchased the land situated at […] in Côte St. Luc. [ 6 ] A two bedroom semi-detached bungalow was built thereon.  Mr. and Mrs. Rubin raised their family here. [ 7 ] When Mrs. Rubin and her family moved into the property, the basement was not finished yet.  The foundation walls were visible. [ 8 ] In the late 1950’s or early 1960’s, they hired contractors to finish the basement.  A family room and a powder room (sink and toilet) were built.  Repair work on cracks in the foundation walls were done around that period.  In the late 1990’s, they renovated the bathroom on the ground floor with the help of their son Irwin Rubin. [3] Irwin is involved in the commercial real estate business. [ 9 ] In the winter of 2002, while the Rubin’s were in Florida, water pipes in the house froze because the furnace broke down. Irwin took care of contacting the insurer and organizing the repairs with appropriate contractors. They received compensation from their insurer. [ 10 ] Upon returning from Florida, Mrs. Rubin testified that the house was “ livable ”. [ 11 ] The same year, the basement was renovated.  Mrs. Rubin wanted to add a bedroom and a complete bathroom. [ 12 ] Although the renovations were incomplete, Mrs. Rubin decided in 2004 to sell the house.  She was no longer able to take care of her husband who was diagnosed with Alzheimer’s disease. They moved into an assisted living senior residence. [ 13 ] At that time, the Plaintiffs were living in a condominium. They were looking for a house with a backyard to raise their child.  Mrs. Fiori worked as an assistant manager in a pharmacy and Mr. Ferreira was a ″ demolisher ″.  In 2006, he had a workplace accident.  He has been unable to work since then. [ 14 ] Prior to purchasing Mrs. Rubin’s house, they visited the inside and the outside of the house on three or four occasions. [ 15 ] According to the real estate listing and the description Mrs. Rubin provided to the Plaintiffs, the house had a new roof, piping, radiators, oil tank, furnace, paved driveway, retaining wall as well as, a renovated kitchen and a newly finished basement. [4] [ 16 ] Mrs. Rubin was a welcoming person.  As Plaintiffs and Mrs. Rubin testified, the contact between the parties was friendly. [ 17 ] At the time of purchase, Plaintiffs did not hire a professional to inspect the house.  Mrs. Rubin did not inform Plaintiffs of the damages suffered in 2002 nor the presence of cracks in the foundation walls or the fact that they had been repaired. [ 18 ] In 2005, Plaintiffs renovated the bathroom on the ground floor and discovered that the floor and walls behind the ceramic tiles were rotten and that mold was present. [5] [ 19 ] They informed Mrs. Rubin and asked her to pay for the repairs as they considered this to be a latent defect.  She refused. [ 20 ] Plaintiffs instituted proceeding before the Small Claims Court reducing their claim from $9,000 to $7,000. [6] [ 21 ] On August 23, 2007, Justice Julie Veilleux concluded that there were latent defects but reduced the amount claimed to $2,333 as Mrs. Rubin “ should not be liable for Plaintiff’s wish to renovate his bathroom and upgrade it ”. [7] [ 22 ] In the late fall or winter of 2010, the Plaintiffs noticed a damp smell in the basement.  It reminded them of the smell in the ground floor bathroom when they discovered the mold. [ 23 ] They contacted Raffaele Maccio a licenced construction contractor.  With the use of a camera under the basement raised floor he discovered what he considered to be a major crack in one of the foundation walls.  He called Sam Leo, a structural engineer. [ 24 ] It was impossible to conduct a complete assessment with the camera as the raised floor was on a checkered steel structure that blocked the camera.  In order to assess the magnitude of the problem, the drywall and the raised floor were progressively removed.  An excavation around the house was executed.  Three significant cracks were discovered in the foundation walls as well as a crack in the footing of the property. The cracks ran through the entire depth of the foundation walls. [ 25 ] This demolition and excavation work also highlighted that cracks in the foundation walls had previously been repaired some years before by applying black tar and attaching steel bars over the cracks to prevent them from opening.  The steel bars were attached on the inside and outside walls of the foundation.  These repairs did not stop the cracks from enlarging as stated by all experts involved in the present case [8] . [ 26 ] None of these cracks or repairs were visible when Plaintiffs purchased the property as they were either, inside behind the dry wall or, outside below the ground level. [ 27 ] According to expert Leo, poorly designed and built foundation walls were the cause of the defects.  The footing was not wide enough and “ there exist none or negligent steel reinforcement in these foundation walls ” [9] preventing the cracks caused by differential settlement from opening. [ 28 ] On June 6, 2011, Plaintiffs sent a demand letter to Mrs. Rubin outlining their findings and giving her 10 days to take cognisance and responsibility for the foundation’s condition. [10] They notified Mrs. Rubin that should she fail to do so, she would be held liable for the cost of repairs and other damages. [ 29 ] Irwin asked his friend expert Goodz, an architect, to do a visual inspection on June 20, 2011.  At that time, no excavation was done outside but the interior foundation walls were exposed and the problematic cracks were visible. [ 30 ] Following his visit, expert Goodz wrote a report in the form of an email of less than a page.  He noticed cracks on the inside foundation walls, evidence of some repairs and separation of the cracks post repairs.  He wrote that it was impossible to determine when these repairs were made. [11] According to him, these cracks should be repaired by the injection of epoxy grout. [ 31 ] On the outside, he noticed cementitious coating over the foundation walls.  As for cracks, his email highlighted only hairline cracks and emphasized that these outside cracks did not correspond to the locations of cracks he noticed inside. [ 32 ] Mrs. Rubin’s former attorney, M e Glen M. Derstenfeld, responded to Plaintiffs demand letter that no latent defects existed and ended his letter with the following: “ Moreover, any claim filed by your client shall be responded to by way of cross-demand, seeking damages from your client for frivolous and manifestly unfounded proceedings ”. [12] [ 33 ] Plaintiffs proceeded with the correction work as per expert Leo’s engineering plan. [13] [ 34 ] Longer and larger steel plates were anchored on the foundation walls over cracks to prevent them from enlarging even more and on the corners of the foundation walls. Underpinnings were installed under the footing at five locations in order to increase the surface on which the house sits to avoid any more cracking.  A double waterproof membrane was fixed around the foundation walls.  A French drain and a sump pump were also installed to reduce the hydraulic pressure on the foundation walls. The finishing of the basement was redone as well as the landscaping. [ 35 ] Since the Plaintiffs did not have the financial means to have the entire work executed in 2011, the work was executed in the summer and fall 2011 in the back and side of the house and in 2012 in the front. [ 36 ] Plaintiffs claim $124,056.93 [14] for the costs of the above described work. [ 37 ] Because they believe Mrs. Rubin was aware of these defects and she failed to divulge them, Plaintiffs are also claiming the following damages: − $25,000 for anxiety, pain and stress; − $25,000 for loss of enjoyment related to the use of their home; and − $25,000 as punitive damages. [ 38 ] In addition, they seek the provisional execution of the present judgment and the reimbursement of their attorney fees incurred by reason of Mrs. Rubin’s abusive defence and conduct during the litigation. [ 39 ] The Court was informed, three to four weeks before the hearing, that Mrs. Rubin withdrew the mandate of her attorney as she did not have the financial capacity to pay his fees for a ten day hearing.  She announced that she would be representing herself. [ 40 ] On the afternoon the first day of the hearing, Mrs. Rubin who was assisted by Irwin, informed the Court that the hearing was overwhelming for her.  She said she needed her attorney.  Mrs. Rubin was 90 years old at the time. [ 41 ] The Court adjourned until the next morning to allow her attorney to be present. [ 42 ] Her attorney was present for the remaining of the hearing. 3. ISSUES [ 43 ] The present case raises the following questions: ➢ Are the cracks in the foundation walls and footing latent defects? ➢ Are Plaintiffs entitled to: a) $124,056 as the cost for repairs; b) $25,000 for anxiety, pain and suffering; c) $25,000 for loss of enjoyment related to the use of their home; d) $25,000 as punitive damages? ➢ Has Mrs. Rubin behaved in an abusive manner? ➢ Should the provisional execution of the present judgment be ordered? 4. ANALYSIS 4.1 Are the cracks in the foundation walls and footing latent defects? 4.1.1 The law [ 44 ] As per Section 1726 of the Civil Code of Quebec the vendor has the legal obligation to guarantee the sold good against latent defects which render the good “ unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. ” [ 45 ] In order to benefit from the legal warranty of Section 1726 C.C.Q. four conditions must be met.  The defect must (1) be serious; (2) exists at the time of the sale; (3) be hidden; and (4) unknown to the buyer.  As the Court of Appeal reminds us in Leroux v. Gravano [15] : [40]   Pour se prévaloir de la garantie légale contre les vices cachés, quatre conditions doivent donc être respectées : (i) que le bien soit affecté d’un vice grave, l’intensité de cette gravité ayant été définie par la jurisprudence à partir des expressions « impropre à l’usage » et « diminuent tellement son utilité »; (ii) que le vice existait au moment de la vente; (iii) que le vice soit caché, qualité qui s’évalue objectivement et qui est accompagnée d’une obligation de s’informer; et (iv) que le vice soit inconnu de l’acheteur, qualité qui s’évalue subjectivement et dont le fardeau de preuve appartient au vendeur. […] [42]   Concernant la troisième condition, la jurisprudence enseigne que le vice qui est caché est un vice que l’acheteur raisonnablement prudent et diligent n’aurait pas constaté. Il s’agit d’une norme objective . La qualification d’un vice comme caché ou apparent est une question de droit; la Cour d’appel peut conséquemment, sans intervenir dans les conclusions factuelles du juge de première instance, rectifier une erreur de qualification en s’appuyant sur ces mêmes conclusions factuelles. [43]   Notre Cour précisait dans Marcoux , le danger que constitue le fait d’attribuer à l’acheteur l’obligation de moyens de l’inspecteur pré-achat et d’ainsi fausser la norme objective, la seule qui existe : [20]       Notre Cour rappelle que selon les termes de l’ article 1726 in fine C.c.Q. , il existe une seule norme applicable, celle qui examine la conduite de l’acheteur prudent et diligent . Le juge de première instance ne peut écarter cette règle en appréciant la conduite de l’inspecteur et en imputant à l’acheteur, en l’absence de signes annonciateurs d’un vice potentiel, ce qu’il considère un manque de prudence et un manquement aux règles de l’art de la part de l’inspecteur. Le test de l’ article 1726 C.c.Q . est celui de l’acheteur prudent et diligent et non pas celui de l’expert tatillon sur qui reposerait une sorte d’obligation de résultat . [21]       Par ailleurs, l’inspection pré-achat n’est pas une expertise. En principe, cet examen doit être attentif et sérieux quoique plutôt rapide et non approfondi. En l’absence d’un indice révélateur, l’acheteur ou l’inspecteur n’a pas à ouvrir les murs ou creuser autour des fondations. [Renvois omis; Nous soulignons.] [Emphasis added] [ 46 ] A defect will not be considered hidden if it can be observed by “ a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert .” [16] The jurisprudence and doctrine has determined that if at the time of purchase, they are signs that raise suspicions of a potential defect and the purchaser does not conduct a more in-depth inspection by using an expert for example, the defect found later might not be considered hidden as the purchaser will not have acted as a reasonably prudent and diligent buyer. [17] Generally, it is only in the presence of suspicious signs that a buyer might have the obligation to resort to an expert to benefit from the legal warranty. [ 47 ] Again in Leroux v. Gravano [18] , the Court of Appeal wrote: [44]   Quoiqu’il ne soit pas nécessaire d’ouvrir les murs, de creuser les fondations, il n’en demeure pas moins qu’un indice puisse soulever des soupçons et requérir de pousser l’examen. [45]   Dans St-Louis , la Cour écrivait : [39]       En résumé, lorsque l'immeuble présente un indice permettant de soupçonner l'existence d'un vice potentiel, l'acheteur prudent et diligent, qui n'a pas fait appel à un expert, doit le faire ou vérifier autrement et de façon satisfaisante ce qui est suspect. Dans le cas où l'acheteur a déjà fait appel à un expert, la présence de signes annonciateurs d'un vice potentiel oblige l'expert à faire une inspection plus approfondie. S'il ne la fait pas et qu'un vice est mis à jour, la conclusion que le vice n'était pas caché s'imposera ; [Nous soulignons.] [53]   Malgré l’obligation d’un acheteur de pousser son étude d’un immeuble avant de l’acheter lorsqu’il a connaissance d’un indice de nature à semer le doute dans son esprit, l’acheteur n’a plus...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\ngligente peut être sanctionnée, ces termes ne visant qu’à déterminer l’intensité de la faute génératrice de responsabilité. (…) [196] À mon avis, la partie qui ne respecte pas le principe de proportionnalité et qui, de ce fait, compromet la justice et l’équité, s’aventure en chemin périlleux. Lorsque ce non-respect échappe au pouvoir de surveillance et d’encadrement du tribunal et qu’il se perpétue au cours de l’instance, un juge pourrait certes conclure, a posteriori, au caractère déraisonnable de la procédure et sanctionner l’abus qui en résulte. [197] En pareille situation, il y aura abus si une personne prudente et diligente, au regard du déroulement de l’instance et du procès, conclurait à une utilisation excessive ou déraisonnable de la procédure et donc, à la faute ou à la négligence de son auteur, en considérant les coûts et le temps exigés, la finalité de la demande, l’importance des principes qu’elle soulève ou de l’intérêt en jeu. [198] Bien entendu, ce standard doit être analysé en fonction des circonstances propres à chaque situation, le juge d'instance bénéficiant à cet égard d'une large discrétion dans l'appréciation d’une conduite qui s’avère déraisonnable. Il est d’ailleurs bien établi qu’une cour d'appel doit faire preuve de réserve à l'égard d'un jugement qui se prononce sur ces matières. [References omitted] [Emphasis by the Court] [ 165 ] As Justice Pierre A. Gagnon stated in Dion v. Simard [49] the proportionality principle forces a party to question itself as to the opportunity and usefulness of each step it takes in its pending case. [ 166 ] The original claim was for approximately $128,000. [ 167 ] Mrs. Rubin refused to admit any of the invoices [50] filed by Plaintiffs.  She also refused to admit other documents, amongst which were receipts and permits issued by the city of Côte St. Luc for the repair work [51] , the listing of her own real estate agent [52] and even the writ of execution for the Small Claims Court judgment [53] .  She forced Plaintiffs to request a 10 day hearing in order to properly file those exhibits.  Although there were two questionable invoices (work on the chimney and a portion of expert Maccio’s invoice which were both subtracted from the claim as soon as questions were raised by Mrs. Rubin’s attorney), the remaining invoices, except for two, were all admitted on Day 4 of the hearing by Mrs. Rubin’s attorney.  Had it been done before it would not have been necessary to set a 10 day hearing. [ 168 ] On June 20, 2014, during a case management conference when the hearing dates were fixed, Justice Jacques R. Fournier indicated in the minutes of that conference: “L’un ou l’autre des parties ou les deux ne respectent pas les règles de la proportionnalité » [ 169 ] In light of her complete denial of some of the above mentioned exhibits, the Court considers that Mrs. Rubin did not obey the principle of proportionality. [ 170 ] It is impossible for the Court to know how long her denial of the exhibits delayed the holding of the hearing.  However, the Court believes that the delay will be compensated, at least in part, with the legal interests and the special indemnity the Court will grant. [ 171 ] Furthermore, a few weeks prior to the beginning of the hearing, Mrs. Rubin withdrew the mandate of her attorney alleging she was unable to pay his fees for a 10 day hearing. [ 172 ] On the first morning of the hearing, she explained that her expert witnesses were in Florida and unavailable. She did not inform them in a timely fashion of the hearing dates.  Since they were not properly assigned by Mrs. Rubin or her attorney, the Court informed her that it was her responsibility to assure their presence if she needed them. [ 173 ] After the first half day of hearing, she informed the Court that she needed the services of her attorney and wanted an adjournment to allow her attorney to be present. [ 174 ] The Court agreed to adjourn to the next morning informing her that she could be held liable for Plaintiffs’ costs for the loss of half a day. [ 175 ] Upon the return of her attorney on the second day of the hearing, her attorney informed the Court that he wanted Mrs. Rubin’s experts to testify and one of them would have to be heard in person and would not be available before the Friday (expert Turner).  He was in contact with expert Goodz to find a time to have him testify through videoconference. [ 176 ] The Court and Plaintiffs accepted to accommodate Mrs. Rubin to have expert Goodz testify whenever he was available on videoconference.  The presentation order of Plaintiffs’ evidence was therefore modified to allow that videoconference. [ 177 ] As for the expert Turner, the Court decided that he would have to testify by videoconference if his turn would come before Friday, fifth day of the hearing. [ 178 ] Finally, expert Turner testified on Friday. [ 179 ] All those steps taken to accommodate the expert witnesses of Mrs. Rubin caused extra fees for Plaintiffs as expert Leo had to comeback two days in Court to be present during the testimony of expert Goodz (Day 4 of hearing) and expert Turner (Day 5 of hearing) in addition to the extra day caused by the adjournment of the hearing on the first day in the middle of expert Leo’s testimony. [ 180 ] The extra costs for the presence of expert Leo during two days in addition to his testimony will be compensated by the legal costs the Court will grant in favour of Plaintiffs as it will include all their expert costs. [ 181 ] What will not be compensated with the interests, special indemnity and judicial costs is the legal fees incurred by Plaintiffs for the extension of the hearing resulting from Mrs. Rubin’s abusive conduct. [ 182 ] Plaintiffs’ attorney informed the Court that his fees were $3,000 per day. [ 183 ] The Court cannot grant Plaintiffs’ attorney fees for the three extra days they claim because the hearing took six days instead of three as discussed during a case management conference call with a judge a few days prior to the beginning of the hearing. [ 184 ] Notwithstanding the reasons for which Plaintiffs’ and Mrs. Rubin’s attorneys envisioned with that judge that three days would be sufficient, that was done just a few days before the beginning of the hearing.  Up until that time, Plaintiffs’ attorney was preparing himself for a 10 day hearing. [ 185 ] Upon the return of Mrs. Rubin’s attorney to the case, it was clear that the hearing would last more than three days.  Although it is impossible to assess the exact number of days the hearing should have lasted and acknowledging Mrs. Rubin’s attorney effectiveness as he jumped on a moving train, the Court considers that the equivalent of a whole day was wasted.  This hearing should have been over in five days. [ 186 ] Therefore the Court will grant $3,000 to Plaintiffs, in addition to the costs of their experts to compensate them for the abusive behaviour of Mrs. Rubin. 4.4 Provisional execution [ 187 ] Articles 660(10 o ) and (12 o ) C.C.P. provides for the automatic provisional execution of the judgment when the Court adjudicates on an abuse of procedure and rules on legal costs but only for the portion of legal costs not exceeding $15,000.  The Court does not even have to grant it in its conclusions. [54] [ 188 ] Therefore, there will a be provisional execution by application of the law for the amount of $3,000 granted by the Court for abuse and on the legal costs up to a maximum of $15,000 granted by the court including the expert fees of $11,238.30, as determined below. [ 189 ] Article 661 C.C.P. allows the Court to order provisional execution if an appeal is likely to cause serious harm to Plaintiffs.  Provisional execution must be granted for serious reasons. [ 190 ] Examples of provisional execution are found in the jurisprudence when for example the debtor tries to exhaust the other party in order to discourage it from pursuing its case [55] . [ 191 ] Plaintiffs allege the following facts to support their demand for the provisional execution of the present judgment: ➢ Mrs. Rubin is 90 years old and is in fragile health as stated by her; ➢ She said that she was unable to pay her attorney fees for a ten day hearing; ➢ She tried to exhaust them by contesting without reasons many exhibits and not assigning her experts in the hope of having an adjournment; [ 192 ] In the present case, even if Mrs. Rubin did not file numerous proceedings in the court file, the Court is convinced that Mrs. Rubin tried to exhaust the Plaintiffs in the hope that they would abandoned their legal proceedings against her. [ 193 ] She did not respond seriously to the demand letter sent by plaintiffs in June 2011. She tried to delay the hearing as much as possible by forcing Plaintiffs to request a ten day hearing by abusively refusing to admit many exhibits, changing her mind about the presence of her attorney after half a day of hearing and forcing Plaintiffs’ experts to be present before the Court for two extra days. [ 194 ] Furthermore, there is a risk that Mrs. Rubin will try to delay payment of the sums granted by the present judgment as she might have difficulty paying them as she claims to not have the financial capacity to pay her attorney for a ten day hearing.  In addition, it took a writ of seizure to execute the Small Claims Court judgment of $2,333. [ 195 ] Plaintiffs explained that fixing the latent defects financially drained them.  They had to remortgage their house, could not pay expert Maccio and received money from Mr. Ferreira’s mother.  Now they also have to pay their attorney for the hearing and its preparation. [ 196 ] These elements are, in the present case, sufficient for the Court to order the partial provisional execution of the present judgment for the amount of $60,000. 4.5 Experts fees, interests and special indemnity [ 197 ] Plaintiffs’ experts were useful for the Court. [ 198 ] Expert Leo has filed three invoices for the total amount of $7,979.27.  This includes preparation of his report and preparation for the hearing as well as his presence in court for Day 1 and 2 of the hearing. [ 199 ] Expert Leo had to come back when Mrs. Rubin’s experts testified on Day 4 and 5 of the hearing. Regarding Day 4 of the hearing, the Court will grant half day for his fees as the testimony of expert Goodz was in the afternoon only. As for Day 5, the Court will grant a whole day. [ 200 ] The total fees of expert Leo granted by the Court are $11,238.30 [56] . [ 201 ] Expert Maccio candidly informed the Court that he was not charging any fees to be present before the Court, it was included in his services. [ 202 ] Except for the moral damages granted, the legal interests and special indemnity will be calculated as of November 1, 2011 since expert Leo’s pictures [57] show that the work was still not completed in October 2011 and the main portion of the work for which the Court grants compensation was executed in 2011. The legal interests and special indemnity on the moral damages granted will be calculated as of June 2011 which is the date of the demand letter and the date on which Plaintiffs’ damages began. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 203 ] GRANTS the Demand of Plaintiffs in part ; [ 204 ] CONDEMNS Defendant to pay Plaintiffs the amount of $111,495 for the latent defects with legal interests and special indemnity provided by Article 1619 C.C.Q. as of November 1, 2011; [ 205 ] CONDEMNS Defendant to pay $5,000 to Plaintiff Jorge Ferreira and $5,000 to Plaintiff Loriana Fiori for moral damages with legal interests and special indemnity provided by Article 1619 C.C.Q. as of the date of the demand letter of June 6, 2011; [ 206 ] CONDEMNS Defendant to pay Plaintiffs $3,000 for abuse with legal interests and special indemnity provided by Article 1619 C.C.Q. as of the date of the present judgment; [ 207 ] ORDERS the provisional execution of the present judgment for $60,000 (in addition to legal costs and the amount of $3,000 for abuse) notwithstanding appeal; [ 208 ] WITH LEGAL COSTS in favor of Plaintiffs, including $11,238.30 in expert fees. __________________________________ Chantal Lamarche, j.s.c. M e Guy Sirois GUY SIROIS AVOCAT Attorney of the Plaintiffs M e Howard L. Tatner HOWARD L. TATNER Attorney of the Defendant Dates of hearing: January 9, 10, 11, 12, 13 and February 22, 2017. [1] Defendant has informed the Court that she preferred to be addressed as Mrs. Rubin, her married name. [2] Plaintiffs also allege latent defects in the electrical wiring of the basement.  However, no evidence was adduced on this issue and the financial compensation asked by Plaintiffs does not include any amount for these defects. [3] The Court will use the first name of Irwin Rubin for ease of reading and without any disrespect. [4] Exhibit P-16. [5] Exhibit P-14. [6] $7,000 was the limit for Small Claims Court. [7] Exhibit P-13, par. 11. [8] Mr. Daniel Turner, structural engineer and Mr. Murray Goodz, an architect called by Mrs. Rubin to testify as an expert witnesses as well as Mr. Sam Leo structural engineer and Mr. Raffaele Maccio construction contractor, both called by Plaintiffs to testify as expert witnesses. [9] Exhibit P-3. [10] Exhibit P-4. [11] Exhibit D-2A. [12] Exhibit P-5. [13] Exhibit P-3. [14] As they announced during the hearing, at closing arguments, Plaintiffs amended their claim for the costs of the work and reduced it from $156,669 to $124,056. [15] 2016 QCCA 79 , par, 40, 42 and 43. [16] Article 1726 al.2 C.C.Q. [17] Marcoux v. Picard , 2008 QCCA 259 , par. 21 ; Jacques DESLAURIERS, « Chapitre I – Le droit commun de la vente », dans Collection de droit 2016-2017, École du Barreau du Québec, vol. 5, Obligations et contrats , Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2016, p. 204 et 205. [18] Leroux v. Gravano , 2016 QCCA 79 , Supra , note 15, par. 44, 45 and 53. [19] Béique v. Rodier , 2009 QCCS 1648 , par. 34 . [20] Martineau v. Dupont , 1999 CanLII 11245 (QC CS) . [21] Rochefort v. Succession de Matte, 2017 QCCS. [22] Brès v. Giraud , 2016 QCCS 6307 ; Marcoux v. Picard , Supra , note 17; Paiement v. Archambault, 2016 QCCA 1586 . [23] Sect. 1728 C.C.Q. [24] Mrs. Rubin’s testimony and paragraph 27 of her Re-Amended Plea. [25] Exhibit D-3A, p. 2. [26] Banque de Nouvelle-Écosse v. Raymond , 1987 CanLII 294 (QC CA) . [27] The estimated cost of the under pilling was assessed by expert Turner during his examination at $2,500 per pile and approximately 10 piles would have been needed. [28] Exhibit P-16. [29] Exhibit D-2A, par. 7. [30] Exhibit P-21, pictures # 42 and # 44 of October 18, 2011. [31] Exhibit P-7e) invoice: $7,057, proof of payment $2,700; Exhibit P-7n) invoice $1,264, proof of payment $700. [32] Exhibit P-12. [33] Exhibit P-7J. [34] Verville v. 9146-7308 Québec inc. , 2008 QCCA 1593 ; Marcoux v. Picard , Supra , note 17; Paiement v. Archambault , Supra , note 22. [35] Paiement v. Archambault , Supra , note 22, par. 9 ; Gagnon v. Lacroix 2017 QCCS 127 , par. 246 to 252 . [36] Exhibit P-7d), P-7d)-1 and P-7d)-2. [37] Exhibit P-13. [38] Exhibit P-7j). [39] Exhibit P-12 invoice of la Compagnie Paint’Ex enr. [40] $12,461 - $2,575 = $9,886 – 20% ($1,977) = $7,909. [41] Exhibit P-7J-1 and P-12-1. [42] Exhibit P-7-I. [43] $124,056 - $12,461 (Top-Notch) + $\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 4\\nP-7d)-2. [37] Exhibit P-13. [38] Exhibit P-7j). [39] Exhibit P-12 invoice of la Compagnie Paint’Ex enr. [40] $12,461 - $2,575 = $9,886 – 20% ($1,977) = $7,909. [41] Exhibit P-7J-1 and P-12-1. [42] Exhibit P-7-I. [43] $124,056 - $12,461 (Top-Notch) + $7,909 (Top-Notch) - $8,009 (Durasec’s invoices for French drain and waterproof membrane) = $111,495. [44] Gardner v. Desjardins , 2016 QCCS 6158 ($5,000); Gagnon v. Lacroix , Supra , note 35, ($5,000; the work was executed over a period of 1½ years) and Fournier v. Cadrin , 2008 QCCS 927 ($10,000 for both plaintiffs that had to live with their parents for two months ($5,000 per plaintiff)). [45] CQLR, c. C-12 . [46] Article 52 C.C.P. [47] Acadia Subaru v. Michaud , 2011 QCCA 1037 (Requête pour autorisation de pourvoi à la Cour suprême rejetée (C.S. Can., 2012-01-12) 34383). [48] 2015 QCCA 14 . [49] 2017 QCCQ 332 , par. 62 and 63 . [50] Exhibits P-7 and P-12. [51] Exhibits P-7A and P-7B. [52] Exhibit P-16. [53] Exhibit P-15. [54] Denis FERLAND et Benoît EMERY, Précis de procédure civile du Québec , 5 e éd., vol. 1 « (Art. 1-301, 321-344 C.p.c. », Montréal, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2015, par. 2-2088. [55] Leclerc v. Clark , 2007 QCCS 5800 , par. 66 et 67 . [56] $3,449.25 (Exhibit P-3B) + $2,356.99 (Exhibit P-3C) + $2,173.03 (Exhibit P-3C) + $1,086 (half day on Day 4) + $2,173.03 (Day 5) = $11,238.30. [57] Exhibit P-21.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2017qccs1810-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.022224679589271545, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2017qccs1810-en\", \"title\": \"Ferreira c. Szeptynska (Rubin), 2017 QCCS 1810 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2017qccs1810-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.022224679589271545, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2017qccs1810-en\"}, \"token_count\": 8199, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2017qccs1810-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2011qccq15139\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2011qccq15139-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Zawidzki c. Swinghammer, 2011 QCCQ 15139 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2011 QCCQ 15139 (CanLII) | Zawidzki c. Swinghammer | CanLII Zawidzki c. Swinghammer, 2011 QCCQ 15139 (CanLII) Date: 2011-11-10 File number: 500-22-167321-101 Citation: Zawidzki c. Swinghammer, 2011 QCCQ 15139 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/fp8mc > Zawidzki c. Swinghammer 2011 QCCQ 15139 COURT OF QUEBEC CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-22-167321-101 DATE: November 10, 2011 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE ELIANA MARENGO, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ RICHARD ZAWIDZKI and ANNA LO FARO Plaintiffs v. LAURI SWINGHAMMER Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] On June 20, 2006, plaintiffs bought defendant's home, for $380,000.00.  Alleging latent defects, they are now suing him, in damages, for $36,991.79. THE FACTS [ 2 ] Before purchasing the 42 year old cottage, located at 45 Aspen Lane, in Hudson, plaintiffs had it inspected. [ 3 ] The inspection report (exhibit P-4) contained the following relevant findings and recommendations: \\\"REPORT SUMMARY [ … ] INTERIOR Install an air exchanger and a Humidex system in order to control the moisture of this home. EXTERIOR Create a positive grade around the foundation.  The key to the humidity in this home is a control of the high water table, keep the water away from the home and keep a two foot clearance around the slab.  Decorative stone would be a good choice. Each of these items will likely require further evaluation and repair by licensed tradespeople.  Obtain competitive estimates for these items. (…)\\\" (page 1) \\\" Site Drainage : Action Necessary – There are areas of the lot that need adjustment to allow water to run off of the property.  There are areas that may allow water to interfere with the foundation. (page 4) \\\" Driveway Condition : Attention Needed – The driveway needs attention and minor repair to prevent further deterioration. Regular maintenance to the gravel and drainage will be required every year. [ … ] Walkway Condition:         Action Necessary – The walkways need action.  Repair or replacement is indicated.  There is heaving noted in the walkways that needs to be repaired to prevent further deterioration or a trip hazard. Action Necessary – The walkway slope is such that water either does not run off or run-off is directed towards the house.  This run-off may interfere with the foundation.  The driveway should slope to allow water to run away from the structure and foundation.\\\" (page 5) \\\"Type of Foundation: Slab on grade – Refers to a concrete slab poured on a prepared gravel or sand base at grade level.\\\" (page 7) \\\" Earth-to-Wood Clearance: Action Necessary – There is earth-to-wood contact.  This condition needs immediate action.  This contact can cause water infiltration and give access to insects.  It is suggested that there should be a minimum of a 4\\\" clearance between the earth and any wood siding or framing materials. Structural Caulking : Attention Needed – Several spots around the structure were noted that need to be caulked.  Caulk should be applied to areas where brick and wood siding meet, trim around window frames or doors, and piping and service penetrations.  Also, any cracks that allow moisture or wind entry should be caulked to prevent deterioration.\\\" (page 10) [ 4 ] Exactly three years after they bought the property, plaintiffs discovered that there were cracks in the foundation. [ 5 ] Plaintiff Lo Faro discovered these cracks quite coincidentally, as she was \\\"digging out flowers next to the aluminium siding next to the foundation wall.\\\"  She first saw \\\"a small crack\\\", and, after digging some more, \\\"saw more of the crack on the cement part (of the foundation).\\\" [ 6 ] After consulting landscapers, who were unable to advise, plaintiffs contacted foundation specialists Héneault et Gosselin Inc., Roger Bisson Inc. and finally Groupe Atlantech Inc. (\\\"Atlantech\\\"), with a view to assess the cracks which had been uncovered. [ 7 ] In September, 2009, plaintiffs received a repair estimate from each of the above (exhibit P-9), and decided to hire Atlantech to execute the repairs. [ 8 ] Atlantech essentially proposed to build a new concrete foundation to support the affected exterior wall along 25 feet; to reinforce the existing foundation; and to waterproof the foundation with a membrane, for a total cost of $15,750.00. [ 9 ] Plaintiffs entered into a contract with Atlantech, on October 21, 2009 (exhibit P‑11). [ 10 ] In November, 2009, plaintiffs retained the services of architect Richard Santo (\\\"Santo\\\"), with a view to assess the situation. [ 11 ] In his report, dated November 13, 2009 (exhibit P-12), Santo wrote: \\\" OBSERVATIONS: The Unipave walkway has been removed and landscaping was destroyed which included trees, shrubs and plants. Front and side foundation walls have been excavated to expose them to just above the footing. The bricks on the front façade of playroom appear to have been added after garage construction (to blend wall into front wall of house), and are supported by a steel angle bolted to the existing 8\\\" thick foundation wall.(Bricks are suspended outside of foundation wall!). This steel angle is held into existing foundation wall with bolts.  (Vertical brick loading is transferred in top foundation wall via bolts)  The length of the horizontal crack extends 22 ft on front of playroom foundation bulging outwards, the corner is broken, multitude of horizontal and vertical cracks continue around to the side wall, continue past the brick chimney 26\\\" horizontally to the breezeway addition built in 1989 by vendor.  All cracks show signs of previous attempt at repair. (See photos) The horizontal cracks below ground have formed, opened, and repairs are noted.  Foundation wall is now cracked right through. The garage concrete floor slab now has a level finished interior ceramic tile floor, in very good condition. DISCUSSION: The front wall loaded in such a way gives eccentric force and an imbalance in the loading of the foundation wall (probably without any steel reinforcing). Foundation walls are designed to take vertical loads and dissipate them into the soil from center of the footing. (Soil must have a superior capacity than building loads imposed) The broken foundation wall now cannot support existing loads and has failed, and will collapse. Unsupported floor of family room is in danger of movement and cracking (the finished floor tiles). PROBLEMS: The foundation wall is unsafe, and must be replaced immediately before further costly structural damages occur. SOLUTION: A new concrete foundation wall has to be repaired monolithically on extended footing to support the two walls, as well as bricks. Re-support the floor slab underneath. Repair resulting cracks or holes in walls and floors caused by repair works. CONCLUSION: The defect could not be discerned prior to the sale.  It was discovered only later on excavating the earth next to the foundation walls. This defect is not recent, and was there when the house was sold recently in 2006. Some repairs have been attempted by others in the past below the ground (broken joint shows patching cement) An attempt to repair by vendor? This defect needs immediate repair. An engineer's (member of Order of Engineers of Quebec) stamped plan and specifications must be provided by contractor selected prior to start of work.  Plans and specifications must be followed. A City building permit is required. Budget to carry out all work. ± $ 25 000 plus taxes.\\\" [ 12 ] Plaintiffs duly notified defendant, before proceeding with the work; however, defendant ultimately chose not to visit the site. [ 13 ] The work was carried out over the course of several months.  The project included landscaping and drainage work, as appears from exhibit P-17. [ 14 ] Plaintiffs' total claim amounts to $38,217.95, plus Santo's fees, as per their amendments at trial. [ 15 ] Part of the claim ($10, 000.00) is for \\\"perte de jouissance, perte de temps, stress, etc.\\\" (par. 32 of the introductory motion), plaintiffs contending that defendant was aware of the alleged defect and wilfully chose not to disclose it. [ 16 ] Defendant, on the other hand, flatly denies having had prior knowledge of the cracks in the foundation. [ 17 ] Defendant's expert, engineer David Khudaverdian, an expert in building envelopes, concrete restoration and waterproofing, assessed the problem, and basically concluded as follows: \\\"Our comments following our review of these documents are as follows: 1.          The presence of cracks in the concrete foundation wall is very common and should be expected, especially in a home nearly 50 years old, built in the 1960's; 2.          In the absence of a basement level and waterproofing membrane, the crack does not lead to the possibility of any risk of water ingress within the home; 3.          There have never been any signs of reported building movement of cracking within the home or along the exterior masonry wall directly above the foundation wall reported by home owners, or the many experts (including the pre-purchase inspector) who reviewed the condition of the home. The points above would suggest that despite the presence of the crack in the foundation wall likely since 1979 (and possibly since the time of original construction), the wall and the home itself has remained structurally stable. Regarding the cost for damages being claimed, we note that description of work relating to the contractor quotations obtained included many supplementary repair items unrelated to repairing the crack in the foundation wall, including: • Pouring of new concrete footings; • Installation of new waterproofing membrane system along the foundation wall; • Piles installation; • Pouring of new concrete slab; • Installation of new below grade drainage layer; • Pouring of new concrete within gap below suspended floor slab. All of the above items are considered improvements to the existing structure and home, and are supplementary to addressing the crack in the foundation wall.  Consequently, the cost for any of the works performed above should not be considered within the damages being claimed by the Owners (Lo Faro/Zawidski).  At this time, we do not have any confirmation of the actual scope or cost of the repair work executed. Based upon our own calculations, a reasonable budget cost required to execute the repairs to address the crack in the foundation wall, including excavation and backfill, would be in the order of $ 9,500 plus taxes. In addition, we should point out that the generally accepted useful service life of a residential structure is often considered to be in the order of 100 years.  Given that the home itself is in the order of 50 years old, it is logical to suggest that the costs being claimed to improve the existing foundation wall and extend the useful life of the home, should consequently further be reduced by half.\\\" (exhibit D-3, pages 1 and 2) ANALYSIS [ 18 ] In virtue of article 1726 of the Civil Code of Québec , the seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. [ 19 ] The issue, in this case, is not whether the defect was latent or whether it existed at the time of the sale. [ 20 ] Given that the cracks were below ground and the foundation is slab on grade, they were indeed hidden. [ 21 ] Furthermore, both Santo and Khudaverdian agreed that the cracks existed at the time of the sale.  In fact, they may have existed and been created, at the time of the original construction. [ 22 ] The issue is rather whether or not the cracks in question rendered the property unfit or diminished it usefulness to such an extent as to warrant a recourse under article 1726 C.C.Q. [ 23 ] According to Santo, the broken foundation wall could not support the load created by the brick wall added by defendant in 1992; and, the unsupported floor of the family room was in danger of movement and cracking.  He concluded that the affected area of the wall was unsafe and needed to be replaced immediately, before further costly structural damage occurred. [ 24 ] According to Khudaverdian, despite the presence of these cracks in a restricted area of the foundation, \\\"the wall and the home itself (had) remained structurally stable\\\". [ 25 ] Khudaverdian concluded that the load on the inverted steel shelf angle under the bricks was negligeable, given the strength of the concrete.  Khudaverdian also declared that this type of installation (which Santo condemned) was quite \\\"common, used and proven\\\", and that he had seen similar installations involving three (3) stories of bricks, with no damage to the concrete.  He stated that the cracks probably appeared at the time of construction and worsened over time, due to water seepage and cycles of freezing and thawing. [ 26 ] Khudaverdian repeatedly underlined the absence of structural distress to the building.  Indeed, there was no evidence of movement therein, namely no damage to the masonry wall directly above the problem area or inside the home.  There were no cracks in the walls or tiles of the family room, and all doors and windows opened and closed properly. [ 27 ] Furthermore, Khudaverdian did not understand or agree with the extent of the repairs carried out by plaintiffs, who, according to him, \\\"went overboard\\\".  He failed to understand why a thickening wall was built to support a brick wall which had been shown to be well-supported.  It had been there for \\\"16 to18\\\" years; had \\\"stayed plum\\\"; and \\\"the so-called damaged foundation wall was directly beneath it, so it (had) stood the test of time.\\\" [ 28 ] Khudaverdian opined that, if the masonry wall and inverted steel shelf angle installation had been a problem, \\\"Movement would have been reflected in the masonry, in the interior finish and around the doors and windows, which would not have closed.\\\" [ 29 ] In short, according to Khudaverdian, the cracks in the foundation wall, which were located in a \\\"relatively small area\\\", had no structural effect on the rest of the home.  Had they not been repaired, they may have widened, but the structure would have stayed in the same condition \\\"for many many years\\\". [ 30 ] At pages 140 to 142 of Vente, Louage, Contrat d'entreprise ou de service [1] , Professor Jacques Deslauriers writes: \\\"A. Les caractéristiques du vice donnant ouverture au recours pour vices caches Pour réussir un recours fondé sur des défauts cachés, l'acheteur doit prouver toutes les caractéristiques permettant de conclure au caractère caché du défaut, telles qu'énoncées ci-après: son caractère caché, sa gravité, l'ignorance qu'avait l'acheteur de son existence et l'existence de ce défaut au moment de la vente. [ … ] 1) Vice grave ( art. 1726 C.c.Q. ) a) Principes généraux Le défaut invoqué doit présenter un caractère suffisamment grave pour rendre le bien impropre à l'usage auquel il était destiné.  Le vice ne doit pas nécessairement empêcher toute utilisation du bien; il suffit qu'il en réduise notablement l'utilité ou en empêche l'usage normal .  La gravité du défaut doit être telle qu'elle justifie un recours en resolution de la vente ou en reduction du prix.  Autrement dit, l'acheteur n'aurait pas acheté ou n'aurait pas payé si haut prix s'il avait connu ces défauts.  Ces affirmations sont en fonction du degré de gravité du vice affectant le bien.  D'ailleurs, l' article 1604 C.c.Q. empêche la résolution d'un contrat si le défaut du débiteur de l'obligation, en l'occurrence le vendeur débiteur de la garantie, est de peu d'importance.  Il n'y a lieu alors qu'à la diminution proportionnelle de la prestation, évaluée selon les circonstances. b)  Exemples et applications Pour illustrer la gravité d'un défaut donnant ouverture à un recours pour défaut caché, on peut citer certains exemples tirés de la jurisprudence et concernant des ventes immobilières ou autres. i) Édifices La découverte lors des travaux de rénovation d'un défaut des poutres, jadis calcinées par un incendie ou confectionnées de façon inhabituelle, sans affecter la solidité de l'immeuble, ne constitue pas un défaut justifiant un recours pour défauts cachés. L'aménagement des fondations d'un immeuble sous la nappe phréatique constitue un défaut caché. Toutefois, l'anomalie dans des fondations reposant de façon néanmoins stable sur le roc depuis 20 ans ou l'absence de dale de béton dans un sous-sol,...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... ou confectionnées de façon inhabituelle, sans affecter la solidité de l'immeuble, ne constitue pas un défaut justifiant un recours pour défauts cachés. L'aménagement des fondations d'un immeuble sous la nappe phréatique constitue un défaut caché. Toutefois, l'anomalie dans des fondations reposant de façon néanmoins stable sur le roc depuis 20 ans ou l'absence de dale de béton dans un sous-sol, n'affectant pas la solidité d'un édifice, ne donnent pas non plus ouverture à un recours pour vices cachés.\\\" (emphasis added) [ 31 ] Plaintiffs had an obligation to prove their allegations, by a preponderance of evidence (articles 2803 and 2804 C.C.Q.). [ 32 ] Respectfully, the Court is unable to grant more weight and credence to Santo's opinion over Khudaverdian's, and, if anything, prefers Khudaverdian's theory over Santo's, as it appears to be more logical and consistent with the facts. [2] [ 33 ] Given that the cracks had probably existed for many years; given that the imperfect foundations had supported a 2 car garage, later converted into a family room with a ceramic floor upon which lay a 2,500 to 3,000 pound billiard table, for years, without evidence of distress to the building; given that the masonry wall was erected in 1992 and remained intact for years; given that, some 18 years later, there were still no signs of structural distress to the building; given that no samples were taken and no tests conducted with a view to assess the strength and durability of the concrete; given the absence of conclusive and preponderant evidence as to the alleged diminished usefulness (\\\"déficit d'usage\\\")  of the foundation wall; the Court is unable to find in favour of plaintiffs: \\\" [ 105 ] Le professeur Jeffrey Edwards écrit au sujet des vices cachés ce qui suit: Le vice réprimé est celui qui porte atteinte à une certaine fin, soit l'usage du bien. Il ne s'agit pas d'un vice de construction ou de fabrication dont la finalité peut être bénigne ou anodine.  Lorsque le vice obstrue la fin prescrite, le bien souffre d'un déficit d'usage. En réalité, la garantie réprime moins la présence d'un vice que l'état défectueux qu'il provoque.  Elle assure l'inexistence d'un déficit d'usage ou, de façon plus concise, l'usage du bien.\\\" [3] [ 34 ] In La vente , Professor Pierre-Gabriel Jobin states as follows: \\\"157. Vice grave – La première condition exige que le vice soit grave – condition qu'il ne faut pas confondre avec l'existence même du vice.  Le texte du Code indique les critères de la gravité du vice : celui-ci doit être tel que, si l'acheteur l'avait connu, il n'aurait pas acheté le bien ou l'aurait acheté mias à un moindre prix.  Pour decider si un vice est assez grave pour donner ouverture à la garantie, on ne considère pas seulement le coût de sa réparation par rapport à la valeur du bien : on regarde tous les aspects, dont notamment la baisse de la valeur marchande du bien, la diminution de son usage normal (ou déficit d'usage), les inconvénients, actuels ou prévisibles, du vice pour l'acheteur, étant entendu que les attentes légitimes de l'acheteur sont plus grandes pour un bien neuf que pour un bien usagé – parfois une même lacune ne constitue pas un vice pour un bien passablement usagé alors qu'elle l'est pour un bien neuf.  Un juge a écrit que \\\" la garantie légale contre les vices cachés s'éteint progressivement avec le temps \\\". [4] [ 35 ] It is to be noted that the diminished usefulness or \\\"deficit d'usage\\\" of the property must be evaluated as it existed at the time of the sale, as the seller is not bound to warrant the buyer that the property will not suffer a diminished usefulness in the future [5] . [ 36 ] As regards the expenses incurred for landscaping, drainage, the walkway and the driveway, they were foreseeable, given the inspector's findings and recommendations relating thereto (pages 1, 4, 5 and 10 of report P-4). [ 37 ] What is more, other expenses, such as the installation of a membrane, constitute an improvement [6] . [ 38 ] What should be made of plaintiffs' allegations of bad faith on defendant's part? [ 39 ] The evidence showed that the foundation walls had been repaired in the past. [ 40 ] The first owner of the home, George Ellerbeck, credibly testified that he did not carry out these repairs. [ 41 ] Defendant, who was the only other owner of the home, repeatedly answered, \\\"I don't recall.\\\", to several questions posed by the lawyers regarding these repairs. [ 42 ] It seems improbable that defendant, who is a mechanical engineer, had the breezeway and new garage built, in 1989, and then the exterior masonry wall built, in 1992, and did not see or was not made aware of the presence of cracks, repaired or not, in the foundation.  After all, said additions required excavating around the same area. [ 43 ] What effect, if any, does this have on plaintiffs' recourse? [ 44 ] Article 1728 C.c.Q. states as follows: \\\"1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer.\\\" [ 45 ] Deslauriers states the following, on the subject of the seller's bad faith: \\\"(…) Les recours pour vices cachés peuvent être intentés malgré la bonne foi du vendeur; des dommages-intérêts pourront s'ajouter au remboursement du prix, total ou partiel selon le cas, si le vendeur connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer les défauts dont le bien vendu était affecté ( art. 1728 C.c.Q. ). Par ailleurs, l'acheteur qui invoque le dol doit, pour avoir gain de cause, prouver ce dol en démontrant qu'il a été incité à contracter par les manoeuvres ou les réticences du vendeur ou d'un tiers à la connaissance de celui-ci ( art. 1401 C.c.Q .).  Si le dol est prouvé, ceci pourra permettre à l'acheteur d'obtenir gain de cause, même si le défaut invoqué n'a pas toutes les caractéristiques d'un défaut caché.\\\" [7] (emphasis added) [ 46 ] In Pominville c. Demers [8] , the Court of Appeal decided as follows: \\\"Les appellants n'ont démontré aucune erreur dans le jugement de première instance, qui justifierait l'intervention de la Cour; D'une part, la juge a conclu de son appreciation de la prevue à la bonne foi de l'intimé lors de sa transaction avec les appellants.  Elle écrit: Or, le défendeur avant de vendre a habité l'immeuble pendant dix-huit ans sans n'éprouver aucun problème qui aurait pu être relié à l'incendie.  On ne peut dans les circonstances assimiler sa conduite à un dol.  Il a de bonne foi informé les demandeurs d'une inondation et n'a pas pensé à les aviser de l'incendie puisqu'il était survenu il y a plus de vingt ans et qu'il n'en avait pas éprouvé de consequences fâcheuses.\\\" [ 47 ] Although it is probable that, at some point, defendant became aware of the existence of the cracks in the foundation, there is no evidence that he purposely and fraudulently withheld this information from plaintiffs, with a view to incite them to buy, as the building showed no signs of distress.  Of course, had the cracks affected the building structurally, he would have had no choice but to disclose this information.  However, this was not the case. [ 48 ] Furthermore, the Court does not know which cracks defendant saw; if they were repaired when he saw them; or if he himself had them repaired. [ 49 ] However, it is also probable, that the cracks, as they appeared in 2006, had deteriorated over time, having been exposed to water run-off from the adjacent downspout and the surrounding land and to the effects of freezing and thawing, as reported by Khudaverdian.  Moreover, this is exactly what the inspector predicted would happen (exhibit P‑4, report summary). [ 50 ] In conclusion, plaintiffs did not convince the Court that the defects in question jeopardized the structural soundness of the cottage, and that the present recourse was warranted, under the circumstances. [ 51 ] Finally, as there is no evidence that defendant wilfully withheld information from plaintiffs regarding the fissures in the foundation, with an express view to mislead them and incite them to buy, the action cannot be maintained. WHEREFORE THE COURT HEREBY: DISMISSES plaintiffs' action, with costs, including expert's costs, which the Court limits to $1,500.00 (article 477 C.C.P.). __________________________________ ELIANA MARENGO, J.C.Q. Henry Altschuler Lazare & Altschuler Attorneys for plaintiffs Marie-Pierre Charland Séguin & Associés Avocats Inc. Attorneys for defendant Dates of hearing: September 14, 15 & 16, 2011 [1] Montréal, Wilson & Lafleur ltée, 2005. [2] Rouillard vs St-Martin , 2009 QCCA 221 , par. 7 . [3] ABB inc. vs Domtar inc ., 2005 QCCA 733 . [4] P.G. JOBIN, La vente , 3e edition, Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2007, p. 200 ff. [5] Provost vs Roy, 2009 QCCQ 13444 , pars. 27 and 29; Bédard vs Couture-Ferland, 2011 QCCQ 4667 . [6] Lahaie c. Lapperrière , 2009 QCCA 1285 , par. 99 . [7] Supra , footnote 1, page 140. [8] AZ-95011574, J.E. 95-1144 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2011qccq15139-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.021836180239915848, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2011qccq15139-en\", \"title\": \"Zawidzki c. Swinghammer, 2011 QCCQ 15139 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2011qccq15139-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.021836180239915848, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2011qccq15139-en\"}, \"token_count\": 6446, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2011qccq15139-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs2874\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs2874-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Melatti c. Cvesper, 2022 QCCS 2874 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2022 QCCS 2874 (CanLII) | Melatti c. Cvesper | CanLII Date: 2022-07-28 File number: 00-17-106500-195 Citation: Melatti c. Cvesper, 2022 QCCS 2874 (CanLII) Melatti c. Cvesper 2022 QCCS 2874 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MontrEal No.: 500-17-106500-195 DATE: July 28, 2022 ( RECTIFIED August 4, 2022 ) _____________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE MARIE-ANNE PAQUETTE, Chief Justice _____________________________________________________________________ DANY MELATTI Plaintiff v. EDUARD CVESPER ANNA GRIGGLE CVESPER Defendants _____________________________________________________________________ RECTIFIED JUDGMENT _____________________________________________________________________ OVERVIEW ................................................................................................................................... 2 FACTUAL BACKGROUND ......................................................................................................... 2 LEGAL PRINCIPLES ................................................................................................................... 3 ANALYSIS ..................................................................................................................................... 4 1. Latent defect unknown to a diligent purchaser ......................................................... 4 2. Sufficiently serious Defect ............................................................................................ 6 3. Defect existING at the time of the sale ...................................................................... 7 4. Notice sent within a reasonable delay ....................................................................... 7 5. Damages ......................................................................................................................... 8 FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: .................................................................................. 9 ______________________________________________________________________ OVERVIEW [ 1 ] The Plaintiff Dany Melatti ( Buyer ) found an old underground oil tank and contaminated soil on the property he bought ( Property ) in 2015 from 6045111 Canada Inc. ( Canada Inc . or Vendor ) to build his own residence.  He is suing the former owners of the Property, Defendants Mr. Eduard Cvesper and his wife, Mrs. Anna Griggle Cvesper ( Defendants ), for what he claims to be a latent defect.  The Defendants oppose that they were unaware of alleged defects, that Mr. Melatti would have found the defect if he had done a proper pre-sale inspection, that they did not sell the Property directly to Mr. Melatti (they sold it to Canada Inc., which afterwards sold it to Mr. Melatti) and that the excavation and decontamination work was completed before they were called upon to witness the problem. [ 2 ] For the reasons below, the Court holds that the soil contamination and the presence of the underground oil tank are latent defects, as they existed at the time of the sale, were not visible for a reasonably prudent buyer and are serious.  The Court also finds that an earlier notice to the Defendants would not have made any difference in their management or in their assessment of the situation. [ 3 ] The Court also concludes that the Defendants were unaware of the problem.  Finally, as the warranty against latent defects is attached to the property sold and as the default existed at the time the Defendants sold the property to Canada Inc. (which in turn sold the property to Mr. Melatti), the Defendants remain liable for the price reduction related to the defect which Mr. Melatti found. [ 4 ] Therefore, the Defendants will be ordered to pay for the costs of the decontamination and extra excavation work due to the presence of the underground tank and of the contaminated soil.  All costs, fees and expenses incurred to remove the tank and decontaminate the Property amount to $195,209.18, which the Defendants will be condemned to pay.  However, the $15,000.00 claim for the legal fees, troubles, stress and inconveniences will be dismissed, as the Defendants had no knowledge of the defect and are therefore not liable for damages other than the reduction in the sales price or the cost to remedy the defects found. FACTUAL BACKGROUND [ 5 ] Mr. Melatti bought the Property from Canada Inc. on January 19, 2015, with the intent to demolish the two abandoned buildings standing on it and build his primary residence.  The two abandoned buildings were a small family home and a sevenplex heated with an oil furnace and an above ground storage tank. [ 6 ] Canada Inc. had acquired the Property from the Defendant Eduard Cvesper in 2012, who had acquired it from his wife, the Defendant Anna Griggle Cvesper, in 2003.  Mrs. Cvesper owned the Property since 1980. [ 7 ] Therefore, prior to selling it to Canada Inc. in 2012, the Defendants have owned and occupied the Property for over thirty years. [ 8 ] After obtaining all the relevant demolition and construction authorizations, Mr. Melatti began excavation work on the Property in June 2017.  While excavating, he discovered a buried damaged oil tank. [ 9 ] An expert evaluation revealed that the soil was contaminated with oil and required decontamination before construction work could resume.  Mr. Melatti had the tank removed and had the soil decontaminated to avoid any further delays in the construction work which was underway. [ 10 ] Mr. Melatti informed Canada Inc. of the situation on June 29, 2017.  On July 17, 2017, the real estate agent notified the Defendants, who visited the site three days later. [ 11 ] In addition, the Defendants received formal written notice of latent defects from Canada Inc. (August 18, 2017) and from Mr. Melatti (April 25, 2018). LEGAL PRINCIPLES [ 12 ] The Court needs to establish if the underground oil tank and resulting soil contamination are covered by the Civil Code of Quebec ’s legal warranty against latent defects: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. […] 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but also to make reparation for the injury suffered by the buyer. […] 1739. A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective shall give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. Where the defect appears gradually, the time begins to run on the day that the buyer could suspect the seriousness and extent of the defect. The seller may not invoke the tardiness of a notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it. [ 13 ] The legal warranty against latent defects applies if the defect was (1) latent, unknown to a reasonable and diligent purchaser, (2) sufficiently serious, and (3) existed at the time of the sale. [1] The purchaser also needs to notify the seller within a reasonable time after discovering the defect (1739 C.c.Q. ). [ 14 ] Each of these criteria will be analyzed in turn. ANALYSIS 1. Latent defect unknown to a diligent purchaser [ 15 ] Authors Jobin and Cumyn provide the following summary of the applicable law with respect to the latent character of a defect: 173 (…) , le Code édicte que la défectuosité ne doit pas avoir été apparente lors de la vente, en un mot que le vice est caché (telles des fourmis dans la charpente d'une maison). S'il ne l'est pas, la loi présume que l'acheteur le connaissait et il n'y a pas de garantie, sauf stipulation contraire. L'article 1726 précise qu'il faut entendre par un vice apparent celui « qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert ». (…) L'article 1726, alinéa 2 du Code dit bien : « [...] acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert. » Désormais, ce n'est pas le recours, ou non, à une inspection préachat par un spécialiste du domaine qui détermine le caractère caché du vice, mais le comportement prudent et diligent de l'acheteur agissant par lui-même. (…) Aujourd'hui, selon nous, l'obligation de recourir à un spécialiste ne peut se justifier que de façon exceptionnelle lorsque l'examen initial fait par l'acheteur révèle un indice sérieux de vice potentiel que seul un spécialiste peut identifier, ou encore quand le vendeur révèle certains faits préoccupants; alors, et alors seulement, d'après nous, l'acheteur profane ne peut pas effectuer seul « l'examen prudent et diligent » prescrit par l'article 1726 et l'intervention du spécialiste s'impose.(…). [2] [ 16 ] A purchaser is thus not systemically required to call upon an expert to investigate the property he seeks to purchase .  There has to be signs for it to be necessary. [ 17 ] Furthermore, numerous authorities confirm that soil contamination, by its very nature, is often considered a latent defect. [3] [ 18 ] In the case at hand, Mr. Melatti purchased the Property in 2015, at which point the buildings had been abandoned and were boarded up by the Vendor. [ 19 ] Mr. Frank Carnuccio , who has been a real estate agent for over 35 years, assisted the Defendants in selling the Property to Canada Inc. in 2012 and was also involved in the sale between Canada Inc. and Mr. Melatti in 2015. [ 20 ] Mr. Carnuccio confirmed the presence of the aboveground storage tank next to one of the two buildings.  However, he never saw or perceived any sign of an underground tank or soil contamination.  Thus, he never considered further investigations to exclude such potential defect prior to both sales. [ 21 ] In the spring of 2016, Mr. Joey Baldacchino, Director of the Mr. Melatti’s construction company, visited the Property with Mr. Melatti.  Both buildings were abandoned and run down, and needed to be demolished.  He noticed an above ground tank close to one of the buildings, but saw no signs that could have indicated the presence of an additional underground tank or of soil contamination. [ 22 ] In June 2017, when Mr. Melatti began excavation work on the Property, certain areas quickly appeared to be contaminated. [ 23 ] Giuseppe Ciconte, from Merineau Construction ( Merineau ), performed the excavation and decontamination work.  He first witnessed the contamination while excavating. The contaminated soil was clearly distinguishable by its color and smell. [ 24 ] The potential source of the contamination was found on June 26, 2017, when Merineau discovered a damaged underground oil tank.  The tank was quickly dug out and put on the side of the Property. [ 25 ] In the case at hand, the Court is satisfied that the contamination was hidden and could not have been perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer at the time of the purchase of the Property.  Nothing prior to the sale suggested the presence of an underground oil tank or of soil contamination.  To discover it would have required expert assistance and investigations which Mr. Melatti was not required to do. [ 26 ] While the aboveground tank indicated that the Property was one of the buildings was heated with oil, Mr. Melatti was not or could not have been aware that an underground storage tank was also embedded in the soil.  There was no indication of any kind which might have suggested the presence of this tank, and even less so of any potential soil contamination. [ 27 ] As such, a reasonable, prudent and diligent purchaser could not have known and would not have been inclined to further investigate or require and expert investigation prior to the sale. [ 28 ] The Court disagrees with the Defendants’ submission that the Buyer should have proceeded with an expert evaluation to exclude soil contamination at the time of purchase. [ 29 ] Admittedly, the buildings were abandoned since 2012.  However, this led to structural issues which were not suggestive of any potential soil contamination.  Understandably also, these structural issues were of little to no relevance to Mr. Melatti, as he was planning to demolish the two buildings.  Finally, the sole presence of an aboveground tank was not suggestive of potential soil contamination. The aboveground tank showed no sign of leakage or of visible contamination in the surrounding area.  The Defendants’ submission that the presence of this aboveground oil tank should have led to soil contamination pre-purchase investigations is unrealistic and unjustified. 2. Sufficiently serious Defect [ 30 ] The sole presence of a contaminant does not in and of itself trigger the application of the legal warranty. [4] There has to be a certain degree of seriousness to it. [5] [ 31 ] In the case at hand, the Buyer asked Mr. Baldacchino to revisit the Property in late June 2017 after he had discovered and dug out the tank.  Mr. Baldacchino attested that it was perforated and heavily damaged by corrosion.  He also witnessed the extent of the contamination which he claims went deep into the soil. [ 32 ] Mr. Baldacchino then acted as a consultant during the decontamination process. [ 33 ] After the soil was inspected and evaluated, Merineau removed the contaminated soil from the Property. [ 34 ] Vincent Beaudin, from Le Groupe Solroc, testified as an expert in soil decontamination.  The strong odor and soil coloration he witnessed in late June and early July 2017 was typical of oil contamination.  To establish the levels of contamination, he performed two analyses from samples taken on June 27 and July 5, 2017. [ 35 ] His report and testimony confirm the presence of soil contaminants and the need for decontamination. [6] [ 36 ] The level of contamination found was not compatible with a residential use of the Property. [7] This finding shows that the defect was sufficiently serious.  It was necessary to quickly proceed with the decontamination in order to mitigate any further delays and additional costs to the construction of Mr. Melatti’s residence. [ 37 ] Therefore, the level of contamination found and its impact on the Buyers’ project qualify as serious. 3. Defect existING at the time of the sale [ 38 ] The Court holds that the soil was contaminated well before the sale to Canada Inc. and the sale to Mr. Melatti. [ 39 ] The underground tank and soil contamination were discovered in June 2017.  However, the evidence establishes that the tank was buried for decades and nothing suggests that the presence of the underground oil tank and the soil contamination could result from events which would have occurred after the Defendants sold the Property. 4. Notice sent within a reasonable delay [ 40 ] On June 26, 2017, the Buyer discovered the damaged underground oil tank and significant soil contamination. [ 41 ] On June 29, 2017, Mr. Melatti met with the Canada Inc.’s representative and gave him notice of latent defects. [ 42 ] Defendants (Mrs. Griggle) testified that on July 17, 2017, they heard for the first time about contamination found while excavating on the Property.  They were informed accordingly by Mr. Carnuccio , real estate agent, and were asked for a financial contribution, which they categorically refused to make. [ 43 ] On July 20, 2017, Mr. Melatti, Mr. Carnuccio (real estate agent), the representative of Canada Inc. and the Defendants visited the Property.  This visit lasted approximately 20 to 30 minutes.  At that time, the excavation was completed and the oil tank was excavated. [ 44 ] On the same date, Mr. Melatti sent a written notice of latent defects to Canada Inc., advising that it should inspect the Property and take appropriate actions to eradicate the contamination. [8] [ 45 ] On or around August 18, 2017, Canada Inc. sent a written notice of latent defects to the Defendants, reiterating the existence of contamination and informing them that the situation required their immediate attention. [9] [ 46 ] Mr. Melatti afterwards invited the Defendants to revisit the Property and to conduct the verifications and investigations they deemed necessary.  The Defendants refused. They also did not conduct their own verifications and investigations at any time thereafter. On April 25, 2018, Mr. Melatti sent a formal written notice to the Defendants. [10] [ 47 ] While it is true that the Buyer removed the underground tank and proceeded quickly with the decontamination of the Property, the Court notes that the Defendants were informed of the situation and visited the Property within a few weeks of the Buyer’s discovery. [ 48 ] If there was any delay in the denunciation, there was clearly no prejudice to the Defendants as they showed then, and now, no interest or belief that they needed to investigate or intervene on this matter.  Defendants maintained from the beginning that they have no responsibility whatsoever with regards to the underground tank and soil contamination.  They cannot fault the Buyer for having allegedly notified them too late or proceeding too quickly with the decontamination. [ 49 ] The Court notes that by doing so, Mr. Melatti avoided delaying the construction of his residence, and potentially incur additional delays and costs as a result. [11] [ 50 ] The Court of Appeal has already stated that a default in notification, if it exists, must lead to a real prejudice to the seller. [12] There is no such prejudice here. 5. Damages [...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n.  They cannot fault the Buyer for having allegedly notified them too late or proceeding too quickly with the decontamination. [ 49 ] The Court notes that by doing so, Mr. Melatti avoided delaying the construction of his residence, and potentially incur additional delays and costs as a result. [11] [ 50 ] The Court of Appeal has already stated that a default in notification, if it exists, must lead to a real prejudice to the seller. [12] There is no such prejudice here. 5. Damages [ 51 ] Mr. Melatti claims a reduction of the purchase price corresponding to all costs, fees and expenses incurred to remove the tank and decontaminate the Property.  He claims no additional damages for costs associated with delays in construction or «frais de chantier».  However, he seeks $15,000.00 in damages for the legal fees, troubles, stress and inconveniences related to the latent defect found on the Property. [ 52 ] The Court must consider all the appropriate circumstances (art. 1604, al.3 C.C.Q.) to determine the reasonable [13] price that would have been paid had the Buyer known of the latent defects (art. 1726 C.C.Q.). [14] Here, it is reasonable to assume that the reduction in the purchase price is in the same magnitude as the decontamination and additional excavation costs incurred. [ 53 ] The bills submitted by the Buyer, mostly from Merineau and Le Groupe Solroc [15] who performed the excavation and decontamination, support the allegation that the excavation work, decontamination work and costs incurred as a result of the contamination amount to $195,209.18, broken down as follows: • Soil testing (Solroc)                     $28,766.75 • Excavation (Merineau                 $17, 246.25 • Decontamination (Merineau)     $149,196.18 The Court considers this amount appropriate and will grant the Plaintiffs’ claim accordingly.  The evidence shows that these costs were incurred to cure the contamination found and were reasonable.  The Defendants offered no serious evidence to the opposite. [ 54 ] However, Mr. Melattis’ claims of $15,000.00 for the legal fees, trouble, stress and inconvenience suffered will be dismissed.  Admittedly, the Defendants were uncooperative, notably by refusing (1) to acknowledge the existence of contamination and their potential liability, (2) to discuss the possibility of a settlement, and (3) for refusing to make numerous admissions for the production of documents. [ 55 ] Still, this does not justify a claim for damages, in addition to the reduction in the sales price or the costs incurred to cure the defect.  Knowledge of the defect is required to support the award of damages.  In the case at hand, the fact that they resided on the Property for over thirty years does not establish that they knew or should have known about the contamination or about the presence of the underground tank.  There is no evidence to support the existence of such knowledge by the Defendants. [ 56 ] As per the conclusions sought, interest at the legal rate and additional indemnity will accrue from the date of service (February 5, 2019). FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [ 57 ] GRANTS IN PART the claim; [ 58 ] CONDEMNS the Defendants to pay in solidum to the Plaintiff the sum of $195,209.18 plus legal interest and additional indemnity from February 5, 2019; [ 59 ] THE WHOLE WITH LEGAL COSTS . __________________________________ MARIE-ANNE PAQUETTE C hief Justice Me Anas Qiabi Gattuso Bouchard Mazzone Attorneys for the Plaintiff Me Jean Trottier Attorney for the Defendants Hearing dates: May 18, 19, 20, 2022 Additional exhibits filed by Plaintiff (May 25, 2022) and written additional submissions by Plaintiff (May 25 and 26, 2022) and Defendants (May 26, 2022) [1] ABB inc. v. Domtar inc. , 2007 CSC 50 , par. 47-55 ; Lahaie c. Laperrière , 2009 QCCA 1285 , par. 33-34 . [2] Pierre-Gabriel JOBIN, avec la collaboration de Michelle CUMYN, La vente , 3 e éd., Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2007, n o 159, p. 204 . [3] Argayova c. Fernandez , 2012 QCCA 1243 , par. 3-4 ; Varàd i c. Stella Paquette inc. , 2018 QCCS 4904 ; Laquerre c. Joseph , 2016 QCCS 5 . [4] 125385 Canada inc. c. Groupe Collège LaSalle inc ., 2006 QCCA 522 , par. 2 . [5] Varàdi c. Stella Paquette inc. , 2018 QCCS 4904 , par. 39-43 . [6] Exhibit P-8. [7] Exhibit P-8. [8] Exhibit P-4. [9] Exhibit P-5. [10] Exhibit P-5. [11] Placements Beauvais-chabot inc . c. Fogel , 2014 QCCA 548 , par.11-13. [12] Claude Joyal inc. c. CNH Canada Ltd , 2014 QCCA 588 , par. 35-37 ; voir aussi Laquerre c. Joseph , 2016 QCCS 5 , par. 87-93 et De La O c. Sasson , 2015 QCCS 713 , par. 38 . [13] Société en commandite de l’Avenir c. Familia St-Jérôme inc ., 2019 QCCA 1155 , par.24. [14] Lahaie v. Lapierre , 2009 QCCA 1285 , par. 95 . [15] Exhibits P-7, P-7A, P-7B.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs2874-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02341269887983799, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs2874-en\", \"title\": \"Melatti c. Cvesper, 2022 QCCS 2874 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs2874-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02341269887983799, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs2874-en\"}, \"token_count\": 5675, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs2874-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Pace c. Lintzeris, 2024 QCCQ 3813 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2024 QCCQ 3813 (CanLII) | Pace c. Lintzeris | CanLII Date: 2024-08-07 File number: 500-32-717183-224 Citation: Pace c. Lintzeris, 2024 QCCQ 3813 (CanLII) [NDLE : La traduction française non officielle de cette décision se trouve à la suite de la version originale.] Pace c. Lintzeris 2024 QCCQ 3813 COURT OF QUÉBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF MONTRÉAL “Civil Division” No: 500-32-717183-224 DATE: August 7, 2024 ______________________________________________________________________ PRESIDED BY THE HONOURABLE MÉLANIE DUGRÉ, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ FERNANDO PACE and ALEXANDRA NUNEZ-VILLOTA Plaintiffs v. SOPHIA LINTZERIS Defendant __________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Fernando Pace and Alexandra Nunez-Villota (the Buyers ) allege the existence of a latent defect in the property they bought from Sophia Lintzeris ( the Seller ) in June 2021. [ 2 ] The latent defect relates more specifically to the in-ground pool, which has been affected by a leak. The Buyers are claiming the cost of repairs. [1] [ 3 ] The Seller contends that the pool is excluded from the warranty of quality and that given its age (30 to 40 years), the Buyers could not expect a brand new pool. ISSUES [4] a)  Was the pool excluded from the inspection mandate? b) Is the pool excluded from the warranty of quality? c) Are the problems affecting the pool a latent defect under the warranty of quality? d) If so, what is the amount of damages to which the Buyers are entitled? [5] For the reasons explained below, the Court finds that the pool was excluded from the inspection mandate. However, the pool is covered by the warranty of quality and the problems affecting the pool meet the criteria of a latent defect. [6] In civil matters, the burden of proof is on Plaintiff’s shoulders, in accordance with the principle provided under article 2803 of the Civil Code of Québec ( C.C.Q. ), which states that “[a] person seeking to assert a right shall prove the facts on which his claim is based”. [7] The Court decides on the balance of probabilities, pursuant to article 2804 C.C.Q. , which states that “[evidence] is sufficient if it renders the existence of a fact more probable than its non-existence”. In other words, the Court must determine what is more probable and plausible, rather than improbable and implausible. [8] In addition, when the evidence adduced is not sufficiently convincing, or if the evidence is contradictory and it is impossible for the judge to identify the truth, the party upon whom the burden of proof lies will lose. [2] ANALYSIS a) Was the pool excluded from the inspection mandate? [9] Prior to the purchase of the house, the Buyers proceeded to an inspection in April 2021. [10] Obtaining an inspection report is not mandatory for the warranty of quality to apply. [11] On the other hand, a buyer who retains the services of an inspector still has a duty to examine the property based on the standard of the prudent and diligent buyer. [3] [12] The inspection report dated April 22, 2021 specifies under the Limitations and Conditions section that “[items] exterior [and/or] unattached to the building, such as […], swimming pools, […], are not covered in this inspection”. [4] [13] The Court finds that the swimming pool was not included in the mandate of the inspector. As a result, the analysis must be based exclusively on the criterion of the prudent and diligent buyer. b) Was the pool excluded from the warranty of quality ? [14] The Seller alleges that the warranty of quality does not apply to the pool. To support this argument, she relies on the Promise to Purchase form dated April 7, 2021, [5] which provides under section 11.5 that a few items, including washer/dryer, refrigerator, garage door opener and “[…] pool accessories” are “[…] sold without any legal warranty of quality, at the buyer’s own risk, but must be in working order at the time of delivery of the immovable”. [15] The inspection carried out on the pool in July 2021, after the Buyers noticed a water level problem, revealed the presence of a leak caused by a ruptured return pipe. [16] The Court is of the view that the return pipe is an integral part of the pool. It cannot be dissociated from the pool, nor can it be qualified as “accessory”. [17] Had the parties’ intent been to exclude the pool itself from the warranty, the provision to that effect should have been clearer. [18] With the current wording chosen by the parties, the Court concludes that the pool and its inherent components, such as the return pipe, are covered by the warranty of quality. c) Are the problems affecting the pool a latent defect under the warranty of quality? [19] The warranty of quality is owed by the seller to the buyer. [6] [20] Although the pool is covered by the warranty of quality, the reported problems must still meet the conditions of a latent defect to trigger the Seller’s liability. [21] To qualify as latent, a defect must meet the following criteria: [7] a) It must be serious, which means that it must interfere with a normal use of the good. The buyer must show that he would not have paid the same price, had he been aware of the defect. b) It must be unknown to the buyer at the time of the sale. c) It must be latent, which means that it cannot be identified by a prudent and diligent buyer, and it has not been reported by the seller. d) It must be anterior to the sale. [22] In addition, the buyer has the obligation to give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable delay of its discovery [8] , and to file his legal action within the three-year prescription delay. [9] [23] Evidence reveals the following facts. [24] The Buyers inspected the house in April. Although the snow had melted and the pool was partly visible, it was not in operation, and the water was dark. [25] The Buyers did not notice any obvious or visible issue with the pool. [26] Their inspector confirmed same. Even though the pool is excluded from the inspection mandate, the inspector did mention in his report that the “[…] pool appeared to be in good condition” and “[there] are no apparent cracks”. [10] [27] After they took possession of the house in June 2021, the Buyers noticed that there seemed to be a problem with the water level of the pool, which kept decreasing. [28] At that time, the Buyers were not living in the house due to renovation work, and they were planning to move in September. Yet, they came to the house every few days to supervise the work. [29] At the beginning of July, the water level was so low that the pump stopped working. [30] The Buyers questioned the Seller and were told that she had also encountered this issue, which was due to evaporation, according to her. [31] However, the inspection revealed the presence of a leak caused by the ruptured return pipe. [32] Some repairs were performed on July 22, 2021, for a total amount of $2,069.55. [11] [33] The Buyers reported the issue to the Seller, who agreed to pay $1,800 for the repair. [12] [34] The Seller acknowledges that no transaction was signed between the parties and that the Buyers, by accepting this payment, did not renounce to any future claims. [35] The Buyers noticed some improvements after the repair, but issues remained. They still had to add water to the pool on a regular basis, but every three or four days instead of daily. [36] In the fall of 2021, the Buyers obtained a full inspection report of the pool [13] and a new quote for further repair. [14] [37] Additional leaks were suspected in the skimmer and at the level of LED lights. [38] In January 2022, the Buyers sent the Seller a demand letter claiming an additional amount of $13,561.30. [15] [39] On January 24, 2022, the Seller answered the demand letter, in which she denied owing any further amount to the Buyers. [40] When assessing the criteria of a latent defect, the Court first notes that having to add water to the pool every few days to protect the pump is a serious issue, which interferes with the normal use of the pool. [41] Second, the evidence shows the Buyers were not aware of the problem at the time of the sale. [42] Third, the defect was latent, as no issue was noticeable either by the Buyers themselves or by their inspector in April 2021, when the pool was not in operation. There was no way for them to see or suspect the broken return pipe. [43] It was only when they took possession of the house, and after the pool had been started for the 2021 summer season, that the Buyers were able to notice that the water level was decreasing. [44] Finally, the defect was anterior to the sale, since the Seller admitted that she too had to add water to the pool from time to time. However, she believed this was due to evaporation and the fact that children were splashing water when playing in the pool. [45] The Seller also contends that, given the age of the pool (30 to 40 years), the Buyers could not expect it to be in perfect condition. [46] Caselaw does confirm that the warranty of quality does not extend to deterioration of a property caused by its wear and tear or obsolescence. The prudent and diligent buyer should, before purchasing older property and equipment, examine them carefully and anticipate certain repairs. [16] [47] The author Jeffrey Edwards also mentions: [17] Les détériorations dues à l’usure, au vieillissement ou à la vétusté ne constituent donc pas des vices, car, en raison de la révision à la baisse de l’usage attendu, elles n’occasionnent aucun déficit d’usage au sens de la garantie : l’usage protégé varie ainsi selon l’état de l’usure, du vieillissement et de la vétusté du bien au moment de la vente. [48] The Court finds that there is a distinction between expected wear and tear of a good and a defect affecting its normal functioning. [49] The photos contained in the inspection report show that the pool site and the pool itself are rather old and could benefit from improvement. [50] The Buyers are not complaining about this. [51] On the other hand, the Buyers could expect the pool to be functioning normally without having to continually add water to protect the pump. Normal wear and tear has nothing to do with the defect identified. [52] For all these reasons, the Court concludes that the Buyers have met their burden of proof to show that the conditions for a latent defect in the pool are met. d) If so, what is the amount of damages to which the Buyers are entitled? [53] The Buyers explained that they obtained a quote in December 2021 for $13,561.30. [18] [54] The Seller refused to assume the cost of additional repairs, and the Buyers decided to wait to carry out the work because they were planning to redo the concrete around the pool. [55] As a result, the situation remained the same during the summer of 2022, whereby the Buyers were regularly adding water to the pool to protect the pump. [56] The Buyers finally had the repairs done in June 2023. The cost was lower than expected and they paid $8,048.24. [19] [57] The Seller contends that the work carried out significantly improves the condition and value of the pool and goes beyond repairing the leak that caused the water level issues. [58] On this point, caselaw [20] establishes that plaintiffs cannot unduly enrich themselves to the detriment of their seller by purchasing a new good when the one purchased was heavily used or advanced in age. [59] The Court however disagrees with the Seller’s argument. [60] The invoice details the work carried out: replacement of the skimmer, replacement of LED lights, filling up of light niche and removal of cable anchors. [61] Most of these repairs addressed issues identified in October 2021 as being potential sources of the leak. [62] The Court believes that the work performed was meant to repair the defect on the pool which existed at the time of the sale and could not be identified or suspected by the Buyers. [63] The Court therefore concludes that the Buyers are entitled to the amount paid for the repairs. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: GRANTS Fernando Pace and Alexandra Nunez-Villota’s application; CONDEMNS Sophia Lintzeris to pay to Fernando Pace and Alexandra Nunez-Villota $8,048.25, plus interests at the legal rate and the additional indemnity under article 1619 C.C.Q. since the demand letter of January 13, 2022; CONDEMNS Sophia Lintzeris to pay to Fernando Pace and Alexandra Nunez-Villota the legal costs of $217 for the application. __________________________________ MÉLANIE DUGRÉ, J.C.Q. Hearing date: July 8, 2024 TRADUCTION NON OFFICIELLE RÉALISÉE PAR SOQUIJ, NON RÉVISÉE PAR LE JUGE Pace c. Lintzeris 2024 QCCQ 3813 COUR DU QUÉBEC Division des petites créances CANADA PROVINCE DE QUÉBEC DISTRICT DE MONTRÉAL « Chambre civile » N o : 500-32-717183-224 DATE : 7 août 2024 ______________________________________________________________________ SOUS LA PRÉSIDENCE DE L’HONORABLE MÉLANIE DUGRÉ, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ FERNANDO PACE et ALEXANDRA NUNEZ-VILLOTA Demandeurs c. SOPHIA LINTZERIS Défenderesse __________________________________________________________________ JUGEMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [1] Fernando Pace et Alexandra Nunez-Villota (les acheteurs) allèguent l’existence d’un vice caché dans la propriété qu’ils ont achetée de Sophia Lintzeris (la venderesse) en juin 2021. [2] Le vice caché concerne plus précisément la piscine creusée, qui a un problème de fuite. Les acheteurs réclament le coût des réparations [21] . [3] La venderesse prétend que la piscine est exclue de la garantie de qualité et que, compte tenu de l’âge de la piscine (de 30 à 40 ans), les acheteurs ne pouvaient s’attendre à ce que celle-ci soit à l’état neuf. QUESTIONS EN LITIGE [4] a) La piscine était-elle exclue du mandat d’inspection ? e) La piscine est-elle exclue de la garantie de qualité ? f) Les problèmes affectant la piscine constituent-ils un vice caché aux termes de la garantie de qualité ? g) Le cas échéant, à quel montant les acheteurs ont-ils droit à titre de dommages-intérêts ? [5] Pour les motifs qui suivent, la Cour conclut que la piscine était exclue du mandat d’inspection. Cependant, la piscine est couverte par la garantie de qualité et les problèmes affectant la piscine remplissent les critères d’un vice caché. [6] En matière civile, le fardeau de la preuve repose sur les épaules du demandeur, conformément au principe énoncé à l’ article 2803 du Code civil du Québe c (C.C.Q.), qui prévoit que « celui qui veut faire valoir un droit doit prouver les faits qui soutiennent sa prétention. » [7] La Cour décide selon la prépondérance des probabilités en vertu de l’article 2804 C.C.Q., qui prévoit que « la preuve qui rend l’existence d’un fait plus probable que son inexistence est suffisante » . Autrement dit, la Cour doit déterminer ce qui est le plus probable et vraisemblable, plutôt que ce qui est improbable et invraisemblable. [8] De plus, lorsque la preuve présentée n’est pas suffisamment convaincante, ou que les éléments de preuve sont contradictoires et ne permettent pas au juge de déterminer la vérité, la partie à qui incombe le fardeau de la preuve succombera [22] . ANALYSE e) La piscine était-elle exclue du mandat d’inspection ? [9] Avant l’achat de la maison, les acheteurs ont procédé à une inspection en avril 2021. [10] Pour que la garantie de qualité s’applique, il n’est pas nécessaire d’obtenir un rapport d’inspection. [11] Par ailleurs, un acheteur qui retient les services d’un inspecteur a tout de même l’obligation de procéder à l’examen de la propriété selon la norme de l’acheteur prudent et diligent [23] . [12] Le rapport d’inspection daté du 22 avril 2021 précise, à...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n1. [10] Pour que la garantie de qualité s’applique, il n’est pas nécessaire d’obtenir un rapport d’inspection. [11] Par ailleurs, un acheteur qui retient les services d’un inspecteur a tout de même l’obligation de procéder à l’examen de la propriété selon la norme de l’acheteur prudent et diligent [23] . [12] Le rapport d’inspection daté du 22 avril 2021 précise, à la section Limites et conditions , que [ traduction ] « [les éléments] extérieurs [et/ou] non fixés au bâtiment, comme les […], piscines […], ne sont pas couverts dans cette inspection » [24] . [13] La Cour conclut que la piscine n’était pas incluse dans le mandat de l’inspecteur. L’analyse doit donc être fondée exclusivement sur le critère de l’acheteur prudent et diligent. f) La piscine était-elle exclue de la garantie de qualité ? [14] La venderesse allègue que la garantie de qualité ne s’applique pas à la piscine. Au soutien de cette prétention, elle s’appuie sur la promesse d’achat datée du 7 avril 2021 [25] , laquelle prévoit à son article 11.5 que certains éléments, y compris la machine à laver, la sécheuse, le réfrigérateur, l’ouvre-porte de garage et […] les accessoires de piscine sont [ traduction ] « […] vendus sans aucune garantie légale de qualité, aux risques et périls de l’acheteur, mais ils doivent être en bon état de marche au moment de la prise de possession de l’immeuble ». [15] L’inspection qui a été faite de la piscine en juillet 2021, après que les acheteurs ont remarqué un problème avec le niveau de l’eau, a révélé la présence d’une fuite causée par le bris d’un tuyau de retour. [16] La Cour est d’avis que le tuyau de retour fait partie intégrante de la piscine. Il ne peut être dissocié de la piscine ni être qualifié d’accessoire . [17] Si les parties avaient l’intention d’exclure la piscine de la garantie de qualité, la disposition à cet effet aurait dû être plus claire. [18] Compte tenu de la formulation que les parties ont choisie, la Cour conclut que la piscine et ses composantes inhérentes, comme le tuyau de retour, sont couvertes par la garantie de qualité. g) Les problèmes affectant la piscine constituent-ils un vice caché aux termes de la garantie de qualité ? [19] Le vendeur est tenu à la garantie de qualité envers l’acheteur [26] . [20] Même si la piscine est couverte par la garantie de qualité, la venderesse ne sera tenue responsable que si les problèmes signalés remplissent les conditions pour constituer un vice caché. [21] Pour être caché, un vice doit répondre aux critères suivants [27] : e) Il doit être grave, ce qui signifie qu’il doit empêcher l’usage normal du bien. L’acheteur doit montrer qu’il n’aurait pas payé le même prix s’il avait été au courant du vice . f) Il doit avoir été inconnu de l’acheteur au moment de la vente . g) Il doit être caché, ce qui signifie qu’il ne pouvait être décelé par un acheteur prudent et diligent et qu’il n’a pas été signalé par le vendeur. h) Il doit avoir existé au moment de la vente . [22] De plus, l’acheteur doit, par écrit, dénoncer le vice au vendeur dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte [28] et intenter son action dans un délai de trois ans [29] . [23] La preuve révèle les faits suivants. [24] Les acheteurs ont inspecté la maison en avril. Même si la neige avait fondu, la piscine n’était qu’en partie visible, elle n’était pas en marche et l’eau était sombre . [25] Les acheteurs n’ont remarqué aucun problème évident ou visible relativement à la piscine. [26] Cela a été confirmé par l’inspecteur. Même si la piscine était exclue du mandat d’inspection, l’inspecteur a mentionné que [ traduction ] « […] la piscine semble en bon état » et [ traduction ] « [qu’il] n’y a aucune fissure apparente » [30] . [27] Après avoir pris possession de la maison en juin 2021, les acheteurs ont remarqué qu’il semblait y avoir un problème avec le niveau de l’eau, qui baissait constamment dans la piscine. [28] À l’époque, ils n’habitaient pas dans la maison en raison des travaux de rénovation et ils prévoyaient d’y emménager en septembre. Ils venaient tout de même à la maison tous les quelques jours afin de superviser les travaux. [29] Au début de juillet, le niveau de l’eau était si bas que la pompe a cessé de fonctionner. [30] Les acheteurs ont questionné la venderesse, qui leur a dit qu’elle avait également fait face à ce problème causé, selon elle, par l’évaporation. [31] Cependant, une inspection a révélé la présence d’une fuite causée par le bris du tuyau de retour. [32] Une réparation, dont le coût s’est élevé à 2 069,55 $, a été effectuée le 22 juillet 2021 [31] . [33] Les acheteurs ont signalé le problème à la venderesse, qui a accepté de payer 1 800 $ pour la réparation [32] . [34] La venderesse reconnaît que les parties n’ont signé aucune transaction et que les acheteurs n’ont pas renoncé à toute autre réclamation future en acceptant ce paiement. [35] Les acheteurs ont remarqué une amélioration après la réparation, mais le problème a persisté. Ils devaient encore ajouter de l’eau régulièrement, tous les trois ou quatre jours plutôt que tous les jours . [36] À l’automne 2021, les acheteurs ont obtenu un rapport d’inspection complète de la piscine [33] et une nouvelle évaluation pour une autre réparation [34] . [37] On soupçonnait la présence d’autres fuites dans l’écumoire et au niveau des lumières DEL. [38] En janvier 2022, les acheteurs ont envoyé à la venderesse une mise en demeure lui réclamant une somme additionnelle de 13 561,30 $ [35] . [39] Le 24 janvier 2022, la venderesse a répondu à la mise en demeure en niant devoir une somme additionnelle aux acheteurs. [40] En évaluant les critères de vice caché, la Cour constate premièrement que le fait d’avoir à ajouter de l’eau dans une piscine tous les jours afin de protéger la pompe constitue un grave problème, qui nuit à l’usage normal de la piscine. [41] Deuxièmement, la preuve montre que les acheteurs n’étaient pas au courant du problème au moment de la vente. [42] Troisièmement, le vice était caché puisqu’aucun problème ne pouvait être décelé par les acheteurs ou leur inspecteur en avril 2021. La pompe n’étant pas en marche, il leur était impossible de constater ou de soupçonner le bris du tuyau de retour. [43] Ce n’est que lorsqu’ils ont pris possession de la maison, et après la mise en marche de la piscine pour l’été 2021, que les acheteurs ont pu remarquer que le niveau de l’eau baissait. [44] Enfin, le vice existait au moment de la vente, la venderesse ayant admis qu’elle aussi devait ajouter de l’eau dans la piscine de temps à autre. Cependant, elle croyait que cela était dû à l’évaporation et au fait que les enfants faisaient des éclaboussures en jouant dans la piscine. [45] La venderesse prétend également que, compte tenu de l’âge de la piscine (de 30 à 40 ans), les acheteurs ne pouvaient s’attendre à ce que celle-ci soit en parfait état. [46] La jurisprudence confirme que la garantie de qualité ne s’étend pas à la détérioration de la propriété causée par son usure ou sa vétusté. Avant d’acheter une propriété ou de l’équipement ayant un certain âge, l’acheteur prudent et diligent devrait les examiner soigneusement et prévoir certaines réparations [36] . [47] L’auteur Jeffrey Edwards [37] mentionne également que : Les détériorations dues à l’usure, au vieillissement ou à la vétusté ne constituent donc pas des vices, car, en raison de la révision à la baisse de l’usage attendu, elles n’occasionnent aucun déficit d’usage au sens de la garantie : l’usage protégé varie ainsi selon l’état de l’usure, du vieillissement et de la vétusté du bien au moment de la vente. [48] La Cour conclut qu’une distinction doit être faite entre l’usure prévisible d’un bien et un vice affectant son fonctionnement normal. [49] Les photos contenues dans le rapport d’inspection montrent que l’aménagement de la piscine et la piscine elle-même ont un certain âge et pourraient être améliorés. [50] Les acheteurs ne s’en plaignent pas. [51] Par ailleurs, les acheteurs pouvaient s’attendre à ce que la piscine fonctionne normalement, sans qu’il soit nécessaire d’y ajouter de l’eau constamment afin de protéger la pompe. L’usure normale n’a rien à voir avec le vice décelé . [52] Pour tous ces motifs, la Cour conclut que les acheteurs se sont déchargés de leur fardeau de prouver que les conditions pour conclure à un vice caché de la piscine sont remplies. h) Le cas échéant, à quel montant les acheteurs ont-ils droit à titre de dommages-intérêts ? [53] Les acheteurs ont expliqué qu’ils avaient obtenu, en décembre 2021, une évaluation s’élevant à 13 561,30 $ [38] . [54] La venderesse refuse d’assumer le coût de réparations supplémentaires et les acheteurs ont décidé d’attendre avant de faire les travaux parce qu’ils prévoient refaire le béton autour de la piscine. [55] Par conséquent, la situation était la même pendant l’été 2022, les acheteurs devant ajouter régulièrement de l’eau à la piscine afin de protéger la pompe. [56] Les acheteurs ont finalement fait faire des réparations en juin 2023. Le coût était moins élevé que prévu et le prix payé était de 8 048,24 $ [39] . [57] La venderesse prétend que les travaux exécutés améliorent de manière appréciable l’état et la valeur de la piscine et ne se limitent pas à la réparation de la fuite qui causait les problèmes de niveau de l’eau. [58] À ce sujet, la jurisprudence [40] établit que les demandeurs qui ont acheté un bien qui était vieux ou très usé ne peuvent s’enrichir injustement au détriment de leur vendeur en achetant un bien neuf. [59] Cependant, la Cour ne retient pas la prétention de la venderesse. [60] La facture précise les travaux qui ont été exécutés, soit le remplacement de l’écumoire, le remplacement des lumières DEL, le colmatage d’une niche de lumière et l’enlèvement de câbles d’ancrage. [61] Pour la plupart, ces travaux concernent des problèmes décelés en octobre 2021 comme des causes possibles de la fuite. [62] La Cour estime que les travaux exécutés visaient à réparer le vice qui affectait la piscine au moment de la vente et qui ne pouvait être décelé ni soupçonné par les acheteurs . [63] Par conséquent, la Cour conclut que les acheteurs ont droit à la somme qu’ils ont versée pour les réparations. POUR CES MOTIFS, LA COUR : ACCUEILLE la demande de Fernando Pace et de Alexandra Nunez-Villota ; CONDAMNE Sophia Lintzeris à verser à Fernando Pace et Alexandra Nunez-Villota 8 048,25 $ avec intérêt au taux légal et l’indemnité additionnelle prévue à l’article 1619 C.C.Q., à compter de la mise en demeure du 13 janvier 2022 ; CONDAMNE Sophia Lintzeris à verser à Fernando Pace et Alexandra Nunez-Villota des frais de justice de 217 $ pour la demande. __________________________________ MÉLANIE DUGRÉ, J.C.Q. Date d’audience : 8 juillet 2024 [1] Exhibit P-12, invoice for $8,048.25. [2] Léo Ducharme, Précis de la preuve , 6th ed. (Montréal: Wilson & Lafleur, 2005) at para. 146 . [3] Lamontagne v. Chiasson , 2017 QCCS 4651 at para. 60 . [4] Exhibit P-1 at 76. [5] Exhibit D-2. [6] Art. 1726 C.C.Q. [7] Leroux v. Gravant , 2016 QCCA 79 at para. 40 . [8] Art. 1739 C.c.Q. [9] Art. 2925 C.c.Q. [10] Supra note 4 at 6. [11] Exhibit P-6, invoice for $1,800 plus tax. [12] Exhibit P-5. [13] Exhibit P-3. [14] Exhibit P-8. [15] Exhibit P-7. [16] Naud v. Normand , 2007 QCCA 1814 ; Dinu v. Tremblay , 2018 QCCQ 8914 ; Auchinteck v. Gerkes , 2022 QCCS 2637 ; Yuan v. Lancione , 2023 QCCQ 7835 at para. 21 . [17] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois, 2d ed. (Montréal: Wilson & Lafleur, 2005) at 155. [18] Supra note 14. [19] Supra note 4. [20] Artinian v. Wong , 2017 QCCQ 9806 at para. 63 ; Larente v. Lépine , 2019 QCCQ 336 at para. 33 . [21] Pièce P-12, facture de 8 048,25 $. [22] Léo Ducharme, Précis de la preuve , 6 e éd. (Montréal : Wilson & Lafleur, 2005) au par. 146 . [23] Lamontagne c. Chiasson , 2017 QCCS 4651 , au par. 60 . [24] Pièce P-1, p. 76. [25] Pièce D-2. [26] Art. 1726 C.C.Q. [27] Leroux c. Gravant , 2016 QCCA 79 au par. 40 . [28] Art. 1739 C.C.Q. [29] Art. 2925 C.C.Q. [30] Supra note 4, p. 6. [31] Pièce P-6, facture de 1 800 $ plus taxes. [32] Pièce P-5. [33] Pièce P-3. [34] Pièce P-8. [35] Pièce P-7. [36] Naud c. Normand , 2007 QCCA 1814 ; Dinu c. Trembl\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n[29] Art. 2925 C.C.Q. [30] Supra note 4, p. 6. [31] Pièce P-6, facture de 1 800 $ plus taxes. [32] Pièce P-5. [33] Pièce P-3. [34] Pièce P-8. [35] Pièce P-7. [36] Naud c. Normand , 2007 QCCA 1814 ; Dinu c. Tremblay , 2018 QCCQ 8914 ; Auchinteck c. Gerkes , 2022 QCCS 2637 ; Yuan c. Lancione , 2023 QCCQ 7835 au par. 21 . [37] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois, 2 e éd. (Montréal : Wilson & Lafleur, 2005), p. 155. [38] Supra note 14. [39] Supra note 4. [40] Artinian c. Wong , 2017 QCCQ 9806 au par. 63 ; Larente c. Lépine , 2019 QCCQ 336 au par. 33 .\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02402745932340622, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\", \"title\": \"Pace c. Lintzeris, 2024 QCCQ 3813 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02402745932340622, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\"}, \"token_count\": 7211, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2024qccq3813-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2013qccq11772\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2013qccq11772-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Xia c. Ali, 2013 QCCQ 11772 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2013 QCCQ 11772 (CanLII) | Xia c. Ali | CanLII Date: 2013-10-08 File number: 500-32-128220-110 Citation: Xia c. Ali, 2013 QCCQ 11772 (CanLII) Xia c. Ali 2013 QCCQ 11772 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-128220-110 DATE: October 8 th , 2013 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE GATIEN FOURNIER, Q.C.J. ______________________________________________________________________ YANG XIA LI YAN DUAN Plaintiffs v. YASWANTI ALI Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ INTRODUCTION [ 1 ] The Plaintiffs claim an amount of $5,800 from the Defendant. They allege that the immovable that they purchased from the Defendant was affected by latent defects. [ 2 ] The Defendant denies that the immovable sold was affected by latent defects, stating that the defects were, at the time of the purchase, apparent. ISSUE [ 3 ] Are the Plaintiffs entitled to claim an amount of $5,800 from the Defendant for latent defects? RELEVANT FACTS [ 4 ] The immovable (the \\\"Property\\\") of the Defendant was put up for sale during the summer of 2010. Christopher Gaudin (Gaudin) was the real estate broker who was mandated for the sale of the Property. [ 5 ] The Plaintiffs visited the Property with Gaudin on July 17, 2010. The visit of the Property lasted no more than 30 minutes. A promise to purchase resulted from that visit. [ 6 ] The parties agreed on the final terms of the sale on July 24, 2010 after the inspection of the Property. Defects were detected by the inspector mandated by the Plaintiffs and a reduction in the price of the Property of an amount of $2,500 was agreed upon by the Defendant. [ 7 ] The Plaintiffs allege that the reduction in the amount of $2,500 was to compensate for the water leakage in the washing room and to upgrade the electrical panel from 100 amperes to 200 amperes. [ 8 ] The Defendant contends that the reduction in the amount of $2,500 took into consideration the bulk of the problems raised by the inspector at the time of the inspection. [ 9 ] Gaudin would have been informed by the Plaintiffs that problems had been detected in the course of the inspection and that they were requiring a reduction of $2,500 in the selling price. However, Gaudin was not informed of the exact nature of the problems. [ 10 ] The Plaintiffs allege in their application that the Property is affected by the following 2 latent defects: the roof was leaking and water came out of the wall in the basement. [ 11 ] The leakage from the roof would have come from the electrical post. With respect to the water in the basement, it would have come from the front right hand side corner of the Property, precisely where the Plaintiffs' inspector suspected a problem [1] . [ 12 ] The Plaintiffs obtained 2 estimations. According to these estimations, the leaking in the basement would cost $2,750 plus taxes to be fixed and the replacement of the electrical post would cost $2,300 plus taxes. [ 13 ] Once the Defendant was informed of the alleged latent defects, she mandated her own expert to proceed with an inspection of the Property. That inspection was made during the month of May, 2011. ANALYSIS AND DECISION [ 14 ] Article 1726 of the Civil code of Québec [2] (C.c.Q.) applies. It reads like this: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. [ 15 ] Considering the evidence adduced, the Court concludes that the defects raised by the Plaintiffs were apparent defects as defined in article 1726 of the C.c.Q. Water leakage in the basement [ 16 ] The Plaintiff's own inspection report, prepared one month prior to the purchase, referred specifically to a water leakage risk as indicated in Photo 4. The Plaintiffs' inspector wrote that \\\"the moisture in the basement is not very good, it may indicate it has water leakage risk , we highly suggest monitoring it in the future.\\\" [3] . The Plaintiff's inspector concluded in his report by saying that, \\\"as the pictures show, some problems need repairing as soon as possible.\\\" [4] (our underlining). [ 17 ] The Defendant's expert noticed, in the course of his visit in May 2011, essentially what was noticed by the Plaintiffs' inspector. He also arrived at the same conclusion with respect to the water leakage in the basement. Three pictures taken in the basement were included in his report and his findings are to the effect that \\\"on all three areas, one can see that there is efflorescence staining seen as white marks to the two sides of the cracks. This staining is caused by water seepage and is consistent with the Plaintiffs' claim that water seeps at these areas.\\\" [5] . [ 18 ] The Defendant's expert confirmed that the defects he noticed in the basement would have been apparent to any home inspector in the business. [ 19 ] The Plaintiffs were not prudent and diligent in proceeding with the purchase of the Property without further investigation with respect to the water leakage risk raised by their own inspector. [ 20 ] They rather decided to take that risk and to proceed with the purchase. They now should bear the responsibility resulting from their own decision. [ 21 ] They state that the Defendant personally confirmed that there was no water leakage in the basement. This is denied by the Defendant. Gaudin also indicated that the Plaintiffs never spoke directly with the Defendant. The Defendant was sick during that period and she remained confined to her bed for most of the time. [ 22 ] Furthermore, if the Defendant had provided such a guarantee, as stated by the Plaintiffs, the Court does not understand why there is no such declaration included in the deed of sale that would protect the Plaintiffs in the case of future water leakage. [ 23 ] All in all, the Court is of the opinion that the defect was either known by the Plaintiffs or apparent as defined in article 1726 C.c.Q. Electrical post [ 24 ] No one was called to explain why the electrical post should be considered as a latent defect and why the post itself should be replaced. [ 25 ] The Plaintiffs' explanations only consisted in saying that the electrician recommended that the electrical post be replaced. [ 26 ] The Plaintiffs also allege that the replacement of the electrical post will cost $2,300 plus taxes. The estimation provided by the Plaintiffs, however, is not to that effect. The estimation from the electrician rather indicates that the amount of $2,300 plus taxes is for a 200 amperes electrical panel. There is no mention whatsoever about an electrical post. The Plaintiffs contend that this amount of $2,300 also included the cost of replacing the post. Possibly, but the electrician was not there to confirm that information. [ 27 ] The Defendant's expert went on the roof of the Property and noticed that the base of the electrical post was patched. It appears that this intervention would have resolved a water leakage problem. [ 28 ] The evidence adduced by the Plaintiffs did not support their claim with respect to a latent defect regarding the electrical post. [ 29 ] The Plaintiffs have not demonstrated, on a balance of probability, that they are entitled to claim the amount of $5,800 from the Defendant in damages for latent defects. The conditions for such a claim are simply not present. FOR THOSE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the Plaintiffs' application, each party bearing their own costs. __________________________________ GATIEN FOURNIER, Q.C.J. Date of hearing: September 23, 2013 [1] See exhibit P-2, photo 4. [2] Civil code of Quebec , L.R.Q., 1991, c. 64. [3] See exhibit P-2, p. 7. [4] Idem , p. 25. [5] See exhibit D-2, p. 6....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2013qccq11772-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.020555555820465088, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2013qccq11772-en\", \"title\": \"Xia c. Ali, 2013 QCCQ 11772 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2013qccq11772-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.020555555820465088, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2013qccq11772-en\"}, \"token_count\": 2074, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2013qccq11772-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs1688\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs1688-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Guo c. Caon, 2022 QCCS 1688 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"--- BEGIN PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 0\\n2022 QCCS 1688 (CanLII) | Guo c. Caon | CanLII Date: 2022-05-09 File number: 500-17-100890-170 Citation: Guo c. Caon, 2022 QCCS 1688 (CanLII) Guo c. Caon 2022 QCCS 1688 SUPERIOR COURT Civile Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL N o : 500-17-100890-170 DATE : May 9, 2022 _____________________________________________________________________ PRESIDING : THE HONOURABLE PAUL MAYER, J.S.C. _____________________________________________________________________ JIANG GUO Plaintiff v SUSAN CAON (DEL NEGRO) Defendant/Counter-Claimer/Plaintiff in warranty and LES AMÉNAGEMENTS PAYSAGERS DE L’ARTISAN INC. Defendant in warranty _____________________________________________________________________ JUDGEMENT _____________________________________________________________________ TABLE OF CONTENTS 1.  INTRODUCTION 5 2.  THE LEGAL ISSUES     5 3.  DID MRS. CAON BREACH HER CONTRACTUAL UNDERTAKING?           5 3.1   The context     5 3.1.1  The parties    5 3.1.1.1  Mrs Guo      5 3.1.1.2  Mrs Caon    6 3.1.2  The Property 6 3.1.3  The promise to purchase    6 3.1.4  The inspection          8 3.1.5  The discovery           9 3.1.6  The parties meet       11 3.1.7  The deed of sale       11 3.1.8  The inspections        11 3.1.8.1  Multi-Pro    12 3.1.8.2  Sherlock     13 3.1.8.3  Daniel Turner         15 3.1.8.4  Jamal Maarouf       18 3.1.8.5  Mario Tétrault        19 3.1.8.6  Mr. Turner responds         20 3.1.9  The parties and experts meet         20 3.1.10  Mr. Tétrault’s supplementary report        21 3.1.11  CIEBQ expert report          22 3.1.12  Mold is discovered 22 3.1.13  The corrective work           23 3.1.14  The ligitation           25 3.2   The position of the parties    27 3.2.1  Mrs. Guo        27 3.2.2  Mrs. Caon      28 3.3   The law 28 3.4   Analysis and decision           30 3.4.1  The scope and breadth of the undertaking           30 3.4.2  What corrective work needed to be done to solve the problem?          32 3.4.3  Evaluating contradictory expert reports   33 3.4.3.1  Mr. Daniel Turner  36 3.4.3.2  Mr. Jamal Maarouf 37 3.4.3.3  Mr. Mario Thériault            37 3.4.4  Conclusion    40 4.  DOES THE WATER INFILTRATIONS IN THE PROPERTY CONSTITUTE A LATENT DEFECT?  41 4.1   The position of the parties    41 4.1.1  Mrs. Guo        41 4.1.2  Mrs. Caon      41 4.2   The law 41 4.3   Analysis and decision           44 4.3.1  Did the water infiltration exist prior to the sale?  45 4.3.2  Was the defect sufficiently serious?         45 4.3.3    Could the defect be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer? Was it hidden?     47 4.3.4  Was the defect unknown by Mrs. Guo at the time of the sale?  49 4.3.5  Does article 1728 C.c.Q. apply to this case?        50 5.  WAS MRS. GUO’S CONSENT VITIATED BY FRAUD OR FEAR?    51 5.1  The position of the parties     51 5.1.1  Mrs. Guo        51 5.1.2  Mrs. Caon      51 5.2   The law 51 5.3   Analysis and decision           52 5.3.1  Fraud 52 5.3.2  Fear    54 5.3.3  Conclusion    55 6.  WHAT ARE THE DAMAGES SUFFERED BY MRS. GUO?   55 6.1   The position of the parties    55 6.1.1  Mrs. Guo        55 6.1.2  Mrs. Caon      55 6.2.   The law           56 6.3   Analysis and decision           58 6.3.1  The corrective work 58 6.3.2  Moral damages         59 6.3.3  Expert fees    59 7.  IS MRS. GUO ENTITLED TO OBTAIN THE PAYMENT OF HER EXTRAJUDICIAL FEES?                 60 7.1   The position of the parties    60 7.1.1  Mrs. Guo        60 7.1.2  Mrs. Caon      61 7.2   The law 61 7.3   Analysis and decision           63 7.3.1 Before the proceedings        63 7.3.2. During the proceedings       64 7.3.2.1 Untruthful and disingenuous claims:      64 7.3.2.2. Delaying the proceedings            67 7.4   Conclusion      69 FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT :   71 * * * 1. INTRODUCTION [ 1 ] Mrs. Jiang Guo (“Mrs. Guo”) seeks redress from Mrs. Susan Caon (“Mrs. Caon”) who sold her a house with a water infiltration issue in the basement. [ 2 ] She asks the Court to order Mrs. Caon to reimburse her for the costs she incurred to repair the problem and her expert fees, the moral damages she suffered and the cost of litigating this matter, given the vendors’ alleged bad faith and abuse of the legal process. She also claims that her consent to purchase the property was vitiated by fraud and fear. [ 3 ] Mrs. Caon vigorously contests the claim. [ 4 ] She says that she did everything she could to correct the issue but Mrs. Guo prevented her from doing so. She submits that the water infiltration is not a latent defect as it was divulged before the sale. Finally, she believes that the damages being claimed are exaggerated and unwarranted as there was a much less expensive and easier solution available. 2. THE LEGAL ISSUES [ 5 ] The Court is seized of the following questions: 1) Did Mrs. Caon breach her contractual undertaking? 2) Was the water infiltration issue a latent defect at the time of the sale? 3) Was Mrs. Guo’s consent vitiated by fraud or fear? 4) If so, what damages did she suffer? 5) In the event that Mrs. Guo prevails in her claim, is she entitled to obtain the payment of her extrajudicial fees? 3. Did Mrs. Caon breach her contractual undertaking? 3.1   The context 3.1.1 The parties 3.1.1.1  Mrs Guo [ 6 ] Mrs. Guo is a housewife and a full-time mom. She holds a Bachelor’s degree in electrical engineering and a Masters of Business Administration. [ 7 ] She, her husband and two daughters, applied to immigrate to Canada from China in November 2011. [ 8 ] In July 2016, they received confirmation of their Quebec permanent residency status. They arrived in Montreal shortly after on August 13. They rented a small apartment hotel while they searched for a home they could move into as soon as possible, preferably a place close to the youngest daughter’s school. 3.1.1.2  Mrs Caon [ 9 ] Mrs. Caon was a nurse from 1981 to 2012. [ 10 ] She has been a self-employed private lender, property manager and developer, and a real estate investor for a number of years. Since 2013, she has been actively involved in purchasing properties, renovating them and flipping them for a profit. [ 11 ] She is the mother of three children born from her marriage with the Honourable Manlio Del Negro, a judge of the Court of Quebec (“Mr. Del Negro”). At the time of the events described herein, he was a criminal defence attorney also actively involved in real estate investments and private mortgage lending. 3.1.2 The Property [ 12 ] In June 2008, Mrs. Caon purchased a vintage residential property situated on the aptly named [Street A], in the city of Westmount (“the Property”) [1] . It is a beautiful red brick, Queen Anne style, slate roof building with a separate garage located at the bottom of a slope, some two thirds of the way down Westmount Summit. [ 13 ] Given its dilapidated state, the property was sold without legal warranty. Mrs. Caon and her husband completely gutted it to the studs and renovated it entirely to be a comfortable family home. They moved in during the month of November 2019. It was their pride and joy, a huge undertaking as extensive repairs and restorations were needed after years of neglect. [ 14 ] Among other things, the aged house had a basement with water infiltration issues. The three foot thick, 130 years old foundation is made of rubble field stone and mortar. Humidity had caused significant damages to the Property’s interior plumbing and mechanical fixtures. Evidence of fungal growth had been found [2] . [ 15 ] In February 2015, after living in the house for a few years, Mrs. Caon and Mr. Del Negro decided to sell it as their children were moving away. [ 16 ] It was listed for sale for $4.485 M with a prestigious and successful real estate broker specializing in luxury homes. There were many visits but no offers. The asking price was reduced several times. A year and a half after having been listed, the asking price was about $3.7 M. 3.1.3 The promise to purchase [ 17 ] Two days after arriving in Canada, on August 15, 2016, Mrs. Guo, her husband and daughters visited the Property. It was one among several others seen over a few days with a real estate broker, Mrs. May Zhu. [ 18 ] Mrs. Guo had two documents submitted by the real estate agent of Mrs. Caon in hand when she visited it. [ 19 ] The first, was a mandatory form from the Authority of Real Estate Brokerage in Quebec (“OACIQ”), a seller’s declaration signed by Mrs. Caon in February 2015 [3] . It indicated, among other things, at clause D-3.1, that there had been water infiltrations in the basement or elsewhere. [ 20 ] The second, was a page and a half list of the extensive renovations Mrs. Caon and her husband had carried out to modernize and beautify it. Emphasis was placed on the landscaping done by an award winning landscaper according to a design of an Architect, the luxurious improvements made and the high quality appliances and fixtures. At the top of this list, is the fact that French drains had been installed around the outside of the house, the foundation had been waterproofed, the basement floor had been dug out and redone, and insulated with a one inch Styrofoam and a vapor barrier. [ 21 ] On seeing clause D-3.1, Mrs. Guo asked herself: “What is this?” Her understanding of water infiltration was that “it can be a small thing, but it can also be a big thing… Water infiltration always has consequences.” [4] [ 22 ] She therefore asked her real estate agent to obtain more information about it. Air quality was a particular concern given that her daughter suffered from serious allergies and a respiratory asthma condition. This was one of the reasons they left China. [ 23 ] She recalls asking several times for an answer. [ 24 ] The following day, the issue was cleared up. Mrs. Caon’s broker told Mrs. Guo’s broker verbally that the document was erroneous. There was no water infiltrations. The “yes” in Section D-3.1 should have been a “no”. [ 25 ] Given her concern about the water infiltration issue, Mrs. Guo insisted that her agent obtain a new seller’s declaration to confirm it. As it was not forthcoming, she asked that a clause be added in the promise to purchase that specifically addressed it [5] . Section 12.1 reads as follows: “Seller declares that there are not or have never been water infiltrations in the basement or elsewhere.” (D-3.1 of D588379 should be “no”)”. [ 26 ] On August 23, Mrs. Guo made an offer to purchase the Property for $3.22 M [6] . Following three counterproposals, the parties agreed on a purchase price of $3.3 M [7] . [ 27 ] For her part, given that the purchaser was a Chinese immigrant, Mrs. Caon insisted on obtaining a non-refundable deposit of $200,000 in the event the signing of the deed of sale did not take place because Mrs. Guo prevented it from occurring: […]  A non-refundable deposit of Two Hundred Thousand Dollars ($200,000) shall be remitted to Sotheby’s International Realty Quebec in Trust by bank draft or wire transfer within 48 hours of fulfillment of all conditions. […] in the event that the signing of the act of sale does not take place on or before the date specified at clause 11.1 of the Promise to Purchase because the BUYER voluntarily hinders same or voluntarily prevents it from occurring, the non-refundable deposit held in trust by Sotheby’s International Realty Quebec shall be remitted to the SELLER as liquidated damages with no further recourse by either party. [ 28 ] The signing of the deed of sale was scheduled for October 31. 3.1.4 The inspection [ 29 ] The promise to purchase specified that Mrs. Guo had eight days to inspect the Property. [ 30 ] On September 1, she closely examined it with a home inspector, Mr. Patrice Lagacé, from the firm AL Inspection inc. (“AL”). They spent some four hours visiting it from top to bottom. He took many pictures and made copious notes about what he saw. [ 31 ] The 42-page inspection report is thorough. It is noted that, due to materials and furniture covering the basement floor, it was impossible to pronounce himself on its entire condition. [ 32 ] The report outlines a number of deficiencies about the Property, including: • lintels that are corroded at top of garage door; • some cracks in the masonry and mortar repairs to do; • some parts of the wood on a terrace is damaged; • soil slope brings water near the foundation; • a water stain on third floor furnace room that merited further investigation; • a material deformation detected in the basement washroom that required further investigation. [ 33 ] Evidently, Mr. Del Negro was not impressed with the report. On September 4, he wrote his broker: Hello to you all, Who is this bozo? […] The house is about 135 years old so it’s not a new house but the points he outlines are trivial and he doesn’t know what he is talking about when he mentions that guard rails should be installed around the front of the garden walls. Also the guard rails around the balcony on the north side conform or the westmount regulations. […] Let us know but most of his comments are bull shit!!! [8] [ 34 ] None of the deficiencies were alarming for Mrs. Guo. She was satisfied with this report. She informed the broker that she would go forward with the purchase. The $200,000 non-refundable deposit was received by Mrs. Caon’s real estate broker on September 12. 3.1.5 The discovery [ 35 ]\\n\\n\\n--- END PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 5\\n... aux dépens de la partie à qui incombe le fardeau de la preuve sur cette question. C’est tout comme si la preuve nécessaire n’avait pas été établie. Ce principe devrait être repris par la Cour suprême où, sous la plume de l’honorable juge Pigeon, la Cour ajoutait : Aux prises, comme c’est habituellement le cas dans les affaires de ce genre, avec des opinions scientifiques contradictoires, le juge de première instance s’est dit incapable d’éliminer la confusion créée par les témoignages divergents des experts. Dans ces circonstances, la justice demande qu’on s’efforce d’éliminer la confusion. (…) En 1985, le juge Monet, s’exprimant au nom de la Cour d’appel, suggérait avec encore plus de précisions le cheminement à suivre par un juge de première instance en pareille matière : Lorsque la preuve offerte de part et d’autre est contradictoire, le juge ne doit pas s’empresser de faire succomber celui sur qui reposait la charge de la preuve mais il doit chercher d’abord à découvrir où se situe la vérité en passant au crible tous les éléments de conviction qui lui ont été fournis et c’est seulement lorsque cet examen s’avère infructueux qu’il doit décider en fonction de la charge de la preuve. Pour guider le juge dans cette recherche, les tribunaux ont élaboré deux types de critères : ceux reliés à l’expertise même et ceux reliés à l’expert. La valeur probante d’une expertise sera d’autant plus élevée que les faits sur lesquels elle repose sont prouvés. L’honorable juge Gilles Hébert expose ces principes à partir de deux déclarations de la Cour suprême et d’un arrêt de la Cour d’appel. Les connaissances, l’attitude et les méthodes de l’expert doivent être analysées. Le Tribunal se penchera sur le ou les moyens par lesquels l’expert a acquis son expertise. La formation théorique est importante. Les tribunaux accordent toutefois une préférence à l’expérience pratique. [ 239 ] Having set out the guidelines hereinabove, it is now appropriate to examine more closely the experience and the expert reports submitted by the three principle experts heard by the Court. 3.4.3.1 Mr. Daniel Turner [ 240 ] Mr. Turner is a graduate from McGill University where he obtained a Bachelor of Engineering and was a Faculty Scholar. He is also a graduate from Cornell University (on a General Motors Fellowship) where he obtained a Masters of Engineering. His c.v. illustrates his vast practical experience of some 35 years in respect of hundreds of commercial and residential projects. [76] [ 241 ] Mr. Turner’s testimony was impressive. The Court formed a favorable impression of him as a serious and rigorous professional. He investigated the water infiltration problem thoroughly. He did not cut corners in his quest to discover the source of the problem. [ 242 ] He had two test pits dug on both sides of the house to assess the state of the foundation system (membrane, French drain, state of the rubble stone and mortar, waterproofing or lack thereof). He was adamant at trial that this step was necessary to properly understand and tackle the water infiltration issue. [ 243 ] The Court appreciates the fact that he remained in his lane of expertise. He did not hesitate to differ more precise questions, regarding membranes for example, to Mr. Maarouf, who he believes is better equipped to do so. [ 244 ] His testimony was solid and fact based. He did not waver when questioned both in chief and under cross-examination. [ 245 ] He was truthful and admitted on several occasions that he agreed with Mr. Tétrault’s expertise regarding the deficiencies in the landscaping. [ 246 ] Material evidence, including pictures [77] and videos [78] supported his explanations and findings. The presence of roots going through the membrane and the dampness of the membrane were exposed to the Court. Moreover, his account regarding those elements was corroborated by both Mr. Maarouf and Piccioni. [ 247 ] In short, his testimony was nuanced, truthful and convincing. 3.4.3.2  Mr. Jamal Maarouf [ 248 ] Mr. Maarouf is a soil and materials engineer since 1990. He has considerable experience in this field and has worked in many jurisdictions including Morocco, France, Russia and Canada. More recently, he oversaw the soil and material conformity at the Maestro condominium development in downtown Montreal. His credentials are not in question. [ 249 ] During his examination, he explained in great detail and quite eloquently how the backfill was deficient and how it contributed to the water infiltrations found in the residence. His assessment of the French drain and why it was deficient (absence of geotextile, lack of a second sum pit, situated at the wrong height) is persuasive. His account that the membrane was not applied on a smooth surface and the problems that it caused was insightful. [ 250 ] Mr. Maarouf visited the Property on multiple occasions and supervised the execution of the work. He has an in-depth knowledge of this file, which is illustrated in the clear answers he provided both in his examination in chief and his cross examination. [ 251 ] In cross examination, while being questioned on the National Building Code , he underscored that these norms cannot be applied “textually” without context. He explained that the location of the Property and the type of aged foundation need to be closely considered when deciding what material (membrane) was needed and how best to prepare the foundation wall before it was applied. [ 252 ] The Court considers his testimony to be credible and convincing. 3.4.3.3  Mr. Mario Thériault [ 253 ] Mr. Tétrault has been a mechanical engineer since 1986. He earned a Ph.D. in that field in 1992 from the University of Sherbrooke. In 2011, he completed an “attestation d’études collégiales” in building inspection at Collège Montmorency. Since 2011, the has taught building inspection to cégep students in the Beauce-Appalaches and Rimouski regions. His c.v. indicates that he started working in the field of construction/ renovation and water infiltrations in 2008. [79] Prior to that, from 1993 to 2008, he taught mechanical engineering (robotics, automated production) at l’École de Technologie Supérieure. During the hearing, he estimated that he has testified as an expert witness, mostly in defence over a hundred times. [ 254 ] As mentioned, Mr. Tetreault’s assessment of the water infiltrations at the Property is drastically different from that of the other experts. He submits that the deficient landscaping executed by L’Artisan is the sole issue. He contends that all other deficiencies are minor and “ne contient pas de déficit d’usage.” [80] [ 255 ] The Court considers that there are many concerns about his learned opinion. [ 256 ] To begin, Mr. Tetreault’s expertise regarding structures and soil is not on par with those of Mr. Turner and Mr. Maarouf. Both of them have over 30 years of practical experience in their respective fields. While the Court recognizes that he has worked in the construction/renovation field for over 15 years, his niche remains teaching, mechanical engineering and house inspections. [ 257 ] Mechanical engineering is one of the broadest engineering disciplines. It is the application of engineering principles and problem-solving techniques from the design to the manufacturing of objects, machines, systems like cars, HVAC systems, sensors and switches. [ 258 ] Another conspicuous element of concern is that Mr. Tétrault argued against making two test pits to examine the state of the foundation. The Court considers that this has a serious impact on his credibility as a neutral observer. [ 259 ] He dismissed Mr. Turner’s suggestion to do so. In an email he sent to Me Caisse, Mrs. Caon’s lawyer at the time, he contended that paying $6,800 for two test pits is « très excessif ». He stated that the source of the problem was « très claire » : « À mon avis, le seul élément qu’il reste à vérifier est la présence de la membrane liquide sur le mur arrière en creusant un trou de de 2 pieds de profondeur sous la porte. Cela prendrait au plus, 4h de travail à une seule personne (300$ + tx). » [81] [ 260 ] He even stated at trial « Je serais allé le creuser moi-même. » [ 261 ] Respectfully, such a position lacks the rigor required from an expert witness. [ 262 ] The evidence is clear, there had been significant water infiltration problems when Mrs. Caon purchased the Property back in 2008. Corrective waterproofing work was carried out in 2009. Following this work, further infiltrations occurred. Then, in the Fall of 2016 and in the Spring and Summer of 2017, Mrs. Guo witnessed more water infiltrations. [ 263 ] Faced with a recurring problem and after consulting professionals, a sound and reasonable decision was made to investigate further by excavating test pits which would expose the state of the foundations. [ 264 ] The Property in question is a very expensive one. The presence of repetitive water infiltrations needed a robust response. Before jumping to conclusions and finding an easy and inexpensive solution, a proper, thorough and careful analysis was necessary. Only a complete examination of the foundation would do to formulate conclusions as to the cause of the said infiltrations. [ 265 ] As Mr. Turner so aptly said, serious problems mandate serious solutions. Digging two test pits was one of them. Simply turning over the earth and making a two feet deep hole in the back of the Property would not have exposed the foundation and allowed for a complete assessment. [ 266 ] Thirdly, Mr. Tétrault got the sequence of events mixed up in his initial expert report. He writes: « Notre cliente aurait emménagé dans la propriété en novembre 2009. Au printemps 2010, elle aurait noté un peu d’humidité dans la salle mécanique située à l’arrière du bâtiment. Elle aurait fait installer un drain français et poser une membrane liquide avec un isolant rigide. Aucune infiltration d’eau ou d’humidité n’aurait été observée par la suite. » [82] [ 267 ] Perhaps, this is disinformation he received from Mrs. Caon or maybe he just got the sequence of events wrong. In any event, it is not factual. The water infiltrations occurred in the late Summer of 2010 after and not before the waterproofing work carried out in August 2009. [ 268 ] Fourtly, the Court finds that Mr. Tetreault’s repeated disagreement on certain factual elements where there is consensus among the other experts and witnesses are troubling. A few of them immediately come to mind. [ 269 ] To begin, he insisted on the fact that the membrane was dry and had to be removed with a shovel type tool or a jack hammer. He disagreed that large portions of the membrane were damp as Mr. Turner, Mr. Maarouf and Mr. Piccioni testified. Yet, a video and pictures clearly show that large pieces of the membrane were soaked when removed. The Court cannot side with Mr. Tétrault when faced with such convincing evidence, both material and testimonial. [ 270 ] As well, Mr. Tétrault emphasized that while there was some material in the backfill that did not respect the National Building Code because of their size, “on a pas vu où c’était lorsqu’ils ont escavé”, which according to him means that its impact on the foundation cannot be known. [ 271 ] Further, Mr. Tétrault insisted that the backfill material respected the Code and that “du remblai de sable” is very rarely installed and was not needed, in direct contradiction to what soil expert Mr. Maarouf asserted. Once again, Mr. Tétrault fails to consider the particular location and age of the Property built on rubble stone foundations sitting at the bottom of a steep mountain. [ 272 ] Fifthly, the Court remarks that Mr. Tetreault’s testimony fluctuated in respect to the necessity that the membrane be applied on a smooth surface. He was difficult to follow at times. [ 273 ] He stated at first that Draineau had applied an Aqua Bloc 770-06 membrane on a “raisonnablement lisse” surface, when that is not at all true. Then, during the hearing, he stated that it had been applied on an “irregular surface”. [ 274 ] Finally, on the last day of his testimony, he pulled a surprise. He stated that he had just noted that in Draineau’s invoice of March 5, 2010 [83] , a Mulcoperm liquid membrane had been installed. Although he could not find the specifications for this particular product, he did unearth those of a competitor, Grace, that subsequently went bankrupt. He explained that the specifications for the liquid membrane for this product states that it can be applied on “irregular profiles” [84] . [ 275 ] The Court considers that this is not a serious or credible exercise. He was clearly pulling at threads here and wasting precious Court time. [ 276 ] For one, he is using the application details of a competing product. As well, the specifications he submits do not refer to a 130 years old rubble stone foundation. What can be seen from the drawing made for illustration purposes is that it shows a concrete wall and the membrane applied all the way to the footing of the foundation. [ 277 ] Finally, the Court considers that Mr. Tétrault lacked neutrality and impartiality. [ 278 ] His first report is entitled a “Rapport d’expertise technico-légale”. In it, he makes legal determinations as to whether certain deficiencies constitute “un déficit d’usage”. [ 279 ] What’s more, in his report of January 29, 2019, he concludes a section entitled « Responsabilité » that L’Artisan is legally liable for the water infiltrations: « En aménagement le niveau du terrain plus haut que la membrane liquide, l’entrepreneur en aménagement paysager a créé le problème d’infiltration d’eau dans le bâtiment et, conséquemment, il en devient responsable ». [85] [ 280 ] While it is possible, he did not understand the scope of his mandate, which does not include giving legal opinions, left to the Court to determine, what taints his reports and testimony negatively is the underlying idea that part of his mandate was to find a culprit, someone responsible for the infiltrations. [ 281 ] What also strikes the imagination is that Mr. Tétrault finds only one contractor, L’Artisan, to be responsible. It is evident to all other observers that Draineau did inadequate and incomplete foundation waterproofing work. In such a case, the Court considers that a reasonably rigorous, neutral, impartial and objective expert would have found that at least two contractors potentially contributed to the water infiltration problem. 3.4.4 Conclusion [ 282 ] To conclude, the Court considers that Mr. Turner and Mr. Maarouf were the more convincing experts. They have the most direct experience with the subject at hand. Their assessment of the situation proved to be convincing. The solution they proposed is reasonable in these circumstances. [ 283 ] Given Mrs. Caon’s undertaking in the deed of sale, she is liable to pay for the cost of repairs required to correct Draineau’s and L’Artisan’s deficient work of 2009 and 2010. This is the work that satisfactorily remedied the water infiltrations observed in the electrical room of the Property. 4. DoES the water infiltrations in the property constitute A latent defect? 4.1   The position of the parties 4.1.1 Mrs. Guo [ 284 ] Mrs. Guo submits that the water infiltrations constitute latent defects. She is of the opinion that: • the defect did exist at the moment of the sale; • she was unaware of its presence given the explanation given to her that there had been a “very minor problem” that had been solved in 2010. This cannot constitute knowledge; • the infiltrations could not be discovered when she visited the Property prior to the purchase; and • the infiltrations diminished the Property’s usefulness, notably because the family could not fully enjoy the basement (movie room) because of the high humidity levels and Mrs. Guo’s daughter’s respiratory problems. 4.1.2 Mrs. Caon [ 285 ] Mrs. Caon argues that, if defects did exist, they had been disclosed before the sale. [ 286 ] Alternatively, Mrs. Guo acted negligently by not following her inspector’s recommendations. [ 287 ] Moreover, she submits that Mrs. Guo is not a penniless victim and had in fact sought legal counsel prior to the closing. She should have been more thorough in her investigation. 4.2   The law [ 288 ] Article 1716 C.c.Q. provides that: The seller is bound to deliver the property and to warrant the ownership and quality of the property These warranties exist by operation of the law, whether or not they are stipulated in the contract of sale. [ 289 ] The warranty of quality is found at article 1726 C.c.Q...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 6\\n... submits that Mrs. Guo is not a penniless victim and had in fact sought legal counsel prior to the closing. She should have been more thorough in her investigation. 4.2   The law [ 288 ] Article 1716 C.c.Q. provides that: The seller is bound to deliver the property and to warrant the ownership and quality of the property These warranties exist by operation of the law, whether or not they are stipulated in the contract of sale. [ 289 ] The warranty of quality is found at article 1726 C.c.Q. that states that: The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. [ 290 ] Hence, for the Court to determine the presence of latent defects, four elements must be present: 1. the defect must be “important”; 2. it must have existed at the time of the sale; 3. it must have been hidden; and 4. the buyer must have been unaware of the defect [86] ; [ 291 ] The burden of proving the first three elements rests on Mrs. Guo. [87] [ 292 ] As it pertains to the fourth and last criterion, the Supreme court in ABB inc. v. Domtar inc. stated that: « aucune présomption de connaissance ne pèse sur l’acheteur, qui est toujours présumé de bonne foi. Le fardeau de prouver la connaissance réelle du vice repose donc sur le vendeur. » [88] [ 293 ] This onus will arise solely when plaintiff succeeds in proving the first three criteria. [ 294 ] The author Jeffrey Edwards describes apparent defect as being : « celui que l’acheteur aurait dû, en raison de son devoir d’examen, constater. Le vice apparent peut être complètement ignoré de l’acheteur. » [89] [ 295 ] An apparent defect is, essentially, one that can be deciphered by a diligent buyer “without the need to resort to an expert.” The norm of the “diligent buyer” is an objective one. Namely, « le test consiste à se demander si un acheteur raisonnable placé dans les mêmes circonstances aurait dû déceler le vice au moment de la vente. » [90] [ 296 ] The Courts have repeatedly stated that a buyer does not, in the absence “d’indice visible qui soulève des soupçons” [91] have to open walls and conduct intrusive searches in a building they wish to purchase. In Garage Yves Collins St-Tite, s.e.n.c. v. Carpentier , Justice Richard Poudrier, analyzing the jurisprudence and doctrine writes that : « Selon la doctrine et la jurisprudence, l’examen de l’acheteur a été qualifié de différentes façons : ainsi on a exigé qu’il soit tantôt attentif, tantôt normal, tantôt raisonnable ou sérieux, sans être approfondi. Les Tribunaux ont aussi précisé qu’en l’absence d’indice visible qui soulève des soupçons, l’acheteur n’a pas à ouvrir un mur ou creuser des fondations pour constater l’état du bien. » [92] [ 297 ] The obligation of diligence on the part of the buyer, will thus vary depending on the circumstances. This has been restated by the Court of appeal in the case of Paulin v. Gauthier [93] , where the Court summarized the obligations of the buyer in matters of latent defects like this: o L’acheteur prudent et diligent doit procéder à un examen visuel attentif et complet de l’immeuble et doit demeurer à l’affût de tout indice pouvant laisser soupçonner un vice o Si l’acheteur soupçonne un problème, il doit prendre les mesures raisonnables pour connaître l’état réel du bâtiment, dont le recours aux services d’un expert o I l doit vérifier ou faire vérifier ce qui est suspect, sans quoi le vice peut être qualifié d’apparent, selon les circonstances de l’affaire o L’acheteur devra être plus vigilant selon la vocation de l’immeuble; un immeuble à vocation commerciale ou industrielle devra le conduire à un examen plus minutieux qu’un immeuble à vocation résidentielle o L’acheteur prudent et avisé doit examiner très soigneusement un vieil immeuble ne pas examiner les effets de l’âge sur les composantes peut constituer une imprudence. (our underlinings) [ 298 ] The warranty of quality does not come to the rescue of those who have been negligent. Art. 1728 C.c.Q. provides that: If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but also to make reparation for then injury suffered by the buyer. [ 299 ] Once again, the buyer must prove, by preponderance of probabilities, that the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect. [94] Good faith is presumed pursuant to article 2805 C.c.Q. [95] [ 300 ] The Court of Appeal in Placement Jacpar Inc. v. Benzakour recognized that the conduct of the seller can have repercussions on the duty of diligence that is required on the part of the prospective buyer. Justice Lebel speaking for the Court states that: « Le propre comportement du vendeur jouera son rôle à l'occasion dans l'appréciation du caractère du vice. Ainsi, ces fausses représentations ou le dol du vendeur quant à l'état de la chose ou aux causes réelles de vices apparents, dégageront parfois l'acquéreur de l'obligation d'explorer davantage. Le dol ou le mensonge du vendeur peut rendre un vice juridiquement caché, surtout lorsque l'acquéreur a demandé une explication au sujet des manifestations d'un défaut et qu'on l'a orienté sur une fausse piste .  On ne peut reprocher à l'acquéreur de soupçonner la mauvaise foi de son vendeur. Ainsi, dans B.et R. Gauthier Ltée c. Lemieux (C.A.Q. 200-09-000096-773 , 2 septembre 1980 ), la Cour d'appel avait confirmé un jugement de la Cour supérieure qui accueillait une action en diminution de prix. L'acheteur s'était inquiété du mauvais état apparent d'un plancher.  Le vendeur lui avait représenté qu'il s'agissait d'une question mineure qui se corrigerait d'elle-même à la fin des travaux. L'acquéreur n'avait pas à explorer davantage. Il pouvait se satisfaire de l'explication qu'on lui donnait.  Il n’avait pas à faire  des recherches  ou vérifications additionnelles si on lui représentait que l'état des choses n'était pas grave. » [96] [ 301 ] Logically, if the seller lies, or conceals, or withholds information or leads the buyer to believe that the situation is less problematic when questioned on certain elements of a property, he can be found to have breached his obligation of good faith. In such an event, he won’t be able to invoke the recklessness or negligence of the buyer. [97] 4.3   Analysis and decision [ 302 ] Do the water infiltrations in the Property’s basement constitute latent defects? [ 303 ] As stated, article 1726 C.c.Q. stipulates that a vendor has an obligation to warrant the quality of a property sold. In order to succeed in a claim for talent defects, Mrs. Guo must prove that the defect: a) existed prior to the sale; b) was serious, the nature of which must be appreciated objectively; c) was unknown to them, i.e. it was hidden; and d) was notified to Mrs. Caon in writing within a reasonable period of time. [ 304 ] There is no debate over whether the written notice was given within a reasonable delay. Mrs. Caon was notified of the water infiltration within days of the sale. She was also informed at every step of the way during the subsequent years of discovery. [ 305 ] We shall thus analyse the other three conditions. 4.3.1 Did the water infiltration exist prior to the sale? [ 306 ] This is the simplest criterion to fulfill in this case. The fact that there were water infiltration problems at the time of the sale is no longer disputed. [ 307 ] Mr. Tétrault recognized in his reports and during his testimony that he witnessed water infiltrations that caused high humidity levels: “À partir d’une ouverture existante sur le coin droit de la pièce arrière, nous avons observé de la moisissure sur le mur de fondation, la dégradation d’un montant et des taux d’humidité élevés (supérieur à 20%) de 23.2% et 42.9%. Ces lectures confirment une infiltration d’eau active. » [98] [ 308 ] Mrs. Caon’s lawyer recognized and reiterated during pleadings that there was a water infiltration problem at the time of the sale. According to him, the only debate really was about the scope of the corrective work to be done to fix it. [ 309 ] During his testimony, Mr. Daniel Turner stated that it would be very unlikely that there would not have been other episodes of water infiltrations between 2010 and the time of sale, in November 2016, considering that substantial infiltrations that occurred in the Spring and Summer of 2017. [ 310 ] This first criterion is fulfilled. 4.3.2 Was the defect sufficiently serious? [ 311 ] The Court determines that the water infiltrations problem was a serious one. [ 312 ] Mrs. Guo’s credible testimony is to the effect that she did not use the basement for a period of close to four years, between the time she moved in and the time the corrective work was done. [ 313 ] She testified that the humidity levels in the basement were very high and that this prevented her family, most importantly her daughter who suffers from allergies and asthma, from using the full space of the house. [ 314 ] Sherlock Inspections House inc.’s report dated April 7, 2017, indicates high levels of humidity in the basement’s mechanical room, ranging from 55.8% up to 87.6 %. [99] [ 315 ] When Mr. Tétrault inspected the Property on October 13, 2017, he found that the ambient humidity levels in the electrical room was excessively high (64.5%). After carrying out a water test, he noted that the wood studs became saturated (100% humidity) on the right side of that room. [100] [ 316 ] When Mr. Tétrault returned to the basement electrical room on May 15, 2020, he noted that the ambient air humidity in that room was 76.4% that he acknowledged “nous apparait élevé”. [101] [ 317 ] Mrs. Guo testified that, now that the corrective work has been accomplished, the humidity levels in the basement are closer to 25%. [ 318 ] Mr. Daniel Turner explained in a very thorough and credible manner the gravity of the latent defects found at the residence. In his opinion, the whole foundation waterproofing system was inadequate and outright problematic. The membrane was not applied correctly and it was not waterproof. There were multiple holes in the foundation (mortar had disintegrated), the French drain didn’t function properly and the backfill was not appropriate. He explained that these deficiencies allowed the water to infiltrate the basement, through the mechanical room. [ 319 ] He testified that, because of the Property’s particular characteristics (sitting at the bottom of the mountain, some 130 years old rubble stone walls), its rear wall acted as “a dam” which captured surface and ground water flowing from the surrounding properties. [ 320 ] Had the foundations been properly repaired, keeping in mind the specific needs of such foundations, when Mrs. Caon bought the Property in 2008, no such infiltrations would have occurred. [ 321 ] Mr. Maarouf, a soil engineer at Solroc, testified to the same effect. He stated that « toute la montagne coule vers le bâtiment ». Moreover, he said that the liquid membrane was not properly adhered to the foundation wall. He evaluates that 25% to 50% of it was not properly doing so. What’s more, the French drain was not protected by a geotextile membrane. [ 322 ] In his report, he noted 13 deficiencies [102] , including the backfill’s heterogeneous composition that exacerbated hydrostatic pressure on the foundations. He also stated that the sump pit was not functioning adequately as the water in the French drain had to go up, from the east side of the property to the sump pit located on the west side. He claims this defect created a “pool” of stagnant water that we can see in pictures submitted into evidence. [103] [ 323 ] He concluded that, given the long list of deficiencies that were discovered, Mrs. Guo had no other choice but to proceed with the important work necessary to correct the serious water infiltration problem besetting this vintage Property. [ 324 ] In light of this evidence, the Court determines the defects were sufficiently important. This is an old and valuable property that needed a long-term solution. 4.3.3 Could the defect be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer? Was it hidden? [ 325 ] Mrs. Caon claims that Mrs.Guo acted negligently and could have deciphered the water infiltration problems had she paid closer attention and followed her inspector’s recommendations. [ 326 ] Respectfully, the Court disagrees for a number of reasons. [ 327 ] First of all, the Civil Code specificies that a seller is not bound to warrant against latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect. Such a defect is defined as one that can be perceived by a prudent buyer “ without the need to resort to an expert. ” [104] [ 328 ] In the present case, Mrs. Guo did in fact hire a specialist who spent four hours inspecting the Property. At no time did he find an “apparent” defect related to water infiltrations. [ 329 ] How could he? Pictures of the mechanical room at the time of the inspection show the space was cluttered and there were boxes and other moveable that concealed the two openings into the wall. [105] [ 330 ] The jurisprudence does not require a prospective buyer or their inspector to open walls and conduct extensive tests to see if latent defects exist. [ 331 ] While no one is denying that the Property was old at the time of the purchase, it is important to keep in mind that Mrs. Guo took the precaution to have it inspected. She also relied on the detailed list of all the work Mrs. Caon represented had been done to the Property. This document would lead a reasonable person to believe that a fully renovated property with great old bones was for sale. Waterproofing of the foundations and the installation of a new French drain is specifically mentioned. [ 332 ] For a reasonable person, this indicates the good state of the foundations and French drain. Mrs. Guo was not buying an old property in an abysmal state, like Mrs. Caon had done in 2008 without legal warranty. She was purchasing a luxuriously expensive home that had been fully and thoroughly renovated in one of Canada’s most prestigious neighborhoods. [ 333 ] When read carefully, AL inspection’s report does not signal “d’indice visible qui soulève des soupçons”. It notes general issues that can be found in this type of properties. For instance, under “type of foundation” there is a boiler plate clause that states that: “Ancestral foundation detected. Ancestral foundation tend to crumble, crack and cause water infiltration without notice. It is recommended to ensure with a qualified specialist that is adequately waterproof and that its drainage is appropriate and repair if needed, in order to limit water infiltration risk, structural effect and any consequences due to a negligence of this recommendation. (corresponding to vendor’s declaration foundation drainage was redone)”. [106] (our underlining) [ 334 ] This section of the report is coded 2, meaning “repair maintenance or further investigation recommended.” [ 335 ] Does the fact that Mrs. Guo did not seek additional investigation regarding the foundations after reading the report make her negligent? [ 336 ] The Court does not think so. [ 337 ] The inspector solely expresses a general observation that ancestral foundations tend to crack and crumble. He does not state that he has observed a serious issue or any water infiltrations in this specific home. Had it been the case, Mrs. Guo’s refusal to investigate further could have been blameworthy. However, this is not what happened here. [ 338 ] Mrs. Caon’s attorney cites the case of Cistellini v. Jinchereau [107] arguing that it supports Mrs. Caon’s claim that Plaintiff should have “listened to her inspector’s recommendations” and have the foundations more thoroughly inspected. [ 339 ] With respect, the Court disagrees. Cistellini can be distinguished from the present case...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 10\\n[ 492 ] In addition, in January 2019, Mrs. Caon delayed making amendments to her defense that caused the postponement of her scheduled examination. [ 493 ] On March 22, 2019, she again cancelled her scheduled examination that had been set by Justice Collier to be on April 2, 2019, because of a vacation. [ 494 ] Mrs. Caon as previously stated, communicated Draineau’s invoice for the initial waterproofing work in November 2019, nine months after the undertaking to do so, in April 2019. After all that time, it turned out to be a fake. This is not the transparency and cooperation expected of litigants. [ 495 ] Following Solroc’s July 2019 report on mold and spores, Mrs. Caon insisted that her expert, Mr. Tétrault conduct his own assessment and file a report. This blocked and delayed the setting down the case for a trial. Having reviewed it, the Court concludes that it was an unnecessary expertise that added nothing to the trial as it merely confirmed the existing expert report. Nor was it useful to prompt Mrs. Caon to respect her contractual undertaking and pay for the cost to decontaminate. [ 496 ] Then, is also of concern, the fact that Mrs. Caon settled with l’Artisan on the eve of the trial. Settlements are normally encouraged. But, in this particular case, this last-minute surprise deprived Mrs. Guo the opportunity to cross-examine an important witness and prevented the Court from hearing and reading crucial evidence. [ 497 ] It is also necessary to point out a certain lack of empathy for others and a sense of entitlement that was in evidence during the trial. At one point, during the hearing, Mrs. Caon made a motion to postpone the last few days of the trial because of a vacation even though a decision had already been made by the Court to proceed. [ 498 ] Finally, the Court would be remiss not to mention one last point. Reading the examination of April 17, 2019 of Mrs. Caon by Me Rassam was a sad and painful exercise. [ 499 ] There were constant interruptions and needless argumentation over the simplest of questions. One can witness the wrangling going on for pages and pages. One can also perceive the clock ticking as time is wasted and the questioner is being distracted. The distress and frustration felt by Mrs. Guo’s attorney jumps out of the page and it is palpable. This manner of proceeding is well below the bar of what is expected pursuant to article 20 C.C.P. 7.4   Conclusion [ 500 ] The Court considers this case to be a good example of one where the cultural shift required by parties by the new Code of Civil Procedure got lost along the way. [ 501 ] There is nothing wrong with a good and honest debate. However, trials by obstruction, delay and ambush are not acceptable. Cooperation and transparency are required. Parties are expected to participate in good faith. Evidence is to be preserved and divulged early on in the process. [ 502 ] Certain sections of articles 18 to 20 C.C.P. applicable herein are as follows: 18. The parties to a proceeding must observe the principle of proportionality and ensure that their actions, their pleadings, including their choice of an oral or a written defence, and the means of proof they use are proportionate, in terms of the cost and time involved, to the nature and complexity of the matter and the purpose of the application. […] 19. […] the parties control the course of their case insofar as they comply with the principles, objectives and rules of procedure and the prescribed time limits. They must be careful to confine the case to what is necessary to resolve the dispute, and must refrain from acting with the intent to cause prejudice to another person or behaving in an excessive or unreasonable manner, contrary to the requirements of good faith. […] 20. The parties are duty-bound to co-operate and, in particular, to keep one another informed at all times of the facts and particulars conducive to a fair debate and make sure that relevant evidence is preserved. They must, among other things, at the time prescribed by this Code or determined in the case protocol, inform one another of the facts on which their contentions are based and of the evidence they intend to produce. [ 503 ] The learned authors Ferland and Emery remind us that: « Le nouveau Code de procédure civile impose des devoirs nouveaux aux parties, notamment de coopération, de transparence et de divulgation, en tout temps, en vue de permettre un débat loyal et pour permettre au tribunal d’assurer le respect du principe de la proportionnalité des actes de procédure et des moyens de preuve (considération des coûts anticipés) et la saine gestion de l’instance (Disposition préliminaire art. 18, 19, 20). » [140] [ 504 ] Having considered all the circumstances mentioned hereinabove, the Court is not convinced by the demonstration made by Mrs. Guo that Mrs. Caon’s conduct of the proceedings amounted to abuse of procedure envisioned by article 51 C.C.P. [ 505 ] Rather, it considers that there were valid legal arguments made about the scope of the undertaking and the validity of the solution proposed by Mrs. Guo’s experts. There was a healthy and loyal debate to be had on both sides. There were several complex issues to be determined. This is not a case of outright abuse. [ 506 ] Mrs. Caon did not ignore the water infiltration and humidity when she was notified in the Fall of 2016 and the Spring and Summer of 2017. Her and her husband were actively investigating the problem. They tried to find a quick and inexpensive solution. A proper slope away from the house sounded like a common sense solution. [ 507 ] They also relied on Mr. Tétrault’s expert opinion that reinforced their own views that the solution proposed by Mrs. Guo’s experts was overly exaggerated, expensive and overkill for what they considered to be a minor problem that was not as serious as it was. [ 508 ] A close examination of the plumitif does not illustrate a dramatic overabundance of the legal process on her part. [ 509 ] To use the words of Justice Dalphond in the Court of Appeal decision of Royal Lepage Commercial inc. v. 109650 Canada Ltd [141] , there is no clear and overwhelming « indices de mauvaise foi (telle l’intention de causer des désagréments à son adversaire plutôt que le désir de faire reconnaître le bien-fondé de ses prétentions…) ou à tout le moins des indices de témérité » in this case. [ 510 ] The futility of the counter-claim was recognized and dropped in a reasonable manner. [ 511 ] The Court considers that a reasonable and prudent person would not appraise that Mrs. Caon’s defence was rash, bold or headstrong. Nor was she immoderate, reckless or foolhardy. [ 512 ] Having said that, there is nevertheless a number of substantial breaches during the proceedings that merit a sanction: the delays, the concealment, the lack of cooperation and transparency, all of which are deeply concerning. The breaches were substantial. [ 513 ] In Lavoie v. Latouche , the Court of Appeal maintained a Superior court judgment that had condemned the appellants to pay $10,000 in legal fees pursuant to article 342 of the Code of Civil Procedure. « Les fautes attribuables uniquement aux appelants ( défaut de respecter des engagements , utilisation de prétextes pour retarder la transmission de renseignements financiers pertinents communiqués uniquement pendant le procès avec huit mois de retard , demande à leur avocat d’étirer inutilement les débats devant le tribunal, etc.) constituent, à n’en pas douter, des manquements importants de leur part, destinés essentiellement à embêter les intimés, à entraver le cours du procès et à faire perdre le temps des parties et du tribunal. Ces manquements importants justifiaient, à eux seuls, l’imposition d’une sanction proportionnelle à la gravité manifeste de ceux-ci . En fixant celle-ci à 10 000 $, le juge a fait une utilisation avisée du pouvoir discrétionnaire que lui confère l’article 342 C.p.c. pour laquelle les appelants ne démontrent pas d’erreur révisable. » [142] [ 514 ] Given all the circumstances of this case and applying its discretion to impose a penalty pursuant to article 342 C.C.P., the Court will order Mrs. Caon to pay Mrs. Guo the sum of $50,000. [ 515 ] This sum is a fair and reasonable amount that should adequately compensate Mrs. Guo’s legal fees incurred because of the abovementioned misconduct. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT : [ 516 ] GRANTS the Plaintiff’s Re-Re-Re-Amended Originating Application, in part; [ 517 ] CONDEMNS the Defendant to pay Plaintiff the sum of $245,240.53 (taxes included), the whole with interest at the legal rate as of November 1, 2020, plus the additional indemnity provided for pursuant to Article 1619 C.c.Q. [ 518 ] CONDEMNS the Defendant to pay the Plaintiff the sum of $5,000 for the damages for stress, anxiety and inconvenience; [ 519 ] CONDEMNS the Defendant to pay the Plaintiff the sum of $50,000 as a penalty pursuant to article 342 C.C.P. [ 520 ] THE WHOLE, with costs, including those of the experts of the Plaintiff that total $65,884.02. _________________________________ Paul Mayer, j.s.c. Me Stéphani e Rassam Me Krystyn Pietras Stein & Stein inc. Attorneys for the Plaintiff Me Frédéric Dupont Segal Santillo Attorneys for the Defendant/Counter-Claimer/Plaintiff in warranty Dates of hearing: April 11, 12, 13, 14, 19, 20 and 21, 2022. [1] SC-3. [2] E-2. [3] P-3. [4] Pre-trial examination of Mrs. Jiang Guo by Me Ling Fu, October 11, 2018, p. 22. [5] P-1. [6] Ibid. [7] P-2. [8] E-9. [9] P-4. [10] Pre-trial examination of Mrs. Jiang Guo by Me Ling Fu on October 11, 2018, p. 26; P-4 a). [11] Supra note 8 . [12] Pre-trial examination of Mrs. Susan Caon by Me Stéphanie Rassam, April 17, 2019, p. 113. [13] P-5. [14] Supra note 8 . [15] Supra note 12 , p. 121. [16] P-6. [17] P-7, p. 14. [18] P-8. [19] P-9. [20] P-10. [21] Ibid . [22] Examination in chief of Mrs. Caon on April 13, 2022. [23] D-27. [24] P-11. [25] P-12. [26] P-13. [27] P-14. [28] P-17. [29] Supra note 10 , p. 81. [30] P-18. [31] Ibid. [32] P-19. [33] P-25. [34] P-20. [35] Ibid. [36] P-23. [37] “The two existing trenches need to be excavated deeper up to the footing level. By doing so, we can determine the position of French drain relative to the interior slab.” [38] P-17. [39] D-1. [40] P-27. [41] D-8. [42] P-51. [43] D-2. [44] D-15, pp. 19-23. [45] P-40, pp. 99-103. [46] Ibid , pp. 59-66. [47] D-12. [48] P-31. [49] P-24. [50] P-26. [51] P-30. [52] P-32. [53] Ibid . [54] Ibid. [55] P-33. [56] D-15, p. 3. [57] Ibid , p. 10. [58] Ibid, p. 15. [59] Ibid, p. 14 . [60] Ibid, p. 16 . [61] P-37. [62] Supra note 12 , pp. 151, 162 and 165. [63] art. 1458 C.c.Q. : Every person has a duty to honour his contractual undertakings. Where he fails in this duty, he is liable for any bodily, moral or material injury he causes to the other contracting party and is bound to make reparation for the injury; neither he nor the other party may in such a case avoid the rules governing contractual liability by opting for rules that would be more favourable to them. [64] Jean-Louis Baudoin, Patrice Deslauriers et Benoît Moore, La responsabilité civile , 8 e éd., Cowansville, EYB, 2014, p. 176, par. 183. [65] 2018 QCCQ 851 , par. 23 , 27, 28 and 29. [66] E-9. [67] D-1, p. 24. [68] D-15, p. 9. [69] P-40. [70] 2011 QCCS 391 . [71] 2010 QCCA 1505 , par. 29 . [72] Jean-Claude Royer, Sophie Lavallée, La preuve civile , 4 e éd. (Cowansville, Qc : EYB, 2008) at p. 346-8 (references omitted). [73] [2002] AZ-50107787 , par. 59 . [74] Pierre Tessier, La preuve devant le tribunal civil , Collection de droit, Barreau du Québec, 1996-1997, vol. 2, EYB, 209 et 210. [75] 2004 CanLII 56953 (QC CS) , [2005] R.J.Q. 205 , AZ-50289786 , par. 111 . [76] P-25 b). [77] P-25; P-27, pp. 6 and 18; P-39. [78] P-43. [79] D-1, p. 40. [80] Ibid, pp. 23-24. [81] P-17. [82] D-1, p. 4. [83] D-14. [84] D-25. [85] D-2. [86] Cistellini v. Jinchereau , 2012 QCCS 1776 , par. 40 . [87] art. 2804 C.c.Q. : A person seeking to assert a right shall prove the facts on which his claim is based. A person who claims that a right is null, has been modified or is extinguished shall prove the facts on which he bases his claim. Evidence is sufficient if it renders the existence of a fact more probable than its non-existence, unless the law requires more convincing proof. [88] ABB inc. v. Domtar inc., 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , 2007 3 SCR 461 , par. 54 . [89] Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois , 2 e éd., 2008, Wilson et Lafleur, Montréal, p. 171, par. 372. [90] ABB inc. v. Domtar inc., préc., par. 42. [91] Garage Yves Collins St-Tite, s.e.n.c. v. Carpentier , 2006 QCCQ 12608 , par. 36 . [92] Ibid. [93] Paulin v. Gauthier 2014 QCCA 189 . [94] Verreault v. Légaré , AZ-50067894 , p.21. [95] art. 2805 C.c.Q. : “Good faith is always presumed, unless the law expressly requires it to be proved”. [96] Placement Jacpar Inc. v. Benzakour , 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , AZ-89011869 . [97] Ibid. [98] D-12, pp. 3-6. [99] P-9, p. 10. [100] D-1, p. 6. [101] D-12, p. 3. [102] P-40. [103] P-39. [104] art. 1726 C.c.Q. [105] D-3, p. 35. [106] Ibid, p. 5. [107] 2012 QCCS 1776 . [108] Ibid, par. 68 et 70 [109] Ibid, par. 75. [110] D-3. [111] 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 SCR 461 , at par. 54 . [112] P-41. [113] Pierre-Gabriel Jobin et Nathalie Vézina, Les obligations , 7 e éd., Cowansville, EYB, 2013, p. 338. [114] Ibid, pp. 338-339. [\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 11\\n] Ibid, par. 68 et 70 [109] Ibid, par. 75. [110] D-3. [111] 2007 SCC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 SCR 461 , at par. 54 . [112] P-41. [113] Pierre-Gabriel Jobin et Nathalie Vézina, Les obligations , 7 e éd., Cowansville, EYB, 2013, p. 338. [114] Ibid, pp. 338-339. [115] art. 2805 C.c.Q. [116] Supra note 113 , p. 348 . [117] See par. 64 of the present judgement. [118] D-14. [119] See par. 33 of the present judgement. [120] P-4. [121] SC-12, 3 rd paragraph. [122] art. 1604 al. 1 C.c.Q. [123] Verville v. 9146-7308 Québec inc. , 2008 QCCA 1593 , par. 56 . [124] Chahrouri v. Gazaille , 2011 QCCS 3911 , par. 177 . [125] Pierre-Gabriel Jobin et Michelle Cumyn, La vente , 3 e éd., Cowansville, EYB, 2007, no 180, p. 239-240. [126] 2019 QCCS 1086 , par. 285 . [127] See articles 18 to 22, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56, 341 and 342. [128] Code of Civil Procedure , art. 51. [129] Denis Ferland et Benoît Emery, Précis de procédure civile du Québec , vol.1, 5 e éd., Montréal, EYB, 2015, p. 248, par. 561. [130] 2018 QCCS 1140 . [131] art. 1375 C.c.Q : The parties shall conduct themselves in good faith both at the time the obligation arises and at the time it is performed or extinguished. [132] Supra note 12 , pp. 37-39 . [133] Ibid, p. 89 . [134] Supra note 12 , pp. 116-120 . [135] D-27. [136] Ibid. [137] Ibid. [138] See par. 77 of the present judgement. [139] Supra note 10 , p.38. [140] Supra note 129 , par. 1129. [141] [2007] QCCA 915, p. 10. [142] 2019 QCCA 2116 .\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs1688-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.01972835510969162, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs1688-en\", \"title\": \"Guo c. Caon, 2022 QCCS 1688 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs1688-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.01972835510969162, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs1688-en\"}, \"token_count\": 15326, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2022qccs1688-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq1502\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq1502-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Robertson c. Minkoff, 2014 QCCQ 1502 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2014 QCCQ 1502 (CanLII) | Robertson c. Minkoff | CanLII Robertson c. Minkoff, 2014 QCCQ 1502 (CanLII) Date: 2014-02-27 File number: 550-32-020859-135 Citation: Robertson c. Minkoff, 2014 QCCQ 1502 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/g64gf > Robertson c. Minkoff 2014 QCCQ 1502 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF GATINEAU TOWN OF GATINEAU Civil Division No: 550-32-020859-135 DATE: 27 février 2014 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE PATSY BOUTHILLETTE, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ KIMBERLEY ANN ROBERTSON , […], Gatineau (Québec) […] Plaintiff v. STEPHEN MINKOFF AND DANA PEMENT , […], Ottawa (Ontario) […] Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ INTRODUCTION [ 1 ] Mrs. Robertson is claiming an amount of 7,000$ from Mr. Minkoff and Mrs. Pement for latent defects discovered after buying their property. [ 2 ] Mr. Minkoff and Mrs. Pement state that if there are some defects, they are minors and were apparent at the time of the sale. QUESTION IN DISPUTE [ 3 ] The Court has to decide if the property has latent defects at the time of the sale. [ 4 ] If so, what would be the amount of the damages due to Mrs. Robertson? APPLICABLE LAW «1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer. 1739. A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective may give notice in writing of the defect to the seller only within a reasonable time after discovering it. The time begins to run, where the defect appears gradually, on the day that the buyer could have suspected the seriousness and extent of the defect. The seller may not invoke tardy notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it.» [ 5 ] To decide if the property has latent defects the Court has to determine if: ▪ the defect is latent and exists at the time of the sale; ▪ the defect is serious enough ▪ the defect is unknown to the buyer THE FACTS [ 6 ] The house is sold on August 3 rd , 2012 by Mr. Minkoff and Mrs. Pement to Mrs. Robertson. [ 7 ] Mrs. Robertson proceeded with an inspection of the house by a professional. [ 8 ] According to her, the inspector is unable to discover the defects at the time of the inspection. [ 9 ] On the 23 rd of August 2012, Mrs. Robertson acknowledges certain problems with the property, namely: a)         the dishwasher is broken b)         the indicator light for the Bi-Energy thermostat is broken c)         the seal from a window is broken d)         the basement fan do not vent out e)         there are some holes that were drilled to drain water from the backyard into the garage [ 10 ] On August 31 st 2012, Mrs. Robertson sends a letter to Mr. Minkoff and Mrs. Pement to inform them of the problems she is experiencing with her new property. ANALYSIS T he defects: Dishwasher [ 11 ] The appliances were sold with the house without any warranty. Furthermore, the proof presented to the Court shows that the dishwasher was working at the time of the sale. Bi-Energy System [ 12 ] Mrs. Robertson explains that Mr. Minkoff told her that the light over the thermostat for the Bi-Energy was not working at the time of the visit. [ 13 ] Mr. Minkoff explains to Mrs Robertson that they never used the system. [ 14 ] The inspector or Mrs. Robertson in prudent and diligent buyer, should have asked more questions concerning the fact that the vendors never used the system. Questions should have been asked concerning the agreement with Hydro Quebec the cost of those services. [ 15 ] This defect could have been found before the sale of the house without any major investigation. The window [ 16 ] It is admitted that the window was covered by something at the time of the visit of the house by the inspector. [ 17 ] The problem could not have been found at the time of the visit. [ 18 ] Mrs. Robertson is claiming an amount of 350.67$ for the replacement of the said window. [ 19 ] The Court has to decide if the defect is serious enough.  In regard to the value of the house and the general aspect of it. The Court does not qualify the defect as a serious defect. The bathroom fan [ 20 ] There is no proof of the cost to repair this problem, even if the defect exists. Slope of the land [ 21 ] Upon inspection the inspector declares that there is a slope and that it will have to be taken care of. [ 22 ] The fact that Mrs. Robertson did not realise the extent of the problem is not a justification. [ 23 ] More investigations should have been done concerning this matter. The defect was not latent at the time of the sale. CONCLUSION [ 24 ] According to articles 2803 and 2804 C.c.q., Mrs. Robertson has the burden to prove the presence of hidden defects according to the law, she did not. «2803. A person wishing to assert a right shall prove the facts on which his claim is based. A person who alleges the nullity, modification or extinction of a right shall prove the facts on which he bases his allegation. 2804. Evidence is sufficient if it renders the existence of a fact more probable than its non-existence, unless the law requires more convincing proof. FOR THESE GROUNDS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the Plaintiff's claim, CONDEMNS the plaintiffs to the costs in the amount of 156 $. __________________________________ PATSY BOUTHILLETTE, J.C.Q. Date of hearing: December 20, 2013....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq1502-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.021880343556404114, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq1502-en\", \"title\": \"Robertson c. Minkoff, 2014 QCCQ 1502 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq1502-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.021880343556404114, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq1502-en\"}, \"token_count\": 1550, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq1502-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq2348\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq2348-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Loiselle c. Yablonsky, 2009 QCCQ 2348 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2009 QCCQ 2348 (CanLII) | Loiselle c. Yablonsky | CanLII Loiselle c. Yablonsky, 2009 QCCQ 2348 (CanLII) Date: 2009-02-27 File number: 500-32-095296-069 Citation: Loiselle c. Yablonsky, 2009 QCCQ 2348 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/22vfh > Loiselle c. Yablonsky 2009 QCCQ 2348 COURT OF QUEBEC « Small Claims Division» CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL « Civil Division » N° : 500-32-095296-069 DATE : February 27, 2009 ______________________________________________________________________ IN THE PRESENCE OF THE HONOURABLE SUZANNE HANDMAN, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ JOCELYN LOISELLE -and- ANDRÉE BOUCHARD Plaintiffs vs. BETTY YABLONSKY Defendant/Plaintiff in warranty -and- JOEL PINSKY and KATHERINE PINSKY CHERYL BABOUSHKIN STEIN MICHAEL ZUNENSHINE BÉATRICE CABRAL SONIA BERTHELET YVON BERTHELET PETER ELLIOT Defendants in warranty ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Jocelyn Loiselle and Andrée Bouchard, the current owners of the property in question, sued Betty Yablonsky for $7,000, alleging that the home they bought from her is affected by a hidden defect, namely pyrite. [ 2 ] Mrs. Yablonsky, who had bought this home from Katherine Murray Pinsky and Joel Pinsky, denied responsibility and called in her vendors in warranty. [ 3 ] Mrs. Pinsky, in turn, denied responsibility and sued her vendor, namely Cheryl Baboushkin Stein in warranty. [ 4 ] Michael Zunenshine, the representative of the contractor, Belcourt Construction Company, was called in warranty by Mrs. Pinsky. In addition, Mrs. Pinsky called the following persons in warranty: Béatrice Cabral, Sonia Berthelet and Yvon Berthelet, real estate agents, and Peter Elliot, building inspector. The Question in Issue: [ 5 ] The issue is whether the pyrite that Plaintiffs discovered after they purchased their house was apparent or whether it constituted a hidden defect, for which their vendor is liable. The responsibility of successive sellers is also in issue. The evidence: [ 6 ] Plaintiffs, Jocelyn Loiselle and Andrée Bouchard, bought their home from Betty Yablonsky on May 22, 2003, conditional upon a building inspection. [ 7 ] Plaintiffs' building inspector, Peter Elliot, found a number of cracks. Plaintiffs were concerned about the possibility of pyrite but did not investigate for this substance. The real estate agents of the vendor and purchaser considered that had pyrite existed in a house over 40 years old, it would have already appeared. Moreover, the vendors had indicated that the cracks were stable. [ 8 ] The cracks were repaired, by Mr. Elliot's company, Acton Injection Inc.. A year later, a crack appeared in the wall in the same place. Mr. Loiselle covered it with cement but the following year, in 2005, the crack reappeared. [ 9 ] Mr. Loiselle then contacted Inspec Sol Inc. to test for pyrite. Inspec Sol confirmed the presence of this substance in the property's basement and garage. [ 10 ] Extensive repairs were involved to remove the filler material and reconstruct the basement and garage. The total cost came to $51,019.55 less a grant of $11,400, which Plaintiffs received from the City. Plaintiffs are seeking compensation from their vendors. a) The background: [ 11 ] Belcourt Construction Company built the house, now owned by Plaintiffs. It sold the property to Mrs. Baboushkin (Stein) in September 1967. Her husband did not recall any cracks in the property when they lived in the house. No one had heard about pyrite then. [ 12 ] Mrs. Baboushkin Stein sold her home to Joel and Katherine Pinsky in 1979. Contrary to Mr. Stein's testimony, Mrs. Pinsky claimed that when she and her husband purchased their home, there were numerous cracks, including spider cracks on the garage floor, as well as cracks on the floor of the unfinished side of the basement; the other half was covered by panelling and carpet. The Pinskys were told by the inspector that this was normal home settling. No one considered pyrite at that time. [ 13 ] The Pinskys sold their property to Morris Grintuch and Betty Yablonsky Grintuch on January 4, 1993. Mrs. Yablonsky noted a crack in one of the basement walls. Shortly after moving into her home, Mrs. Yablonsky suffered water infiltration in the basement. She contacted her vendors (the Pinskys), who hired Mr. Elliot. He repaired a crack, situated behind the wood paneling in the basement. [ 14 ] Mrs. Yablonsky had no further leaks or problems. She was unaware of pyrite in her home. Mrs. Yablonsky, who acquired her husband’s half ownership in the home, sold the property to the Plaintiffs in May 2003. At the time, Mrs. Yablonsky had advised Mr. Loiselle that the cracks had been stable. [ 15 ] Plaintiffs hired Amerispec to inspect their home. Mr. Elliot, who was then working for Amerispec, carried out what he described as a limited building inspection. It took place in the winter, with snow blocking a large part of the foundation. [ 16 ] Mr. Elliot had noted several cracks in the concrete slab of the garage floor and in the garage foundation walls. He was not alarmed; cracks in themselves are not indicative of pyrite since 70% to 80% of foundations crack and every basement has cracks. [ 17 ] The signs indicating the presence of pyrite are swelling of the floor slab and spider web cracking as well as efflorescence on the floor perimeter. Mr. Elliot maintained that when he did his inspection, the signs were not sufficient for him to recommend pyrite testing; the slab was not significantly swollen and the wall was not displaced laterally. b) The nature of pyrite: [ 18 ] Pyrite is a substance that oxidizes in the presence of water. The greater the amount of water, the more quickly the oxidization occurs. Because of this process, pyrite will swell. [ 19 ] Pyrite pushes laterally and causes cracks in the foundation walls. The gyproc deforms and cracks. The cement slab will swell and follow the movement and it will crack as well. [ 20 ] The phenomenon is slow; the movement varies from .2 to 1 mm per year. The first signs generally appear after 10 years. Measurements concern the potential for swelling (the “IPPG”). The accepted standard is a maximum index of 10. The thickness of the concrete slab is also important. The national building code's standard is 75 mm. c) The expert reports: [ 21 ] Inspec Sol produced a report at Plaintiffs' request. It found the IPPG index in the basement to be 13 and concluded that there was a small risk of expansion from the pyrite. The index in the garage was found to be 30, which led it to conclude that the potential for expansion was from small to average: “L'examen pétrographique de l'échantillon de pierre concassée nette prélevé sous la dalle de plancher du sous-sol a permis de calculer un nombre prétrographique de 159 et un indice IPPG de 13. À la lumière des résultats obtenus…et en supposant que le remblai granulaire est uniforme, nous confirmons que le remblai sous-jacent à la dalle du sous-sol présentent (sic) un potentiel de gonflement d'origine pyrétique faible. (…) L'examen pétrographique de l'échantillon de pierre concassée prélevé sous la dalle de plancher du garage a révélé la présence d'une quantité appréciable (25%) de shale, laquelle roche est reconnue pour son potentiel de gonflement et ses faibles propriétés mécaniques. L'analyse a permis de calculer un nombre pétrographique de 249 et un indice IPPG de 30. À la lumière des résultats obtenus…et en supposant que le remblai granulaire est uniforme, nous confirmons que le remblai sous-jacent à la dalle du garage présente un potentiel de gonflement d'origine pyrétique faible à moyen.” [ 22 ] Terratech's report, done at the request of Defendant, Mrs. Yablonsky, found a thickness of 140 mm with an IPPG index of 6 in the basement and concluded there to be a negligible risk of expansion due to the pyrite. The material in the garage had a thickness of 820 mm and the index was found to be 22, which led Terratech to conclude that the potential for expansion due to pyrite was from small to average: “Au sous-sol, les matériaux granulaires recoupés sur une épaisseur de 140 mm présentent un IPPG de 6, ce qui classifie les matériaux granulaires comme ayant un potentiel de gonflement négligeable dû à la présence de pyrite. (…) Les matériaux granulaires récupérés sous la dalle du garage présentent, quant à eux, un potentiel de gonflement faible à moyen dû à la présence de pyrite. Nous sommes d'avis que les désordres observés peuvent être, en tout ou en partie, reliés au gonflement du remblai ainsi qu'à la sulfatation de la dalle de béton et que ces désordres pourraient continuer à évoluer dans le temps.” [ 23 ] Alain Blanchette, a civil engineer and geologist, in charge of projects at Terratech, had noted three cracks on the exterior foundation wall, cracks in the cement floor slab inside the basement and five (5) mm of unevenness. He concluded, based on the results that the basement was not sufficiently affected to justify the work done. [ 24 ] The situation in the garage was different. The defects were significant and worrisome. The cement slab floor had swollen and it was uneven. There were several cracks in the concrete slab as well as four cracks in the foundation walls of the garage. He also found that the exterior wall had displaced laterally by 15 mm, which is typical of the effects of pyrite. The index measured was 22 and the thickness was 820 mm. [ 25 ] He concluded that work was required to deal with the pyrite affecting the garage. In his view, this problem existed from the time that the house had been constructed. He estimated that the cost of the repairs would run from $4000 to $7000. Analysis: [ 26 ] Much of the evidence centered on the existence of pyrite, its measurement and its evolution. [ 27 ] While the evidence was contradictory as to the amount of pyrite in the house and the need to repair the basement, there is no dispute that pyrite was found and that the garage required repair. [ 28 ] Before deciding whether the repairs were needed and their cost, it first must be determined whether pyrite constituted a hidden defect. [ 29 ] A vendor is responsible for hidden defects in property that he or she has sold. The principles governing such defects are established by the Civil code of Quebec and have on numerous occasions been dealt with by our jurisprudence. [ 30 ] Section 1726 of the Civil Code of Quebec reads as follows: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. [ 31 ] A seller is required to guaranty that, at the time of sale, the property is free from latent defects, which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or, which diminish its usefulness such that the buyer would not have bought the property or would not have paid so high a price, had he been aware of them. [ 32 ] The defect must have certain gravity. It must constitute a problem that a prudent and diligent purchaser could not discover. [ 33 ] The legal guarantee does not extend to hidden defects that are known to the purchaser or that are apparent. A defect that escapes the notice of an inexperienced buyer is considered apparent when its existence could be detected by someone more competent; the possibility of discovering a defect is sufficient to render the problem apparent rather than hidden. [ 34 ] The foregoing principles are applicable in the present case. ******** [ 35 ] Mrs. Pinsky submitted that having already repaired the foundation in 1993, Mr. Elliot, as a prudent and diligent professional, having noted the presence of cracking during his 2003 inspection, should have recommended a pyrite inspection. [ 36 ] For Mr. Blanchette, the number of cracks, the displacement of the wall and the swelling of the concrete slab were indications of a problem related to the presence of pyrite. He was of the view that at the time of the 2003 inspection, the wall displacement would have been noticeable and that the cracked foundation wall warranted that an expertise be carried out to determine the cause of the cracks. [ 37 ] However, he admitted that the cracks on the walls were not automatically symptomatic of pyrite and could be caused by many different factors. While the prime indicator was the wall displacement between two cracks, he acknowledged that because of snow covering the garage wall's foundation, some of the cracks on the exterior wall of the garage might not have been visible. [ 38 ] In light of the evidence, the Court cannot conclude that there were signs that should have raised suspicions as to the existence of pyrite when Plaintiffs purchased their home. [ 39 ] The conditions governing an action for hidden defects are present: the defect was not apparent; it was not possible for a prudent and diligent purchaser to have noticed this problem and the existence of pyrite constitutes a serious problem that existed prior to the sale. [ 40 ] Both expert reports establish the existence of pyrite in the basement and in the garage. The difference relates to the index found and as to the gravity of the problem. In the case of the basement, Inspec Sol, which measured an IPPG of 13, concluded that the risk of expansion from the pryrite was small while Terratech, having noted an index of 6, concluded there to be a negligible risk of expansion. [ 41 ] In the garage, while the IPPG noted by Inspec Sol was 30 and that noted by Terratech was 22, both expert reports considered the potential for expansion of pyrite to range from small to average. [ 42 ] In sum, the evidence indicates that the problem of pyrite affecting the garage was sufficiently serious to require repair. The estimated value was $4,000 to $7,000. However, Plaintiffs have spent over $50,000 to repair the basement and garage. [ 43 ] Plaintiffs have established to the satisfaction of the Court their entitlement to the sum of $7,000 against Defendant, Betty Yablonsky, which is the maximum amount foreseen by the provisions of Book VIII of the Code of Civil Procedure governing small claims. The actions in Warranty: [ 44 ] The conditions that are applicable to recourse for hidden defects apply in the same manner to the actions in warranty that each party has instituted against his or her vendor. [ 45 ] Defendant, Mrs. Yablonsky who is responsible for the damages suffered by Plaintiffs, instituted an action in warranty against the Pinskys. Mrs. Pinsky, in turn, instituted an action in warranty against Mrs. Baboushkin Stein. Ultimately Michael Zunenshine, the representative of the contractor, Belcourt Construction Company, was called in warranty. [ 46 ] There are successive sellers involved in this case. As the Court of Appeal held in the case of Hay c. Jacques [1] , each vendor must assume the warranty of the hidden defects resulting from his deed of sale, irrespective of when the manifestations of the defects appeared. “Les vices cachés à la base de l'action intentée en 1984 existaient-ils lors des ventes antérieures? Dans l'affirmative, chacun de vendeurs doit assumer la garantie des vices cachés résultant de son acte de vente, sans égard, en théorie du moins, à la date où les manifestations de ces vices sont apparues.” [ 47 ] Honourable Joseph R. Nuss explained, referring to the Supreme Court judgment of Kravitz [2] , that each sub-purchaser of a given property acquires the claim of legal warranty against latent defects owed by the first vendor to the first purchaser: “…when a sub-purchaser acquires ownership of the thing he becomes the creditor of the legal warranty against latent defects owed by the first seller to the first purchaser….the claim is transferred to the sub-purchaser in its entirety…” [ 48 ] Each sub purchaser may take action against his vendor or against the first seller [3] . [ 49 ] In the present case, the Defendant, Mrs. Yablonsky, has a valid claim...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\nclaim of legal warranty against latent defects owed by the first vendor to the first purchaser: “…when a sub-purchaser acquires ownership of the thing he becomes the creditor of the legal warranty against latent defects owed by the first seller to the first purchaser….the claim is transferred to the sub-purchaser in its entirety…” [ 48 ] Each sub purchaser may take action against his vendor or against the first seller [3] . [ 49 ] In the present case, the Defendant, Mrs. Yablonsky, has a valid claim against the Pinskys. Mrs. Pinsky has a valid claim against Mrs. Baboushkin Stein. Mrs. Baboushkin Stein was the first purchaser, having bought the property from Belcourt Construction Company. [ 50 ] Normally, the first seller would be liable for the damages involved. Michael Zunenshine was sued. However, he was not the first vendor. It was his company, Belcourt Construction Company, that sold the property. Therefore, no action can be maintained against Mr. Zunenshine. As for his company, it went bankrupt many years ago and it no longer is in existence. [ 51 ] In light of the foregoing, the subsequent seller in the chain becomes Mrs. Baboushkin Stein and she is liable for the damages suffered by Plaintiffs. [ 52 ] In addition to the foregoing, an action was taken against Peter Elliot, as building inspector. This claim cannot be upheld in light of the conclusion that the pyrite constituted a hidden defect. The claims against the real estate agents, Béatrice Cabral, Sonia Berthelet and Yvon Berthelet, are also unfounded for the same reason. [ 53 ] Finally, Mr. Elliot requested that his costs be reimbursed. However, since he is a Defendant in warranty, he is not entitled to costs. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: GRANTS the action of Jocelyn Loiselle and Andrée Bouchard against Betty Yablonsky; CONDEMNS Betty Yablonsky to pay Jocelyn Loiselle and Andrée Bouchard the sum of $7,000 with interest at the legal rate and the additional indemnity foreseen by article 1619 of the Civil code of Quebec since the institution of the action, plus the judicial fees of  $155; GRANTS the action in warranty of Betty Yablonsky against Joel Pinsky and Katherine Pinsky; CONDEMNS Joel Pinsky and Katherine Pinsky, solidarily, to compensate Betty Yablonsky for all sums that she is required to pay in virtue of this judgment; GRANTS the action in warranty of Katherine Pinsky against Cheryl Baboushkin Stein; CONDEMNS Cheryl Baboushkin Stein to compensate Katherine Pinsky for all sums that she is required to pay in virtue of this judgment; DISMISSES the action of Katherine Pinsky against Michael Zunenshine; DISMISSES the action of Katherine Pinsky against Sonia and Yvon Berthelet; DISMISSES the action of Katherine Pinsky against Béatrice Cabral; DISMISSES the action of Katherine Pinsky against Peter Elliot. THE WHOLE , with costs. __________________________________ Suzanne Handman, J.C.Q. Dates of hearing: May 29, and October 29, 2007, October 27, 2008 [1] (1999) R.J.Q. 2318 . [2] General Motors Products of Canada Limited v. Leo Kravitz , (1979) 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , 1 S.C.R. 790. [3] CDL 7000 Holdings L.P. c. Scanaxa, S.A. et al. , 2004 CanLII 20726 (QC CS) , 500-17-016711-031, June 10, 2004 (S.C.).\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq2348-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.019813518971204758, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq2348-en\", \"title\": \"Loiselle c. Yablonsky, 2009 QCCQ 2348 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq2348-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.019813518971204758, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq2348-en\"}, \"token_count\": 4945, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2009qccq2348-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs6010\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs6010-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Abou-Khalil c. Sheinberg, 2019 QCCS 6010 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2019 QCCS 6010 (CanLII) | Abou-Khalil c. Sheinberg | CanLII Abou-Khalil c. Sheinberg, 2019 QCCS 6010 (CanLII) Date: 2019-07-19 File number: 500-17-106403-192 Citation: Abou-Khalil c. Sheinberg, 2019 QCCS 6010 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/jm554 > Abou-Khalil c. Sheinberg 2019 QCCS 6010 SUPERIOR COURT (Civil division) CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL No:         500-17-106403-192 DATE: July 19, 2019 BY THE HONOURABLE PATRICK BUCHHOLZ, J.S.C. JULIANA ABOU-KHALIL and DAVID MEISELS Plaintiffs V. LESLI SHEINBERG and AVI WINKLER Defendants/Plaintiffs in warranty V. NORA ALIXANIAN and VAHE SETRAKIAN Defendants/Plaintiffs in sub-warranty and MAIDASHIER Defendant in sub-warranty and DANIEL SOLOV and L'OFFICIER DE LA PUBLICITÉ DES DROITS DE LA CIRCONSCRIPTION FONCIÈRE DE MONTRÉAL Mis en cause JUDGMENT 1. OVERVIEW AND CONTEXT [ 1 ] By her amended application dated July 3, 2019, the Defendant in sub-warranty seeks the dismissal of the \\\"Acte d'intervention forcé en arrière-garantie amendé\\\" dated June 28, 2019 filed by the Plaintiffs in sub-warranty, which seeks to bring her into a suit commenced by the Principal Plaintiffs in January of this year. [ 2 ] The Principal Plaintiffs have sued the Defendants on the basis of the warranty against latent defects after discovering an oil contamination on their Côte St-Luc property situated at 5614 Greenwood Avenue (the \\\"Property'') which they purchased in July 2017 for the price of $760,000. [ 3 ] According to the Principal Plaintiffs' originating application1, approximately 160 tons of contaminated soil will need to be removed from the Property 2 . [ 4 ] The Principal Plaintiffs' suit seeks the nullity of the 2017 purchase, or alternatively, damages in excess of $450,000. [ 5 ] The Defendants acquired the Property from the Defendants in warranty/Plaintiffs in sub-warranty in 2011. [ 6 ] The Defendants in warranty/Plaintiffs in sub-warranty purchased the Property from the Defendant in sub-warranty in 1978. This is the sale that is at issue in this proceeding. [ 7 ] According to the court record, all of the above sales were transacted with legal warranty. 1 \\\"Demande introductive d'instance en nullité d'une vente en raison de vice caché et dommages\\\" (the \\\"DII\\\"). 2 DII, para. 36. 2. LEGAL PRINCIPLES [8] To succeed with her application to dismiss, the Defendant in sub-warranty must establish that the suit commenced against her is unfounded at law even if the alleged facts are true 3 • [9] The alleged liability of the Defendant in sub-warranty is based on the warranties against legal eviction and latent defects as they existed at the time of the sale in 1978 4 : Art. 1508. The seller is obliged by law to warrant the buyer against eviction of the whole or any part of the thing sold, by reason of the act of the former, or of any right existing at the time of the sale, and against incumbrances not declared and not apparent at the time of the sale. [ ... ] Art. 1522. The seller is obliged by law to warrant the buyer against such latent defects in the thing sold, and its accessories, as render it unfit for the use for which it was intended, or so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it, or would not have given so large a price, if he had known them. [10] lt is well established that, upon hearing a preliminary application to dismiss on the basis of a proceeding being unfounded in law, courts must be prudent. [11] ln the Supreme Court of Canada decision of Canada (Attorney-General) v. Confédération des syndicats nationaux5, Justices Lebel and Wagner wrote about how such prudence requires that the lack of basis in law be \\\"plain and obvious\\\": [17] Dismissing an action at a preliminary stage can have very serious consequences, however.   The courts must therefore be cautious in exercising this power. As a result, an action will not be dismissed at this point in the proceedings unless it is plain and obvious that it lacks a basis in law (Bohémier v. Barreau du Québec, 2012 QCCA 308 (CanLII) , at para. 17 ; Ville de Hampstead v. Jardins Tuileries Ltée, 1991 CanLII 3170 (QC CA) , [1992] R.D.J. 163 (C.A.) ; Cheung v. Borsellino, 2005 QCCA 865 (CanLII) ; Association provinciale des constructeurs d'habitations du Québec inc. v. Société d'habitation et de développement de Montréal, 2011 QCCA 1033 (CanLII) ). [18] ln this regard, the Quebec Court of Appeal noted that, [TRANSLATION] \\\"given the serious consequences of dismissing an action without considering it on its merits, litigation should not be ended at an early stage on a motion to dismiss absent a situation that is plain and obvious\\\" (Entreprises Pelletier & Garon (Toitures inc.) v. Agropur Coopérative, 2010 QCCA 244 , [2010] R.D.I. 24, at para. 4 (emphasis added)). 3 Art. 168 (2) Code of Civil Procedure, CQLR, c. C-25.01 (\\\"C.C.P\\\") 4 Art. 1508 and 1522 Civil Code of Lower Canada (\\\"C.C.L.C.\\\"). 5 [2014] 2 SCR 477 , 2014 SCC 49 (CanLll) , para. 17-21 . [19] This \\\"plain and obvious\\\" situation that opens the door to the dismissal of an action must be apparent from the allegations set out in the motion to institute proceedings and the exhibits filed in support of it (Groupe Jeunesse Inc.; R. v. Québec (Société des alcools), 1998 Canlll 13129 (Que. C.A.); Saint-Eustache (Ville de) v. Régie intermunicipale Argenteuil Deux-Montagnes, 2011 QCCA 227 (Canlll)). [20] However, although the tacts alleged in the motion must be assumed to be true (Westmount (City) v. Rossy, 2012 sec 30, 2012 SCC 30 (CanLII) , [2012] 2 S.C.R. 136, at para. 15 ; Oznaga v. Société d'exploitation des loteries et courses du Québec, [1981] 2 S.C.R. 113), the court is not bound by the legal characterization of those facts (Gillet v. Arthur, 2004 CanLII 47873 (QC CA) , [2005] R.J.Q. 42 (C.A.), at para. 25 ; Racine v. Harvey, 2005 QCCA 879 (Canlll), at para. 10 ; Société des alcools; Bohémier , at para. 17 ). [21] As a result, a judge deciding whether an action should be dismissed must determine whether the allegations of tact set out in the motion to institute proceedings are [TRANSLATION] \\\"of such a nature as to open the door to the conclusions being sought\\\" by the applicant (Association provinciale des constructeurs d'habitations du Québec inc., at para. 14). ln every case, the dismissal of the action will be appropriate only if all the facts to be considered are set out in the motion to institute proceedings and there is no question that the relevant legal rule applies to them (H. Reid and C. Carrier, Code de procédure civile du Québec: jurisprudence et doctrine (30th ed. 2014), at para. 165/200). [12] The principles to be applied on an application for dismissal based on article 168(2) C.C.P. were set out in a number of cases, notably the decisions of the Court of Appeal in Gauthier, and Bohémier 7 . Those principles are as follows (excerpt from Gauthier, para. 14): • Les allégations de la requête introductive d'instance sont tenues pour avérées, ce qui comprend les pièces déposées à son soutien; • Seuls les faits allégués doivent être tenus pour avérés et non pas la qualification de ces faits par le demandeur; • Le tribunal n'a pas à décider des chances de succès du demandeur ni du bien-fondé des faits allégués. Il appartient au juge du fond de décider, après avoir entendu la preuve et les plaidoiries, si les allégations de faits ont été prouvées; • Le tribunal doit déclarer l'action recevable si les allégations de la requête introductive d'instance sont susceptibles de donner éventuellement ouverture aux conclusions recherchées; 6 Fanous c. Gauthier, 2018 QCCA 293 (Canlll) . 7 Bohémier v. Barreau du Québec , 2012 QCCA 308 (Canlll) . • La requête en irrecevabilité n'a pas pour but de décider avant procès des prétentions légales des parties. Son seul but est de juger si les conditions de la procédure sont solidaires des faits allégués, ce qui nécessite un examen explicite mais également implicite du droit invoqué; • On ne peut rejeter une requête en irrecevabilité sous prétexte qu'elle soulève des questions complexes; • En matière d'irrecevabilité, un principe de prudence s'applique. Dans l'incertitude, il faut éviter de mettre fin prématurément à un procès. • En cas de doute, il faut laisser au demandeur la chance d'être entendu au fond. 3. ANALYSIS [13] Applying these legal principles to the matter at hand, there are certainly allegations in the proceedings that the source of the contamination or the contamination itself may have existed at the time the Defendant in sub-warranty sold her home in 1978 8 . For the purposes of the present application, the court must presume those allegations to be true. [14] Defendant in sub-warranty's attorney argues that the contamination in question cannot be a latent defect or a legal defect of eviction as there were no enforceable environmental norms at the time of the sale in 1978. lt is true that certain general environmental norms and more specific environmental regulations only came later and cannot be applied to a sale that occurred in 1978 to argue legal eviction. [15] That being said, section 20 of the Environmental Quality Act9 was in force at the time of the sale and it is not \\\"plain and obvious\\\" to the Court that its application cannot lead to liability under section 1508 C.C.L.C. in this case 10 . ln Goulet v. Gauthier 11 , justice Beaupré recently discussed the issue in this manner: [93) Les demandeurs en garantie Roy et Gauthier, pour contrer l'inexistence du Règlement ou de la Politique de 1998 lors de leur transaction de 1994 avec madame Roy, invoquent la prohibition générale de contaminer contenue à l'article 20 LQE , entrée en vigueur en 1972. Ils proposent qu'il s'agit là d'une limitation de droit public que leur vendeuse Roy a violé en leur vendant un terrain contaminé aux hydrocarbures lors de leur transaction de 1994. 8 See for example para. 5 of the \\\"Acte d'intervention forcée en arrière-garantie amendé\\\". 9 Environment Quality Act, caLR c. a-2. 10 Goulet v. Gauthier, 2018 aCCS 3236; see also 9046-6640 Québec Inc. V. Bastien, 2019 QCCS 1054 , para. 35-36 . 11 Goulet v. Gauthier, 2018 aCCS 3236, para. 93-101. [94] Selon la procureure de Roy, le préjudice à la qualité du sol au sens de l'article 20 in fine de la LQE était en quelque sorte en période de latence et n'a été révélé que lors de la découverte des hydrocarbures par le demandeur principal Goulet en 2013. Elle ajoute que le Tribunal doit donc faire preuve de prudence avant de rejeter la demande en garantie de Roy et Gauthier, tout comme il doit aussi en toute logique faire preuve de prudence avant de rejeter les autres demandes en garantie en litige. [95] Cet argument a du poids, compte tenu de certains principes qu'il convient de considérer à la lumière de ceux précités applicables à l'examen d'une demande en irrecevabilité pour motif d'absence de fondement en droit. [96] Premièrement, avant qu'il ne soit nommé à cette Cour monsieur le juge Michel Yergeau, auteur reconnu en droit de l'environnement, écrivait ce qui suit dans son ouvrage Loi sur la qualité de l'environnement , texte annoté, au sujet de l'article 20 de la LQE: « On peut considérer l'article 20 comme étant la pierre angulaire de la Loi. C'est lui qui impose une prohibition générale de contaminer ou polluer l'environnement. L'ensemble de la Loi s'articule autour de cette prohibition, (...) [ ... ] On notera que la prohibition s'applique autant à celui qui émet, dégage, dépose ou rejette dans l'environnement, qu'à celui qui permet d'émettre, déposer, dégager ou rejeter un contaminant. » [97] Selon Yergeau, trois questions permettent de déterminer s'il y a eu contravention à l'article 20 : « - Y a-t-il eu dégagement dans l'environnement? - Les matières dégagées constituaient-elles des contaminants? - La présence de ces contaminants dans l'environnement est-elle susceptible de causer un des préjudices énumérés à la fin de l'article? » [98] Rappelons le libellé de la fin de l'article 20 : « La (...) prohibition s'applique à l'émission, au dépôt, au dégagement ou au rejet de tout contaminant, dont la présence dans l'environnement est prohibée par règlement du gouvernement ou est susceptible de porter atteinte à la vie, à la santé, à la sécurité, au bien-être ou au confort de l'être humain, de causer du dommage ou de porter autrement préjudice à la qualité du sol , à la végétation, à la faune ou aux biens . » [99] Deuxièmement, rappelons que selon la Cour d'appel et la doctrine, la détermination de l'existence d'une violation de l'article 20 nécessite une évaluation et une appréciation, lesquelles, il faut en convenir, constituent en conséquence et à tout le moins des questions mixtes de droit et de fait. [100] Troisièmement, dans son opinion pour une formation unanime de la Cour d'appel dans l'arrêt Courses automobiles Mont-Tremblant inc. c. lredale, madame la juge Bich soulignait que le libellé de l'article 20 « ... est assez large et peut sans doute inclure l'idée de « capacité latente » tout comme elle inclut certainement un événement « occasionnel ». [101] Quatrièmement, les auteurs Jobin et Cumyn sont d'avis qu'une limitation de droit public découlant de la violation d'une loi ou d'un règlement en matière environnementale offre un recours à l'acheteur du bien sur la base de la garantie légale du droit de propriété prévue à l' article 1725 C.c.Q. , sans nécessité de démonstration d'une diminution de jouissance. L'acheteur a le fardeau de prouver les éléments constitutifs de la violation de la loi ou de la règlementation. Ces considérations pourraient aussi être applicables sous le régime de la garantie contre l'éviction afférente aux transactions conclues sous le C.c.B.-C. [16] I would add that it also not \\\"plain and obvious\\\" to this Court that the extent of the contamination alleged in this case or its source (for purposes of the analysis, presumably the cracked copper oil line) cannot be a latent defect under article 1522 C.C.L.C. [17] While it is true, as properly argued by counsel for the Defendant in sub-warranty, that a \\\"mere\\\" or \\\"simple\\\" contaminant or contamination is not necessarily a defect, the Court does not accept the argument that i) a crack in an oil-carrying copper line, or ii) an environmental contamination, could not in 1978, under circumstances as presently alleged, be either a latent or legal defect, or both. [18] The size and nature of the crack and the extent of the...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n2 C.C.L.C. [17] While it is true, as properly argued by counsel for the Defendant in sub-warranty, that a \\\"mere\\\" or \\\"simple\\\" contaminant or contamination is not necessarily a defect, the Court does not accept the argument that i) a crack in an oil-carrying copper line, or ii) an environmental contamination, could not in 1978, under circumstances as presently alleged, be either a latent or legal defect, or both. [18] The size and nature of the crack and the extent of the soil contamination, as they may have existed at the time of the applicable sale, are important questions of fact. And whether they are sufficient to be defects at law, in 1978, under articles 1508 or 1522 C.C.L.C. would be important questions of mixed tact and law that are best left to the trial judge. [19] The Courtis mindful of the Principal Plaintiffs' allegations that approximately 160 tons of soil will need to be removed and that the neighbor may also be affected by the contamination. As the court record stands, we are not in the presence of a mere contaminant or some negligible contamination that clearly provides no legal recourse against the Defendant in sub-warranty. The extent of the contamination is alleged to be significant and very costly to remove, the whole in relation, it should be said, to the value of the Property. [20] lt may well be that, at trial, the Defendant in sub-warranty will have no liability in this matter, but it would not be prudent to pre-emptively dismiss the case against her on the tacts as alleged in the file. 4. CONCLUSIONS [21] Given the analysis above, the Court will deny the application at issue. [22] For similar reasons, the Court also cannot conclude that the Plaintiffs in sub- warranty's \\\"Acte d'intervention force en arrière-garantie\\\" is abusive under article 51 C.C.P. WHEREFORE, THE COURT: [23] DISMISSES the Defendant in sub-warranty's application to dismiss; [24] THE WHOLE, with costs to follow suit. __________________________________ PATRICK BUCHHOLZ, J.C.S. Mtre. Andréanne Lavoie THERRIEN LAVOIE, AVOCATS S.E.N.C.R.L. For the Plaintiffs Mtre. François Goyer IMK S.E.N.C.R.L/IMK L.L.P. For the Defendants/Plaintiffs in sub-warranty Mtre. Patrick Lapierre Antoine Sabourin, stagiaire BLAKE, CASSELS & GRAYDON S.E.N.C.R.L. For the Defendant in sub-warranty Hearing date: July 5, 2019\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs6010-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02126545086503029, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs6010-en\", \"title\": \"Abou-Khalil c. Sheinberg, 2019 QCCS 6010 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs6010-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02126545086503029, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs6010-en\"}, \"token_count\": 4207, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2019qccs6010-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2023qccq1754\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2023qccq1754-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Agaton c. Singh, 2023 QCCQ 1754 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2023 QCCQ 1754 (CanLII) | Agaton c. Singh | CanLII Agaton c. Singh, 2023 QCCQ 1754 (CanLII) Date: 2023-03-23 File number: 760-32-701894-216 Citation: Agaton c. Singh, 2023 QCCQ 1754 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/jws3s > Agaton c. Singh 2023 QCCQ 1754 COURT OF QUEBEC « Small Claims Division » CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF BEAUHARNOIS LOCALITY OF SALABERRY-DE-VALLEYFIELD « Civil Chamber » N° : 760-32-701894-216 DATE : March 23, 2023 ______________________________________________________________________ PRESIDING: THE HONOURABLE CÉLINE GERVAIS, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ PAOLA AGATON Plaintiff c. SUKDEV SINGH and BALJINDER KAUR KHUN Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGEMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Paola Agaton claims from Sukdev Singh and Baljinder Kaur Khun the amount of $10,946, representing the damages suffered after the discovery of hidden defects in the house she bought from Mr. Singh and Mrs. Khun.  Those defects are related to leaks in the incoming water pipe and heating problems with the furnace. [ 2 ] In their contestation, Mr. Singh and Mrs. Khun state that they have never occupied the property, which was rented and that they were not aware of those problems.  They also plead that the inspection report recommended an inspection by camera as the house was older than 50  years.  They further plead that they could not do an expertise as the excavation was in progress when they received the Letter of Demand.  They also argue that Mrs. Agaton’s claim is not taking into consideration any depreciation as the pipe was in place since the house was built. THE FACTS: [ 3 ] On July 7, 2020, Mrs. Agaton buys Mr. Singh and Mrs. Khun’s house, located on [Street A] in Chateauguay, which was built in 1961, for an amount of $259,000.  The house was previously inspected by Inspections Bourkas Inc. who filed a written report (Exhibit P-9).  The price was reduced by $10,000 after the inspection. [ 4 ] As was said previously, Mr. Singh and Mrs. Khun have never lived in that house they bought in 2017. [ 5 ] Shortly after buying the house, Mrs. Agaton hired a contractor to do some repairs in the basement; he noticed leaking water during his work.  Mrs. Agaton called the City of Chateauguay who offers its citizens a free service to check pipes.  The City employee came with a camera and saw that there was an issue with the pipe because of a nearby tree, which was existing for many years. [ 6 ] The report issued by the City (Exhibit P-5) and dated July 30, 2020, states that:  “ we detected a water leak on the service entrance of your residence.  (…)  The inspection confirms that the leak is located on your property, between the curb stop and your property or inside the house, on your internal plumbing.  A notice will be sent shortly asking for the completion of the work necessary for the security of the network.” [ 7 ] The text messages filed as Exhibit P-7 show that Mr. Singh was aware of the situation as of August 21, 2020.  He spoke over the phone with Mrs. Agaton and confirmed that the situation was not covered by his home insurance.  Mrs. Agaton sent the estimate and the City report by text to Mr. Singh in early September. [ 8 ] The repairs were done on September 15, 2020, by Transport Verelli for a cost of $5,231.36 (Exhibit P-11). [ 9 ] On October 5, 2020, Mrs. Agaton’s attorney sends a Letter of Demand to Mr. Singh (Exhibit P-2).  Mr. Singh’s attorney responded on November 2, 2020, (Exhibit P-3) stating that Mrs. Agaton had not follow her inspector’s recommendation to have the pipes checked by camera and that the cause of the leak was not explained. [ 10 ] In their response dated November 20, 2020, Mrs. Agaton’s attorneys declare that another problem was discovered regarding the furnace which stopped working, as it had two heat elements working out of four.  The Demand said that an amount of $3,429.14 was paid to replace the furnace. [ 11 ] The Demand was filed on March 5, 2021.  Mrs. Agaton claims $10,946 which represents the costs paid to replace the pipe and the furnace, $1,217.66 paid to have the front yard leveled after the work performed on September 2020 as well as $1,000 for damages, trouble and inconvenience. ANALYSIS AND DECISION: [ 12 ] To conclude that there is a latent defect, the following four elements must be present: - The defect must be serious, i.e. it must have serious adverse effects on the use of the property and the buyer would not have paid so high a price had he been aware of the defect; - The buyer must not be aware of the defect at the time of the sale; - The defect must be hidden, i.e. it could not have been perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance; - The defect must have existed before the sale, since the warranty concerns the condition of the property at the time of the sale. [ 13 ] Besides those conditions, a notice has to be sent to the seller to inform him of the situation. [ 14 ] Section 1739 of the Quebec Civil Code reads as follows: “ 1739 . A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective shall give notice in writing of the defect to the seller within a reasonable time after discovering it. Where the defect appears gradually, the time begins to run on the day that the buyer could suspect the seriousness and extent of the defect. The seller may not invoke the tardiness of a notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it.” [ 15 ] The denouncing of the discovery of a hidden defect is essential to a buyer’s recourse against his seller. The Civil Code provides for such a step to give the seller some information about the hidden defect for which a claim is made, to evaluate the problem and to have it seen by an expert in order to help him make an offer, deny the claim, or have it repaired at his own costs. The water pipe [ 16 ] It seems that the broken pipe was the one first installed when the house was built in 1961 which means it was 59 years old.  The Court asked the City’s witness what was the lifespan of such a pipe.  He answered that, depending on the quality of the material used, a main water pipe could last 50 or 60 years. [ 17 ] There is a difference between a situation that constitutes a hidden defect and one which involves a house part that has reached the end of its lifespan.  The latter does not constitute a hidden defect. [1] As was explained in Krukowski c. Mellos [2] :  “ the wear of a property is not necessarily a hidden defect. Therefore, the loss of normal use is not a defect in the case where it results from wear, use and the age of the property .” [ 18 ] We have to conclude that the leaking pipe does not fall within the definition of a hidden defect, as it has arrived at the end of its lifespan. The furnace [ 19 ] At the hearing, Mr. Singh and Mrs. Khun said that the furnace had been replaced shortly before the sale of the house and that it was still guaranteed.  The Court asked them to send the documents to support their argument.  The Court received a sale contract signed on February 1, 2020, between them and AT Climatisation.  However, this contract was not related to the furnace but was for the installation of a cooling system with a HVAC compressor, filter, humidity control, which was connected to the furnace and the electric panel.  Mrs. Agaton’s claim is not in regard of that system. [ 20 ] The inspection report, on page 21, says the following about the electric forced air furnace:  “ the unit was installed in 1980.  This unit is functional however due to age, has a higher risk of failure.  Annual service is required.  A disposable 4 inch filter is recommended.  The duct system and furnace must be cleaned.  The evaporator coils are in good condition.” [ 21 ] No one testified at the hearing regarding the lifespan of a furnace, but it had probably been reached it, as it was 40 years old when it broke. [ 22 ] But there is a more important point.  This problem was not denounced to Mr. Singh and Mrs. Khun before the work was executed, contrary to section 1739 of the Quebec Civil Code , cited above. [ 23 ] In an urgent situation, a buyer could be excused from sending such a notice.  The repair of a furnace could be seen as such an emergency situation.  However, we note from the invoices filed as Exhibit P-8 that maintenance of the furnace was done on October 21, 2020.  The thermostat was changed on October 29 and the furnace was replaced on November 17, 2020. [ 24 ] There was enough time between October 21 and November 17 to inform Mr. Singh and Mrs. Khun that another problem had been discovered and give them the opportunity to come and examine the problem.  The situation was disclosed to them by the November 20, 2020 letter, at which time the furnace had already been replaced. [ 25 ] This claim cannot be granted. [ 26 ] Considering the circumstances, the Court dismisses Mrs. Agaton’s claim, each party paying their own costs. FOR ALL THESE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the Plaintiff's claim; THE WHOLE , each party paying their own legal costs. __________________________________ CÉLINE GERVAIS, J.C.Q. NOTICE TO THE PARTIES SECTION III EXTRACT FROM THE CODE OF CIVIL PROCEDURE OF QUÉBEC FILING OF PLEADINGS AND DOCUMENTS. 108 (2) Any document or real evidence that is filed in the record as an exhibit must remain in the record until the end of the proceeding, unless all the parties consent to its being removed.  Once the proceeding has ended, the parties must retrieve the exhibits they have filed; otherwise, the court clerk may destroy them one year after the date on which the judgment becomes final or the date of the pleading terminating the proceeding.  In either case, the chief justice or chief judge, if of the opinion that the exhibits can still be useful, may stay their destruction . [1] Jeffrey EDWARDS, La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois, 2 e éd., Montréal, Wilson & Lafleur, 2008, p. 155, no. 334. [2] 2013 QCCQ 1478 ....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2023qccq1754-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02034568041563034, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2023qccq1754-en\", \"title\": \"Agaton c. Singh, 2023 QCCQ 1754 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2023qccq1754-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.02034568041563034, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2023qccq1754-en\"}, \"token_count\": 2534, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2023qccq1754-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2010qccs4173\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2010qccs4173-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Zhang v. Palladini, 2010 QCCS 4173 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2010 QCCS 4173 (CanLII) | Zhang v. Palladini | CanLII Zhang v. Palladini, 2010 QCCS 4173 (CanLII) Date: 2010-06-30 File number: 500-17-031942-066 Other citation: AZ-50669949 Citation: Zhang v. Palladini, 2010 QCCS 4173 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/2chgt > Zhang v. Palladini 2010 QCCS 4173 JC1987 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL No: 500-17-031942-066 DATE: June 30, 2010 ______________________________________________________________________ IN THE PRESENCE OF: THE HONOURABLE SUZANNE COURTEAU, J.S.C. ______________________________________________________________________ XIAO DONG ZHANG SUN HONG Plaintiffs v. DOMINICO PALLADINI JOANNE ORTENZI ALANA PALLADINI RENATO NAVARRA ELLEN CHIARELLA Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Was the property at [...], Montreal, affected by latent defects at the time it was sold to Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun by the five Defendants? [ 2 ] Were the vendors bound by a contractual warranty declaring that the property did \\\" not derogate from the laws and regulations concerning the protection of the environment \\\"? THE RELEVANT FACTS [ 3 ] On June 27, 2002, Defendants sold an apartment building to Plaintiffs Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun. [ 4 ] In the Deed of sale, [1] the five vendors declared: \\\"16) The property does not derogate from the laws and regulations concerning the protection of the environment.\\\" [ 5 ] The property is a 3 storey residential building with 12 apartment units. [ 6 ] Defendants bought the building around 1990. [2] At that time, it was already heated by gas, seemingly installed around 1965. Previously, the property had been heated by oil, stored in an underground tank near the building. [ 7 ] Until 2005, the oil reservoir had remained in the ground, the pipes connected to the reservoir still installed on the rear outside wall of the building. [ 8 ] Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun visited the property with their real estate agent [3] and one of the owners, Renato Navarra, sometime in April 2002. They claim they did not walk around the building or go to the back; the previous oil heating system would not have been discussed. [ 9 ] Interested in buying the building, Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun hired a building inspector, Sunny Li, to inspect the property and advise them as to any defect or deficiency. [ 10 ] A three‑hour inspection was conducted by Mr. Li, in the presence of Mr. Zhang and Mr. Navarra. They walked outside, around the building, visited all the apartments, inspected the roof as well as  the laundry room, locker room and furnace room in the basement and the parking garages. [ 11 ] No written report of this inspection was filed in evidence, nor is the date of the inspection established. Xiao Dong Zhang claims he never received a written report. [ 12 ] The parties enter in negotiation with a Promise to Purchase , [4] submitted by Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun on April 25, 2002, for a price of $450,000. [ 13 ] Counterproposals are exchanged [5] between April 25 and May 4, where the vendors indicate their \\\" final \\\" price at $465,000. Two days later, on May 6, Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun make a \\\" final \\\" c ounterproposal [6] at $458,000. This and all previous documents indicate that the buyers wish to have the property inspected. [7] [ 14 ] The last counterproposal is accepted by the five vendors, [8] the next day, on May 7, 2002. [ 15 ] Finally, on May 17, 2002, Amendments and a notice of fulfilment of conditions are signed by all: the \\\" final \\\" price is reduced to $456,500, the inspection is said to be complete. [9] [ 16 ] A few years later, in 2005, Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun decide to sell their property. A prospective buyer makes an offer and requests the inspection of the building. [ 17 ] Sunny Li turns out to be the building inspector whose services are retained by this prospective buyer. He immediately declares his conflict of interest having acted for Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun three years earlier. The prospective buyer hires another inspector. [ 18 ] Mr. and Mrs. Zhang as well as Mr. Li testify that they would have had a meeting, at that time in 2005, whereby Mr. Li would have informed them that he had seen pipes at the rear of the property, and that these pipes indicated that the building had previously been heated by oil. Mr. Li would then have mentioned that there might be a possibility of an underground oil tank on the property. [ 19 ] Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun thereby sent a letter of demand [10] to their vendors, the five Defendants, alleging their knowledge of the existence of an underground oil tank. [ 20 ] In April 2005, Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun accept an Offer to Purchase [11] their property. The following conditions are included in the offer: [12] \\\" 1.          All the expenses relating to the financing, including without limiting the generality thereto, appraisal, inspection and Environmental Phase I reports SHALL be paid by the BUYER. 2.          The SELLER declares that there might be an underground oil tank. If BUYERS' environmental, inspection reports, AND/OR other studies reveal and confirm the EXISTENCE and LOCATION of the oil tank, the SELLER shall be responsible for all the cost of removing the tank and cleaning out the soil if there is any contamination AND furnish the BUYER and the CREDITOR a Environmental VERIFICATION (characterization) report of the soil surrounding the tank removal site – so to prove the quality of the work done, which should satisfy the CREDITOR. 3.          If any environmental risks other than the underground oil tank are determined in BUYERS Environmental Phase I report, the SELLER shall be responsible for dealing with and finding a solution that shall satisfy the CREDITOR. \\\" [ 21 ] Mr. and Mrs. Zhang hire a contractor, Raynald Fradette of Raynovaction. An environmental study, \\\" Caractérisation environnementale des sols \\\", is conducted by Inspec‑Sol Inc. (thereafter \\\" Inspec-Sol \\\"): it shows the presence of an underground oil tank and contamination of the soil by heating oil. [13] [ 22 ] The oil tank is carefully dug out of the ground. According to Mr. Fradette of Raynovaction, the 8 000 litres reservoir is rusted, shows holes and is still about one quarter full. [14] The oil had penetrated the grounds around the old reservoir and had propagated to the neighbour's property. Raynovaction has to dig, retrieve and dispose [15] of the oil tank and of contaminated soil as far as beneath the foundations and concrete slab of the next building. Its invoices [16] total $112,212.64. [17] [ 23 ] Raynald Fradette as well as Mr. and Mrs. Zhang [18] confirm that the full amount of $112,212.64 was paid. Decontamination is confirmed by Inspec‑Sol in January 2006. [19] [ 24 ] The deed of sale is finally executed between Xiao Dong Zhang, Hong Sun and the new buyers, in February 2006. [20] [ 25 ] In their \\\" mise en demeure\\\" of 2005, Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun allege that Defendants knew of the existence of an underground oil tank, claim $3,170 plus taxes being the cost of an environmental report and ask that the five vendors \\\" solve the problem completely by either taking the tank out or changing the soil when necessary \\\". [21] [ 26 ] Legal proceedings are instituted on July 10, 2006, against Defendants. In November 2008, Sunny Li, the inspector, is added as a Defendant. A discontinuance is filed to withdraw the action against him in April 2009. [ 27 ] Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun claim: [22] \\\"36.       THAT Plaintiffs are entitled to and do hereby claim from Defendants solidarily, the following amounts, namely: a. The sum of $112,212.64 representing remedial work; b. The sum of $25.00 representing the cost of a permit; c. The sum of $25,000.00 representing loss of time and damages sustained as a result of having to involve themselves in the remedial work and all matters related thereto; d. The sum of $20,000.00 representing legal fees incurred and to be incurred with respect to their claim. TOTAL:           $157,237.64 \\\" [ 28 ] Defendants contest the action saying that Xiao Dong Zhang, Hong Sun and their inspector Sunny Li failed \\\" even to report on the two clearly visible pipes on the side of the building \\\" [23] and did not act as prudent and diligent buyers, as they should have enquired about the existence of an underground oil reservoir, in view of these pipes. THE QUESTIONS IN ISSUE [ 29 ] The Court must decide the following questions: 1- Were there latent defects on this property at the time of the sale, in June 2002? 2- Were the vendors, all five Defendants to the present action, bound by a contractual warranty in the Deed of sale? DISCUSSION 1. latent defects [ 30 ] A vendor is bound to warrant that a property sold by him has no latent defects. [ 31 ] Articles 1726 and 1728 of the Civil Code of Quebec read: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. [...] 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer. [ 32 ] Recently, the Supreme Court of Canada reiterated [24] the four conditions essential to this legal warranty: the defect must be hidden, sufficiently important, it must exist at the time of sale and be unknown to the buyer. [ 33 ] Honourable Justices LeBel and Deschamps write: [25] \\\"50 […] However, regardless of how the defect is characterized, it must have four characteristics , all of which are essential to the warranty:  it must be latent , must be sufficiently serious , must have existed at the time of the sale and must have been unknown to the buyer . 51 The latency of the defect is assessed objectively , that is, by reviewing the buyer’s examination of the good in light of what a prudent and diligent buyer of identical expertise would have done: […] 52        […] The loss must also be serious , that is, it must render the good unfit for its intended use or must so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have purchased it at the price paid.  […] 53         The defect must also have existed at the time of the sale . […] 54         It is not enough for the defect to be latent.  It must also be unknown to the buyer , as is required by the concluding words of art. 1522 in fine C.C.L.C.  This criterion is assessed subjectively.  Unlike the seller, the buyer is not subject to a presumption of knowledge, since the buyer is always presumed to be in good faith.  As a result, the burden of proving actual knowledge of the defect always rests on the seller […]\\\" (emphasis added) [ 34 ] Justice Christiane Alarie, in a recent judgment, expresses the principles on hidden defects: [26] \\\"[47] Le bien vendu doit être exempt de vices cachés le rendant impropre à l'usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l'acheteur ne l'aurait pas acheté ou n'en aurait pas donné si haut prix s'il les avait connus. [48] L'acheteur a le fardeau de la preuve du vice et des conditions d'existence de la garantie. [49] Le vice doit être inconnu de l'acheteur au moment de la vente. [50] La défectuosité ne doit pas être apparente au moment de la vente (en ce sens, le vice doit être « caché »). Pour évaluer le caractère apparent ou caché du vice, les tribunaux considèrent la compétence technique de l'acheteur, le cas échéant, la nature et l'âge du bien. [51] Le Code civil du Québec prévoit que le vice doit être grave. [52] Pour décider si un vice est suffisamment grave pour donner ouverture à la garantie, on considère les inconvénients qu'il présente pour l'acheteur. Il n'est pas nécessaire que ce vice empêche toute utilisation du bien, mais qu'il en réduise l'utilité de façon importante en regard des attentes légitimes de l'acheteur. [53] Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente. Il suffit qu'il ait existé de façon latente, sans nécessairement, s'être clairement manifesté. [54] Le vice doit être dénoncé dans un délai raisonnable de sa découverte. [55] Lorsque le vice est grave, l'acheteur a le choix entre demander la résolution de la vente ou une diminution de prix. Seul le vice dont la gravité ou l'importance par rapport à l'ensemble de la vente est suffisamment grave, peut justifier la résolution de la vente. Le tribunal peut refuser la résolution et n'accorder qu'une diminution de prix quand le vice est de peu d'importance. [ 56]        La bonne foi du vendeur n'entre pas en ligne de compte dans la détermination de l'application de la garantie de qualité.\\\" [ 35 ] Plaintiffs submit that these defects, namely the presence of an underground oil tank and extensive contamination of the soil around it, were latent. [ 36 ] Defendants argue that the pipes were apparent along an outside wall of the property. They submit that not only should the buyers and their expert have seen them, but that they did indeed see them during the inspection. They submit that an additional reduction in the sale price was granted to take into consideration the possibility of existence of an underground oil tank. [ 37 ] Let us analyze the four conditions required to give effect to a legal warranty against latent defects. A. The defect must be hidden [ 38 ] As stated at art. 1726 in fine C.C.Q., an apparent defect can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer, without the need of expert assistance. [ 39 ] The Court of Appeal applies this norm: [27] \\\"[28] […] il existe une seule norme applicable, celle qui examine la conduite de l'acheteur prudent et diligent. [...] [ 40 ] Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun had no experience or knowledge in this matter. It was their first property. They hired an expert, a building inspector, to help them examine the building they wanted to buy. They were prudent and diligent in doing so. [ 41 ] Inspector Li testifies that he did not see the pipes on the outside wall of the building in 2002. He claims there was snow and debris in the backyard. Evidence shows that this inspection was done at the beginning of May 2002. [28] One of the pipes stood out from the ground for a few feet, the other came from the ground and was attached with a metal clamp, a few feet higher on the back wall of the building. Mr. Zhang was with his inspector while walking around the building during the inspection. The pipes were visible for him also. Evidence does not show that he asked inspector Li any questions about these pipes. [ 42 ] Interestingly, inspector Li easily saw the pipes when he walked around the property in 2005. He says it was summertime but evidence reveals that it was in February, just before prospective buyers obtained an environmental report [29] (February 14, 2005) and when Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun wrote a letter of demand [30] to Defendants (February 18, 2005). [ 43 ] There is no doubt, and evidence is non-contradicted on the matter, that the mere presence of these pipes indicates that the building was once heated with oil. The existence of an underground oil tank seems therefore possible, if not likely. [ 44 ] Evidence also shows that soil sampling and chemical analysis can determine potential contamination: a Phase II environmental site assessment and investigation would have cost between $2,695 [31] and $3,170, [32] in 2005, according to expert documents filed before the Court. [ 45 ] The facts submitted in evidence establish that the buyers, Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun, with their inspector Mr. Li, could...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... building was once heated with oil. The existence of an underground oil tank seems therefore possible, if not likely. [ 44 ] Evidence also shows that soil sampling and chemical analysis can determine potential contamination: a Phase II environmental site assessment and investigation would have cost between $2,695 [31] and $3,170, [32] in 2005, according to expert documents filed before the Court. [ 45 ] The facts submitted in evidence establish that the buyers, Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun, with their inspector Mr. Li, could easily have seen the pipes along the outside wall of the building and could readily have investigated to confirm the presence of an old oil tank and contamination in the ground on the property they wished to buy. [ 46 ] Justice Rochon, now with the Court of Appeal, writes: [33] \\\"[…] L'acheteur prudent et diligent d'un immeuble procède à un examen visuel attentif et complet du bâtiment . Il est à l'affût d'indice pouvant laisser soupçonner un vice . Si un doute sérieux se forme dans son esprit il doit pousser plus loin sa recherche . D'une part, on ne peut exiger d'un acheteur prudent et diligent une connaissance particulière dans le domaine immobilier. D'autre part, on ne peut conclure au vice caché si le résultat d'un examen attentif aurait amené une personne prudente et diligente à s'interroger ou à soupçonner un problème. À partir de ce point l'acheteur prudent et diligent doit prendre des mesures raisonnables, selon les circonstances, pour connaître l'état réel du bâtiment. Il ne saurait se replier sur son manque de connaissance si son examen lui permet de soupçonner une anomalie quelconque. Il faut donc examiner, suivant chaque cas d'espèce, la conduite d'un acheteur prudent et diligent . Antérieurement à 1994 on exigeait également de l'acheteur qu'il soit prudent et diligent. Sans revenir à l'ancienne règle jurisprudentielle au sujet des experts, il est possible dans certains cas que le fait de ne pas recourir à un expert pourra être perçu en soi, comme un manque de prudence et de diligence. Le tribunal ne veut pas réintroduire dans notre droit une exigence spécifiquement exclue par le législateur en 1994. Par ailleurs, cette exclusion ne saurait être interprétée comme autorisant l'acheteur à agir de façon insouciante ou négligente. Cet acheteur ne fera pas preuve de prudence et de diligence alors qu'il existe des indices perceptibles pour un profane , s'il ne prend pas les moyens (y compris le recours à des experts le cas échéant ) de s'assurer que l'immeuble est exempt de vice .\\\" (emphasis added) [ 47 ] Author Jeffrey Edwards tells of the buyer's obligation: [34] \\\"378 – […] Or, dans la doctrine et la jurisprudence on a unanimement reconnu l'existence d'une obligation impérative de l'acheteur d'examiner le bien qu'il projette d'acquérir. De fait, cet examen permet de distinguer le vice occulte de celui qui ne l'est pas. Lorsque le vice se révèle à l'examen, il est apparent; dans le cas contraire, il est occulte. L'intensité de l'examen joue donc un rôle capital dans la détermination du caractère occulte . […] […] 381 –  […]  Cependant, dans certaines circonstances, l'acheteur ne peut satisfaire à la norme de l'acheteur prudent et diligent en agissant seul . Envisageons le cas d'un acheteur profane désireux d'acquérir un ordinateur d'un certain âge, d'une grande complexité et à un prix élevé, c'est‑à‑dire plusieurs millions de dollars, d'un vendeur ordinaire; ou encore celui d'un acheteur profane d'un immeuble qui, constatant certains indices inquiétants, n'a pas la connaissance nécessaire pour écarter la possibilité d'un vice. Dans les deux hypothèses, l'acheteur agissant seul n'a pas la connaissance requise pour effectuer et compléter un examen selon la norme d'un acheteur « prudent et diligent » . S'il omet de demander de l'aide, il agit de manière négligente. […]\\\" (emphasis added) [ 48 ] The Court does not believe inspector Li when he claims he did not see the pipes in the back because of snow and debris, in 2002. His credibility is assessed further in the present judgment. [ 49 ] Photographs [35] show how these pipes would likely have been installed on the building. No evidence has been submitted to convince the Court that debris or snow, in May 2002, would have hidden these pipes from plain view. Even if there was still snow in the Montreal area in May, which has not been established and is not likely, one of the pipes attached to the outside wall of the building was more than five feet high and could be seen by anyone the least bit diligent in his inspection. Mr. Li and Mr. Zhang walked around the building during the inspection: the pipes were clearly visible. [ 50 ] Even if Mr. Li seeks to limit his obligation by referring to the Standards of Practice [36] of the Quebec Association of Building Inspectors, the Court believes that it was Mr. Li's mandate to report on these pipes, obvious on the back wall of the building. [ 51 ] Inspector Li owed professional obligations to his clients, Mr. and Mrs. Zhang: - these pipes clearly indicated the presence of an oil heating system; (art. 9.1 A.(1) and glossary of the Standards of Practice ); [37] - an oil heating system had to include an oil tank; - the possibility of soil contamination due to an old underground oil tank could be easily verified; - the costs related to the digging out of an old underground oil tank and decontamination could be important; - his clients were first time owners of an apartment building and relied on him for advice. [ 52 ] Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun had the burden to establish that this defect was hidden. They did not. B. Sufficiently important [ 53 ] No doubt that this condition has been met in the present matter. [ 54 ] The oil reservoir had to be emptied, removed from a narrow space between two buildings, the soil had been contaminated quite deeply, and required delicate operations that lasted many weeks. [38] [ 55 ] The costs at $112,212.64 have been established. [39] C. At the time of sale [ 56 ] The presence of an old rusted underground oil tank was discovered only in 2005, so was the contamination of the soil. [ 57 ] Notwithstanding the fact that the Court believes it could, and should, have been discovered at the time of sale, in June 2002, it was not. [ 58 ] Expert Raymond Morel, Director of Environment at Inspec‑Sol, testifies that it is impossible to determine the date, or even the period, when contamination of the soil began. [ 59 ] According to this expert, the life span of an oil tank depends on various factors such as the type of reservoir, its thickness, the degree of acidity in the soil, the quantity of water underground, the type of pipes used, the maintenance, amongst others. No evidence is submitted as to any of these elements. [ 60 ] The burden [40] to prove facts on which their claim is based lies with Plaintiffs Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun. Article 2804 C.C.Q. reads: 2804. Evidence is sufficient if it renders the existence of a fact more probable than its non-existence, unless the law requires more convincing proof. [ 61 ] It is not contested that an underground oil reservoir was situated on the property at the time of sale. [ 62 ] Contamination, on the other hand, was only discovered in 2005. [ 63 ] No direct proof establishes the presence of contamination in 2002. No presumption [41] can be inferred in the absence of serious, precise and concordant facts to confirm it. None have been established. [ 64 ] The presence of an underground oil tank at the time of sale has been established; contamination has not. D. Unknown to the buyers [ 65 ] A defect can only be qualified as latent if it is unknown to the buyer. [ 66 ] Indeed, if the buyers and their expert see or discover the defect, it is no longer latent: the legal warranty cannot be invoked. [ 67 ] Pierre‑Gabriel Jobin suggests: \\\"158 –  […] Il arrive parfois que le vendeur dénonce le vice à l'acheteur ou que ce dernier, ou son expert, le découvre avant la vente : il n'existe alors aucune garantie , sauf formulation d'une réserve ou stipulation contraire. Bien que l'acheteur ait en principe le fardeau de la preuve, c'est souvent, en pratique, au vendeur de prouver la connaissance du vice par l'acheteur.\\\" (emphasis added) [ 68 ] The Supreme Court of Canada, as previously mentioned, affirms that no presumption of knowledge is imposed on the buyer. [42] [ 69 ] Renato Navarra, his wife Ellen Chiarella and daughter‑in‑law Alana Palladini, testify that Xiao Dong Zhang and inspector Li specifically knew about the pipes and the likely existence of an underground oil reservoir, before the sale. [ 70 ] Renato Navarra remembers the inspection done by Mr. Li with Xiao Dong Zhang, in his presence. He claims that they both stopped, looked at the pipes on the rear outside wall of the building, entered into a discussion, asked him to open one of the garage doors to look inside and resumed their discussion. At this point, Renato Navarra testifies that Mr. Li told him there might be an old oil tank in the ground and that it would cost around $10,000 to dig it out and remove it. He wanted the vendors to reduce the sale price of an additional $10,000. [ 71 ] Renato Navarra, knowing that the lowest price decided upon by all Defendants was $465,000, labelled as \\\" final \\\" in counterproposal of May 4, [43] said he would agree to a $5,000 reduction. Mr. Zhang would have accepted it then and there, in presence of Mr. Li. [ 72 ] Mr. Navarra refers to the counterproposal of May 6, [44] indicating a \\\" final \\\" price of $458,000, which also took into account other small repairs observed by inspector Li, to the roof, leaky faucets and a water pipe. [ 73 ] Alana Palladini testifies of a conversation amongst all five vendors after they had decided that the lowest price they would accept would be $465,000. After discussion with Renato Navarra concerning a possible underground oil tank, and other minor corrections, they agreed to a reduction to $458,000. She also testifies that she reminded Xiao Dong Zhang of all this in a telephone conversation she claims they had, after receiving the letter of demand in February 2005. [45] [ 74 ] Renato Navarra and Alana Palladini's memory seems very clear as to these events and the reduction of the price that ensued, although Mr. Navarra's recollection is not so good as to the actual numbers and the details of the price reduction. [ 75 ] Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun deny everything and say none of these discussions ever happened. [ 76 ] Plaintiffs submit that Renato Navarra's version of events is impossible because the inspection by Mr. Li would have been done after the May 7 counterproposal which indicated $458,000 as the \\\" final \\\" price. Furthermore, no counteroffer would reflect Renato Navarra's pretensions of a reduction of $5,000. Finally, they ask, wouldn't a prudent vendor have asked for a written acknowledgement and a release with regards to the oil tank? They conclude in saying that it was the vendors' obligation to inform them of the presence of an underground oil tank. [ 77 ] Defendants plead some contradictions in Xiao Dong Zhang's testimony before the Court and in his examination on discovery, as to number of visits, dates of visits/inspection, problems noted by the inspector. They also attack Mr. Li's competence and credibility: how can he claim he did not see the pipes in May 2002, but says he saw them in February 2005 and decided to contact Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun to inform them of this, three years later? They say they remember their conversation to further reduce the price to $458,000. [ 78 ] Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun are presumed of good faith. [46] [ 79 ] But they have the burden of proof [47] to convince the Court of the presence of latent defects. They say they ignored them. [ 80 ] Defendants offer specific testimonies that contradict them. [ 81 ] Which is most probable? [ 82 ] The Court considers that Defendants' pretensions are more likely than Plaintiffs' for the following reasons: - Xiao Dong Zhang says he was with inspector Li at all times during the three‑hour inspection, except on the roof; - Mr. Navarra specifically remembers stopping in front of the two pipes, in the back of the building, where Mr. Zhang and Mr. Li would have looked at them and entered into a discussion; - Mr. Li says he inspected all concrete foundations of the building, meaning that he very likely saw the pipes which were attached with a bracket over the foundations on the back wall in 2002; [48] - Mr. Li cannot be believed when he says that he did not see the pipes during his inspection in May 2002; - Mr. Li's testimony is evasive, incomplete and unreliable. He does not remember when he inspected the property, did not bring his records notwithstanding a subpoena, says he submitted a written report then contradicts himself. He gives the impression of wanting to be as vague as possible not to commit himself; - Counterproposal of May 4 indicates the price of $465,000 as \\\" final \\\", confirming Renato Navarra and Alana Palladini's testimonies; - Counterproposal of May 6 also indicates the price of $458,000 as \\\" final \\\" and is accepted by all the parties, confirming a further reduction in price after a \\\" final \\\" counteroffer. [ 83 ] Mr. Li was Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun's mandatory for the purpose of the inspection of the property for latent defects. [ 84 ] Evidence convinces that Xiao Dong Zhang, Hong Sun and their expert knew about the pipes indicating the likely presence of an underground oil tank. A prudent and diligent buyer would have investigated further and found the presence of a reservoir and contamination of the soil around the reservoir, if present. [ 85 ] Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun have not established the four conditions required to invoke the legal warranty of quality for latent defects. * * * [ 86 ] Notwithstanding the Court's conclusion on the issue of latent defects, a second argument must be discussed. 2. contractual warranty [ 87 ] In the Deed of sale, Defendants declared that the property being sold to Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun did not \\\" derogate from the laws and regulations concerning the protection of the environment \\\". [49] [ 88 ] Article 1458 C.C.Q. reads: 1458. Every person has a duty to honour his contractual undertakings. Where he fails in this duty, he is liable for any bodily, moral or material injury he causes to the other contracting party and is liable to reparation for the injury; neither he nor the other party may in such a case avoid the rules governing contractual liability by opting for rules that would be more favourable to them. [ 89 ] Article 1458 C.C.Q. must be read in conjunction with other dispositions of the Civil Code , namely the specific chapter concerning the sale contract . Article 1725 C.C.Q. refers to a specific warranty owed by the seller of an immoveable, the warranty of ownership : 1725. The seller of an immovable is warrantor towards the buyer for any violation of restrictions of public law affecting the property which are exceptions to the ordinary law of ownership. The seller is not warrantor towards the buyer where he has given notice of these restrictions to the buyer at the time of the sale, where a prudent and diligent buyer could have discovered them by reason of the nature, location and use of the premises or where such restrictions have been registered in the registry office. [ 90 ] The first paragraph of art. 1725 C.C.Q. imposes an obligation to the vendor to give the buyer notice of any violation of restrictions of public law affecting the property. Laws and regulations concerning the protection of the environment fall in this category. [50] [ 91 ] Justice Louis Crête examined a somewhat similar contractual guarantee in a sale contract. He believes that important violations are covered by the guarantee: [51] \\\"[49] Avec respect, le tribunal ne croit pas que la clause sur laquelle s'appuient les défendeurs ait une portée aussi large qu'ils veuillent le croire.  Sans aller jusqu'à dire qu'il s'agit là d'une clause de style, contrairement à celle portant expressément sur l'absence de MIUF, il faut néanmoins se rendre compte que, prise littéralement, la clause serait susceptible de couvrir toutes les dérogations, si min...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\n... of pipes indicating the previous use of oil heating equipment on the property. [ 121 ] The Court believes that the reasoning applies to the contractual guarantee clause in the deed of sale. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT : [ 122 ] DISMISSES Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun's action against Dominico Palladini, Joanne Ortenzi, Alana Palladini, Renato Navarra and Ellen Chiarella. [ 123 ] WITH COSTS . __________________________________ SUZANNE COURTEAU, J.S.C. M e Melvin S. Schiff phillips, friedman, kotler Attorneys for Plaintiffs M e Marta Elsa Solà Attorney for Defendants Dates of hearing: January 26, 27 and 28, 2010 [1] Deed of Sale dated June 27, 2002 and executed before M e Pierre Coutu, Notary; and registered at the Land Register for Montreal, under number 5371579, Exhibit P-1. [2] Deed of Sale dated December 20, 1990 executed before M e Louise Langelier, Exhibit D-1. [3] Listing Agreement, Exhibit P-8. [4] Offer to Purchase together with a series of counteroffers annexed thereto, Exhibit P‑9 en liasse . [5] Ibid. [6] Ibid . [7] Ibid , section B2.4 of PP54599. [8] Supra footnote 4. [9] Ibid . [10] Letter dated February 18, 2005 from Plaintiffs to Defendants, Exhibit P-6. [11] Copy of the Offer to Purchase submitted by Rukiye Yildirim to Plaintiffs in relation to the property situated at [...], Montreal, Quebec, Exhibit P‑2. [12] Ibid. , ann. G, clauses 1, 2 and 3. [13] Report prepared by Inspec‑Sol Inc. dated August 25, 2005, Exhibit P‑3 and Facture d'Inspec‑Sol inc. du 15 août 2005 , Exhibit D‑8. [14] Facture de la compagnie Sarp ., Exhibit D‑6. [15] Facture de Cintec Environnement du 28 juin 2005 , Exhibit D‑7. [16] Series of invoices issued by Raynovaction to Plaintiffs, Exhibit P‑5 en liasse . [17] Receipt from Raynovaction – Gestion Rénovation 2000 dated February 7, 2006 for the sum of $112,212.64, Exhibit P‑10. [18] Chèque du 10 août 2006, endos et reçu , Exhibit D‑9 en liasse ; Traite bancaire en dollars canadiens , Exhibit D‑10; Chèque daté du 9 août 2006 , Exhibit D‑13 and Application pour transfert , Exhibit D‑14. [19] Report prepared by Inspec‑Sol Inc. dated January 11, 2006, Exhibit P‑4. [20] Deed of Sale entered into between Plaintiffs and Aibibula Rouzimaiti and Rukiye Can, dated February 14, 2006, and registered at the Land Register of Montreal, under number 13 060 234, Exhibit P‑7. [21] Letter from S & P Québec consulting engineers dated February 14, 2005, Exhibit D‑4. [22] Reamended Motion Introductive of Suit in Damages, paras. 36(a), (b), (c) and (d). [23] Plea of Defendants, para. 16. [24] ABB Inc . v. Domtar Inc. , 2007 SCC 50 , paras. 50 and ff. [25] Ibid. [26] Laforest v. Chabot , 2008 QCCS 4340 , paras. 47-56 . [27] St-Louis v. Morin , 2006 QCCA 1643 , para. 28 . [28] Supra footnote 4. [29] Communication from Gas Metro, Exhibit D‑3. [30] Supra footnote 10. [31] Facture d'Inspec‑Sol inc. du 15 août 2005 , Exhibit D-8. [32] Supra footnote 21. [33] Lavoie v. Comtois , 1999 CanLII 11787 (QC CS) , [2000] R.D.I. 36 (S.C.). [34] Jeffrey E dwards , \\\"La Garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois\\\", Montreal, Wilson & Lafleur, 1998, n o 378, pp. 167 and 168 and n o 381, pp. 170 and 171. [35] Photographs, Exhibit D‑5 en liasse . [36] Mise en demeure addressed to Defendant Sunny Li dated July 11, 2006, Exhibit P‑13. [37] Art. 9.1 A.(1) and Glossary of the Standards of Practice of the Quebec Association of Building Inspectors, effective January 1, 2002. [38] Supra footnote 16. [39] Series of invoices issued by Raynovaction to Plaintiffs, Exhibit P‑5 en liasse and Receipt from Raynovaction – Gestion Rénovation 2000 dated February 7, 2006 for the sum of $112,212.64, Exhibit P‑10. [40] C.C.Q., art. 2803. [41] C.C.Q., art. 2846 and ff. [42] ABB Inc . v. Domtar Inc. , supra footnote 24, para. 54. [43] Supra footnote 4. [44] Ibid . [45] Supra footnote 10. [46] C.C.Q., art. 2805. [47] C.C.Q., art. 2803. [48] Supra footnote 16. [49] Supra footnote 1. [50] Denys-Claude lamontagne , Droit de la vente , 3rd ed., Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2005, p. 101. [51] Hatziefremidis v. Constructions Les terrasses du moulin inc. , 2003 CanLII 29287 (QC CA) , [2003] R.D.I. 119, para. 49 (S.C.). [52] O.C. 108‑96, 24 January 1996, G.O.Q. 1996.II.1180. [53] O.C. 156‑99, 24 February 1999, G.Q.Q. 1999.II.227. [54] An Act respecting petroleum products and equipment , R.S.Q., chapter P‑29.1, version in force between October 22, 1999 and November 26, 2003. [55] Direction de la sécurité des équipements pétroliers, ministère des Ressources naturelles du Québec, Comment tenir son registre, la vérification de mon équipement pétrolier, un investissement pour l'avenir , Québec, 1999. [56] Ibid . [57] Construction Code , c. B-1.1, r.0.01.01. [58] Regulation respecting petroleum products and equipment , c. P‑29.1, r.2, version in effect in 2002. [59] Pierre-Gabriel jobin, La Vente , 2nd ed., Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2001. [60] Pierre-Gabriel jobin , Jacques deslauriers , \\\"Le droit commun\\\", in Collection de droit 1999‑00, École du Barreau du Québec, vol. 5, Obligations et contrats, Cowansville, Éditions Yvon Blais, 1999, p.168 and 169. [61] Larin v. Curadeau , J.E. 97-475 (C.A.) . [62] Immeubles Maude inc. v. Farazli , [1991] N o AZ-91011825 (C.A.) and Bordeleau v. Lauzon , [1995] N o AZ‑95033033, p. 3-5 (C.Q.)...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n[51] \\\"[49] Avec respect, le tribunal ne croit pas que la clause sur laquelle s'appuient les défendeurs ait une portée aussi large qu'ils veuillent le croire.  Sans aller jusqu'à dire qu'il s'agit là d'une clause de style, contrairement à celle portant expressément sur l'absence de MIUF, il faut néanmoins se rendre compte que, prise littéralement, la clause serait susceptible de couvrir toutes les dérogations, si minimes soient-elles, à cet équilibre écologique qui a pu avoir existé ici avant la révolution industrielle.  Le tribunal prend connaissance judiciaire des préoccupations grandissantes et légitimes des citoyens face à l'écologie de leur milieu de vie.  Les normes environnementales édictées par les divers paliers de législation depuis trente ans font écho à ces préoccupations. Cela dit, il existe vraisemblablement bien peu d'immeubles, de terrains, de lacs et de rivières exempts de toute pollution ou répondant scrupuleusement à toutes les normes environnementales élevées en vigueur présentement. Dans un tel contexte , il est manifeste qu'une clause contractuelle par laquelle un vendeur atteste que son terrain ne déroge pas aux lois et règlements relatifs à la protection de l'environnement ne peut se référer qu'aux atteintes graves en la matière , celles où le \\\"pollueur payeur\\\" est susceptible d'encourir une responsabilité importante en raison des dangers réels que son terrain peut poser pour la santé des citoyens et des êtres vivants.  On pense, par exemple ici, aux déversements et la présence d'hydrocarbures ou d'autres substances toxiques dans le sol (arsenic, plomb, BPC, etc.), lesquels empoisonnent littéralement le milieu ambiant au point de le rendre, à toutes fins utiles, impropre à un usage normal.\\\" (emphasis added) [ 92 ] The second paragraph of art. 1725 C.C.Q. establishes three situations where the vendor is no longer warrantor of violations of restrictions of public law towards the buyer: • if he has given notice of these restrictions of public law; • if a prudent and diligent buyer could have discovered them by reason of the nature, the location or the use of the premises; or • when such restrictions have been registered in the registry office. [ 93 ] Provincial regulations, Regulations to amend the Petroleum Products Regulation, were enacted in 1996 [52] and 1999. [53] They were in effect in June 2002. The Act respecting petroleum products and equipment , as applicable in 2002, defines [54] \\\"high-risk petroleum equipment\\\": \\\"high-risk petroleum equipment means petroleum equipment possessing one or more of the following characteristics: 1) petroleum equipment, one or more components of which is partially or completely buried, and having a capacity of (a) 500 or more litres, if it is used for gasoline, diesel fuel or used oil from a motor vehicle or hydraulic equipment; (b) 4,000 or more litres, if it is used for heating oil, except equipment used for residential heating; [...]\\\" [ 94 ] The oil reservoir, here, holds 8 000 litres of heating oil and is completely underground. It cannot be considered to be for the exclusive use of residential heating since this expression refers to residential heating for \\\" unifamilial \\\" dwellings. [55] [ 95 ] Indeed, the Direction de la sécurité des équipements pétroliers of the ministère des Ressources naturelles offered guidelines to the general public concerning the important modifications adopted in 1999: [56] \\\" n nouveau cadre légal L e gouvernement du Québec a introduit des changements importants à la Loi sur l'utilisation des produits pétroliers ainsi qu'à son règlement d'application. Ce nouveau cadre légal est entré en vigueur le 1 er mai 1999. Les modifications apportées à la loi , qui s'appelle maintenant la Loi sur les produits et les équipements pétroliers , visent principalement à réduire les problèmes attribuables aux fuites de produits pétroliers en responsabilisant davantage les propriétaires et utilisateurs d'équipements pétroliers . [...] Afin d'accroître la sécurité des personnes et des biens ainsi que la protection de l'environnement, de nouvelles exigences ont été établies. [...] e régime de vérification C ' est dans cette optique de responsabilisation des propriétaires et des utilisateurs d'équipements pétroliers que des vérifications devront être effectuées par des vérificateurs agréés par le ministère des Ressources naturelles lors de l'installation, du remplacement ou de l'enlèvement d'un équipement pétrolier à risque élevé. Ces vérifications permettront de prévenir plus efficacement les éventuelles fuites et, par conséquent, de réduire les frais de décontamination. De plus, tous les équipements pétroliers à risque élevé seront soumis périodiquement à des vérifications. [...] ÉQUIPEMENTS À RISQUE ÉLEVÉ é q u i p e m e n t s   p é t r o l i e r s S ystème d'entreposage souterrain Système d'entreposage de surface Réservoirs utilisés à des fins lucratives Essence 500 litres et + 2 500 litres et + Tout Diesel 500 litres et + 10 000 litres et + Tout 4 000 litres et + 10 000 litres et + Tout Huiles usées 500 litres et + Ne s'applique pas Ne s'applique pas 2 À l'exclusion des équipements utilisés pour le chauffage résidentiel de type unifamilial [ 96 ] Since 2007, the definition of \\\" high-risk petroleum equipment \\\" can be found in the Construction Code: [57] \\\"8.01.     In this Chapter, unless the context indicates otherwise, [...] 'high-risk petroleum equipment' means petroleum equipment having one of the following characteristics: (1)        petroleum equipment, one or more components of which is partially or completely buried, having a capacity of (a)        500 or more litres, when it is installed to store motor fuel; or (b) 4,000 or more litres , if it is installed to store fuel oil , except petroleum equipment of less than 10,000 L used for heating a single-family dwelling ; [...]\\\" (emphasis added) [ 97 ] This definition clearly excludes residential \\\" unifamilial \\\" dwellings from the Act respecting petroleum products and equipment , but confirms its application to other buildings and owners of buildings with underground oil tanks of more than 4 000 litres. [ 98 ] The previously mentioned definition of \\\" high-risk petroleum equipment \\\" therefore applies to the present matter. [ 99 ] Returning to regulations in force in 2002, some specific dispositions apply to unused underground oil reservoirs. [ 100 ] Section 130 of the Regulation respecting petroleum products and equipment reads: [58] 130.      Where the permit holder or owner of petroleum equipment decides to no longer take petroleum products from an underground storage system or has not taken petroleum products therefrom for more than 2 years , he shall (1) empty the tank , piping and motor fuel dispensers of any petroleum product ; (2)     purge the tank of all vapours until the flammable vapour concentration is less than 20% of the lower flammability limit, remove the tank and piping from the ground, remove them from the site and inspect the surrounding soil for contamination; (3)        inform the involved branch of the Ministère de l'Environnement and the involved  municipality of any contamination related to petroleum products; (4) dispose of the tank in accordance with section 171 or have it recertified according to the requirements of section 125 or 126. [...] (emphasis added) [ 101 ] This Regulation imposes an obligation to all owners of underground storage for petroleum products to empty their reservoir and dispose of it according to s. 171, if they have not used it for more than two years. [ 102 ] Defendants became owners of the property around 1990. They were bound to dispose of the underground oil tank on their property, since they had not been using it for the two previous years. This obligation constitutes, according to the Court, a restriction of public law affecting the property. [ 103 ] According to para.1725(1) C.C.Q., Defendants would be warrantors of violations to this obligation towards their buyers. Defendants had a general obligation to deliver to Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun a property that respected the laws and regulations concerning the protection of the environment, unless exceptions of para. 1725(2) C.C.Q. applied. [ 104 ] Does the second paragraph of art. 1725 C.C.Q. receive application in the precise circumstances of this sale between Plaintiffs and Defendants? [ 105 ] The Court believes that the second exception of para. 1725(2) C.C.Q., relating to \\\" where a prudent and diligent buyer could have discovered them by reason of the nature, location and use of the premises \\\" should be examined. The other two exceptions do not apply. [ 106 ] Is the meaning of these words clear? Does one easily understand what they mean? [ 107 ] The Court reads that para. 1725(2) C.C.Q. means that no warranty is offered to a buyer against restrictions of public law affecting a property if, by being prudent and diligent, this buyer could have discovered these restrictions \\\" by reason of the nature, location or use of the premises \\\". [59] [ 108 ] Authors and courts have discussed this exception to a general obligation: they have often applied the same criteria and tests as in matters of hidden defects. [ 109 ] Authors Jobin and  Deslauriers [60] suggest that this warranty is submitted to certain conditions: \\\" i) …, ce régime particulier ne vise que les violations à des limitations et non les limitations elles-mêmes, qui existent au moment de la vente, et non celles qui sont imposées par l'administration après la conclusion du contrat. … iv)  la limitation de droit public doit être occulte ou cachée; il n'y a pas de garantie dans le cas où \\\"un acheteur prudent et diligent aurait pu [la] découvrir par la nature, la situation et l'utilisation des lieux\\\" … (emphasis added) [ 110 ] Honourable Justice Forget of the Court of Appeal writes: [61] \\\" Les limitations du droit public , découlant d'un règlement de zonage qui restreint l'usage d'une propriété, ont tantôt été assimilées à des vices cachés , tantôt à des servitudes non apparentes. Dans un cas comme dans l'autre, on doit y retrouver un élément occulte ; en d'autres termes, le vice doit être caché ou la servitude ne doit pas être apparente. Pour ce faire, il ne suffit pas de dire que le vendeur n'en a pas fait mention, mais il faut établir qu'un acheteur normalement prudent et avisé aurait été induit en erreur . Bien que l'on ne puisse prétendre qu'il s'agisse d'un vice caché, ni même d'une servitude à proprement dit, puisqu'il n'y a ni fond servant ni fond dominant, les mêmes principes s'appliquent, selon moi, par analogie, lorsqu'on invoque qu'une mesure de droit public limite l'usage d'une propriété et, en conséquence, en diminue la valeur . (emphasis added) [ 111 ] It is Defendants' burden to convince the Court that these restrictions or violations to restrictions of public law affecting the property could have been \\\" discovered \\\" by prudent and diligent buyers. [ 112 ] This question of prudence, diligence and knowledge has already been examined in detail in the first section of the present judgment pertaining to hidden defects. [ 113 ] The Court has already come to the conclusion that any prudent and diligent prospective buyer of this property would have noticed the two pipes still affixed to the exterior back wall of the building, an indication, clearly, that it had previously been heated by oil that had to be stored in a reservoir, very likely underground since no out‑of‑ground oil tank was apparent. A prudent and diligent prospective buyer would have investigated and found the old reservoir and the contamination if present. [ 114 ] In situations like these, the courts have considered that the second exception of para. 1725(2) C.C.Q. should receive application. [ 115 ] Honourable Justice Rousseau‑Houle of the Court of Appeal expresses it as follows: [62] \\\"Dans le doute, le double critère élaboré dans l'affaire Kraus c. Nakis Holding, [1969] C.S. 261 demeure une norme qui paraît toujours juste. Le juge Albert Mayrand a établi dans cette décision que les mesures de droit public qui limitent l'usage de la propriété foncière constituent des servitudes légales d'utilité publique qui doivent être dénoncées par le vendeur lorsqu'elles échappent au droit commun de la propriété et qu'elles ne peuvent être découvertes par des indices objectifs comme la nature ou la situation des lieux. Ce double critère du caractère ordinaire ou exceptionnel des servitudes de droit public d'une part , et de la présence ou de l'absence d'indices matériels , d'autre part , fait désormais partie du droit positif [...] [...] Si on peut affirmer que le caractère légal d'une servitude n'apparaît plus entraîner en soi, une présomption de connaissance, certaines décisions laissent cependant entrevoir que lorsqu'il s'agit d'une servitude de droit public établie par une loi, les tribunaux s'attachent davantage à rechercher si l'acheteur avait connaissance de la servitude (Voir Desjardins c. Théorêt, [1983] C.S. 1233 ; Normand c. Scholle Inc., [1982] C.S. 1133 , appel rejeté C.A. Mtl. 1987 CanLII 286 (QC CA) , no. 500-09-001574-821, 27 janvier 1987). Dans le présent litige, l'acheteuse et son mandataire ne connaissaient peut-être pas l'existence des règlements provinciaux et municipaux. Indépendamment de toute présomption de connaissance, le premier juge a estimé qu' il y avait suffisamment d'indices matériels pour révéler l'existence des servitudes non déclarées . Il a jugé que les vices en cause étaient on ne peut plus apparents et qu'ils seraient même intolérables, en eux-mêmes, indépendamment de toute réglementation ou législation. Pour apprécier le caractère apparent ou non des défauts dont se plaignait l'acheteur, le premier juge a tenu compte des éléments et circonstances propres à la présente affaire dont l'expérience du mandataire de l'acheteuse, l'âge de l'immeuble et les circonstances de l'achat. L'appelante n'a pas démontré que le premier juge avait mal apprécié les faits et qu'il s'était mal dirigé en droit. Son pourvoi doit donc être REJETÉ AVEC DÉPENS.\\\" (emphasis added) [ 116 ] The Court needs not reassess the facts in issue. [ 117 ] The conclusion reached stands: these were sufficient indications to allow Mr. Zhang and Mrs. Sun to notice that there was possibly an old underground oil tank on this property. Further minimal investigation would have confirmed it, as well as leaks and contamination of the soil surrounding the oil reservoir, if already present in 2002. [ 118 ] Mr. Zhang and Mrs. Sun did not act as prudent and diligent buyers; as well, their inspector, Mr. Li, failed to act diligently and report clear signs of the likely presence of an underground oil reservoir. Had these pipes been investigated at the time, in 2002, they would have certainly revealed the presence of the oil tank and, if present, some contamination of the soil surrounding the reservoir. [ 119 ] The Court believes that the second exception defined in para.1725(2) C.C.Q. should receive application. [ 120 ] The vendors, the five Defendants, in the present matter, were not warrantors of violations to restrictions of public law affecting this property because the buyers, Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun, could have discovered the presence of an underground oil tank, and possibly contamination of the soil, by reason of the use of the premises and the location of pipes indicating the previous use of oil heating equipment on the property. [ 121 ] The Court believes that the reasoning applies to the contractual guarantee clause in the deed of sale. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT : [ 122 ] DISMISSES Xiao Dong Zhang and Hong Sun's action against Dominico Palladini, Joanne Ortenzi, Alana Palladini, Renato Navarra and Ellen Chiarella. [ 123 ] WITH COSTS . __________________________________ SUZANNE COURTEAU, J.S.C. M e Melvin S\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2010qccs4173-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.018414322286844254, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2010qccs4173-en\", \"title\": \"Zhang v. Palladini, 2010 QCCS 4173 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2010qccs4173-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.018414322286844254, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2010qccs4173-en\"}, \"token_count\": 13400, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2010qccs4173-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2025qccq2812\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2025qccq2812-fr\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Simard c. Brunet, 2025 QCCQ 2812 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2025 QCCQ 2812 (CanLII) | Simard c. Brunet | CanLII Date : 2025-04-07 Numéro de dossier : 750-32-013622-223 Référence : Simard c. Brunet, 2025 QCCQ 2812 (CanLII) Simard c. Brunet 2025 QCCQ 2812 COUR DU QUÉBEC « Division des petites créances » CANADA PROVINCE DE QUÉBEC DISTRICT DE SAINT-HYACINTHE LOCALITÉ DE SAINT-HYACINTHE « Chambre civile » N° : 750-32-013622-223 DATE : 7 avril 2025 ______________________________________________________________________ SOUS LA PRÉSIDENCE DE L’HONORABLE ISABELLE LABRANCHE, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ JEANNE D’ARC SIMARD Partie demanderesse c. RICHARD BRUNET et GENEVIÈVE SAULNIER Partie défenderesse ______________________________________________________________________ JUGEMENT ______________________________________________________________________ APERÇU [ 1 ] Le 23 juillet 2021, Jeanne d’Arc Simard achète l’immeuble de Richard Brunet et de Geneviève Saulnier situé sur le bord du fleuve à Matane moyennant un prix de vente de 105 000 $ [1] . Cette acquisition comme résidence secondaire estivale intervient sans aucune garantie légale ainsi qu’aux risques et périls [2] . [ 2 ] Mme Simard n’a pas retenu les services d’un inspecteur préachat et procède à sa propre inspection. [ 3 ] Elle réclame 15 000 $ [3] puisqu’elle reproche aux vendeurs de fausses déclarations quant à l’état de l’immeuble. Elle prétend que les vices qui l’affectent justifient sa démolition. Elle invoque également leur mauvaise foi puisqu’ils n’ont pas collaboré à la fixation de la visite de son entrepreneur devant évaluer les travaux à exécuter après la signature de l’offre d’achat [4] . La propriété est effectivement détruite [5] . [ 4 ] M. Brunet et Mme Saulnier contestent cette demande et nient toute responsabilité puisque la vente est intervenue sans aucune garantie légale ainsi qu’aux risques et périls de l’acheteuse. De plus, ils soutiennent que Mme Simard n’a pas fait preuve de prudence et de diligence avant d’acquérir leur propriété qu’ils utilisaient comme chalet trois saisons et dont la construction originale date de plus de 60 ans [6] . Ils considèrent également qu’en plus d’être injustifiés, les dommages-intérêts réclamés sont exagérés. QUESTIONS EN LITIGE [ 5 ] 1. L’immeuble vendu est-il affecté de vices cachés ? [ 6 ] 2. Les vendeurs en sont-ils responsables comme la vente est intervenue sans aucune garantie ainsi qu’aux risques et périls de Mme Simard ? [ 7 ] 3. Mme Simard a-t-elle été prudente et diligente ? [ 8 ] 4. Quel est le montant de la réduction du prix de vente applicable si l’immeuble est affecté de vices cachés ? ANALYSE ET DÉCISION [ 9 ] Pour les motifs qui suivent, le Tribunal rejette la demande de Mme Simard avec les frais de justice [7] . [ 10 ] 1. L’immeuble vendu est-il affecté de vices cachés ? [ 11 ] 2. Les vendeurs en sont-ils responsables comme la vente est intervenue sans aucune garantie ainsi qu’aux risques et périls de Mme Simard ? [ 12 ] 3. Mme Simard a-t-elle été prudente et diligente ? [ 13 ] Le Tribunal ne peut donner raison à Mme Simard quant à ces questions. [ 14 ] Le fardeau de la preuve en matière civile repose sur la prépondérance des probabilités. Il se décline en un fardeau de présentation et un fardeau de persuasion. [ 15 ] Les articles 2803 et 2804 du Code civil du Québec le confirment : 2803. Celui qui veut faire valoir un droit doit prouver les faits qui soutiennent sa prétention. Celui qui prétend qu’un droit est nul, a été modifié ou est éteint doit prouver les faits sur lesquels sa prétention est fondée. 2804. La preuve qui rend l’existence d’un fait plus probable que son inexistence est suffisante, à moins que la loi n’exige une preuve plus convaincante. [ 16 ] Nous reproduisons les propos de l’honorable Monique Dupuis, J.C.Q. qui l’illustrent: [13] La preuve présentée à la Division des petites créances doit répondre aux règles habituelles de preuve. Ainsi, la partie à qui incombe la charge de la preuve doit l'établir de façon prépondérante au moyen d'éléments factuels pertinents en droit et en faits. La règle de la prépondérance de la preuve exige que celui à qui elle incombe démontre que l'existence des faits qu'il invoque est plus probable que leur inexistence . Il ne s'agit pas de démontrer par une certitude absolue ces faits. En l'absence de prépondérance de preuve, la partie qui ne peut se décharger de son fardeau perd sa cause [8] . (nos soulignements) [ 17 ] Comme le symbolise l’honorable Nathalie Drouin, J.C.Q., Mme Simard doit présenter une preuve claire et convaincante, qui dépasse la simple possibilité, sans qu’il soit par ailleurs nécessaire d’atteindre la certitude; on parle d’un seuil de conviction de plus de 50 % permettant de faire pencher la balance en sa faveur [9] . [ 18 ] Elle doit donc établir que l’existence du vice caché est plus probable que son inexistence. [ 19 ] En effet, un recours judiciaire en vices cachés repose sur les critères suivants : 43 En effet, afin de réussir la démonstration du bien-fondé de leur démarche à l'égard des vices cachés, les demandeurs doivent donc rencontrer certains critères bien précis 4 , lesquels sont prévus à l' article 1726 du Code civil du Québec : 1726. Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l'acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l'usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l'acheteur ne l'aurait pas acheté, ou n'aurait pas donné si haut prix, s'il les avait connus. Il n'est, cependant, pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l'acheteur ni le vice apparent; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert. 44 Ainsi, pour qu'un défaut constitue un « vice caché » au sens de la loi, il doit être : - Caché; le défaut doit être invisible ou non perceptible malgré un examen raisonnable que ferait un acheteur prudent et diligent dans les mêmes circonstances, sans qu'il soit nécessaire de recourir à l'examen d'un expert. - Grave; le défaut doit rendre le bien impropre à son usage ou en diminuer l'utilité de façon telle que l'acheteur ne l'aurait pas acheté ou n'aurait pas payé un prix si élevé s'il avait su. - Inconnu de l'acheteur; si le vendeur a dénoncé le défaut à l'acheteur de façon claire et sans l'induire en erreur, ce dernier ne pourra prétendre à un vice caché. - Existant au moment de la vente; le défaut doit être antérieur à la vente, ou du moins sa cause doit l'être lorsque les effets du défaut se matérialisent après la vente. 45 Une cinquième exigence, la dénonciation, est prévue à l' article 1739 du Code civil du Québec : 1739. L'acheteur qui constate que le bien est atteint d'un vice doit, par écrit, le dénoncer au vendeur dans un délai raisonnable depuis sa découverte. Ce délai commence à courir, lorsque le vice apparaît graduellement, du jour où l'acheteur a pu en soupçonner la gravité et l'étendue. Le vendeur ne peut se prévaloir d'une dénonciation tardive de l'acheteur s'il connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer le vice. 46 Ainsi, dans un délai raisonnable, l'acheteur doit informer le vendeur que le bien est affecté d'un défaut, et ce, pour lui permettre de vérifier la situation et/ou de remplir ses obligations. Dans certains cas, la dénonciation ne sera pas exigée, notamment en cas d'urgence ou de risque de dégradation du bien. […] 52 Et s'il est d'usage répandu de se prévaloir d'une inspection préachat par un spécialiste en la matière, cela n'est pas une exigence absolue. 53 D'ailleurs, l' article 1726 du Code civil du Québec est clair sur la question; il suffit d'agir de façon prudente et diligente « sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert ». 54 Cette précision a fait son apparition lors de la refonte du Code civil du Québec en 1991, s'agissant d'une codification de l'approche jurisprudentielle nuancée et préconisée par la Cour d'appel notamment dans l'affaire Jacpar. 55 En matière de vices cachés, la prudence et la diligence attendues d'un acheteur varie selon les circonstances particulières de l'affaire, l'âge de l'immeuble, le contexte ou le statut de l'acheteur ou du vendeur, ou encore selon leurs comportements respectifs 6 . [10] (nos soulignements) [ 20 ] Dans l’arrêt Leroux c. Gravano [11] , la Cour d’appel du Québec résume ainsi les dimensions objective et subjective des conditions d’application de cette garantie de qualité : [40] Pour se prévaloir de la garantie légale contre les vices cachés, quatre conditions doivent donc être respectées : (i) que le bien soit affecté d’un vice grave, l’intensité de cette gravité ayant été définie par la jurisprudence à partir des expressions « impropre à l’usage » et « diminuent tellement son utilité »; (ii) que le vice existait au moment de la vente; (iii) que le vice soit caché, qualité qui s’évalue objectivement et qui est accompagnée d’une obligation de s’informer; et (iv) que le vice soit inconnu de l’acheteur, qualité qui s’évalue subjectivement et dont le fardeau de preuve appartient au vendeur. […] [42] Concernant la troisième condition, la jurisprudence enseigne que le vice qui est caché est un vice que l’acheteur raisonnablement prudent et diligent n’aurait pas constaté. Il s’agit d’une norme objective. La qualification d’un vice comme caché ou apparent est une question de droit […]. (nos soulignements) [ 21 ] De plus, comme l’a exprimé, l’honorable Steve Guénard, J.C.Q. dans Dubuc‑Quevillon c. Machado , quant à l’obligation de l’acheteur de se renseigner [12] : 56 En effet, l' article 1726 C.c.Q. ne réfère pas à un vice visible . Le législateur y réfère à une notion juridique de vice caché et de vice apparent. Le caractère apparent d'un vice peut découler de la constatation d'un indice, voire de plusieurs, amenant l'esprit vers une nécessaire vérification additionnelle. 57 En effet, l'acheteur ne peut demeurer passif. Il a lui-même une obligation positive de se renseigner 36 . L'inspection qu'il effectue doit être raisonnable, sérieuse et attentive 37 , bien qu'elle puisse demeurer sommaire 38 . L'obligation de cet acheteur consciencieux en est une de moyens, et non de résultat. 58 La référence à un expert n'est pas normalement requise, conformément au libellé de l' article 1726 (2) C.c.Q , précité. Cependant, chaque cas est d'espèce et les gestes devant être posés par un promettant-acheteur s'évalueront, au cas par cas, à la lumière de l'ensemble des circonstances. (nos soulignements) 59 La Cour Suprême du Canada, dans l'arrêt ABB inc c. Domtar inc 39 , résume ainsi, succinctement, le test applicable : 42 Dans le cadre du régime de garantie contre les vices cachés, l'expertise de l'acheteur représente aussi un élément pertinent de l'analyse, mais à un niveau différent de celle du vendeur. En effet, alors que l'expertise de ce dernier permet de déterminer l'étendue de son obligation de dénonciation, l 'expertise de l'acheteur sert plutôt à évaluer si le vice est caché ou apparent. Ainsi, plus l'acheteur connaît le bien qu'il acquiert, plus le vice affectant ce bien est susceptible d'être considéré comme apparent. Le vice apparent est celui que l'acheteur a décelé ou qu'il aurait pu déceler au moment de la vente en raison de ses connaissances (art. 1523 C.c.B.C. et art. 1726 , al. 2 C.c.Q. ). Cette exigence impose donc à l'acheteur une obligation de se renseigner en procédant à un examen raisonnable du bien. Dans tous les cas, le test consiste à se demander si un acheteur raisonnable placé dans les mêmes circonstances aurait pu déceler le vice au moment de la vente. 51 Le caractère caché du vice s'apprécie selon une norme objective, c'est-à-dire en évaluant l'examen fait par l'acheteur en fonction de celui qu'aurait fait un acheteur prudent et diligent de même compétence : P.-G. Jobin, « Précis sur la vente », dans La réforme du Code civil (1993), vol. 2, 359, p. 466; M. Pourcelet, La vente (5e éd. 1987), p. 149. Autrement dit, on ne s'interroge pas simplement sur l'ignorance du vice; on cherchera aussi à déterminer si un acheteur raisonnable placé dans les mêmes circonstances aurait constaté le vice. [Le Tribunal souligne] 60 Ainsi, un vice n'est pas caché du seul fait qu'un acheteur ne l'aperçoit pas ou qu'il n'en détecte pas la présence 40 . Il n'est pas caché non plus du seul fait qu'un acheteur n'en apprécie pas la gravité 41 . (notre soulignement) [ 22 ] Quant aux caractères caché et inconnu du vice, les auteurs, Pierre-Gabriel Jobin et Michelle Cumyn, traitent ainsi du devoir de se renseigner ainsi que de renseigner respectivement de l’acheteur et du vendeur : La Cour suprême a décidé que, dans la vente, les obligations d'informer le cocontractant et, pour celui-ci, de se renseigner sont absorbées par le droit de la garantie de qualité ; c'est ainsi que l'acheteur a une obligation de se renseigner, en personne raisonnable selon les circonstances. L'acheteur doit procéder à un examen raisonnable du bien et seuls les vices qui échappent à un tel examen sont jugés cachés. Les tribunaux se réservent manifestement un large pouvoir...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\nla dénonciation : a) la finalité de la dénonciation est triple, soit (1) permettre au vendeur de vérifier s’il s’agit d’un vice pour lequel il peut être tenu responsable en vertu de la garantie de qualité, (2) constater les dommages causés et, (3) le cas échéant, effectuer son obligation de garantie – travaux correctifs ou remplacement du bien – à moindre coût; b) dans le cas d’un vice fondé sur la garantie de qualité, la dénonciation doit décrire le vice, alors que celui fondé sur la garantie du droit de propriété, elle doit préciser la nature du droit ou la prétention du tiers; c) elle doit être faite par écrit, cette condition de forme n’étant toutefois pas impérative selon la jurisprudence; d) elle doit être faite dans un délai raisonnable de la connaissance du vice allégué, sauf si le vendeur connaît le vice ou qu’il ne peut l’ignorer, ce dernier ne pouvant alors se plaindre de la tardivité de la dénonciation, sous réserve du délai de prescription applicable; e) elle constitue une condition de mise en œuvre du recours fondé sur la garantie de qualité, bien que certaines exceptions puissent être invoquées, notamment en cas d’urgence, de négation de responsabilité par le vendeur ayant connaissance du vice ou encore de renonciation, expresse ou implicite, au bénéfice de la dénonciation; f) en raison de l’objectif recherché par les dispositions relatives aux garanties légales, soit la protection de l’acheteur, le défaut de dénonciation entraînera l’irrecevabilité du recours entrepris s’il en résulte un préjudice réel pour le vendeur, par opposition à un simple préjudice de droit. [39] En l’instance, bien que le vice ait dûment été dénoncé au vendeur, la dénonciation doit être considérée comme insuffisante ou inadéquate, puisqu’elle ne permet pas la réalisation des trois objectifs visés par cette démarche. [40] Le vendeur est informé du vice, mais on l’empêche de constater le dommage causé, de même qu’on l’empêche de corriger le vice, le cas échéant, à moindre coût. [41] Le but de la dénonciation est atteint lorsque le vendeur est informé d’un vice avant les travaux et lorsqu’il a eu l’occasion de vérifier la nécessité et le coût de ceux ‑ ci. [42] Il faut également conclure que l’insuffisance de la dénonciation est de nature à préjudicier aux droits du vendeur. Ce dernier se voit privé de toute opportunité, non seulement de vérifier l’existence du vice et de le réparer, mais également de rassembler les éléments de preuve permettant de repousser la présomption prévue à l’ article 1729 C.c.Q. et de faire la démonstration que le défaut résulte d’une mauvaise utilisation du bien. [44] C’est l’intransigeance de Thivierge qui rend insuffisante la dénonciation en l’instance. Dans les circonstances, le tribunal doit rejeter la réclamation. [44] (nos soulignements) [ 45 ] Il s’agit d’une condition de fond à la mise en œuvre de la garantie de qualité, si applicable [45] . [ 46 ] Finalement, le Tribunal préfère le témoignage des vendeurs qui est davantage concluant et convaincant tout en étant compatible avec la preuve documentaire [46] . Quant au fardeau de la preuve relevant de Mme Simard, la balance ne penche donc pas de son côté [47] . [ 47 ] La non-production d’un rapport d’expertise ne permet pas à Mme Simard de soutenir une preuve de fait personnel fautif, de connaissance de vices cachés non dévoilés et de l’existence de ceux-ci. La connaissance de vices cachés, ni le fait personnel fautif ne sont présumés contrairement à la bonne foi [48] . Mme Simard n’a pas établi les fausses déclarations auxquelles elle prétend. [ 48 ] Quant au respect des règles de preuve et de procédure par les parties, le Tribunal adhère à l’extrait suivant de la décision rendue par l’honorable Jean‑François Mallette, J.C.Q. : [23] En effet, le régime procédural applicable à la Division des petites créances a été adopté afin de favoriser l’accès à la justice . [13] Malgré la procédure simple et moins formelle, il ne s’agit pas d’un tribunal d’équité. Les règles de droit et de preuve y trouvent application [14] . [49] (notre soulignement) [ 49 ] 4. Quel est le montant de la réduction du prix de vente applicable si l’immeuble est affecté de vices cachés ? [ 50 ] Le Tribunal n’a pas à répondre à cette question ayant conclu que la preuve ne permet d’écarter l’effet de la clause de non-garantie légale ainsi qu’aux risques et périls de Mme Simard ni d’établir l’existence de vices cachés. POUR CES MOTIFS, LE TRIBUNAL : [51] REJETTE la demande de la demanderesse, Jeanne d’Arc Simard; [52] LE TOUT , avec les frais de justice contre la demanderesse, Jeanne d’Arc Simard incluant les frais judiciaires applicables au dépôt de la contestation de 211 $ payables par la demanderesse, Jeanne d’Arc Simard aux défendeurs, Richard Brunet et Geneviève Saulnier. __________________________________ ISABELLE LABRANCHE, J.C.Q. Date d’audience : 3 octobre 2024 [1] Le prix de vente annoncé est de 115 000 $, pièce P-1; les vendeurs ont acheté l’immeuble avec garantie légale le 20 mars 2013 au coût de 42 000 $; procès-verbal d’audience du 3 octobre 2024 ordonnant sa production. [2] Acte de vente notarié du 23 juillet 2021, pièce P-6, page 2; l’annonce, la déclaration du vendeur et l’offre d’achat sont au même effet, pièces P-1 à P-3. [3] Avec intérêts au taux légal, et l’indemnité additionnelle prévue à l’ article 1619 du Code civil du Québec ainsi que les frais de justice. [4] Du 27 juin 2021, pièce P-2. [5] Soumission du 7 mars 2022 provenant de Les Entreprises de Constructions St-Raymond inc. d’une somme de 9 265 $ plus les taxes applicables et facture numéro 47318 du 31 août 2022 au même montant totalisant 10 652,43 $, pièce P-15; le cabanon est aussi déconstruit le 31 mai 2023 par le même entrepreneur pour un montant de 1 416,84 $, pièce P-15. [6] Annonce, pièce P-1. [7] Article 340 du Code de procédure civile . [8] Pierre-Louis c. 9235-5255 Québec inc. (Animal Expert) , 2023 QCCQ 4363 ; articles 2803 et 2804 du Code civil du Québec . [9] Aubry c. Gestion V Millette inc. (9385-5870 Québec inc.) , 2024 QCCQ 803 , paragraphe 41 ; article 2804 du Code civil du Québec . Voir également : Bosnière c. Arbour , 2022 QCCQ 8972 (CanLII) , paragraphes 35 et 36 , citant à la note 29 : F.H. c. McDougall , 2008 CSC 53 , EYB 2008-148155 , par. 46 . [10] Aubry c. Gestion V Millette inc. (9385-5870 Québec inc.) , précité, note 9, citant aux notes 4 à 6: ABB inc. c. Domtar inc., 2007 CSC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 RCS 461, EYB 2007-126361; Placements Jacpar Inc. c. Benzakour , 1989 QCCA 976 (CanLII) et Bélanger c. Alarie , 2018 QCCS 2919 , EYB 2018-296240 (CanLII). [11] 2016 QCCA 79 . [12] 2023 QCCQ 6537 , citant aux notes 36 à 41 : ABB inc c. Domtar inc , 2007 CSC 50 , EYB 2007-126361 ; Landry c. Bonneville , 2018 QCCA 1666 , EYB 2018-302756 ; Marcoux c. Picard , 2008 QCCA 259 , EYB 2008-129346 ; Rivard c. Asselin , 2019 QCCA 302 , EYB 2019-307566 ; Placement Jacpar inc. c. Adbelamid Benzakour , 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) ; 2007 CSC 50, EYB 2007-126361 ; Garcia Lorenzo c. Miga (Migas Home Inspections) , 2016 QCCA 1661 , EYB 2016-271415 ; Beaudet c. Bastien , 2007 QCCQ 13454 , EYB 2007-127587 ; Thomassin c. Fortin , 2020 QCCS 920 , EYB 2020-349853 ; Cistellini c. Juchereau, 2012 QCCS 1776 , EYB 2012-205844 ; Lévesque c. Harvey , 2007 QCCQ 13458 . [13] Pierre-Gabriel JOBIN et Michelle CUMYN, La vente , 4 e édition, Montréal, Éditions Yvon Blais , 2017, paragraphe 173 (références omises). [14] ABB inc. c. Domtar inc . , 2007 CSC 50 (CanLII) , [2007] 3 R.C.S. 461, paragraphes 51 et 54 ; supra , notes 11 et 13. [15] 2019 QCCQ 5443 , citant entre autres aux notes 8 et 9 : Gestion Paradigme inc. c. Belisle , 2012 QCCS 3545 , paragraphes 255 et 259 . [16] Tremblay c. Immeubles Perron ltée , 2024 QCCA 719 , citant aux notes 4 et 10 : Blais c. Laforce , 2022 QCCA 858 , EYB 2022-453124 ; Théberge c. Durette , 2007 QCCA 42 , paragraphe 83 . [17] Acte de vente notarié du 23 juillet 2021, pièce P-6, page 2; articles 2818 et 2819 du Code civil du Québec . [18] Pièces P-1 à P-3. [19] Pièce P-7 du 23 juillet 2021. [20] Tremblay c. Immeubles Perron ltée , précité, note 16, paragraphe 31; article 1733 du Code civil du Québec , 1er alinéa. [21] N’étant pas un vendeur professionnel, la présomption de connaissance de l’ article 1729 du Code civil du Québec ne s’applique pas; article 1733 du Code civil du Québec , 1er alinéa. [22] Pièce P-6; Tremblay c. Immeubles Perron ltée , précité, note 16, paragraphes 21 à 23; articles 1732 et 1733 du Code civil du Québec , 2 e alinéa. [23] Article 2805 du Code civil du Québec . [24] Tremblay c. Immeubles Perron ltée , précité, note 16, paragraphe 24; pour un cas d’application similaire, voir l’autorité déposée par les défendeurs : Ste-Marie c. Rodrigue , 2024 QCCQ 321 ; le Tribunal ne réfère pas à l’autre autorité produite par les défendeurs puisque la vente en cause y est intervenue avec la garantie légale : Thibault c. Lavoie , 2019 QCCS 562 . [25] Denys-Claude LAMONTAGNE, Droit de la vente , 4 e édition, Montréal, Éditions Yvon Blais, 2019, paragraphe 230 . [26] Placement Jacpar inc. c. Benzacour , 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , EYB 1989-63151 (C.A.). [27] Lettre de Constructions Réjean Audy inc. du 4 novembre 2021, pièce P-8; Déclaration pour valoir témoignage de Benoit Miron du 20 novembre 2021, pièce P-10; photographies non datées, pièce P-5 et photographies du 25 novembre 2021, pièces P-11 et P-12 dont M. Miron serait l’auteur alors qu’il réfère à celles du plombier; article 554 du Code de procédure civile . [28] Les témoignages d’opinion sont réservés aux experts et les témoins ordinaires se doivent de témoigner sur des faits : article 2843 du Code civil du Québec . Cependant, un témoin qui n’est pas un profane dans une matière peut être entendu sur des faits dont il a une connaissance personnelle. Ce principe reconnu dans Graat c. La Reine , 1982 CanLII 33 (CSC) , [1982] 2 RCS 819, paragraphes 58 et 59 est toujours d’actualité. Pour des cas d’application, voir : 9251-5402 Québec inc. c. 7872579 Canada inc ., 2018 QCCA 1978 , paragraphe 8 ; Maman c. Compagnie Blanca Canada inc. , 2015 QCCA 1899 , paragraphe 5 ; Dionne c. Climatisation Labelle 1996 inc ., 2015 QCCS 5107 , paragraphes 87 à 89 ; Pétrifond Fondation compagnie ltée c. Construction GMR inc ., 2010 QCCQ 11617 , paragraphe 25 . Voir également : Agropur c. Cegerco Construction inc. , 2005 CanLII 32078 (QC CS) , EYB 2005-94595 (C.S.), page 56 et suivantes et Aubry c. Gestion V Millette inc. (9385-5870 Québec inc.) , précité, note 9, paragraphe 82. Voir aussi les paragraphes 95 et 96 où le Tribunal disqualifie à titre de témoin expert, l’entrepreneur qui a réalisé les travaux correctifs, mais pas comme témoin ordinaire. [29] Supra , note 27. [30] Pièces P-5, P-11 et P-12. [31] Annonce, pièce P-1. [32] Pièces D-1 et D-2. [33] Déclaration du vendeur, pièce P-3. [34] Annonce, pièce P-1. [35] Pièce P-4 avec sa preuve de réception du 5 octobre 2021. [36] Supra , note 27. [37] Idem . [38] Supra , note 5. [39] Le coût de cette nouvelle construction serait de 425 000 $. [40] 2017 QCCQ 5448 . [41] 2007 QCCQ 7549 , cité avec approbation dans : Mouakarrassou c. Google inc. , 2021 QCCQ 13045 ; Mouradian c. Syndicat de copropriétaires du 3825 Lévesque Ouest , 2020 QCCQ 3894 (CanLII) , 2020 QCCQ3894; Clairoux c. Plante , 2012 QCCQ 4084 ; Gestion Jude s.e.n.c. c. Desgagné , 2012 QCCQ 2812 et par la doctrine entre autres dans CHATELAIN C\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 4\\nakarrassou c. Google inc. , 2021 QCCQ 13045 ; Mouradian c. Syndicat de copropriétaires du 3825 Lévesque Ouest , 2020 QCCQ 3894 (CanLII) , 2020 QCCQ3894; Clairoux c. Plante , 2012 QCCQ 4084 ; Gestion Jude s.e.n.c. c. Desgagné , 2012 QCCQ 2812 et par la doctrine entre autres dans CHATELAIN C., article 560, Le grand collectif – Code de procédure civile : commentaires et annotations , volume 2 (Articles 360 à 836), 9 e édition, Luc Chamberland (dir.), 2024, EYB2024GCO571. [42] Clairoux c. Plante , précité, note 41. [43] Article 1479 du Code civil du Québec ; Chamberland c. Rossignol , 2013 QCCQ 5952 , paragraphe 75 . [44] 2015 QCCQ 11292 . [45] Adzakpa c. Placements Déméco inc. , précité, note 41, paragraphe 73. [46] Article 2845 du Code civil du Québec . [47] Articles 2803 , 2804 , 2844 et 2845 du Code civil du Québec . [48] Articles 1729 et 2805 du Code civil du Québec , voir également : Charbonneau c. Beaucage , précité, note 15, paragraphes 133 à 137. [49] Lormeau c. Nissan Canada , 2022 QCCQ 3941 , citant aux notes 13 et 14: Lajeunesse c. Assoc. Provinciale des constructeurs d’habitations du Québec Inc ., 2002 CanLII 23692 (C.Q.) et Dubé c. Deslauriers , 2016 QCCQ 3956 .\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2025qccq2812-fr\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 2557.0, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2025qccq2812-fr\", \"title\": \"Simard c. Brunet, 2025 QCCQ 2812 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2025qccq2812-fr\", \"@search.score\": 2557.0, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"lexical\", \"search_type\": null, \"kind\": \"keyword\", \"query\": \"(\\\"vice caché\\\" vente immobilière conditions caractère grave antérieur à la vente inconnu de l’acheteur réellement caché Cour du Québec 2025)\"}, {\"source\": \"lexical\", \"search_type\": null, \"kind\": \"keyword\", \"query\": \"(garantie de qualité vente immobilière Cour du Québec 2025)\"}, {\"source\": \"lexical\", \"search_type\": null, \"kind\": \"keyword\", \"query\": \"(garantie de qualité vente immobilière Cour du Québec 2025)\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2025qccq2812-fr\"}, \"token_count\": 6458, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2025qccq2812-fr\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2006qccs4166\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2006qccs4166-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Kennedy c. Winyard, 2006 QCCS 4166 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"--- BEGIN PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 0\\n2006 QCCS 4166 (CanLII) | Kennedy c. Winyard | CanLII Kennedy c. Winyard, 2006 QCCS 4166 (CanLII) Date: 2006-08-01 File number: 500-17-016813-035 Other citations: AZ-50385949 — EYB 2006-108390 — JE 2006-1691 Citation: Kennedy c. Winyard, 2006 QCCS 4166 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/1p21b > Kennedy c. Winyard 2006 QCCS 4166 JS 0964 SUPERIOR COURT CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF MONTRÉAL No. 500-17-016813-035 DATE : August 1, 2006 ______________________________________________________________________ IN THE PRESENCE OF : THE HONOURABLE JOEL A. SILCOFF, J.S.C. ______________________________________________________________________ CYNTHIA KENNEDY Plaintiff v. CLAIRE WINYARD and ROYAL LEPAGE ÉLITE Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGEMENT ______________________________________________________________________ I. INTRODUCTION [ 1 ] On August 15, 2002, Cynthia Kennedy ( “Kennedy” ) purchased a residential property from Claire Winyard ( “Winyard” ) situate at 541 Beaconsfield Boulevard in Beaconsfield, Québec, (the “Property” ) for an amount of one hundred and sixty thousand dollars. Kennedy claims the Property was affected by latent defects. [ 2 ] In her Motion to Institute Proceedings, Kennedy originally sought a condemnation in damages solidarily against Winyard and Royal Lepage Élite in the total amount of $73,400 representing the costs of repairs necessary to correct the latent defects complained of and to compensate her for the … pain, suffering, stress and inconvenience endured. [ 3 ] A Declaration of Settlement out of Court was filed at trial, as between Kennedy and Royal Lepage Élite. Winyard was not a party to the Settlement and there is no evidence as to the terms of the S ettlement or that Winyard consented to assume sole liable for the amounts claimed by Kennedy. [ 4 ] Winyard contests Kennedy’s claim. She alleges that Kennedy was well aware of the state of disrepair of the Property, that she had visited it on numerous occasions prior to the purchase and that she had informed Winyard, repeatedly, of the nature of the extensive renovations required and which she, in fact, intended to carry out subsequent to the purchase. [ 5 ] Winyard alleges, moreover, that the defects in question were apparent, visible to the naked eye and present during each of Kennedy’s visits to the Property, with the possible exception of certain concealed parts of the electrical system, which Kennedy indicated she intended to modernize in any event, since the system was the original one, installed at the time of construction of the Property in 1956, and was accordingly inadequate by current standards. [ 6 ] Winyard seeks a condemnation in damages against Kennedy in an amount of $85,000 resulting from the … severe stress caused by the subject wrongful claim filed by Plaintiff, which is frivolous, libellous, erroneous, malicious and without legal or moral foundation of any nature . II. PERTINENT FACTS AND PROCEEDINGS [ 7 ] For over 20 years and at all times relevant to the present proceedings, Kennedy owned and operated a business specializing in residential home painting and renovations. She also did home decorating. Her business performed general home renovations assisted by a general “handymen” employed by her as well as by various sub-contractors, hired on a contract basis, to meet the requirements of her contracts with various home owners. She refers to these sub-contractors as “my contractors”. In this sense, although she claims to be only a “house painter” she admits to having acted as a general contractor in home renovations. Her role would be that of a coordinator or project manager acting on behalf of her clients. [ 8 ] Winyard is licensed real estate broker associated with Royal Lepage Élite ( “Royal Lepage” ). [ 9 ] Kennedy and Winyard were acquainted on a personal basis for many years. They often discussed matters of mutual interest, including the residential real estate market. Winyard had previously acted as Kennedy’s broker for the purchase of her previous residence located at 52 Morgan Road in Baie D’Urfé. [ 10 ] On May 29, 2002, Kennedy entered into an Exclusive Brokerage Contract with Royal Lepage Elite for the sale of her Morgan Road residence (the “ Brokerage Contract ” ). Winyard was the exclusive listing agent named under the Brokerage Contract both for the sale of the Morgan Road residence and for the eventual purchase of a replacement residence. [ 11 ] On July 1, 2002, Kennedy accepted a Promise to Purchase the Morgan Road residence. [ 12 ] Shortly after acceptance, by Kennedy, of the Promise to Purchase the Morgan Road residence, Winyard agreed to purchase a new residence for herself on rue Marceau on Île-Perrot. Accordingly, her residence at 541 Beaconsfield Boulevard, the Property, became available for sale. [ 13 ] Winyard retained the services of Kennedy to co-ordinate the renovation work to be performed in her newly acquired residence on Île-Perrot. Kennedy was the project coordinator until, as she testified, … she got thrown of the Marceau job by Winyard. [ 14 ] On July 16, 2002, Kennedy signed and delivered a Promise to Purchase the Property to Winyard in consideration of a purchase price of $160,000. The Promise to Purchase was accepted by Winyard the same day. The following extracts of the Promise to Purchase are relevant for the purposes of the present proceedings: 5.1       DECLARATIONS Unless stipulated otherwise hereinafter, the BUYER has examined the IMMOVABLE and declares that he is satisfied therewith … yes option for building inspection waived . 7.1       ACT OF SALE The parties undertake to sign an act of sale before purchaser's notary, on or before 14 th August 2002 . The BUYER shall be the owner upon the signing of the act of sale. [ 15 ] Kennedy had previously visited the Property as far back as the spring 2002. In her Motion to Institute Proceedings, she alleges in paragraph 11: 11. Pursuant to Plaintiff's visits to Defendant Winyard's residence, and their conversations, Plaintiff assessed the condition of the residence, and could perceive that there was work to be done on the residence. However only superficial work, such as cleaning, interior / exterior painting, updating of the kitchen, the bathroom, and tiles, refinishing floors etc was apparent and could be perceived as being required to be done on the residence; 12. Although it appeared to Plaintiff that only some superficial sprucing up was in order, she nevertheless told her agent that she wished to have a building inspection of Defendant Winyard's residence. To the full knowledge of Defendant Winyard, Plaintiff always had building inspections as conditions, and had building inspections performed, on all her prior offers to purchase on residences. In fact Defendant Winyard had acted as Plaintiff's purchasing agent for the purchase of her former residence located at Morgan Road, for which holding a building inspection was a condition, and was performed. Further, prior to the purchase of Defendant Winyard (sic) residence, at the suggestion of Defendant Winyard, Plaintiff made having a building inspection a condition of another offer to purchase on a residence, which she had made through Defendant Winyard, in June 2002, but which purchase fell through. Thus to the full knowledge of Defendant Winyard, and at Defendant Winyard's own suggestion Plaintiff had always made building inspections a condition of all other offers to purchase made by Plaintiff. Thus building inspections were always suggested by Defendant Winyard to Plaintiff, with the exception however of her own residence. However, to the full knowledge of Defendant Winyard, such building inspection on her own residence would have been difficult to conduct prior to the closing, due to the excessive amounts of personal belongings remaining in the residence, making it impossible for Plaintiff's inspector to have a building inspector get across a room; [ 16 ] Kennedy’s allegations in this regard, are contested and contradicted by the testimony of Winyard and that of several witnesses called by Kennedy on her behalf . The Court will analyze in detail, in Section VI of this Judgment, the conflicting evidence concerning the apparent state of disrepair of the Property and the fact of Kennedy having waived a building inspection. [ 17 ] The Deed of Sale of the Property was signed before Kennedy’s notary on August 15, 2002 and immediately thereafter, renovations were commenced by Kennedy or persons employed by her. [ 18 ] On August 20, 2002, at the request of Kennedy, Neil Maloney, of Maloney Home Inspection Inc. performed a post-purchase inspection of the Property. A Building Inspection Report was prepared and delivered the same day (the “Maloney Report” ). [ 19 ] Some of the corrective measures to rectify the defects complained of were commenced immediately following the execution of the Deed of Sale before receipt of the Maloney Report. Others were performed in the days and weeks following. It is not known when or if in fact all of the corrective measures were ever completed. [ 20 ] Nine months later, by letter dated March 16, 2003 but only received on May 30, 2003, counsel for Kennedy advised Winyard of the presence of: … numerous hidden defects found to exist in the residence, and the stress caused thereby to our client thereby, (sic) our client had suffered and continues to suffer increased physical illness and the associated pain, as well as stress, inconvenience, and the loss of income related thereto. [ 21 ] She claims damages in the total amount of $73,400. [ 22 ] The Property was sold by Kennedy to a third party in January 2005 in consideration of a purchase price of $265,000. III. POSITIONS OF THE PARTIES (1) Kennedy: [ 23 ] In the Motion to Institute Proceedings, Kennedy alleges, inter alia : 26.        Although Plaintiff purchased the residence knowing that it would require some work, only cosmetic work was apparent or could be detected. She did not anticipate, nor could she have anticipated the hidden defects which only revealed themselves subsequent to the purchase, and which Defendant Winyard failed to disclose to her, in particular in relation to those latent defects which she knew of, or which she could not have been unaware of. […] 30. Accordingly Defendant is not only entitled to require the reimbursement of the costs of repairs already effected on an urgent basis, as well as the costs of repairs to be effected, but she is also entitled to claim from Defendants all other damages suffered by her, including the increase of pain, and suffering, stress, and inconvenience caused to her. Plaintiff currently assess the damages she suffered at $73,400.00, comprised of the following: 1   demolition and rebuilding of the playroom                    $15,000.00 2   electrical repair work                                                    $10,000.00 3   front entrance concrete and brick work                        $15,000.00 4   inspectors estimate for work (exclusive of above)       $23,400.00 5   Pain, suffering, stress, and inconvenience                  $10,000.00 TOTAL                                                                               $73,400.00 the whole as appears from various invoices, a copy of which is transmitted to the Defendants with the service of the present Motion as Exhibit P-7; (2) Winyard: [ 24 ] In her Defence , Winyard pleads, inter alia : 34.        That Plaintiff knew exactly what she was purchasing and visited the premises on multiple occasions prior to the purchase of said property and informed Winyard on numerous occasions in respect of the extensive nature of the renovations to be carried out by her, no matter what the condition of the subject property, all of which renovations are claimed in paragraph 30 of the subject Motion; 35.        That it appears that Plaintiff carried out an inspection on or about the 20 th of August 2001, and did not have the courtesy to invite Winyard to be present at said alleged building inspection; that Winyard believes she was “set‑up” by Plaintiff, in respect of the outrageous claim herein; 36.        That Plaintiff proceeded with the said post-purchase building inspection for self-serving purposes in order to extract “a better price” from Defendant Winyard by attempting to have Winyard pay for the renovations which Plaintiff fully intended to proceed with prior to the engagement of the building inspector; 37.        That the subject inspection report of Maloney involved defects of an apparent nature, visible to the naked eye, that were present during the period of the numerous visits by Plaintiff to the subject premises, with the sole exception of the alleged electrical system which Plaintiff intended to modernize in any event, since the electrical system was the original 1956 system that was put into the building when built; IV. QUESTIONS OF FACT AND OF LAW [ 25 ] The Court identifies and proposes to analyze the following determinative questions of fact and of law: (i)         At the time of sale, was the Property affected by latent defects, as contemplated by article 1726 C.C.Q ., thereby engaging the responsibility of Winyard for the damages claimed by Kennedy? In the affirmative; (ii)        What are the damages suffered by Kennedy for which Winyard may be held liable? and (iii)       What are the damages suffered by Winyard for which Kennedy may be held liable? V. ADMISSIONS [ 26 ] In an Admission, signed by counsel for each of the parties on June 14, 2006, they confirm: Defendant, through her attorney, admits that Plaintiff had requested an inspection for all the previous properties she purchased or contemplated purchasing, before 541 Beaconsfield, through Defendant, and Plaintiff acknowledges said admission. VI. ANALYSIS (i) At the time of sale, was the Property affected by latent defects, as contemplated by article 1726 C.C.Q ., thereby engaging the responsibility of Winyard for the damages claimed by Kennedy? [ 27 ] From the proof administered through both the ordinary and the sole expert witnesses heard, the Court considers the following elements to be of particular importance. (1) The Witnesses (a) Kennedy [ 28 ] Kennedy asserts she was not aware of the full extent of the state of disrepair of the Property and of the repairs required. The Court finds her assertion lacking in credibility. She acknowledges that the house looked like it hadn’t been painted for 20 years; that one room which looked like a smoking room required extensive work; and that there was a lot of labour intensive work to be done on the outside. She admits, moreover, that the Property was in a general state of disrepair and, in particular, that there were cracks on the walls and ceilings. She admits, as well, that … you cannot be certain what caused the cracks until you do a further, more detailed inspectio n. Yet she declined to do so prior to purchasing the Property. [ 29 ] Although, in the case of all of her previous residential purchases or for which Promises to Purchase were delivered but not accepted, Kennedy always insisted on a pre-purchase inspection, why proceed, in the case of the subject Property, without such an inspection? Why did she refuse to accept Winyard’s suggestion to have a pre‑purchase inspection performed? Why perform a post-purchase inspection? [ 30 ] She claims the reason for her not insisting on a pre-purchase inspection was that … such a building inspection would have been difficult to conduct prior to the closing, due to the excessive amounts of personal belongings remaining in the residence, making it impossible for Plaintiff’s inspector to have a building inspector get across the room. [ 31 ] Although in examination in chief, Kennedy categorically denied that Winyard ever suggested she should have a pre-purchase inspection, in rebuttal she moderated her testimony in this regard. She testified: I do not recall Claire (Winyard) insisting on me having a building inspection. She repeated: I wanted an inspection condition but it was impossible for an inspection to be done in the house. [ 32 ] The Promise to Purchase was accepted on July 16, 2002. The Deed of Sale was signed one month later, on August 15, 2002. Had indeed, Kennedy wanted a pre‑purchase inspection performed, she could have made it a condition of the Promise to Purchase and that in the intervening period, if indeed it was a problem, Winyard would have been required to clear the Property of some of her personal effects in order that such an inspection might be performed prior to closing. No such provision was in the Promise to Purchase and there is no evidence that Kennedy made any attempts to have the Property inspected during the approximately 30 day period between the Promise to Purchase and the Deed of Sale . [ 33 ] The Court finds that Kennedy was most aware of poor condition of the Property and that seeing she always intended to carry out extensive renovations, she was not concerned. [ 34 ] If, indeed, as she claims, she was not aware of the full extent of the defects in the Property prior to the purchase, the Court finds that Kennedy, a knowledgeable person involved in the home painting and renovation business for over 20 years, seeing the visible signs of d\\n\\n\\n--- END PREPENDED CHUNKS ---\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... day period between the Promise to Purchase and the Deed of Sale . [ 33 ] The Court finds that Kennedy was most aware of poor condition of the Property and that seeing she always intended to carry out extensive renovations, she was not concerned. [ 34 ] If, indeed, as she claims, she was not aware of the full extent of the defects in the Property prior to the purchase, the Court finds that Kennedy, a knowledgeable person involved in the home painting and renovation business for over 20 years, seeing the visible signs of deterioration in the Property, was negligent in not pursuing an in depth pre-purchase investigation with a qualified building inspector. By so doing she could have determined the full extent of the defects in the Property, assuming, as she maintains, she was not already aware of such defects. (b) Derek Nayler (“Nayler ” ) [ 35 ] Nayler testified on behalf of Kennedy. He is a general contractor specializing in home renovations. He prepared an … estimate of the costs involved for the work already done [to the Property] and to complete the job. [ 36 ] His estimate lacks precision and does not assist the Court in assessing the actual cost of repairs. He admits not having seen the basement area prior to the commencement of renovations. Nor can he recall having seen the “illegal wiring” referred to in his estimate. (c) Lina Rocheford (“Rocheford”) [ 37 ] Rocheford testified on behalf of Kennedy. She visited the Property prior to the purchase and confirms that there was a smell of urine in the house. In her words, it was very evident – mind boggling! It was very disturbing. You go into each room and walk out fast because of the smell. [ 38 ] With respect to the outside of the Property, she confirms … it was overgrown, it was quite a mess. You could not tell which were trees and which were weeds. [ 39 ] She summarized her description of the Property by saying … everything was old, ... brittle and coming apart. The house was a mess. You could see the cracks in the foundation next to the steps. It was pretty bad. (d) Walter Thievessen (“Thievessen”) [ 40 ] Thievenssen testified on behalf of Kennedy. He is a retired electrician who had previously done work in the Property in or around May 1999 when Winyard was still owner. He was called to repair a short circuit in the electrical system. [ 41 ] He described the Property in the following manner: The basement was a mess. The electrical panel was very unprofessional. It looked like something had been done not properly. There were loose wires hanging around near the panel. The whole place looked horrible. I didn’t want to do anything. I just didn’t want to be connected with the place. I was just appalled with the whole place. Everything looked really run down. To my standards, I just didn’t want to have anything to do with the place. [ 42 ] He confirmed that the entire electrical system had been redone by someone other than him. The evidence is unclear precisely what work was done, by whom and at what cost. (e) Ed Koenderink (“Koenderink”) [ 43 ] Koenderink testified on behalf of Kennedy. He worked for her as a home renovator. He claims to do “everything” — plumbing, electrical work, framing, plastering, carpentry, painting and flooring. He did some of the renovation work in Winyard’s new home on Île-Perrot. Asked how he would describe the Property, he replied in the following manner: The house was a 50 year old house in need of complete renovations — outdated panels, outdated kitchen… The walls and floor were horribly outdated. There were visible signs of moisture, the smell of animals. The electrical wiring was in disrepair, 50 years old; typical wiring for something that was 50 years old. Just by looking at the fuse panel, you could tell it was old. The grounds were completely overgrown. The front stairs were finished had to be completely redone. [ 44 ] In cross examination, he confirmed that the problems with the electrical system were not unusual for a home of that age. (f) Dean Smith (“Smith”) [ 45 ] Smith was the only witness who testified on behalf of Winyard. He is her “house mate”. After vacating the Property, he offered to clean up the remaining debris left behind but was told by Kennedy that it would not be necessary as she intended to do substantial renovations and that her workmen would do what was necessary. [ 46 ] He confirmed that, at all times relevant to the present proceedings, he and Winyard kept 5 cats and 3 dogs in the Property. [ 47 ] To his personal knowledge, Kennedy had seen the fuse panel and was fully aware that the system had only 100 amps. (g) Winyard [ 48 ] Winyard maintains that she had always, in the past and, in particular, with respect to the purchase of the Property, advised Kennedy that a pre-purchase inspection was highly recommended. Kennedy told her that she knew the Property, intended to do a complete renovation and accordingly, saw no need to have such an inspection. [ 49 ] Seeing that Kennedy refused to have a pre-purchase inspection performed, in order to protect herself, Winyard insisted that the following mention be made in the Promise to Purchase : … option for building inspection waived. [ 50 ] She claims the difficulty to perform a pre-purchase inspection due to the alleged “clutter” in the Property was never raised by Kennedy prior to signing the Promise to Purchase , nor was it ever raised prior to closing. Moreover, she adds, such allegations are totally without merit or foundation. If indeed there had been a pre-purchase inspection clause in the Promise to Purchase and, if the inspector had been denied access to the Property in order to perform the required inspection, she would have had to rectify the impediment. However, according to Winyard, Kennedy “absolutely refused” to have a pre-purchase inspection performed. [ 51 ] She denies ever telling Kennedy that the Property was in good condition. On the contrary, she claims to have shown Kennedy some of the major defects in the Property. (h) Neil Maloney (“Malony”) [ 52 ] Malony testified on behalf of Kennedy. He was hired to do a post-purchase inspection. He visited the Property only once, on August 20, 2002, some 5 days after closing. At the time, renovations to the main bathroom had already commenced. [ 53 ] His report, dated August 20, 2002, is produced. [ 54 ] The majority of observations in the Maloney Report only served to confirm the existence of many of the apparent defects which were visible to the naked eye, with the possible exception of the deficiencies in the electrical system. [ 55 ] At page 12 of the Maloney Report, under the heading “IMPROVEMENT RECOMMENDATIONS”, he writes: For the items listed below, further investigation by another professional is warranted . In this respect, Maloney testified he is only a generalist in building inspections. He confirms that he recommended, amongst other things, that an electrician be retained in order to inspect the electrical system as he had noted several serious deficiencies. There is no evidence that his recommendations were ever followed. [ 56 ] In cross examination, Maloney confirmed that all of the visible signs of decay in the Property led him to believe that this 46 year old property … was tired and that there was likelihood that serious repairs would be required. [ 57 ] He confirmed that the strong smell of urine and feces, present in the Property on the day of his inspection, would have existed prior to the purchase. (2) Was the Property affected by latent defects? [ 58 ] The obligations of Winyard, as seller of the Property, are governed by the provisions of articles 1726 through 1728 C.C.Q . 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without any need of expert assistance. 1727. If the property perishes by reason of a latent defect that existed at the time of the sale, the loss is borne by the seller, who is bound to restore the price; if the loss results from superior force or is due to the fault of the buyer, the buyer shall deduct from his claim the value of the property in the state it was in at the time of the loss. 1728. If the seller was aware or could not have been unaware of the latent defect, he is bound not only to restore the price, but to pay all damages suffered by the buyer. [ 59 ] The scope and application of these provisions were reviewed by Rochon, J. (as he then was) in Lavoie v. Comtois [1] and followed by the Court of Appeal in Blanchard v. Guertin [2] . In Lavoie , Rochon, J. writes : …Ceci étant dit comment doit-on interpréter les dispositions du Code civil du Québec à cet égard? L'acheteur prudent et diligent d'un immeuble procède à un examen visuel attentif et complet du bâtiment. Il est à l'affût d'indice pouvant laisser soupçonner un vice. Si un doute sérieux se forme dans son esprit il doit pousser plus loin sa recherche. D'une part, on ne peut exiger d'un acheteur prudent et diligent une connaissance particulière dans le domaine immobilier. D'autre part, on ne peut conclure au vice caché si le résultat d'un examen attentif aurait amené une personne prudente et diligente à s'interroger ou à soupçonner un problème. À partir de ce point l'acheteur prudent et diligent doit prendre des mesures raisonnables, selon les circonstances, pour connaître l'état réel du bâtiment. Il ne saurait se replier sur son manque de connaissance si son examen lui permet de soupçonner une anomalie quelconque. Il faut donc examiner, suivant chaque cas d'espèce, la conduite d'un acheteur prudent et diligent. Antérieurement à 1994 on exigeait également de l'acheteur qu'il soit prudent et diligent. Sans revenir à l'ancienne règle jurisprudentielle au sujet des experts, il est possible dans certains cas que le fait de ne pas recourir à un expert pourra être perçu en soi, comme un manque de prudence et de diligence. Le tribunal ne veut pas réintroduire dans notre droit une exigence spécifiquement exclue par le législateur en 1994. Par ailleurs, cette exclusion ne saurait être interprétée comme autorisant l'acheteur à agir de façon insouciante ou négligente. Cet acheteur ne fera pas preuve de prudence et de diligence alors qu'il existe des indices perceptibles pour un profane, s'il ne prend pas les moyens (y compris le recours à des experts le cas échéant) de s'assurer que l'immeuble est exempt de vice. […] En fait, les demandeurs ne pouvaient ignorer ce vice. Ils en ont peut-être sous‑estimé l'ampleur mais cela n'a pas pour effet de transformer un vice apparent en vice caché. (Our emphasis) [ 60 ] In Blanchard [3] , the Court of Appeal writes at paragraphs 15, 17, 18 and 20: 15. Cela dit, il est indéniable que la pourriture de la structure de bois de la tourelle n'était pas visible. Toutefois, le nombre et la gravité des fissures apparentes, de traces d'efflorescence et des marques de colmatage, œuvre du vendeur, étaient suffisants pour alerter un acquéreur prudent et commander une vérification en profondeur de l'état du bâtiment en faisant, si nécessaire, appel à un expert. […] 17. Tous les experts entendus reconnaissent qu'une expertise préachat aurait souligné la mauvaise qualité des travaux du vendeur et la nécessaire infiltration d'eau par les fissures ouvertes. Jacques Reeves, l'architecte assigné par le couple Sauriol, tout en affirmant que l'état de pourriture de la structure ne pouvait pas être décelé, reconnaît cependant qu'il eût signalé les risques du bâtiment: R. Dans mon rapport pré-achat? Q. Oui. R. J'aurais indiqué oui, il y a des fissures et il y a des risques d'infiltration d'eau, il faudrait faire une vérification plus approfondie. 18. Par ailleurs, selon l'ingénieur Pomminville, un témoin assigné par les vendeurs, il aurait été prudent au regard de l'âge et de l'état des parements du bâtiment de faire appel à l'hydrographie et à la thermographie. […] En somme, un examen un tant soit peu attentif de la surface des parements aurait mis en évidence une abondante et sévère fissuration qui, compte tenu de l'âge du bâtiment, laissait présager un problème plus sérieux, ce qui ouvrait la porte à une investigation plus poussée. Or, sans par ailleurs être intrusif, des tests permettaient de renforcer la conviction d'une situation déjà considérée problématique. […] 20. Certes, les appelants n'auraient pas eu la certitude de l'existence de la structure pourrie de la tourelle, mais suffisamment d'indices pour demander au vendeur une expertise plus poussée ou une garantie explicite de bon état, ou renoncer à l'achat ou, enfin, acquérir malgré tout en acceptant d'assumer le risque d'un défaut sérieux et sa conséquence, les coûts afférents à la réparation. (Our emphasis) [ 61 ] The warranty of quality imposed upon a seller, as contemplated by articles 1726 C.C.Q . and following does not protect a negligent or reckless buyer from his own acts and omissions, nor of those of professionals hired by him to perform a pre-purchase inspection. In this regard, the Court of Appeal, LeBel, J.A. (as he then was), in Placement Jacpar Inc . v. Benzakour , [4] writes, at page 2315: Le régime juridique des garanties légales du vendeur ne constitue pas un système de protection de l'acheteur imprudent ou incompétent. Il impose un standard de conduite à l'acquéreur d'un bien meuble ou immeuble. C'est à la définition de cette norme que se relie le problème de l'expertise. On ne saurait en effet toujours considérer comme caché ce qui n'a pas été constaté ou ce qui a été mal compris. Dans un arrêt souvent cité, ( Dallaire c. Villeneuve [1956] B.R. 6 ) M. le juge Pratte affirmait que le législateur n'avait pas conçu la garantie contre les vices cachés pour remédier à la légèreté ou à l'imprudence d'un acheteur: Sur ce point, la doctrine, plus d'une fois affirmée par cette Cour, est à l'effet qu'un défaut n'est pas caché par cela seul que l'acheteur ne l'a pas aperçu, ou qu'il n'en a pas apprécié la gravité; et que celui qui n'a pas les connaissances requises pour juger de l'état d'une chose qu'il va acheter doit s'assurer le concours d'une personne capable de le renseigner : l'art. 1522 C.C . n'est pas destiné à remédier aux conséquences de la légèreté ou de l'ignorance. (Id., 9 et 10) En achetant un bien, l'acquéreur est tenu à une obligation de prudence et de diligence. Cette dernière exige qu'il prête une attention suffisante à l'objet qu'il examine. ( Arcand c. Clément [1971] C.A. 69 ) Il faut évaluer le comportement de l'acquéreur par rapport à la norme objective de l'acheteur raisonnable et prudent. […] Depuis la jurisprudence est revenue à une position plus nuancée — sans doute, moins exigeante pour l'acquéreur — moins soucieuse, peut-être, de la vieille maxime caveat emptor . Elle impose l'examen de l'ensemble des circonstances de chaque cas. Elle prend en considération des facteurs tels que le statut du vendeur, s'il est professionnel ou non, celui de l'acquéreur, la nature, l'âge et le prix du bâtiment, le type de vice et, parfois, le comportement même des parties. (Citations added to text / our emphasis) [ 62 ] See as well Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur [5] , at pages...\\n\\n\\n\\n--- BEGIN APPENDED CHUNKS ---\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n'examen de l'ensemble des circonstances de chaque cas. Elle prend en considération des facteurs tels que le statut du vendeur, s'il est professionnel ou non, celui de l'acquéreur, la nature, l'âge et le prix du bâtiment, le type de vice et, parfois, le comportement même des parties. (Citations added to text / our emphasis) [ 62 ] See as well Jeffrey Edwards, La garantie de qualité du vendeur [5] , at pages 170 and 171: Il paraît tenir pour acquis que la norme de l'acheteur raisonnable est toujours inconciliable avec l'aide obligatoire d'un expert. En général, le principe est exact. Cependant, dans certaines circonstances, l'acheteur ne peut satisfaire à la norme de l'acheteur prudent et diligent en agissant seul. Envisageons le cas d'un acheteur profane désireux d'acquérir un ordinateur d'un certain âge, d'une grande complexité et à un prix élevé, c'est-à-dire plusieurs millions de dollars d'un vendeur ordinaire; ou encore celui d'un acheteur profane d'un immeuble qui, constatant certains indices inquiétants, n'a pas la connaissance nécessaire pour écarter la possibilité d'un vice. Dans les deux hypothèses, l'acheteur agissant seul n'a pas la connaissance requise pour effectuer et compléter un examen selon la norme d'un acheteur « prudent et diligent ». S'il omet de demander de l'aide, il agit de manière négligente. [ 63 ] Counsel for Kennedy suggests that Winyard was aware of the full extent and the seriousness of defects complained of and should have disclosed them to her. She argues that, in the circumstances, due to Winyard’s alleged reticence and lack of complete disclosure, Kennedy was led to believe that the defects observed were not of a serious nature, and that, accordingly she had no obligation to pursue further investigation of the full extent of the defects observed. [ 64 ] Although the provisions of article 1726 C.C.Q . have somewhat modified the rights and obligations of the parties previously existing under article 1523 and following of the Civil Code of Lower Canada , the buyer's recourses in the presence of le dol ou le mensonge du vendeur have not changed in substance. [ 65 ] Notwithstanding Kennedy’s assertions in this regard, the Court finds no credible evidence of the existence of either le dol ou le mensonge on the part of Winyard. More particularly, the credible evidence does not support counsel's submissions that Kennedy was induced into a false sense of security due to the alleged fraud, false representations or assurances of Winyard concerning the lack of seriousness of the defects observed. [ 66 ] There is no credible evidence that Winyard ever made any representations to Kennedy concerning the state of disrepair of the Property other than to acknowledge and warn her that it needed extensive work. [ 67 ] The Court finds that the defects complained of were “apparent” as contemplated by law and the jurisprudence. The visible signs and manifestations of the underlying serious problems affecting the Property should have prompted any prudent and diligent purchaser, to conduct a pre-purchase investigation to determine the extent and cause of the defects noticed, especially in light of the fact that the Property was approximately 56 years old and showing visible signs of decay, as unanimously acknowledged by Kennedy’s very own witnesses. [ 68 ] The Court must conclude that Kennedy was either negligent in not requiring a pre-purchase inspection or, at the very least, knew what she was purchasing and assumed the risks inherent in acquiring a property showing such noticeable signs of disrepair and decay. If, as she claims, she was not aware of the full extent of the disrepair of the Property, she certainly could not be unaware of these noticeable signs or indications of serious decay throughout. All of the other witnesses heard on this matter confirmed, quite dramatically, the lamentable condition of the Property. [ 69 ] If we are to accept Kennedy’s professed ignorance as to the extent of the disrepair of the Property and her assertion that had she been so aware, she would never have purchased same or would not have paid so high a price, then the Court finds that in failing to have a pre-purchase inspection performed, Kennedy was the author of her own misfortune and must bear the consequences. Her conduct does not meet the standards of prudence and diligence imposed consistently by the jurisprudence and the doctrine. [ 70 ] If, on the other hand, as is the finding of the Court, Kennedy knew exactly what she was purchasing and was well aware of the extensive nature of the renovations which she intended to carry out, regardless of the condition of the Property, then in such case Kennedy’s claim must similarly fail. [ 71 ] The Court finds the assertions of Winyard in this regard more credible and, accordingly, must answer the first question of fact and of law in the negative. [ 72 ] Moreover, and under reserve of the foregoing, the Court finds that Kennedy was in default to respect her obligation to advise Winyard in writing of the alleged defects in question in a timely fashion and within a reasonable delay as required by article 1739 C.C.Q . [ 73 ] Kennedy claims to have advised Winyard verbally of the defects complained of immediately following receipt of the Maloney Report. Winyard denies ever having seen the Maloney Report prior to the commencement of the present proceedings or of having been made aware, by Kennedy, of its content before receipt, on or about May 30, 2003, of the Demand letter dated March 16, 2003. [ 74 ] Notwithstanding the requirements of article 1739 C.C.Q . to provide a notice in writing within a reasonable delay, the jurisprudence has accepted, in certain specific circumstances, that the notice may be verbal, … à la condition que le vendeur soit effectivement avisé de la situation reprochée et que cela lui permette de procéder à une vérification. [6] [ 75 ] The Court having heard both parties on this subject finds no credible evidence to support Kennedy’s contentions that she had ever advised Winyard verbally or otherwise, of the defects complained of prior to the March 16, 2003 demand letter. Winyard’s denial of Kennedy’s contentions in this respect is more credible. Indeed, the only mention in the demand letter itself, of any prior complaint by Kennedy to Winyard subsequent to the purchase of the Property, was limited to the alleged presence of flees in the house. Our client called Ms Winyard to let her know she had mail, and informed her of the flea problem, in good faith letting Ms Winyard know that she might consider spraying herself (sic) at her new residence since she had evidently just moved the problem there. (Our emphasis) [ 76 ] See as well in this regard, Sévigny et Gagnon v. Lefrançois et Roberge [7] . [ 77 ] The Court finds that Kennedy has failed to respect the notice requirements of article 1739 C.C.Q . and for this additional reason her claim must be denied. (ii) What are the damages suffered by Kennedy for which Winyard may be held liable? [ 78 ] In light of the Court's findings concerning the preceding question of fact and law, it is not necessary to address the cost of the corrective measures purportedly undertaken by Kennedy, nor the damages claimed by her for … the pain, suffering , stress and inconvenience allegedly endured. [ 79 ] However, since these issues have been raised by the parties in their pleadings, and for the benefit of the higher courts should one or other of the parties choose to seek relief by way of appeal, to the extent that these matters are not already addressed, the Court will further analyze the evidence, or lack thereof, in this regard. [ 80 ] Kennedy claims an amount of $63,400 representing the costs of repairs already effected and those to be effected plus an additional amount of $10,000 for pain and suffering, … the whole as appears from various invoices, a copy of which is transmitted to the Defendants with the service of the present Motion as Exhibit P-7. [ 81 ] Although entitled “Various Invoices”, Exhibit P-7 is nothing more than an estimate, prepared by Derek Nayler more than one year after closing, for some of the alleged repairs. As previously mentioned in the analysis above, his estimate lacks precision and does not assist the Court in assessing the actual costs of repairs. There are no supporting documents produced to justify the estimates. Moreover, his estimates do not even correspond with of the costs of repairs claimed by Kennedy, the details of which are set out in paragraph 30 of the Motion to Institute Proceedings. [ 82 ] Kennedy claims to have paid several workers for work performed on the Property and to have received other services, free of charge, from her brothers and sisters. There is no evidence of any invoices submitted by the paid workers, nor is there any evidence of payment to any one or more of them. Similarly there is no evidence of the precise nature of the work performed by any of them, including her brothers and sisters. [ 83 ] The only invoice produced in support of her claim was a Contrat de Service/Facture dated August 16, 2002 for services rendered by “L’Ex Terminateur J.M.” to control fleas present in the Property. The Facture produced for these services indicates “N/C” or no charge. [ 84 ] Aside from Exhibit P-7, and with the exception of the vague and ambiguous testimony of Kennedy, there is no evidence whatsoever, admissible in law, which could possibly justify a condemnation against Winyard for the amounts claimed for the costs of repairs. [ 85 ] Concerning the … increase of pain, and suffering, stress, and inconvenience caused to her , Kennedy testified that the stress associated with the Property caused her increased pain from the neuralgic condition she has had for quite some time. Medical reports produced on her behalf confirm that Kennedy had suffered from neuralgic pain since 1999 and that the pain is intensified when she is under stress. A list of prescription drugs taken by Kennedy to address this and other medical conditions is produced. [ 86 ] Although the Court is satisfied that Kennedy does indeed suffer from neuralgia, and has in the past taken medication to treat the condition, there is insufficient credible evidence to support her claim that there is a causal connection between the “stress” caused by the alleged latent defects in the Property and the increased pain and discomfort associated with her medical condition . [ 87 ] In light of the foregoing, Kennedy’s action will be dismissed. (iii) What are the damages suffered by Winyard for which Kennedy may be held liable? [ 88 ] In her Defence, Winyard alleges: 41.        That Defendant Winyard has been under doctor’s care since June 2003, and has been treated for depression and dizziness, heart palpitations, severe stress, skin rashes and dryness and other symptoms of severe stress caused by the subject wrongful claim filed by Plaintiff, which is frivolous, libelous, erroneous, malicious and without legal or moral foundation of any nature; 42.        That the subject proceedings have caused enormous anxiety and anguish unto Claire Winyard, in respect of which she reserves the remedy to amend the Cross-Demand herein to a sum substantially larger than the damages incurred to date, inclusive of damages in all areas to herself including loss of income, health and other prejudice suffered by her arising from the subject proceedings and abusive claim of Plaintiff; [ 89 ] She seeks a condemnation against Kennedy in an amount of $85,000 in compensation for the damages suffered. [ 90 ] There is no credible evidence, as to fault, causality or quantum, to support this claim and accordingly it will be dismissed, but without costs, seeing the findings of the Court with respect to the principal demand. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT : [ 91 ] DISMISSES Kennedy’s Motion to Institute Proceedings ; [ 92 ] THE WHOLE with costs; [ 93 ] DISMISSES Winyard’s Cross-Demand; [ 94 ] THE WHOLE without costs. ____________________________________ JOËL A. SILCOFF, J.S.C. Me. Sharon Kennedy Mc Ininch Mac Dougall Attorneys for Cynthia Kennedy Me. Alain Mongeau Attorney for Claire Winyard Dates of hearing : June 12, 13, 14 and 15, 2006 [1] 1999 CanLII 11787 (QC CS) , [2000] R.D.I. 36. [2] (C.A.) 500-09-012354-023; April 23, 2004. [3] Id., note 2. [4] 1989 CanLII 976 (QC CA) , [1989] R.J.Q. 2309 (C.A.). [5] Montréal, Wilson et Lafleur, 1998. [6] 2006 QCCQ 4989 (CanLII) , 650-32-001956-056 (C.Q.), March 3, 2006; authorities cited. [7] 2001 CanLII 56872 (QC CS) , 200-17-001507-995 (C.S.), December 6, 2001, Godbout, J.\\n\\n\\n--- END APPENDED CHUNKS ---\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2006qccs4166-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.018373794853687286, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2006qccs4166-en\", \"title\": \"Kennedy c. Winyard, 2006 QCCS 4166 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2006qccs4166-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.018373794853687286, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2006qccs4166-en\"}, \"token_count\": 11421, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccs/2006qccs4166-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq10376\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq10376-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Conrad c. Construction Fairway inc., 2014 QCCQ 10376 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2014 QCCQ 10376 (CanLII) | Conrad c. Construction Fairway inc. | CanLII Conrad c. Construction Fairway inc., 2014 QCCQ 10376 (CanLII) Date: 2014-10-24 File number: 500-32-125353-104 Citation: Conrad c. Construction Fairway inc., 2014 QCCQ 10376 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/gf8ws > Conrad c. Construction Fairway inc. 2014 QCCQ 10376 COURT OF QUEBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUEBEC DISTRICT OF MONTREAL TOWN OF MONTREAL Civil Division No: 500-32-125353-104 DATE: October 24, 2014 ______________________________________________________________________ BEFORE THE HONOURABLE SCOTT HUGHES, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ GERALD WAYNE CONRAD […]Waverley (Nova Scotia) […] Plaintiff v. CONSTRUCTION FAIRWAY INC. 5858 de la Côte des Neiges, suite 602 Montreal (Quebec) H3S 2S1 Defendant ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] Gerald Conrad is claiming damages from Fairway Construction Inc. (Fairway), the builder, in 1968, of his home. [ 2 ] Mr. Conrad alleges that the ¨backfill¨ used under the cement floor slab of his home was contaminated by pyrite. He was obliged to have the pyrite removed prior to selling his home in 2010, at the purchaser's demand. [ 3 ] Mr. Conrad claims some $ 11,005.71 that he limits to $ 7000 in order to fall within the jurisdiction of the Small Claims Division. [ 4 ] Fairway contests both the necessity to remove the backfill, as well as the damages claimed. THE FACTS [ 5 ] Mr. Conrad acquired his home in 1999. This home had been built by Fairway in 1968. It had changed hands approximately five (5) times before Mr. Conrad purchased it. Most of the past owners are either deceased or have not been retraced. [ 6 ] In 2010, Mr. Conrad put his home up for sale in preparation of his retirement and move to Nova Scotia. A potential purchaser discovered pyrite when inspecting the property and rescinded his offer. [ 7 ] Mr. Conrad then accepted another offer to purchase, conditional to his removing the contaminated backfill and replacing the cement slab in the garage, as well as repairing certain cracks in the foundation elsewhere. [ 8 ] This work, initially evaluated at $ 14,278.68, ultimately cost less. According to Mr. Conrad, the contractor discovered that most of the backfill under the garage slab was in fact original earth. Therefore, a large section in the center of the garage did not require removal. The contractor, after negotiation, accepted to reduce its price to $ 11,005.31. [ 9 ] Two experts were heard at trial. In his report dated August 3rd, 2010 [1] , François Hamel concluded that the pyrite swelling potential index (SPPI) under the garage slab was 7 and that under the basement slab was 17. The SPPI is an indication of the quality of the backfill and not a percentage of pyrite. Mr. Hamel stated that both these levels constitute a ¨low risk¨ of swelling. This lead him to conclude as follows, concerning the basement slab: Considérant l'IPPG faible calculé, l'âge avancé du bâtiment (construit en 1968 selon les informations obtenues) ainsi que l'épaisseur plutôt faible de la pierre de remblai rencontrée au sondage S1 (12,0cm), il est improbable que des désordres significatifs associés au gonflement par la pyrite apparaissent ultérieurement . Néanmoins, considérant le caractère gradué du remblai, nous ne pouvons totalement exclure que des désordres superficiels puissent survenir au niveau de la dalle si les conditions propices au développement du processus de sulfatation sont rencontrées, soit l'humidité, l'oxygène, la chaleur, etc. Toutefois, nous sommes d'avis que ceux-ci n'affecteraient pas les murs de fondation du bâtiment . (Emphasis added) ( Exhibit D-1) And concerning the garage slab: Lors du relevé visuel, plusieurs fissures plutôt rectilignes et de type en étoile, d'ouverture fine à importante (ouverture maximale de 10mm), ont été observées en surface de la partie visible du plancher de béton. Des dénivelés mineurs (amplitude d'environ 5mm) accompagnaient également ces fissures par endroits . Il est toutefois de notre avis, en fonction des connaissances actuelles et de l'expérience acquise par histoires de cas, que les désordres relevés ne seraient pas directement associés au gonflement sulfatique du remblai sous la dalle de béton . Ceux-ci pourraient s'être produits pour différentes raisons (déformations différentielles, retrait du béton, etc) que la présente étude ne permet pas de préciser. Compte tenu de l'IPPG évalué en laboratoire ainsi que des caractéristiques de la pierre de remblai (nette, nature pétrographique, dureté élevé, porosité faible, etc), il est improbable que du gonflement significatif associé à la pyrite ne surviennent ultérieurement . (Emphasis added) And as a general conclusion: Par conséquent, aucune intervention ni travaux de réfection motivés par la présence d'une pierre de remblai potentiellement gonflante ne sont recommandés. (Exhibit D-1) [ 10 ] As for Mr. Guy Forest [2] , retained by Mr. Conrad, he evaluated the SPPI at 22 in the garage and at 28 in the basement. This constitutes a ¨low to moderate¨ risk.         Mr. Forest concludes concerning the basement: The sample of backfill from the basement has a swelling potential qualified as low to moderate (SPPI of 28). The damages observed on the concrete slab (cracks and heaving) could in part be related to the presence of pyrite, but there may also be other causes , which cannot be determined by the present study (subsidence, shrinkage, etc.). The geologist is of the opinion that some damages could evolve in the future limited to an aesthetic level. By ¨damages¨ we man cracks with or without heaving on the concrete slab, without affecting the wall foundations . (Emphasis added) ( Exhibit P-3) and as to the garage: The sample of backfill from the garage has a swelling potential qualified as low to moderate (SPPI of 22). The damages observed on the concrete slab (cracks and heaving) could in part be related to the presence of pyrite, but there may also be other causes, which cannot be determined by the present study (subsidence, shrinkage, frost, etc.). The geologist is of the opinion that some damages could evolve in the future. By ¨damages¨ we mean cracks with or without heaving on the concrete slab. Since the backfill is relatively thick, there could also be lateral thrusts on the wall foundations . (Emphasis added) ( Exhibit P-3) [ 11 ] His general conclusion is: It is presently not imperative to do repairs in the basement and the garage . The visual survey shows no important visible damages as at today (approximately 42 years) and it is possible that it continues this way . Your property may be eligible to the provincial grant program regarding pyrite. We suggest you contact your municipality and ask them if it is the case. (Emphasis added) ( Exhibit P-3) [ 12 ] The discrepancies between the SPPI levels found by each expert is probably due to the different sites used for excavation. As well, Mr. Forest adds the following reservation to his report: The conclusions are based on the assumption that the nature of the samples taken and the conditions underneath them are representative of the crushed stones and the conditions underneath the concrete slab . (Emphasis added) ( Exhibit P-3) THE LAW [ 13 ] Mr. Conrad has sued Fairway directly without suing his vendor. Although typically each purchaser would sue its vendor all through the chain of property, the Civil code of Quebec specifically allows the recourse as instituted: 1730. The manufacturer , any person who distributes the property under his name or as his own, and any supplier of the property, in particular the wholesaler and the importer, are also bound to warrant the buyer in the same manner as the seller . [3] (Emphasis added) [ 14 ] The presence of pyrite in the backfill under a floor slab is worrisome to vendors and purchasers alike. However, its sole presence is not conclusive proof that the immoveable is affected by a latent defect: [28] Cela dit, la présence de pyrite dans le remblai de la fondation d'une construction ne constitue un vice caché que si la pyrite a commencé à réagir et s'accompagne d'un grand désordre, c'est-à-dire de dommages importants, ou qu'il y a probabilité d'un tel désordre. On ne saurait autrement parler d'un déficit d'usage qui rend l'immeuble impropre à l'usage auquel on le destine ou en diminue sérieusement son utilité. Le juge Walters l'exprime ainsi dans Petit c. Ricard : [126] Comme le vice allégué en l'espèce découle de la présence de pyrite sous la dalle de béton du sous-sol, il importe de rappeler que la seule existence d'un matériau impropre n'est pas en soi un vice caché au sens de la garantie de qualité. Il faut en outre qu'il nuise à l'usage que l'on peut raisonnablement escompter d'un bien de pareille nature. L'existence d'un matériau impropre ou défectueux n'est suffisante que lorsque la menace sur l'usage du bien est grande et réelle. Il appartient donc à l'acheteur de «démontrer l'existence de désordres importants ou la probabilité de désordres importants à l'avenir». (références omises) [4] [ 15 ] The relevant articles of the Civil code concerning latent defects are: 1726. The seller is bound to warrant the buyer that the property and its accessories are, at the time of the sale, free of latent defects which render it unfit for the use for which it was intended or which so diminish its usefulness that the buyer would not have bought it or paid so high a price if he had been aware of them. The seller is not bound, however, to warrant against any latent defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect; an apparent defect is a defect that can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer without the need to resort to an expert. And: 1739. A buyer who ascertains that the property is defective may give notice in writing of the defect to the seller only within a reasonable time after discovering it. Where the defect appears gradually, the time begins to run on the day that the buyer could have suspected the seriousness and extent of the defect. The seller may not invoke the tardiness of a notice from the buyer if he was aware of the defect or could not have been unaware of it. [ 16 ] In order to conclude to such a defect, the Court must be convinced that: 1) The alleged defect is serious and that it reduces the purchaser's use of the property; 2) It existed prior to the sale; 3) It is hidden notwithstanding a serious examination by the purchaser prior to the purchase; 4) The purchaser has notified the vendor of the defect, within a reasonable delay of its discovery. [ 17 ] According to the Civil Code of Québec , the burden of proving each of these elements lies on the shoulders of Mr. Conrad: 2803. A person seeking to assert a right shall prove the facts on which his claim is based. A person who claims that a right is null, has been modified or is extinguished shall prove the facts on which he bases his claim. 2804. Evidence is sufficient if it renders the existence of a fact more probable than its non-existence, unless the law requires more convincing proof. THE ANALYSIS [ 18 ] This debate here is whether or not a defect has been proven and if it was serious enough to reduce the use of the property by a reasonable purchaser. [ 19 ] Although the experts contradict each other on certain points, there are many more points of convergence than of disparity. [ 20 ] Also, both of the experts appear well versed in their science and have defended their opinions clearly. [ 21 ] This being the case, the preponderance of evidence is not in favour of Mr. Conrad. It was his burden to prove that the level of pyrite under the slabs was the cause of the alleged defects. The expert testimony does not allow such a conclusion. The Court has no reason to prefer Mr. Forest's testimony to that of Mr. Hamel. In such a situation, the only possible conclusion is that preponderance has not been met. [ 22 ] There is more. A detailed study of Mr. Forest's testimony shows that in fact, there is no serious defect. [ 23 ] As for the basement, the experts agree that the damages to the floor are aesthetic and that the foundation walls are not be affected by whatever pyrite there might be. [ 24 ] As for the garage, the experts agree that the cracking could have others causes than the quality of the backfill. It is ¨possible¨ that it will remain the same in the future. Therefore, it is only ¨possible¨ that it might evolve. [ 25 ] The only disagreement lies in the ¨probability of damages other then aesthetic¨. Mr. Forest considers ¨Since the backfill is relatively thick, there could be lateral thrust on the wall foundation¨. Could be does not constitute a preponderance of evidence. [ 26 ] Also, this ¨possibility¨ put forward by Mr. Forest depends on his conclusion that the backfill is ¨relatively thick¨ and on the reservation he added to his report that ¨the condition underneath them are representative of the crushed stones and the condition underneath the concrete slab¨. [ 27 ] The facts show that the contractor found a large quantity of original earth under the garage slab. This was particularly so in the centre of the slab where most of the cracks were found. Mr. Conrad stated that this earth did not need to be removed. [ 28 ] The Court concludes that Mr. Forest's hypothesis as to the quantity of backfill was not founded. This would explain why he added the reservation to his report. The facts are that the condition underneath the slab greatly varied. This renders what he considered as a possibility to be even less so. [ 29 ] Whatever pyrite was present was not sufficient in quantity nor did it constitute a sufficient risk of swelling to be deemed a defect. [ 30 ] Also, the simple fact that the succession of owners of the home, built forty-two (42) years earlier, never suffered any limitation to their use, is confirmation that there is not sufficient seriousness, even if there were a defect. [ 31 ] Judge Richard Landry wrote the following concerning the seriousness required of the alleged latent defects: 1 º le vice doit posséder une certaine gravité L'acheteur doit prouver que le vice cache rend le bien «impropre à l'usage auquel on le destine» ou «diminue son utilité» au point où il «ne l'aurait pas acheté» ou »n'aurait pas donné si haut prix». Pour être considéré comme un «vice», celui-ci doit engendrer un «deficit d'usage », c'est-à-dire qu'il doit, en tout ou en partie, empêcher un acheteur raisonnable de se servir du bien acheté pour l'usage auquel il est normalement destine (ex: infiltration d'eau, système de disposition des eaux usées inadequate, etc.). Cela élimine de la garantie les dégradations, imperfections ou anomalies qui ne privent pas l'acheteur de l'utilisation de ce bien . Est également écarté le vice causé par la perte d'usage «normale», c'est-à-dire provoquée par l'usure, le vieillissement ou la vétusté prévisible (ex: toiture ayant atteint sa durée de vie utile). Est donc écarté le vice mineur ou de peu d'importance qui n'aurait pas empêché un acheteur raisonnable d'acquérir le bien ou n'aurait pas entraîné une réduction significative du prix convenu . Pour apprécier la gravité du vice, le Tribunal peut tenir compte du coût des travaux de réparations, l'importance de la nuisance ou des inconvénients subis par l'acheteur, la diminution réelle de la valeur du bien et le temps que l'acheteur a pris pour le faire réparer. [5] (Emphasis added) [ 32 ] This reasoning applies both to the replacement of the garage slab and to the minor repairs done to the foundation wall. On preponderance, the Court concludes that the cracks were not caused by pyrite and that, in any case, they were not sufficiently serious to justify application of the legal warranty. [ 33 ] Mr. Conrad was in the unenviable position of...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... ur, la diminution réelle de la valeur du bien et le temps que l'acheteur a pris pour le faire réparer. [5] (Emphasis added) [ 32 ] This reasoning applies both to the replacement of the garage slab and to the minor repairs done to the foundation wall. On preponderance, the Court concludes that the cracks were not caused by pyrite and that, in any case, they were not sufficiently serious to justify application of the legal warranty. [ 33 ] Mr. Conrad was in the unenviable position of selling his home to a tough negotiating purchaser. This does not constitute a defect. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: DISMISSES the application; WITHOUT costs. __________________________________ SCOTT HUGHES, J.C.Q. Dates of hearing: September 9 th , 2013, December 16 th , 2013 and June 5 th , 2014 [1] Exhibit D-1. [2] In his report dated: June 28, 2010. [3] See also General Motors Products of Canada Ltd c. Kravitz 1979 CanLII 22 (SCC) , [1979] 1 S.C.R. 790. [4] Leblanc c. Dupuy 2014 QCCS 3226 . [5] Lafond-Tremblay c. Bellerose , 2011 QCCQ 15312 , paragr. 39...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq10376-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.016129031777381897, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq10376-en\", \"title\": \"Conrad c. Construction Fairway inc., 2014 QCCQ 10376 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq10376-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.016129031777381897, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq10376-en\"}, \"token_count\": 4264, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2014qccq10376-en\"}, {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq2821\", \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq2821-en\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Dedam c. Lévesque, 2019 QCCQ 2821 (CanLII)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... 2019 QCCQ 2821 (CanLII) | Dedam c. Lévesque | CanLII Dedam c. Lévesque, 2019 QCCQ 2821 (CanLII) Date: 2019-04-03 File number: 145-32-700059-187 Citation: Dedam c. Lévesque, 2019 QCCQ 2821 (CanLII), < https://canlii.ca/t/j07tc > Dedam c. Lévesque 2019 QCCQ 2821 COURT OF QUÉBEC Small Claims Division CANADA PROVINCE OF QUÉBEC DISTRICT OF BONAVENTURE TOWN OF CARLETON Civil Division No: 145-32-700059-187 DATE: April 3, 2019 ______________________________________________________________________ BY THE HONOURABLE DENIS PARADIS, J.C.Q. ______________________________________________________________________ Anthony Dedam Plaintiff v. Francis Lévesque And Solange Lévesque Defendants ______________________________________________________________________ JUDGMENT ______________________________________________________________________ [ 1 ] In June 2016, Mr. Dedam bought a house from Mr. and Mrs. Lévesque for $ 69,000.00. He argues in court that the house is affected by many latent defects. He capped his claim at $ 15,000.00 in order to file his claim before the small claims division. [ 2 ] The defendants claim that they owe nothing to the plaintiff. It's not about latent defects but about renovating and upgrading the house, they argue, among other things. THE EVIDENCE [ 3 ] Mr. Dedam knows this house. Before it belonged to Mr. Lévesque, it was a Mr. Grégoire who owned it. He acquired the property as an investment, not to live there. Incidentally, he rents the house to his niece Ocean Methot and her spouse Dennis Wysote. [ 4 ] Before buying, Mr. Dedam visited the house twice. He did not notice anything abnormal. Although he knew that the construction dated back to 1960, he did not have it inspected. [ 5 ] Mr. Dedam blames the sellers for not revealing to him the many problems with the house, which he describes as latent defects. The septic tank must be replaced, the electrical system that dates from the construction of the house must be redone, and there is a lack of insulation, in addition to the bad condition of the doors and windows and the presence of insects. [ 6 ] In September 2016, Mr. Dennis Wysote and Ms. Ocean Methot became aware of these deficiencies. This is mentioned at paragraph 6 of the application. Ms. Methot says she pays exorbitant heating costs due to poor insulation and drafts. [ 7 ] Mr. Lévesque acquired the house in June 2016. He bought it for his daughter. However, in the end, she got a job elsewhere, and he put it up for sale shortly after buying it. He did an inspection of the house, he says, which did not reveal defects but renovations to be made. He did not live in the house. [ 8 ] He did some renovations, including repairing a part of the roof and repainting a few rooms, but nothing more. [ 9 ] Mr. Dedam visited twice. Following his first visit, he made a deposit and returned with an attendant before concluding the sale. Mr. Lévesque remembers that they looked at the electrical panel and examined the oil furnace. [ 10 ] As far as he knows, the septic system dates from the construction of the house in 1960. [ 11 ] Mr. Lévesque concludes by mentioning that he was only informed of the alleged defects two years after the sale, which is not a reasonable period according to him. LAW, ANALYSIS AND DECISION [ 12 ] The seller is bound to deliver a good free of latent defects. However, he is not bound to warrant any defect known to the buyer or any apparent defect. An apparent defect can be perceived by a prudent and diligent buyer, without the need to resort to an expert. [ 13 ] Also, the buyer who discovers a latent defect must report it to the seller, in writing, within a reasonable time after its discovery. [ 14 ] On the other hand, the civil law places the burden of proof on the plaintiff, who must prove the facts on which his claim is based. It is therefore for Mr. Dedam to prove the facts which support his claim, on a balance of probabilities. [ 15 ] With respect, the plaintiff has not convinced the Court of the validity of his claim. [ 16 ] Mr. Dedam bought a house built in 1960. When he mentions that the electrical installations are obsolete, he is right, but this is not a latent defect within the meaning of the Civil Code. [ 17 ] As for the doors and windows, they are original to the house. It is not surprising that their energy efficiency is diminished or destroyed. This is not a latent defect, but deterioration due to the passage of time. [ 18 ] Also, Mr. Dedam was reckless in buying a house at a price of $ 69,000.00 without pushing his research any further. There were reasons to be cautious, such as the age of the house, the electrical panel, and the original doors and windows. In fact, the defects he alleges in court are all apparent defects. [ 19 ] Even if the court believed that they were latent defects, it is clear that they were denounced late to the seller. As of September 2016, Mr. Dedam was aware of the alleged defects and it was only on October 26, 2018 that he sent a formal notice [1] to Mr. Lévesque. FOR THESE REASONS, THE COURT: [21] DISMISSES the application. [22] THE WHOLE WITH COSTS. __________________________________ Denis Paradis, J.C.Q. Date of hearing : March 18 th , 2019 [1] Piece P-1....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq2821-en\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.01666666753590107, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq2821-en\", \"title\": \"Dedam c. Lévesque, 2019 QCCQ 2821 (CanLII)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq2821-en\", \"@search.score\": 0.01666666753590107, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"JURISPRUDENCE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:jurisprudence\", \"query\": \"Dans une vente d’immeuble, l’acheteur reproche un vice caché ayant entraîné des dommages liés à des infiltrations et à la dégradation de composantes. La Cour examine si le défaut était antérieur à la vente, s’il était d’une gravité suffisante et s’il demeurait réellement caché malgré une diligence normale. Elle souligne l’ignorance de l’acheteur au moment du transfert et la preuve requise; le tribunal statue sur la réduction du prix et/ou la résolution selon l’analyse du critère.\"}], \"document_id\": \"/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq2821-en\"}, \"token_count\": 1310, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/en/jurisprudence/qc/qccq/2019qccq2821-en\"}], \"doctrine\": [{\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2014-2-i936e636b-69cb-9a50-c18f-9011f3b47f5c-la-garantie-legale-revisitee\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2014-2-i936e636b-69cb-9a50-c18f-9011f3b47f5c-la-garantie-legale-revisitee\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Lachance, Dany - La garantie légale revisitée, dans Cours de perfectionnement du notariat (2014)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... La garantie légale revisitée La garantie légale revisitée Dany Lachance * Table des matières SYNTHÈSE INTRODUCTION 1. DÉFINITION DE LA GARANTIE LÉGALE 2. LA GARANTIE DU DROIT DE PROPRIÉTÉ 2.1 Étendue 3. LA GARANTIE DE QUALITÉ 3.1 Étendue 3.2 Critères d’application 3.2.1 Grave 3.2.2 Non apparent 3.2.3 Inconnu de l’acheteur 3.2.4 Antérieur à la vente 3.3 Vice environnemental 3.4 Obligations de l’acheteur 3.4.1 Prudence et diligence 3.4.2 Inspection préachat obligatoire 3.4.3 Acheteur professionnel 4. LA GARANTIE LÉGALE, EN RÉSUMÉ ! 5. EXCLUSION DE LA GARANTIE LÉGALE 5.1 Exclusion totale 5.1.1 Théorie et impact 5.1.2 Rédaction 5.2 Exclusion de la garantie de qualité 5.2.1 Théorie et impact 5.2.2 Rédaction 5.3 Exclusion de la garantie du droit de propriété 5.3.1 Théorie et impact 5.3.2 Rédaction 5.4 Garantie légale des biens meubles 5.4.1 Théorie 5.4.2 Rédaction 5.5 Déclarations du vendeur 5.6 Clause préventive 5.6.1 Devoir de conseil du notaire face à l’exclusion de garantie légale 5.6.2 Utilité de la clause préventive : éviter des recours ? 5.7 Certificat de localisation 5.8 Instructions hypothécaires CONCLUSION BIBLIOGRAPHIE Législation citée Jurisprudence citée Doctrine citée Site Internet cité [Page 325] SYNTHÈSE On pourrait aisément croire que la garantie légale est bien connue des notaires praticiens, surtout ceux œuvrant en droit immobilier. Pourtant, à la lecture de certains contrats notariés, mais surtout par un survol de la jurisprudence, force est de constater que nous aurions certainement avantage à revisiter nos notions en matière de garantie légale, et ce, afin de bien remplir notre devoir de conseil. Expliquer et vulgariser la garantie légale vient avec la compréhension de ce qu’elle inclut, des obligations intrinsèques à chacune des parties, mais également de ce qui est permis d’exclure et de quelle façon. Notre devoir de conseil étant très large, celui-ci peut être modulé par la rédaction de clauses dans nos contrats notariés précises et adaptées et par la signature de clauses préventives étoffées. L’achat sans garantie légale nous exempte-t-il de l’obligation d’effectuer un examen des titres ? Qu’en est-il de l’obligation du vendeur de fournir un certificat de localisation ? Et la garantie légale des biens meubles lors d’une transaction immobilière ? Autant de questions auxquelles nous tenterons de répondre, ou du moins d’y apporter des pistes de solutions, le tout après avoir revisité nos notions de base en matière de garantie légale. [Page 327] INTRODUCTION La garantie légale est sans aucun doute une notion juridique avec laquelle tous les notaires ont à travailler, mais avec laquelle il est également facile d’oublier ses bases et de peut-être omettre certains éléments essentiels. Avec le rythme effréné des ventes immobilières, le notaire est appelé à conseiller son client sur la garantie légale, que ce soit lors de la rédaction d’une promesse d’achat ou encore lors de la conclusion d’une vente. Savons-nous vraiment ce qu’elle implique ? Et surtout, sommes-nous réellement en mesure de bien l’expliquer à notre clientèle lorsqu’elle est incluse, ou encore, lorsqu’elle fait l’objet d’une exclusion ? La garantie légale devrait probablement faire l’objet de beaucoup plus d’explications auprès des différents intervenants en matière immobilière et il revient au notaire, par son devoir de conseil et son expertise juridique, d’accomplir cet exploit. Loin de nous l’idée de vouloir, tout au long de ce texte, faire une revue exhaustive de cette notion, puisqu’elle l’a déjà été à maintes reprises, mais plutôt de donner des conseils pratiques aux notaires, afin d’alléger leur responsabilité professionnelle et de mieux cerner leurs obligations face aux clients. Nous insisterons également sur l’utilité des clauses préventives, pratique qui tend à se développer, et qui a une utilité non négligeable en matière immobilière. 1. DÉFINITION DE LA GARANTIE LÉGALE Le principe de la garantie légale a, maintes et maintes fois, été abordé dans la doctrine actuelle et par la jurisprudence afin de pouvoir mieux la comprendre et de pouvoir circonscrire son domaine d’application. Elle peut être définie comme étant l’une des obligations du vendeur, lors de la vente d’un bien immobilier ou mobilier, par laquelle le vendeur est tenu de garantir au futur acheteur le droit de propriété et la qualité du bien vendu 1 . Bien entendu, tout acheteur désire avoir un bien dépourvu de défectuosités, et surtout qui puisse faire l’objet de l’utilisation projetée. Heureusement, cette garantie existe de plein droit, de par l’effet de la loi 2 et seule une [Page 328] exclusion claire et précise pourra y faire échec 3 . Un acheteur constatant un vice de titre ou encore un vice caché pourra donc entreprendre des procédures judiciaires pour faire réparer les dommages subis ou pour obtenir compensation. De façon plus détaillée, la garantie légale comporte deux volets, soit la garantie du droit de propriété et la garantie de qualité. Il s’agit de deux aspects complètement différents, qui ont chacun leur utilité et leurs critères d’application. Nous commencerons par aborder succinctement la garantie du droit de propriété pour ensuite regarder plus profondément la garantie de qualité. 2. LA GARANTIE DU DROIT DE PROPRIÉTÉ 2.1 Étendue La garantie du droit de propriété, présente aux articles 1723 à 1725 du Code civil du Québec et reliée à l’obligation du vendeur de fournir un titre clair 4 , peut se résumer à l’effet que le vendeur « doit garantir l’acheteur contre l’existence de tout droit qui prive celui-ci de son droit de propriété ou le restreint 5 ». En effet, plusieurs limitations peuvent survenir et elles peuvent être de deux ordres, soit une limitation de droit public 6 ou encore une limitation de droit privé 7 . Elle doit cependant exister au moment de la vente pour que l’acheteur puisse se prévaloir de cette garantie, à moins que la limitation résulte de faits que l’on peut imputer à la responsabilité du vendeur 8 , et elle ne doit pas avoir été déclarée par le vendeur préalablement à la vente 9 . Un notaire, dans sa pratique courante, aura l’occasion de voir plusieurs exemples de limitations du droit de propriété, telles que [Page 329] l’empiètement d’un tiers, l’existence d’une servitude, l’existence d’hypothèques légales ou conventionnelles, l’existence d’un avis de vente pour taxes ou encore d’un préavis de saisie avant jugement, une restriction en matière de zonage, de construction ou encore en matière d’environnement 10 . Inutile de dire que la majeure partie de la responsabilité professionnelle du notaire en matière de transaction immobilière se retrouve dans la détection de la présence de limitations du droit de propriété. Il est de la responsabilité du notaire, en faisant un examen des titres minutieux et en étudiant les différents documents fournis au soutien de la transaction, tel que le certificat de localisation, de relever les limitations applicables, d’apporter les correctifs appropriés, s’il y a lieu 11 , ou encore de dénoncer aux clients lesdites limitations afin que ces derniers achètent en toute connaissance de cause ou puissent négocier en conséquence avec le vendeur. Le devoir de conseil du notaire prend ici tout son sens, car l’acheteur devra être conseillé sur les impacts et résultantes de prendre la propriété telle quelle, avec la privation d’une partie de son droit de propriété ou encore d’exiger une correction ou une compensation. Il ne faut donc pas minimiser l’explication de l’impact des servitudes existantes sur la propriété, s’assurer de la compréhension du client et plus encore, s’assurer de la qualité de l’examen des titres, car l’obligation du vendeur de fournir un titre clair est en quelque sorte déchargée sur le notaire de l’acheteur 12 . Il est de mise, lors de chaque transaction immobilière, d’y accorder une attention particulière afin d’éviter de faire des gestes machinaux ou donner des explications trop générales. Afin de s’assurer de la bonne compréhension du client sur toute limitation de droit de propriété trouvée et qui ne sera pas corrigée ou qui continuera d’affecter la propriété après la conclusion de la transaction, nous suggérons fortement l’utilisation d’une clause préventive. Il serait approprié dans la rédaction de la clause préventive de reprendre le libellé de l’arpenteur-géomètre, ou du moins d’y décrire de façon assez précise le problème soulevé et d’y consigner les explications adéquates, afin de s’assurer que l’acheteur comprend l’ampleur du problème et jusqu’à quel niveau la limitation découverte affectera son droit de propriété. La clause préventive sera plus longuement abordée dans les prochaines lignes. [Page 330] 3. LA GARANTIE DE QUALITÉ 3.1 Étendue Le deuxième volet de la garantie légale et sans conteste celui le plus connu de tous, est la garantie de qualité, mieux connue sous la garantie contre les vices cachés. La définition légale de cette notion est présente à l’article 1726 du Code civil du Québec et expose : Le vendeur est tenu de garantir à l’acheteur que le bien et ses accessoires sont, lors de la vente, exempts de vices cachés qui le rendent impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté, ou n’aurait pas donné si haut prix, s’ils les avaient connus. Il n’est cependant pas tenu de garantir le vice caché connu de l’acheteur ni le vice apparent ; est apparent le vice qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert. En d’autres termes, le bien ainsi acheté doit avoir un défaut ou un déficit au niveau de son usage ou de son utilité qui remet en question l’achat même du bien ou le prix payé, lequel vice devait être caché au moment de la vente, et ce, selon l’appréciation des attentes raisonnables de l’acheteur concerné 13 . On peut donc en déduire qu’un bris ou une anomalie, qui n’aurait aucun lien avec l’usage ou l’utilisation ne sera pas considéré comme un vice au sens de la loi 14 . De plus, il ne faut pas confondre la non-conformité ou l’âge du bien et la notion de vice caché qui donne ouverture à la garantie de qualité 15 . En effet, même un bien conforme pourra être affecté d’un vice caché. Plus un bien acheté est vieux, moins l’exigence sur sa fonctionnalité ou quant aux attentes sur son utilisation sera grande 16 . Le vice pourra également prendre plusieurs formes, soit être matériel (détérioration), fonctionnel (défaut usage) ou encore conventionnel [Page 331] (défaut usage spécifique) 17 . Le vice matériel concerne une détérioration ou un bris au niveau du bien comme tel, c’est donc un vice visible qui peut être constaté de façon certaine puisqu’il touche son intégrité, sa conception. Le défaut fonctionnel quant à lui touche quelque chose d’un peu plus intangible, soit le fait pour le bien de ne pouvoir servir à l’usage pour lequel il est destiné. On pensera facilement à un terrain zoné résidentiel mais sur lequel aucune construction ne pourra être levée dû à un moratoire ou une restriction imposé par la Ville. Dans le même ordre d’idées, une maison nouvellement construite qui serait inhabitable à la suite d’un défaut de fabrication, ne pourra servir à l’usage destiné et sera, en conséquence, affectée d’un vice fonctionnel. Finalement, le vice conventionnel, qui est beaucoup plus subjectif, affectera également l’usage du bien, mais l’usage spécifique que l’acheteur voulait en faire et qui était pour lui une des conditions essentielles pour l’achat dudit bien. Un bon exemple de ce type de vice pourrait être un acheteur qui envisage d’acquérir un immeuble de sept logements et pour lequel il soumet une offre d’achat croyant avoir trouvé l’immeuble idéal avec suffisamment de logements pour être rentable. Malheureusement, on découvre par la suite que ledit immeuble est actuellement non conforme au règlement de zonage en vigueur puisque ce dernier n’autorise que les habitations de trois logements et moins dans ce secteur, mais qu’il bénéficie de droit acquis, ayant été construit avant l’entrée en vigueur de la réglementation. Cependant, puisque l’un des logements a été construit sans permis de la Ville, les droits acquis ne touchent que six des sept logements. Puisqu’il existe une probabilité que l’acheteur ne puisse reconstruire le sept logements en cas de destruction totale et de perte des droits acquis, et qu’il était essentiel pour lui d’avoir plusieurs logements pour être rentable et surtout pouvoir rencontrer ses échéanciers financiers, il pourra avoir un recours contre son vendeur. Bien entendu, tout est une question de preuve, et l’acheteur devra prouver le caractère « essentiel » de cette condition pour le qualifier de vice conventionnel. Il est maintenant reconnu que l’acheteur possède un recours contre son propre vendeur, mais également contre tous les autres vendeurs antérieurs du bien affecté du vice caché, à la condition que ce vice ait été existant au moment de la vente en question et à la condition que le lien de causalité soit conservé. On parle évidemment [Page 332] ici de la notion de vente de référence 18 . Ceci pourrait donc être exprimé par le notaire à un vendeur, lors d’une transaction, afin de le rassurer sur la portée de sa responsabilité dans l’éventualité où l’acheteur se retournerait contre lui en matière de vice caché. La majorité des clients ne se soucient pas de la garantie légale en général, sans doute par incompréhension, mais voilà un point qui pourra rassurer les vendeurs. Il est également important de retenir que la garantie couvre non seulement le bien en question, mais également tous ses accessoires, qui devront, eux aussi, être exempts de vices cachés 19 . Dans le cas des transactions immobilières, on peut considérer comme accessoire tout équipement...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 1\\n... , sans doute par incompréhension, mais voilà un point qui pourra rassurer les vendeurs. Il est également important de retenir que la garantie couvre non seulement le bien en question, mais également tous ses accessoires, qui devront, eux aussi, être exempts de vices cachés 19 . Dans le cas des transactions immobilières, on peut considérer comme accessoire tout équipement qui fait partie et qui est intégré à la propriété, comme par exemple les fosses sceptiques, les puits artésiens, les garages ou autres constructions secondaires 20 . Tel qu’exposé précédemment, l’article 1726 du Code civil du Québec contient la définition légale de ce qu’est un vice caché. Cet article est fort riche puisqu’il établit également les critères d’application du recours en vice caché, lesquels critères doivent être présents de façon simultanée afin de donner la possibilité à l’acheteur de poursuivre son vendeur en réparation 21 . 3.2 Critères d’application 3.2.1 Grave La notion de gravité du vice est quelque peu subjective puisqu’elle sera appréciée à la lumière de plusieurs faits : l’intensité du vice, les attentes ou l’utilisation que projetait l’acheteur, l’intégrité physique du bien et la valeur économique relativement à son âge 22 . La gravité du vice est donc une question de faits et pourra être constatée en temps réel, lorsque l’utilisation du bien est déjà affecté, ou [Page 333] encore, lorsqu’il existe une probabilité forte que l’utilisation en soit affectée 23 . On regardera donc jusqu’à quel point l’immeuble est touché et quelle sera l’importance de la limitation de son utilisation. De plus, il y aura évaluation de la diminution de la valeur causée par l’existence du vice découvert 24 . Il est donc difficile de juger rapidement ou d’émettre une opinion sur la gravité d’un vice, sans avoir une connaissance exhaustive des décisions récentes rendues en la matière ou d’avoir une expertise particulière dans ce domaine. 3.2.2 Non apparent La non-apparence du vice, ou encore son caractère « caché », signifie que ce dernier ne pouvait être détecté par l’acheteur prudent et diligent, le tout sans qu’il soit nécessaire que ledit acheteur ait eu recours à un expert en matière d’inspection du bien 25 . En effet, aucune disposition législative n’oblige l’acheteur à obtenir l’avis d’un expert, bien au contraire 26 . La responsabilité de prudence et de diligence de l’acheteur sera abordée dans les prochaines lignes, mais il est possible de la résumer en disant qu’un examen raisonnable du bien est suffisant, mais que l’acheteur ne doit pas se voiler la face et doit approfondir son inspection ou avoir recours à l’expertise nécessaire lorsqu’il y a des indices donnant lieu à un vice 27 . Bien qu’il ne soit pas nécessaire pour l’acheteur de recourir à un expert, notons qu’il a été reconnu qu’un acheteur qui néglige de faire une inspection préachat et qui découvre par la suite un vice qui aurait pu être découvert lors de l’inspection, verra son recours tomber, le vice perdant ainsi son caractère de non apparent puisqu’il aurait pu être trouvé par une simple inspection d’un spécialiste 28 . Inutile de rappeler que le comportement de chaque acheteur sera apprécié en fonction des faits propres à chaque situation et qu’il s’agit, chaque fois, d’un cas d’espèce. Notons également que les déclarations du vendeur auront un impact sur la non-apparence du vice puisque celui-ci a l’obligation [Page 334] de mentionner à l’acheteur tout fait qui pourrait avoir un impact sur la transaction et donc, de tout vice dont il pourrait être affecté. Tout ce qui aura été dénoncé par le vendeur, dans les documents préachat ou encore lors d’échanges verbaux, n’aura plus le caractère de « caché » et ne pourra pas donner lieu à un recours en vertu de la garantie légale contre ce dernier. À l’inverse, si le vendeur embellit la situation ou décide de cacher des éléments matériels ou factoriels à l’acheteur afin qu’il ne découvre pas le vice, celui-ci le rendra non apparent 29 , donnant ouverture à un recours, et cela pourra même être considéré comme du dol face aux tribunaux 30 . Il est donc essentiel que le vendeur prenne le temps de bien répondre aux questions de l’acheteur afin qu’il ne se fasse pas reprocher d’avoir tenté de camoufler une irrégularité et d’avoir à se défendre contre un recours en vice caché et un recours en dommages-intérêts pour dol. Dans la pratique courante des transactions immobilières avec courtier, un formulaire contenant les déclarations du vendeur, souvent présenté en annexe de l’offre d’achat, permet au vendeur de déclarer plusieurs événements ou faits qui touchent la propriété vendue. Afin de s’assurer que l’acheteur a bien pris connaissance de ce document et en comprend la portée, il y a lieu de se questionner sur la pertinence, pour le notaire, d’annexer la déclaration du vendeur à l’acte de vente. Bien que cela puisse certainement avoir plusieurs avantages, dont entre autres celui de protéger le vendeur, cela entraîne une plus grande responsabilité sur la tête du notaire. Avons-nous réellement besoin de cette responsabilité supplémentaire et est-il réellement essentiel de se l’imposer pour la sécurité des transactions ? Poser la question c’est y répondre. Plusieurs ententes peuvent se dérouler entre les parties et il n’appartient pas au notaire de les reprendre toutes systématiquement. D’ailleurs, les actes de vente notariés contiennent majoritairement une clause voulant que les avant-contrats ne sont pas annulés par l’acte de vente, sauf en cas d’incompatibilité. Le notaire n’est donc pas obligé de reprendre les déclarations du vendeur pour que celles-ci tiennent toujours. 3.2.3 Inconnu de l’acheteur Évidemment, pour demeurer un vice caché et donner ouverture aux recours en garantie de qualité, la défectuosité doit être inconnue de l’acheteur au moment de la vente. Si ledit vice était [Page 335] connu, il perdrait en même temps son caractère d’occulte. Il est donc essentiel que l’acheteur n’en ait pas connaissance. La notion de connaissance du vice par l’acheteur découle en partie de l’obligation pour le vendeur de déclarer tout ce qui pourrait affecter la qualité du bien. En effet, l’acheteur pourra parfois constater lui-même certains vices, mais il appartient au vendeur de déclarer tout fait qui pourrait s’apparenter à un vice 31 . Notons que l’importance de cette obligation de divulgation par le vendeur sera modulée en fonction de la qualité du vendeur. Un vendeur ordinaire aura une obligation moindre ; on ne peut s’attendre à ce qu’il déclare des faits dont il ignore l’existence ou qu’il ne peut savoir, dû à son manque d’expérience. À l’inverse, le vendeur professionnel se verra imposer une présomption de connaissance du vice, et donc devra déclarer beaucoup plus de choses à un futur acheteur 32 . Est assimilé à un vendeur professionnel, l’entrepreneur en construction, les vendeurs professionnels ou le vendeur de matériaux de construction 33 . On comprend donc que le fardeau de preuve sera facilité, dans ces cas, pour l’acheteur. De plus, les mêmes commentaires au niveau des déclarations mensongères ou dolosives trouvent ici application, à l’effet qu’elles peuvent aider à rendre inconnu de l’acheteur un vice qui aurait pu autrement être connu, et donc entraîner la responsabilité du vendeur. Dans la pratique notariale, il est important de savoir qu’un droit publié ou un problème faisant l’objet d’une forme de publicité pourrait être considéré comme connu de l’acheteur, car il existe une présomption de connaissance. En effet, deux courants doctrinaux s’affrontent actuellement afin de déterminer l’existence ou non de cette présomption. Un jugement, maintes fois cité et qui reprend ces deux courants, vient reconnaître l’existence de la présomption de connaissance pour tout droit publié et en conséquence, cela ne pourra pas être considéré comme un vice caché, le caractère « inconnu » de l’acheteur ne pouvant être rempli 34 . [Page 336] La même présomption de connaissance pourrait s’appliquer à un acheteur professionnel, et donc son fardeau de preuve sera beaucoup plus grand 35 . Le notaire sera bien avisé de dénoncer tout vice découvert, par exemple en faisant l’examen des titres ou par le biais de divers documents qui lui sont remis, afin d’alléger sa responsabilité et de s’assurer que le client acheteur soit bien au fait de cette situation. En effet, la présomption de connaissance jouant contre l’acheteur concernant tout document publié, elle engendre la responsabilité, pour le notaire, de faire preuve de minutie quant à la découverte de faits publiés qui pourraient affecter la propriété et qui devront être mentionnés à l’acheteur. Nous suggérons, encore une fois, le recours à la clause préventive afin de bien dénoncer le vice affectant la propriété et de conserver une preuve de cette dénonciation, d’éviter un malentendu par la suite ou encore un recours en responsabilité professionnelle contre le notaire. 3.2.4 Antérieur à la vente Le dernier critère d’application est l’antériorité du vice, lequel doit être existant au moment de la vente, et donc du transfert de propriété 36 . Cette preuve repose sur les épaules de l’acheteur, mais l’antériorité sera présumée en matière de vente par un vendeur professionnel 37 . De plus, la preuve sera facilitée par une découverte rapide du vice et la dénonciation rapide par l’acheteur à son vendeur 38 . Plus le vice est découvert tôt à la suite de la vente, plus facile il sera de prouver qu’il existait au moment du transfert de propriété et surtout, moins d’excuses ou d’arguments pourront être avancés par le vendeur pour tenter de s’exonérer de sa responsabilité. En résumé, lorsque l’acheteur découvre sur son bien un vice, un défaut assez grave pour diminuer son utilisation ou encore sa valeur de façon significative et que celui-ci existait au moment de la vente, n’était pas apparent et n’était pas connu de l’acheteur, il y aura ouverture d’un recours contre les anciens propriétaires en garantie légale de qualité afin d’obtenir réparation du préjudice subi. Notons également que ce recours peut être assorti, dans certains cas et conditions qui ne seront pas abordés ici, d’un recours en dommages-intérêts. [Page 337] 3.3 Vice environnemental Puisque les créanciers hypothécaires et les divers intervenants en matière immobilière sont de plus en plus sensibles relativement à la protection de l’environnement et à tout ce qui pourrait l’affecter, il est important d’insister sur un type de vice bien précis, soit le vice environnemental. Un vice caché environnemental sur un immeuble « consiste en la présence de contaminants sur celui-ci et qui le rend inapte à servir à l’usage voulu par l’acheteur » 39 . Cela est donc intimement relié à l’obligation du vendeur de garantir la qualité de l’immeuble vendu, et donc de garantir qu’il est exempt de vices cachés. Rappelons que le vice caché est un défaut du bien qui affecte son usage et soumis à certains critères ; cela pourrait s’étendre à la contamination d’un sol. Un auteur cite plusieurs exemples tirés de la jurisprudence, soit : [...] des déchets enfouis dans le terrain de la maison vendue, la contamination de l’eau potable, l’isolant de la maison avec la mousse isolante d’urée-formol (MIUF), la présence de pyrite dans le matériau de remblai sous la dalle de béton au sous-sol de la maison, du sol meublé d’un ancien cimetière qui empêche de construire dalle sur sol [...] 40 C’est également ici que toute la notion de garantie légale sur l’immeuble et ses accessoires prend tout son sens car certains de ces accessoires pourraient être affectés d’un vice environnemental. On pense aisément aux fosses sceptiques qui déversent certaines matières dans l’environnement ou encore à un puits artésien n’offrant pas l’eau potable promise ou attendue 41 . Collègues notaires, soyez attentifs lors de transactions afin de vous assurer que l’acheteur ne se sera pas voilé la face et aura obtenu les inspections nécessaires. Bien que l’expertise ne soit pas requise, il est sans aucun doute prudent et diligent de faire faire quelques vérifications sommaires pour certains accessoires ou encore, en cas de doute, quant à la contenance du sol. Des conseils judicieux et la rédaction de clauses appropriées auront pour effet d’octroyer à votre travail une valeur ajoutée. [Page 338] 3.4 Obligations de l’acheteur Malgré l’existence d’une garantie légale de facto s’appliquant aux transferts de propriété, il importe de rappeler et d’insister sur certaines obligations qui incombent tout de même à l’acheteur. 3.4.1 Prudence et diligence L’acquisition d’un bien immobilier est sans conteste l’un des plus grands investissements effectués dans une vie. À ce titre, tout acheteur qui projette d’acquérir une propriété doit s’informer, poser les bonnes questions, et surtout doit entreprendre les démarches adéquates auprès des professionnels compétents afin d’effectuer les vérifications nécessaires dès qu’une possibilité de vice est soulevée, afin de dissiper tout doute 42 . Il est donc demandé audit acheteur d’agir de façon prudente et diligente et d’avoir un comportement qu’une personne raisonnable placée dans la même situation aurait, comportement qui a été maintes fois défini par la jurisprudence 43 . Le comportement de l’acheteur sera toujours une question de fait dans les circonstances, et ce sera au tribunal de juger si celui-ci a eu un comportement prudent et diligent. Certains critères d’appréciation ont d’ailleurs été établis par la jurisprudence, tels que l’examen visuel attentif du bâtiment, être à l’affut des indices, l’approfondissement en cas de doute, prendre les mesures raisonnables pour connaître l’état réel du bâtiment, etc. 44 . De plus, certains facteurs modulant cette obligation ont été établis par la doctrine et la jurisprudence : le statut du vendeur, le statut de l’acheteur, la nature du bien, l’âge du bien, le prix du bien, le type de vice et le comportement des parties 45 . Un acheteur s’est déjà fait reprocher par le tribunal de ne pas avoir approfondi les mentions relevées au rapport d’inspection...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2014-2-i936e636b-69cb-9a50-c18f-9011f3b47f5c-la-garantie-legale-revisitee\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.0320020467042923, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2014-2-i936e636b-69cb-9a50-c18f-9011f3b47f5c-la-garantie-legale-revisitee\", \"title\": \"Lachance, Dany - La garantie légale revisitée, dans Cours de perfectionnement du notariat (2014)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2014-2-i936e636b-69cb-9a50-c18f-9011f3b47f5c-la-garantie-legale-revisitee\", \"@search.score\": 0.0320020467042923, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:doctrine\", \"query\": \"La garantie de qualité en vente impose une responsabilité spécifique lorsque le vice répond aux conditions cumulatives : gravité, antériorité, caractère réellement caché et ignorance de l’acheteur. La doctrine insiste sur la méthode d’appréciation « d’hypothèse raisonnable » du degré de dissimulation et sur la charge probatoire relative à la nature et à l’origine du défaut. Elle distingue aussi le vice caché des manquements à une obligation d’information ou des défauts apparents, et discute l’incidence d’une inspection diligente sur l’élément « inconnu ».\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2014-2-i936e636b-69cb-9a50-c18f-9011f3b47f5c-la-garantie-legale-revisitee\"}, \"token_count\": 6990, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2014-2-i936e636b-69cb-9a50-c18f-9011f3b47f5c-la-garantie-legale-revisitee\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/collection-de-droit-2025-2026-vol-6-c-baab7d4c-adbe-89a2-f597-755e097125dd2025-chapitre-i-le-droit-commun-de-la-vente\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/collection-de-droit-2025-2026-vol-6-c-baab7d4c-adbe-89a2-f597-755e097125dd2025-chapitre-i-le-droit-commun-de-la-vente\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Benadiba, Aurore - Chapitre I - Le droit commun de la vente, dans Collection de droit 2025-2026, Volume 6 - Obligations et contrats, Titre II - La vente dans le Code civil et la Loi sur la protection du consommateur\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 10\\n... agissant en personne prudente et diligente. Un profane ne doit pas être obligé de faire appel à un expert pour tenter de découvrir la violation à une limitation de droit public. On sait que, selon le régime général de la garantie des limitations de droit privé, l’acheteur qui découvre un risque d’éviction doit en donner avis au vendeur, par écrit et dans un délai raisonnable (art. 1738 C.c.Q.). Doit-on imposer cette condition même à l’acheteur qui est en face d’une violation à une limitation de droit public 286 ? D’après nous, il faut donner au vendeur la possibilité de contester les prétentions de l’administration et, en conséquence, l’acheteur doit aviser le vendeur de la violation à une limitation de droit public qu’il découvre 287 . Pour réclamer des dommages-intérêts, il doit prouver les avoir subis malgré ses efforts pour en limiter l’étendue. Sa demande sera rejetée s’il revend l’immeuble sans aviser son vendeur des problèmes vécus et que le trop faible prix de revente résulte de son empressement excessif à revendre l’immeuble 288 . 3. La garantie contre l’empiétement L’article 1724 C.c.Q. prescrit que le vendeur est garant de tout empiétement exercé par lui-même et par un tiers qui aurait, à sa connaissance, commencé à l’exercer avant la vente 289 . Il s’agit de ce qu’on appelle communément un trouble de fait. Le trouble de fait est un acte matériel qui diminue ou qui entrave la jouissance du bien vendu, mais sans nécessairement que le vendeur prétende s’appuyer sur un droit quelconque pour agir ainsi. Pour illustrer cette règle, imaginons qu’un acheteur achète un terrain, mais que d’un côté sa haie soit plantée ou que sa clôture soit érigée 290 sur le terrain du voisin. Il y a alors empiétement exercé par le vendeur sur le terrain du voisin. Ce dernier pourra un jour réagir en demandant que la haie soit déplacée de façon à respecter les limites du terrain, ou bien encore, demander une compensation pécuniaire pour accepter que la haie demeure là où elle est. Il pourrait arriver aussi que, de l’autre côté, ce soit le voisin qui ait construit une clôture 45 centimètres à l’intérieur du terrain acheté. L’acheteur devra peut-être prendre un recours pour obliger son voisin à respecter la ligne de séparation des terrains et même, le cas échéant, agir dans certains délais pour empêcher l’accomplissement de la prescription. Un empiètement peut aussi résulter d’un droit de coupe de bois consenti à un tiers avant la vente 291 ; un droit de coupe de bois est opposable à des acquéreurs 292 , ou encore de l’installation de tuyaux ou de composantes d’un champ d’épuration 293 . Si le vendeur, en utilisant son propre lot, contigu à celui qu’il vient de vendre, dépasse la ligne qui sépare les lots et empiète sur le terrain vendu, cette situation est couverte par la garantie des faits personnels, dont le régime, examiné ci-dessous, est très sévère (art. 1732 C.c.Q.). [Page 213] La seule façon pour le vendeur d’échapper à cette garantie est de déclarer l’empiétement lors de la vente, de préférence dans l’acte de vente. Cette garantie fait partie de la garantie du fait personnel du vendeur. 4. Les modifications conventionnelles En général, les parties peuvent déroger aux dispositions du Code civil sur la garantie du droit de propriété, car elles sont supplétives, sans être impératives. Ainsi, les parties peuvent, selon l’article 1732 C.c.Q., augmenter ou diminuer les obligations du vendeur. Mais il existe par contre certaines restrictions impératives : on peut les regrouper autour du vice de titre connu du vendeur et de la garantie du fait personnel du vendeur. Ces principes rejoignent l’article 1474 C.c.Q., qui empêche toute personne d’exclure ou de limiter sa responsabilité dans le cas de préjudice matériel causé par autrui par insouciance, négligence ou faute grossière. a) Le cas du vice du titre connu du vendeur Une clause d’exonération ou de limitation de responsabilité devient inopérante quand le vendeur, connaissant le vice ou ne pouvant pas l’ignorer lors de la vente, ne l’a pas déclaré à l’acheteur (art. 1733 C.c.Q.). Il serait contraire à la bonne foi de garder le silence sur un vice du titre et de prétendre en même temps, par la vente, transférer à l’acheteur un bon titre de propriété. Lorsque le législateur écrit que le vendeur ne pouvait ignorer le vice du titre, il veut dire que ce vendeur était présumé le connaître. Les tribunaux devront décider, cas par cas, quel vendeur est présumé avoir connu le vice du titre. Ils voudront sans doute faire une analogie avec la garantie de qualité, dans laquelle le législateur, aujourd’hui comme hier, prévoit une présomption de connaissance du vice caché; ainsi, un vendeur professionnel sera très souvent présumé avoir connu le vice 294 . La présomption de connaissance pourrait s’appliquer au promoteur immobilier qui a acquis un vaste lot, l’a subdivisé, puis a vendu séparément les subdivisions sur lesquelles il a construit des maisons (art. 1728 C.c.Q.) 295 . Cette règle impérative sur la clause exonératoire comporte elle-même une exception : quand la vente est faite aux risques et périls de l’acheteur et que le vendeur n’est pas un vendeur professionnel, il est complètement libéré de son obligation de garantie, même quand il connaissait le vice ou ne pouvait l’ignorer et qu’il ne l’a pas dénoncé (art. 1733 , al. 2 C.c.Q.). L’acheteur pourra cependant prouver un dol selon l’article 1401 C.c.Q. Si le vendeur a vendu sciemment le bien d’autrui, ceci n’empêchera pas d’invoquer la nullité de la vente en vertu des articles 1713 et suivants C.c.Q. Cependant, un vendeur professionnel ne pourra, dans la majorité des cas, invoquer ni une clause d’exonération ni une clause d’achat aux risques et périls de l’acheteur, ce qui assure à ce dernier la protection minimale de la garantie légale du droit de propriété ou de la qualité 296 . b) Le cas de la garantie du fait personnel du vendeur La garantie du fait personnel du vendeur est fondée sur le vieux brocard selon lequel « celui qui doit garantie ne peut évincer ». Le transfert du droit de propriété est l’un des effets essentiels produits par la vente. Le législateur a donc maintenu le principe selon lequel le vendeur ne peut exclure sa garantie pour ses faits personnels, qu’il s’agisse d’un vice du titre ou d’un empiétement (art. 1732 C.c.Q.). Plus précisément, la règle vise l’empiétement attribuable au vendeur, et l’exercice, par un tiers contre l’acheteur, d’un droit réel qu’a consenti le vendeur. Par exemple, le vendeur aurait vendu deux fois le même bien. Il en est ainsi de l’exercice d’un droit réel par le vendeur lui-même contre l’acheteur, par exemple, une servitude en faveur d’un autre immeuble dont le vendeur est demeuré propriétaire 297 . Il n’existe qu’une façon pour le vendeur d’échapper à la garantie de son fait personnel : c’est de déclarer l’empiétement, le droit ou le vice du titre lors de la vente, de préférence dans l’acte de vente lui-même. La moralité juridique ne permet pas au vendeur de troubler l’acheteur, directement ou indirectement, à moins que la convention ne lui en ait réservé le droit expressément et clairement (art. 1723 C.c.Q.) 298 . [Page 214] Le caractère fondamental de la garantie du fait personnel du vendeur est illustré par le fait que la garantie subsiste malgré la stipulation de certaines clauses qui, dans un autre contexte, peuvent produire des effets juridiques. Ainsi, à moins évidemment que le vice du titre n’ait été déclaré lors de la vente, une clause de vente aux risques et périls de l’acheteur ne protégera pas le vendeur contre son fait personnel 299 . Il en sera de même d’une clause suivant laquelle « l’acheteur acquiert l’immeuble avec toutes ses servitudes actives et passives, apparentes et non apparentes » 300 . C- La garantie de qualité 1. La garantie contre les vices cachés a) La notion L’objectif du législateur en concevant les règles de la garantie de qualité est d’assurer à l’acheteur la pleine utilité du bien vendu. L’expression « garantie de qualité » a remplacé en partie, dans le Code civil du Québec, l’expression traditionnelle « garantie contre les vices cachés » du Code civil du Bas-Canada; la garantie de qualité inclut aussi une règle sur la durabilité (art. 1729 C.c.Q.) 301 . Cette garantie de qualité s’applique uniquement à la vente d’un bien corporel. Elle ne s’applique pas dans le cas de biens incorporels comme les actions d’une personne morale. Ainsi, ceux qui achètent des actions d’une corporation qui leur donne le droit d’habiter un logement doivent être considérés comme des locataires de la personne morale. Si le logement comporte des défectuosités, ils doivent exercer des recours conformément aux règles du louage et non pas en leur qualité d’acheteurs 302 . Dans un premier temps, nous traiterons de la garantie contre les vices cachés (garantie de qualité) 303 . Nous verrons ensuite tour à tour la garantie de durabilité selon le Code civil et la Loi sur la protection du consommateur , la garantie d’aptitude selon cette dernière loi, la garantie de bon fonctionnement de cette même loi, et nous verrons ailleurs comment la garantie contre les vices de construction et les autres garanties du contrat d’entreprise sont intégrées dans la vente d’un immeuble à usage d’habitation, construit par un promoteur ou un entrepreneur (art. 1794 C.c.Q.) 304 . Un vice caché peut être défini comme une défectuosité qui rend le bien « impropre à l’usage auquel on le destine ou qui diminue tellement son utilité que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté, ou n’aurait pas donné si haut prix », s’il l’avait connue (art. 1726 , al. 1 C.c.Q.). Observons que l’acheteur d’un immeuble voué à la démolition ne peut réclamer de réduction ou de dommages-intérêts pour défaut caché, si la démolition révèle la présence d’amiante, nécessitant des précautions additionnelles pour procéder à la démolition; la démolition d’un immeuble n’est pas un usage 305 . De fait, un défaut qui ne cause pas d’inconvénient ou ne diminue pas l’usage d’un bien ne donne pas ouverture à un recours pour vice caché. Ainsi en est-il de l’isolation d’une ancienne maison, au moyen de fragments de charbon de bois, selon une méthode et des normes anciennes, en vigueur lorsque cette maison a été vendue 306 . Les dommages causés plusieurs années après la vente par l’écoulement d’un réservoir d’hydrocarbures désaffecté, quand les lois en vigueur au moment de la vente n’en exigeaient pas l’enlèvement, ne donnent pas ouverture à un recours pour défaut caché. Au moment de la vente, les lieux permettaient l’usage auquel ils étaient destinés 307 . La défectuosité peut être simplement matérielle, lorsque le bien est détérioré ou brisé, par exemple une automobile neuve dont la peinture est égratignée 308 . La [Page 215] défectuosité peut être plutôt fonctionnelle, lorsque le bien ne peut servir à l’usage auquel il est destiné en raison notamment d’un défaut de conception ou de fabrication. Ce sera le cas d’un moteur qui fonctionne mal ou d’une automobile dont l’habitacle n’est pas étanche 309 . Si tous les moteurs d’un modèle donné présentent la même défectuosité de fonctionnement, on pourra songer à un défaut de conception. Si la défectuosité se constate sur un moteur ou quelques-uns seulement, il s’agira peut-être d’un défaut de fabrication, mais le fabricant pourra tenter de réfuter cette allégation en démontrant un mauvais usage ou un mauvais entretien (art. 1729 C.c.Q.). L’incendie qui détruit un tracteur presque neuf résulte plutôt d’un défaut de fabrication, à moins que le vendeur ne démontre qu’un mauvais usage a été la cause de l’incendie 310 . L’incendie provoqué par une sécheuse dont le tambour de rotation a cessé de fonctionner à cause d’une installation et d’un entretien ne suivant pas les directives du fabricant, lui permet de repousser la présomption d’un défaut de conception 311 . Par contre, un système de ventilation, mal installé par le fabricant ou ses préposés, qui ne ventile pas suffisamment un atelier de recyclage de plastique pour respecter les normes environnementales, problème difficile à corriger, permet de résoudre la vente 312 . Un autre exemple de défectuosité fonctionnelle serait l’incapacité d’une pellicule d’emballage de produits congelés sous vide, en l’occurrence des côtes levées, de résister à la perforation par les os et la cuisson 313 . L’appréciation d’un vice matériel ou fonctionnel se fait en fonction de l’usage normal du bien, selon un test objectif 314 . Mais le test peut parfois être subjectif, dans le cas d’une défectuosité conventionnelle. On dit qu’il y a une défectuosité conventionnelle quand le bien ne peut servir à l’usage spécifique que les parties avaient en vue lors de la vente; c’est le cas notamment de l’usage inhabituel révélé par l’acheteur au vendeur lors de la conclusion du contrat 315 , d’un système informatique inapte à accomplir les fonctions pour lesquelles il a été acquis 316 ou d’un système censé permettre de réduire la consommation d’énergie alors qu’en réalité, il l’augmente 317 . Un terrain peut être propice pour différents usages, mais être impropre à la culture biologique. Si l’acheteur n’a pas spécifié cet usage particulier à son vendeur, il risque d’être privé de son recours 318 . Que la défectuosité soit matérielle, fonctionnelle ou conventionnelle, le vice caché est parfois dangereux. Il...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 11\\n... augmente 317 . Un terrain peut être propice pour différents usages, mais être impropre à la culture biologique. Si l’acheteur n’a pas spécifié cet usage particulier à son vendeur, il risque d’être privé de son recours 318 . Que la défectuosité soit matérielle, fonctionnelle ou conventionnelle, le vice caché est parfois dangereux. Il peut causer la mort, des blessures ou des dommages à d’autres biens de l’acheteur. Par exemple, l’incendie causé par une cheminée ramonée à une fréquence insuffisante ne résulte pas d’un défaut intrinsèque de la cheminée ou de son installation 319 . Ainsi, un transformateur ou un câblage défectueux pourrait causer un incendie 320 ou des matériaux défectueux peuvent provoquer un écroulement 321 . Il demeure toujours possible en défense pour le vendeur d’invoquer le mauvais usage ou le mauvais entretien selon l’article 1729 C.c.Q. 322 . Dans les cas qui mettaient en cause un acheteur et un vendeur, la jurisprudence de l’époque du Code civil du Bas-Canada avait tendance à déterminer la responsabilité du vendeur en recourant simplement aux principes de la garantie contre les vices cachés 323 . Il doit continuer d’en être ainsi sous l’empire du Code civil du Québec; en effet, entre l’acheteur et le vendeur, il ne saurait être question d’appliquer les nouvelles dispositions sur la responsabilité du fabricant concernant le défaut de sécurité d’un bien (art. 1468 et 1469 C.c.Q.). Selon leur formulation même, ces dispositions ne s’appliquent qu’à l’égard des tiers, victimes des dommages résultant du défaut de sécurité du bien, en dehors de tout rapport contractuel 324 . On se rappellera que le code de 1994 empêche l’option entre la responsabilité extracontractuelle et la responsabilité contractuelle (art. 1458 , al. 2 C.c.Q.). [Page 216] Le fabricant pourra néanmoins se dégager de sa responsabilité en démontrant que la victime connaissait ou était en mesure de connaître le défaut du bien ou de prévoir le préjudice (art. 1473 C.c.Q.). Nous verrons plus loin les recours des sous-acquéreurs. Lors de l’allégation d’un défaut de sécurité, le fabricant pourra faire état du non-respect des règles prescrites pour l’entretien et l’utilisation du bien 325 . En règle générale, il incombe à l’acheteur de prouver le vice et les conditions d’existence de la garantie (art. 2803 C.c.Q.). Est-il besoin de rappeler que la garantie contre les vices cachés est une obligation de résultat et que, dans le cas d’une telle obligation, il suffit au créancier de démontrer l’absence de résultat 326 . Cependant, la jurisprudence est plus ou moins constante : certaines décisions exigent seulement de l’acheteur qu’il prouve la défectuosité elle-même, l’absence de résultat. D’autres décisions, critiquables, semblent exiger de plus la preuve de la cause, ou l’explication précise de la défectuosité, bien qu’elles permettent à l’acheteur de prouver cette cause par une présomption de fait 327 . b) Les conditions d’existence d’un défaut caché Le Code civil du Québec prévoit cinq conditions relatives à la garantie des vices. Si le vendeur connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer l’existence du vice, il peut, en plus de défrayer la correction du vice, être condamné à indemniser l’acheteur des dommages qu’il a subis en raison de ce vice. 1) La gravité du défaut Le vice doit être grave : il doit diminuer l’usage du bien de telle sorte que, si l’acheteur l’avait connu, il n’aurait pas acheté le bien ou il l’aurait acheté à un moindre prix (art. 1726 , al. 1 C.c.Q.). Pour décider si un vice est assez grave pour donner ouverture à la garantie, on considère tous les aspects de la question (notamment les inconvénients du vice pour l’acheteur et le coût de sa réparation) 328 . Ainsi, on refusera de réduire le prix de vente d’une propriété pour la seule raison que la porte et le plafond du garage étaient isolés avec de la MIUF, alors que l’ensemble de la propriété ne l’était pas. Quoi qu’il en soit, la présence de MIUF dans les murs d’un immeuble n’est pas un défaut grave en l’absence de la preuve des effets nuisibles de ce produit sur la santé des personnes qui l’habitent et de la diminution de valeur de l’immeuble de ce fait 329 . Mêmes conclusions pour la vermiculite 330 . Même remarque pour un isolant contaminé à l’amiante qui, s’il n’est pas manipulé, est un isolant efficace et non nuisible 331 . L’insuffisance d’approvisionnement d’eau par un puits, ou le gel fréquent des tuyaux 332 , peut être considérée comme grave 333 , à moins que le problème ne découle de la vétusté des installations ou de la nature des choses 334 . Un vendeur n’est pas tenu de garantir qu’un puits artésien produise de l’eau en aussi grande quantité qu’un réseau d’aqueduc municipal 335 . Le fait de dire que le terrain vacant est desservi par un système d’aqueduc et d’égout municipal ne donne pas lieu à une réclamation pour défaut caché, parce que le terrain n’est pas raccordé, puisqu’il était possible pour l’acheteur de faire les vérifications qui s’imposaient, d’autant plus si l’agent d’immeuble a exprimé des doutes à ce sujet 336 . On peut également donner comme exemple le vide sanitaire sous une maison, contaminé par des chats qui l’avaient considéré comme une gigantesque litière 337 , un grenier hanté par des chauves-souris 338 , envahi par des [Page 217] rats 339 , des ratons laveurs 340 , ou des murs où des abeilles ont installé leurs ruches 341 et des fourmis charpentières 342 . Il en est de même d’infiltrations d’eau 343 , de la contamination de l’air 344 ou de moisissures à l’intérieur des murs d’un immeuble 345 ou d’une roulotte 346 surtout si elles sont pathogènes 347 . Par contre, si le plancher de béton ne joint pas le mur des fondations d’une maison, cela peut certes constituer un vice caché, mais ce ne sera pas un vice dont la gravité donnera ouverture au recours si la stabilité de cette maison construite depuis une vingtaine d’années n’est pas mise en péril et que l’on n’a pas à se plaindre d’humidité ou d’infiltrations d’eau 348 ; par ailleurs, des fondations construites sous la nappe phréatique constituent un défaut grave 349 . Un drain agricole, incapable de drainer la pluie, même d’une rare intensité, est dysfonctionnel, et constitue un vice caché, car une telle averse n’est pas un cas de force majeure 350 . Toutefois, un drain ne constituera pas un défaut caché s’il fonctionne, même s’il n’est pas installé selon les normes courantes 351 . Si le drain est obstrué régulièrement en raison de la trop forte concentration d’ocre ferreuse du terrain, ceci donne droit à une diminution de prix et à des dommages-intérêts si le vendeur a camouflé le défaut 352 . Toutefois, si le drain français est obstrué, ce qui ne paraît pas anormal compte tenu de l’âge de la propriété, la garantie des vices cachés est écartée. La jurisprudence a établi à maintes reprises que la présence d’un drain français vétuste ne constitue pas un vice caché. Les détériorations liées à l’usure normale d’un bien ne constitue pas un vice caché couvert par la garantie légale de qualité 353 . Une pierre calcaire, destinée à rénover le parement extérieur des murs d’une résidence, mais qui se détériore, se décolore et s’effrite sous l’action de l’eau est affectée d’un vice grave 354 . Si elle a été vendue par un vendeur professionnel, l’acheteur a droit à des dommages-intérêts. Par ailleurs, si le défaut d’un revêtement extérieur d’un immeuble se manifeste après la vente, alors que cet aspect ne laissait entrevoir aucune anomalie, on ne peut reprocher à l’acheteur d’avoir poussé plus loin son investigation. Il n’avait pas à penser que les travaux de rénovation antérieurs avaient été mal faits 355 . Un défaut d’insonorisation dans un immeuble haut de gamme peut donner lieu à une demande de résolution pour défaut caché 356 . Un défaut d’isolation thermique des murs 357 ou des portes-fenêtres qui ne sont pas conçues pour être installées à l’extérieur d’un immeuble donne droit à une diminution du prix de l’immeuble concerné, de même qu’à des dommages-intérêts, si cette lacune était connue du vendeur qui ne l’a pas dénoncée 358 . La mention erronée de l’année de construction d’un immeuble ne constitue pas un défaut caché assez grave pour donner ouverture à la résolution de la vente, surtout s’il était possible de vérifier la vraie date de la construction en consultant le certificat de localisation 359 . Les animaux eux aussi peuvent être affectés de défauts cachés, compromettant leur santé, ou leur utilité. Ainsi, un chien de race atteint d’une dysplasie aux deux hanches, malformation congénitale et héréditaire, peut nécessiter des opérations aussi coûteuses et aléatoires 360 . [Page 218] Un chat peut mourir à brève échéance d’une péritonite féline 361 . Une vache censée être d’une lignée de race et destinée à la reproduction qui ne peut plus être utilisée à des fins de reproduction de bovins de race est affectée d’un défaut caché s’il s’avère qu’elle n’appartient pas à la lignée décrite dans le contrat, et qu’elle ne peut donner lieu à une descendance de race pure. Il s’agit aussi d’une erreur sur l’objet du contrat 362 . Les pattes d’un cheval peuvent être affectées de malformations 363 . Des terrains remblayés avec de la pyrite comportant un potentiel pétrographique de gonflement important 364 ou d’ocre ferreuse 365 sont affectés d’un défaut caché. Par ailleurs, la présence de pyrrhotite dans le granulat de béton peut constituer à la fois un vice caché et un vice de construction ou un vice de réalisation de l’ouvrage. Un chevauchement des deux notions juridiques est possible lorsqu’il y a une perte de l’ouvrage et aussi la preuve d’un déficit d’usage. Un double régime d’indemnisation peut en découler 366 . Toutefois, la seule présence de pyrite dans un matériau de remblai ne constitue pas en soi un vice caché, si ce fait n’entraîne pas un déficit d’usage 367 . Si une maison est construite sur un terrain instable parce qu’antérieurement traversé par un cours d’eau mal remblayé, l’acheteur peut réclamer des dommages du vendeur, lui-même constructeur 368 . Si le problème potentiel d’un terrain a été dénoncé par le vendeur qui a remis à l’acheteur une étude géotechnique, ce dernier ne peut prétendre l’avoir ignoré parce qu’il n’a pas compris la signification de cette étude faisant état du problème 369 . L’acheteur peut toutefois se libérer en informant l’acheteur qu’il ne garantit pas la stabilité d’un terrain, parce qu’aucune expertise n’a été faite pour la vérifier 370 . Notons que le vice caché doit entraîner une diminution de l’usage d’un bien. Si un immeuble a été acquis pour être démoli, les inconvénients résultant de la présence d’amiante comme isolant ne constituent pas un vice, car la démolition n’est pas un usage 371 . D’autre part, la simple présence d’un réservoir d’hydrocarbures ne constitue pas un vice caché, si ceci n’empêche pas l’usage auquel l’acheteur destinait le lieu au moment de son achat, à plus forte raison si cet acheteur a fait ses travaux correctifs sans envoyer l’avis prévu par l’article 1739 C.c.Q. 372 . La Loi sur la qualité de l’environnement 373 oblige l’enlèvement de tout réservoir désaffecté d’hydrocarbures, et quand un immeuble est vendu, le vendeur doit avertir l’acheteur de la présence d’un tel réservoir, dont la présence diminue la valeur de la propriété. Son silence à cet égard peut être considéré comme un dol 374 , à moins que les circonstances et le temps écoulé n’aient justifié le vendeur de n’en point parler 375 . L’inspecteur préachat ne peut être tenu de « deviner » la présence de ces réservoirs, si aucun signe extérieur ne permet d’en constater l’existence 376 . Par contre, si la réhabilitation d’un terrain contaminé par des hydrocarbures est nécessaire pour se conformer, le vendeur peut être poursuivi pour le coût de cette réhabilitation 377 . La présence de tuyaux d’évents, très apparents, ou d’odeurs suspectes d’hydrocarbures enlève à la présence de réservoirs d’hydrocarbures, les caractéristiques de défaut caché, et incitait les acheteurs à un examen plus poussé 378 . À l’inverse, lorsqu’au moment de l’achat de la maison, aucun indice ne permettait de détecter une contamination avant la sortie de terre du réservoir, le vice n’est pas apparent. Toutefois, la contamination qui résulte de la perforation du réservoir d’hydrocarbures peut constituer un vice caché. Par ailleurs, la contamination cause clairement un déficit d’usage lorsqu’elle s’étend au-delà des normes [Page 219] gouvernementales, causant ainsi une perte de valeur de l’immeuble; elle ne nécessite pas de preuve d’un refus de financement ou de réduction de prix à la revente 379 . Dans le même sens, pour une contamination des sols en raison d’un réservoir de mazout perforé, le vice a été considéré comme grave et ayant un impact économique sur la valeur de l’immeuble. Ce vice a entraîné un déficit d’usage, obligeant les acheteurs à se reloger pendant les travaux de réhabilitation et de caractérisation 380 . 2) Le défaut ignoré par l’acheteur Le vice doit être inconnu de l’acheteur au moment de la vente (art. 1726 , al. 2 C.c.Q.) 381 . Le vendeur qui n’a pas dénoncé le vice...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 12\\n... icit d’usage, obligeant les acheteurs à se reloger pendant les travaux de réhabilitation et de caractérisation 380 . 2) Le défaut ignoré par l’acheteur Le vice doit être inconnu de l’acheteur au moment de la vente (art. 1726 , al. 2 C.c.Q.) 381 . Le vendeur qui n’a pas dénoncé le vice lors de la vente a généralement le fardeau de prouver cette connaissance de l’acheteur 382 . Souvent, le vendeur a de la difficulté à faire cette preuve : il se tournera alors vers la condition suivante de la garantie, et alléguera que le vice était apparent et que l’acheteur aurait dû le voir. Si le vendeur a dénoncé les problèmes à l’acheteur, par exemple des infiltrations d’eau 383 , ou l’absence de drain français adéquat 384 , ceci empêche le recours. Si une fosse septique n’est pas conforme, mais que des indices de désuétude sont apparents, ceci peut empêcher les acheteurs de recouvrer une diminution de prix. Ces indices de désuétude auraient dû inciter l’acheteur à faire un examen plus approfondi 385 . Il en est ainsi aussi pour un terrain que l’acheteur savait être contaminé pour lequel il a payé un moindre prix, en connaissance de cause 386 . 3) Le défaut caché, non apparent Le vice doit être « caché », c’est-à-dire que la défectuosité n’a pas été apparente au moment de la vente 387 ; la disposition précise qu’un vice apparent est celui « qui peut être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin de recourir à un expert » 388 (art. 1726 , al. 2 C.c.Q.). Par exemple, si l’acheteur n’a pas tenu compte du fait que les bardeaux étaient usés, et que l’inclinaison de la galerie était anormale, parce qu’inversée, il s’agit de vices apparents et non de vices cachés, ce qui peut entraîner le rejet de ses recours 389 . L’acheteur qui n’inspecte pas le bien 390 , ne suit pas les recommandations de l’inspecteur 391 , fait une visite désinvolte, sans poser les questions usuelles sur l’âge de la maison, ni porter attention à l’âge de celle-ci 392 , n’agit pas avec prudence et diligence. Si dans ces circonstances, il n’a pas vu les signes révélateurs des problèmes, ses recours risquent d’être rejetés 393 . Cette précision met fin à une controverse sur la nécessité, ou non, pour l’acheteur inexpérimenté de faire examiner le bien par un expert – seul le vice qui avait échappé à l’expert compétent et diligent pouvant être considéré comme caché –; la précision met fin également à l’application d’une jurisprudence plus récente selon laquelle l’acheteur devait, selon le test de la personne prudente et diligente, avoir recours à un expert dans certaines circonstances 394 . Si l’expert ou l’inspecteur préachat malgré sa diligence n’a pas vu le problème, ou s’il a été [Page 220] camouflé 395 , le défaut est vraiment caché 396 . De plus, un acheteur peut réclamer de son vendeur, la perte de valeur subie lors de la revente, si les défauts qu’il a découverts l’ont obligé à réduire son prix lors d’une revente 397 . Compte tenu de l’âge centenaire de l’immeuble et du type de construction de l’époque, l’acheteur devait s’attendre à des détériorations après l’acquisition. Par ailleurs, ne pas recourir aux services d’un expert ou d’un professionnel de la construction avant l’achat peut s’avérer imprudent dans le cas de l’achat d’un vieil immeuble. Dans ce cas, il peut être reproché à l’acheteur un défaut de prudence et de diligence 398 . L’absence d’expert ne rend pas irrecevable le recours de l’acheteur, mais l’examen de l’expert évite à l’acheteur un achat qu’il pourrait regretter. L’expert ne voit pas les défauts vraiment cachés, mais il peut en déduire l’existence par des symptômes apparents qui ne sont pas toujours significatifs pour un profane. En percevant un défaut avant la vente, ceci démontre l’existence du défaut au moment de la vente, et l’acheteur devrait agir en conséquence 399 . Si l’expert ne perçoit pas un vice qu’il aurait dû percevoir, l’acheteur peut lui réclamer des dommages-intérêts en raison d’une faute professionnelle, commise lors de l’exécution de son contrat d’inspection 400 . Si, par contre, l’acheteur passe outre les remarques de l’inspecteur préachat, il ne peut s’en prendre qu’à lui-même et sa demande sera rejetée, ou partiellement accueillie 401 , surtout si les manifestations du vice signalées par l’inspecteur étaient apparentes 402 . On ne peut reprocher à un inspecteur préachat une inspection négligente, si on lui a demandé de dénoncer des symptômes apparents, et que par la suite, les vices du terrain où était construit l’immeuble ont été découverts en procédant à un forage géotechnique, qui révèle l’insuffisance de la capacité portante d’un sous-sol argileux 403 . Les tribunaux peuvent conclure que le vice était apparent lorsque les acheteurs ont suffisamment d’indices pour pousser leurs vérifications, tel que conseillé par leur inspecteur préachat. Le fait qu’il s’agisse d’une autoconstruction, malgré l’expérience du vendeur, ne permet pas à des acheteurs de faire fi des recommandations de l’inspecteur préachat 404 . L’examen du bien, par l’acheteur ou son expert, doit se faire de façon usuelle. Les tribunaux conservent un large pouvoir d’appréciation sur ce qui constitue un examen raisonnable. L’examen doit être attentif et sérieux, bien qu’il puisse être rapide et ne pas être approfondi. L’examen d’un camion conforme aux exigences de la SAAQ constitue un examen diligent 405 . L’examen sommaire d’une vache peut permettre de constater si elle est affectée d’une inflammation des mamelles, dite « mammite ». Il est d’usage, lors d’un achat de vaches laitières, de faire une traite qui révèle leur capacité laitière. La mammite n’est donc pas un défaut caché 406 . En général, le caractère caché du vice s’apprécie de façon objective, mais, en réalité, certains facteurs subjectifs sont pris en compte. Ainsi, les tribunaux considèrent la compétence technique de l’acheteur le cas échéant, la nature et l’âge du bien, notamment d’un immeuble 407 , le prix convenu – un faible prix étant un indice qui doit inciter l’acheteur à une plus grande vigilance. Les tribunaux seront plus exigeants quand l’examen a été accompli par un acheteur doté de connaissances techniques, par exemple un architecte ou [Page 221] un ingénieur 408 . Ainsi, on pourra s’attendre que l’acheteur d’un cheval de course, étant lui-même censé être un expert connaissant les possibilités de défauts graves susceptibles d’affecter un tel animal, soit prudent lors de son examen et de son achat 409 . Mais ni l’acheteur ni son expert ne sont obligés de prendre des mesures inhabituelles (par exemple, creuser autour des fondations ou dans le terrain pour constater l’état d’un champ d’épuration) 410 , ou des fondations 411 , ou du réseau de drainage 412 , ou la présence d’un réservoir de mazout 413 , ou encore d’ouvrir des murs ou des planchers 414 pour chercher des vices, sauf si un indice visible soulève des soupçons 415 . Un terrain jonché de débris, de vieux pneus, d’objets en fer, visibles par un visiteur, n’est pas affecté de défauts cachés même si ceux-ci ont donné lieu à une ordonnance relative à la qualité de l’environnement 416 . Même constat pour un terrain contaminé par des hydrocarbures où l’on ne peut reprocher à l’acheteur de ne pas avoir procédé à des excavations pour être qualifié d’acheteur prudent et diligent 417 . L’acheteur, le cas échéant, peut même appeler en garantie le propriétaire antérieur de l’immeuble, selon la règle de Kravitz (art. 1442 C.c.Q.), à la condition toutefois de respecter les délais raisonnables une fois que le problème est constaté et que ce vendeur antérieur a effectivement été fautif, de façon à être tenu à la garantie 418 . Par ailleurs, il peut arriver que la chaîne des garanties soit rompue si le vice était apparent lors d’une vente antérieure ou s’il y avait une clause exonératoire ou de vente aux risques et périls valide. En résumé, le vendeur profane d’un bien immeuble poursuivi pour vice caché qui a acheté « à ses risques et périls » ne pourra espérer obtenir l’aide des auteurs profanes de son vendeur même si ces derniers ont vendu avec les garanties légales. En effet, cette clause d’exclusion est un « avertissement sérieux fait à l’acheteur que le bien vendu est sans aucune garantie en dépit des vices qui peuvent l’affecter » 419 . Si l’acheteur doit être prudent et diligent, le vendeur doit dévoiler les vices qu’il connaît. Il a donc une obligation de renseignement à cet égard. Quant au fabricant, assimilé à un vendeur professionnel, la Cour suprême lui impose une présomption de connaissance, ce qui entraîne pour lui l’obligation de verser des dommages-intérêts. Pour le fabricant, la présomption de connaissance est extrêmement difficile à renverser. Il doit prouver qu’au moment de la fabrication, le bien était exempt de défaut, et que les défauts résultent d’un fait ou d’un intervenant dont il n’est pas responsable 420 . Des représentations trompeuses du vendeur (particulièrement pour apaiser des craintes de l’acheteur devant l’indice d’un vice) peuvent conduire les tribunaux à juger caché un vice qui, autrement, serait apparent 421 . Si un immeuble est « vendu tel quel », ou « tel que vu », cela n’implique pas une renonciation à la garantie légale. Un défaut caché est justement un défaut [Page 222] qui ne se voit pas et qui est inconnu. La mention « tel que vu » ne viserait que les défauts apparents 422 . 4) L’antériorité du défaut à la vente • La règle générale Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente (art. 1726 , al. 1 C.c.Q.) 423 . Par cette condition, le législateur évite que le vendeur ne soit tenu responsable d’une défectuosité résultant du mauvais usage ou du mauvais entretien du bien par l’acheteur, par une autre personne, ou de travaux ou aménagements accomplis après la vente 424 . Comme mentionné ci-dessus, l’acheteur aurait normalement le fardeau de prouver que le vice est antérieur à la vente; en pratique, il bénéficie souvent d’une présomption de fait notamment en dénonçant le vice tôt après la délivrance 425 . Si le vendeur prétend que le vice est dû à un usage abusif par l’acheteur, ou même qu’il est attribuable à un cas fortuit postérieur à la délivrance, c’est à lui de le démontrer 426 . Pour qu’un vice soit antérieur à la vente, il n’est pas nécessaire qu’il se soit manifesté complètement avant cette vente; il suffit qu’il ait existé de façon latente. Par exemple, la rapidité avec laquelle se manifeste un problème permet de conclure que le vice existait avant la vente 427 . Ainsi, l’acheteur peut découvrir après la vente que l’eau de son puits n’est pas potable. Malgré cette constatation postérieure à la vente, la cause de cette contamination peut être antérieure à la vente 428 . Encore faut-il que le fait constaté constituait un vice au moment de la vente. Ainsi, un réservoir de mazout souterrain peut représenter un défaut de conformité constituant un vice aujourd’hui, aux termes de la loi et des règlements concernant l’environnement et l’utilisation des produits pétroliers, mais qu’au moment de la vente qui a eu lieu une vingtaine d’années plus tôt, rien ne l’interdisait 429 . La Cour du Québec a statué que la découverte d’un défaut caché entre le moment de la promesse de vente et la signature de l’acte de vente n’altérait pas l’obligation du vendeur ni les recours de l’acheteur 430 . • La présomption d’antériorité du défaut contre le vendeur professionnel De plus, en cas de vente par un vendeur professionnel, le Code civil du Québec prévoit que « l’existence d’un vice au moment de la vente est présumée lorsque le mauvais fonctionnement du bien ou sa détérioration survient prématurément par rapport à des biens identiques ou de même espèce [...] » (art. 1729 C.c.Q.) 431 . Le moteur d’un bateau neuf ne doit pas tomber en panne au point de nécessiter le remplacement du moteur 432 . Considérons par exemple des pneus rechapés qui éclatent après moins de 100 kilomètres 433 . La loi ne précise pas la notion de vendeur professionnel, mais on suppose que le vendeur professionnel est celui qui vend des biens sur une base régulière et non pas celui qui, fût-il réputé être spécialisé dans son domaine, fait occasionnellement des ventes. Ainsi, l’amateur de chevaux qui vend un cheval de temps à autre n’est pas pour autant un vendeur professionnel 434 . Celui qui vend un camion usagé dont il n’a plus besoin ne peut être qualifié de vendeur professionnel même si à l’occasion il répare des camions 435 . Une banque qui revend un immeuble qu’elle a pris en paiement en exerçant ses droits hypothécaires contre son débiteur n’est pas un vendeur professionnel. Son activité habituelle n’est pas de vendre des immeubles, mais plutôt de se livrer à des activités de prêts ou de financement 436 . Une compagnie pétrolière qui se départit d’un terrain dont elle n’a plus besoin n’est pas non plus un vendeur professionnel 437 . La banque et la compagnie [Page 223] pétrolière ne sont donc pas présumées connaître les défauts cachés affectant les immeubles qu’elles vendent, bien que la preuve de leur connaissance puisse résulter de présomptions de fait sérieuses et convergentes. Elles sont admises à plaider leur ignorance surtout si...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/document/collection-de-droit-2025-2026-vol-6-c-baab7d4c-adbe-89a2-f597-755e097125dd2025-chapitre-i-le-droit-commun-de-la-vente\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02982456237077713, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/collection-de-droit-2025-2026-vol-6-c-baab7d4c-adbe-89a2-f597-755e097125dd2025-chapitre-i-le-droit-commun-de-la-vente\", \"title\": \"Benadiba, Aurore - Chapitre I - Le droit commun de la vente, dans Collection de droit 2025-2026, Volume 6 - Obligations et contrats, Titre II - La vente dans le Code civil et la Loi sur la protection du consommateur\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/document/collection-de-droit-2025-2026-vol-6-c-baab7d4c-adbe-89a2-f597-755e097125dd2025-chapitre-i-le-droit-commun-de-la-vente\", \"@search.score\": 0.02982456237077713, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:doctrine\", \"query\": \"La garantie de qualité en vente impose une responsabilité spécifique lorsque le vice répond aux conditions cumulatives : gravité, antériorité, caractère réellement caché et ignorance de l’acheteur. La doctrine insiste sur la méthode d’appréciation « d’hypothèse raisonnable » du degré de dissimulation et sur la charge probatoire relative à la nature et à l’origine du défaut. Elle distingue aussi le vice caché des manquements à une obligation d’information ou des défauts apparents, et discute l’incidence d’une inspection diligente sur l’élément « inconnu ».\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/collection-de-droit-2025-2026-vol-6-c-baab7d4c-adbe-89a2-f597-755e097125dd2025-chapitre-i-le-droit-commun-de-la-vente\"}, \"token_count\": 9944, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/document/collection-de-droit-2025-2026-vol-6-c-baab7d4c-adbe-89a2-f597-755e097125dd2025-chapitre-i-le-droit-commun-de-la-vente\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/dunton-articles-i1c865c06-a079-4468-b678-978f8c9f09c9-rappel-des-principes-de-base-en-matiere-de-vice-cache\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/dunton-articles-i1c865c06-a079-4468-b678-978f8c9f09c9-rappel-des-principes-de-base-en-matiere-de-vice-cache\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Laurent, Bernier - Rappel des principes de base en matière de vice caché (2020)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Rappel des principes de base en matière de vice caché Rappel des principes de base en matière de vice caché Bernier Laurent 2020-11-13 Table des matières La dénonciation du vice Les recours La Loi sur la protection du consommateur (LPC) Nous avons tous une certaine notion de ce qu’est un vice caché. C’est un terme bien connu dans le monde de l’immobilier parce que malheureusement trop souvent applicable. Le présent article vise à rappeler les principes qui permettent l’ouverture des recours en raison de vices cachés ainsi que certaines des compensations possibles. Les vices cachés découlent de la garantie de qualité qu’un vendeur a l’obligation de fournir à l’acheteur d’un bien. Par conséquent, c’est un concept qui s’applique autant aux biens immeubles qu’aux biens meubles. La garantie légale contre les vices cachés n’est pas d’ordre public, c’est-à-dire qu’on peut convenir dans le contrat de l’écarter, habituellement en échange d’un prix de vente en conséquence. La définition de vice caché est à l’article 1726 du Code civil du Québec (C.c.Q.). Les quatre critères suivants doivent être remplis pour que le vendeur en soit tenu responsable : Le vice doit être caché ; Le vice doit être suffisamment grave ; Le vice doit être inconnu de l’acheteur ; Le vice doit exister au moment de la vente. Tous ces critères ont fait l’objet d’une jurisprudence abondante. Sans élaborer en détails sur chacun d’eux, une précision s’impose sur le premier. Pour qu’un vice soit considéré comme « caché », « il ne doit pas pouvoir être constaté par un acheteur prudent et diligent sans avoir besoin d’avoir recours à un expert » 1 . Bien que ça ne soit pas obligatoire, faire faire une inspection préachat par un expert est une excellente façon pour un acheteur de montrer sa prudence et sa diligence, particulièrement dans un domaine qu’il ne connait pas bien. La Cour d’appel a bien résumé l’état du droit quant à l’obligation de prudence et diligence de l’acheteur : « En résumé, lorsque l’immeuble présente un indice permettant de soupçonner l’existence d’un vice potentiel, l’acheteur prudent et diligent, qui n’a pas fait appel à un expert, doit le faire ou vérifier autrement et de façon satisfaisante ce qui est suspect. Dans le cas où l’acheteur a déjà fait appel à un expert, la présence de signes annonciateurs d’un vice potentiel oblige l’expert à faire une inspection plus approfondie. S’il ne la fait pas et qu’un vice est mis à jour, la conclusion que le vice n’était pas caché s’imposera. » 2 La dénonciation du vice Lors de la découverte d’un vice caché, l’acheteur doit le dénoncer par écrit au vendeur dans un délai raisonnable sauf si le vendeur connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer le vice 3 . Dans le cas d’un vice se manifestant graduellement, le délai raisonnable commence à courir à partir du moment où l’acheteur a pu en soupçonner l’étendue et la gravité. Ce moment est très important puisqu’il devient également le point de départ de la prescription de trois ans 4 . L’objectif principal de la dénonciation est de permettre au vendeur de constater et de remédier au vice, ce qui évite souvent d’engager de coûteuses procédures judiciaires. De plus, dans le cas d’immeubles, il est possible que le vendeur ait lui-même acheté d’un autre vendeur et qu’il poursuive celui-ci à son tour. Cet autre vendeur devra lui-aussi avoir pu constater le vice allégué avant que les réparations ne soient faites pour pouvoir se défendre. Les tribunaux sont sévères à l’endroit des acheteurs qui intentent des poursuites judiciaires sans avoir préalablement dénoncé adéquatement les vices cachés au vendeur. La conséquence peut aller jusqu’au rejet pur et simple du recours et il est donc fortement recommandé de ne pas prendre de chance et de dénoncer par écrit les vices le plus tôt possible. Les recours S’il est admis que le vice est effectivement un vice caché et que le vendeur en est responsable, l’acheteur peut obtenir la nullité de la vente 5 (et donc la restitution des prestations de part et d’autre) si le « bien est affecté d’un vice substantiel et que le coût des travaux correctifs par rapport au prix d’achat est important » 6 . Si la nullité de la vente n’est pas une option, l’acheteur peut obtenir une diminution du prix de vente, habituellement à la hauteur des coûts des réparations moins la plus-value apportée au bien par les travaux 7 . Finalement, si le vendeur connaissait ou ne pouvait ignorer l’existence du vice, l’acheteur peut demander, en plus de la nullité ou de la diminution du prix de vente, des dommages et intérêts pour le préjudice directement causé par le défaut caché. La Loi sur la protection du consommateur (LPC) Pour les contrats de consommation 8 , la LPC prévoit des règles différentes du Code civil du Québec afin de faciliter la preuve et la procédure pour le consommateur. Pour en bénéficier, il faut bien entendu que le contrat de vente soit soumis à cette loi qui, elle, est d’ordre public contrairement aux paragraphes précédents. Les articles 37 et 38 LPC prévoient qu’un bien doit pouvoir servir à l’usage auquel il est normalement destiné pendant une durée raisonnable eut égard à son prix, les modalités du contrat et les conditions d’utilisation du bien. Ces dispositions englobent donc indirectement la notion de vice caché. Les recours de la LPC 9 sont aussi plus sévères à l’égard des commerçants. Le consommateur peut demander la nullité rétroactive ou pour le futur seulement du contrat, la diminution du prix de vente, des dommages intérêts et des dommages punitifs lorsqu’applicable. 1. St-Louis c. Morin , 2006 QCCA 1643, para. 27. 2. Ibid ., para. 39. 3. Art. 1739 C.c.Q. 4. Art. 2880 et 2925 C.c.Q. 5. Art.1590 et 1604 C.c.Q. 6. Gazaille c. Lebeau , 2016 QCCS 6238, para. 62. 7. Bélanger c. Alarie , 2018 QCCS 2919, para. 126. 8. Art. 2, LPC. 9. Art. 272, LPC....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/dunton-articles-i1c865c06-a079-4468-b678-978f8c9f09c9-rappel-des-principes-de-base-en-matiere-de-vice-cache\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.03205128386616707, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/dunton-articles-i1c865c06-a079-4468-b678-978f8c9f09c9-rappel-des-principes-de-base-en-matiere-de-vice-cache\", \"title\": \"Laurent, Bernier - Rappel des principes de base en matière de vice caché (2020)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/dunton-articles-i1c865c06-a079-4468-b678-978f8c9f09c9-rappel-des-principes-de-base-en-matiere-de-vice-cache\", \"@search.score\": 0.03205128386616707, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:doctrine\", \"query\": \"La garantie de qualité en vente impose une responsabilité spécifique lorsque le vice répond aux conditions cumulatives : gravité, antériorité, caractère réellement caché et ignorance de l’acheteur. La doctrine insiste sur la méthode d’appréciation « d’hypothèse raisonnable » du degré de dissimulation et sur la charge probatoire relative à la nature et à l’origine du défaut. Elle distingue aussi le vice caché des manquements à une obligation d’information ou des défauts apparents, et discute l’incidence d’une inspection diligente sur l’élément « inconnu ».\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/dunton-articles-i1c865c06-a079-4468-b678-978f8c9f09c9-rappel-des-principes-de-base-en-matiere-de-vice-cache\"}, \"token_count\": 1465, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/dunton-articles-i1c865c06-a079-4468-b678-978f8c9f09c9-rappel-des-principes-de-base-en-matiere-de-vice-cache\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-1975-1-icfa6aa9c-a839-eee4-ab54-b9d84fc41304-la-garantie-du-vendeur-dimmeuble\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-1975-1-icfa6aa9c-a839-eee4-ab54-b9d84fc41304-la-garantie-du-vendeur-dimmeuble\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Taschereau, Georges - La garantie du vendeur d’immeuble, dans Cours de perfectionnement du notariat (1975)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... La garantie du vendeur d’immeuble La garantie du vendeur d’immeuble Georges Taschereau * Table des matières Introduction I. GARANTIE CONTRE L’ÉVICTION 1. garantie légale contre l’éviction 1.1. Contenu de l’obligation 1.1.1. Garantie du fait personnel 1.1.2. Garantie du fait des tiers 1.2. Sortes d’éviction 1.3 Recours de l’acheteur 1.3.1. Moment de l’exercer 1.3.2. Étendue du recours 1.3.2.1 Restitution totale ou partielle du prix 1.3.2.2. Dommages-intérêts 1.3.2.2.1 Plus-Value et moins-value 1.3.2.2.2 Impenses 2. Garantie conventionnelle contre l’éviction 2.1. Conventions relatives à la garantie du fait personnel 2.1.1. Clauses augmentant la garantie légale 2.1.2. Clauses diminuant ou éteignant la garantie légale 2.2. Conventions relatives à la garantie du fait des tiers 2.2.1. Conventions ajoutant à la garantie légale 2.2.1.1. Garantie contre tous troubles et évictions 2.2.1.2. Garantie légale et libre de tous privilèges et hypothèques 2.2.1.3. Garantie franc et quitte 2.2.2. Conventions diminuant la garantie légale 2.2.2.1. Vente avec exonération de la garantie des servitudes occultes 2.2.2.2. Vente sans garantie ou sans autre garantie que celle de ses faits et gestes 2.2.2.3. Vente aux risques et périls de l’acheteur II. GARANTIE CONTRE LES VICES CACHÉS 1. Garantie légale contre les vices cachés 1.1. Le principe de la garantie contre les vices cachés 1.2. Conditions de la garantie contre les vices cachés 1.2.1. Vice nuisible à l’usage de l’immeuble vendu 1.2.2. Vice caché 1.2.3. Vice antérieur à la vente 1.2.4. Vice inconnu de l’acheteur 1.3. Effet de la garantie contre les vices cachés : Recours 2. Garantie conventionnelle contre les vices cachés 2.1. Les clauses augmentant la garantie 2.2. Les clauses limitant ou supprimant la garantie Conclusion * Conférence donnée à la session des cours de perfectionnement de la Chambre des notaires du Québec tenue à Montréal les 21 et 22 mars 1975. [Page 267] Introduction Suivant les dispositions de l’article 1491 du Code civil le vendeur est tenu envers l’acheteur de deux obligations : une obligation de délivrance et une obligation de garantie contre l’éviction et contre les vices cachés. Par l’exécution de son obligation de délivrance, le vendeur met l’acheteur en possession de l’immeuble vendu de façon à ce qu’il en jouisse et en dispose à son gré. Il est donc tout à fait normal que le vendeur n’empêche pas cette jouissance par son fait et empêche que des tiers viennent troubler l’acheteur dans sa jouissance : il lui doit garantie contre l’éviction due à son fait personnel comme au fait des tiers. En plus de cette garantie contre l’éviction, le vendeur doit à l’acheteur garantie que l’immeuble vendu sera propre à l’usage de l’acheteur, d’où la garantie contre les vices cachés. La possession de l’immeuble par l’acheteur à la suite de la vente doit être paisible et utile à l’acheteur. Si ce dernier est évincé ou troublé dans sa possession, ou si l’immeuble est affecté de défauts cachés qui le rendent impropre à l’usage auquel le destine l’acheteur ou diminuent tellement son utilité que ce dernier ne l’aurait pas acheté ou n’en aurait pas donné si haut prix s’il les avait connus, la jouissance de l’acheteur peut être nulle ou grandement réduite. C’est en ce sens que l’on dit que l’obligation de délivrance se complète nécessairement par l’obligation de garantie, cette dernière étant une conséquence de la première. Je tiens au cours de cet exposé à vous faire un rappel de ces notions fort complexes que sont la garantie contre l’éviction et la garantie contre les vices [Page 268] cachés. Ce rappel appuyé par une étude de notre jurisprudence en la matière nous permettra de mieux comprendre la portée de l’expression garantie légale que nous utilisons souvent machinalement dans la rédaction de nos actes de vente et saisir l’importance en maintes occasions de la modifier par une clause spécifique au contrat, afin de mieux protéger l’acheteur. I. GARANTIE CONTRE L’ÉVICTION 1. garantie légale contre l’éviction 1.1. Contenu de l’obligation L’obligation imposée au vendeur par l’article 1508 du Code civil consiste à garantir à l’acheteur “à raison de quelque acte du vendeur, ou de quelque droit existant au temps de la vente”. Examinée sous un autre angle, cette obligation est double : le vendeur doit à l’acheteur garantie de son fait personnel et garantie du fait des tiers. 1.1.1. Garantie du fait personnel Le vendeur doit s’abstenir de tout geste susceptible d’empêcher ou de nuire à la jouissance de l’immeuble par l’acheteur. Le vendeur d’une terre empêcherait ou au moins nuirait à sa jouissance par l’acheteur s’il empiétait sur cette dernière en cultivant la terre voisine dont il aurait conservé la propriété. Il empêcherait aussi cette jouissance s’il se déclarait à la suite de la vente titulaire d’une servitude créée par lui par destination du père de famille préalablement à l’acte de vente contre la terre vendue et en faveur de la terre voisine dont il aurait conservé la propriété, l’autorisant à y puiser de l’eau pour son usage personnel. Dans le premier cas, la jouissance de l’acheteur est troublée par une atteinte qui n’est fondée sur aucun droit, et, dans le second, par une atteinte prétendument fondée sur un droit. Dans les deux cas, le vendeur doit garantie à l’acheteur peu importe si le fait dont il est l’auteur est accompli avec bonne ou mauvaise foi et peu importe si ce fait est antérieur ou postérieur à la vente. Cette obligation de garantie du vendeur quant à son fait personnel lui impose de s’abstenir de tout acte susceptible de menacer la possession paisible de l’acheteur. Ainsi, si Pierre vend un immeuble à Jean et à la suite de cette vente hypothèque en garantie d’un emprunt ce même immeuble en faveur d’un tiers qui procède à l’enregistrement de son hypothèque avant que Jean procède à l’enregistrement de son titre, Pierre devra garantie à Jean, même si le délai à procéder à l’enregistrement de l’acte de vente est imputable à Jean. L’obligation de garantie du vendeur quant à son fait personnel ne saurait cependant servir de base à un recours de la part de l’acheteur qui imputerait au fait personnel du vendeur un trouble ne lui donnant pas autrement ouverture à un recours en garantie. Ce serait le cas par exemple si Pierre constituait contre son immeuble une servitude apparente et le vendait par la suite à Jean : ce [Page 269] dernier ne pourrait lors d’un recours contre Pierre écarter les limites imposées par l’article 1519 du Code civil à son recours basé sur l’existence de cette servitude en invoquant le seul fait que la servitude a été constituée par Pierre. Ce serait là sanctionner la négligence de l’acheteur. 1.1.2. Garantie du fait des tiers Le vendeur garant envers l’acheteur de son fait personnel est aussi garant envers lui du fait des tiers. Cette obligation, simple obligation négative dans le premier cas puisque le vendeur doit simplement s’abstenir de faire quoi que ce soit, est beaucoup plus lourde dans le second cas : le vendeur doit protéger l’acheteur et prendre sa défense en cas de trouble provenant d’un tiers. Cette obligation de garantie du vendeur, illimitée quant à son fait personnel, est néanmoins limitée quant au fait des tiers. Si le trouble causé par un tiers à l’acheteur est un trouble de fait, ce tiers ne se prévalant d’aucun droit, le vendeur n’aura pas à intervenir et l’acheteur se défendra seul. Au contraire, si le trouble causé par un tiers est un trouble de droit, le vendeur devra prendre fait et cause. L’obligation du vendeur quant au fait des tiers est aussi limitée en ce sens que la cause de l’éviction doit exister au moment de la vente pour donner à l’acheteur ouverture à un recours en garantie contre son vendeur. Le tiers qui invoque un droit ayant existé en sa faveur avant la vente de l’immeuble manifeste qu’il existait lors de la vente un défaut de droit chez le vendeur et ce dernier en est garant envers l’acheteur. Si la jouissance de l’acheteur est troublée par un fait postérieur à la vente provenant d’un tiers, ce trouble ne pourra, et cela va de soi, être imputé au vendeur et donner à l’acheteur ouverture à un recours en garantie contre lui. Ainsi, dans l’arrêt Richer v. Dame Fournelle 1 il a été jugé que l’acheteur d’une terre et d’un aqueduc se trouvant sur la propriété d’un voisin, avec droit de puiser de l’eau dans une source sur cette propriété voisine, ne pouvait intenter une action en diminution du prix et en dommages-intérêts dirigée contre le vendeur, si ce voisin, en construisant un nouveau réservoir, privait l’acheteur de puiser de l’eau à la source. Il s’agissait d’un trouble causé hors la connaissance du vendeur lors de la vente, donc en aucune façon imputable à son fait personnel mais bien à un fait attribuable à un tiers et postérieur à la vente. 1.2. Sortes d’éviction L’éviction dont l’acheteur pourrait se prétendre la victime ou menacé de l’être peut être totale ou partielle. Une éviction est dite totale si l’acheteur se voit obligé de délaisser complètement l’immeuble qu’il vient d’acheter au profit d’un tiers qui revendique cet immeuble comme étant sa propriété ou au profit d’un créancier hypothécaire, ou s’il effectue un nouveau paiement destiné à [Page 270] désintéresser le tiers dont le droit apparaît clairement de façon à conserver l’immeuble. Il y a éviction partielle si l’acheteur doit délaisser une partie seulement de cet immeuble, divise ou indivise, ou s’il doit payer à un tiers une créance privilégiée inférieure à la valeur de l’immeuble de façon à le conserver. La perte d’une servitude active dont l’existence a été affirmée lors du contrat et sur laquelle l’acheteur comptait, constitue également une éviction de l’immeuble acheté et donne ouverture au recours en garantie contre le vendeur. La découverte d’une servitude ou autre charge non déclarée à l’acte de vente donne enfin droit à l’acheteur de l’immeuble d’appeler son vendeur en garantie lorsque cette charge ou servitude est non apparente. C’est ce qui découle des articles 1508 et 1519 du Code civil. Il convient cependant de préciser que le caractère apparent d’une servitude ou autre charge doit être apprécié de façon expansive. Les servitudes ou charges dites apparentes privant l’acheteur de son recours en garantie contre le vendeur sont non seulement celles qui crevaient les yeux ou étaient évidentes au moment de la vente, ni celles à la connaissance de l’acheteur, mais également celles que ce dernier était censé connaître. Il importe d’abord de classer parmi les charges qu’un acheteur est censé connaître les servitudes naturelles ou légales même occultes dont il est fait état au Code civil : ces servitudes ne sont pas de véritables servitudes mais elles forment partie du droit commun pour autant que la propriété est concernée et l’acheteur est censé les connaître. Il importe également de classer parmi ce groupe les rentes seigneuriales constituées, que notre jurisprudence a toujours considérées comme une charge de droit commun ne faisant pas l’objet de l’obligation de garantie du vendeur et dont l’acheteur devient responsable à compter de la vente. Il faut enfin inclure dans ce groupe les taxes municipales, générales ou spéciales, les taxes scolaires et les répartitions d’église, dont l’acheteur est responsable à compter de la vente. Je vous réfère à ce sujet à l’exposé lucide de M e Roger Comtois dans La Revue du Notariat 2 . Notre jurisprudence assimile cependant à des servitudes occultes donnant ouverture au recours en garantie de l’acheteur les servitudes établies administrativement comme par exemple les restrictions imposées par les règlements municipaux de constrution et de zonage. L’acheteur n’est pas en droit d’ignorer les servitudes naturelles ou légales et ne doit s’en prendre qu’à lui-même s’il a oublié leur existence ; il est par contre excusé de ne pas connaître ces nombreuses charges imposées par les autorités administratives. Dans l’arrêt Kraus v. Nakis Holding Limited 3 il a été jugé qu’un règlement de zonage qui défend d’occuper un immeuble comme place d’affaires constitue [Page 271] une charge au sens de l’article 1508 du Code civil. Il crée une sorte de servitude légale puisqu’il est l’œuvre du législateur provincial qui a délégué à cette fin ses pouvoirs à l’autorité municipale. Le règlement de zonage impose une limitation au droit de propriété comme la chose est prévue à l’article 406 du Code civil. On ne peut donc reprocher à l’acheteur son ignorance de ce règlement parce que l’acheteur a droit de présumer au moment où il achète que l’immeuble, tel que construit et tel qu’occupé au moment de l’achat, est conforme à tous les règlements en vigueur. Dans l’arrêt Léveillé v. Moreau 4 il a été jugé que les restrictions apportées par un règlement municipal au droit d’ériger un immeuble à logements destinés à être loués constituent une charge non apparente de nature à justifier une demande d’annulation du contrat de vente d’une maison lorsque l’acheteur a déclaré rechercher une maison avec des revenus. L’arrêt Piersanti v. Dame Laporte 5 nous fournit un autre exemple de la façon dont les tribunaux traitent...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 3\\n... sumer que l’acheteur n’aurait pas acheté s’il en avait été instruit. L’élément déterminant le droit ou l’absence de droit de l’acheteur à son recours est suivant le cas son ignorance ou sa connaissance de la servitude au moment du contrat, et j’en viens à la conclusion que la pertinence de cet élément disparaît lorsqu’il y a de la part du vendeur “garantie contre tous troubles quelconques”. L’acheteur peut très bien acheter un immeuble en connaissant l’existence d’une servitude passive et en comptant que le vendeur en obtiendra l’extinction suivant son obligation de garantie ainsi exprimée. Quoiqu’une telle interprétation de cette clause ait été reçue à date avec beaucoup d’hésitation par nos tribunaux, il est recommandable de l’utiliser seulement après avoir bien informé les parties des conséquences qu’elle peut apporter. 2.2.1.2. Garantie légale et libre de tous privilèges et hypothèques Cette clause oblige le vendeur à faire radier tous privilèges et hypothèques grevant l’immeuble vendu, même ceux qui seraient payés ou éteints mais non radiés, et donne à l’acheteur ouverture à son recours en garantie contre le vendeur après la découverte d’un privilège ou d’une hypothèque, à condition toutefois qu’il ait au préalable sommé son vendeur d’y remédier. C’est le sens de l’arrêt rendu dans la cause Langlois v. Carpentier 9 . [Page 281] Si la vente a été faite de cette façon, ou si le vendeur s’est engagé à faire disparaître certaines charges ou une en particulier en dedans d’un certain délai, et qu’il ne le fait pas, l’acheteur, après avoir mis le vendeur en demeure, pourra prendre action pour faire résilier la vente et réclamer des dommages ou, dans les cas où c’est possible, exiger que le vendeur fasse disparaître la charge et qu’à défaut du vendeur, l’acheteur puisse le faire aux frais du vendeur. C’est le sens du jugement rendu dans l’arrêt Trudeau v. Molleur 10 . 2.2.1.3. Garantie franc et quitte La clause de garantie franc et quitte implique la promesse par le vendeur que l’immeuble vendu est absolument libre de tous privilèges, hypothèques, servitudes et charges quelconques, résultant directement ou indirectement à une convention, et l’oblige donc à les faire disparaître. Les expressions “avec garantie légale et libre de toutes charges, privilèges et hypothèques” et “avec garantie contre toutes charges” auront le même effet. M e Roger Comtois s’exprime comme suit : Nous voyons plutôt dans la clause de franc et quitte une garantie formelle, il est vrai, mais limitée aux charges, privilèges et hypothèques résultant soit directement soit indirectement d’une convention. Le vendeur qui a stipulé cette garantie serait sans doute responsable des hypothèques dont il a grevé l’immeuble, et des privilèges créés par la loi, mais à l’occasion de conventions passées par le vendeur, tels le privilège ouvrier qui a son origine dans un contrat d’entreprise ou de travail ou encore le privilège de vendeur 11 . À titre d’exemple de charges ne résultant pas directement ou indirectement d’une convention et partant ne faisant pas l’objet de la garantie de “franc et quitte”, on peut mentionner les cotisations municipales ou d’église. Au sujet de ces cotisations, M e Comtois s’exprime comme suit : Ainsi soutiendrons-nous, d’accord avec la doctrine et la jurisprudence, que le vendeur d’un bien ne peut être tenu responsable des taxes et cotisations spéciales que s’il a formellement et spécialement déclaré que sa propriété était libre ou exempte de telles taxes ou cotisations 12 . 2.2.2. Conventions diminuant la garantie légale Les conventions qui diminuent la garantie légale du vendeur quant au fait des tiers sont aussi permises par l’article 1507 C.c. qui prévoit que les parties peuvent par des conventions particulières diminuer les effets de la garantie légale ou l’exclure entièrement. Ces clauses de diminution ou d’exonération de garantie du fait des tiers seront parfois générales ou parfois particulières dans leur application. [Page 282] 2.2.2.1. Vente avec exonération de la garantie des servitudes occultes Comme clause particulière dans son application, nous avons la clause dite d’exonération de la garantie des servitudes occultes. Quoique valide, cette clause comporte certaines restrictions : 1° Tout d’abord, le vendeur ne peut pas par une telle clause s’exonérer de la garantie des servitudes occultes qu’il a lui-même établies à moins de les dénoncer à l’acheteur ; dans un tel cas, la cause première de l’éviction serait réputé être le fait personnel du vendeur antérieurement à la vente et non le trouble causé par le tiers. 2° Même si la servitude occulte n’a pas été établie par le vendeur luimême et si l’acte de vente contient une clause générale de non garantie des servitudes occultes, le vendeur pourra être tenu à la garantie s’il ne les fait pas connaître à l’acheteur. Le silence du vendeur, surtout si les servitudes sont de nature à diminuer beaucoup la valeur de l’immeuble, sera considéré comme un dol par réticence et permettra d’annuler la clause de non garantie quant aux servitudes occultes contenue au contrat. L’annulation de cette clause aura pour effet de faire revivre le droit commun et de rendre l’article 1519 C.c. applicable purement et simplement. 3° Même si le dol du vendeur ne peut être établi ou présumé, le vendeur pourra ne pas être exonéré de son obligation de garantie des servitudes occultes si la clause à cet effet est tellement vague que l’acheteur ne saurait en mesurer les conséquences ; le tribunal en sera juge. Ainsi, ne saurait être considérée comme une clause ayant pour effet d’exonérer le vendeur de son obligation de garantie des servitudes occultes une clause dans laquelle l’acheteur déclarerait bien connaître l’immeuble vendu et ne pas en exiger de plus amples informations. Serait aussi vraisemblablement considérée comme n’exonérant pas le vendeur de cette obligation, la clause “avec toutes les servitudes actives et passives, apparentes ou occultes attachées au dit immeuble”, à moins que les circonstances le justifient autrement. Le législateur a en principe prévu pour protéger l’acheteur évincé un régime spécial plus avantageux que le régime de droit commun prévu aux articles 1070 et suivants du Code civil. Il est bien privé de son recours en garantie basé sur l’existence d’une servitude occulte non déclarée lorsqu’il en a eu connaissance lors de la vente : il a, dit-on, contracté de façon aléatoire et tenu compte de l’existence de la servitude dite occulte dans la fixation du prix. Je ne puis cependant en venir à la conclusion que l’acheteur a entendu renoncer à tous recours lorsqu’il a acheté sujet à la garantie légale un immeuble “avec toutes les servitudes actives et passives, apparentes ou occultes”. Ce serait là faire fi de l’intention du législateur. L’acheteur ne pourrait à mon sens en invoquant une clause ainsi rédigée se dégager des servitudes d’utilité publique dont il profite autant que ses voisins. [Page 283] Le vendeur ne devrait par contre être par une telle clause, exonéré de son obligation de garantie quant à une servitude occulte “de telle importance qu’il y ait lieu de présumer que l’acheteur n’aurait pas acheté s’il en avait été instruit” (article 1519 C.c.) ou n’en aurait pas donné si haut prix. 2.2.2.2. Vente sans garantie ou sans autre garantie que celle de ses faits et gestes Ces deux clauses souffrent du même manque d’effet quant au fait personnel du vendeur et ont les mêmes effets quant à la garantie du fait des tiers. Je parle d’absence d’effet quant au fait personnel du vendeur en raison de l’impossibilité pour le vendeur de se garantir de façon générale de son obligation de garantie de son fait personnel, même par l’insertion d’une clause au contrat prévoyant que le vendeur ne sera soumis à aucune garantie. Cette prohibition est édictée à l’article 1509 du Code civil. Ces deux clauses ont par contre les mêmes effets quant à la garantie du fait des tiers. Elles affranchissent le vendeur de payer à l’acheteur évincé des dommages et intérêts, mais ne l’autorisent pas à conserver le prix. Nous avons établi tout à l’heure que la restitution du prix était fondée sur la répétition de l’indu, et partant, si le vendeur même exonéré de toute garantie le retenait, il en résulterait un enrichissement sans cause. 2.2.2.3. Vente aux risques et périls de l’acheteur L’article 1510 du Code civil apporte néanmoins certaines restrictions à l’effet de ces clauses. Il prévoit en effet que le vendeur est tenu à la restitution du prix, à moins que l’acheteur n’ait connu lors de la vente le danger de l’éviction, ou qu’il n’ait acheté à ses risques et périls. C’est que, en pareil cas, la vente est un contrat aléatoire : l’acheteur n’a pas acheté la chose vendue, mais l’espoir de la conserver. L’acheteur a tenu compte de la possibilité d’éviction lorsqu’il a fixé le prix d’achat, de telle sorte que le prix de cet espoir de l’acheteur restera légitimement acquis au vendeur même en cas de non réalisation de cet espoir. II. GARANTIE CONTRE LES VICES CACHÉS 1. Garantie légale contre les vices cachés Après avoir étudié les normes de l’obligation de la garantie légale et déterminé de quelle façon il est possible aux parties de faire échec par la convention à ces normes, portons maintenant notre attention sur cet autre aspect de la garantie que doit fournir le vendeur à l’acheteur : la garantie contre les vices cachés. Comme nous le disions au début de notre exposé l’obligation de garantie contre l’éviction et contre les vices cachés n’est que la conséquence de l’obligation [Page 284] du vendeur de délivrer l’immeuble vendu. Le transfert de propriété oblige le vendeur à livrer la chose convenue à la jouissance paisible et utile de l’acheteur. Cette jouissance sera paisible à condition que l’acheteur ne soit pas évincé, et elle lui sera utile à condition que la chose ne souffre pas de vices cachés. 1.1. Le principe de la garantie contre les vices cachés Le vendeur tenu de l’obligation de délivrance ne remplit pas pleinement son obligation si l’acheteur découvre un vice qui ne lui était pas connu. Comme le stipule l’article 1522 du Code civil, le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur à raison des défauts cachés de la chose vendue et de ses accessoires, qui la rendent impropre à l’usage auquel on la destine ou qui diminuent tellement son utilité que l’acquéreur ne l’aurait pas achetée ou n’en aurait pas donné si haut prix s’il les avait connus. La notion de garantie contre les vices cachés se rapproche essentiellement de la notion de garantie contre l’éviction : l’acheteur d’un immeuble atteint d’un vice caché ne subit-il pas en définitive une éviction d’usage ? Nonobstant cette similitude, le Code civil traite de la garantie des défauts cachés sous une rubrique particulière et c’est, dit-on, le fruit du désir du législateur de traiter avec moins de rigueur le vendeur en cas de vice caché consistant en un défaut matériel de la chose qu’en cas d’éviction causée par un trouble du titre. Le recours de l’acheteur fondé sur un vice caché de l’immeuble acheté s’apparente au recours pour erreur et au recours pour dol. Le vice caché peut en effet enlever à l’immeuble l’une des qualités substantielles qui ont incité l’acheteur à l’acheter, ou peut avoir été dissimulé intentionnellement par le vendeur à l’acheteur. Le consentement de l’acheteur est ainsi vicié par une erreur ou un dol et l’acheteur peut intenter une action en annulation contre le vendeur, mais il peut également, si les conditions requises sont réunies, invoquer le seul défaut matériel de l’immeuble et intenter une action en garantie. De toute façon, si un vice caché ne se cumule pas avec un vice de consentement, le seul recours de l’acheteur consistera en une action en garantie. 1.2. Conditions de la garantie contre les vices cachés Pour que le vendeur se trouve tenu à la garantie contre les vices cachés, le vice dont il se plaint devra revêtir un certain caractère : il devra être nuisible à l’usage de l’immeuble vendu, véritablement caché, antérieur à la vente et ignoré de l’acheteur lors de la vente. 1.2.1. Vice nuisible à l’usage de l’immeuble vendu Le vice dont se plaint l’acheteur devra porter sur une qualité substantielle de l’immeuble qui le rend impropre à l’usage auquel le destinait l’acheteur de sorte que ce dernier ne l’aurait pas acheté s’il l’avait connu. À défaut d’indication [Page 285] au contrat de l’usage auquel l’acheteur destine l’immeuble, il y aura lieu à la garantie du vendeur seulement si l’immeuble est impropre à l’usage auquel il est normalement censé destiné. Il se peut en effet que l’acheteur envisage un emploi de l’immeuble que le vendeur ne peut pas soupçonner ; comment alors tenir rigueur au vendeur si l’acheteur ne lui a pas, lors du contrat, dénoncé cet usage éventuel ? Si, par contre, le défaut invoqué par l’acheteur se borne à diminuer l’usage de l’immeuble, la situation sera à peu près semblable à celle qui prévaudrait en cas d’éviction partielle et le tribunal devra apprécier si cette diminution d’utilité est telle que la vente devrait être résiliée et le prix restitué, ou si l’acheteur ne devrait avoir droit qu’à une diminution de prix. 1.2.2. Vice caché L’article 1522 du Code civil prévoit que le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur à raison des défauts cachés de la chose vendue et l’article 1523 stipule que le v...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 4\\n... rait être résiliée et le prix restitué, ou si l’acheteur ne devrait avoir droit qu’à une diminution de prix. 1.2.2. Vice caché L’article 1522 du Code civil prévoit que le vendeur est tenu de garantir l’acheteur à raison des défauts cachés de la chose vendue et l’article 1523 stipule que le vendeur n’est pas tenu des vices apparents et dont l’acheteur a pu luimême connaître l’existence. Le vice, pour donner ouverture au recours en garantie, ne devra donc pas, lors de la vente, se révéler à l’examen de l’immeuble vendu. Le vendeur sera tenu de l’obligation de garantie si le vice est caché, mais ne le sera pas si le vice est apparent. Comment déterminer le caractère apparent ou caché d’un défaut. Si le défaut dont se plaint l’acheteur crève les yeux, l’acheteur ne pourra certes pas s’en plaindre et le vendeur ne lui devra pas garantie. Mais tous les vices ne crèvent pas les yeux et il importe de ne pas pénaliser le vendeur d’un défaut par cela seul que l’acheteur ne l’a pas aperçu ou n’en a pas perçu la gravité. L’article 1523 impose en fait une obligation plus grande à l’acheteur, lorsqu’il stipule que le vendeur n’est pas tenu des vices apparents dont l’acheteur a pu lui-même connaître l’existence. En se basant sur cette rédaction, les tribunaux ont tout d’abord établi que les vices apparents étaient ceux qui pouvaient être décelés par un examen sérieux de la part de l’acheteur lui-même. Ainsi considéré, un vice pouvait être qualifié de vice caché quant à un certain acheteur et de vice apparent quant à un autre. Jugeant par la suite intolérable l’insécurité découlant de l’appréciation du défaut sur une base purement subjective par rapport à l’acheteur, les tribunaux ont extensionné le sens de l’expression “a pu connaître” de l’article 1523 du Code civil. Ils ont cherché à apprécier le caractère caché ou apparent d’un vice en se demandant si le vice aurait pu être découvert par une personne disposant des mêmes connaissances techniques que l’acheteur, sans tenir compte de la condition spécifique de l’acheteur au moment où il a contracté. [Page 286] Aujourd’hui, les tribunaux vont beaucoup plus loin et cherchent même parfois à apprécier le caractère apparent ou caché d’un vice sur une base purement objective, indépendamment de l’acheteur. Suivant cette appréciation objective, un vice pourrait être considéré comme apparent si, échappant aux yeux d’un acheteur inexpérimenté, son existence pourrait être constatée tout de suite par quelqu’un de plus compétent. Dans une telle hypothèse, l’acheteur serait considéré en faute pour n’avoir pas utilisé les services d’une personne qui s’y connaissait mieux que lui. L’arrêt Dallaire v. Villeneuve 13 nous fournit un bel exemple de cette interprétation. Cette obligation de consulter un expert est cependant moins essentielle pour un acheteur d’immeuble neuf acquis directement du contracteur, puisque le contracteur-vendeur ne pourrait jamais plaider sa non-responsabilité pour les défauts causés par son propre manque de compétence à moins de négligence patente de l’acheteur. À certaines occasions, les tribunaux ont non seulement imposé à l’acheteur l’obligation de consulter des gens plus compétents, des experts capables de déceler par un examen normal les vices que lui-même pouvait ne pas déceler, mais l’ont privé de son recours contre le vendeur lorsque ces experts n’ont pas, aux yeux du tribunal, examiné l’immeuble de façon assez intensive. On en est venu à apprécier le travail des experts d’après des critères objectifs d’excellence qui semblent dépasser l’esprit de l’article 1523 du Code civil : c’est la conclusion qui se dégage du jugement rendu par un banc de cinq juges, dont deux dissidents, de la Cour d’appel dans l’arrêt Levine v. Frank W. Horner Limited 14 dont nous donnerons les faits plus loin. 1.2.3. Vice antérieur à la vente Pour donner lieu à la garantie, le vice, ou au moins le germe du vice doit être antérieur à la vente. Cette condition n’est pas exprimée dans la loi, mais est néanmoins indispensable ; le vendeur ne saurait garantir un défaut postérieur à la vente car, à la suite du transfert de propriété, res perit domino. 1.2.4. Vice inconnu de l’acheteur L’article 1522 du Code civil stipule non seulement que le vice soit caché, mais qu’il soit inconnu de l’acheteur. Si l’acheteur connaît le vice, caché ou apparent, il ne peut s’en plaindre et il est censé renoncer à la garantie de ce vice ; il a acheté en connaissance de cause et a tenu compte de l’existence du vice au moment de la fixation du prix. La situation de l’acheteur à cet égard est la même, qu’il se plaigne d’un vice qui lui cause éviction ou d’un vice caché. [Page 287] La bonne foi de l’acheteur sera présumée et il appartiendra au vendeur de prouver que l’acheteur connaissait ce vice pour que la demande de ce dernier soit repoussée. S’il apparaît toutefois que le vice est si normal que l’acheteur devait de toute façon en soupçonner l’existence, le fardeau de la preuve sera renversé et il lui appartiendra de prouver le contraire. 1.3. Effet de la garantie contre les vices cachés : Recours Ainsi, si le vice matériel dont se plaint l’acheteur revêt les quatre caractères ci-dessus, ce vice donnera ouverture au recours de l’acheteur en garantie contre son vendeur. Le vendeur sera alors tenu de restituer à l’acheteur tout ou partie du prix et en plus de lui payer des dommages-intérêts s’il est de mauvaise foi. Aux termes de l’article 1526 du Code civil, l’acheteur a le choix de rendre la chose et de se faire restituer le prix, ou de garder la chose et de se faire rendre une partie du prix suivant évaluation. Dans la première hypothèse, on donnera à son recours le vocable d’action rédhibitoire et dans la seconde hypothèse le vocable d’action estimatoire ou quanti minoris. L’action rédhibitoire est, en définitive, une action en résolution de la vente et l’action estimatoire, une action en diminution de prix. L’acheteur seul aura la faculté de choisir son recours, et pourra même modifier sa demande tant et aussi longtemps qu’un jugement ne sera pas intervenu sur sa première procédure ou tant que le vendeur n’aura pas acquiescé à sa première demande. Toutefois, si l’acheteur a aliéné l’immeuble et ne peut le rendre au vendeur, il n’aura pas cette option et devra nécessairement intenter une action estimatoire. Il est possible que l’existence du vice caché et la résolution de la vente qui en résulte accompagnée de la restitution du prix cause préjudice à l’acheteur. Si par exemple Jean achète en 1975 un immeuble qu’il paie vingt mille dollars ($20,000.00) et exerce en 1980 une action rédhibitoire fondée sur l’existence d’un vice caché et obtient jugement, il devra vraisemblablement payer un plus haut prix pour l’achat d’un immeuble semblable. Comme l’acheteur évincé a droit, aux termes de l’article 1514 du Code, de réclamer du vendeur ce que l’immeuble vaut au-dessus du prix de la vente, l’acheteur dont l’immeuble souffre d’un vice caché pourra suivant l’article 1527 réclamer tous les dommagesintérêts subis par lui. Le droit de l’acheteur à des dommages-intérêts existera toutefois à condition que l’acheteur soit de bonne foi et que le vendeur soit de mauvaise foi. La bonne foi de l’acheteur dont l’immeuble est atteint d’un vice caché s’évaluera de la même façon que celle de l’acheteur victime d’une éviction : il devra avoir ignoré lors de la vente ou n’être pas légalement présumé avoir connu le vice caché dont il se plaint. Quant au vendeur, il sera réputé de mauvaise foi s’il connaissait lors de la vente l’existence du vice ou s’il est légalement présumé l’avoir connu. Cette mauvaise foi du vendeur sera assimilée à un dol de sa part, dol dont l’acheteur devra faire la preuve. [Page 288] Cette preuve de la mauvaise foi du vendeur sera plus au moins facile à faire selon les circonstances. Le constructeur-vendeur d’un immeuble neuf sera facilement présumé avoir connu les vices de la chose en raison de ses connaissances techniques. Il sera d’autre part beaucoup plus difficile de faire la preuve de la mauvaise foi du vendeur d’un immeuble construit il y a vingt ans puisqu’au départ l’acheteur sera présumé avoir acheté une bâtisse dépréciée et avoir accepté les risques qui y sont inhérents. Il sera souvent difficile de coiffer du bon vocable le recours de l’acheteur contre le vendeur. L’arrêt Bellerose v. Bouvier et Dauphinais 15 nous en donne un exemple frappant. Dans cette espèce, l’acheteur d’un établissement de commerce avait été victime d’un dol incident fondé uniquement sur la fausseté de ce que les vendeurs lui avaient représenté quant au débit de l’établissement. Il s’était pourvu contre son vendeur par action en dommages-intérêts. Le juge de première instance avait traité la demande comme une action en diminution de prix et l’avait rejetée pour le motif que le dol ne donnait ouverture qu’à l’action en annulation, l’action en diminution de prix prise par l’acheteur n’étant ouverte que dans les cas visés par les articles 1501 et 1526 du Code civil, c’est-à-dire exclusivement à raison d’un défaut de contenance ou d’un défaut caché. Ce jugement fut renversé par la Cour d’appel, et je me permets de vous citer un passage des notes du juge Pratte qui manifeste jusqu’à quel point les recours de l’acheteur en cas de vice caché et en cas de dol se confondent : Si [dit le juge Pratte] l’action en diminution de prix est ouverte contre le vendeur qui n’a fait aucune représentation à l’acheteur relativement aux vices de la chose vendue, pourquoi le même recours ne pourrait-il pas être exercé contre le vendeur qui a faussement représenté l’existence. Il a donc raison de dire qu’il s’agit d’une action en dommages-intérêts. Mais le préjudice dont il veut être indemnisé a consisté en ce qu’il aurait payé pour quelque chose qu’il n’a pas eu ; et le demandeur ne requiert rien qui ne se traduirait pas, en définitive et contre son vendeur, en une réduction du prix qu’il a payé. Le premier juge a donc eu raison de dire qu’il s’agissait d’une action en diminution de prix. En l’espèce, le demandeur n’eut pas droit à des dommages-intérêts parce qu’il n’a pas réussi à rapporter la preuve certaine d’un préjudice. Ce qu’il est important de noter, c’est que la Cour d’appel a dans cette espèce reconnu à l’acheteur victime d’un dol comme à l’acheteur d’un immeuble affecté d’un défaut caché, d’une part le recours en diminution de prix et par extension le recours en restitution du prix, et d’autre part le recours en dommages-intérêts lorsque cet acheteur peut faire la preuve d’un préjudice. Nonobstant cette similitude de recours réservé à l’acheteur victime d’un dol et à l’acheteur d’un immeuble affecté d’un vice caché, il n’en demeure pas moins que l’acheteur au moment d’exercer son recours devra faire preuve de grande prudence. La cour pourra en effet accepter que le vendeur d’un immeuble ne cherche pas à le déprécier et même le maquille un peu. Pour qu’il y ait dol, il faudra plus : ces manœuvres du vendeur devront faire naître l’erreur [Page 289] dans l’esprit de l’acheteur et le déterminer à acheter. Et cette distinction est très importante en raison de l’exigence rigoureuse imposée par l’article 1530 du Code à l’acheteur d’un immeuble atteint d’un vice caché et désireux d’intenter une action rédhibitoire ou estimatoire, accompagnée ou non d’une réclamation de dommages-intérêts. Suivant cet article, il devra intenter ce recours avec diligence raisonnable suivant la nature du vice et suivant l’usage du lieu où la vente s’est faite. Ce délai sera computé non pas à compter du jour de l’acte de vente mais à compter du jour où le vendeur a délivré l’immeuble à l’acheteur, car ce n’est qu’à partir de ce moment que l’acheteur pourra se rendre compte du vice. Comme le manifeste la rédaction de l’article 1530, cette durée sera laissée à l’entière appréciation du juge, à moins, comme nous le verrons ci-après, que les parties en conviennent autrement au contrat. Si le recours de l’acheteur de l’immeuble vicié n’est pas, dans l’interprétation du tribunal, intenté dans un délai raisonnable, ce dernier sera débouté et devra, pour avoir droit à une restitution totale ou partielle du prix et des dommages-intérêts, prouver la faute intentionnelle du vendeur. La preuve de cette faute intentionnelle sera certes beaucoup plus lourde et l’avantage de la prescription plus longue du délai pour intenter l’action basée sur le dol du vendeur sera souvent anéanti par cette preuve plus lourde. Que se passera-t-il par contre si, au moment où l’acheteur intente son recours en garantie, la bâtisse a péri ; si par exemple elle s’est écroulée. La situation est prévue à l’article 1529 du Code civil. Si la bâtisse achetée d’un constructeur-vendeur s’écroule en raison d’un vice de structure, le vendeur devra en supporter les conséquences et l’acheteur pourra intenter son action rédhibitoire et obtenir la restitution du prix bien qu’il ne soit pas capable de rendre la bâtisse à son vendeur. Par contre, si le bâtiment s’écroule lors d’un ouragan, c’est-à-dire par cas fortuit, ou par la négligence de l’acheteur,...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-1975-1-icfa6aa9c-a839-eee4-ab54-b9d84fc41304-la-garantie-du-vendeur-dimmeuble\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.02683013491332531, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-1975-1-icfa6aa9c-a839-eee4-ab54-b9d84fc41304-la-garantie-du-vendeur-dimmeuble\", \"title\": \"Taschereau, Georges - La garantie du vendeur d’immeuble, dans Cours de perfectionnement du notariat (1975)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-1975-1-icfa6aa9c-a839-eee4-ab54-b9d84fc41304-la-garantie-du-vendeur-dimmeuble\", \"@search.score\": 0.02683013491332531, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:doctrine\", \"query\": \"La garantie de qualité en vente impose une responsabilité spécifique lorsque le vice répond aux conditions cumulatives : gravité, antériorité, caractère réellement caché et ignorance de l’acheteur. La doctrine insiste sur la méthode d’appréciation « d’hypothèse raisonnable » du degré de dissimulation et sur la charge probatoire relative à la nature et à l’origine du défaut. Elle distingue aussi le vice caché des manquements à une obligation d’information ou des défauts apparents, et discute l’incidence d’une inspection diligente sur l’élément « inconnu ».\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-1975-1-icfa6aa9c-a839-eee4-ab54-b9d84fc41304-la-garantie-du-vendeur-dimmeuble\"}, \"token_count\": 9924, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-1975-1-icfa6aa9c-a839-eee4-ab54-b9d84fc41304-la-garantie-du-vendeur-dimmeuble\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/therrien-articles-if089bf3a-2448-4f89-ab5d-99d1216a314e-recours-en-cas-de-vente-sans-garantie-legale\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/therrien-articles-if089bf3a-2448-4f89-ab5d-99d1216a314e-recours-en-cas-de-vente-sans-garantie-legale\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Jalbert, Mélissa - Recours en cas de vente sans garantie légale (2021)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... Recours en cas de vente sans garantie légale Recours en cas de vente sans garantie légale Mélissa Jalbert 2021-06-25 Un acheteur à la recherche d’une nouvelle maison décide de présenter une offre d’achat dans laquelle il renonce à la garantie légale de qualité, et ce, afin de se démarquer parmi les nombreuses offres présentées au vendeur et de s’assurer d’obtenir la propriété convoitée. La vente a effectivement lieu et quelques semaines suivant la prise de possession, il découvre la présence d’importantes infiltrations d’eau au sous-sol de la résidence. Cette problématique n’avait pas été portée à sa connaissance préalablement à la vente et n’était pas visible lors de la visite de l’immeuble. Dans cette situation, l’acheteur pourra-t-il entreprendre un recours contre le vendeur sur la base de la découverte d’un vice caché? Non. La vente d’un immeuble faite sans la garantie légale de qualité exclut généralement la possibilité pour un acheteur d’entamer une procédure judiciaire contre l’ancien propriétaire lors de la découverte d’un vice caché. Un recours pourrait toutefois être envisagé en cas de mauvaise foi du vendeur. En effet, la vente effectuée sans la garantie légale de qualité ne fait pas obstacle aux règles contractuelles ordinaires ni à l’obligation de bonne foi qui doivent gouverner les parties. Ainsi, s’il s’avère que le vendeur a usé de manœuvres dolosives préalablement à la vente, c’est-à-dire si celui-ci a sciemment caché l’existence d’un vice important affectant la propriété, sa responsabilité pourrait être engagée. Le dol peut, par exemple, être causé par des mensonges de la part du vendeur, mais également par son silence ou sa réticence à révéler les informations alors à sa connaissance et qui pourraient avoir une incidence sur l’intention de l’acheteur d’acheter ou non sa propriété. En somme, le vendeur doit avoir eu l’intention de tromper l’acheteur sur l’état réel de l’immeuble. L’acheteur aura alors le fardeau de prouver la mauvaise foi du vendeur, car la bonne foi se présume. Dans une telle situation, si l’acheteur est en mesure de démontrer que son consentement a été vicié par le dol de son cocontractant, mais également qu’il porte sur un élément essentiel de la transaction, à savoir que s’il l’avait su, il n’aurait jamais acheté la propriété ou encore, ne l’aurait pas fait au prix payé, il pourrait entreprendre un recours contre le vendeur afin de faire annuler la vente ou encore, afin d’obtenir une compensation financière, selon le cas. S’il est incapable de démontrer le dol du vendeur, l’acheteur n’aura pas de recours....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/therrien-articles-if089bf3a-2448-4f89-ab5d-99d1216a314e-recours-en-cas-de-vente-sans-garantie-legale\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.031054403632879257, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/therrien-articles-if089bf3a-2448-4f89-ab5d-99d1216a314e-recours-en-cas-de-vente-sans-garantie-legale\", \"title\": \"Jalbert, Mélissa - Recours en cas de vente sans garantie légale (2021)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/therrien-articles-if089bf3a-2448-4f89-ab5d-99d1216a314e-recours-en-cas-de-vente-sans-garantie-legale\", \"@search.score\": 0.031054403632879257, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 7, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:situation\", \"query\": \"Problème découvert après la possession d’un immeuble : fissures, infiltration ou défaut structurel dissimulé. Acheteur n’a pas eu l’info avant l’acte, aucun indice visible lors de la visite. Litige en Cour du Québec sur la gravité du défaut et la preuve que le vendeur l’avait avant la vente.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:doctrine\", \"query\": \"La garantie de qualité en vente impose une responsabilité spécifique lorsque le vice répond aux conditions cumulatives : gravité, antériorité, caractère réellement caché et ignorance de l’acheteur. La doctrine insiste sur la méthode d’appréciation « d’hypothèse raisonnable » du degré de dissimulation et sur la charge probatoire relative à la nature et à l’origine du défaut. Elle distingue aussi le vice caché des manquements à une obligation d’information ou des défauts apparents, et discute l’incidence d’une inspection diligente sur l’élément « inconnu ».\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/therrien-articles-if089bf3a-2448-4f89-ab5d-99d1216a314e-recours-en-cas-de-vente-sans-garantie-legale\"}, \"token_count\": 653, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/therrien-articles-if089bf3a-2448-4f89-ab5d-99d1216a314e-recours-en-cas-de-vente-sans-garantie-legale\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/wilson-et-lafleur-livres-1-c-98709d5d-9b4a-48e4-b32c-d43fd34badfc-4-2-2-section-ii-la-qualification-cachee-du-vice-implique-plus-quun-trait\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/wilson-et-lafleur-livres-1-c-98709d5d-9b4a-48e4-b32c-d43fd34badfc-4-2-2-section-ii-la-qualification-cachee-du-vice-implique-plus-quun-trait\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Edwards, Jeffrey - Section II - La qualification cachée du vice implique\\n                        plus qu’un trait dans La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois, 2e éd. (2008)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois, 2e éd. Table des matières Sous-section I Les conditions afférentes au bien lui-même A. Le vice doit posséder une certaine gravité B. Le vice doit précéder la vente Sous-section II Les conditions afférentes à l’acheteur A. Le vice doit être inconnu B. Le vice doit être occulte Section II - La qualification cachée du vice implique plus qu’un trait 358 – La garantie protège l’usage du bien en proscrivant la présence d’un vice « caché ». Dans le contexte de la garantie, le terme « caché » est toutefois trompeur. Celui-ci laisse présager qu’il suffit que le vice soit uniquement masqué ou clandestin. Certes, pour être qualifié de caché suivant la garantie, le vice doit être occulte. Cependant, le vice [Page 165] interdit par l’ article 1726 C.c.Q . doit satisfaire à d’autres conditions, non moins essentielles. Le vice doit être antérieur à la vente, grave et inconnu de l’acheteur. Le terme « caché » dans l’expression « vice caché » possède donc, au sein de la garantie, un sens juridique unique et précis. Les conditions menant à la qualification cachée du vice se divisent en deux camps. Nous examinerons d’abord celles qui relèvent du bien lui-même, soit de la gravité du vice et de l’antériorité de celui-ci par rapport à la vente. Nous traiterons ensuite des conditions mettant directement en cause l’acheteur, soit celles qui concernent les caractères inconnu et occulte du vice. Sous-section I Les conditions afférentes au bien lui-même A. Le vice doit posséder une certaine gravité 359 – Pour que le vice soit interdit selon la garantie, le déficit d’usage entraîné ne doit pas être d’une quelconque importance. La perte d’usage doit être grave. La doctrine a toutefois trop souvent confondu la condition de gravité et l’existence d’un déficit d’usage. Lorsque le vice rend le bien « impropre à l’usage » ou diminue son « utilité 790 », il est souvent considéré déjà comme grave 791 . Or, le déficit d’usage relève plutôt de l’existence du vice. Il n’y a donc pas lieu de revenir sur la question en ce qui a trait à la gravité de celui-ci. La condition de gravité ne vérifie donc pas l’existence d’un déficit d’usage mais réglemente uniquement son intensité. Ainsi, la difficulté se situe dans le choix et l’application du critère fixant cette intensité. 360 – Le critère déterminant est énoncé dans l’ article 1726 C.c.Q . Seul le vice entraînant un déficit d’usage au point « que l’acheteur ne l’aurait pas acheté, ou n’aurait pas donné si haut prix » est réprimé 791.1 . Nous pourrions songer ici à une norme juridique plus générale, tel le caractère « sérieux » ou « important » du vice. La nature rudimentaire de la norme est due en fait à son origine antique. La norme fut élaborée dans le contexte primitif de la vente des biens au marché public. Elle possède [Page 166] toutefois certains avantages. D’abord, elle est simple d’application. La norme cherche essentiellement à déterminer l’effet d’une connaissance hypothétique du vice par l’acheteur sur les conditions clés intervenues au moment de la vente. Seuls deux effets sont envisagés : l’acheteur n’aurait point procédé à la vente ou n’y aurait procédé que moyennant une diminution du prix. Tout effet moindre ne justifie pas la gravité requise. L’acheteur peut être excessivement déçu en raison du vice. À moins de pouvoir établir, à tout le moins, qu’il aurait exigé une réduction du prix, la garantie ne lui est d’aucun secours. Nos tribunaux ont estimé que le vice ne satisfaisant pas à cette norme minimale ne peut être qualifié de grave 792 . La gravité au sens de la garantie se limite donc à deux normes possibles, soit la non-conclusion du contrat ou la baisse du prix convenu. Ces deux degrés trouvent écho dans les recours traditionnels de la garantie, soit la résolution de la vente et la réduction du prix 793 . Chaque niveau de gravité débouche donc sur un recours précis en réparation. 361 – Une difficulté persiste dans l’application de cette double norme. Celle-ci peut tout autant se référer à l’acheteur véritable du bien qu’à l’acheteur raisonnable théorique. Pour l’acheteur véritable, la gravité serait acquise par la preuve qu’il n’aurait, en raison de ses motifs particuliers, ni procédé à la vente ni payé le prix entier. La formulation de l’ article 1726 C.c.Q . n’écarte pas cette interprétation en ne faisant référence qu’à l’acheteur et non à l’acheteur raisonnable. Par ailleurs, certains tribunaux paraissent appuyer une évaluation subjective de la gravité 794 , allant même jusqu’à invoquer l’état psychologique propre à l’acheteur 795 . 362 – Cette approche nous paraît erronée et contraire à la logique de la garantie. L’usage protégé est celui de l’acheteur raisonnable. Les désirs subjectifs ne sont protégés que dans la mesure où ils sont conformes aux attentes d’un tel acheteur. Le critère de la gravité réglemente ici l’intensité du déficit à cet usage. L’évaluation de la gravité doit donc logiquement être aussi objective que celle du déficit d’usage. De fait, il est impossible de mesurer subjectivement un déficit d’usage ne possédant aucun élément subjectif. Un courant de jurisprudence, plus conséquent par rapport à la dynamique de la garantie, refuse de [Page 167] qualifier de grave le vice dont la gravité dépend des attentes personnelles de l’acheteur 796 , notamment sur le plan esthétique 797 . En ce sens, une déclaration de l’acheteur à savoir qu’il aurait moins payé ne suffit pas ; il lui faut établir qu’un acheteur raisonnable aurait exigé une diminution du prix. Lorsque le vice n’occasionne notamment qu’une baisse infime de valeur, la condition de la gravité n’est pas satisfaite 798 . 363 – Il ne paraît pas inopportun d’examiner si la condition de gravité du vice doit également être observée en droit de la consommation. En ce qui a trait à la responsabilité spéciale pour vice caché prévue à l’ article 53 de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur , la réponse ne peut faire de doute. En se basant sur la notion de vice caché, l’article incorpore par référence toutes les conditions afférentes à ce terme selon le Code civil . La situation est différente pour les garanties d’aptitude à l’usage et de durabilité raisonnable prévues aux articles 37 et 38 de cette loi. Leurs textes ne parlent ni d’un vice ni de son caractère caché. Ils énoncent plutôt des résultats précis, soit que le bien doit servir à son usage, et ce, pendant une durée raisonnable. Les autorités, tant doctrinales que jurisprudentielles, semblent s’entendre sur l’idée que, suivant ces articles, l’acheteur-consommateur bénéficie d’une protection à l’endroit de toute perte anormale de l’usage du bien 799 . Il s’ensuit, à notre avis, que toute diminution de l’usage, même minime, survenant à l’intérieur de la période visée, est sujette à réparation. 364 – La condition de gravité énoncée dans le Code permet de mesurer l’intensité des effets du vice. Tout comme l’existence du vice, la gravité de celui-ci s’apprécie suivant son effet sur l’usage protégé. L’autre condition afférente au bien fixe le vice dans le temps. [Page 168] B. Le vice doit précéder la vente 365 – La condition selon laquelle le vice doit précéder la vente possède également une origine antique. Elle ne constitue que l’application propre à la garantie de la maxime res perit domino du droit romain : une fois le droit de propriété transmis, l’acheteur assume les risques du bien. En ce qui concerne la garantie, l’acheteur assume les risques d’une perte de qualité se produisant après la vente 799.1 . Seul est prohibé le vice entachant le bien avant la conclusion du contrat. 366 – En vertu du Code civil du Bas Canada , la condition d’antériorité du vice se justifiait à même la doctrine 800 et la jurisprudence 801 . Dans le Code civil du Québec , cette condition est codifiée dans l’ article 1726 C.c.Q . qui limite la garantie expressément aux vices touchant le bien « lors de la vente 802 ». Le vendeur n’est donc pas tenu de répondre des vices naissant après le transport du droit de propriété. 367 – Il importe de distinguer toutefois le moment de l’existence du vice de celui de sa manifestation. Le vice peut exister au moment de la vente dans un état latent. Rien alors ne laisse présager sa présence. Il s’agit, par exemple, de la pièce fissurée ne montrant aucune manifestation ou de l’animal atteint d’une maladie pendant la période d’incubation. Après la vente, le vice apparaît au grand jour. Dans des cas du genre, les tribunaux ont décidé qu’il suffit que le vice soit simplement en germe à l’occasion de la vente pour satisfaire à la condition d’antériorité 803 . 367.1 – Tel que mentionné ailleurs 803.1 , le législateur a introduit l’article 1729 C.c. Q. afin de faciliter la preuve, surtout dans les causes de [Page 169] nature technique, de l’existence du vice. Par le fait même, le législateur a facilité le fardeau de preuve que le vice existait au moment de la vente. En effet, l’ article 1729 C.c.Q . crée une présomption juridique, dans la vente conclue par un vendeur professionnel, que le vice existait au moment de la vente lorsque le mauvais fonctionnement du bien ou sa déterioration survient prématurément par rapport à des biens semblables. En effet, le vendeur professionnel est tenu de garantir que le bien est en tous points propre à l’usage auquel il est destiné. Le fardeau lui appartient donc d’établir que la défaillance prématurée ne relève pas de lui 803.2 , à défaut de quoi la présomption légale doit recevoir application. La présomption n’est toutefois pas absolue. Elle peut être repoussée par la preuve prépondérante que la défaillance constatée est attribuable à l’acheteur 803.3 . 368 – En ce qui a trait au droit de la consommation, précisons que les autorités sont divisées sur la question à savoir si l’antériorité de la cause d’inaptitude est une condition des garanties d’aptitude à l’usage et de durabilité raisonnable prévues aux articles 37 et 38 de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur 804 . Ces garanties ne sont pas basées sur le concept de vice caché. Elles assurent plutôt à l’acheteur-consommateur des résultats précis, c’est-à-dire que le bien servira à son usage et ce, pendant une durée raisonnable. À notre avis, il n’y a donc pas lieu de requérir [Page 170] la preuve d’antériorité. Néanmoins, celle-ci peut être un critère pertinent dans l’appréciation du respect des objectifs visés par ces garanties. Si la diminution de l’usage s’explique par l’entretien déficient ou l’usage abusif du bien, l’usage auquel le bien est « normalement » destiné, selon l’ article 37 , ou l’usage « normal », selon l’ article 38 , ne peut être perturbé. Dans le même sens, rappelons que l’usage négligent ou abusif d’un bien ne peut être une source de vice au sens de la garantie du droit commun. 369 – Les conditions de la qualification cachée du vice selon le droit commun dépendent aussi de l’acheteur, soit de sa prise de conscience de l’existence du vice. Sous-section II Les conditions afférentes à l’acheteur A. Le vice doit être inconnu 370 – Le vice connu de l’acheteur n’est pas un vice caché. Seul le vice que ce dernier ignore peut recevoir cette qualification. Dans le Code civil du Bas Canada , cette condition aussi dépendait de la doctrine 805 et de la jurisprudence 806 . Le Code civil du Québec , pour sa part, la codifie en écartant formellement le « vice caché connu de l’acheteur 807 ». La plupart des auteurs s’exprimant en vertu de l’ancien Code distinguaient la condition de non-connaissance du vice de celle de son caractère occulte 808 . La formulation du nouveau Code ne laisse aucun doute sur le fait qu’elles constituent des conditions distinctes en énonçant que le vendeur n’est tenu ni du « vice caché connu de l’acheteur ni [du] vice apparent 809 ». En revanche, la doctrine, en vertu de l’ancien Code, a eu un mal considérable à départager conceptuellement ces conditions. Des critères semblables furent généralement utilisés pour évaluer le caractère connu et le caractère occulte du vice 810 : [Page 171] lorsque le vice devait être décelé par l’acheteur, il était à la fois apparent et connu. 371 – La confusion entre les deux conditions n’est pas récente. Les édiles romains n’avaient pas formellement prescrit une condition concernant le caractère connu du vice, mais ils avaient plutôt obligé le vendeur à dénoncer tout vice du bien 811 . Le vendeur fut alors tenu d’inscrire les vices qui lui furent connus sur un tableau adjacent au bien au moment de la vente 812 . Il s’aquittait ainsi de son devoir de dénonciation et l’acheteur était réputé avoir accepté le bien avec les vices inscrits. En revanche, la condition concernant le caractère occulte du vice s’est développée comme une exception à l’obligation de dénonciation. Les édiles décidèrent progressivement que le vendeur n’était pas tenu d’indiquer le vice que l’acheteur pouvait lui-même remarquer. Le vice fut dans ce cas considéré comme apparent 813 . 372 – À l’issue des différentes mutations de la garantie à travers les âges, l’obligation de dénonciation du vice par le vendeur est devenue, en droit moderne, une condition de la non-connaissance de l’acheteur. Le critère décisif s’est déplacé du geste du vendeur à l’esprit de l’acheteur et s’est étendu à la connaissance provenant de toute source. La distinction essentielle entre le vice connu et le vice apparent tient désormais au caractère subjectif de la connaissance du vice par l’acheteur. Le vice connu est celui dont l’acheteur possède une connaissance réelle, notamment par l’entremise d’une dénonciation. Quant au vice apparent, c’est celui que l’acheteur aurait dû, en raison de son devoir d’examen, constater. Le vice apparent peut être ent...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n... expert dans le domaine du bien, plus il est obligé de procéder à un examen approfondi. Le même vice peut donc être jugé occulte à l’endroit d’un acheteur profane et apparent pour un acheteur professionnel 851 . [Page 179] Il s’agit là d’une application particulière d’une règle générale de l’appréciation objective selon laquelle « les aptitudes particulières peuvent parfaitement, à la différence des inaptitudes, être prises en considération 852 ». L’acheteur possédant des connaissances spécialisées ne peut dès lors se mettre à l’abri de sa négligence en invoquant la norme de l’acheteur raisonnable moyen. À l’exception de certaines ouvertures doctrinales en ce sens 853 , la majorité des auteurs et l’ensemble de la jurisprudence au Québec paraissent cependant ignorer tout du caractère variable de la norme objective de l’acheteur dans l’appréciation du caractère occulte du vice. Les tribunaux québécois en sont néanmoins venus au même résultat par la voie d’un développement encore plus influent dans l’évaluation du caractère occulte. 383 – Comme nous pouvons le constater, la condition traitant du caractère occulte du vice est d’application plus difficile que les autres. La gravité s’établit par la constatation du niveau du déficit d’usage. L’antériorité n’est qu’une simple question de fait. La connaissance se limite généralement à la preuve directe d’une dénonciation. Quant à la condition du caractère occulte, elle n’est en réalité qu’une application particulière d’un concept beaucoup plus vaste et fondamental. Sous le couvert d’un examen par un acheteur prudent et diligent, la condition véhicule essentiellement une responsabilité fondée sur la faute 854 . Le vice occulte est celui qui échappe à un acheteur consciencieux. Le vice apparent est celui qu’un acheteur négligent omet de déceler. Or, la détermination d’une faute ne dépend jamais de l’analyse d’un ou de deux éléments. Elle oblige à l’examen de tout fait pertinent aux circonstances. Notre doctrine a toujours su apprécier que la détermination du caractère occulte est une question de fait impliquant l’ensemble des circonstances de la vente 855 . Une telle énonciation est toutefois peu instructive et ne facilite en rien l’application de la condition. 384 – Une fois la question de la norme pertinente réglée 856 , force nous est de constater une certaine prise de conscience au sein de la [Page 180] jurisprudence québécoise, à savoir que la détermination du caractère occulte d’un vice relève d’une analyse factuelle particulière. Au cours des dernières années, les tribunaux ont commencé à privilégier la mise en évidence et l’ordonnancement des faits susceptibles d’influer sur la qualification de ce qui est occulte. Dès 1985, la majorité de la Cour d’appel détermine les facteurs pertinents à l’analyse circonstancielle 857 . Quatre ans plus tard, par l’arrêt Placement Jacpar , la Cour d’appel statue unanimement que la détermination du caractère occulte « impose l’examen de l’ensemble des circonstances de chaque cas. Elle prend en considération des facteurs tels que le statut du vendeur, s’il est professionnel ou non, celui de l’acquéreur, la nature, l’âge et le prix du [bien], le type de vice et, parfois, le comportement des parties 858 . » L’arrêt fournit enfin une grille précise pour délimiter et agencer les faits pertinents nécessaires à la qualification occulte. 385 – Nous déborderions évidemment le cadre du présent ouvrage en procédant à la systématisation des faits selon les facteurs précisés 859 . Il nous paraît toutefois indispensable d’indiquer sommairement les grandes lignes de la logique véhiculées par ces facteurs et qui sont déjà tracées par la jurisprudence. D’abord, le statut de l’acquéreur permet au tribunal de varier l’intensité de l’examen nécessaire selon les connaissances techniques de l’acheteur, conformément à la règle admise depuis longtemps en droit français 860 : plus l’acheteur est expert ou professionnel du bien, plus il est tenu de procéder à un examen approfondi 861 . Le statut du vendeur permet aussi au tribunal de varier l’intensité de l’examen suivant l’état professionnel ou ordinaire [Page 181] de celui-ci ; la jurisprudence atténue d’ailleurs l’ampleur de l’examen lorsque l’acheteur acquiert le bien d’un vendeur professionnel 862 . Pour sa part, le facteur de la nature du bien autorise le tribunal à varier l’intensité de l’examen en fonction de la technicité du bien. L’acheteur s’oblige donc à un examen plus vigilant en acquérant un bien technique d’occasion 863 ; de même, l’acheteur d’un immeuble à vocation commerciale ou industrielle est tenu à un examen plus fouillé qu’en matière résidentielle 864 . L’âge du bien justifie aussi une modification dans l’examen requis 865 : plus le bien est âgé, plus l’acheteur se voit obligé d’effectuer un examen approfondi 866 . Le prix joue également un rôle : plus il est élevé, plus l’acheteur se doit de procéder à un examen poussé du bien 867 . L’acheteur raisonnable se doit en effet d’accorder plus d’attention à l’achat d’un immeuble qu’à celui d’un parapluie. Par ailleurs, le type de vice exerce un effet au moment de la qualification occulte : les tribunaux ont notamment décidé que plus un vice se manifeste par des signes précurseurs, plus il doit être qualifié d’apparent 868 . Si l’existence du vice ne se révèle que par des travaux exceptionnels, par exemple d’excavation ou de démolition, il s’agit d’un [Page 182] vice occulte 868.1 . Enfin, l’arrêt Placement Jacpar mentionne que le comportement des parties doit être considéré dans la détermination du caractère occulte d’un vice : notamment les actes frauduleux du vendeur peuvent rendre occulte un vice qui serait autrement apparent 869 . De plus, les propos erronés, même de bonne foi, du vendeur peuvent avoir le même effet 869.1 , de même que la remise de documents [Page 183] fiables 869.2 . Lorsque le vendeur dénonce des problèmes existants, l’acheteur doit en faire un examen approprié 869.3 . Une nouvelle méthode d’évaluation du caractère occulte du vice émerge. Consciente de la relativité de la qualification occulte, la jurisprudence entreprend la systématisation des faits pertinents. Sous l’égide de chaque facteur cerné, une série de règles favorise la prévisibilité et l’uniformité de la qualification occulte. 386 – L’appréciation circonstancielle du caractère occulte s’est amorcée sous l’empire de l’ancien Code. Elle nous semble destinée à s’intensifier en droit actuel. Elle résulte du contact d’un principe général fondé sur la faute et des contextes sans cesse plus complexes que celui-ci est appelé à réglementer. À notre avis, la méthode circonstancielle est une réaction à l’incertitude qui plane trop souvent à l’occasion de la qualification occulte du vice. 387 – Enfin, précisons brièvement qu’en droit de la consommation, la jurisprudence est divisée au sujet de l’application de la condition du caractère occulte au regard des garanties d’aptitude à l’usage et de durabilité énoncées aux articles 37 et 38 de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur . Certains arrêts considèrent que la cause d’inaptitude doit être occulte alors que d’autres rejettent une telle condition 870 . Selon nous, il faut insister sur la formulation différente de ces garanties par rapport à la garantie de qualité du droit commun. Les garanties statutaires en question sont de conception indépendante et ne se réfèrent aucunement à la notion de vice caché. Elles visent ainsi à assurer des résultats, soit l’usage normal et la [Page 184] durabilité raisonnable du bien, sans égard au caractère clandestin de la source de l’état défectueux 871 . 388 – La condition du caractère occulte complète ainsi le tableau des exigences auxquelles le vice doit satisfaire selon la garantie de qualité du droit commun. Après que le phénomène perturbant l’usage du bien est qualifié de vice et répond aux conditions de la qualification cachée, il devient l’objet proscrit par la garantie, le vice caché. Ce dernier constitue la clé d’accès aux instruments de réalisation de la protection de la garantie. Nous étudierons ensuite le rôle des fondements de la garantie dans l’accueil réservé à l’éventualité qu’elle a tant cherché à prévenir. [Page 185] 790. Article 1726 C.c.Q . ; article 1522 C.c.B.C. 791. F. Langelier , Cours de droit civil , T. 5, supra note 627, pp. 71-72 ; L. Faribault , Traité de droit civil du Québec , T. 11, supra note 446, p. 276 ; T. Rousseau-Houle , Précis du droit de la vente et du louage , supra note 41, p. 122 ; M. Pourcelet , La vente , supra note 512, p. 150. En droit français, nous constatons la même confusion : L. Guillouard , Traités de la vente & de l’échange , T. 1, supra note 33, p. 435, n o 420 ; T. Huc , Commentaire théorique & pratique du Code civil , T. 10, supra note 568, p. 202, n o 148 ; G. Baudry-Lacantinerie et L. Saignat , Traité théorique et pratique de droit civil, De la vente et de l’échange , T. 18, supra note 33, p. 352, n o 417 ; G. Beudant , Cours de droit civil français , T. 11, supra note 566, p. 204, n o 254. 791.1. ABB Inc. c. Domtar Inc. , [2007] 3 R.C.S. 461, para. 52. 792. Bessette c. Raymond , supra note 582 ; Pouliot c. Champlain Auto Ford Inc. , supra note 647 ; Proulx c. Boisvert , [1980] R.L. 141 (C.P.) ; Gaspan S.A. c. Chase Commercial Centre Ltd. , J.E. 82-960 (C.S.), conf. J.E. 88-1208 (C.A.) ; Côté c. Gagnon , B.E. 98BE-427 (C.Q.) ; Bourque c. Madjarian , B.E. 2001BE-917 (C.Q.) ; Godin c. Labranche , J.E. 2001-1984, [2001] R.D.I 756, REJB 2001-26632 (C.Q.) ; Gauthier c. Bureau , EYB 2006-103953 (C.Q.) ; Tremblay c. Tourigny , EYB 2006-112402 (C.Q.). 793. Article 1526 C.c.B.C. ; Voir infra n o 531 et s. 794. Benoît c. Sanctuaire du Mont-Royal Ltée , supra note 505, 2875 : « [...] tout en prenant bien sûr en compte l’élément subjectif résultant de l’âge, du mode de vie passé et des attentes bien exprimées ». 795. Cook c. Warren , supra note 698, 1546 et s. 796. Edelstein c. Shaw , [1974] R.L. 314 (C.P.) ; Pominville c. Demers , supra note 754 (C.P.). 797. Fortin c. Grimard , supra note 736 (l’endroit du thermostat) ; Labis c. Montreal West Automobile Ltd. , C.A.P. 88C-27 (peinture) ; Lavoie c. Boucher , B.E. 2005BE-299, EYB 2003-81759 (C.Q.). 798. Joncas c. Blouin , [1952] R.L. 554 (C.S.) (p. 563, plomberie : 200 $/45 000 $) ; Rousseau c. Gagnon , [1987] R.J.Q. 40 (C.A.) (p. 44, toit : 5 000 $ (10 000 $/2 (dépréciation))/400 000 $) ; Tremblay c. Lebel , [1992] R.D.I. 346 (C.Q.) (p. 349, infiltration d’eau : 100 $) ; Hubert c. Aubry , [1993] R.D.I. 17 (C.S.) (p. 21, infiltrations d’eau : 1 200 $/74 500 $). L’évaluation essentiellement subjective de la gravité du vice est réservée au domaine de l’erreur. 799. T. Rousseau-Houle , « Garanties légales contre les vices cachés », (1986) 46 R. du B. 676, 768-679 ; N. L’Heureux , Droit de la consommation , supra note 689, p. 69, n o 57. Champagne c. Hyundai Auto Canada Inc. , [1988] R.J.Q. 2317 (C.P.). Quant aux arrêts affirmant que les articles 37 et 38 de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur créent des garanties indépendantes de la notion de vice caché, voir Champagne c. Hyundai Auto Canada Inc., op. cit. , Gaudreault c. Foyers Econo Inc. , C.Q.Q. n o 200-02-008235-881, le 8 mai 1991 (M. le juge A. Verge) ; Lavoie c. Chicoutimi Chrysler Plymouth (1990) Inc. , C.Q. Chicoutimi, n o 150-32-000611-919, le 14 janvier 1992 (M. le juge J. Simard). 799.1. Lafrance c. Prévost , REJB 2001-26354 (C.S.) ; Fraser c. Leboeuf , EYB 2005-96824, J.E. 2005-2148 (C.S.) ; Cinelli c. Brunelle , J.E. 2007-768, 2007 QCCS (C.S.). La même règle s’applique lorsque le vendeur se prévaut du recours en matière de garantie de qualité contre un vendeur antérieur : MacLellan c. Larin , J.E. 2007-394, EYB 2007-113271, 2007 QCCS 212 (C.S.) . 800. L. Faribault , Traité de droit civil du Québec , T. 11, supra note 446, pp. 280-281 ; T. Rousseau-Houle , Précis du droit de la vente et du louage , supra note 41, pp. 136 et s. ; M. Pourcelet , La vente , supra note 512, p. 151. 801. Rivard c...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 6\\n... entre la garantie énoncée à l’ article 37 de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur et la garantie en matière de louage en vertu du Code civil du Québec . De fait, l’ article 1854 , alinéa 2 C.c.Q . remplace l’ancienne garantie contre le « vice caché » par une garantie assurant que le bien puisse « servir à l’usage pour lequel il est loué ». La garantie de louage n’accorde donc pas non plus d’importance au caractère occulte ou apparent du phénomène qui entrave l’usage du bien. P.-G. Jobin , « La vente dans le Code civil et la Loi sur la protection du consommateur », dans Collection de droit (1996-97), vol. 5, Obligations, contrats et prescription, Cowansville, Éd. Y. Blais Inc., 1996, 149, 167. Voir aussi A. Olivier , « Fitness for Purpose in the Contract of Lease under the Civil Code of Quebec », supra note 607, 224-225....\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/wilson-et-lafleur-livres-1-c-98709d5d-9b4a-48e4-b32c-d43fd34badfc-4-2-2-section-ii-la-qualification-cachee-du-vice-implique-plus-quun-trait\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.0317540317773819, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/wilson-et-lafleur-livres-1-c-98709d5d-9b4a-48e4-b32c-d43fd34badfc-4-2-2-section-ii-la-qualification-cachee-du-vice-implique-plus-quun-trait\", \"title\": \"Edwards, Jeffrey - Section II - La qualification cachée du vice implique\\n                        plus qu’un trait dans La garantie de qualité du vendeur en droit québécois, 2e éd. (2008)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/wilson-et-lafleur-livres-1-c-98709d5d-9b4a-48e4-b32c-d43fd34badfc-4-2-2-section-ii-la-qualification-cachee-du-vice-implique-plus-quun-trait\", \"@search.score\": 0.0317540317773819, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:doctrine\", \"query\": \"La garantie de qualité en vente impose une responsabilité spécifique lorsque le vice répond aux conditions cumulatives : gravité, antériorité, caractère réellement caché et ignorance de l’acheteur. La doctrine insiste sur la méthode d’appréciation « d’hypothèse raisonnable » du degré de dissimulation et sur la charge probatoire relative à la nature et à l’origine du défaut. Elle distingue aussi le vice caché des manquements à une obligation d’information ou des défauts apparents, et discute l’incidence d’une inspection diligente sur l’élément « inconnu ».\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/wilson-et-lafleur-livres-1-c-98709d5d-9b4a-48e4-b32c-d43fd34badfc-4-2-2-section-ii-la-qualification-cachee-du-vice-implique-plus-quun-trait\"}, \"token_count\": 6658, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/wilson-et-lafleur-livres-1-c-98709d5d-9b4a-48e4-b32c-d43fd34badfc-4-2-2-section-ii-la-qualification-cachee-du-vice-implique-plus-quun-trait\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2002-2-ie2dead07-dfa9-618c-5fc1-c2d9522ec3a7-la-garantie-de-qualite-dans-les-contrats-de-vente-au-dela-des-apparences\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2002-2-ie2dead07-dfa9-618c-5fc1-c2d9522ec3a7-la-garantie-de-qualite-dans-les-contrats-de-vente-au-dela-des-apparences\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Edwards, Jeffrey - La garantie de qualité dans les contrats de vente : au-delà des apparences, dans Cours de perfectionnement du notariat (2002)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... La garantie de qualité dans les contrats de vente : au-delà des apparences La garantie de qualité dans les contrats de vente : au-delà des apparences Jeffrey Edwards * Table des matières I. INTRODUCTION II. LE RECOURS DIRECT DU SOUS-ACQUÉREUR ET SES CONSÉQUENCES a) L’application de la règle en matière de meubles b) L’application de la règle en matière d’immeubles c) La responsabilité extracontractuelle des biens entachés d’un défaut de sécurité et sa relation avec le régime des garanties d’ordre contractuel III. LES RÈGLES D’EXCLUSION DE LA GARANTIE DE QUALITÉ a) La garantie existe de plein droit b) Les règles de forme des exclusions c) Les règles de fond des exclusions i) Les limites contractuelles d’ordre général ii) Les limites par rapport aux actes personnels iii) Les limites par rapport au vice caché connu iv) Les limites par rapport au vice caché que le vendeur ne peut ignorer v) Les limites à l’exclusion aux risques et périls de l’acheteur vi) Conclusion IV. CLAUSES D’EXCLUSION COURANTES ET COMMENTAIRES a) Formulaires des contrats de vente d’un immeuble de la Chambre des notaires du Québec b) Société canadienne d’hypothèques et de logement c) Clause usuelle d’une compagnie de fiducie d) Clause usuelle de revente d’une compagnie de financement après la prise en paiement V. L’APPLICATION DE GARANTIES AUTRES QUE CELLES DE LA VENTE VI. LES CRITÈRES ET LES MODES D’ÉVALUATION DU VICE CACHÉ VII. LES RÈGLES ET LES DÉLAIS D’AVIS VIII. CONCLUSION [Page 233] SYNTHÈSE Examen et critique juridique des textes de référence et d’usage courant, à la fois des clauses contractuelles et des textes de loi, dans le but de faire ressortir leur vrai sens et leur portée juridiques, l’objectif de la conférence consiste à faire une analyse pratique, à la lumière de la jurisprudence de dernière heure, des principaux éléments, des développements et des difficultés en matière de garanties dans les contrats de vente. On abordera plus particulièrement les aspects suivants : • recours direct du sous-acquéreur en matière de meubles ; • recours direct du sous-acquéreur en matière d’immeubles ; • règles d’exclusion de la garantie ; • clauses d’exclusion de la garantie et leur efficacité ; • garanties dans les ventes d’entreprises ; • cas et difficultés pratiques ; • autres garanties applicables dans les contrats de vente ; • critères et mode d’évaluation permettant de distinguer le vice caché d’un vice qui ne l’est pas ; • règles et délais d’avis permettant de protéger les droits lors de la découverte d’un problème de qualité ; • règles quant à l’étendue des dommages dont le vendeur peut être tenu responsable, notamment les règles applicables au vendeur professionnel. [Page 235] I. INTRODUCTION Tous les juristes, et en particulier les notaires, sont appelés de manière constante à rédiger, à conseiller et à se prononcer en matière de garanties de vente. Pour ce faire, les sources normales se résument au libellé de la garantie en question et aux articles pertinents du Code civil du Québec . Ces sources auraient pu être suffisantes dans le contexte d’une liberté contractuelle absolue et de dispositions codifiées supplétives et non ambiguës. Cependant, ce n’est pas le cas de la garantie de qualité. Le régime applicable en droit québécois fourmille de règles spéciales, d’interprétations jurisprudentielles particulières, de dispositions impératives et de principes interdépendants d’autres règles du Code civil du Québec , de sorte que la teneur littérale d’une clause de garantie peut être complètement différente de son véritable effet juridique. L’objectif du présent texte est de faire ressortir sommairement les principales notions particulières qui influent sur la détermination de la portée et de l’effet des garanties de qualité dans les contrats de vente. II. LE RECOURS DIRECT DU SOUS-ACQUÉREUR ET SES CONSÉQUENCES a) L’application de la règle en matière de meubles La garantie de qualité de la vente se présente dans notre droit comme une obligation d’ordre purement contractuel qui n’incombe qu’au vendeur immédiat. Cette apparence est trompeuse ou, à tout le moins, incomplète. La garantie de qualité de la vente, en plus d’être une obligation contractuelle personnelle, est une obligation contractuelle de source légale du fait de l’article 1716 C.c.Q., en ce que sa création est dictée et réglementée par un texte de loi. Pour ce motif, la garantie légale de qualité a fait l’objet d’une interprétation jurisprudentielle particulière. En 1979, dans le célèbre arrêt General Motors Products of Canada c. Kravitz 1 , la Cour suprême a décidé que la garantie de qualité de la vente constituait un accessoire intimement lié au bien, qui était transmis avec la propriété de [Page 236] celui-ci 2 . Il en résultait que M. Kravitz, le propriétaire d’une automobile neuve de marque Oldsmobile fabriquée par General Motors, était, de ce fait même, créancier de l’obligation de garantie que General Motors devait à son acheteur immédiat, soit son concessionnaire. Cette règle de l’accessoire qui est transmis avec la propriété d’un bien a été codifiée dans le Code civil du Québec aux termes de l’article 1442. Il faut noter, cependant, que le législateur ne s’est pas contenté de codifier la règle dite de Kravitz . Il a également inséré, dans les dispositions spécifiques traitant de la garantie de qualité dans le Code civil du Québec , une nouvelle disposition renforçant davantage les recours du sous-acquéreur. En effet, afin d’éliminer toute ambiguïté quant à l’opération du recours et d’écarter certains écueils d’ordre théorique soulevés par la doctrine et la jurisprudence 3 , le législateur a ajouté l’article 1730 C.c.Q. qui prévoit que le fabricant, le distributeur, l’importateur, le grossiste et tout fournisseur – en fait, tous les intermédiaires commerciaux dans la chaîne des ventes d’un bien – sont tenus à la garantie de qualité du vendeur envers le sous-acquéreur, « comme s’ils agissaient à ce titre » 4 , c’est-à-dire à celui du vendeur. Il faut donc se rappeler, lors de la rédaction des conditions de la garantie, que tout vendeur professionnel de biens meubles est, de droit, débiteur non seulement de la garantie légale de qualité à l’endroit de son acheteur immédiat, mais aussi de tout acheteur subséquent et, le cas échéant, il faudra conseiller le vendeur à cet égard. Nous verrons plus tard certaines des conséquences de cette règle. b) L’application de la règle en matière d’immeubles Hormis l’innovation du législateur par l’introduction de l’article 1730 C.c.Q., l’existence même de la règle du recours direct en vertu de la garantie de qualité en matière de meubles ne surprend guère, car elle fait partie de notre droit depuis l’arrêt Kravitz 5 . Il en est autrement en ce qui a trait à l’application de la règle en matière d’immeubles. D’une part, l’arrêt Kravitz concernait un meuble, à savoir une automobile fabriquée par General Motors. D’autre part, rien dans les motifs du jugement de la Cour suprême restreignait son effet à la vente de biens meubles. D’ailleurs, certaines autorités auxquelles la Cour suprême s’est référée [Page 237] pour justifier le raisonnement dans son jugement avaient trait à un contexte immobilier 6 . De même, le texte de la codification dans le Code civil du Québec de la règle dite de Kravitz concernant la transmission d’un droit accessoire lors du transfert du droit de propriété ne limite nullement son application à la vente de biens meubles. L’article 1442 C.c.Q. est inséré dans le Livre « Des obligations » sous la rubrique « Des effets du contrat à l’égard des tiers – Dispositions générales ». En 1999, la Cour d’appel du Québec était appelée à trancher la question de l’application de la règle de Kravitz en matière de vente d’immeubles 7 . Dans l’arrêt Hay c. Jacques 8 , la Cour d’appel a répondu affirmativement. Les trois juges de la Cour d’appel étaient unanimes à reconnaître que la règle de Kravitz s’appliquait à la vente immobilière. Cependant, il y eut deux opinions émises ayant des motifs légèrement différents. Selon la majorité de la Cour 9 , le sous-acquéreur d’un immeuble pouvait, de ce fait, recourir en justice, en principe, contre le premier vendeur (notamment, le vendeur-entrepreneur 10 ou le vendeur ayant acquis le terrain, qui fait construire le bâtiment pour ensuite revendre le tout), de même que contre tout vendeur intermédiaire dans la chaîne des ventes. Selon l’opinion minoritaire 11 , il n’était pas nécessaire de se prononcer sur la responsabilité des vendeurs intermédiaires, car selon les faits de l’instance, le vendeur en question, à l’encontre duquel le recours direct fut dirigé, était le premier vendeur de l’immeuble. Selon la Cour d’appel, la reconnaissance d’un recours direct n’augmentait pas la responsabilité du vendeur. Le premier vendeur et le vendeur intermédiaire pouvaient être également tenus de répondre du vice existant au moment de leur vente respective suivant les recours récursoires traditionnels qui remontaient la chaîne des ventes. En ce sens, le recours direct ne fait en sorte que le dernier acquéreur peut se prévaloir de la garantie de qualité accordée aux acheteurs intermédiaires par leurs propres vendeurs. Il importe donc de se rappeler que, selon cet arrêt de principe de la Cour d’appel, le sous-acquéreur d’un immeuble est autorisé à poursuivre directement, sur la base d’un vice caché, le premier vendeur, et que la majorité de la Cour a autorisé un recours de cette nature contre les vendeurs intermédiaires. [Page 238] c) La responsabilité extracontractuelle des biens entachés d’un défaut de sécurité et sa relation avec le régime des garanties d’ordre contractuel Aux termes du Code civil du Québec , le législateur ne s’est pas contenté de bonifier, au profit de l’acheteur, le régime légal de garantie de qualité pour les biens défectueux. Il a également introduit dans le Code civil une nouvelle responsabilité extracontractuelle des biens entachés d’un défaut de sécurité. Cette nouvelle responsabilité est encadrée par les articles 1468 , 1469 et 1473 du Code civil du Québec 12 . Il s’agit d’une responsabilité extracontractuelle moderne qui s’est inspirée de la Directive européenne du 25 juillet 1985 relative aux produits défectueux 13 , celle-ci s’étant préalablement inspirée du régime de Strict Liability en vigueur aux États-Unis d’Amérique depuis le milieu des années 1960. Cependant, il faut bien cerner le domaine d’application restreint de cette nouvelle responsabilité. Selon les Commentaires du ministre de la Justice, ce régime de responsabilité extracontractuelle est ouvert à la victime d’un bien entaché d’un défaut de sécurité uniquement lorsque cette victime est un tiers par rapport aux débiteurs soumis à la responsabilité, à savoir le fabricant, le distributeur, le grossiste, l’importateur, le fournisseur ou le détaillant du bien, et donc lorsqu’il n’existe pas [Page 239] entre eux et la victime un régime d’indemnisation de nature contractuelle tel que la garantie de qualité ou l’obligation de renseignement 14 . En effet, l’un des choix législatifs importants effectués par le législateur aux termes de la réforme du Code civil était d’infirmer la décision de la Cour suprême dans Wabasso Ltd. c. National Drying Machinery Co. 15 , permettant jadis l’option entre les régimes contractuel et extracontractuel. L’article 1458 , alinéa 2 C.c.Q. énonce que le cocontractant ne peut « se soustraire de l’application du régime contractuel de responsabilité pour opter en faveur de règles qui [lui seraient] plus profitables ». Étant donné la consolidation que nous venons de commenter du recours direct contractuel au profit du sous-acquéreur aux termes du Code civil du Québec , le sous-acquéreur se voit muni « d’armes » de nature contractuelle importantes contre les personnes nommées à l’article 1468 C.c.Q. La politique législative prohibant l’option entre les deux régimes fait donc en sorte que l’acheteur bénéficiaire d’un recours direct ne puisse opter pour la responsabilité extracontractuelle des biens entachés d’un défaut de sécurité. Il n’en demeure pas moins que la responsabilité extracontractuelle des biens entachés d’un défaut de sécurité est un autre élément essentiel avec lequel le vendeur professionnel doit composer dans la délimitation de sa responsabilité générale. En effet, les vendeurs professionnels énumérés à l’article 1468 C.c.Q. sont tous susceptibles, à titre de tiers à l’égard de la victime, de tomber sous le coup de ses dispositions et d’être appelés à l’indemniser. III. LES RÈGLES D’EXCLUSION DE LA GARANTIE DE QUALITÉ a) La garantie existe de plein droit Les auteurs français classiques écrivaient que la garantie est de la nature de la vente, mais pas de son essence. C’est dire, de prime abord, que la simple conclusion juridique d’un contrat de vente donne, de droit et sans qu’il y ait besoin d’une stipulation particulière, naissance à la garantie légale, incluant la [Page 240] garantie de qualité. Aux termes du Code civil du Québec , l’article 1716 reprend cette règle, en énonçant que « ces garanties existent de plein droit, sans qu’il y soit nécessaire de les stipuler dans le contrat de vente ». Il en découle, notamment, que pour exclure la garantie, il ne suffit pas de ne pas l’inclure dans le texte du contrat. Il faut une exclusion claire pour en écarter la présence. Il faut noter, néanmoins, l’existence d’une seule exception d’exclusion de plein droit. L’article 1731 C.c.Q. édicte que la garantie de qualité est exclue de la vente faite sous « l’autorité de la justice »....\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n... ur ». Selon le texte de l’alinéa 2, cette exception est restreinte aux vendeurs dits « non professionnels ». Cependant, il est loin d’être clair si l’objectif du législateur était de restreindre la liberté contractuelle du vendeur professionnel ou d’élargir celle du vendeur ordinaire. Une interprétation littérale du texte paraît autoriser une exclusion efficace de la garantie par le vendeur privé qui ne révèle pourtant pas les vices cachés dont il a connaissance. Cependant, cette solution serait un non-sens et serait diamétralement opposée aux règles de bonne foi 42 , de même qu’à la règle impérative de l’article 1732 C.c.Q., selon laquelle le vendeur ne peut exclure sa responsabilité pour son fait personnel 43 . Il est plus probable que l’emploi d’une exclusion conformément à cet alinéa n’accorde une protection efficace au vendeur ordinaire que dans la mesure où il ne s’est livré à aucun acte de dol. [Page 247] vi) Conclusion L’existence de plusieurs règles de fond qui limitent la liberté contractuelle en matière de garantie influe grandement sur l’effet des exclusions contractuelles dans les contrats de vente. Ce n’est pas alors la clarté de la formulation de l’exclusion qui détermine son efficacité ni l’intention des parties. Indépendamment même d’une formulation cristalline quant à toute exclusion, les règles de fond interviennent alors pour modifier l’effet réel de la stipulation d’exclusion de garantie. Examinons ensuite comment ces règles de forme et de fond exercent leur effet à l’endroit des clauses contractuelles et d’exclusion de la garantie. IV. CLAUSES D’EXCLUSION COURANTES ET COMMENTAIRES a) Formulaires des contrats de vente d’un immeuble de la Chambre des notaires du Québec 44 i) Prendre l’immeuble dans l’état où il se trouve, déclarant l’avoir vu et examiné à sa satisfaction 45 Tel que nous l’avons déjà indiqué 46 , et ce, selon une abondante jurisprudence, cette clause n’opère pas l’exclusion de la garantie légale de qualité. ii) Cette vente est faite sans autre garantie que celle de ses faits personnels 47 Cette exclusion gagnerait à être reformulée. En premier lieu, il n’est pas clairement possible, selon la formulation, de savoir à quelle personne le mot « ses » s’applique. On peut imaginer qu’il s’agisse du vendeur 48 , mais la clause n’est pas claire. En deuxième lieu, la clause accorde à l’acheteur une garantie par rapport aux faits personnels du vendeur. En effet, nous avons vu que, selon l’article 1732 [Page 248] C.c.Q., le vendeur ne peut se dégager de ses faits personnels, et que cette règle est de nature impérative. Il y a cependant une différence entre le fait de ne pas pouvoir exclure la responsabilité pour des faits personnels fautifs et le fait d’offrir une garantie à l’égard de ceux-ci. La qualification de cet engagement à titre de garantie formelle vient d’un article du Code civil du Bas Canada 49 . Le Code civil du Québec , quant à lui, a abandonné cette qualification, et il ne nous paraît donc ni nécessaire ni approprié de continuer à l’employer. En troisième lieu, il ne paraît guère idéal de formuler une exclusion en périphrase. Il est préférable d’exclure ou de ne pas exclure. L’exclusion par défaut ou pour toute « autre » responsabilité nous paraît introduire une ambiguïté inutile. En quatrième lieu, le dessein probable du rédacteur de cette clause est d’exclure la garantie légale. À cet égard, il vaudrait mieux que l’exclusion le déclare expressément, en précisant que la garantie de propriété et la garantie de qualité sont exclues, et ce, de préférence mais non obligatoirement, en mentionnant les articles du Code civil du Québec qui créent ces garanties. iii) Cette vente est faite sans garantie et sans restriction du prix de vente au cas où le titre de propriété serait vicié, sauf la garantie de ses faits personnels 50 Cette clause, qui vise à exclure la garantie de propriété, soulève les mêmes commentaires que celle que nous venons d’examiner, sauf qu’elle énonce sans ambages, au profit de l’acheteur, une « garantie » des faits personnels présumément de la part du vendeur. iv) Cette vente est faite sans aucune garantie et aux risques et périls de l’acheteur 51 Cette clause est généralement acceptable. Elle peut toutefois être bonifiée. En effet, une certaine jurisprudence exigeante en matière de garantie de qualité oblige le vendeur à se référer spécifiquement, dans la clause d’exclusion, à la garantie de qualité ou à la garantie contre les vices cachés (de préférence en spécifiant l’article pertinent du Code civil du Québec ) afin de confirmer l’intention claire de l’acheteur de renoncer à cette protection juridique d’ordre légal. [Page 249] b) Société canadienne d’hypothèques et de logement La Société canadienne d’hypothèques et de logement (SCHL) inclut dans ses offres de ventes la clause suivante : L’acheteur doit accepter la propriété dans son état actuel sans aucune garantie légale à l’égard du droit de propriété à l’exception de la garantie du vendeur quant à ses actes et faits personnels. De plus, le vendeur vend sans garantie légale de qualité, notamment en ce qui a trait à la garantie contre les vices cachés ainsi que la garantie de durabilité, tant pour l’immeuble que pour les mobiliers et accessoires. L’avantage de cette clause est qu’elle exclut spécifiquement la garantie légale de propriété et la garantie légale de qualité. Elle restreint l’engagement par rapport aux faits personnels, à la garantie de propriété uniquement. De plus, afin d’éliminer toute ambiguïté, le rédacteur a précisé que la vente excluait la garantie contre les vices cachés. Deux autres éléments appellent des commentaires. La « garantie de durabilité » est également exclue. Premièrement, il est difficile de savoir exactement à quelle garantie le rédacteur de la clause se réfère. Il nous paraît probable qu’il s’agit de la règle de preuve énoncée à l’article 1729 C.c.Q. lors de la vente conclue par le vendeur professionnel. Selon cet article, l’existence d’un vice est présumée lorsque le bien se détériore d’une manière prématurée. Tel que mentionné aux Commentaires du ministre de la Justice concernant cet article 52 , la règle s’est inspirée de la Loi sur la protection du consommateur . La formulation de l’article paraît indiquer qu’elle est de nature impérative. La jurisprudence est restée muette jusqu’à maintenant sur cette question. Deuxièmement, il nous paraît superflu de préciser que l’exclusion comprend également des accessoires du bien vendu. Les accessoires font partie de l’objet vendu 53 . L’exclusion de la garantie à l’endroit du bien principal emporte donc exclusion pour l’accessoire. c) Clause usuelle d’une compagnie de fiducie Une clause usuelle d’exclusion de garantie, que nous trouvons fréquemment utilisée dans les contrats conclus par une compagnie de fiducie qui vend un immeuble, et ce, typiquement dans le cadre d’une succession, se lit comme suit : L’acheteur par les présentes, accepte la propriété ci-haut décrite dans l’état où elle se trouve, l’ayant visitée et s’en déclare satisfait. L’acheteur reconnaît que le prix de [Page 250] vente a été établi en considération du fait que la présente vente est faite sans aucune garantie de la part du vendeur, sauf de ses faits personnels, le vendeur n’ayant jamais occupé les lieux. Ainsi, il est une condition essentielle de la présente qu’elle soit faite sans aucune garantie de la part du vendeur en ce qui concerne tout vice caché, de quelque nature que ce soit, ni aucune autre garantie, sauf de ses faits personnels, et l’acheteur reconnaît et accepte l’exclusion de garantie et le fait qu’il n’aura aucun recours légal contre le vendeur. Certains commentaires s’imposent. Il aurait été plus clair de se référer à la garantie de qualité et à la garantie de propriété. Le rédacteur aurait pu également se référer aux articles du Code civil du Québec qui les créent. Nous noterons que la clause ne réfère pas à une vente faite aux risques et périls de l’acheteur. Enfin, soulignons que la clause en question est probablement partiellement non opposable à l’acheteur. Il est évidemment contradictoire de réserver, d’une part, les droits de l’acheteur quant aux faits personnels du vendeur et, d’autre part, de faire admettre à l’acheteur qu’il « n’aura aucun recours légal contre le vendeur ». d) Clause usuelle de revente d’une compagnie de financement après la prise en paiement Une clause d’exclusion fréquemment utilisée lors de la revente d’un immeuble par des compagnies de financement après reprise de possession se lit comme suit : L’acheteur reconnaît que le vendeur n’a jamais habité la propriété et il déclare l’accepter dans son état actuel après l’avoir soigneusement examinée, renonçant expressément à tous recours relatifs aux vices de construction apparents ou cachés, l’acheteur achetant à ses risques et périls. Cette clause gagnerait à être davantage précisée. Il vaudrait mieux se référer spécifiquement à la garantie de qualité ou à la garantie contre les vices cachés, en précisant les articles du Code civil du Québec pertinents plutôt que de se référer à tous recours relatifs aux vices de construction apparents ou cachés. En effet, la garantie de qualité protège l’acheteur contre des phénomènes qui ne sont pas uniquement des vices de construction, comme la présence de fourmis, de rats ou de vermine, la localisation d’un bâtiment dans une zone d’inondation, l’utilisation antérieure non conforme ainsi que bien d’autres phénomènes qui, n’étant pas des vices de construction, ne sont donc pas exclus par cette clause. V. L’APPLICATION DE GARANTIES AUTRES QUE CELLES DE LA VENTE Il est important de noter que le contrat de vente peut faire naître, au profit de l’acheteur, des garanties en matière de qualité qui ne sont pas celles du [Page 251] contrat de vente. Ces garanties proviennent du contrat d’entreprise ou de service. En vertu de l’article 1796 C.c.Q., la vente de toute unité immobilière à usage d’habitation, bâtie ou à bâtir, par un vendeur-entrepreneur ou un promoteur immobilier est assujettie aux garanties du contrat d’entreprise ou de service. Deux autres garanties légales distinctes et supplémentaires doivent donc être notées. En premier lieu, l’article 2118 C.c.Q. accorde une protection de cinq ans contre toute perte de l’immeuble 54 . Cette responsabilité n’est pas uniquement encourue par le vendeur-entrepreneur, mais également de manière solidaire par l’architecte et l’ingénieur, s’ils ont surveillé les travaux, ainsi que par le sousentrepreneur pour les travaux qu’il a réalisés. En deuxième lieu, l’article 2120 C.c.Q. crée en faveur du propriétaire une garantie contre les malfaçons existantes au moment de la réception de l’ouvrage ou qui sont découvertes dans l’année qui en suit la réception. Cette responsabilité est conjointe et est engagée par toutes les personnes mentionnées précédemment. Signalons que cette protection supplémentaire n’est pas uniquement réservée à l’acheteur d’une unité destinée à l’habitation. L’article 2124 C.c.Q. au chapitre du contrat d’entreprise ou de services assimile le promoteur immobilier qui vend, même après son achèvement, un ouvrage qu’il a construit ou fait construire, à un entrepreneur, et donc le soumet aux garanties que nous venons d’examiner. VI. LES CRITÈRES ET LES MODES D’ÉVALUATION DU VICE CACHÉ Énumérons brièvement les conditions permettant de vérifier ou d’infirmer, selon le cas, l’existence d’un vice caché. De prime abord, soulignons que le seul fait de ne pas être apparent ne suffit pas. En effet, il y a quatre conditions (et non une seule) qui doivent être satisfaites afin de pouvoir qualifier juridiquement un vice de « vice caché » conformément à l’article 1726 C.c.Q. : l’antériorité, la gravité, l’ignorance de l’acheteur et le caractère occulte du vice. Normalement, la présence des trois premières conditions se vérifie aisément en répondant aux questions suivantes : Est-ce que le vice existait avant la vente ? Est-ce que les conséquences du vice sont suffisamment graves que l’acheteur n’aurait pas acheté le bien ou ne l’aurait pas acheté sans insister pour obtenir une réduction du prix ? Est-ce que l’acheteur avait connaissance du vice avant la vente ? [Page 252] En revanche, la question beaucoup plus difficile, dont les réponses remplissent une part importante de nos recueils d’arrêts, est de savoir si le vice est occulte ou apparent. Depuis l’arrêt Placement Jacpar inc. c. Benzakour 55 de la Cour d’appel dont l’opinion unanime a été rendue sous la plume du juge LeBel, J.C.A. (tel qu’il était alors), les tribunaux québécois ont adopté une appréciation circonstancielle sur la question de la qualification occulte d’un vice. M. le juge LeBel s’était alors exprimé ainsi : [La détermination du caractère occulte] impose l’examen de l’ensemble des circonstances de chaque cas. Elle prend en considération des facteurs et le statut du vendeur, s’il est professionnel ou non, celui de l’acquéreur, la nature, l’âge et le prix [du bien], le type de vice et parfois le comportement des parties. 56 Il faut donc éviter de croire que le caractère occulte d’un vice est quelque chose de fixe. Ce n’est pas parce qu’un vice particulier a été considéré comme occulte – et donc a satisfait à la dernière condition pour être qualifié de vice caché –, lors d’une vente impliquant certaines parties qu’il doit nécessairement l’être pour toujours. Il faut examiner chaque fois l’ensemble des circonstances. Il est même concevable, et cela s’est déjà produit, que le même vice soit considéré comme apparent lors de la vente d’un bien à un acheteur professionnel et qu’il soit considéré comme occulte lorsque ce même bien est vendu à un acheteur ordinaire 57 . Prenons chacun des éléments mentionnés par le juge LeBel pour illustrer la manière dont ces éléments influent sur la qualification occulte d’un vice. Chacun des facteurs a la possibilité d’augmenter ou de diminuer l’ampleur de l’ex...\\n\\n\", \"url\": \"https://app-dev.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2002-2-ie2dead07-dfa9-618c-5fc1-c2d9522ec3a7-la-garantie-de-qualite-dans-les-contrats-de-vente-au-dela-des-apparences\", \"captions\": [], \"highlights\": {}, \"extra_data\": {}, \"score\": 0.028588563203811646, \"from_reperage\": false, \"metadata\": {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2002-2-ie2dead07-dfa9-618c-5fc1-c2d9522ec3a7-la-garantie-de-qualite-dans-les-contrats-de-vente-au-dela-des-apparences\", \"title\": \"Edwards, Jeffrey - La garantie de qualité dans les contrats de vente : au-delà des apparences, dans Cours de perfectionnement du notariat (2002)\", \"url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2002-2-ie2dead07-dfa9-618c-5fc1-c2d9522ec3a7-la-garantie-de-qualite-dans-les-contrats-de-vente-au-dela-des-apparences\", \"@search.score\": 0.028588563203811646, \"is_anchor\": false, \"cited_by_jurisprudence\": false, \"citing_decisions_count\": null, \"law\": null, \"article_number\": null, \"convergence\": 6, \"graph_endorsement\": 0.0, \"provenance\": [{\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"query\", \"query\": \"Garantie de qualité et vice caché en droit civil québécois dans une vente immobilière : conditions relatives au caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché, pour un litige devant la Cour du Québec en 2025.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"proposition\", \"query\": \"garantie de qualité et vice caché en vente immobilière\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:terminologie\", \"query\": \"Recours en garantie de qualité en matière de vente immobilière : vice caché au sens du Code civil du Québec. Caractère grave, antérieur à la vente, vice inconnu de l’acheteur et réellement caché; régime de la responsabilité du vendeur et conditions d’exonération en cas de connaissance ou d’inspection.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:consequences\", \"query\": \"Vices cachés : exigences d’ouverture déterminent le remède (résolution ou réduction du prix) et le fardeau de preuve de l’acheteur. Appréciation du « réellement caché » et de la connaissance. Discussion sur la distinction avec la garantie légale de conformité et la prescription applicable au recours.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"reformulation:position_inverse\", \"query\": \"Moyens de défense du vendeur : absence de vice grave, défaut postérieur à l’acte, ou vice observable lors d’une inspection raisonnable. Contestation du caractère « réellement caché » et de l’ignorance de l’acheteur. Conclusion au rejet du recours en garantie de qualité faute de conditions d’exercice.\"}, {\"source\": \"semantic\", \"search_type\": \"DOCTRINE\", \"kind\": \"hyde:doctrine\", \"query\": \"La garantie de qualité en vente impose une responsabilité spécifique lorsque le vice répond aux conditions cumulatives : gravité, antériorité, caractère réellement caché et ignorance de l’acheteur. La doctrine insiste sur la méthode d’appréciation « d’hypothèse raisonnable » du degré de dissimulation et sur la charge probatoire relative à la nature et à l’origine du défaut. Elle distingue aussi le vice caché des manquements à une obligation d’information ou des défauts apparents, et discute l’incidence d’une inspection diligente sur l’élément « inconnu ».\"}], \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2002-2-ie2dead07-dfa9-618c-5fc1-c2d9522ec3a7-la-garantie-de-qualite-dans-les-contrats-de-vente-au-dela-des-apparences\"}, \"token_count\": 6886, \"total_cost\": 0.0, \"original_title\": \"\", \"original_url\": \"https://app.caij.qc.ca/fr/doctrine/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2002-2-ie2dead07-dfa9-618c-5fc1-c2d9522ec3a7-la-garantie-de-qualite-dans-les-contrats-de-vente-au-dela-des-apparences\"}, {\"id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2011-2-i8cdb5562-9b5b-d4e2-ad6f-43b1f0a4c5d8-la-garantie-de-qualite-developpements-recents-et-difficultes-decoulant-de-lapplication-du-recours-direct-aux-vendeurs-multiples\", \"document_id\": \"/fr/doctrine/document/cours-de-perfectionnement-du-notariat-2011-2-i8cdb5562-9b5b-d4e2-ad6f-43b1f0a4c5d8-la-garantie-de-qualite-developpements-recents-et-difficultes-decoulant-de-lapplication-du-recours-direct-aux-vendeurs-multiples\", \"author\": null, \"publication_source\": null, \"title\": \"Edwards, Jeffrey - La garantie de qualité : développements récents et difficultés découlant de l’application du recours direct aux vendeurs multiples, dans Cours de perfectionnement du notariat (2011)\", \"content\": \"CHUNK INDEX: 0\\n... La garantie de qualité : développements récents et difficultés découlant de l’application du recours direct aux vendeurs multiples La garantie de qualité : développements récents et difficultés découlant de l’application du recours direct aux vendeurs multiples Jeffrey Edwards * Table des matières SYNTHÈSE INTRODUCTION 1. LE DOMAINE D’EXCLUSION DE LA GARANTIE DE QUALITÉ 2. LE SENS PARTICULIER DE VICE EN VERTU DE LA GARANTIE DE QUALITÉ 3. L’APPRÉCIATION DU CARACTÈRE OCCULTE OU NON APPARENT DU VICE 4. LES AUTRES CONDITIONS ESSENTIELLES DEVANT ÊTRE RESPECTÉES 5. LES EXIGENCES CONCERNANT LES DÉLAIS 6. LA PRÉSOMPTION DE CONNAISSANCE DU VENDEUR PROFESSIONNEL 7. LE RECOURS DIRECT ET SON APPLICATION AUX VENDEURS MULTIPLES CONCLUSION BIBLIOGRAPHIE Lois Doctrine Jurisprudence [Page 225] SYNTHÈSE Ce texte comporte deux objectifs principaux. Dans un premier temps, l’auteur fera le point sur les développements jurisprudentiels en matière de garantie de qualité. Les développements concernant les éléments juridiques particuliers suivants seront examinés, à savoir : 1. le domaine d’exclusion de la garantie ; 2. le sens particulier de vice en vertu de la garantie légale de qualité ; 3. l’appréciation du caractère occulte ou non apparent du vice ; 4. les autres conditions essentielles devant être respectées ; 5. la présomption de connaissance du vendeur professionnel. Dans un deuxième temps, l’auteur soulignera les difficultés d’adaptation de ces notions lors de la mise en œuvre de la garantie de qualité par le biais du recours direct et de son application aux transactions immobilières où les vendeurs multiples sont simultanément obligés à la garantie de qualité envers le dernier acheteur. [Page 227] INTRODUCTION Le présent texte vise à faire le point sur les développements jurisprudentiels récents en matière de la garantie de qualité, plus précisément, en ce qui a trait : 1) aux exclusions de la garantie de qualité ; 2) à la notion de vice ; 3) à l’appréciation du caractère occulte ou non apparent du vice ; 4) aux autres conditions essentielles devant être respectées pour que la garantie de qualité trouve application ; 5) aux exigences concernant les délais ; 6) à la présomption de connaissance du vendeur professionnel et 7) au recours direct en garantie de qualité. C’est ce dernier aspect qui présente le plus de difficultés pratiques, difficultés qui seront soulignées et analysées au cours de cette conférence. 1. LE DOMAINE D’EXCLUSION DE LA GARANTIE DE QUALITÉ Le domaine d’exclusion de la garantie de qualité ne peut être abordé sans d’abord présenter le domaine de l’application de la garantie de qualité. En effet, il est important de rappeler que la garantie de qualité existe de plein droit lors de la vente 1 . C’est pour cela que la doctrine française avait pris coutume d’énoncer que la garantie est de la nature de la vente. Rappelons que la garantie de qualité trouve application s’il existe, notamment, un vice grave, non apparent, inconnu de l’acheteur et qui est antérieur à la vente 2 . Nous discuterons de chacune de ces conditions plus tard. [Page 228] Précisons également que la garantie de qualité trouvant assise dans le droit de la vente n’est pas restreinte à celui-ci. En effet, cette même garantie peut naître également de la conclusion de certains contrats autres que la vente, tels que l’échange 3 , la dation en paiement 4 , le bail 5 , le crédit bail 6 et l’emphytéose 7 . En premier lieu, le domaine d’exclusion est circonscrit par les ventes exclues selon le droit commun. L’article 1731 C.c.Q. 8 énonce que la garantie de qualité ne s’applique pas à la vente faite « sous l’autorité de la justice ». La même règle s’applique au contrat de consommation, car la vente judiciaire n’est pas soumise à la Loi sur la protection du consommateur (ci-après « L.p.c. ») 9 . Il y a lieu de distinguer deux types de ventes « sous l’autorité de la justice » : les ventes judiciaires forcées et les ventes judiciaires volontaires . En effet, le libellé de l’article 1531 du Code Civil du Bas-Canada , aujourd’hui l’article 1731 C.c.Q., prévoyait explicitement que l’exclusion légale de la garantie de qualité ne touchait que les ventes judiciaires forcées 10 , à savoir les ventes sur saisie ou en exécution d’un jugement, de même que les autres formes de dépossession de l’État 11 . Toutefois, la formulation de l’article 1731 C.c.Q. est plus large et, en principe, toute vente faite « sous l’autorité de la justice » est visée. En effet, il y a lieu de croire que cette exclusion de droit commun s’étend maintenant aux ventes judiciaires volontaires des personnes placées sous un régime de protection. Ceci est compréhensible étant donné que les personnes incapables ne peuvent pas réellement donner leur consentement, sauf par l’intermède d’une fiction juridique, soit par l’intermédiaire de leur représentant, et que tout le processus est encadré par le Tribunal 12 . En deuxième lieu, l’exclusion de la garantie de qualité peut résulter de la volonté des parties au contrat de vente. En effet, l’article 1732 C.c.Q. prévoit que les parties peuvent, par leur contrat, diminuer l’effet des obligations de la garantie [Page 229] légale ou l’exclure entièrement 13 . Ainsi, si la garantie peut être considérée de la nature de la vente, la garantie n’est pas de son essence . Ainsi, pour exclure la garantie de qualité, sous réserve de certaines limites que nous analyserons plus loin, il est possible de prévoir une clause à cet effet dans le contrat de vente, sauf si c’est un contrat de consommation étant donné que les dispositions de la L.p.c. sont d’ordre public 14 . Toutefois, pour être efficace et opposable, la clause de non-garantie doit prévoir expressément, de manière claire et non équivoque, que la vente est faite sans garantie de qualité, et de préférence en précisant les articles pertinents prévus au Code civil du Québec 15 . Par exemple, depuis longtemps, une clause stipulant que l’acheteur accepte le bien dans son état, est considérée comme une clause de style qui n’opère pas l’exclusion de la garantie de qualité 16 . Tel qu’illustré par la jurisprudence, l’intention d’exclure la garantie de qualité s’évalue en fonction non seulement du libellé de la clause de non-garantie, mais encore du comportement des parties 17 . Ainsi, le comportement du vendeur qui rassure l’acheteur relativement à l’existence d’une garantie peut avoir pour effet de rendre inopposable une clause d’exclusion de garantie, autrement claire 18 . Par ailleurs, il est à noter que les clauses de non-garantie de qualité sont soumises à une interprétation restrictive 19 et donc, en cas de doute quant à l’intention d’exclure la garantie de qualité, cette dernière subsiste 20 . [Page 230] En troisième lieu, la possibilité d’exclure la garantie de qualité par voie contractuelle comporte plusieurs limites. En effet, le vendeur ne peut pas se dégager de ses faits personnels tel que stipulé à l’article 1732 in fine C.c.Q. Il est à noter que sont considérés comme des « faits personnels » tant les actes dolosifs que les actes non dolosifs 21 . Par exemple, selon la jurisprudence récente, le vendeur ne peut pas se dégager de sa responsabilité en présence des propos rassurants ou des fausses déclarations du vendeur pour induire en erreur l’acheteur 22 , c’est-à-dire l’omission du vendeur de révéler à l’acheteur des vices dont il avait connaissance 23 . Ainsi, l’exclusion de la garantie de qualité n’est pas opposable à l’acheteur si le vice avait été apparent n’eût été des artifices, déclarations ou manœuvres frauduleuses ou propos rassurants du vendeur ou si le vendeur connaissait le vice, mais a omis de le révéler à l’acheteur 24 . La même règle découle également de l’article 1733 C.c.Q. 25 . Cependant, lorsque le vendeur est un non-professionnel et que la vente se fait « aux risques et périls de l’acheteur » 26 la connaissance du vice par le vendeur ordinaire ne rend pas inopposable une clause d’exclusion de la garantie de qualité 27 , sauf en cas de dol 28 . Il faut garder à l’esprit qu’aucune exclusion de garantie n’est permise pour le vendeur professionnel étant donné que ce dernier est présumé connaître le vice, à moins qu’un tel vendeur réussisse à repousser la présomption qui pèse contre lui 29 . La présomption de connaissance du vice qui vise les vendeurs professionnels sera abordée plus tard dans le cadre de ce texte. [Page 231] Également, en vertu des limites contractuelles d’ordre général, soit l’article 1474 C.c.Q., rappelons que la responsabilité civile ne peut être limitée quant au préjudice corporel ou moral, de même que pour les dommages causés par une faute intentionnelle ou lourde 30 . Ceci s’applique également en matière de responsabilité du vendeur relativement à des vices cachés. 2. LE SENS PARTICULIER DE VICE EN VERTU DE LA GARANTIE DE QUALITÉ Un vice au sens général est constitué d’une dérogation aux normes établies. Ces normes peuvent être celles du fabricant ou encore celles suivies dans le secteur industriel impliqué 31 . Les normes peuvent être écrites ( Code national du bâtiment , codes applicables aux différents secteurs concernés, réglementation fédérale, provinciale ou municipale) ou encore non écrites, mais observées (les bonnes pratiques de l’industrie et les règles de l’art non codifiées). Cependant, un vice au sens de la garantie de l’article 1726 C.c.Q. reçoit une interprétation plus nuancée. Ces dérogations aux normes constituent des vices dès qu’elles entraînent une destruction totale ou partielle de l’utilité ou de [Page 232] l’usage auquel l’acheteur est en droit de s’attendre 32 . Aussi, ce sont les « attentes raisonnables de l’acheteur » qui déterminent et définissent l’ampleur de l’utilité protégée en vertu de la garantie de qualité 33 . Afin de présenter un portrait global de ce qui constitue ou non un vice dans le domaine de la garantie de qualité, il est utile de faire un survol de la jurisprudence récente à ce sujet. Par exemple, ont été considérés comme des vices cachés : 1) des problèmes d’infestation par des colonies de fourmis 34 , 2) des problèmes d’infestation de chauves-souris 35 , 3) des problèmes de fondation 36 , 4) un lac contaminé aux cyanobactéries qui empêche les acheteurs de se baigner 37 , 5) des problèmes de ventilation dans la toiture d’un immeuble 38 , 6) des problèmes de fonctionnement du drain 39 , 7) des problèmes de gel touchant le système d’alimentation en eau 40 , 8) des problèmes de moisissure 41 , 9) des problèmes de sol contaminé à l’huile de chauffage 42 , 10) des problèmes de sol contaminé par la présence de matériaux de remblai 43 ou encore 11) des problèmes de sol contaminé par la présence de pyrite. Toutefois, la Cour a souligné que la seule présence de pyrite dans un matériau de remblai ne constitue pas en soi un vice au sens de la garantie de qualité 44 . En effet, il faut démontrer la probabilité de désordres pouvant nuire à l’usage ou à l’utilité du bien à l’avenir 45 . Il n’est pas nécessaire que des dégâts importants se soient produits pour que la présence de pyrite soit considérée comme un vice, la possibilité que de tels dégâts importants se produisent suffit 46 . [Page 233] La garantie protège en effet les attentes raisonnables, et donc, objectives de l’acheteur. Des craintes ou des appréhensions uniquement subjectives ne sont pas protégées. Or, lorsque l’existence des problèmes de ce genre (par exemple, pyrite, ocre ferreuse, amiante) est confirmée et que ces problèmes occasionnent des travaux inévitables ou encore, avant même la réalisation de ces travaux, une baisse de valeur de l’immeuble, nous sommes d’avis que le seuil de protection des attentes raisonnables de l’acheteur est atteint. Ainsi, un tel problème constitue déjà un vice indemnisable en vertu de la garantie de qualité et il faut examiner si ce vice satisfait aux autres conditions applicables (non apparent, grave, connu de l’acheteur, antériorité) 47 . Il faut cependant que le vice existe encore si l’acheteur demande l’annulation de la vente ou une réduction du prix, un vice déjà réglé par le vendeur ne suffit pas 48 . De même, le défaut d’usage qui résulte de la mauvaise utilisation du bien après la vente ne constitue par un vice 49 . Nous avons constaté que certains des problèmes qualifiés de « vice » en vertu de la garantie et entraînant des déficits d’usage ne s’inscrivent pas dans l’interprétation traditionnelle du terme « vice », en ce sens qu’ils ne constituent pas des dérogations aux normes reconnues, à titre d’exemples : les fourmis, les chauves souris et l’affaissement des fondations entraîné par des arbres à proximité ou des travaux municipaux 50 . Ainsi, même en l’absence d’une dérogation aux normes reconnues, la vérification et validation objectives d’une diminution d’usage normal (évaluée selon les attentes raisonnables de l’acheteur) suffisent pour qualifier le phénomène de vice en vertu de la garantie de qualité. Un développement jurisprudentiel constant qui découle logiquement de ce raisonnement est à l’effet que le respect des normes applicables de l’époque n’est pas une défense au recours alléguant un vice caché si l’usage fourni par le bien est en deçà des attentes raisonnables de l’acheteur 51 . [Page 234] 3. L’APPRÉCIATION DU CARACTÈRE OCCULTE OU NON APPARENT DU VICE Une des conditions de l’application de la garant...\\n\\nCHUNK INDEX: 2\\n... qu’un vendeur qui était entrepreneur en construction, mais « autoconstructeur » de sa maison, habitée par ce dernier pendant plusieurs années, n’était pas un vendeur professionnel 88 . Les faits particuliers en cause dans un dossier peuvent varier et peuvent être déterminants. [Page 241] La présomption de connaissance du vice est réfragable 89 , mais seulement dans des circonstances particulières et les moyens pour le faire varient selon le type de vendeur professionnel en question 90 . La présomption à laquelle est tenu le fabricant est la plus difficile à repousser. La Cour suprême a énoncé que les moyens sont limités, soit la mauvaise utilisation du bien, le cas fortuit ayant lieu après la vente et le risque de développement inconnaissable suivant l’état des connaissances spécifiques et techniques au moment de la vente du bien 91 . Nous pouvons alors parler d’une présomption, en pratique, « presque irréfragable », pour le fabricant 92 . En ce qui concerne l’entrepreneur en construction, la présomption de connaissance du vice est également « presque irréfragable ». En effet, même si on reconnaît la possibilité de réfuter la présomption, le haut niveau de diligence qui leur est imposé par la loi rend très limité le spectre des moyens réels pour effectivement repousser cette présomption 93 . Le fardeau pour repousser la présomption de connaissance du vendeur professionnel spécialisé est très élevé. Le vendeur doit prouver qu’il ne pouvait soupçonner ou déceler le vice par un moyen raisonnable, notamment en apportant la preuve d’une conduite irréprochable comme vendeur professionnel dans le secteur commercial impliqué 94 . Enfin, pour un vendeur professionnel non spécialisé, le fardeau pour repousser cette présomption est moins lourde, car le test applicable alors est celui de la négligence pour un tel vendeur dans le secteur d’activité commercial concerné. 7. LE RECOURS DIRECT ET SON APPLICATION AUX VENDEURS MULTIPLES L’application du recours direct à la garantie de qualité soulève des interrogations sérieuses, surtout dans le domaine des immeubles. [Page 242] Le recours direct vise à protéger l’acheteur d’un bien qui a un vice caché en lui permettant d’intenter un recours en garantie de qualité, non seulement contre son vendeur immédiat, mais également contre les vendeurs antérieurs du bien, en autant que le bien était vicié au moment de la vente antérieure impliquée. Selon ce raisonnement, ceux qui auraient vendu par le passé un bien défectueux, en obtenant un prix correspondant à la pleine valeur du bien, devraient être mis à contribution pour le coût des travaux correctifs lorsque le problème est enfin découvert. D’abord une création de la jurisprudence, tirant son origine du droit français 95 , la Cour suprême, en 1979, dans l’arrêt Kravitz 96 , a fondé le recours sur la théorie que la garantie de qualité est un accessoire intimement liée au bien vendu et qui se transmet avec lui lors de chaque vente du bien abandonnant en principe la conception traditionnelle, selon laquelle la garantie contre les vices cachés était une créance personnelle née au moment de chaque vente 97 . Lors de la réforme du Code civil, le recours direct a été codifié à l’article 1442 C.c.Q. qui se lit comme suit : 1442. Les droits des parties à un contrat sont transmis à leurs ayants cause à titre particulier s’ils constituent l’accessoire d’un bien qui leur est transmis ou s’ils lui sont intimement liés . [L’italique est nôtre.] La conception traditionnelle impliquait que le fabricant ne pouvait être condamné qu’en vertu de la garantie accordée à son acheteur immédiat. Ainsi, l’insolvabilité du vendeur immédiat, même non responsable des vices cachés en question, rendait le recours de l’acheteur illusoire 98 . Avec les nouvelles techniques de commercialisation des biens 99 , la conception classique des obligations de garantie entre vendeurs successifs a fait place à une théorie plus moderne qui permet au sous-acquéreur de poursuivre directement non seulement son propre vendeur, mais également tous les vendeurs intermédiaires, et ce en remontant au premier vendeur 100 . L’arrêt Kravitz concernait un bien meuble, soit une automobile. Cependant, en 1999, la Cour d’appel du Québec a confirmé, ce qui était conforme aux textes applicables, que le principe dégagé dans Kravitz s’appliquait de manière généralisée à la vente de tout bien, et donc également aux biens immeubles 101 . [Page 243] La théorie de l’accessoire adoptée par l’arrêt Kravitz faisait l’objet de critiques sérieuses au moment de la réforme du Code civil en 1994 102 . Le problème principal demeure les difficultés à déterminer la vente de référence pour examiner les conditions d’application de la garantie de qualité pour le recours intenté par le dernier acheteur. Est-ce la dernière vente ? La première vente ? Une des ventes intermédiaires ? Selon la théorie juridique de l’accessoire du bien, la garantie issue de chaque vente serait ensuite transmise et retransmise au sous-acquéreur 103 . Or, cela impliquerait que le sous-acquéreur n’aurait droit à la garantie du premier acheteur que sous certaines conditions. Par conséquent, l’exclusion de la garantie légale de qualité ou la non-application de la garantie de qualité en raison du non-respect d’une des conditions essentielles analysées précédemment, à l’occasion d’une vente dans la chaîne des ventes, empêcherait l’acquéreur d’intenter un recours efficace contre les vendeurs antérieurs à ladite vente, en particulier le premier vendeur du bien vicié. Ainsi, par exemple, la dénonciation du vice par le premier vendeur entraînerait-elle l’exclusion de la garantie de qualité à l’égard de tout acheteur subséquent étant donné qu’il n’est pas possible de transmettre plus de droits qu’on ne possède soi-même 104 ? En serait-il de même si le premier acheteur découvre le vice, mais ne le dénonce pas dans un délai raisonnable 105 ? En poussant ce raisonnement à l’extrême, une jurisprudence très limitée avait soutenu momentanément que l’acheteur ne pourrait pas exercer un recours en garantie pour vices cachés après la revente du bien, et ce même contre son propre vendeur 106 . Heureusement, la Cour d’appel a remis les pendules à l’heure en spécifiant que le recours en vertu de la garantie de qualité pour vices cachés demeure un droit personnel et donc, malgré la revente de l’immeuble, l’acheteur ne perd pas la qualité et l’intérêt pour agir contre ses vendeurs pour vices cachés 107 . Ceci est réaffirmé par la jurisprudence récente 108 . Cependant, il est légitime de se demander alors comment la garantie de qualité peut constituer à la fois un droit accessoire du bien et un droit personnel 109 . Considérant ce qui précède, il nous est permis d’avancer que la justification du recours direct ne réside guère dans la transmission d’un droit, mais constitue [Page 244] un droit en soi, distinct, reposant sur la garantie de qualité attribuée à l’occasion de la vente. Mais, dans le cas de plusieurs ventes successives du bien, la question posée précédemment demeure : quelle est la vente de référence selon laquelle les conditions d’application d’un vice caché doivent être vérifiées ? Bien qu’en principe, en matière de vente de biens meubles, la vente de référence soit la dernière vente, soit la vente impliquant l’acheteur ultime, ce n’est pas toujours le cas. En effet, dans certains cas, la vente de référence est la vente en litige qui peut correspondre à la dernière vente, mais non nécessairement ; il peut également s’agir de la première vente, voire d’une vente intermédiaire. En effet, en 1994, aux termes de l’article 1730 C.c.Q., le législateur a renforcé le recours direct en venant préciser que les vendeurs intermédiaires dans la chaîne de vente d’un bien sont tenus à la garantie de qualité envers le sous-acquéreur comme s’ils agissaient à titre de vendeurs immédiats 110 . Le législateur établit donc un lien contractuel direct, par fiction juridique, non seulement entre le fabricant et l’acheteur, mais aussi entre ce dernier et tout distributeur ou fournisseur, voire tout entrepreneur d’un bien immeuble. La jurisprudence est venue confirmer que l’article 1730 C.c.Q. s’applique également à la vente immobilière. Par conséquent, chacun des vendeurs visés par cet article est responsable directement envers chaque acquéreur subséquent des vices cachés relativement au bien. Ainsi, par exemple, l’exclusion de garantie entre le fabricant, le distributeur ou le détaillant n’affecterait plus le recours du sous-acquéreur contre ces derniers 111 . Cette interprétation se comprend aisément étant donné que le fondement de la garantie contre les vices cachés vise à assurer l’usage du bien 112 . Or, les vendeurs visés par l’article 1730 C.c.Q. n’acquièrent pas le bien pour son usage, mais dans le but de le revendre. Le législateur vise à protéger l’usage de ce bien par le dernier acheteur. Cet article, visant tant les biens meubles que les biens immeubles 113 , peut s’appliquer également au vendeur-entrepreneur. En vertu des articles 1442 et 1730 C.c.Q., en ce qui concerne les biens meubles, c’est en principe la dernière vente intervenue entre les parties qui devrait être prise en considération afin de déterminer si la garantie de qualité trouve application, à l’exception de la [Page 245] condition d’antériorité qui devrait être examinée uniquement par rapport à la vente du vendeur impliqué à notre avis. Or, par souci d’équité, en matière de vente d’un immeuble résidentiel, l’application de cet article doit se faire avec prudence. En effet, en matière de vente immobilière résidentielle, il faudrait à notre avis tenir compte des circonstances réelles de chaque vente, prise individuellement 114 , à moins d’abus. Ainsi, concernant les biens immeubles, la vente de référence peut varier notamment selon que nous sommes en présence d’un immeuble commercial ou résidentiel et si l’immeuble a été vendu par un vendeur professionnel ou non. Pour l’acheteur qui a acheté une maison sans garantie légale de qualité 115 , cette exclusion paraît valable par rapport au vendeur antérieur, sauf si le vendeur était un professionnel, soit un vendeur entrepreneur. En effet, tel qu’analysé précédemment, un tel vendeur est présumé connaître le vice et ne peut donc pas exclure la garantie de qualité, à moins de repousser la présomption. Donc, pour résumer, l’objectif de la garantie de qualité étant de permettre l’usage du bien, la détermination de la vente de référence pour décider de l’application ou non des conditions d’application de la garantie de qualité contre les vices cachés varie en fonction du type du bien, meuble ou immeuble, du type de vendeur, professionnel ou ordinaire et, bien sûr, de la condition d’application. En ce sens, la question de la « vente de référence » applicable pour juger les conditions de la garantie ressemble à celle concernant la présomption de connaissance dont l’application et l’intensité varient selon le type de vendeur, professionnel ou non, spécialisé ou non, fabricant ou non. Tout comme les tribunaux ont réussi à développer, au fil des ans, des règles cohérentes et prévisibles concernant la présomption de connaissance, les tribunaux se voient maintenant conférés par le législateur la tâche importante de développer, surtout en matière immobilière, les règles et les distinctions requises par rapport à la « vente de référence » afin de permettre une application prévisible et équitable du recours direct de la garantie de qualité. CONCLUSION Pour conclure et résumer, la garantie de qualité contre les vices cachés est l’objet, même de nos jours, de nombreuses controverses et soulève encore des passions, notamment en ce qui concerne l’application du recours direct, et ce, surtout en matière immobilière. Le législateur a donc, tout comme pour l’application de la présomption de connaissance, délégué la gestion nuancée de cette question aux tribunaux. [Page 246] BIBLIOGRAPHIE Lois Code civil du Québec , L.Q. 1991, c. 64 . Loi sur la protection du consommateur , L.R.Q., c. P-40.1 . Doctrine DESLAURIERS , J. , « Le droit commun de la vente », dans École du Barreau , Collection de droit 2010-2011 , Cowansville , Éditions Yvon Blais , 2010. EDWARDS , J. , La garantie de qualité en droit québécois , 2 e éd. , Montréal , Wilson & Lafleur , 2008, par. 275 et s . EDWARDS , J. ,